window.COURSE = {"built": "2026-10-05 01:46", "weeks": [{"key": "01", "dir": "week01_intro", "date": "Mon 16 Feb 2026", "topic": "Introduction to Robot Learning", "summary": "Course logistics (paper discussions, four homeworks, group projects on SO-101 arms, 20/40/40 grading), then why robotics is hard: Shakey and sense-plan-act, robots falling when state estimation fails, Moravec's paradox. Ends with why now (cheap humanoid hardware, data scaling, foundation models) and the framing that runs through the whole course: robotics as multimodal sequence modeling.", "key_ideas": ["Robot learning = solving robotics (perception, control) with machine learning (imitation, RL, representation learning)", "Moravec's paradox: abstract thinking is easy for AI, sensorimotor skill is hard", "A policy maps image + language instruction to an action; a VLM maps image + prompt to text - same shape of problem", "Scaling needs three ingredients: large datasets, large models, scalable evaluation", "Imitation learning assumes i.i.d. data; in RL actions change future states"], "papers": [], "homework": ["hw1_pytorch_tutorial"], "lecture": {"id": "X0k14u6pSxw", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 1: Introduction to Robot Learning | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "1:04:35", "description": "Lecture 1 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [], "paragraphs": [{"t": 1, "text": "Okay. I think we'll uh we'll kind of get started. Um So, I'm uh Mark Pollefeys, um professor here in the department, but I'm also um I am in the Department of Computer Science, but I'm also working at uh Microsoft uh leading the Spatial AI Lab. Um and uh Oyer is uh Oyer Mes is one of the researchers uh in the lab. There, he's in particular really driving things forward on robot learning and so on, and we identified that there was actually this was something that was missing at ETH in the curriculum a uh a course really focused on robot learning. And so um we had uh we decided that then Oyer uh could essentially uh fill in that gap. Um and so that's uh what you'll have here. So, I'll serve as the mentor also for the course. A lot of people from the lab will actually also help as TAs and so on. Um so, you'll also see there will be"}, {"t": 62, "text": "some robots to play with actually during the course, all these kind of things. So, we've taken care of all those things on on the ETH side. Um so, um although Oyer from at Microsoft will essentially teach this course, it's fully integrated uh within ETH and we'll have all of the usual things and resources and so on. Okay. And so, without further ado, uh I'll I'll uh let uh Oyer kind of um uh take over. Um may maybe one one more small thing. Yeah. Uh so, Oyer was um you know, did his PhD not too far from here um uh at um uh in um uh where is it again? Uh Freiburg. Oh, you have it there. Uh Okay, perfect. Yeah, in Freiburg. Uh wait. Uh there. Um and um uh and then essentially after that did his uh postdoc uh at Berkeley uh worked there with Sergey Levine and and people like that. Uh and then now he's uh here in Zurich. And so, yeah, I'll leave it"}, {"t": 121, "text": "at that. Thank you. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Um yeah, thanks everybody for for coming to this first lecture. Do you hear me okay also in the back? Yes? Awesome. Uh yeah, really appreciate you all coming. I'm very excited for this course. Also appreciate you all coming despite the the weather today. As as Mark pointed out, I spent the last 2 year in in sunny California. And the winters there were definitely different to the winters in Zurich. But still super excited to be here. And you know, talk a little bit about robot learning. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, maybe like I will today start first with some of the course logistics. So, if in case you need to reach me, uh my contact details are here. Um but more importantly, like first I want to talk a little bit about like to show you the why behind this"}, {"t": 180, "text": "course. So, the the collage here represents essentially work I have been doing during my time in uh Framework, in Berkeley, and also now in Zurich. And so, these are not really just like cool demos, but they are like experimental results of the very robot learning frameworks that we will be studying in this course in this semester. Um so, from the drawer opening task here with the Google robot on the right, uh to mobile navigation, well, I think the pointer doesn't do, or drone flying. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh we will use the same imitation learning and reinforcement learning algorithms. Um or these are using the same algorithms that you will be implementing in this semester. first with the individual homework assignments and later with real world group projects."}, {"t": 246, "text": "Of course managing a course at this scale is a massive team effort and I'm incredibly fortunate to be joining this semester by a team of exceptional TAs. Our team includes Alexi, Jonas, Liam, Nicola, Tianxu, Simon, Nicole and Sado as well as Carl who's on the next slide and they are working really hard to help create and validate the homework assignments. And they will also be your main point of contact to answer questions and help with um the exercises on the Thursday sessions. Maybe like the TAs that are here you can stand up like so people can see who you are."}, {"t": 302, "text": "And by the way like we still have open TA positions because like the course seems to be very popular. We have 290 enrolled students right now. So if you're interested in helping also TA this course especially if you're like still a master students please also contact Liam, Jonas, Alexi and so forth. We will be excited to to talk to you about if you want if you can help us with the TAs. So without further ado the plan for today is to cover like the course logistics first and the goals the goals and logistics Um, then um, a little bit on like why you should like study robot learning. Um, so, um, this course is not really, uh, about spending all all the semester just like training VLAs,"}, {"t": 360, "text": "uh, etc., but it is, um, trying to be a comprehensive curriculum where we will first kind of cover the basic algorithmic frameworks, as I said before, from imitation through reinforcement learning, um, and [clears throat] also expose you to different tools and philosophies, uh, also in, uh, robot learning. And so, um, we will cover, uh, fundamentals first in the first kind of half, uh, uh, in the first part of the course, um, and then we will also have some hands-on, uh, experience with the real robots, um, and we will also cover, uh, frontier models and more advanced topics, uh, in robot learning towards the end of the um, of the course. But, uh, really most important goal of this, uh, course is to inspire you. Um, robot learning is really a fascinating, um, field because, like, you get exposed to everything from, uh, from frontier high-level frontier modeling down to like low-level PD control that actually moves the robot"}, {"t": 420, "text": "hardware, um, and in computer vision and NLP, as you all have seen, we have see, uh, we have witnessed this like massive paradigm shift, right? Where we've went from, um, like individual, uh, kind of like tailored specialist models that were doing like all these things, like translation, object detection, image captioning, and so forth, um, to kind of this more, um, transformer-based foundation models, um, and uh, like this lead to essentially like the chat GPT moment, right? In uh, computer vision and and like in AI in general, right? And in robotics, we are kind of standing on the shoulder of giants, right? So, we have decades of incredible progress in model-driven control and more recently we have begun &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; adopting the like transformative power of many of the advances made in the other communities like in vision and NLP."}, {"t": 480, "text": "And as you can see like by by this arrow like and specifically the word you in in it in the slide, we are now kind of on the verge of the chat GPT moment for robotics and my hope is that by the end of the semester some of you will find this field interesting enough to to essentially like join and you know, help us build it. All right. So, before we start talking about robot learning, I will briefly cover the course structure. So, we will here is the the website with all the information. On Mondays, we will have first around 45 minutes of lecture usually instruct given by me where we will as I said before cover like a lot of the algorithmic framework and fundamentals followed by paper discussion from like student-led paper discussions. I"}, {"t": 541, "text": "will talk about this in the next slide in a second. And then throughout the course at the end of this, we will have guest kind of lectures or spotlights not maybe not every week but on most of the weeks we will have like around 20 minutes or something from like different um researchers from both industry and academia kind of presenting some of their advances and cool work and trying to kind of fit them like topic-wise that Um, that these researchers more or less like um, are experts in the uh, field or topic that we will be covering like that particular day. Um, on Thursday uh, we will have the TA led uh, practice session. Um, here um, we will cover essentially the homework assignments. Um, discuss or give feedback, um, answer any questions you might have"}, {"t": 600, "text": "about the homeworks. Um, on some weeks like the TAs will also cover the solutions for the previous uh, assignments and so forth. And uh, in general um, there will be uh, four assignments first uh, individual assignments before we move to the uh, group projects with the real robots. Um, so for the paper discussions we will be doing on Mondays. Um, in the project website uh, for each uh, session um, you will find uh, around three different papers. And um, how we will try to do this, we will see if uh, it works with the number of students we have. Um, but the idea is that we will more or less have groups of four students. Um, and we will essentially have um, like one group that kind of will present the paper and another group that will"}, {"t": 660, "text": "you know, be kind of reviewer two or will try to like point out maybe some um, like some things that they could have done better or critique a little bit the the paper. Um, and so this will be around approximately 15 minutes per paper um, ideally. Uh, and um, so I have put the link um, on the website, but if you uh, if you want you can also just scan the QR code here right now. Um, this will take you to the form where you can sign up for one of the papers um that you like, that you would like to uh present that day um and during the semester. And you can also um decide if you want to more be on the group that presents the paper or yes, we have a question. This is separate from the project group, yes. Yes, so this will be like essentially like after the lecture like today, you know, like I will be talk Well, today"}, {"t": 721, "text": "there is no reading club but like starting next week after the first 40-45 minutes of lecture, um this will be student-led uh paper discussion. Yes. Yeah. Sorry, I didn't get the question. How many papers? Just one. One. Yeah. Yeah, yeah. So, I I think also because of the high number of students, I don't know how many will keep enrolled but uh I think like it should be one. I mean, obviously like I would recommend everybody to like read all the papers that are recommended for uh for all um uh lectures but in terms of like kind of presenting with your team, it will be just one. Cuz um there are also like other like assignments so, you know, doesn't make sense to uh ask too much."}, {"t": 781, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um speaking of uh individual homework, um we as I said before, we have um divided the uh course into two parts. In the first half, um you will be uh working on individual homework assignments. Um and here are more or less the the topics and the dates uh you will have to uh submit um these assignments. Uh we will use an autograder um to check um your assignments. And um yes, I think the first assignment should be out by the end of this lecture. Is this correct? Yes, the TA says yes. Cool. Uh and here is the uh GitHub link uh where uh it will be published. But this you can also find on the project course. Maybe one small advice um"}, {"t": 840, "text": "for the homework uh assignment, um this all a lot of the homeworks, maybe not the first one, but um they are based on training like uh neural networks, uh robot control policies, and uh although we try to make the homeworks faster to train by making the environments more simple, the domain simple, that you know, like it doesn't require a data center to run this model. Um in general, these still take some uh time to train. So, my advice uh is that please don't start the night before the deadline uh trying to solve like your exercise because you know, your model might not um have enough time to train or converge or maybe you made a mistake, you need to like retrain your model. So, uh like please like um start before like the the deadline night. Um"}, {"t": 901, "text": "for the course grading, um I'm sure everybody will do amazing uh for everything. Um we have decided more or less to divide this um in around 20% for the paper uh presentation and discussion, uh 40% for the practical homework, and uh 40% for the group project that will that you will do in the second half of the course with the real hardware. So there is no final exam but you know there is like you know some some work to do so you kind of get familiarized with all the frameworks and and tools and research in robot learning. Um Yes. So feedback is really welcome. It's my first time teaching a course at this scale."}, {"t": 960, "text": "Also for our TAs it's the first time and we are really trying hard to you know offer a great course but for sure we will make some mistakes because you know we are making things as we are going we will try to send every few weeks surveys to like a subset of the students to kind of get feedback on you know what are we doing well what can we improve and so on. So yeah just wanted to let you know that also because of the large number of students it makes things a bit more more difficult maybe to scale but we are trying our best and hope that we can we will offer a great course. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; All right and with this I think we have covered the logistics."}, {"t": 1021, "text": "Oh there's one more question. Sorry we I need the microphone I don't hear. Yeah just because it's always a problem with project based courses um how is the compute situation, let's say? Yeah, um, so for the first, um, like for the first homework assignments, the um, you should not need like a lot of compute. It should be possible to run these on like a like like a desk like a PC. Um, I think for the second half of the course where we will do like the real robot group projects, um, we are trying to get access to uh more compute um so that we could also do a little bit more, you know, like use bigger models and, you know, do a bit more cool stuff, um, but we are working on it."}, {"t": 1081, "text": "Um, so you said that like in the Excel sheet it says presenting group and what it like which size group. Should we sign up for groups like on a platform or how do you manage that? For the uh read it for the paper discussion? &gt;&gt; Yeah. The question is if you should sign up for one group? No, the question is that like should we just write our names or should we like I don't know right make a group on a Google Docs or I don't know how you work with that. I think you can just write your name, I think. It's it's fine. Let's not over complicate it. &gt;&gt; Like this is different from, you know, the groups that you will be doing later for the real robot like project. It's just like to present a paper. Could you maybe talk a bit more about the final project like what it's going to be or like what roughly the goal is there? Uh"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "yeah, um, so uh I think I have a slide later, but um essentially we have uh purchased like a fleet of 50 SO101 robots um thanks to the generosity of Mark. Um and um so these are like really cool robots um it's like from from Leo Robot um which also have like a puppeteering setup. Um so it's really easy also, you know, to collect like some demonstrations if you want to like uh like try to learn a new task with the robot. Um we [clears throat] will um the idea is that we will offer a set of canonical projects. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Sorry. Uh just because the load for the TAs would be way too big if we did like 50 different like uh group projects with these robots. Um but we will try to make uh to find a way um so that there is like these set of canonical projects"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "that, you know, you um kind of should be like more or less straightforward um to implement and you get some hands-on experience. Um but ideally we will also try to give some time in case, you know, like um you want to do some more advanced things with it and, you know, you're like creative and want to do some uh maybe more more complex things, we will try to fit this in also. Um but yeah, we'll we'll get to that like in around 6 weeks or so. Yeah. &gt;&gt; Okay, thank you. All right. Okay, that's a lot of questions, but yeah. So I just want to ask for clarification on the paper discussions again. So each group So there is like the criti- criticizing part and there is the presentation part. So does each group has to just choose one of these or does each group has to present at some point and then criticize also at some point? So I think um the original idea is that you either uh present or criticize. Um I"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "think we will see how many students are cuz we have to fit somehow all the students in this time slot. So, we will start with that and you know, if we see that this is not working out like we can maybe adapt it or something, but that would be the idea that you are at least either work on the um on one of the teams at least. Yeah. All right. Oh, Mark Mark also has a question. No, no, it was it was to clarify if I think for the projects we'll talk about it later also, but but I think the idea was on the one hand there will be this kind of technical projects. You know, that should give you allow you to get kind of good grades and everything, but then there will also be more challenging projects also for some of you. I know some of you are PG students also in the class here and so on. So, depending on where you're at and and what you're interested in, you might also be able to do really projects that go further also if you're PG student and align maybe with your research and then we can select an element of that that would fit as a"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "project. So, we'll have that flexibility um to do one of the canonical projects uh with your team or actually also to kind of go for something that's more challenging that's maybe you need already more background to do it or you need resources that we might not be able to provide, uh but if you already as a PG student have a robotic platform available, then maybe it makes sense to try something on that. So, that's that's a little bit the way we're thinking about it at this point. Mhm. Thanks. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; All right. Um so, now that we have cleared the logistics, um I want to move on a little bit into um robotics and robot learning. So, uh I'm going to start with a little bit of history um because as you all know, robots really have um long captured like our minds like um for kind of serving like as a blueprint for for what we are like building uh today in terms of robots, right? And uh this timeline is just It's like, you"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "know, like a cinematic timeline, um but it's also a sort of like a history of like the expectations, um our evolving expectations for like autonomy. Um and so in the early uh 20th century, uh like we started with like characters like the Q automaton or um like in Metropolis. Uh so these are like really old. It's like uh um over [clears throat] um around like 100 uh years old, right? And um by the '60s, uh Rosie the Robot um is kind of like a very popular um character which shifted um you know, like the first um like the first robots were kind of showing more like the industrial automation automation kind of like a blueprint, right? And then the our expectations shifted more towards utility and service. And then"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "with Star Wars with R2-D2 and so on, um like we started to think of robots um more um as social agents with distinct personalities instead of just like tools. Um and you know, kind of like more uh collaborative um uh personalities that then also led to things like Wall-E, right? But but moving to reality, like why do we actually build robots? So, while science fiction kind of, you know, explores our imagination, the the why is um kind of grounded in the necessity um and the tremendous potential that robots have for good in our society. Um because we are moving towards a future where um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh autonomous systems perform tasks that are fundamentally uh either dangerous um or um kind of distant and demanding. So, this can be uh, from the frontiers of like,"}, {"t": 1502, "text": "you know, interplanetary exploration um, to, you know, helping explore uh, like the depths of our oceans, um, where, you know, these are really hostile environments uh, for humans to survive in. Um, and [clears throat] also robots allow us to um, automate um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; some like, like, let's say dirty and dangerous jobs, uh, like, let's say, underground mining, for example. Um, or they can help after um, natural disasters and sort of like search and rescue operations um, to keep like people out of harm's way. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; as well as um, they also have a lot of applications in in in like in our health health care system and in medicine, right? Um, but perhaps more more critically, we are also looking at robots to solve um, like a um, like kind of like a looming social"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "crisis because as our western uh, societies grow older, um, we will need more people to um, help us um, and having um, robots that can assist and sort of perform personalized uh, assistance and care at home uh, will hopefully makes our lives also a little bit easier and reduce the burden on on our health care systems. Um, I want to start now with um, uh, a video that maybe some of you already have seen. Um, this is, is it playing? This is Shakey the robot. So, to understand where we are now in robotics, I think it's good to kind of see where it all started. At SRI, we are experimenting with a mobile robot. We call him Shakey. Our goal is to give Shakey some of the abilities associated with intelligence,"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "abilities like planning and learning. Even though the tasks we give Shakey seem quite simple, the programs needed to plan and coordinate his activities are complex. The main purpose of our research is to learn how to design these programs so that robots can be employed in a variety of tasks ranging from space exploration to industrial automation. Shakey operates in this experimental environment of rooms, doorways, and simple objects. The top lens belongs to Shakey's television camera. He uses it to obtain visual information about his own environment or other objects. &gt;&gt; These walls were made high enough so that Shakey cannot see over them with his television camera. Shakey uses these feelers or cat whiskers to tell him if he has bumped into anything. He can push objects with his push bar."}, {"t": 1683, "text": "The programs controlling Shakey reside in a large computer with 300,000 36-bit words of storage. &gt;&gt; Um so, this was kind of like the state of the art to the computer over a radio link. Wait. This was kind of the state of the art um in 1966. This was a robot developed in Stanford. Um and um um it essentially was like planning everything. It kind of didn't follow a script, but it was like doing planning. Um Are any of you familiar with the A* path planning algorithm? Well, oh, amazing. Um so, that algorithm was actually developed for this robot. Uh fun fact. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so yeah, um so this this is kind of where it all started. Shaky was doing the maybe many of you know like this sense plan act kind of"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "loop. As you as you've just seen it had like this this TV camera to kind of like do the visual scene analysis and then um it had like sonar range finders to detect the distances to all and it was like essentially um using A* for you know minutes or half an hour an hour before it decided you know like what is the next move I can do and then you know push the objects and so on. Um sorry. Um and from there like I think in the kind of 70s, 80s and 90s as you probably all know like we kind of moved towards more of this like industrial automation where you know we use like like kind of like industrial robotic arms to for manufacturing like let's say a car assembly and so"}, {"t": 1802, "text": "forth and these actually don't work too different from like Shaky the robot in the 60s it's very very similar. Um they sense the world um plan a geometric plan in in like their known map and then act with like really high precision. Um and this is like this has been incredibly successful but only in structured environments because essentially like they are state machines. So they're only as good as the logic that we can program into them and these robots are more or less like you know maybe now less but um, they used to be like very very blind. Um, so if, you know, a part on the assembly line like, uh, kind of like change, like they would not really like, uh, adapt, uh, to those changes. And so, the course, um, the goal in our course is to move beyond these like closed world assumption. Uh, we want to explore how robots can move, uh, past"}, {"t": 1862, "text": "rigid state transitions, uh, and instead kind of like use data-driven, uh, algorithms, um, to handle like, uh, you know, edge cases of the real world. Um, maybe as an, kind of like a more visual example, um, you might know some of these videos from from YouTube. Um, uh, for example, these are like, uh, some humanoid, uh, robots. Um, some of them also from Boston Dynamics, uh, which are like represent some of the most impressive hardware, uh, that we have built. Um, but even with like really world-class engineering, um, they typically have been relying on, um, near-perfect state estimation, right? Um, and so, this is like also, um, um, these are videos from the DARPA Robotics Challenge in 2015, where it kind of like the, uh, humanoid robotics, um, kind of started to gain more momentum. And, uh,"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "I mean, it's it's really interesting to see like how even simple tasks as like, you know, opening a door or like opening a valve like makes robot like flip backwards. It's [laughter] like uh, it's it's it's it's really funny. Um, and then, a few years later, like this is, uh, Boston Dynamics, um, the Atlas robot. Um, you can see you already see like they have QR codes all over the place because, you know, that's what they used to kind of like have this like perfect like world model or like state estimation to understand where is everything at all times and if you are even like you know a few centimeters off like you know like catastrophic things can happen. And um you know even like more recently um um yeah. Ouch. Um similar um issues also happen when"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "they do like things like parkour, right? So these are like really more or less almost blind movements or they assume perfect knowledge of like all the environment and uh even the slightest like um like error in perception or or planning results in you know these these robots like uh falling falling and you know breaking like their expensive hardware. Um this brings us to uh Moravec's paradox um who was a faculty at CMU um who formulated um this paradox back back in 1988 um and this kind of like captures why it is like so challenging uh why robotics is so challenging compared to other branches in AI. Um so he observed that essentially abstract thinking so the high-level reasoning we uh humans um typically find difficult to do, right? Uh like complex mathematics or like strategic planning or you know"}, {"t": 2041, "text": "playing chess or something is actually relatively easy for an AI to compute um and we have had algorithms that you know beat like human chess players and so forth uh for decades now. Um but the real paradox is um sensory motor skills, right? So basic physical interaction that animals and even toddlers find effortless uh is incredibly hard for AI. Um so um because essentially there is like no training data. They cannot like crawl the internet to understand like how to, you know, like grasp a bottle and and and drink from it. Um and you can see this like a little bit on this this images over here. Um so uh here is an example uh from my PhD uh 2019. I was trying to grasp this this mug desperately with my PR2 robot in Freiburg. And uh you know, this was based on point"}, {"t": 2100, "text": "cloud observations, you know, analytical grasp pose estimation and whatnot. And you see like it doesn't doesn't really work. And here, boom. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; It It breaks the the the the mug in two cuz it grasped too hard. So uh Yeah, we've made some progress in the since then, but it's it's really like fascinating, right? And then you you you see like this uh orangutans like they can even use like tools just like by looking at others and imitating and it looks so effortless, right? But this even this basic just like grasping a a a simple mug with a robot hand is like so difficult. Um Yeah. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So this brings us to the core of this course. So really if we want to move past um this like, you know, uh rigid state of machines on the factory floor, we need like a different approach. And uh robot learning is the study um of"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "algorithms that enable a robot to um acquire [clears throat] new skills or adapt to an unstructured environment um by learning from data and experience um rather than relying on, let's say, like explicit uh or hand-coded programming. And um this diagram like I tried to illustrate this a little bit um on the challenge we face. So on the one side, we have kind of the robotics, uh which is like rooted in the physical world, um and traditional, you know, we kind of traditional and we engineer solutions to it, we build models for it, and so on. And on the other hand, we have machine learning, uh which has really like thrived in, you know, the digital world, um with data-driven solutions um that can generalize with massive amounts of data. And robot learning is essentially the synthesis of these two. So, it's bringing about um it's about bringing generalization into the physical world uh to solve problems that are too complex to uh engineer uh manually."}, {"t": 2224, "text": "And um how do we actually bridge this gap um by uh of Moravec's paradox? So, the answer really lies in robot learning. So, in this course, we will define robot learning um simply as solving robotics via machine learning. And so, on the left um here I have put the pillars of robotics, um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, let's say perception and control. These are, let's say, the what of like how the robot sees the world and how the robot physically uh moves within it. And as I said before, um like historically, we've like done a lot of like great like model-based work on this. And on the right, we have the how, the machine learning. Um this is like the toolbox we will try to use um to, you know, fill some some gaps with this data-driven approaches. And we will look look into imitation learning, reinforcement learning,"}, {"t": 2280, "text": "representation learning, and all these things from uh machine learning. Now, before we get deeper into the algorithms, maybe let's um talk about how we define robots. Um so, on the left, we have dishwasher. Uh in the middle, we have the da Vinci, the surgical medical robot. And on the right, we have chat GPT on the phone. Um maybe um you could like talk for a 30 seconds to your neighbor and discuss if you think that any of these are a robot and if so, why? Somebody wants to share what they have discussed very quickly. Alexa, just put the mic to someone you find. Oh, Elisabetta. Uh yeah, we were discussing a bit and uh yeah, we think that dishwasher definitely it is a robot because it kind"}, {"t": 2342, "text": "of change the physical state of the world by operating something. ChatGPT definitely it is not a robot in a traditional sense because you can have some some intellectual uh interaction with it, but then the word stays as it is. And while the the machine that doctor uses, it kind of depend on whether it is only analytical or whether you can actually perform something by using it and uh if it is actually changing the world, but if you're controlling the machine yourself, then yeah, it is not really a robot because it's not automating the task. Yeah. Yeah, thanks. Yeah, that's that's that's really a great summary. So, you correctly identified, right? So, for like the surgical robot like this is essentially like a teleoperation. I mean, it does like some correction for like shaking of the humans and so on, but it's not like"}, {"t": 2400, "text": "autonomous in a sense. It's not like, you know, doing the search surgery like instead of the doctor, right? And for like the ChatGPT on the phone, like it doesn't have like an embodiment, although it has like very like high-level like reasoning uh capabilities, right? Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, yeah, in robot learning, we are in really interested in the intersection. We really want to arrive reliability of an appliance, um, the physical dexterity of a surgical arm, uh, and general-purpose reasoning of a foundation model. Um, good. Uh, we'll continue. Where's my pointer not doing? So, this this brings us to the fundamental question of why don't we already have autonomous robots in our homes, for example? Um, so, for this is a video from 2007, um, from Stanford, um, and it shows a robot called the PR1, um,"}, {"t": 2461, "text": "performing a bunch of useful everyday tasks, um, such as tidying up, um, a room. And, you know, maybe for those of you who are not familiar with uh, robotics, these tasks really seem easy and trivial, um, but, you know, as I said before, like, they are actually quite challenging for AI models at as it requires like common-sense understanding about how to operate in unstructured environments. Um, in reality, this robot was not autonomous, um, but it was being teleoperated, um, which, if you think about it, is actually a great sign because it means that we have had the hardware to perform, you know, like, similar like tasks, like, tidy up tidying up a room, uh, since 2007, um, but what has been missing is essentially the the software or the brain of this to make it autonomous, right? Um,"}, {"t": 2520, "text": "while the PR1 in 2007 showed us what is possible, um, still the hardware landscape has fundamentally shifted, um, on two critical fronts since then. On the left, you see like the explosion of humanoid robots with mood we have moved from let's say like isolated like research prototypes to really a competitive industry of sophisticated even commercially available platforms from companies like Figure, Tesla, Boston Dynamics and so on. And these are like embodiments that are capable of doing now way more dexterous tasks than the PR1 that I was just showing from 2007. But [clears throat] as we discussed before like a robotic platform without a brain is like essentially just a puppet. So this is why the image on the right is um perhaps maybe even more more important or or at least as as important as the"}, {"t": 2582, "text": "hardware because this represents also like um like the progress we have made in AI in general by the availability of you know massive compute through like improved GPUs and and so on, right? So back then in 2007 like we simply didn't have the processing power to train models that could you know that would scale to the required like generalist intelligence. While today's we we read today we really have like this clusters that run [clears throat] this like massive like scaling laws that work for NLP and vision for example. Um We've looked at hardware and compute, but now we also need to talk about you know like the data that kind of like fuels all of this, right? To understand why we had the ChatGPT moment in language, but we are still waiting for it in robotics. You have to look at the sheer scale of information being"}, {"t": 2641, "text": "consumed. To make these numbers a bit more easier to interpret, I tried calculating the average time it would take a human a single human to read or process this entire data sets. So, um so ImageNet it would take around 2 years like constantly just reading like the training set, right? And ImageNet is like now like quite uh it's quite some time ago. Um GPT-2 uh which is a model that still, you know, by AI standards like doesn't show maybe some of this emergent like capabilities uh from like later models, um but still is like a decently sized uh model, it would take around 60 years um to read its entire training corpus. Um and well, like this circles are not really to scale. I'm sorry about that."}, {"t": 2700, "text": "But Llama 3 um it would be like 90,000 years, right? Um so, [clears throat] um yeah, so this really also like is equally um shows you like how progress in like AI has driven um in this recent advances. In terms of Um finally, we come to the models themselves. Um I won't go into too much detail here, but um we've really see like uh as you all know like a breathtaking uh progression in algorithmic intelligence over the last uh few years. You know, we started with um computer vision um moving from simp simple classification um to uh models that uh really like parse the world with near uh pixel-perfect accuracy. Um then came AlphaGo uh which proved that uh reinforcement learning could uh master"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "um uh long-term strategic strategic reasoning, Uh um, out &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; outperforming world champions. And now we even have multi-model models like, uh, Gemini. Um, um, so this is really like impressive, right? And um, I think in, uh, even like, maybe like one one thing to note in AlphaGo is that, uh, it's not like a thing for the AlphaGo, it's not just about like that this model was able to beat, um, like the the world champion, but I think for like scientists, like the most interesting part of that was like this, uh, move 37. Um, maybe some of you have heard it. This was like a move that, um, like the model did like during, I think, the second, uh, match um, that was like so alien that first people thought that, uh, you know, it hallucinated or made a mistake, but then realized that it was kind of like a new strategy that, you know, it came up with by itself, um, that no other human had, uh, thought of"}, {"t": 2821, "text": "doing, uh, before. And so essentially, um, I think for robotics, we also want to move a little bit from, you know, this hand-coded er- era to the discovery era, uh, where robots solve physical problems in ways we also haven't maybe imagined ourselves yet. And this brings us to the North Star of the for the field of robot learning, which is achieving generalist robot policies. Um, as I was saying before, we are moving away from this era of specialist models that only know how to do like one task or one thing, uh, on one specific piece of hardware. And here the vision is really to build, uh, like robot foundation models that can take, you know, like a large robotic data set as an input, um, and output actions for any task, uh, on any robot in any environment. And so in this course, we will cover also like first the algorithmic frameworks, uh, used in robot learning before we dive into some of the more advanced"}, {"t": 2881, "text": "topics that revolve around answering some of these questions like where do we get this data from? How can we model such large heterogeneous distributions and so forth. And I think you know, if you kind of think about in the future, we are not there yet, but think you could imagine a future where you can just like download your kind of like robot favorite robot brain online and then out of the box your robot starts showing like reasonable behavior in a useful manner you know, for any robot like in a kind of plug and play sort of fashion. To close out this introduction, I want to show that we aren't just talking about like a theoretical future, but this is one that is being actively built right now. So on the left here, you can see the Open Next Embodiment Data Set. This was a massive international collaboration"}, {"t": 2940, "text": "with over 200 institutions around the world in 2004 that was trying to create like let's say like the ImageNet Data Set for robotics and we pulled together data from like 22 I think different robot embodiments. Everything from you know, single arms to dual arms to navigation robots and so forth to essentially create like this huge data set in like common like format that allowed essentially the community to start like developing and iterating on kind of this more like generalist robot policies for the first time and on the right side you can see some of these examples some example models that are first models that have been being trained on this data plus some additional like octo crude RT2 from Google Pi zero and so"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "forth. So, to finish this introduction, I want to give you a sneak preview of how this works. So, let's start with a basic example. So, when you typically train a robot policy, the model takes us an input a current image observation and a task instruction. Typically, it's a natural language instruction nowadays. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Such as like pick up the spoon and then the model predicts a sequence of actions like end effector poses that allows the robot to achieve this goal. Now, imagine that instead of doing this for a single task, we want to train this on a larger diverse heterogeneous data set. So, if we squint a bit and we replace the robot image with the more regular internet image like here like the Statue of Liberty. And we replace the instruction"}, {"t": 3062, "text": "with something like caption the scene, please. And then the model like answers like oh, this is this picture depicts the Statue of Liberty. This starts to look really familiar for you know, everybody who has you know, paid attention in computer vision, right? Because this you know, starts looking like a vision language model. So, the key idea the key idea that much of the latest advances in robot learning build upon is that we can view robotics as a similar problem where we are trying to learn a powerful a powerful model that maps from images and instructions to answers, just that in our case the answers are not text, but they are robot actions. &gt;&gt; [clears throat]"}, {"t": 3120, "text": "&gt;&gt; So, this means that um the way we by formulating the robotics problem as a multimodal sequence modeling problem, where we have language tokens, image tokens, and action tokens, we can also as an advantage, we can leverage many of the advancements made in other communities like in vision and NLP to drive progress in robot learning. Okay, so now probably like all of you are are thinking this. So, are you saying we can just train a large transformer on robot data and like we are done? Is this all this is about? Not quite. Luckily. Um because there are actually like some key ingredients and differences that makes robotics like harder to just like transfer all of these like things from from the other communities, right? Um like first of all, like the data, robotic data is is scarce."}, {"t": 3181, "text": "We don't have the luxury, although like we I just said we have like the ORC-C data set, it's still, you know, like not not comparable by any means to the data sets that the LLMs and so forth are being trained today. So, cuz typically a human has to like teleoperate and supervise the robot, so collecting robotic data is really really expensive and very time-consuming, so that's one of the issues. Um additionally, [clears throat] robot data is highly multimodal and heterogeneous, as instead of just like training only on natural language, we have several sensing modalities. We need to interact with in the real world. And every robot has typically different sensors and actuators. So that makes it also like more challenging to scale and to learn from. Besides, for robotics, we typically need to run this in real time. Like we"}, {"t": 3242, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So the model size actually matters. We can't take, you know, I don't know, like ChatGPT, like a 100 billion parameter model and put it on our quadruped because we can't like we don't we can't wait for a minute for, you know, the ChatGPT API to return an answer on what the next action should be. We really need like fast real-time control. And [clears throat] finally, evaluating also our models with real robotics hardware is actually quite tedious and inexpensive, too. Um So to begin with, we will start in the course looking at the algorithmic frameworks commonly used in robot learning today. These can be broadly classified into two major families that we will cover extensively throughout the"}, {"t": 3301, "text": "semester. On the left, we have imitation learning, which you can think of as supervised learning for the physical world. So this means that given a labeled action data set, our goal is to learn a function as is shown in the example before that maps an observation to an expert action. And this traditionally, this framework assumes that the inputs are IID, meaning they are independently and identically distributed. This means that each point does not influence any other data point and that all points come like from the same data distribution underlying probability distribution. And so a lot of like classic machine learning theory makes this assumption so that for example if your training data is IID then you know like your training and test performance like are mathematically like linked for"}, {"t": 3360, "text": "example. And then on the right we have reinforcement learning where the objective you know changes from like a behavioral cloning to learning a policy that given a state like is able to predict like the next best action. And this is typically done through trial and error within an environment to maximize our reward and crucially in robotics here the data is not IID. So this means that your actions like when you when you take an action with your robot this directly affects future states creating like a complex feedback feedback feedback loop &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; where as I said like like your action dictates like what the robot will perceive next and also learn next. Um So now bear with me we're going to start slowly this semester in the first half we will"}, {"t": 3421, "text": "focus on learning the fundamentals and the algorithms. We will start next week covering the basics of robot control and Markov decision processes which will provide the mathematical language we use to describe robot environment interactions. From there we will dive deep into the two algorithmic families I just described imitation learning both online and offline reinforcement learning for robot control. And to ensure [clears throat] that you know, you kind of like understand um, the algorithmic frameworks, um, we will um, start um, to ask you to implement these concepts um, in simulation for your first assignments. Um, not the first one, but like I think after the second or third. Um, here uh, we also um, have created uh, I think actually Liam created this uh, simulator in in MuJoCo um, which is using the the"}, {"t": 3482, "text": "same SO101 arm um, that we will later use for the real group projects, like this fleet of 50 robots um, that we mentioned before. Um, so that we have uh, some consistency and you know, maybe then you can reuse some of the code you wrote for your like individual homework assignments for uh, your group project with the with the real robot because it will be the same environment. Um, in the second half of the course uh, we will shift gears from the fundamentals to, you know, kind of the most exciting research that's happening in the field today. Uh, I call it scaling robot learning uh, for lack of a better name. Um, this is where we will move beyond, you know, the algorithms for the single task models and explore a bit how to build more general purpose uh, robot brains that we uh, discussed uh, earlier and we will kind of give an overview and and dive deep into um,"}, {"t": 3541, "text": "architectures, [clears throat] core architectures of the foundation model era uh, including generative models, learning how robots can imagine future uh, states to plan more effectively, um, sequence modeling and um, transformers uh, where we will apply or learn the same architectures and uh, attention mechanisms um, that power GPT but to the to the world of physical actions, um or world models, uh high-level reasoning, and so forth. Um and as I said before, oh, the video's not playing. Uh as I said before, um this is essentially the the robotic uh the robot we will use for the group projects. Um uh so this is the SO101. You can see here someone um is doing the puppeteering uh to collect some data uh with which then we can train some things. Okay. Um I'll try to cut it short. So,"}, {"t": 3602, "text": "um the goal of the course really is to inspire you um that robot learning is a cool field. Um we hope that after the course you will understand uh how we've gone um from the 2007 Stanford uh PR1 demo, um which was doing tidy up tasks, but with uh teleoperation, to the latest, like really impressive uh videos you you see from uh companies like physical intelligence, where you know, if you see it, like it's essentially like the same task, but now it's like autonomous, right? Uh so I think that's like super cool to understand how we've gone from, you know, um like the teleop demo from 2007 to something that, you know, seems to start working on on doing like this task. I think that's uh super uh super exciting. Similarly, we can also look at the field of autonomous driving um to kind of see the same paradigm shift,"}, {"t": 3660, "text": "where we've moved from the DARPA self-driving challenge uh in 2005, uh where vehicles like struggled to uh navigate deserts um using um you know, like all these sensors and kind of like the uh probabilistic robotics um kind of like um uh modeling frame framework um to in know, the latest demos where the Tesla uh, the the the new Teslas drive themselves to the to the new owners uh, which I think is uh, pretty impressive that they they can do like automated delivery. Probably like, you know, only if you're near if you live close by to the factory or something, but like still like it's it's a pretty cool. Um, and so [clears throat] we will focus more on manipulation in this course, but the same concepts and algorithmic frameworks that have driven um, progress uh, also work in in self-driving cars for sure. And finally also for humanoids, right? Where you can see that we have moved from um, the DARPA Robotics Challenge"}, {"t": 3721, "text": "which I was showing before in 2015 uh, where even the most advanced uh, humanoids were struggling with this basic capabilities of like even opening a door uh, or like walking straight um, to um, the the really like startling dexterity of like the latest like uh, figure um, uh, demo that is like super smooth and and dexterous. And really [clears throat] like um, and this is like also result from all this like, you know, robot foundation modeling. Um, and uh, whether it's self-driving self-driving cars in a city or a humanoid preparing a meal, um, the underlying shift is is is kind of the same where we are moving from, you know, like hard coding uh, and um, towards the era of like learned uh, physical intelligence. And so I hope that by the end of the course you will understand like the all the algorithmic"}, {"t": 3780, "text": "machinery um, that makes this transition possible. Um, so I want to finish today's lecture with this question. Why should you study robot learning? Um, as I said before, um, the field offers uh, the the field is incredibly interdisciplinary. Uh it requires really a broad and unique skill set. Um like you are not just like a mechanical engineer or a software engineer. Like you are working really across the entire stack. Um and in this course like you will touch everything from high-level reasoning in foundation models uh and training uh to low-level like uh PID controllers for the robots, right? Uh that make the the actual robot move. Um second, like the advances are universal. Uh as we see in the algorithmic frameworks that you learn here are applicable to almost um all robotic applications from self-driving cars to humanoids or robots exploring"}, {"t": 3841, "text": "this the space. Um so um and &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; I think that um as I said earlier, um these are one of the most exciting times uh to be working in in robot learning and maybe the advances that uh you will make one day will bring us to the ChatGPT moments for robotics. And with this uh thank you for attention and I hope you enjoy the course. &gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 1, "title": "Slide 1: About Me", "slide": "slide-1-about-me-frame-2"}, {"t": 246, "title": "Slide 3: Course Staff: TAs", "slide": "slide-3-course-staff-tas-frame-3"}, {"t": 246, "title": "Slide 4: Course Staff: TAs [continued]", "slide": "slide-4-course-staff-tas-continued-frame-4"}, {"t": 302, "title": "Slide 5: The Plan for Today", "slide": "slide-5-the-plan-for-today-frame-5"}, {"t": 360, "title": "Slide 6: Course Goal", "slide": "slide-6-course-goal-frame-6"}, {"t": 360, "title": "Slide 7: Course Goal: Inspire you!", "slide": "slide-7-course-goal-inspire-you-frame-7"}, {"t": 480, "title": "Slide 8: Course Structure", "slide": "slide-8-course-structure-frame-8"}, {"t": 600, "title": "Slide 9: Paper Discussion", "slide": "slide-9-paper-discussion-frame-9"}, {"t": 781, "title": "Slide 10: Individual Homework", "slide": "slide-10-individual-homework-frame-10"}, {"t": 781, "title": "Slide 11: Homework Advice", "slide": "slide-11-homework-advice-frame-11"}, {"t": 901, "title": "Slide 12: Course Grading", "slide": "slide-12-course-grading-frame-12"}, {"t": 901, "title": "Slide 13: Feedback Welcome", "slide": "slide-13-feedback-welcome-frame-13"}, {"t": 1380, "title": "Slide 14: Robots in Science Fiction", "slide": "slide-14-robots-in-science-fiction-frame-14"}, {"t": 1442, "title": "Slide 15: Why Robots", "slide": "slide-15-why-robots-frame-15"}, {"t": 1561, "title": "Slide 16: Shakey the Robot", "slide": "slide-16-shakey-the-robot-frames-16-23"}, {"t": 1802, "title": "Slide 17: Robot Engineering", "slide": "slide-17-robot-engineering-frame-24"}, {"t": 1862, "title": "Slide 18: When State Estimation Fails", "slide": "slide-18-when-state-estimation-fails-frames-25-31"}, {"t": 1980, "title": "Slide 19: Moravec’s Paradox (1988)", "slide": "slide-19-moravecs-paradox-1988-frames-32-33"}, {"t": 2160, "title": "Slide 20: Robot Learning [Venn diagram]", "slide": "slide-20-robot-learning-venn-diagram-frame-34"}, {"t": 2224, "title": "Slide 21: Robot Learning [definition]", "slide": "slide-21-robot-learning-definition-frame-35"}, {"t": 2224, "title": "Slide 22: Are These Robots?", "slide": "slide-22-are-these-robots-frame-36"}, {"t": 2400, "title": "Slide 23: Why Don’t We Already Have Autonomous Robots?", "slide": "slide-23-why-dont-we-already-have-autonomous-robots-frames-37-39"}, {"t": 2520, "title": "Slide 24: Recent Hardware Advances", "slide": "slide-24-recent-hardware-advances-frame-40"}, {"t": 2641, "title": "Slide 25: Recent AI Advances: Data Scaling", "slide": "slide-25-recent-ai-advances-data-scaling-frame-41"}, {"t": 2641, "title": "Slide 26: Recent AI Advances: Models", "slide": "slide-26-recent-ai-advances-models-frame-42"}, {"t": 2821, "title": "Slide 29: Recent Advances in Robot Learning", "slide": "slide-29-recent-advances-in-robot-learning-frame-43"}, {"t": 2940, "title": "Slide 30: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "slide": "slide-30-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-44"}, {"t": 3001, "title": "Slide 32: [untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]", "slide": "slide-32-untitled-vision-language-model-inputs-and-outputs-frame-45"}, {"t": 3062, "title": "Slide 33: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "slide": "slide-33-key-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-46"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-47"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 35: Ingredients for Scaling Robot Learning", "slide": "slide-35-ingredients-for-scaling-robot-learning-frames-48-51"}, {"t": 3301, "title": "Slide 36: Algorithms for Robotic Learning", "slide": "slide-36-algorithms-for-robotic-learning-frame-52"}, {"t": 3421, "title": "Slide 37: Course Syllabus: 1st Half", "slide": "slide-37-course-syllabus-1st-half-frames-53-56"}, {"t": 3482, "title": "Slide 38: Course Syllabus: 2nd Half", "slide": "slide-38-course-syllabus-2nd-half-frame-57"}, {"t": 3602, "title": "Slide 39: After the Course", "slide": "slide-39-after-the-course-frames-58-59"}, {"t": 3602, "title": "Slide 41: After the Course [continued]", "slide": "slide-41-after-the-course-continued-frames-60-62"}, {"t": 3780, "title": "Slide 42: Why You Should Study Robot Learning", "slide": "slide-42-why-you-should-study-robot-learning-frame-63"}], "toc_source": "estimated from slides (approx.)"}, "guests": [], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 16.02.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (64 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week01_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X0k14u6pSxw (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 1: Introduction to Robot Learning</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - Microsoft - 16.02.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 1: Introduction to Robot Learning Oier Mees - Microsoft - 16.02.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-about-me-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: About Me", "title": "Slide 1: About Me", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<ul>\n<li>From Basque Country, Spain</li>\n<li>PhD in Freiburg, Germany</li>\n<li>PostDoc in Berkeley, USA</li>\n<li>Research at Microsoft</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: four illustrated travel posters: a map of Spain with a pin on the Basque Country, a map of Germany with a pin on Freiburg, the Berkeley campanile, and Zurich.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3.]</span></p>", "text": "From Basque Country, Spain PhD in Freiburg, Germany PostDoc in Berkeley, USA Research at Microsoft [Figure: four illustrated travel posters: a map of Spain with a pin on the Basque Country, a map of Germany with a pin on Freiburg, the Berkeley campanile, and Zurich.] [Note: slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3.]", "t": 1}, {"id": "slide-3-course-staff-tas-frame-3", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Course Staff: TAs", "title": "Slide 3: Course Staff: TAs", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<p>Alexey Gavryushin, Jonas Pai, Liam Achenbach, Nicola Irmiger, Tianxu An, Šimon Sukup, Nicole Damblon, Zador Pataki</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a portrait photo above each name; each name also has an ethz.ch e-mail address, omitted here.]</span></p>", "text": "Alexey Gavryushin, Jonas Pai, Liam Achenbach, Nicola Irmiger, Tianxu An, Šimon Sukup, Nicole Damblon, Zador Pataki [Figure: a portrait photo above each name; each name also has an ethz.ch e-mail address, omitted here.]", "t": 246}, {"id": "slide-4-course-staff-tas-continued-frame-4", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Course Staff: TAs <span class=\"own\">[continued]</span>", "title": "Slide 4: Course Staff: TAs [continued]", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<p>Carl Brander</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: five grey placeholder silhouettes.]</span> Several open TA positions: This could be you!</p>", "text": "Carl Brander [Figure: five grey placeholder silhouettes.] Several open TA positions: This could be you!", "t": 246}, {"id": "slide-5-the-plan-for-today-frame-5", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: The Plan for Today", "title": "Slide 5: The Plan for Today", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Course Goals &amp; Logistics</li>\n<li>Why study Robot Learning?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: screenshot of the course web page (Computer Vision and Geometry Lab header, “Robot Learning - Oier Mees” banner over a collage of robot photos, then course title, semester, lecturer, teaching assistants, catalogue link 263-5911-00L, lecture Mon 16:15-18:00 in room NO C 60, and the format: each session begins with a lecture on the core topic followed by a student-led paper discussion, with short guest lectures on selected weeks).]</span></p>", "text": "Course Goals & Logistics Why study Robot Learning? [Figure: screenshot of the course web page (Computer Vision and Geometry Lab header, “Robot Learning - Oier Mees” banner over a collage of robot photos, then course title, semester, lecturer, teaching assistants, catalogue link 263-5911-00L, lecture Mon 16:15-18:00 in room NO C 60, and the format: each session begins with a lecture on the core topic followed by a student-led paper discussion, with short guest lectures on selected weeks).]", "t": 302}, {"id": "slide-6-course-goal-frame-6", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Course Goal", "title": "Slide 6: Course Goal", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Mastery of Fundamentals:</strong> Imitation, Reinforcement and Policy Learning</li>\n<li><strong>Practical Skills:</strong> Hands-on simulation and real-robot policy deployment</li>\n<li><strong>Frontier Models:</strong> Exploration of Foundation Models for robotics</li>\n<li><strong>Systems Design:</strong> Scalable pipelines for perception, control and reasoning</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Mastery of Fundamentals: Imitation, Reinforcement and Policy Learning Practical Skills: Hands-on simulation and real-robot policy deployment Frontier Models: Exploration of Foundation Models for robotics Systems Design: Scalable pipelines for perception, control and reasoning", "t": 360}, {"id": "slide-7-course-goal-inspire-you-frame-7", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Course Goal: Inspire you!", "title": "Slide 7: Course Goal: Inspire you!", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Top row, left of the “ChatGPT Moment” arrow:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Translation: Seq2Seq… <span class=\"own\">[Figure: unrolled recurrent network]</span></li>\n<li>Object Detection: R-CNN… <span class=\"own\">[Figure: R-CNN pipeline: 1. Input image, 2. Extract region proposals (~2k), 3. Compute CNN features, 4. Classify regions]</span></li>\n<li>Image Captioning: DenseCap.. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: CNN -&gt; region features -&gt; LSTM captioning pipeline]</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>-&gt; <strong>ChatGPT Moment</strong> -&gt; <strong>Transformer</strong> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: one block with a row of <strong>Images</strong> tokens and a row of <strong>Language</strong> tokens]</span> Vision-Language Models, Generative Models…</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Bottom row:]</span> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: covers of the <em>Springer Handbook of Robotics</em> (Siciliano, Khatib, eds.) and <em>Probabilistic Robotics</em> (Thrun, Burgard, Fox)]</span> -&gt; <strong>ChatGPT for Robotics?</strong> / you <span class=\"own\">[above and below the arrow]</span> -&gt; <span class=\"own\">[Figure: collage of robot photos]</span></p>", "text": "[Top row, left of the “ChatGPT Moment” arrow:] Translation: Seq2Seq… [Figure: unrolled recurrent network] Object Detection: R-CNN… [Figure: R-CNN pipeline: 1. Input image, 2. Extract region proposals (~2k), 3. Compute CNN features, 4. Classify regions] Image Captioning: DenseCap.. [Figure: CNN -> region features -> LSTM captioning pipeline] -> ChatGPT Moment -> Transformer [Figure: one block with a row of Images tokens and a row of Language tokens] Vision-Language Models, Generative Models… [Bottom row:] [Figure: covers of the Springer Handbook of Robotics (Siciliano, Khatib, eds.) and Probabilistic Robotics (Thrun, Burgard, Fox)] -> ChatGPT for Robotics? / you [above and below the arrow] -> [Figure: collage of robot photos]", "t": 360}, {"id": "slide-8-course-structure-frame-8", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Course Structure", "title": "Slide 8: Course Structure", "frames": [8, 8], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning</li>\n<li>Mondays 16:15-17:45 :\n<ul>\n<li>Lecture 45 min + 45 min Paper Discussion</li>\n<li>Guest lectures throughout course</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Thursdays 10:15-12:00\n<ul>\n<li>TA Led Practice Sessions</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Individual Homework: 4x assignments</li>\n<li>Group Projects on real SO-101 robots</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: photo of two small SO-101 robot arms on a table, one moved by a person’s hand.]</span></p>", "text": "Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning Mondays 16:15-17:45 : Lecture 45 min + 45 min Paper Discussion Guest lectures throughout course Thursdays 10:15-12:00 TA Led Practice Sessions Individual Homework: 4x assignments Group Projects on real SO-101 robots [Figure: photo of two small SO-101 robot arms on a table, one moved by a person’s hand.]", "t": 480}, {"id": "slide-9-paper-discussion-frame-9", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Paper Discussion", "title": "Slide 9: Paper Discussion", "frames": [9, 9], "html": "<ul>\n<li>3x papers per lecture, available at project course</li>\n<li>15 minutes per paper, groups of 4 students</li>\n<li>Group A presents &amp; defends paper, Group B criticizes it</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Enter paper groups here: <span class=\"own\">[Figure: QR code and a docs.google.com spreadsheet link for signing up.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: screenshot of the “Lecture Tentative Schedule” table from the course page, weeks 1-5 with each week’s paper-discussion readings.]</span></p>", "text": "3x papers per lecture, available at project course 15 minutes per paper, groups of 4 students Group A presents & defends paper, Group B criticizes it Enter paper groups here: [Figure: QR code and a docs.google.com spreadsheet link for signing up.] [Figure: screenshot of the “Lecture Tentative Schedule” table from the course page, weeks 1-5 with each week’s paper-discussion readings.]", "t": 600}, {"id": "slide-10-individual-homework-frame-10", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Individual Homework", "title": "Slide 10: Individual Homework", "frames": [10, 10], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Assignments: https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026</li>\n<li>Submission: Gradescope</li>\n<li>HW1: Pytorch &amp; Numpy Tutorial - 16.02-26.02</li>\n<li>HW2: Robot Control &amp; MDPs - 23.02-05.03</li>\n<li>HW3: Imitation Learning - 02.03-16.03</li>\n<li>HW4: Reinforcement Learning - 16.03-30.03</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the course page later lists different due dates (March 5, March 12, March 26, April 16).]</span></p>", "text": "Assignments: https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026 Submission: Gradescope HW1: Pytorch & Numpy Tutorial - 16.02-26.02 HW2: Robot Control & MDPs - 23.02-05.03 HW3: Imitation Learning - 02.03-16.03 HW4: Reinforcement Learning - 16.03-30.03 [Note: the course page later lists different due dates (March 5, March 12, March 26, April 16).]", "t": 781}, {"id": "slide-11-homework-advice-frame-11", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Homework Advice", "title": "Slide 11: Homework Advice", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Neural networks take some time to train!</li>\n<li>We try to make the homeworks fast to train</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three-panel meme of a man waiting alone, captioned “Me waiting for my neural network to finish training”.]</span></p>\n<p>Don’t start the night before the deadline working on the assignment!</p>", "text": "Neural networks take some time to train! We try to make the homeworks fast to train [Figure: three-panel meme of a man waiting alone, captioned “Me waiting for my neural network to finish training”.] Don’t start the night before the deadline working on the assignment!", "t": 781}, {"id": "slide-12-course-grading-frame-12", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Course Grading", "title": "Slide 12: Course Grading", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Paper Presentation &amp; Discussion (Group): 20 %</li>\n<li>Practical Homework (Coding Assignments): 40 %</li>\n<li>Final Project (Group): 40 %</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: cartoon “Course Grading Forecast”: a morning sun over books (Paper Presentation, 20%), a midday sun over a student coding with a robot arm (Practical Homework, 40%), and a rainbow over a robot with a pot of gold (Final Project, 40%).]</span></p>", "text": "Paper Presentation & Discussion (Group): 20 % Practical Homework (Coding Assignments): 40 % Final Project (Group): 40 % [Figure: cartoon “Course Grading Forecast”: a morning sun over books (Paper Presentation, 20%), a midday sun over a student coding with a robot arm (Practical Homework, 40%), and a rainbow over a robot with a pot of gold (Final Project, 40%).]", "t": 901}, {"id": "slide-13-feedback-welcome-frame-13", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Feedback Welcome", "title": "Slide 13: Feedback Welcome", "frames": [13, 13], "html": "<ul>\n<li>We are working hard to offer a great course</li>\n<li>We will probably make mistakes</li>\n<li>We would love to hear your feedback!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: meme of a speaker in sunglasses at a World Economic Forum podium, captioned “Are we improvising and going to make mistakes? For sure”.]</span></p>", "text": "We are working hard to offer a great course We will probably make mistakes We would love to hear your feedback! [Figure: meme of a speaker in sunglasses at a World Economic Forum podium, captioned “Are we improvising and going to make mistakes? For sure”.]", "t": 901}, {"id": "slide-14-robots-in-science-fiction-frame-14", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Robots in Science Fiction", "title": "Slide 14: Robots in Science Fiction", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: six film and TV stills, captioned:]</span> Q the Automaton, 1918 - Metropolis, 1927 - Star Wars, 1977 - The Jetsons, 1962 - Transformers, 2007 - Wall-E, 2008</p>", "text": "[Figure: six film and TV stills, captioned:] Q the Automaton, 1918 - Metropolis, 1927 - Star Wars, 1977 - The Jetsons, 1962 - Transformers, 2007 - Wall-E, 2008", "t": 1380}, {"id": "slide-15-why-robots-frame-15", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Why Robots", "title": "Slide 15: Why Robots", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: four photos, no text: a Mars rover; a da Vinci surgical robot with a surgeon at the console; miners drilling underground; an elderly person’s hands held by younger hands. Read as: space exploration, surgery, dangerous work, care.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: four photos, no text: a Mars rover; a da Vinci surgical robot with a surgeon at the console; miners drilling underground; an elderly person’s hands held by younger hands. Read as: space exploration, surgery, dangerous work, care.]", "t": 1442}, {"id": "slide-16-shakey-the-robot-frames-16-23", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Shakey the Robot", "title": "Slide 16: Shakey the Robot", "frames": [16, 23], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an embedded historical film playing across eight frames: Shakey, a wheeled robot with a camera and rangefinder mast (overlay text “Research performed by”), researchers working at a blackboard, Shakey navigating a room with large blocks and a ramp, close-ups of its camera head and of its bump-sensor bar.]</span></p>\n<p>Shakey the Robot, Stanford 1966</p>", "text": "[Figure: an embedded historical film playing across eight frames: Shakey, a wheeled robot with a camera and rangefinder mast (overlay text “Research performed by”), researchers working at a blackboard, Shakey navigating a room with large blocks and a ramp, close-ups of its camera head and of its bump-sensor bar.] Shakey the Robot, Stanford 1966", "t": 1561}, {"id": "slide-17-robot-engineering-frame-24", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Robot Engineering", "title": "Slide 17: Robot Engineering", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sense-Plan-Act</li>\n<li>Similar to Shakey (1966)</li>\n<li>Structured Environments</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a person programming an orange industrial robot arm with a handheld teach pendant; a car-body assembly line lined with orange robot arms; a warehouse where small mobile robots carry shelving pods.]</span></p>", "text": "Sense-Plan-Act Similar to Shakey (1966) Structured Environments [Figure: a person programming an orange industrial robot arm with a handheld teach pendant; a car-body assembly line lined with orange robot arms; a warehouse where small mobile robots carry shelving pods.]", "t": 1802}, {"id": "slide-18-when-state-estimation-fails-frames-25-31", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: When State Estimation Fails", "title": "Slide 18: When State Estimation Fails", "frames": [25, 31], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three embedded videos playing across seven frames, each a compilation of robots falling: “DARPA Robotics Challenge 2015” (humanoid robots toppling at a doorway and on rubble), “Boston Dynamics 2018” (an Atlas humanoid falling in a kitchen, labelled Boston Dynamics | TED), “Boston Dynamics 2022” (Atlas on a parkour course with orange barriers and boxes).]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three embedded videos playing across seven frames, each a compilation of robots falling: “DARPA Robotics Challenge 2015” (humanoid robots toppling at a doorway and on rubble), “Boston Dynamics 2018” (an Atlas humanoid falling in a kitchen, labelled Boston Dynamics | TED), “Boston Dynamics 2022” (Atlas on a parkour course with orange barriers and boxes).]", "t": 1862}, {"id": "slide-19-moravecs-paradox-1988-frames-32-33", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Moravec’s Paradox (1988)", "title": "Slide 19: Moravec’s Paradox (1988)", "frames": [32, 33], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Abstract thinking</strong>: hard for animals, easy for AI</li>\n<li><strong>Sensorimotor skills</strong>: easy for animals, hard for AI</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, a PR2 two-armed robot tidying objects on a table in front of an Albert-Ludwigs-Universitat Freiburg “TidyUpRobot” banner, captioned “Mees et al., 2019”; right, two orangutans handling branches.]</span></p>", "text": "Abstract thinking : hard for animals, easy for AI Sensorimotor skills : easy for animals, hard for AI [Figure: left, a PR2 two-armed robot tidying objects on a table in front of an Albert-Ludwigs-Universitat Freiburg “TidyUpRobot” banner, captioned “Mees et al., 2019”; right, two orangutans handling branches.]", "t": 1980}, {"id": "slide-20-robot-learning-venn-diagram-frame-34", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Robot Learning <span class=\"own\">[Venn diagram]</span>", "title": "Slide 20: Robot Learning [Venn diagram]", "frames": [34, 34], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Venn diagram. Left circle <strong>Robotics</strong>: Physical World, Engineer Solutions. Right circle <strong>Machine Learning</strong>: Digital World, Data-driven Solutions. Overlap: <strong>Robot Learning</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: Venn diagram. Left circle Robotics : Physical World, Engineer Solutions. Right circle Machine Learning : Digital World, Data-driven Solutions. Overlap: Robot Learning .]", "t": 2160}, {"id": "slide-21-robot-learning-definition-frame-35", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Robot Learning <span class=\"own\">[definition]</span>", "title": "Slide 21: Robot Learning [definition]", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<p>Solving <strong>Robotics</strong> via <strong>Machine Learning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Robotics: Perception, Control, …</li>\n<li>Machine Learning: Imitation Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Dynamics Learning, Representation Learning, …</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Solving Robotics via Machine Learning Robotics: Perception, Control, … Machine Learning: Imitation Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Dynamics Learning, Representation Learning, …", "t": 2224}, {"id": "slide-22-are-these-robots-frame-36", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Are These Robots?", "title": "Slide 22: Are These Robots?", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three photos: a dishwasher in a kitchen; a da Vinci surgical robot with a surgeon at the console; a phone camera view with a ChatGPT logo, the assistant pointing at a screw with the hint “Use this one for the shelf”.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three photos: a dishwasher in a kitchen; a da Vinci surgical robot with a surgeon at the console; a phone camera view with a ChatGPT logo, the assistant pointing at a screw with the hint “Use this one for the shelf”.]", "t": 2224}, {"id": "slide-23-why-dont-we-already-have-autonomous-robots-frames-37-39", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Why Don’t We Already Have Autonomous Robots?", "title": "Slide 23: Why Don’t We Already Have Autonomous Robots?", "frames": [37, 39], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video of a robot tidying toy blocks in a living room, captioned “Berger and Wyrobeck, Stanford 2007” (one frame is black, mid-video).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Final build adds a thought bubble:]</span> Robot is being teleoperated!</p>", "text": "[Figure: embedded video of a robot tidying toy blocks in a living room, captioned “Berger and Wyrobeck, Stanford 2007” (one frame is black, mid-video).] [Final build adds a thought bubble:] Robot is being teleoperated!", "t": 2400}, {"id": "slide-24-recent-hardware-advances-frame-40", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Recent Hardware Advances", "title": "Slide 24: Recent Hardware Advances", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Humanoid Robots <span class=\"own\">[Figure: lineup of humanoids labelled Boston Dynamics Atlas, Unitree G1, Figure AI Figure 02, Tesla Optimus, Sanctuary AI Phoenix, Agility Digit, Apptronik Apollo, Fourier GR-1, 1x Technologies NEO]</span></li>\n<li>NVIDIA GPUs <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a row of GPU server racks]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Humanoid Robots [Figure: lineup of humanoids labelled Boston Dynamics Atlas, Unitree G1, Figure AI Figure 02, Tesla Optimus, Sanctuary AI Phoenix, Agility Digit, Apptronik Apollo, Fourier GR-1, 1x Technologies NEO] NVIDIA GPUs [Figure: a row of GPU server racks]", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-25-recent-ai-advances-data-scaling-frame-41", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Recent AI Advances: Data Scaling", "title": "Slide 25: Recent AI Advances: Data Scaling", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Avg time for a human to read dataset</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three circles sized by reading time:]</span> Imagenet - 2 years; GPT-2 - 60 years; Llama 3 - 90,000 years</p>", "text": "Avg time for a human to read dataset [Figure: three circles sized by reading time:] Imagenet - 2 years; GPT-2 - 60 years; Llama 3 - 90,000 years", "t": 2641}, {"id": "slide-26-recent-ai-advances-models-frame-42", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: Recent AI Advances: Models", "title": "Slide 26: Recent AI Advances: Models", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three images: instance segmentation of a street scene with cyclists; the AlphaGo match at a Go board; the Gemini logo.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 27 and 28 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 26 to 29.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three images: instance segmentation of a street scene with cyclists; the AlphaGo match at a Go board; the Gemini logo.] [Note: slides 27 and 28 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 26 to 29.]", "t": 2641}, {"id": "slide-29-recent-advances-in-robot-learning-frame-43", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Recent Advances in Robot Learning", "title": "Slide 29: Recent Advances in Robot Learning", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<p><strong>Large Datasets</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: grid of many robot-manipulation scenes from different labs.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models</em>, Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, <strong>Mees</strong> et al., ICRA, 2024. <strong>Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Large Models</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo</li>\n<li>Isaac GR00T</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>RT-2</li>\n<li>…</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Large Datasets [Figure: grid of many robot-manipulation scenes from different labs.] Reference: Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models , Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, Mees et al., ICRA, 2024. Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers) Large Models [octopus emoji] Octo Isaac GR00T π 0 RT-2 …", "t": 2821}, {"id": "slide-30-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-44", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: policy inputs and outputs]</span>", "title": "Slide 30: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: diagram. A camera image of a tabletop (towel, spoon, toy watermelon slice) and the instruction “Pick up the spoon” both feed a <strong>Policy</strong> box, which outputs ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> (picture of a robot arm).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: diagram. A camera image of a tabletop (towel, spoon, toy watermelon slice) and the instruction “Pick up the spoon” both feed a Policy box, which outputs ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … (picture of a robot arm).] [Note: slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32.]", "t": 2940}, {"id": "slide-32-untitled-vision-language-model-inputs-and-outputs-frame-45", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]</span>", "title": "Slide 32: [untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: same diagram layout. A photo of the Statue of Liberty and the New York skyline plus the prompt “Caption the scene” feed a <strong>Vision-Language Model</strong> box, which outputs “The picture shows the Statue of Liberty in NY”.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: same diagram layout. A photo of the Statue of Liberty and the New York skyline plus the prompt “Caption the scene” feed a Vision-Language Model box, which outputs “The picture shows the Statue of Liberty in NY”.]", "t": 3001}, {"id": "slide-33-key-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-46", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "title": "Slide 33: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three groups of tokens in a row, Language (green), Image (blue), Action (purple), each above its source: the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three groups of tokens in a row, Language (green), Image (blue), Action (purple), each above its source: the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ]", "t": 3062}, {"id": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-47", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<p><strong>So we train a large transformer on robot data and we are done?</strong></p>", "text": "So we train a large transformer on robot data and we are done?", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-35-ingredients-for-scaling-robot-learning-frames-48-51", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: Ingredients for Scaling Robot Learning", "title": "Slide 35: Ingredients for Scaling Robot Learning", "frames": [48, 51], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Large Datasets</th>\n<th>Large Models</th>\n<th>Scalable Evaluation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Robot Data is <strong>Scarce</strong></td>\n<td>Robot Data is <strong>Multimodal &amp; Heterogeneous</strong></td>\n<td>Robot Evals are <strong>Tedious &amp; Expensive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collecting Data requires <strong>Human Supervision</strong></td>\n<td>Robots need <strong>High Frequency Control</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Difficult to reproduce</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: pipeline along the bottom: a dataset cylinder -&gt; <strong>Robot Foundation Model</strong> -&gt; collage of robots deployed in many environments. The three columns appear one per build.]</span></p>", "text": "Large Datasets Large Models Scalable Evaluation Robot Data is Scarce Robot Data is Multimodal & Heterogeneous Robot Evals are Tedious & Expensive Collecting Data requires Human Supervision Robots need High Frequency Control Difficult to reproduce [Figure: pipeline along the bottom: a dataset cylinder -> Robot Foundation Model -> collage of robots deployed in many environments. The three columns appear one per build.]", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-36-algorithms-for-robotic-learning-frame-52", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Algorithms for Robotic Learning", "title": "Slide 36: Algorithms for Robotic Learning", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<p><strong>Imitation Learning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Given labeled data: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} = \\{(x_i, y_i)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>, learn <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x) \\approx y</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Assumes: inputs x are independently, identically distributed (i.i.d.)</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Dataset -&gt; Supervised Learning]</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reinforcement Learning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn behavior: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(a \\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>data is <strong>not</strong> i.i.d., actions affect future states</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Figure: loop: Dataset -&gt; RL -&gt; “trial” in the world (globe icon) -&gt; “reward” -&gt; back into Dataset]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Imitation Learning Given labeled data: 𝒟 = { ( x i , y i ) } , learn f ( x ) ≈ y Assumes: inputs x are independently, identically distributed (i.i.d.) [Figure: Dataset -> Supervised Learning] Reinforcement Learning Learn behavior: π ( a ∣ s ) data is not i.i.d., actions affect future states [Figure: loop: Dataset -> RL -> “trial” in the world (globe icon) -> “reward” -> back into Dataset]", "t": 3301}, {"id": "slide-37-course-syllabus-1st-half-frames-53-56", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: Course Syllabus: 1st Half", "title": "Slide 37: Course Syllabus: 1st Half", "frames": [53, 56], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Robot Learning Fundamentals &amp; Algorithms\n<ul>\n<li>Robot Control &amp; MDPs</li>\n<li>Imitation Learning</li>\n<li>Reinforcement Learning (Online &amp; Offline)</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video, “Homework Assignments in Simulation”: three camera views (left_wrist, angle, top) of a simulated robot arm picking up a red cube and placing it in a box, with a teleoperation recording overlay “REC | ep 0/2 | substeps 10 - SPACE rec | ENTER end ep | r reset | ESC quit”.]</span></p>", "text": "Robot Learning Fundamentals & Algorithms Robot Control & MDPs Imitation Learning Reinforcement Learning (Online & Offline) [Figure: embedded video, “Homework Assignments in Simulation”: three camera views (left_wrist, angle, top) of a simulated robot arm picking up a red cube and placing it in a box, with a teleoperation recording overlay “REC | ep 0/2 | substeps 10 - SPACE rec | ENTER end ep | r reset | ESC quit”.]", "t": 3421}, {"id": "slide-38-course-syllabus-2nd-half-frame-57", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: Course Syllabus: 2nd Half", "title": "Slide 38: Course Syllabus: 2nd Half", "frames": [57, 57], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Scaling Robot Learning\n<ul>\n<li>Generative Models</li>\n<li>Sequence Modeling &amp; Transformers</li>\n<li>World Models</li>\n<li>Robot Foundation Models &amp; Embodied Reasoning</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a person at a desk working with two small robot arms, captioned “Group Projects with Real Robots”.]</span></p>", "text": "Scaling Robot Learning Generative Models Sequence Modeling & Transformers World Models Robot Foundation Models & Embodied Reasoning [Figure: a person at a desk working with two small robot arms, captioned “Group Projects with Real Robots”.]", "t": 3482}, {"id": "slide-39-after-the-course-frames-58-59", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: After the Course", "title": "Slide 39: After the Course", "frames": [58, 59], "html": "<ul>\n<li>You will understand:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, the teleoperated living-room tidying robot (“Berger and Wyrobeck, Stanford 2007”); arrow to right, an embedded video (“Physical Intelligence, 2025”) of a mobile two-armed robot cleaning a kitchen and a bedroom, with overlays “autonomous, unseen, 6x speed” and “autonomous, unseen, 10x speed” and a spoken instruction “Please clean up the spill too”.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 40 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 39 to 41.]</span></p>", "text": "You will understand: [Figure: left, the teleoperated living-room tidying robot (“Berger and Wyrobeck, Stanford 2007”); arrow to right, an embedded video (“Physical Intelligence, 2025”) of a mobile two-armed robot cleaning a kitchen and a bedroom, with overlays “autonomous, unseen, 6x speed” and “autonomous, unseen, 10x speed” and a spoken instruction “Please clean up the spill too”.] [Note: slide 40 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 39 to 41.]", "t": 3602}, {"id": "slide-41-after-the-course-continued-frames-60-62", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: After the Course <span class=\"own\">[continued]</span>", "title": "Slide 41: After the Course [continued]", "frames": [60, 62], "html": "<ul>\n<li>You will understand:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, embedded clips from the “DARPA Robotics Challenge 2015” (humanoids falling at a doorway, one climbing out of a utility vehicle); arrow to right, “Figure AI, 2026”: a humanoid robot loading a dishwasher in a kitchen.]</span></p>", "text": "You will understand: [Figure: left, embedded clips from the “DARPA Robotics Challenge 2015” (humanoids falling at a doorway, one climbing out of a utility vehicle); arrow to right, “Figure AI, 2026”: a humanoid robot loading a dishwasher in a kitchen.]", "t": 3602}, {"id": "slide-42-why-you-should-study-robot-learning-frame-63", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Why You Should Study Robot Learning", "title": "Slide 42: Why You Should Study Robot Learning", "frames": [63, 63], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Broad skills from foundation model training to PID control</li>\n<li>Advances often applicable to all robotic applications</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “I want you” recruiting-poster meme captioned “I want you to solve physical AGI - Enlist in robot learning”.]</span></p>", "text": "Broad skills from foundation model training to PID control Advances often applicable to all robotic applications [Figure: “I want you” recruiting-poster meme captioned “I want you to solve physical AGI - Enlist in robot learning”.]", "t": 3780}, {"id": "end-frame-64", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [64, 64], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention and enjoy the course!</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention and enjoy the course!", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-1-about-me-frame-2", "label": "Slide 1: About Me", "html": "slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3."}, {"slide": "slide-10-individual-homework-frame-10", "label": "Slide 10: Individual Homework", "html": "the course page later lists different due dates (March 5, March 12, March 26, April 16)."}, {"slide": "slide-26-recent-ai-advances-models-frame-42", "label": "Slide 26: Recent AI Advances: Models", "html": "slides 27 and 28 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 26 to 29."}, {"slide": "slide-30-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-44", "label": "Slide 30: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "html": "slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32."}, {"slide": "slide-39-after-the-course-frames-58-59", "label": "Slide 39: After the Course", "html": "slide 40 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 39 to 41."}]}, {"key": "02", "dir": "week02_control_mdp", "date": "Mon 23 Feb 2026", "topic": "Robot Control & MDPs", "summary": "Classical robotics toolkit first: rigid-body transforms in SE(2)/SE(3), kinematic chains, degrees of freedom, configuration space vs workspace vs task space, forward and optimization-based inverse kinematics, smooth trajectories (LERP vs quintic splines) and PID tracking. Second half sets up decision making: policies, states vs observations, the Markov property and the MDP objective.", "key_ideas": ["Chain transforms by multiplying homogeneous matrices: T_base_ped = T_base_sensor x T_sensor_ped", "IK is non-unique; solve it by gradient descent with the Jacobian transpose", "LERP gives velocity jumps and acceleration spikes; quintic time scaling starts and ends at rest", "PID: P pulls toward the target, I fights steady offsets like gravity, D damps overshoot", "MDP = (S, A, P, R); the objective is expected (discounted) return over trajectories"], "papers": [{"title": "Simple random search provides a competitive approach to reinforcement learning", "who": "Mania et al., 2018", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.07055"}, {"title": "Deep Reinforcement Learning Doesn't Work Yet", "who": "Irpan, 2018", "url": "https://www.alexirpan.com/2018/02/14/rl-hard.html"}, {"title": "Curiosity-driven Exploration...", "who": "Pathak et al., 2017", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1705.05363"}], "homework": ["hw2_robot_control_mdps"], "lecture": {"id": "5-Bb84eTTqQ", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 2: Robot Control & Markov Decision Processes | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "46:10", "description": "Lecture 2 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Course introduction"}, {"t": 59, "title": "Robot morphology basics"}, {"t": 134, "title": "Rigid body motion"}, {"t": 336, "title": "Chaining coordinate frames"}, {"t": 435, "title": "Articulated robot bodies"}, {"t": 547, "title": "End-effectors and joints"}, {"t": 636, "title": "Degrees of freedom"}, {"t": 756, "title": "Configuration and workspace"}, {"t": 836, "title": "Motion planning"}, {"t": 1004, "title": "Task space representation"}, {"t": 1053, "title": "Kinematics and redundancy"}, {"t": 1159, "title": "Forward and inverse kinematics"}, {"t": 1341, "title": "Trajectory generation"}, {"t": 1554, "title": "PID control fundamentals"}, {"t": 1675, "title": "Introduction to robot learning"}, {"t": 1844, "title": "Policy representation"}, {"t": 2006, "title": "Markov decision processes"}, {"t": 2206, "title": "MDP components"}, {"t": 2336, "title": "Transitions and uncertainty"}, {"t": 2427, "title": "Reward functions"}, {"t": 2510, "title": "Horizon and trajectories"}, {"t": 2689, "title": "Optimal policies"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, yeah, welcome to the second lecture. Today we will talk about robot control and Markov decision processes. We also have received some feedback from you for for the first week. It turns out the first homework assignment was maybe a little challenging. So, we've extended the deadline for that. We've also created a Moodle forum where you can ask questions and see if other students have asked the same questions that our TAs will also try to help and answer. And we have also made our slides and recordings available in case you didn't receive the password to access the content. Here is the the the login details that you need and this will be the same for the future. And yeah, so let's get started for today."}, {"t": 60, "text": "So, we'll start talking about robots because one of the most exciting things about robot learning right now is the diversity of the hardware we are dealing with, right? So, you've got everything from quadrupeds navigating in auto terrain to dexterous hands solving Rubik's Cube, drones flying and so forth. And you know, from the distance this looks like completely different problems, right? Like trying to control all these different robots. Right? And so, the core question is like how do we actually control these very different morphologies because all of these robots have these different like joints and actuators and different physics, but they all you know, kind of share the same fundamental constraint that we want to translate some sort of high-level goal into specific motor torques required to to that specific morphology throughout the world. And"}, {"t": 120, "text": "to build this kind of general foundation model for robotics, we can't just like try to solve it for one robot, but we have to understand the underlying framework for robot control that applies to all of them. And um So, looking at all these different robots from the last slide, how do we find a common language to describe what they're actually doing, right? And it turns out that most of them, almost all all of them, you can describe as a collection of rigid rigid bodies transforming or moving through space. What this means is we assume in this mathematical framework that the robot is a rigid body, meaning that the distance between two points on the robot remains constant. So, this means that it doesn't like squish or or or stretch while moving. And one of the benefits of this is that we essentially only want have to track one"}, {"t": 180, "text": "point on the robot instead of like multiple. And so, here on the left you can see how this would look like for like a planner motion. So, if you have like a car or like a robot that's like navigating on the ground, you would describe this would be like mostly represented by its motion in XY and maybe like an orientation. And so, you would describe this using like the SE2 group, so the special Euclidean group. And it's kind of simple, but you know, it's like the fundamentals for like ground motion. Now, for for something like drones, this would that live in the full 3D world, you would describe this with SE3. This is spatial motion. And so, here now you add um a zip for for altitude um and also like free rotational axis um roll pitch and and yaw. Um"}, {"t": 240, "text": "this is probably like familiar with most of you, but just to kind of go through the basics again, so everybody's on the same point. Um how do we actually represent these in our code? Uh we use homogeneous transformation matrices. Um on the left you see uh this um for the SE3, um which is now 3° of freedom as we said for the X Y and then the uh theta, um and um we represent this with a rotation matrix uh built from sines and cosines as well as a uh translation um uh translational vector. Um and on the on the right side you see kind of the the the equivalent for for the drone or for the SE3, um where we scale this um to 6° of freedom and now we have um like six values uh for the translation and and three for for rotation. Um and like one maybe one thing to um kind of like uh"}, {"t": 300, "text": "sanity check on the math here um is that in order for these matrices to be valid um rotation matrices, they have to satisfy two properties. Uh first uh matrix needs to be uh orthogonal, um um so meaning that it's transpose is equal um to its inverse. And then the second is that it also needs to have a determinant of one. And so this is not like, you know, like a metric, but just uh ensures that when our like when the robot like rotates, um you know, it doesn't like accidentally stretch or or anything, so it remains a true uh rigid body. Okay, uh why do we care about all these matrices from the previous slide? Because in the real world um the robot like typically doesn't have just like one frame, uh but most probably like a whole uh hierarchy of them. Um so, look for example at the self-driving car example. Um so, the car has a base frame um where this is like at the which which"}, {"t": 360, "text": "sits at the center of its gravity, and which we use to kind of like control and send commands to the motor uh to the car. Um but then it will have like some sort of sensor like a lidar or a camera on top um sitting on the roof. Um and for example, now when the sensor perceives like a pedestrian on the on the road, um like it will sense it in its local coordinate. And uh in order for us to, you know, like decide not to run over this pedestrian, we need to transform it back into our base frame where we are doing all the control. Um and um now the the beauty is that um now we can like chain all this like uh homogeneous transformation matrices to simply do this this conversions from uh like where is the pedestrian in the sensor frame to where is it now in in the base frame. Um and maybe like a side note, actually, in order to like we will not cover this in in this lecture, but uh in order to determine how like the"}, {"t": 422, "text": "transformation between the sensor frame to the base frame is, like you would do this via sensor calibration typically. Um All right. So, um so far we've treated robot as uh like a single rigid block moving through the world. Um but in reality, like robots are a bit more complex. Um for example, if you look at this humanoid uh Lola, uh you know, um we will want to uh define this as articulated uh rigid bodies. Um so, this means that um this will have now three components. Uh we will have links um that um like are the individual rigid bodies. Uh then joints that connect the links, and then end effectors, uh which are like essentially um like the uh like for example, the gripper, or like the thing that you use to interact with the world. Um and the key the key takeaway here is"}, {"t": 482, "text": "that um articulate robot uh is essentially just like a chain of like coordinate frames. Um so, uh everything that you know we just covered, like how to like convert and like chain all these matrices, um you would do this to find out for example, where your hand is relative to your humanoid's uh robot frame. You can just like multiply and chain these uh to com- do these computations. Um looking at uh robot arm articulations, um here is how this might look for the Franka, um like a 7° of freedom, um very popular kind of like uh robot arm. Um uh as well as the the SO1 uh 101, the this is a robot that you will be using in the group projects later on, and uh you will see that, you know, despite the the right one being a much simpler robot, um you know, in principle, like the definition is like very similar."}, {"t": 540, "text": "Um so, um from the mathematically, it's like um pretty much the identical. Okay. Um so, we talked about how the arm moves, but um motion has to translate to interaction in the real world, right? And um this happens with the end effector. Um There are like different types of end effectors that one can use to interact with the real world. I highlighted like three different ones that are kind of um um common and popular in robotics. Uh on the left, you see uh the suction gripper, um which uh is from a learning perspective a very uh clean uh action space, cuz it's like binary. Um so it's like either the vacuum is on or off. Um so you don't have to worry about any like additional geometries. Um and this is typically used in like industrial like pick and place kind of applications. Um then in the middle is the parallel uh gripper uh which is what you will find uh that most of the uh industrial robots um that are like"}, {"t": 600, "text": "the Frankas and the SO110s use. Um that you will be using. Um here, you know, it's a 1° of freedom action and you have to worry maybe about the width of the object and requires a bit more precision um to interact with objects. Uh and then on the right we have uh the Dexters hands uh which uh are much more complex end effectors. So instead of just having like one or two degrees of freedom, you might have like over 20. Uh and as we move into MDPs later, um these are how um these will affect how your action space essentially uh changes. Um okay. Uh maybe briefly I'll cover uh joints. So we've looked at the arm and hands, but um uh so the joints um essentially uh are like the mathematical constraint that limits the the relative motion between the two rigid bodies um so that you can uh we can actually control them. Uh here is"}, {"t": 660, "text": "a diagram from um like a modern robotics uh textbook. Um well, you see like kind of the standard vocabulary of different types of joint. Um honestly, the pre- pretty much the most popular joint that that you should care about and that you will be using are the revolute joints. Uh um which allow rotation around a single axis like your elbow. Um All right. Let's talk about degrees of freedom. Um and this is starting to get at the at the to the core of why robot learning um is so hard. Um so, how do we count the total um of freedom uh the total amount of freedom that the robot has to move in space? Uh we call this uh the degrees of freedom of the robot, and it essentially uh defines the number of independent um parameters that uh you would need to define the uh exact state of the robot at any given uh time. Um and so, here uh you can see how this"}, {"t": 721, "text": "is defined for these different um types of joints. Um and in general, if you want to calculate how many uh degrees of freedom the um uh a a certain robot has, you can use this uh formula, this uh Kutzbach formula, where um you start with the total uh number of possible dimensions uh of your rigid body, uh and then you subtract the constraints um for for every uh by every joint that that you have added. Um Okay. Now, we need to talk about the space where all these parameters actually live. Um we and we call this typically the configuration space or the C-space. Um this is essentially um the set of all possible configurations uh a robot can take. Um so, looking at the table um over here, you can see that the the"}, {"t": 781, "text": "space depends entirely on the types of joints that you have. So, for a point on a plane, um it's simply like a Euclidean space, but uh if you look at the like uh two-R arm, uh because it has two revolute joints, um it's not going to be flat, uh but it will be a torus. Um And so, this is kind of important because um when we train our neural network um Um, be a policy, the network has to respect like the different topologies that your uh robot configuration space uh has. Um, so for example, um in the torus case, um so like the kind of the um the distance between two points is is not always like a straight line, right? So if you're like at 356 and you want to get to one, right? Like the shortest path um uh is like just a 2° and not like going back like uh 350° Um Okay, so now the workspace, so we just"}, {"t": 841, "text": "uh covered the C-space. Um the Now we need to map this back into the real world. Um So the workspace is um the simply the set of all points that the robot can reach um in the in the physical world. Um and so um we make this distinction in robot learning because um your agent will often live at two um in two different worlds at once. Like uh it will perceive the tasks in the workspace, uh like seeing a cup on the table, but um it will have to execute the movement in the configuration space by turning its joints. Um And so this mapping is not uh trivial as we will see in the next slides. Um because um there is also, for example, obstacles, right? Um and um for example, here this Imagine in this like simplified kind of uh a robot if we have this this blue circle that represents an obstacle, um"}, {"t": 900, "text": "that would translate to to this uh in the configuration space of the robot, right? Um so this is kind of the forbidden region that your, you know, like robot joints uh should not uh reach. And it's important to understand that this is how robotics is used to do like motion planning in the '90s and in the the Um so the standard approach back then was to explicitly construct this like configuration spaces um and like translate the obstacles into the configuration space. Um and then search for a path that state, you know, like in the in the free space. Um as you can see in the next slide. Wait. Um, right? And so um, in but this like effectively doesn't scale very well to to higher like the higher the dimensions of your robot, um it becomes like pretty much like"}, {"t": 960, "text": "intractable to to do this computations. Um, and so in modern robot learning, uh we don't want to like pre-calculate all this like shapes and obstacles and have like perfect like structured uh world um state. We want to kind of like have a policy that can like learn how to to feel like where the obstacles are like implicitly. Um, recognizing automatically that certain like actions will lead to uh regions um of like bad outcomes, right? Um, so this is kind of like the fundamental difference between um you know, uh hardcoded like geometry to um learned representations that we'll be discussing in the rest of the term. Um, maybe to finalize this kind of definitions let's talk briefly about the task space. Um, the task space for a robot is um essentially the manifold where the objective um um is naturally defined. Um, so and this"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "is like independent of the embodiment. Um, so for example, uh if the task is to uh clean uh this whiteboard, um the the task space will be uh all the surface of that all the points on the surface of that whiteboard. And doesn't matter if this is like a a humanoid, a mobile single arm manipulator, or a or a flying drone that that is going to like wipe the the surface of that. All right. Um question time. So, now we've covered like quite a bit of concepts. Uh so, maybe to make sure that you all understand um how these are applied, uh let's look at the Franka robot and maybe discuss with your uh neighbor for like uh half a minute or 20 seconds uh what the configuration, uh workspace, and uh task space could be."}, {"t": 1081, "text": "Okay, it's it's fine. Like we we we don't have time to figure it out. Um So, the configuration space is uh the Yeah. Does this align Does the Does this align with what you thought? &gt;&gt; It it aligns. No, wait. &gt;&gt; Perfect score. Um yeah, sorry about that. Um so, yeah, we have the uh 7 degrees of freedom uh manifold for the configuration space. Um the workspace, uh as we said before, is like all the physically reachable state uh space in um XYZ. And the task space will be like the six-dimensional pose of the end effector. Um here you can see how the workspace looks. Maybe like one uh thing that is important that we will be be covering now also is um that because, as we discussed before, the the task space is smaller than the configuration space, this uh leads to the redundancy and null space motion. So, this means that um"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "uh for example, if the robot is holding a uh a mug of coffee like perfectly still, um there are many different ways for it to move the elbow while maintaining the same fixed point of the uh end effector um in in space. So, this allows you also to plan around obstacles, for example. All right. We've defined our spaces. Now, we need to cover how we move between them. So, the most fundamental mapping is called forward kinematics. This is like answering the question of imagine you already know all the joint values of your robot of this arm. How do you compute where your end effector is? Right? So, so given given like all these three values, like where is like your end effector? And so, this is a deterministic process. There is only like one possible answer. However, as we said before, this is not"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "a bijective mapping because there might be many different configurations that lead to the same end effector. And this is why the inverse kinematics is a much more difficult problem. Because this is like the problem that we will mostly focus on in in this course, which is, you know, like our policy might predict a new target pose for our end effector, and we need to figure out which joint angles Q will get us there. This is essentially like inverse kinematics. All right. As I said, like the solutions in inverse kinematics are often non-unique. Right? Like here you can see there could be like two very different set of joint angles that lead to the exact same end effector position. And so, this is why for many complex robots there might"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "not be an analytical solution at all to compute this. So, in practice, um, oftentimes like we use some sort of optimization based inverse kinematics where we define a cost function like the distance between the current pose and the target pose and add some second secondary constraints like staying away from obstacles and so forth um that help us solve this with gradient descent. Um, I will not go into too much details but just kind of to get the gist of it. This is how um at the most basic uh form such an optimization based inverse kinematics would work um where we have this this error between like the the current pose and the target pose um and we want to like minimize this. Um, let's say with a squared L2 norm of that error. And um for then the update um and then we kind"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "of iterative iteratively apply this sort of Jacobian method um that is just that tells us like um how a tiny change in each joint angle uh affects the position of the end effector in the real world um and nudge the the joints until the end effector reach um the target position. All right. So imagine we have now um solved we have found um we have run the optimization and found a set of uh perfect joint angles that reach our target position. Um but like how do we actually translate this into motion because the robot will just not teleport from one position to the next, right? Um, so in order to move this, uh, let's say we will have the SO101 and we want to move it like like 1 cm in like sideways in Y direction, uh, we will have to interpolate instead of doing this this uh giant jump. Um, so for this we essentially want to"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "create a series of tiny way points that will like move the end effector to the target goal. So this more simple way you could think of how to do this would be just doing linear interpolation where we treat the joint space um um as a straight line um and so we we we also need to define a time like what is the time we give like our controller to reach that end pose and then we calculate how many way points or discrete steps we need to take um and then yeah we just like interpolate, right? um and so we care about this because as I said before like in for the policy we will uh we will have to like use some sort of like way point generations to um to like interpolate to the target poses our policies produce. um However, um there is a an issue with"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "this approach if we do this linear interpolation. Uh and so while it's very simple um and very easy to write down, it's actually pretty bad for our robot um because this essentially means that you know it will go to like full velocity at the first time step and you know like uh you see like this like how like how this like velocity looks and it will produce like a lot of stress also on the uh on the robot hardware um with um you know also with this infinite spikes of acceleration, right? So it's going like full on and then at the end it's going like from max speed to to zero like from one time step to the next, right? So that's like pretty bad um and so this is why um typically instead of doing this um most controllers do something uh, a little bit more sophisticated with"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "like some polynomial based methods such as quintic splines. Um, the idea is that instead of like doing like simple linear paths, we you represent a trajectory uh, of each joint as a fifth order polynomial. Uh, and then you add like um, like six boundary conditions um, um, to ensure smooth motion. And so, essentially you you encode that the robot should start and end with zero velocity and zero acceleration. Um, so, then the movement looks something like this. You can see like it's a much more smoother uh, like more gentle start and also ends much more uh, gracefully. Um, and so, the also like the uh, Dirac delta uh, spikes are now gone. Um, and this is what, you know, a lot of waypoint generation methods like industry robots like Franka's will will use. Um, one last note on on robot control. So,"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "we have um, determined how we can generate like a set of uh, like first like a set of um, joint configurations and waypoints to interpolate smoothly between them. Um, but in the real world, um, the robot will never follow um, these perfectly just because you tell them tell it to do it so uh, because there are like factors like friction, there is like gravity, uh, air resistance and so on um, that will make the robot not follow like this waypoints perfectly. And so, this is why um, typically um, you use like a PID controller uh, to ensure that your um, robot like is following this uh, trajectory of waypoints uh, as much as possible. Um, I will not go into too much details. I guess many of you already have learned this in other um in other lectures, but uh I try to visualize a little bit how the different"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "um components of the PID controller work uh when you actually um try to follow a trajectory with the robot. Right? And so, you can see for example here how um so, the proportional is just like kind of proportional to the error, the target that you have between the the desired um uh uh trajectory and the one your robot is currently following, right? So, the the further off you are, the the harder it will pull you towards the correct trajectory. Uh and then you have the integral part, which will compensate for example for things like gravity. If like you know your robot is very heavy and you know it's kind of like going down, it will kick in and and pull you back up. Um and then the for example, if you overshoot, then the derivative will kind of help you um get uh dampen this and uh go back. All right. So, um so, now we know how to kind of like this is a kind of a crash"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "course on how we can actually affect motion with our robots and end effectors, right? Uh it's a very basic motion. Um And the question is like, well, are we now done? Well, obviously not because um in in a in the real world, like a robot needs to do more than just like following a straight line. Right? It needs to ideally decide um which action to achieve or to take to achieve some complex like long horizon uh goal, uh especially when the world is uh unpredictable, noisy, or uh unmapped. And so, we have covered like kind of the control parts uh in robotics uh uh here, but now uh what we care more about in this lesson is to the machine learning part. Um Oh, I'd I'll take the questions at the end. Um Sorry. Essentially, for the decision-making, to decide like which is the the next action the robot needs to take to achieve some complex"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "long-term goal. This is an example for example from one of my previous works where we were doing sushi cutting as a task with this by manual Aloha robot. And so this is a a very long horizon task where the robot needs to first like grasp the knife with one hand move it in the right position then grasp it with the second hand so it it will do like as you will see in a second um this this this Okay, it's vibrate it it still vibrates a little bit but you know, and this is very very precise like kind of like manipulation task, right? Cuz now it needs to like go right Okay, this is like a toy sushi. It's not a real sushi but it needs to go right in you know, in there and that's like very very difficult for for a robot to do this like so accurately and precisely. And"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "and so this for example has like 14 degrees of freedom, right? It's like two arms and it's like very high frequency so it's like a lot of actions that your policy needs to predict at every point in time to decide how to do it. And then you have like the low-level controller like the PID and all of that that that like ensures that the motor actually follow what your policy is predicting, right? So now we will cover like the basic mathematical framework that allows you to learn such data-driven policies to enable like policies to solve this sort of like long horizon dexterous tasks. um So, maybe let's first talk about how we can represent this mapping from observations to actions and introduce some terminology. Um this is typically not too different from what you how you might represent an image classifier which maps from input X"}, {"t": 1862, "text": "to some outputs Y like for example here for for for this like cute cat um with the with a neural network. Um now a policy maps from an observation O um like here from this like camera view from like a car um from a snowy road to some output A which in this case could be um actions that tell the car how to drive like straight left right or or backward. Um and so um other than that the symbols like essentially not not many things change so far, right? Um and we denote pi the pi symbol we use the pi symbol to denote the policy and the subscript theta to denote the weights of of the neural network of that policy. Um And so now typically in in a controlled decision-making problem things exist in a temporal context so"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "like at the instance time in T um you know, I'm looking at my camera observations O and with subscript T and I will make a decision based on that denoted AT. Um and then the next time step would be like T plus one and so forth. Um and in this course we will use like integers time steps which start starts at zero and then go one two three and so forth. Um but in the real world there could also be like some sort of continuous notion of time such as like milliseconds for example. Um and so mathematically Um, I said before a policy is just a conditional probability distribution over actions given an observation. Um, and um but there is a a big difference here. Uh, in classification, if you mislabel a cat as a dog, um, the cat doesn't change, but in robotics, if your policy chooses the wrong action, the state of the world changes. So, if your car turns left into a ditch, the next observation that the network will see will, um,"}, {"t": 1982, "text": "obviously be very different. Um and in the most, uh, general case, policies provide distributions over actions. Uh, so they will assign probabilities to all different actions, um, which we will call stochastic policies, um, but there are also special cases like deterministic policies. Uh, they assign a probability of one to to a single action and zero to everything else. Um yeah, so we have to find a policy. Uh, but what is a policy looking at? Um, and so here it is important that uh, we distinguish between states and actions. So, um, for example, looking at the car here on the left, um, like the state is is like the ground truth. Um, let's say it's like the exact position of the car on a map, its velocity, orientation, and so forth. Um, and then, um, on the on the right, this would be like the observation, right? So, like the observation is produced by a state. And this is like some sort of"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "like a lossy mapping, right? Um, and um, the the key insight here is that uh like no matter uh, what is the the weather or or visibility, um, you know, even if it's like, you know, a you know, like a snowy, you know, like uh uh, night driving, like um, the observation might change, right? Like the amount of information you're perceiving from like the sunny clear skies to this, you know, like very bad visibility driving, um, but the state doesn't change. So, the state remains constant. It's It's always the same. Um, and so, this is important because like in robotics, we typically operate with observations, like images from cameras that will give us like partial information about the state of the world. Um, in the language of probabilistic graphical models, we can describe this as a following. Um, so, and the policy pi will give us the"}, {"t": 2103, "text": "conditional distribution um of A given O, and then the state and the action together provide a probability distribution of the next state. Um, and this will call the transition function or the dynamics rule. Um, and maybe one thing to note here is very important that maybe some of you already have heard. Um, this is the Markov property, which means that if you are trying to determine state three, so S3, and you already know S2, then knowing S1 doesn't provide any additional information that helps to determine S3. And this kind of makes sense because as we said, right, like the state already like contains all the information you need about the world. So, knowing the past doesn't help you like determine the the future. Um, but still like even though you might know the state, like predicting the future might be a difficult due to"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "stochasticity. Um, maybe to confound you a bit more, in this class we will mostly use the terminology of S for states and A for actions, which goes back to dynamic programming of like the '50s and '60s with Bellman and so on. Um, but you might have seen it for example in the in the example I did for for the PID control, that sometimes people also use the notation of X for actions. Sorry, X for states and and U for actions, which comes from kind of optimal control theory. But this is exactly essentially the same. Maybe the only thing you should know is that for like rewards and and cost function you have like this this inverse relationship. Oops, sorry. All right. So, now the Markov decision process this is the the formal framework um um that, you know, kind of summarizes"}, {"t": 2221, "text": "everything we'll be talking about from motor trucks to to high-level goals and is defined by by this tuple over here with the state space, action space, the transition probability and a reward function. And the reason why the MDP is fundamentally so important is that it provides us like a some sort of like unit unified mathematical framework that allows us to treat complex physical machines exactly like any other computational agent enabling us to to apply the same learning algorithms to a 27 degrees of freedom like humanoid robot as we do for playing the game of chess. Um Okay, so for the state spaces um there are like two different categories. This can be discrete or continuous."}, {"t": 2281, "text": "So, for the discrete ones as I said for example in the game of chess um there is no no halfway between squares, right? So, you have to like you can kind of see everything. For the continuous space state spaces, this is what we will use mostly in robotics. Like this will be like for example the the joint angles and these are defined by real numbers. Um, and so um, this matters for robot learning because in a discrete world, um, an agent can essentially explore by trying like every different buckets, um, in its state space while in a continuous world there might be a infinite number of states. Uh, and so you cannot like just build a table, um, for every possible states and its value, but you have to use like function approximators and neural networks, um, um, across like, you know, this infinite possibilities. Um, uh, similarly, uh, talking about action"}, {"t": 2341, "text": "spaces and how they influence the world, we have again the same um, categorization between discrete, um, this could be like for example like the keyboard if you're like uh, controlling like the robot in the simulator with the keyboard, um, this would be like discrete, uh, action space while if you use, uh, for example like VR motion controller to teleoperate it, this would be a continuous and much, uh, smoother, um, action space. Um, regarding the transition, um, model, um, in in a perfect world our transition model would be deterministic as shown on the left. Um, so this means that if we command our, uh, car to turn the steering wheel exactly 5°, um, the car would like move in a perfectly predictable arc. Um, but in the real world, um, you know, there is stochasticity. This means that it the road might be slippery, there might be ice, there might be, I don't know, the tires are worn out, and so you might not"}, {"t": 2402, "text": "get, um, that exact predictable behavior. And this is why, uh, in robotics we often use this, uh, this stochastic transition model, um, uh, modeling. Right, for example with the arm it could be as we said that the motors overshoot or there's like some uncertainty because of sensor noise and so on that make like your like robot not follow 100% like the trajectory that you're predicting. Um in MDPs we also define reward functions which model the behavior of the policy. This is mostly used in reinforcement learning setting or paradigm. Typically most of you might be familiar. We can use either sparse rewards that only happen at the end of an episode or if certain conditions are met such as one if the robot like lifts a lifts an object and and zero otherwise or we can also use denser rewards such as the Euclidean distance"}, {"t": 2461, "text": "from like your end effector to the point of the object that you want to grasp which also accelerates learning greatly and I have attached the video doesn't play. I have attached two examples from my PhD where I was teaching an RL agent to do this kind of like pick and place tasks and here you can see the difference that that this might make when training like real world policies with RL for robotics. So you can see the the sparse reward one like fails greatly while the denser reward one can pick up the objects and place them nicely. So I guess if we had trained the the sparse rewards one like longer it might eventually have learned it. All right. We have looked at the individual components of the MDP, the states, the actions and the transitions. But the robot doesn't exist like in a single"}, {"t": 2521, "text": "moment. It operates in sequences and we call the sequences or I already called them trajectories. And this is defined with this tau symbol over there. Um and um the trajectory probability represents the total probability of seeing a specific sequence of uh states and actions. Um so, essentially, it tells us like how likely is a certain trajectory going to happen under the current policy pi. Um and um this is modeled by by these three things: the probability, um the um the raw is the uh represents the probability of the robot starting at a certain state. Uh then we have the policy um at every state, uh like what action does our policy choose? And then we have the uh transition function. So, given the action, where does the physics actually land us? And so, by multiplying these together uh for every time step,"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "we get a complete probability uh of everything that could happen. Um Okay, now that we understand uh how to model a trajectory, uh we need to decide for how long um should we care should the robot care for in the future? And this brings us to the concept of the horizon. Um so, there are like two uh categories, um finite horizon MDPs, uh where a robot might have a strict time limit um um to execute this task. Like, let's say 5 seconds to pick a a moving object. Uh and, you know, once that that is over, like the episode starts again. Um in this setup, the robot might um might change its strategy as it gets closer to the uh to the 5 seconds. Um so, it could be, for example, learn like that at um second four, it becomes more desperate and goes, you know, for like a risky movement or something to achieve the the reward. And then we have um infinite horizon MDPs, um where with which"}, {"t": 2642, "text": "technically um continue forever to infinity or until a certain terminal state um is reached. And this is like more common in autonomous navigation or long-term kind of tasks. Maybe one thing to note here is that like in principle just if you use like this this formulation like there would be no way for like the policy for the robot to know which policy is better if you accumulate like infinite rewards over time. And this is why we typically solve this with a discount factor gamma which you set between 0 and 1 to ensure that the rewards closer in time will get weighted more heavily than rewards in the distant future. Okay, this is the last slide. So putting all this together how this is the learning objective to finding optimal decision-making"}, {"t": 2702, "text": "policies. So first you can look here this is the accumulated reward an agent might receive for either the finite or the infinite infinite um horizon over its lifetime. So you can think of just like the sum or the discounted sums um of the agent. But then because the the world is stochastic like we model this as an expected return. So this average will return like the average of across all possible trajectories that can happen when we follow our policy. And then the optimal policy is the specific strategy that maximizes this expected return. And yeah so it could be like for example this one over here. And yeah. I think with this we can uh,"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "switch to the paper discussions."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Course introduction"}, {"t": 59, "title": "Robot morphology basics"}, {"t": 134, "title": "Rigid body motion"}, {"t": 336, "title": "Chaining coordinate frames"}, {"t": 435, "title": "Articulated robot bodies"}, {"t": 547, "title": "End-effectors and joints"}, {"t": 636, "title": "Degrees of freedom"}, {"t": 756, "title": "Configuration and workspace"}, {"t": 836, "title": "Motion planning"}, {"t": 1004, "title": "Task space representation"}, {"t": 1053, "title": "Kinematics and redundancy"}, {"t": 1159, "title": "Forward and inverse kinematics"}, {"t": 1341, "title": "Trajectory generation"}, {"t": 1554, "title": "PID control fundamentals"}, {"t": 1675, "title": "Introduction to robot learning"}, {"t": 1844, "title": "Policy representation"}, {"t": 2006, "title": "Markov decision processes"}, {"t": 2206, "title": "MDP components"}, {"t": 2336, "title": "Transitions and uncertainty"}, {"t": 2427, "title": "Reward functions"}, {"t": 2510, "title": "Horizon and trajectories"}, {"t": 2689, "title": "Optimal policies"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "aG8NPTPhwkE", "title": "Abishek Gupta: Simulation for Robotic Manipulation, without the Pain [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Abhishek Gupta", "duration": "32:07", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Prof. Abishek Gupta (University of Washington), explores how simulation can help scale robot learning from generating pre-training data to automating evaluation of generalist policies.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Robot learning motivation"}, {"t": 208, "title": "Simulation as a data source"}, {"t": 443, "title": "Overcoming simulation pain"}, {"t": 560, "title": "Automating environment creation"}, {"t": 744, "title": "Simulation for evaluation"}, {"t": 960, "title": "Large-scale behavior generation"}, {"t": 1342, "title": "Real-world adaptation"}, {"t": 1565, "title": "Conclusion and Q&A"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "It's my great pleasure to introduce Professor Abishek Gupta from the University of Washington where he's leading the weird lab, right? And yeah, he focuses on foundation models for robotics and reinforcement learning. And we are super excited to hear from this amazing work with today's topic simulation for robotic manipulation without the pain. So yeah, the the stage is yours Abishek. So thank you here. Thanks for the kind invite and I'm looking forward to talking to you all a bit about um about some of the work we've been working on around simulation for robotics. So uh um uh as Oier kind of alluded to, my group is called the weird lab, the Washington Embodied Intelligence and Robotics Development Lab. And they told me it is okay. So this is the name we chose. And so uh these are a"}, {"t": 60, "text": "lot of my students. They're the ones who did all the work and so I just wanted to highlight them up front. Um okay. So the goal of our group in general um abroad is to deploy robots in the messy unstructured environments, right? So I'm generally lazy person. I don't like to do any work. And I want my robots to all do the work, right? So if I have a kids playroom, I want all of it to be cleaned up by robot. If I have my kitchen doesn't look this clean, but if it was, um I'd like it to be cleaned up by uh by a robot, right? So I don't want to I want to think about settings which are more messy, unstructured, unexpected, right? And so you can't use the the classical paradigm um of sense plan act, which has been used in robotics for the last 50, 60 years. And so um you know, the the fundamental premise of what we think about is is robot learning, right? And so we uh we typically have this sense plan act paradigm in robotics, right? So we start by sensing the world, doing state estimation, then think about doing planning, uh which is like modeling, and then, you"}, {"t": 121, "text": "know, control or planning. Uh and then finally, you execute things on the robot with like um with a low-level controller. And so, this is the typical sense-plan-act paradigm. But, for every domain, you have to do a bunch of engineering. You have to bunch of um cost engineering. You have to do a bunch of model engineering, right? Or primitive engineering. And um you know, the fundamental theme I'm going to kind of keep going back to is I'm super lazy. This is my lazy picture. Data is a metaphor for me. You know, I'm like very lazy, and I want I want to do this without the the effort of having to design controllers and um algorithms per domain, right? And so, you know, that kind of took us from robotics 1.0, which is the sense-plan-act paradigm, um to robotics 2.0, right? Which is uh what you'd often refer to as end-to-end learning or robot learning or data-driven robot learning, right? And the idea being you kind of remove a lot of those components and go from uh perception to control. And so, you go from like your input sensors out to um out to controls that you can run on your"}, {"t": 180, "text": "robot, right? And typically, you'll represent this with a big neural network um that you will train with lots and lots and lots of data. And so, um this sounds like a great principle, right? Like it's worked so well for us in other domains of life, but in robotics, it's not going to be that simple to actualize. And so, uh a lot of this talk is going to be about how to actualize the principle of end-to-end robot learning. Also, if anyone has any questions, please interrupt me. Happy to happy to stop. Okay. So, you maybe to understand what it takes to actualize uh robot learning um with lots of data, it's useful to look at what has happened in other domains, right? So, let's look at things like language or vision, right? Um And you see that there's been this order of magnitude growth from like tiny data sets like MNIST to ImageNet to LAION or um you know, like Twitter sentiments data set, which is a tiny data set couple couple tens of thousands to like common crawl which is trillions of tokens. And um there's of course been a growth but"}, {"t": 241, "text": "an important thing in that growth is that it's been passive, right? So the growth has not been someone goes out and like manually types in a bunch of sentences. A lot of this has come from from the web scale and that's because people are using the internet anyways and so this data is going to grow anyways, right? And so uh the question is can we do that for robotics? Now the tricky thing about robotics is that there's not that many robots deployed um in real diverse applications and so there's not like this passive data stream, right? So there's no data that is just coming in because people are using robots anyways. And so a paradigm that's become uh you know more popular in recent years is that of teleoperation, right? So you have these interesting teleoperation devices. This one's called the UMi. There's others which are based on VR, right? And so you have uh these types of ways of collecting data which are better than what we had before. Um but they're still not passive, right? And so you still have to go into the field and you have to collect all this data and you have to put in all this effort and you have to take your data"}, {"t": 301, "text": "collection device or your robot to K different environments and and that takes a lot of money and resources and time, right? And so um if we had to cover things at the scale that things uh have been covered for vision and language, um then I think this becomes a proposition that I certainly don't have funding for. Right? And so uh what we've been thinking about is can we use cheaper sources of data that might still be useful um but might be a little lower quality in some ways. So specifically we've been thinking about the idea of off-domain data, right? So off-domain data um I mean data sets that are relevant to robotics but are not literally on a robot, right? So for instance data generated through generative models, data like like large-scale video um and specifically the focus of today's discussion will be data from simulation, right? And so, um an important thing to note up front is that off-domain data is wrong data. It's never going to be as good as"}, {"t": 360, "text": "your data on a robot collected by some, you know, high-quality expert operator. But, it can still be useful. And then, today we're going to try and think about specifically about simulation and whether simulation data can be made useful for robots. Right. So, so why simulation? And simulation, um you know, um people are pouring billions of dollars into simulation, and simulation has some fundamental advantages, right? So, um for instance, the data is a lot cheaper in principle cuz you're spending computer on it, not actual um man hours. Um you have privileged information. You know where everything is. You know where the forces are. Um you have simple resets, and because you know where everything is, you might be able to define rewards easily. Um and you can go much faster than real time, right? You can leverage all the advantages of largely parallel computation. And that's led to a lot of the advantage advances that are coming um in, for instance, humanoids or legged robots, uh a lot of them out of ETH, right? Um are coming um because of these advantages of"}, {"t": 420, "text": "simulation. But, I want to highlight a specific term here, a specific term of like it's only cheaper data in principle. And when you actually get [clears throat] down to the the brass tacks of trying to get simulation to work for um robots, especially robot manipulation, there's a lot of hidden cost. And today we're going to try and tackle some of that hidden cost. So, what is this hidden cost, right? So, when I think about, you know, I want to put a robot into an environment, I want to use simulation to let's say generate data or to simplify this robot learning process, um it's not as simple as, you know, uh me as a human says, \"Give me data in this environment.\" It's more a process of you start off with designing and creating an environment. So, some artists, often a PhD student or someone professional, would go in and create an environment in Blender, right? So, they go in and they edit things, they drag things around, they adjust the physics, they adjust the the geometries until something looks good, right? Um and now they have an environment, but"}, {"t": 480, "text": "that took them many many many hours to create. Now when they have an environment, then they need to go actually get robots to do things in that environment, right? So, they have to find ways to like either they collect data in that environment or generate data in that environment and uh for difficult contacted problems, that is also an endeavor of many many many hours. Okay, now you've like generated this data and then you realize, \"Hey, my like simulator doesn't quite match the real world.\" And so, if the simulator doesn't match the real world, then I go and like tweak my simulator, I fix these parameters, I fix these things, and I identify the system, and only then is the simulator effective um in the real world, right? And so, remember, I'm a lazy potato. I don't work, right? Um and I don't want to do complex engineering per domain. So, all of this work that went from, you know, the vision of generating data in a domain to like actually generating data and using it for a particular domain is way too much uh for my lazy potato. And I want to find a way to kind of automate these pieces so I can uh learn with as"}, {"t": 541, "text": "little effort as possible, right? So, um simulation gives us the premise of having less cost of data, but it doesn't really um account for the effort in actually designing those simulations and getting it to work. And so, today we're going to try and remove that effort. Okay. Um so, the three sections we'll talk about really quickly are um starting by generating simulation environments from real data, then trying to generate behavior cheaply in those simulations through reinforcement learning, and lastly is adapting to the real world with minimum amounts of effort. So, let's let's get started. So, the the first thing that we started thinking about is okay, every time you have to create an environment, we have to spend all of this effort and a lot of it is manual design, right? Um and and what we wanted to do instead was kind of use some of the advantage advances in generative AI to um automatically generate fully articulated interactive simulation scenes from images or videos, right? And so, we've been calling this real to sim. Uh but the idea is that,"}, {"t": 602, "text": "you know, a student here goes into the the kitchen, um grabs a short video of the scene, all right? And out comes not just a static mesh, uh but really a fully interactive kitchen scene, right? So, all the objects are movable, the microwaves and the cabinets and the dishwasher. Um and really you want to be able to interact with that scene fully so that you can do, you know, evaluation or data generation or um gaming applications with these kinds of environments, right? And so, um the idea here is that we're not scaling with human effort, you're just scaling with, you know, videos or images that you collect in the environment. And the idea here is that you would produce something that is representative of that environment, not just a generic kitchen. So, um I won't have enough time to go deep into the details here, but the fundamental premise is we use some of the advances in 3D vision, specifically Gaussian splatting, and then use some of the advances in in foundation models, uh specifically grounded SAM and at the time GPT-4, in order to get interaction,"}, {"t": 661, "text": "right? So, you can think of Gaussian splatting as a way to go from uh visuals of a scene, so like multi-view images of a scene or videos, um to high-fidelity meshes and visuals, right? So, you can like think of the the visuals you see on the left as basically all just coming directly from raw camera capture, right? Um and so, you you can reconstruct and get novel views, but the nice part is that you can also get um you know, underlying geometries. And the the the tricky part is tying the visuals to the geometries so that they're align. Um and then once you have this, you can then think, okay, there's like these cabinets. You can separate out the cabinets. You can figure out, are they cabinets? Are they drawers? Are they dishwashers? Are they fridges? And then you can try and articulate them, right? So, you can put on like hinges and joints and so on. Um and then you kind of go back and forth in this process of you a few times to get it interactive scene. Um and what this kind of results in is if you go to a bunch of different kitchens, you apply the same procedure, and you go"}, {"t": 721, "text": "straight from videos to fully interactive kitchen scenes, right? So, there this was no effort. It was just compute, right? You go in with a video, and out comes a fully interactive scene that looks just like the scene that you you recorded, but it's fully interactive with the physics simulation. Um so, that's pretty cool. Um and what do what do we do with this, right? Why do we care? We're we're roboticists. So, the first and simplest thing to do is you can use it for data generation, right? So, you want to like, for instance, learn to like open a drawer or put a cup in it, close the drawer, etc. Now, you have a simulation, and you can use all the nice tools we have in simulation to generate data, and we'll talk a bunch about this in the second section. Um but one point that I want to highlight is that simulation offers a really nice tool for evaluation, right? So, like uh uh big challenge in real world robots is that it's hard to evaluate. It's hard to know whether you're successful or when you're not successful, right? And well, because you have to like have a human in the environment running trials over and over and over and over. So, every new algorithm that you come up"}, {"t": 780, "text": "with, it's a huge process to actually evaluate. Um but one of the nice things about these highly photo realistic realistic simulations is that you can just run evaluations directly in simulation, even for controllers that you trained in the real world, right? And so, what we what we kind of did with these tools that we built was we said, \"Hey, we're going to construct these scenes, right? So, these scenes are uh two scenes that we've constructed in simulation. So, these look like the real world, but they're actually just scenes in simulation, right? You can see like if you want to see it simulation, you can see like tiny little artifacts on the arm or like on the side over here, but I promise you they're simulation. Um and once you have that, you can then say I can take your policies or foundation models and things that I trained in the real world and then transfer them back into simulation, right? And so then you can construct evaluation scenes. So these are a few different scenes at University of Washington and Princeton um and we're able to transfer them directly from the real world back into simulation. And because they're hyperrealistic"}, {"t": 841, "text": "um and visually really realistic and the physics is good, um you can actually run these policies directly in simulation with high degrees of correlation. And so here we're running uh these policies from this company Physical Intelligence um and so they have these policies that were not trained on any simulated data, mostly on real world data, but you can kind of get them from the real world back into simulation so you can run lots and lots and lots of evaluation. And so what's nice about this is that you can then like you want your things to be close to the diagonal, right? So you want there's these two measures of Pearson correlation and MMRV, which is rank violation. You want Pearson to be high and rank violation to be low and you can see that it's like pretty well correlated, but not perfect between sim and real. And the important thing is not that you get the behavior exactly to be the same, but really that you can see things that are better in simulation would also be better in the real world. Right? And there was no way to get here unless you had hyperrealistic simulation. So um"}, {"t": 900, "text": "when we we tried doing this on far less realistic scenes, it worked a lot worse. And so you do have to do a little bit of magic there, so you know, it's not just zero-zero shot, you have to add in a little bit of simulation data into the training mixture. Right? But it's like you add a little bit of simulation data, the models adapt to the general idea of simulation, and then you can evaluate it on new problems. Uh but the important thing is that um we were able to construct these environments for data generation and evaluation just by taking in videos without like a ton of effort in creating any of the scenes. So, it's like a it's gone from a several days effort to a several minutes effort. And so, if you think about the takeaways here, um it's basically that there's no more uh manual environment design. I'm happy I don't have to do the work of doing it and and that's good. Have fun with that. Okay. So, so that's first uh creating environments uh in simulation without the pain, right? But, we are"}, {"t": 960, "text": "roboticists, we want behavior, right? We want robots that are flipping and jumping and doing manipulation and and building things, right? And so, um we will want to actually generate behavior. And to generate behavior, you have to go a step beyond creating the environment. And so, um there's lots of different ways to do it, but two of the tools that people often use are human data collection. So, someone will hop into like a VR interface or some track glove and then try to control robots to do things and you can do this in simulation, too. Or someone goes in and uses tools like reinforcement learning, but writes down like a fancy reward function, right? So, here's a snapshot from one of the reward functions that uh folks have used and you can see that has like a ton of little terms with these random weightings and these like arbitrary designs. And so, um it's it's often a pretty painstaking process, right? And me as a as a lazy potato doesn't want to do that, right? I don't want to go in and collect data and I don't want to sit and have to design these environments and these reward functions and stuff with reinforcement"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "learning. And so, um my goal is to find a way to generate really, you know, high coverage robust data in simulation, but without the pain. And and so, um my students Tyler and Patrick asked, \"Can we we RL in simulation without the pain?\" And so, they wanted to perform uh behavior generation, which is physically accurate in simulation, but without demonstrations, without reward design, and they kind of leverage the fact that simulation is not reality, right? So, simulation and reality are not the same thing. And specifically, um simulation has some privileges that might make your problem of behavior generation easier than the real world, right? So, specifically, we have resets, you have privileged information, you have cheap samples, right? And so, we're going to use these different privileged informations to make our job easier. Specifically, we're going to focus in on the idea of resets. When I say resets, I mean I can set the simulator wherever I want, right? I can't teleport the real world anywhere, but I can teleport the simulation to anywhere,"}, {"t": 1081, "text": "And so, um what we're going to try and do is try to generate behavior, uh but without any of the pain of demonstrations or reward design. We're going to use resets to do so. And so, the key idea um that we're going to leverage is that resets can make exploration easier. So, the hard part about reinforcement learning is often you're like exploring the world, you're trying random stuff, and you have to like it's a needle in a haystack problem, right? So, imagine your goal was in the green star, you started over on the left. Now, you have to like try a bunch of random stuff until you like accidentally hit the goal, right? And that is a huge pain, takes a huge amount of compute, and that's often what you use the reward design to do is to like direct the way you're searching, right? And so, if I'm always starting at the starting point, imagine I'm like wiggling my arm randomly, like I have to randomly chance upon the green star. And that is kind of a pain. But, the idea behind using resets is that I don't have to start at the first point anymore, I can reset myself all the way to the last point, right? And then I'm solving a much easier problem."}, {"t": 1140, "text": "It's like imagine I reset right next to the target, I'm solving a much easier problem. Then I reset one step away, then I reset two steps away, then I reset three steps away, right? And so, if I'm am to use the fact that I can teleport in my simulation, um I can get behavior a lot more easily cuz I'm only solving easy short horizon problems. And so, um the idea that uh my students Patrick and Tyler kind of took is that if you can reset really really broadly in a simulation, so these are some of the examples of reset states. You can see there's like partially assembled partial assemblies, partial grasp, resetting the arm all over the place, right? Um if you can do that, um then you can get your reinforcement to learn behaviors uh without any fancy reward design, with only sparse rewards, with no demonstrations. Um and you can generate these programmatically, right? So, the idea is that you don't you use the fact that you have privileged resets in simulation and you just reset all over the place. And then you run reinforcement learning starting from all"}, {"t": 1200, "text": "of those places. Right? And so, um let me skip through the next part, maybe. But, um what results from this large-scale reinforcement learning, right, is this kind of behavior um that you get without any demonstrations. This is not provided by a human. This is only with a sparse reward. Um and uh you can see that it has like lots of interesting non-prehensile adjustment behavior that you'd expect from a human, like re- rearrangement, wiggling, uh flipping, right? Um you can like do furniture assembly. You can see that it like lifts things over, uses the side of objects to do like non-prehensile and interesting manipulation. Um but, the cool thing is it's the exact same code for both tasks. The only thing you did is you provided them um the CAD models, and it's exactly the same code, no reward design, no demonstrations, right? You can apply this across many many many tasks. Doesn't work for every single problem, but works for a large range of these contact-rich tasks and works for any way."}, {"t": 1261, "text": "Right? So, if you um if you're the lazy potato like me, uh you think about, \"Okay, I don't want to collect more and more, more data. So, is this thing really robust? Uh And you can see that like if you had collected data, it's hard to see, but you can see these a few green axes on this middle plot. Whereas if you do this like really broad reset, you get success anywhere. So, you can place it anywhere and it'll assemble the table for you. Right? And so, you can take this a step further and you can go and assemble a table with with four legs or you can do like other tasks like picking and placing and you know, like I find the one on the bottom right really interesting cuz you're like trying to like rearrange this block against this table, but you can see that it like learns to use the environment in interesting ways. And so, um the cool part here is all of it is emergent, right? None of it is like hand coded. Um all of it is just emergent from large-scale reinforcement learning. Okay. So, um the important takeaway here is that"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "if you use the privileges of simulation, it allows for lots of cheap and robust data generation, but you have to be careful in how exactly you use that simulation, right? And so, for my lazy potato though, it's it's taken me away from environment design and now it's taken me away from reward design, right? So, I'm way happier. I'm able to get behavior in simulation without the pain. Okay. And then the last section is around is around real world deployment. Like we're roboticists, we care about the real world, right? We want robots to do things in the real world. And simulation is inherently wrong, right? So, if simulation and reality were compared, they're inherently wrong. The physics is wrong, the visuals are a little wrong and I could go within and I could adjust the sim and the real the sim to match reality more. I could identify things perfectly, and but that makes me unhappy cuz I'm lazy. And so, I could of course spend more time making simulation accurate, but what I'm going to do instead is I'm going to try and improve autonomously in in real world"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "without putting in effort. So, I'm going to try and see can I run an algorithm autonomously in the real world to go from the behavior on the left, which is failing, to the behavior on the right, which is successful, um without putting in lots of effort, right? And and the important thing to think about here is what simulation is wrong, but it's still useful for certain things, right? So, for instance, simulation is good at capturing the global strategy of a problem, right? If I have to pick up a leg and insert it and rotate it, um it kind of captures that global structure just fine. What it sometimes struggles with is getting the exact physics a little bit wrong, right? So, like if you're getting the forces a little bit wrong or the contacts a little bit wrong. Um and so, what we're going to do is we're going to try and like cover global planning uh or like the global structure of the problem from simulation, and then we're going to try and fix the local acting from the real world. So, we're going to capture the big long long structure of the problem from sim, and fix the little bits of uh of detail from from real data, right? I'm largely going to skip through the details here, but essentially we kind of"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "use world models to do this, like like you've seen a lot of these tools in um in the uh AI literature of like dynamics models or predictive models of the future. And so, we're going to try and uh cast this as a world modeling problem, but we're going to try and um freeze some of the long horizon parts of the problem from simulation, and only adjust the short horizon prediction parts from the real world, right? And so, the paradigm we're going to take is pre-train a lot in pre-train a world model in simulation, and just adjust little parts of it in the real world, right? Um and since we're a little short on time, let's let's let's get straight to the results, but you can see what happens is that, you know, if you transfer things from simulation to reality, at first, you see that it's not quite that wrong, but it gets a little bit of the details wrong, which makes it hard to like insert the leg properly. But if you adjust uh with little bits of uh real data autonomously, you you get like full furniture assembly that looks something like that, right? And so the point here is that I didn't have to put in any effort. The algorithm"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "ran autonomously and fixed itself. Um you can do similar things for um other tasks, too. Again, all the tasks that you see um were transferred directly and they don't work, but if you if you transfer them and adjust a little bit, um it gets a lot better. So, like interesting things to see here is that it like learns how to like flip up the object and like do pretty interesting dynamic motions. Um just by little bits of real world data. You can also apply this to like other problems like quadrupeds and so on. And the interesting thing is it like fixes those little physics mistakes, but it captures the general structure of walking pretty well in simulation. Okay. So, the important part here is that we can make real world adaptation a lot easier through simulation. So, simulation is not a silver bullet, but it can make real world adaptation super fast and super easy. So, within 15 minutes of data collection. So, um the last bit is I don't have to go and fix my simulation and and bridge this sim-to-real gap um"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "as a human, I just allow the data to do it, right? And so the important thing here is that simulation can be a good way to scale up data for robotics, but it's all about managing costs, right? So, managing costs and managing effort. And so you want to do that while representing the diversity of the data. And so that's going to make you or me as a lazy potato happy. That's actually our goal in life. Um these are the papers we covered um and the students who worked on it. Yeah, thanks for your attention. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Abhishek, can you hear me? Um thank you very much for the talk. And uh to anyone in the audience who has to go, you can go, but whoever has questions, we're happy to um take them. So, just raise is a hand. Uh thank you for the talk. Um just a a question on the resetting paper um where you leverage the fact that you're able"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "to reset in simulation environments um but but not in the real world. How how do you define for for different tasks? We saw We saw one example where you're uh doing a construction, so you might for example have um different parts of it already assembled. Uh but have you thought about for for uh other types of tasks, how you define closeness um for your for your problem, and how do you avoid uh when defining closeness a bias uh let's say towards learning a a particular policy um because of that inherent definition of of closeness or that's biased towards it. Oh, you you might have to repeat that for me cuz of the audio. Um uh Good. I heard something about how do you define the reset state, but I like missed a bunch of the details, sorry."}, {"t": 1680, "text": "All right. Let's use this mic. Okay. Okay, good. Sorry, thank [laughter] you. So yeah, that's good. &gt;&gt; Okay, we're going to we're going to try again. Uh can you hear me now? Okay. Yes? Yes, that's perfect. Yeah. Great. Uh so the the question is just um when it comes to resetting in the simulation environment, how do you define for different tasks what it means to be close uh to the end state? We We saw one example there with uh constructing materials, um but for other tasks I can imagine that you might induce, depending on your definition, a a bias towards um towards your learning. Yeah. Yeah, yeah. No, that's a great question. Um so what we did is we we applied it to some general class of assembly problems, right? So we want to like insert the drawer, or assemble the leg, right? And so, um the way we did is we assumed that the the user provides you with a full assembly looks like, like a like a complete assembly, and then we kind of went backwards from there. So, you like start from the complete assembly, and"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "then you start adding a small amounts of noise to your policy, um and that gets you to like near assembly states. Um I think a a relatively general way of doing at least assembly problems is this assembly through disassembly paradigm. Like, you take a fully assembled thing, and you start to disassemble it, and that gives you some nearby states, but I think it's a pretty open question how you do this for the broader class of any problem. It's a good good question. Cool. Uh one more question there. Hi. Thank you for the talk. Um so, for the real to same part, you showed that you were collecting data from the real world and articulating it in simulation, which was very cool. I was wondering if you think that there would be a way to also capture the physical properties of those objects and try to bring them into simulation. Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah. That's a great question. Um so, we've tried this uh a couple of times, uh like figuring out the like"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "physical properties of like friction, or softness, or damping, or things like that. Um and so, like you can kind of poke around with the world and like use that data to set the properties. Um what we found is that often it is less effective to do that than it is to just choose a reasonable range of parameters in simulation, and just train a policy that's robust or adapted to those parameters. So, I think um um at least in the the the task we've considered, which in the first part are not that dynamic, um it hasn't made that big of a difference, but there's lots of interesting ways to do system identification of objects and physics parameters that you can try, but um I think it's an open question on which problems does it make a big difference. Yeah, that's a good question. Okay. Uh thank you so much. Uh what Do you have time for one more question or Yeah? Yeah, of course. Okay. Yeah. Can we take the the mic so the others hear?"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "Okay. We'll figure out the mic situation for next time. Okay, so my question is um for the reconstruction actually um how do you um guarantee that it's that it's robust enough because like I imagine you just do it with a iPhone, right? It has like depth cameras and lidars inside of it, but like with other phones you probably can't do it yet. And also for the object detection No. and segmentation Uh-huh. how can you guarantee that it's actually like correctly detects affordances? It's kind of vague to me, honestly. Yeah. It's a It's an interesting question. Um so the in from the iPhone we don't actually use anything except for the camera images. So we don't actually use the depth and we don't use the lidar and stuff. Um so I guess with these Gaussian planning techniques it's um"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "just just from raw camera images and use this technique called COLMAP to figure out where all the cameras"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Robot learning motivation"}, {"t": 208, "title": "Simulation as a data source"}, {"t": 443, "title": "Overcoming simulation pain"}, {"t": 560, "title": "Automating environment creation"}, {"t": 744, "title": "Simulation for evaluation"}, {"t": 960, "title": "Large-scale behavior generation"}, {"t": 1342, "title": "Real-world adaptation"}, {"t": 1565, "title": "Conclusion and Q&A"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "University of Washington"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 23.02.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (52 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week02_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5-Bb84eTTqQ (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_abhishek_gupta.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 2: Robot Control &amp; Markov Decision Processes</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 23.02.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 2: Robot Control & Markov Decision Processes Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 23.02.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-feedback---actions-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Feedback -&gt; Actions", "title": "Slide 1: Feedback -> Actions", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Extended deadline for 1st homework to March 5th</li>\n<li>Created a moodle forum for QA</li>\n<li>Linked Course Website in MyStudies</li>\n<li>Made slides &amp; recordings available\n<ul>\n<li>login: (blanked out in the recording)</li>\n<li>pwd: (blanked out in the recording)</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Instructions for Paper Discussion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3.]</span></p>", "text": "Extended deadline for 1st homework to March 5th Created a moodle forum for QA Linked Course Website in MyStudies Made slides & recordings available login: (blanked out in the recording) pwd: (blanked out in the recording) Instructions for Paper Discussion [Note: slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-3-robot-motion-frame-3", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Robot Motion", "title": "Slide 3: Robot Motion", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Can be represented as <strong>rigid-body motion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Planar Motion</th>\n<th>Spatial Motion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a car seen from above at position <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x, y)</annotation></semantics></math> with heading angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a quadcopter with axes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x, y, z</annotation></semantics></math> and rotations roll (about <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>), pitch (about <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math>), yaw (about <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math>)]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SE(2): The special Euclidean group of rigid body motion in 2D</td>\n<td>SE(3): The special Euclidean group of rigid body motion in 3D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Can be represented as rigid-body motion Planar Motion Spatial Motion [Figure: a car seen from above at position ( x , y ) with heading angle θ ] [Figure: a quadcopter with axes x , y , z and rotations roll (about x ), pitch (about y ), yaw (about z )] SE(2): The special Euclidean group of rigid body motion in 2D SE(3): The special Euclidean group of rigid body motion in 3D", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-4-mathematical-representation-frame-4", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Mathematical Representation", "title": "Slide 4: Mathematical Representation", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<p><strong>SE(2)</strong> - 3 “Degrees of Freedom”</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>−</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{SE(2)} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\theta &amp; -\\sin\\theta &amp; x \\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; \\cos\\theta &amp; y \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>SE(3)</strong> - 6 “Degrees of Freedom”</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>31</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>32</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>33</mn></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mi>z</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" style=\"text-align: center\"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{SE(3)} = \\begin{bmatrix} r_{11} &amp; r_{12} &amp; r_{13} &amp; x \\\\ r_{21} &amp; r_{22} &amp; r_{23} &amp; y \\\\ r_{31} &amp; r_{32} &amp; r_{33} &amp; z \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∣</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">SO(n) = \\{R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times m} \\mid R^T R = I, \\det(R) = 1\\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the dimension should match the group, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>; the slide writes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m \\times m</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "SE(2) - 3 “Degrees of Freedom” T S E ( 2 ) = [ cos &#8289; θ − sin &#8289; θ x sin &#8289; θ cos &#8289; θ y 0 0 1 ] SE(3) - 6 “Degrees of Freedom” T S E ( 3 ) = [ r 11 r 12 r 13 x r 21 r 22 r 23 y r 31 r 32 r 33 z 0 0 0 1 ] S O ( n ) = { R ∈ ℝ m × m ∣ R T R = I , det &#8289; ( R ) = 1 } [Note: the dimension should match the group, R ∈ ℝ n × n ; the slide writes m × m .]", "t": 180}, {"id": "slide-5-chaining-transforms-frame-5", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Chaining Transforms", "title": "Slide 5: Chaining Transforms", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a Waymo self-driving car with three coordinate frames drawn in: Base Frame (on the car body), Sensor Frame (lidar on the roof) and Pedestrian Frame (on a pedestrian in a lidar point cloud, boxed “Pedestrian detected”). Dotted arrows label the transforms <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sensor</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Base</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{\\text{Base}}_{\\text{Sensor}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ped</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sensor</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{\\text{Sensor}}_{\\text{Ped}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ped</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Base</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{\\text{Base}}_{\\text{Ped}}</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ped</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Base</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sensor</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Base</mtext></msubsup><mo>×</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ped</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Sensor</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{\\text{Base}}_{\\text{Ped}} = T^{\\text{Base}}_{\\text{Sensor}} \\times T^{\\text{Sensor}}_{\\text{Ped}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "[Figure: a Waymo self-driving car with three coordinate frames drawn in: Base Frame (on the car body), Sensor Frame (lidar on the roof) and Pedestrian Frame (on a pedestrian in a lidar point cloud, boxed “Pedestrian detected”). Dotted arrows label the transforms T Sensor Base , T Ped Sensor and T Ped Base .] T Ped Base = T Sensor Base × T Ped Sensor", "t": 360}, {"id": "slide-6-articulated-robot-bodies-frame-6", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Articulated Robot Bodies", "title": "Slide 6: Articulated Robot Bodies", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Robots can be defined as articulated rigid bodies, consisting of:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Link</strong>: a single rigid body</li>\n<li><strong>Joint</strong>: connection between links</li>\n<li><strong>End-Effector</strong>: a device attached to a specific link, like end of arms</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: kinematic-chain diagram of a humanoid next to a photo of the robot, captioned “LOLA Humanoid Robot @ TUM”.]</span></p>", "text": "Robots can be defined as articulated rigid bodies, consisting of: Link : a single rigid body Joint : connection between links End-Effector : a device attached to a specific link, like end of arms [Figure: kinematic-chain diagram of a humanoid next to a photo of the robot, captioned “LOLA Humanoid Robot @ TUM”.]", "t": 422}, {"id": "slide-7-robot-arm-articulations-frame-7", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Robot Arm Articulations", "title": "Slide 7: Robot Arm Articulations", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Franka Emika</strong> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: the arm with links 0-7 coloured, and the same arm with joints 1-7 marked, annotated “spherical shoulder”, “elbow offsets”, “joint axes 5 &amp; 6 intercept”, “non-spherical wrist”]</span></li>\n<li><strong>SO-101</strong> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: the arm in simulation with a coordinate frame and joint angle <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_i</annotation></semantics></math> at each joint; a photo of the arm with joints labelled shoulder pan, shoulder lift, elbow flex, wrist flex, wrist roll, gripper]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Franka Emika [Figure: the arm with links 0-7 coloured, and the same arm with joints 1-7 marked, annotated “spherical shoulder”, “elbow offsets”, “joint axes 5 & 6 intercept”, “non-spherical wrist”] SO-101 [Figure: the arm in simulation with a coordinate frame and joint angle θ i at each joint; a photo of the arm with joints labelled shoulder pan, shoulder lift, elbow flex, wrist flex, wrist roll, gripper]", "t": 482}, {"id": "slide-8-robot-end-effectors-frame-8", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Robot End Effectors", "title": "Slide 8: Robot End Effectors", "frames": [8, 8], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three photos:]</span> Suction Gripper - Parallel Gripper - Dexterous Hand</p>", "text": "[Figure: three photos:] Suction Gripper - Parallel Gripper - Dexterous Hand", "t": 482}, {"id": "slide-9-robot-joints-frame-9", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Robot Joints", "title": "Slide 9: Robot Joints", "frames": [9, 9], "html": "<ul>\n<li>A joint provides constraints on the possible motions of the two rigid bodies it connects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: line drawings of six joint types: Revolute (R), Prismatic (P), Helical (H), Cylindrical (C), Universal (U), Spherical (S).]</span></p>\n<p>Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017</p>", "text": "A joint provides constraints on the possible motions of the two rigid bodies it connects. [Figure: line drawings of six joint types: Revolute (R), Prismatic (P), Helical (H), Cylindrical (C), Universal (U), Spherical (S).] Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-10-degrees-of-freedom-frame-10", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Degrees of Freedom", "title": "Slide 10: Degrees of Freedom", "frames": [10, 10], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Number of independent parameters required to completely specify the configuration or state of the robot</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Joint type</th>\n<th>dof <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></th>\n<th>Constraints <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math> between two planar rigid bodies</th>\n<th>Constraints <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math> between two spatial rigid bodies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Revolute (R)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prismatic (P)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Helical (H)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylindrical (C)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Universal (U)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spherical (S)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Chebychev-Grübler-Kutzbach Formula:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">dof</mtext><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rigid body freedoms</mtext></munder><mo>−</mo><munder><munder><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">joint constraints</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{dof} = \\underbrace{m(N - 1)}_{\\text{rigid body freedoms}} - \\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{J} c_i}_{\\text{joint constraints}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017</p>", "text": "Number of independent parameters required to completely specify the configuration or state of the robot Joint type dof f Constraints c between two planar rigid bodies Constraints c between two spatial rigid bodies Revolute (R) 1 2 5 Prismatic (P) 1 2 5 Helical (H) 1 N/A 5 Cylindrical (C) 2 N/A 4 Universal (U) 2 N/A 4 Spherical (S) 3 N/A 3 Chebychev-Grübler-Kutzbach Formula: dof = m ( N − 1 ) ⏟ rigid body freedoms − ∑ i = 1 J c i ⏟ joint constraints Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-11-configuration-space-frame-11", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Configuration Space", "title": "Slide 11: Configuration Space", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Set of all possible robot configurations</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>system</th>\n<th>topology</th>\n<th>sample representation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>point on a plane</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}^2</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spherical pendulum</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^2</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>180</mn><mi>°</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>180</mn><mi>°</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>90</mn><mi>°</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>90</mn><mi>°</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-180°, 180°) \\times [-90°, 90°]</annotation></semantics></math> (longitude, latitude)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2R robot arm</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2 = S^1 \\times S^1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0, 2\\pi) \\times [0, 2\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rotating sliding knob</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}^1 \\times S^1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^1 \\times [0, 2\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: each row also shows a sketch of the system, its topology (plane, sphere, torus, cylinder) and the flat chart with its edges identified.]</span></p>\n<p>Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017</p>", "text": "Set of all possible robot configurations system topology sample representation point on a plane 𝔼 2 ℝ 2 spherical pendulum S 2 [ − 180 ° , 180 ° ) × [ − 90 ° , 90 ° ] (longitude, latitude) 2R robot arm T 2 = S 1 × S 1 [ 0 , 2 π ) × [ 0 , 2 π ) rotating sliding knob 𝔼 1 × S 1 ℝ 1 × [ 0 , 2 π ) [Figure: each row also shows a sketch of the system, its topology (plane, sphere, torus, cylinder) and the flat chart with its edges identified.] Source: Modern Robotics by K. Lynch 2017", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-12-workspace-frame-12", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Workspace", "title": "Slide 12: Workspace", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<ul>\n<li>All points in space reachable by the robot’s end-effector</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: side and top views of an industrial arm’s working range, shaded: side-view dimensions 580, 580, 411, 112 and 982 (mm); top view with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±</mi><mn>165</mn><mi>°</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 165°</annotation></semantics></math> base rotation, “R121 Minimum turning radius axis 1” and R169.4.]</span></p>\n<p>Source: ABB, Technical Data for The IRB 120 Industrial Robot</p>", "text": "All points in space reachable by the robot’s end-effector [Figure: side and top views of an industrial arm’s working range, shaded: side-view dimensions 580, 580, 411, 112 and 982 (mm); top view with ± 165 ° base rotation, “R121 Minimum turning radius axis 1” and R169.4.] Source: ABB, Technical Data for The IRB 120 Industrial Robot", "t": 781}, {"id": "slide-13-obstacles-in-configuration-space-frame-13", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Obstacles in Configuration Space", "title": "Slide 13: Obstacles in Configuration Space", "frames": [13, 13], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, “An obstacle in the robot’s workspace”: a two-link arm with joint angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (base) and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (elbow), a circular obstacle, the arm tip at A and a target point B. Right, “Configuration Space representation of this obstacle”: a plot of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (0-180) against <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (0-360) in which the obstacle becomes a large irregular blue region, with the configurations <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_B</annotation></semantics></math> marked as points.]</span></p>\n<p>Source: Robot Motion Planning by JC Latombe 1991</p>", "text": "[Figure: left, “An obstacle in the robot’s workspace”: a two-link arm with joint angles α (base) and β (elbow), a circular obstacle, the arm tip at A and a target point B. Right, “Configuration Space representation of this obstacle”: a plot of α (0-180) against β (0-360) in which the obstacle becomes a large irregular blue region, with the configurations q A and q B marked as points.] Source: Robot Motion Planning by JC Latombe 1991", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-14-motion-planning-in-c-space-frame-14", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Motion Planning in C-Space", "title": "Slide 14: Motion Planning in C-Space", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<ul>\n<li>C-Space used for robot motion planning</li>\n<li>Decouples robot geometry from path planning</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a free region <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{C}_{free}</annotation></semantics></math> bounded by obstacle regions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{C}_{obs}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a curved path from start <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_I</annotation></semantics></math> to goal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_G</annotation></semantics></math> that avoids them.]</span></p>\n<p>Source: Planning Algorithms by Steven M. LaValle 2006</p>", "text": "C-Space used for robot motion planning Decouples robot geometry from path planning [Figure: a free region 𝒞 f r e e bounded by obstacle regions 𝒞 o b s , with a curved path from start q I to goal q G that avoids them.] Source: Planning Algorithms by Steven M. LaValle 2006", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-15-task-space-frame-15", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Task Space", "title": "Slide 15: Task Space", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<ul>\n<li>The manifold in which the robot’s task is naturally defined, independent of its embodiment</li>\n<li>If Task Space dimension &lt; Robot’s DoF = Redundancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: robot arms on mobile bases cleaning a long whiteboard wall; a robot vacuum on a floor.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Task: whiteboard cleaning - Task Space: whiteboard surface <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Task: clean room floor - Task Space: vacuum robot’s position on the floor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^2</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>", "text": "The manifold in which the robot’s task is naturally defined, independent of its embodiment If Task Space dimension < Robot’s DoF = Redundancy [Figure: robot arms on mobile bases cleaning a long whiteboard wall; a robot vacuum on a floor.] Task: whiteboard cleaning - Task Space: whiteboard surface ℝ 2 Task: clean room floor - Task Space: vacuum robot’s position on the floor ℝ 2", "t": 960}, {"id": "slide-16-question-time-frames-16-17", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Question Time!", "title": "Slide 16: Question Time!", "frames": [16, 17], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a 7-joint robot arm, captioned “7 DoF”.]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Configuration Space?</th>\n<th>Workspace?</th>\n<th>Task Space?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7D Manifold <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\theta_1 \\dots \\theta_7)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Reachable physical space <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x, y, z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>6D Pose of end-effector SE(3)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 17) replaces the arm photo:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Redundancy -&gt; Null Space Motion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: side and top views of the 7-DoF arm’s working range in millimetres: reach 855 to each side and 1190 up, 360 below the base; top view a full circle of radius 855.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: a 7-joint robot arm, captioned “7 DoF”.] Configuration Space? Workspace? Task Space? 7D Manifold ( θ 1 … θ 7 ) Reachable physical space ( x , y , z ) 6D Pose of end-effector SE(3) [Second build (frame 17) replaces the arm photo:] Redundancy -> Null Space Motion [Figure: side and top views of the 7-DoF arm’s working range in millimetres: reach 855 to each side and 1190 up, 360 below the base; top view a full circle of radius 855.]", "t": 1021}, {"id": "slide-17-forward-kinematics-frame-18", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Forward Kinematics", "title": "Slide 17: Forward Kinematics", "frames": [18, 18], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Given robot joints, what is the end-effector pose?</li>\n<li>Mapping from C to Task Space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f: \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = f(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q \\in \\mathbb{R}^n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\in SE(3)</annotation></semantics></math> deterministic, but not bijective</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a planar three-link arm with joint angles <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3</annotation></semantics></math> and an unknown end-effector position <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>?</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{r}_e = ?</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></p>", "text": "Given robot joints, what is the end-effector pose? Mapping from C to Task Space, f : 𝒞 → 𝒳 x = f ( q ) , q ∈ ℝ n , x ∈ S E ( 3 ) deterministic, but not bijective [Figure: a planar three-link arm with joint angles θ 1 , θ 2 , θ 3 and an unknown end-effector position r → e = ? ]", "t": 1141}, {"id": "slide-18-inverse-kinematics-frame-19", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Inverse Kinematics", "title": "Slide 18: Inverse Kinematics", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Given end-effector pose, what are the joint configurations?</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q = f^{-1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, mapping <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒳</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}: \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same arm with the end-effector position <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{r}_e</annotation></semantics></math> given and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>?</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_1 = ?</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>?</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_2 = ?</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>?</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_3 = ?</annotation></semantics></math> unknown.]</span></p>", "text": "Given end-effector pose, what are the joint configurations? q = f − 1 ( x ) , mapping f − 1 : 𝒳 → 𝒫 ( 𝒞 ) [Figure: the same arm with the end-effector position r → e given and θ 1 = ? , θ 2 = ? , θ 3 = ? unknown.]", "t": 1141}, {"id": "slide-19-inverse-kinematics-non-uniqueness-frame-20", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Inverse Kinematics <span class=\"own\">[non-uniqueness]</span>", "title": "Slide 19: Inverse Kinematics [non-uniqueness]", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Often <strong>non-unique or has no analytical solution</strong></li>\n<li>Optimization-based IKs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two different joint configurations of the three-link arm that reach the same end-effector point <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{r}_e</annotation></semantics></math>, joined by a double arrow with a question mark.]</span></p>", "text": "Often non-unique or has no analytical solution Optimization-based IKs [Figure: two different joint configurations of the three-link arm that reach the same end-effector point r → e , joined by a double arrow with a question mark.]", "t": 1201}, {"id": "slide-20-optimization-based-ik-frame-21", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Optimization-Based IK", "title": "Slide 20: Optimization-Based IK", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Objective:</strong> Minimize the distance between the current end-effector and the target</li>\n<li><strong>Loss:</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target</mtext></msub></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mi>e</mi></munder><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2}\\|\\underbrace{f(\\theta) - x_{\\text{target}}}_{e}\\|^2</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Update:</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_{\\text{new}} = \\theta_{\\text{old}} - \\alpha \\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Gradient of the Loss:</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta) = \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\right)^T e</annotation></semantics></math> (arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>: Jacobian)</li>\n<li><strong>Jacobian Method:</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_{\\text{new}} = \\theta_{\\text{old}} - \\alpha J(\\theta)^T (f(\\theta) - x_{\\text{target}})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Objective: Minimize the distance between the current end-effector and the target Loss: L ( θ ) = 1 2 ∥ f ( θ ) − x target ⏟ e ∥ 2 Update: θ new = θ old − α ∇ θ L ( θ ) Gradient of the Loss: ∇ θ L ( θ ) = ( ∂ f ∂ θ ) T e (arrow at ∂ f ∂ θ : Jacobian) Jacobian Method: θ new = θ old − α J ( θ ) T ( f ( θ ) − x target )", "t": 1260}, {"id": "slide-21-converting-ik-to-motor-commands-frame-22", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Converting IK to Motor Commands", "title": "Slide 21: Converting IK to Motor Commands", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<ul>\n<li>IK gives the target joints, but robot can’t “jump” there</li>\n<li>Time for movement?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the SO-101 arm with an arrow “Move 1cm in Y (0,1,0)”.]</span></p>", "text": "IK gives the target joints, but robot can’t “jump” there Time for movement? [Figure: the SO-101 arm with an arrow “Move 1cm in Y (0,1,0)”.]", "t": 1320}, {"id": "slide-22-trajectory-waypoint-generation-lerp-frame-23", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: LERP", "title": "Slide 22: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: LERP", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Inputs: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">start</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\text{start}}, q_{\\text{target}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^6</annotation></semantics></math>, time T, control frequency f</li>\n<li>Number of Waypoints <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = \\lceil T \\cdot f \\rceil</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Normalized Time Progress <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_i = \\frac{i}{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_i \\in [0, 1]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Linear Interpolation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">start</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">start</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(s_i) = q_{\\text{start}} + s_i \\cdot (q_{\\text{target}} - q_{\\text{start}})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: small SO-101 arm with the arrow “Move 1cm in Y (0,1,0)”.]</span></p>", "text": "Inputs: q start , q target ∈ ℝ 6 , time T, control frequency f Number of Waypoints N = ⌈ T ⋅ f ⌉ Normalized Time Progress s i = i N , s i ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] Linear Interpolation q ( s i ) = q start + s i ⋅ ( q target − q start ) [Figure: small SO-101 arm with the arrow “Move 1cm in Y (0,1,0)”.]", "t": 1441}, {"id": "slide-23-issues-with-lerp-frame-24", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Issues with LERP", "title": "Slide 23: Issues with LERP", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “LERP Trajectory Profiles (1s Move)”, three stacked plots against Time (s) from -0.2 to 1.2. Position <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (m) ramps linearly from 0 at t=0 to 1 at t=1. Velocity <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (m/s) jumps from 0 to 1 at t=0 and back to 0 at t=1. Acceleration <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (m/s<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi></mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2</annotation></semantics></math>) is zero except a spike of about +150 at t=0 and about -150 at t=1.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the velocity step]</span> <strong>Instant Max Speed!</strong> Real motors can’t reach full speed in zero time</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the acceleration spike]</span> <strong>High Jerk, Dirac Deltas</strong> Stress on actuators, vibration</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: “LERP Trajectory Profiles (1s Move)”, three stacked plots against Time (s) from -0.2 to 1.2. Position q ( t ) (m) ramps linearly from 0 at t=0 to 1 at t=1. Velocity q ̇ ( t ) (m/s) jumps from 0 to 1 at t=0 and back to 0 at t=1. Acceleration q ̈ ( t ) (m/s 2 ) is zero except a spike of about +150 at t=0 and about -150 at t=1.] [arrow at the velocity step] Instant Max Speed! Real motors can’t reach full speed in zero time [arrow at the acceleration spike] High Jerk, Dirac Deltas Stress on actuators, vibration", "t": 1441}, {"id": "slide-24-trajectory-waypoint-generation-quintic-splines-frame-25", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: Quintic Splines", "title": "Slide 24: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: Quintic Splines", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t) = a_0 + a_1 t + a_2 t^2 + a_3 t^3 + a_4 t^4 + a_5 t^5</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>6x Boundary Conditions for Smoothness:\n<ul>\n<li>Start &amp; End with no velocity and accel. while keeping position</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">start</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">start</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(s_i) = q_{\\text{start}} + (q_{\\text{target}} - q_{\\text{start}}) \\cdot f(s_i)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_i \\in [0, 1]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Quintic Time Scaling <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>5</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(s_i) = 10 s_i^3 - 15 s_i^4 + 6 s_i^5</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>", "text": "q ( t ) = a 0 + a 1 t + a 2 t 2 + a 3 t 3 + a 4 t 4 + a 5 t 5 6x Boundary Conditions for Smoothness: Start & End with no velocity and accel. while keeping position q ( s i ) = q start + ( q target − q start ) ⋅ f ( s i ) , s i ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] Quintic Time Scaling f ( s i ) = 10 s i 3 − 15 s i 4 + 6 s i 5", "t": 1441}, {"id": "slide-25-trajectory-waypoint-generation-quintic-splines-profiles-frame-26", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: Quintic Splines <span class=\"own\">[profiles]</span>", "title": "Slide 25: Trajectory Waypoint Generation: Quintic Splines [profiles]", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Standard for industry robots like Frankas</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Quintic Spline Trajectory Profiles (1s Move)”, three stacked plots against Time (s) from -0.2 to 1.2. Position <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t)</annotation></semantics></math>: smooth S-curve from 0 to 1 between t=0 and t=1. Velocity <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>: smooth bell, zero at both ends, peak about 1.9 m/s at t=0.5. Acceleration <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>: smooth, zero at both ends, about +5.8 m/s<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi></mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2</annotation></semantics></math> near t=0.2 and about -5.8 near t=0.8. Compare with the LERP spikes on slide 23.]</span></p>", "text": "Standard for industry robots like Frankas [Figure: “Quintic Spline Trajectory Profiles (1s Move)”, three stacked plots against Time (s) from -0.2 to 1.2. Position q ( t ) : smooth S-curve from 0 to 1 between t=0 and t=1. Velocity q ̇ ( t ) : smooth bell, zero at both ends, peak about 1.9 m/s at t=0.5. Acceleration q ̈ ( t ) : smooth, zero at both ends, about +5.8 m/s 2 near t=0.2 and about -5.8 near t=0.8. Compare with the LERP spikes on slide 23.]", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-26-pid-control-frame-27", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: PID Control", "title": "Slide 26: PID Control", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Given trajectory waypoints <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(s_i)</annotation></semantics></math> and current measurement, continuously calculate corrective motor commands <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(u_k)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Tracking error <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e(t) = q_{desired}(t) - q_{measured}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Continuous form <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t) = K_p e(t) + K_i \\int_0^t e(\\tau)\\, d\\tau + K_d \\frac{de(t)}{dt}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Discrete form</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Proportional</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Integral</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Derivative</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k = \\underbrace{K_p e_k}_{\\text{Proportional}} + \\underbrace{K_i \\sum_{j=0}^{k} (e_j \\cdot \\Delta t)}_{\\text{Integral}} + \\underbrace{K_d \\frac{e_k - e_{k-1}}{\\Delta t}}_{\\text{Derivative}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Given trajectory waypoints q ( s i ) and current measurement, continuously calculate corrective motor commands ( u k ) Tracking error e ( t ) = q d e s i r e d ( t ) − q m e a s u r e d ( t ) Continuous form u ( t ) = K p e ( t ) + K i ∫ 0 t e ( τ ) d τ + K d d e ( t ) d t Discrete form u k = K p e k ⏟ Proportional + K i ∑ j = 0 k ( e j ⋅ Δ t ) ⏟ Integral + K d e k − e k − 1 Δ t ⏟ Derivative", "t": 1561}, {"id": "slide-27-pid-control-intuition-frame-28", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: PID Control <span class=\"own\">[intuition]</span>", "title": "Slide 27: PID Control [intuition]", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k = K_p e_k + K_i \\sum_{j=0}^{k} (e_j \\cdot \\Delta t) + K_d \\frac{e_k - e_{k-1}}{\\Delta t}, \\qquad \\Delta t = 1/f</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proportional</strong> - “Spring”: The bigger the tracking error, the harder the robot pulls</li>\n<li><strong>Integral</strong> - “Anti-Gravity”: Helps compensate if gravity is too much</li>\n<li><strong>Derivative</strong> - “Damper”: Stops the arm from from overshooting past a waypoint</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 1/f</annotation></semantics></math>: If frequency too low, “Damper” might not be able to react fast enough</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot arm tracking a desired path (green) through waypoints <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(s_0), q(s_1), q(s_2), \\dots, q(s_N)</annotation></semantics></math>; the actual path (red) wobbles around it. Purple arrows from red to green are labelled “Proportional pull (P)”; a sag below the path is labelled “Gravity (needs Integral)”; the gap between the curves is “Error <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_k</annotation></semantics></math>”; a bump past the path is “Overshoot (needs Derivative)”.]</span></p>", "text": "u k = K p e k + K i ∑ j = 0 k ( e j ⋅ Δ t ) + K d e k − e k − 1 Δ t , Δ t = 1 / f Proportional - “Spring”: The bigger the tracking error, the harder the robot pulls Integral - “Anti-Gravity”: Helps compensate if gravity is too much Derivative - “Damper”: Stops the arm from from overshooting past a waypoint Δ t = 1 / f : If frequency too low, “Damper” might not be able to react fast enough [Figure: a robot arm tracking a desired path (green) through waypoints q ( s 0 ) , q ( s 1 ) , q ( s 2 ) , … , q ( s N ) ; the actual path (red) wobbles around it. Purple arrows from red to green are labelled “Proportional pull (P)”; a sag below the path is labelled “Gravity (needs Integral)”; the gap between the curves is “Error e k ”; a bump past the path is “Overshoot (needs Derivative)”.]", "t": 1561}, {"id": "slide-28-untitled-transition-question-frame-29", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 28: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<p><strong>So we know how to plan &amp; execute robot motion are we done?</strong></p>\n<p>Not quite…</p>", "text": "So we know how to plan & execute robot motion are we done? Not quite…", "t": 1680}, {"id": "slide-29-robot-learning-frame-30", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Robot Learning", "title": "Slide 29: Robot Learning", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<p>Solving <strong>Robotics</strong> via <strong>Machine Learning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Robotics: Perception, Control, …</li>\n<li>Machine Learning: Imitation Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Dynamics Learning, Representation Learning, …</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Solving Robotics via Machine Learning Robotics: Perception, Control, … Machine Learning: Imitation Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Dynamics Learning, Representation Learning, …", "t": 1680}, {"id": "slide-30-sequential-decision-making-frames-31-36", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: Sequential Decision Making", "title": "Slide 30: Sequential Decision Making", "frames": [31, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What is the best sequence of actions to cut the sushi?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video playing across six frames: a two-armed robot workstation with a bowl, a wooden box with a red drawer, a red cup holding utensils, a tray, a cutting board and a toy sushi roll. The arms move the roll onto the board, take a red toy knife from the cup, hand it between grippers and slice the roll into pieces.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>The Ingredients for Robotic Diffusion Transformers</em>, Dasari, <strong>Mees</strong> et. al., ICRA 2024</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the paper appeared at ICRA 2025, as week 6 cites it, not ICRA 2024.]</span></p>", "text": "What is the best sequence of actions to cut the sushi? [Figure: embedded video playing across six frames: a two-armed robot workstation with a bowl, a wooden box with a red drawer, a red cup holding utensils, a tray, a cutting board and a toy sushi roll. The arms move the roll onto the board, take a red toy knife from the cup, hand it between grippers and slice the roll into pieces.] Reference: The Ingredients for Robotic Diffusion Transformers , Dasari, Mees et. al., ICRA 2024 [Note: the paper appeared at ICRA 2025, as week 6 cites it, not ICRA 2024.]", "t": 1862}, {"id": "slide-31-from-pixels-to-decisions-representing-a-policy-frame-37", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: From Pixels to Decisions: Representing a Policy", "title": "Slide 31: From Pixels to Decisions: Representing a Policy", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two parallel pipelines through the same convolutional network diagram.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Image classification: input <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math> (photo of a kitten in a hat) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(y \\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math>: Cat 0.8, Tiger 0.0, Bear 0.0, Dog 0.2</li>\n<li>Policy: observation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o</annotation></semantics></math> (view through a car windscreen of a snowy road) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid o)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math>: Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: two parallel pipelines through the same convolutional network diagram.] Image classification: input x (photo of a kitten in a hat) -> f ( y ∣ x ) -> output y : Cat 0.8, Tiger 0.0, Bear 0.0, Dog 0.2 Policy: observation o (view through a car windscreen of a snowy road) -> π θ ( a ∣ o ) -> action a : Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2", "t": 1862}, {"id": "slide-32-mapping-observations-to-actions-frame-38", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Mapping Observations to Actions", "title": "Slide 32: Mapping Observations to Actions", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: observation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> (snowy road through the windscreen) -&gt; convolutional network -&gt; action distribution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> (Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2), with an arrow from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> closing the loop.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - Policy - Partially Observable</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - Policy - Fully Observable</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> - action; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> - observation; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> - state</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: observation o t (snowy road through the windscreen) -> convolutional network -> action distribution a t (Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2), with an arrow from a t back to o t closing the loop.] π θ ( a t ∣ o t ) - Policy - Partially Observable π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) - Policy - Fully Observable a t - action; o t - observation; s t - state", "t": 1982}, {"id": "slide-33-state-vs-observation-frames-39-40", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: State vs Observation", "title": "Slide 33: State vs Observation", "frames": [39, 40], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a car icon labelled “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> - state”, an arrow to a road photo labelled “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> - observation”, and a reverse arrow crossed out in red. The second build swaps the clear-day road for a dark, snowy night view.]</span></p>\n<p>No matter the weather or visibility, the <strong>state does not change</strong>!</p>", "text": "[Figure: a car icon labelled “ s t - state”, an arrow to a road photo labelled “ o t - observation”, and a reverse arrow crossed out in red. The second build swaps the clear-day road for a dark, snowy night view.] No matter the weather or visibility, the state does not change !", "t": 1982}, {"id": "slide-34-markov-property-frame-41", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: Markov Property", "title": "Slide 34: Markov Property", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>If you know <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1</annotation></semantics></math> not necessary to determine <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_3</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: graphical model. States <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1 \\to s_2 \\to s_3</annotation></semantics></math> along the bottom, the arrows labelled <strong>Transition Function</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each state emits an observation (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1 \\to o_1</annotation></semantics></math> labelled <strong>Lossy Mapping</strong>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_2 \\to o_2</annotation></semantics></math>); each observation maps to an action through <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_1 \\to a_1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_2 \\to a_2</annotation></semantics></math>); <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_1</annotation></semantics></math> feeds into <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_2</annotation></semantics></math>. Portrait captioned “Andrey Markov”.]</span></p>", "text": "If you know S 2 then S 1 not necessary to determine S 3 [Figure: graphical model. States s 1 → s 2 → s 3 along the bottom, the arrows labelled Transition Function p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) . Each state emits an observation ( s 1 → o 1 labelled Lossy Mapping , s 2 → o 2 ); each observation maps to an action through π θ ( o 1 → a 1 , o 2 → a 2 ); a 1 feeds into s 2 . Portrait captioned “Andrey Markov”.]", "t": 2103}, {"id": "slide-35-alternative-notations-frame-42", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: Alternative Notations", "title": "Slide 35: Alternative Notations", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>RL notation (Richard Bellman)</th>\n<th>Control notation (Lev Pontryagin)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> - action</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_t</annotation></semantics></math> - action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> - state</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t</annotation></semantics></math> - state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s, a)</annotation></semantics></math> - reward</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(x, u)</annotation></semantics></math> - cost function</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s, a) = -c(x, u)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: portraits of Richard Bellman and Lev Pontryagin.]</span></p>", "text": "RL notation (Richard Bellman) Control notation (Lev Pontryagin) a t - action u t - action s t - state x t - state r ( s , a ) - reward c ( x , u ) - cost function r ( s , a ) = − c ( x , u ) [Figure: portraits of Richard Bellman and Lev Pontryagin.]", "t": 2160}, {"id": "slide-36-markov-decision-process-frame-43", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Markov Decision Process", "title": "Slide 36: Markov Decision Process", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Define by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M = \\langle \\mathcal{S}, \\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{P}, \\mathcal{R} \\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: loop between the environment (globe) and the robot (arm): the environment sends “state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math>” to the robot, the robot sends “action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math>” back.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>: State space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>: Action space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t \\in \\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>: Transition probability, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} \\sim \\mathcal{P}(\\cdot|, s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>: Reward function, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r: \\mathcal{S} \\times \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t = R(s_t, a_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the signature <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{S} \\times \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the form <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(s_t, a_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> disagree; both conventions are common, the slide mixes them.]</span></p>", "text": "Define by M = ⟨ 𝒮 , 𝒜 , 𝒫 , ℛ ⟩ [Figure: loop between the environment (globe) and the robot (arm): the environment sends “state s t , reward r t ” to the robot, the robot sends “action a t ” back.] 𝒮 : State space, s t ∈ 𝒮 𝒜 : Action space, a t ∈ 𝒜 𝒫 : Transition probability, s t + 1 ∼ 𝒫 ( ⋅ | , s t , a t ) ℛ : Reward function, r : 𝒮 × 𝒜 → ℝ , r t = R ( s t , a t , s t + 1 ) [Note: the signature 𝒮 × 𝒜 → ℝ and the form R ( s t , a t , s t + 1 ) disagree; both conventions are common, the slide mixes them.]", "t": 2160}, {"id": "slide-37-mdp-state-space-frame-44", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: MDP: State Space", "title": "Slide 37: MDP: State Space", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Discrete <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a roulette wheel; a chess board]</span></li>\n<li>Continuous <span class=\"own\">[Figure: the SO-101 robot arm; a quadcopter drone]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Discrete [Figure: a roulette wheel; a chess board] Continuous [Figure: the SO-101 robot arm; a quadcopter drone]", "t": 2221}, {"id": "slide-38-mdp-action-space-frame-45", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: MDP: Action Space", "title": "Slide 38: MDP: Action Space", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Discrete <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a hand of playing cards; keyboard arrow keys]</span></li>\n<li>Continuous <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a joystick; hands on a steering wheel]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Discrete [Figure: a hand of playing cards; keyboard arrow keys] Continuous [Figure: a joystick; hands on a steering wheel]", "t": 2221}, {"id": "slide-39-mdp-transition-model-frame-46", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: MDP: Transition Model", "title": "Slide 39: MDP: Transition Model", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Uncertainty caused by sensor noise, motor overshooting…</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Deterministic</th>\n<th>Stochastic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} = f(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} \\sim \\mathcal{P}(\\cdot\\vert, s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action: turn steering wheel 5°</td>\n<td>Action: turn steering wheel 5°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a car on a dry road with one curved arrow showing where it goes]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same car on an icy patch with many arrows fanning out]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Uncertainty caused by sensor noise, motor overshooting… Deterministic Stochastic s t + 1 = f ( s t , a t ) s t + 1 ∼ 𝒫 ( ⋅ | , s t , a t ) Action: turn steering wheel 5° Action: turn steering wheel 5° [Figure: a car on a dry road with one curved arrow showing where it goes] [Figure: the same car on an icy patch with many arrows fanning out]", "t": 2341}, {"id": "slide-40-mdp-reward-function-frames-47-48", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: MDP: Reward Function", "title": "Slide 40: MDP: Reward Function", "frames": [47, 48], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How to detect if reward conditions are met? <span class=\"own\">[added in the second build]</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sparse Rewards</th>\n<th>Dense Rewards</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>e.g. 1 if grasped, 0 otherwise</td>\n<td>e.g. distance to object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video of a robot arm grasping objects on a cork table]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video of the same arm reaching for objects, with two camera insets labelled “Selected affordance region” and “Detected affordance region center”]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Reference: <em>Affordance Learning from Play for Sample-Efficient Policy Learning</em>, Borja, <strong>Mees</strong> et. al., ICRA 2022</p>", "text": "How to detect if reward conditions are met? [added in the second build] Sparse Rewards Dense Rewards e.g. 1 if grasped, 0 otherwise e.g. distance to object [Figure: video of a robot arm grasping objects on a cork table] [Figure: video of the same arm reaching for objects, with two camera insets labelled “Selected affordance region” and “Detected affordance region center”] Reference: Affordance Learning from Play for Sample-Efficient Policy Learning , Borja, Mees et. al., ICRA 2022", "t": 2402}, {"id": "slide-41-trajectory-probability-frame-49", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: Trajectory Probability", "title": "Slide 41: Trajectory Probability", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Probability of a trajectory <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = (s_0, a_0, s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_T)</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>:</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(\\tau) = \\rho(s_0) \\prod_{t=0}^{T-1} \\pi(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[braces under the three factors]</span> <strong>Initial State Distribution</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(s_0)</annotation></semantics></math>), <strong>Policy</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>), <strong>Transition Function</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>)</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: several trajectories fanning out from a start state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> to end states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{T_1}, \\dots, s_{T_4}</annotation></semantics></math>; one highlighted as a solid path.]</span></p>", "text": "Probability of a trajectory τ = ( s 0 , a 0 , s 1 , a 1 , … , s T ) under policy π : p π ( τ ) = ρ ( s 0 ) ∏ t = 0 T − 1 π ( a t ∣ s t ) P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) [braces under the three factors] Initial State Distribution ( ρ ( s 0 ) ), Policy ( π ), Transition Function ( P ) [Figure: several trajectories fanning out from a start state s 0 to end states s T 1 , … , s T 4 ; one highlighted as a solid path.]", "t": 2521}, {"id": "slide-42-finite-horizon-vs-infinite-horizon-mdps-frame-50", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Finite-Horizon vs Infinite-Horizon MDPs", "title": "Slide 42: Finite-Horizon vs Infinite-Horizon MDPs", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Finite-Horizon</strong>: the task has a strict time limit/steps <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Infinite-Horizon</strong>: interaction continues forever <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(T = \\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>or until a “terminal state” is reached\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discount Factor</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>: Rewards closer in time are higher weighted</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a clock face that spirals into smaller and smaller copies of itself.]</span></p>", "text": "Finite-Horizon : the task has a strict time limit/steps H Infinite-Horizon : interaction continues forever ( T = ∞ ) or until a “terminal state” is reached Discount Factor γ : Rewards closer in time are higher weighted [Figure: a clock face that spirals into smaller and smaller copies of itself.]", "t": 2580}, {"id": "slide-43-the-learning-objective-frame-51", "num": 43, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 43: The Learning Objective", "title": "Slide 43: The Learning Objective", "frames": [51, 51], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Accumulated reward an agent receives: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G = \\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} r_t</annotation></semantics></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G = \\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Expected Return: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} r_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Optimal Policy: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi J(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same trajectory fan from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Accumulated reward an agent receives: G = ∑ t = 0 T − 1 r t or G = ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t Expected Return: J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 r t ] or J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t ] Optimal Policy: π * = arg &#8289; max &#8289; π J ( π ) [Figure: the same trajectory fan from s 0 .]", "t": 2642}, {"id": "end-frame-52", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-1-feedback---actions-frame-2", "label": "Slide 1: Feedback -> Actions", "html": "slide 2 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 1 to 3."}, {"slide": "slide-4-mathematical-representation-frame-4", "label": "Slide 4: Mathematical Representation", "html": "the dimension should match the group, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>; the slide writes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m \\times m</annotation></semantics></math>."}, {"slide": "slide-30-sequential-decision-making-frames-31-36", "label": "Slide 30: Sequential Decision Making", "html": "the paper appeared at ICRA 2025, as week 6 cites it, not ICRA 2024."}, {"slide": "slide-36-markov-decision-process-frame-43", "label": "Slide 36: Markov Decision Process", "html": "the signature <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{S} \\times \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the form <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(s_t, a_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> disagree; both conventions are common, the slide mixes them."}]}, {"key": "03", "dir": "week03_imitation", "date": "Mon 2 Mar 2026", "topic": "Imitation Learning", "summary": "Behavioral cloning and why it breaks: compounding errors from distribution shift (O(eps T^2)), fixed by side-camera augmentation, DAgger (O(eps T)) and human-gated interventions. Then non-Markovian experts and causal confusion, and multimodal demonstrations, handled by Gaussian mixtures, autoregressive discretization, diffusion and latent variable models. Closes with goal- and language-conditioned learning from play data.", "key_ideas": ["BC is supervised learning on non-i.i.d. data: small errors push the robot off the expert's state distribution", "DAgger relabels the states the policy actually visits with expert actions", "Adding history can hurt: the policy may latch onto spurious cues (causal confusion)", "MSE averages the modes of a multimodal expert - the skier hits the tree", "Latent plans: a posterior encodes the demo, a prior proposes plans from start and goal, a KL term ties them"], "papers": [{"title": "Causal Confusion in Imitation Learning", "who": "de Haan et al., 2019", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11979"}, {"title": "Representation learning for visual imitation", "who": "Pari et al., 2021", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01511"}, {"title": "Transporter Networks...", "who": "Zeng et al., 2020", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.14406"}], "homework": ["hw3_imitation_learning"], "lecture": {"id": "Ef4R5s1LqoQ", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 3: Imitation Learning | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "50:37", "description": "Lecture 3 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "All right. Uh welcome everybody um to the third lecture um in robot learning from fundamentals to foundation models. And uh today we're going to talk about um imitation learning. Uh first um before we start um a couple of notes on the course um structure. Uh we received um a bit more feedback uh from last week and uh we decided um to move um from three paper presentations uh per lecture on Mondays to two uh and we will um offload some of the paper presentations uh to some of the Thursday uh sessions uh in in that following room. Uh I think Alexey uh will follow up uh with an email on this uh soon cuz we were waiting to see who uh will stay registered and who is uh uh not continuing the course before we um uh shuffle things around. Um uh regarding the homework assignments,"}, {"t": 60, "text": "um two quick notes. Uh first of all, uh remember the first homework assignment is due in uh about 3 days and there is still quite a lot of um students who have not submitted anything or have started working on this. So, please again don't wait until the very last day to start working on your uh homework assignment. Uh and for those who already have have finished and um successfully uh passed this first assignment, uh we already released um the second homework uh last week. Um and this will be due on March 12th. Uh also quick note, um you know, when you do these assignments like and you uh work on the autograder, you know, sometimes it's easy to see uh if, you know, you you use a lot of AI to solve this cuz maybe like it takes you, you know, uh many hours for the first like exercises and then for the next ones, you know, you you breeze through it in like 10 minutes or something. So, you know, like try your best to solve"}, {"t": 121, "text": "them yourself if possible. Cool. So, let's Oops. Let's start. So, last lecture we defined some notation to help us understand how we can map from images to observations from image observations, sorry, to actions. And we also showed how observations are different for example from states when we formalize policies, right? And so we defined all this notation and what are like partially observable policies versus ones that have like our fully observable. So, today we're going to look at imitation learning as a kind of first way to train such policies in imitation by mimicking expert trajectories. So, we start typically by assuming that we have access to a data set of expert trajectories, you know, that are not like random movements, but"}, {"t": 182, "text": "they are sequences of states and actions like let's say human driving or a human teleoperating a robotic arm that successfully achieves some some goal. And we assume that these demonstrations are optimal or show optimal behavior to achieve a certain goal. And so our goal here will be to to learn this this policy pi theta that imitates this behavior. In other words, we want to find the parameters theta from this neural network that when the robot sees a state S it produces an action that is as close as possible to what the expert would have done. And so in this example, for example, for the driving in the snowing condition it would be like uh, imitating the action that a human driver would have done. Um, the the most, uh, straightforward way how to implement, uh, imitation"}, {"t": 240, "text": "learning is an algorithm called behavior cloning or BC. Um, some of you might already be familiar with it. Um, so again, we we, uh, it's very simple. We start with this data set of optimal, uh, state and action, uh, trajectories provided by the expert. Uh, and now for a deterministic policy, we treat these, uh, we we treat this as a regression problem uh, where our goal is to, um, find, uh, to minimize the difference between the, uh, action the expert took, uh, and the action, uh, um, that our, uh, policy predicts, uh, for the same state. Um, and then formally, we optimize this, um, uh, with, for example, doing a mean squared error loss across our data set. So, during training, we will like sample a batch, um, do a forward pass with the network to get a the predicted actions, compute the loss, um, and then back propagate it into the network parameters. And then you do this iteratively with your favorite optimizer, um, with stochastic gradient descent to"}, {"t": 301, "text": "optimize your policy. Oh, oh, what's going on here? Sorry. Um, to understand, um, Okay, so, this is kind of the the vanilla version of this algorithm. Now, um, we have to look like when does it fail, right? Or how does it work? Um, and, uh, I think we touched upon this, um, already in previous lectures, but, um, essentially, many things can go can go wrong. Sorry. Okay. Uh, essentially, um, there are a few different, uh, differences to, uh, let's say supervised learning, um, um, that we touched upon, I think in some previous lectures like the IID assumption. So, in in supervised learning as we said, we have this IID assumption over the inputs, um,"}, {"t": 360, "text": "or that they are independent and identically distributed. This means that your inputs X are independent of the labels we predict. So, if you're classifying images of cats, your prediction for the image A does not change the content of the image B, right? And now in behavioral cloning things are a little bit different because this IID assumption doesn't really hold. So, let's say that we have like this this this trajectory over here with this four states and this this action set connect them. And now we we start, this is what we're trying to mimic and we start with our policy as at the first state. And then let's say that our policy makes like a tiny error um, because, you know, maybe like it didn't fully converge or for for many different reasons, maybe like there's just a very very small error in our prediction. And so it leads us to a state that's kind of like close to S2, but it's not"}, {"t": 420, "text": "exactly identical, right? It could be just like close to it. And this brings us like slightly off track. And because now this is a new state that we didn't necessarily train on, our next prediction most likely will also be different, right? Because it's like, oh wow, like we haven't seen this state before. So, it it's very likely that our prediction will be kind of wrong again. And so this will then like kind of like start compounding drifting away from the original optimal trajectory. Because we don't have this IID assumption. Um So, the important thing to note here is that um in behavior cloning the the states visited by um the policy uh are not necessarily always the same ones um as the one uh visited by the expert. So,"}, {"t": 480, "text": "there is oftentimes a distribution shift between like the training data set uh that you uh train your policy on and then the test set where when once you deploy your robot. Um and you can imagine this um I mean this is I think very easily understandable. So, it could be tiny things like illumination changes or you know, if we collected, let's say like uh the self-driving car scenario, uh you know, we collected all our training data during daytime and then we drive during nighttime. Or it's like different weather conditions that somehow make the observations look different and then this will affect um the the performance of our policy and it will kind of uh drift away. Um Exactly. So, given everything we just discussed about distributional shift and compounding errors, the natural question is does behavior cloning actually work in the real world?"}, {"t": 540, "text": "And the answer is actually it it can work. It does work. Um here this is a um a video from uh 2016 from um Nvidia um uh for uh self-driving uh a self-driving policy um that, you know, they train a deep neural network to uh map raw pixels from a single front-facing camera directly to steering uh commands. And so, um So, in the beginning, I don't know if they showed us it it didn't work that well. Oh, look, no hands. Um in the beginning, it didn't like work amazing, but then they kind of collected more data uh and kind of got this behavior cloning algorithm to work. Um, and so this is I think one of this like first kind of um, like prototypes we saw from like end-to-end driving being deployed in in like a real world. Um, and um,"}, {"t": 603, "text": "so the question is like why did it actually work, right? Like we just said like okay, we have these issues with, you know, drift uh, for states not visited and for for cars this is like is very very difficult to generalize, right? Um, there is one one case one simple case here for for cars that I want to like uh, work work you through on uh, some tricks that, you know, um, have been developed to make to address this issue. So imagine that we have this car, right? Um, and you know, when we drive um, usually we we try to kind of stay in the center of the lane, right? Like we don't go like doing like crazy curves and drifts and so on, right? So most of our training data will be to just drive straight. Um, something like this, right? Now, with this um, drifting error, um, you know, it can be that for whatever reason"}, {"t": 660, "text": "we do like, you know, some prediction that doesn't go as well and you know, instead of going straight we kind of start drifting slowly towards the side. This is actually not um, this is quite dangerous for cars, right? Uh, um, but the the the problem is compounded by this this this effect that um, you know, this is a state we have never visited before because we all our training data consists of just driving straight. So when the policy like sees this it's like, oh wow, I like this is completely new to me. I have no clue what idea what to do here. Um and also collecting data for a human driver for a such a uh dangerous situation is difficult, right? Like you don't want to like you know crash your car into the side to you know learn how to uh what to do in in such a situation. Uh so that's exactly the problem, right? This is like very difficult state to have in your training data. Um and difficult um to uh to recover from this."}, {"t": 721, "text": "So to solve this um the the this uh Nvidia paper um they uh came up with a strategy that involved using three cameras instead of one. So a center camera or two side uh and two side cameras. Um and so they applied a very simple trick that essentially um they created this you know this they augmented this data set um with examples from the side cameras. And for the uh camera that's looking left um they uh labeled this always as you know steering right. And for the camera that's looking uh right they labeled it all the time as um steer uh left. Right? Um so kind of like creating these virtual actions. And um this essentially then tricks the model into learning a recovery policy without ever needing a human driver to actually crash the car during data collection. Uh so this is like quite quite um quite nice trick. Um"}, {"t": 780, "text": "uh to add this fake data to kind of like simulate uh self-recovery um behaviors. Now um this trick was actually also applied here in Zurich. Um I think I've I forgot to put the the paper name and the year, but it's around like the the same time I think I believe. Um and they applied it not for um, autonomous um, self-driving, but for quadcopter flying uh, through dense forests. Um, and uh, this is actually very cool, um, because it's kind of like a similar situation um, for that is difficult to collect data for flying cuz like we humans like don't naturally fly. Um, and so what they did here is um, do they show? So, uh, I think they'll show this in a bit. Um, what they what they did is they"}, {"t": 841, "text": "collected cameras, uh, sorry, they collected data by having humans uh, walk uh, through the forest with a camera rig around um, around the forehead, like essentially with three GoPros. And then they would like this way get this the same labels of like fly straight, uh, if the camera looks like to the right, um, then fly uh, to the left, um, and so forth. Um, I want to show the the camera setup. Are they showing this now? Yeah, here we go. So, they have this this kind of like three cameras. And here you can see what the the three cameras are are seeing. And then they uh, they add this this virtual labels to to learn then how to how to navigate uh,"}, {"t": 901, "text": "this terrain. So, with like go straight, uh, go right, and go left. Okay, uh, so we know that um, behavior cloning can be bad, right? Um, but how bad can we quantify it, how bad it can be? Um, and so uh, we can for this, um, kind of derive an formal upper bound uh, on its performance. So, suppose we have, um, uh, a time horizon T, um, the number of steps the robot needs to finish this task, uh, and there's some small probability epsilon that our learned, uh, policy pi theta, um, will make a mistake at any given time. Um, and so, in standard supervised learning, as we said before, um, you might expect this, uh, error to be linear, um, something like, uh, epsilon T, um, but if you are, um, uh, but this is not the case in behavioral cloning. And, uh,"}, {"t": 962, "text": "the, uh, the reason for this is that we can define this, uh, cost function, um, where the cost is zero if the action matches the expert, and it's one if it doesn't. And we assume now that, um, the the training states, on our training states, the probability of a mistake is bounded by epsilon. Um, however, um, once we, uh, make a mistake, we move into a state that we haven't seen before. Um, and the analogy here is kind of like this tightrope, uh, walker example, right? So, where, um, there is only a very specific, um, sequence of actions if you want to walk through this tightrope, and like if you drift and fall off, like, um, it's very difficult to recover. I mean, it's not like bad that, you know, uh, you die or like, like you you can have like safety or whatever, but like, like you don't have any demonstration data that shows you how to get back on the on the tightrope, right? Um, and so, um, the"}, {"t": 1020, "text": "probability of making, uh, mistakes for, you know, unseen states is unbounded. Um, yeah, so here's the the animation, uh, again. And, um, so as you can see here the um summary, uh if you make a mistake at step one, um you incur uh a cost of um for all T remaining steps. Um and so if you make your first mistake at step two, then you will um incur cost for T minus one steps. And if you aggregate all these probabilities, um the expected total error um doesn't grow linearly, but it actually grows quadratically. Uh which is very bad. So it's like uh ep- epsilon uh uh T squared. Um and so this doesn't necessarily mean that behavior cloning is is is bad uh per se, but we can kind of bound its its"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "performance, right? Um So we've mathematically kind of like seen that behavior cloning is um you know, like uh not linear, but quadratic quadratic um when it starts drifting uh from the distribution of the states um the expert showed. Um so the question is like how can we fix this? Um And so if the root of the problem is that the states visited by the expert differ from the states visited by the policy, um like uh our first kind of like solution can be to well, can't we make them kind of the same or similar? Can we somehow close this uh distribution uh uh shift? And so this brings us to dagger or data set aggregation, um which is exactly trying to do this. So the core idea is to make the expert and policy distribution converge by doing online rollouts uh during training. So the idea is pretty simple. Uh we first roll out the policy in the"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "real world, um see what states it visits, and then ask um a human to label those states, or or we ask uh uh experts or um what action, like what optimal action they would have taken in this, uh, state. And so, and we don't throw away the old data, we just simply add this new corrective, uh, state-action pairs to our existing data set, um, D, and, um, then we update our policy on this new augmented data set, and repeat the process iteratively. And it can be shown mathematically that, um, this will converge to the distribution of of state that the policy actually sees, um, breaking this this quadratic error growth, uh, earlier and bringing us to to a linear bound, um. So, this is great, um, but, um, one thing to note is that, um, query like querying human experts can be difficult"}, {"t": 1200, "text": "and costly um, in this process, um, like it can take a lot of time, and there also might be other issues like, you know, maybe the action space might be different, uh, from the robot and the human, and if you're like labeling this like in hindsight on the computer. Um, but in in principle, like paradoxically, this means that behavior cloning actually works better if the data, um, contains more mistakes and recoveries. Um, as I said, like while standard dagger is, um, quite expensive, uh, to label, uh, like if you have thousands of trajectories on hindsight, um, and you know, there might be discretization errors and so on, uh, this, uh, we can move to a different version, which we call a human-gated dagger, or it can also be called online intervention, where instead of, um, the robot collecting data and then human labeling later, we can let the robot run under"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "human supervision, like online. So, essentially, this means that when the robot starts making a mistake, uh, a human in the loop can take over and provide a partial demonstration for the remainder of the task. And this is kind of like much more natural and effective as it lets humans take control um only when it's necessary, right? Instead of labeling like the whole thing. Um and it's kind of like equivalent to, you know, if you're trying to learn how to drive a car, uh you know, with your instructor by your side, um then the instructor will like, you know, like take over or grab like the the steering wheel only like when you're about to crash into the curve, right? It's not going to like drive all the time. Um however, this introduces like a new challenge, right? How do you know when an intervention is needed? Like this is like a subtle but, you know, still kind of like difficult question, um especially if you don't want like a human constantly uh supervising the"}, {"t": 1322, "text": "robot and monitoring to decide when to jump in, right? Cuz this is also um kind of uh costly. So, can we move beyond human supervision supervisors uh super supervisors? Um so, in order for the robot to learn uh to recognize its own uncertainty. Um so, can we have the robot kind of ask the human before it crashes? Um here I want to show one possible example for how to automate such intervention uh detectors and this is kind of a uh older work from my PhD. Uh here concretely uh we looked at ambiguities in natural language instruction. So, let's say um like a human asks the robot to fetch a yellow thing for the table, um but there are multiple yellow objects um on the table. In such a scenario, ideally the robot should uh have the ability to detect"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "that um this uh instruction is ambiguous uh and that we need to query the human back for disambiguation. Um so in order to detect if an instruction is ambiguous, we um I won't go too much into details, but we leverage like essentially a max-margin ranking loss um that we trained our comprehension model with. Um so like with pairs of objects and language uh descriptions. Uh and so concretely during the training this loss um aims to guarantee that every correct pairs of sentences and objects has scores um uh by a um uh kind of um um that has scores by a margin um M1 that uh is uh smaller than any other uh pair with a wrong combination of object objects and sentences. And um so this means that at test time,"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "if you have more um objects within the threshold, you can consider them potential targets. And so then like be- beyond our comprehension model, you can also have a generative uh model or a head that is able to um caption and generate referential expressions for these different candidates. Um so that um once we detect um that they are like this number of uh potential like yellow objects um that could be applied to, we uh generate uh referential expressions for each of them and ask the the human about it. &gt;&gt; Robot, fetch the red thing. &gt;&gt; And this it looks something like this. &gt;&gt; Fetching. What else do you want? &gt;&gt; So we asked fetch the red thing. &gt;&gt; Robot, place it behind the colored dish. &gt;&gt; Understood. Placing. What else do you want?"}, {"t": 1504, "text": "&gt;&gt; Robot. &gt;&gt; No, this is the the example with fetch the yellow stuff. &gt;&gt; sure. Do you mean the middle banana? &gt;&gt; And there are like three different uh yellow objects and then we generate these kind of captions and then we can ask uh the the user and then it says yes, like it's the banana that we meant. Uh and then, you know, you can keep uh tidying up your kitchen. &gt;&gt; top of the bottom object. &gt;&gt; All right. So, we've covered um distribution shifts, but there are a few more reasons um that it would be good uh to know why behavior cloning might fail. Uh and one of the reasons um is uh non-Markovian behaviors. Uh so, for the policy uh training on the left, we kind of um said in the last lecture that like the actions have to um be conditioned only on the current state. Um uh or in in in essence on the current"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "observation in in in practice, right? But uh the thing is that humans don't necessarily uh choose the actions uh based on the current state always. Like we don't typically behave like like this. Um so, if we see things twice, we might not um repeat like the same action. Um and this might be due to due different reasons. So, uh one of them is that we, for example, rely on context or history um before we, you know, uh make a decision or take an action. Um it can also be um you know, our emotional state um can also affect on us showing uh different behaviors, uh intent, or things we have learned from the past, right? So, um if you're like a human driver um and you see a cyclist, um you know, like you don't you make some inference, you know, not just by, you know, looking at one image or a snapshot, but it usually like"}, {"t": 1622, "text": "it's based on the fact that you track, you know, like oh, how is like the the cyclist moving in the last 5 seconds, and so you kind of predict how it's going to move, and then this affects like how like you're going to like act as a driver, right? And so if if as a robot you only see like one single image of the cyclist, like the actions that you might choose to predict will be different than if you see how the cyclist is uh um uh uh moving through the last 5 seconds. Um uh and I will talk about this in a second. Maybe also another issue is that sometimes humans have privileged information. This is very clearly demonstrated when you teleoperate robots, right? So, here you can see me teleoperating a robot during my PhD, and as you can see like I'm directly like seeing what the robot is doing, right?"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "But now, you know, what the policy is actually seeing are these camera observations, right? And so, it's very different because, you know, with this camera observations, there are like much more occlusions, for example, that might affect like how you behave than, you know, like the like the human here who can also like move around and see more, and, you know, this will then lead to different decisions than if you teleoperate just looking at the same observations that the uh policy is seeing, right? So, we we also use like the last n frames, maybe, when we are teleoperating this way. Um so, as a forementioned, um uh the if the demonstrations are non-Markovian due to history like this is bad, right? Because, um, when the robot sees the exact same visual state, uh, twice, um, but sees the expert take"}, {"t": 1742, "text": "two different actions, uh, it lacks the historic history context or, um, to understand why. Um, our first kind of idea how to fix this would be, well, let's just add history to our policy, right? It's like very simple. Instead of just taking one frame of the current time step, we can just, uh, take the last, whatever, five or 10 frames and then, you know, train our policy exactly the same way, uh, using our favorite sequence-to-sequence model, right? Like you can take a LSTM, RNN, transformer, whatever, process the last n frames, and then train the same thing with behavior cloning. And so, although this seems like a great idea, um, besides making your model, you know, a bit more computationally expensive, um, adding history, um, to your policy doesn't always make things better. Uh, in fact, it can sometimes, um, make things much worse. Um, because this brings us to causal confusion."}, {"t": 1800, "text": "Um, this is a kind of a very, um, known problem for, uh, um, doing imitation learning with history. Um, the issue is here that when we give, um, a high-capacity, uh, model access to entire history, uh, of observations, um, sensors, and actions, um, they typically have a tendency to infer spurious correlations. So, because it's essentially looking for, um, the easiest way to minimize the loss, um, uh, it always does it doesn't always identify the the the right causal path for it. Um, so, imagine that, uh, for our demonstrations, like every time, uh, the robot opens a drawer, um, the gripper sensor records a spike of exactly, um, 10 Newtons as it grasps the handle. Um, the model will look at this and will think like, \"Aha, I don't need to look at the visual state, uh, of the drawer or the arm's position, uh, because if the I see that if the gripper force history shows, uh, this 10 Newton spike,"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "then I will trigger the pull action.\" Um, and now if you deploy this on the real robot, um, you know, maybe your gripper slips slightly, um, or, you know, it's a bit dusty and, uh, let's say the sensor only reads like 2 Newtons. Um, then the model will be confused because it thinks that, um, the the sensor value causes the opening action rather than the visual state of the handle being, uh, grasped. Um, so that the pull command will like never be triggered. Um, and so, um, this is like a very, kind of, uh, uh, this is like one of the challenges when you train, uh, policies with history, like you have to regular do a lot of regularization and look that they actually, uh, still pay attention, um, to the right, uh, uh, visual states and so on, um, uh, to learn the the causality. Okay, uh, let's move on to another, uh, reason why simple imitation learning can fail and this is, um,"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "what we will be talking about for the remainder of of the lecture mostly, um, because, uh, um, even if we have like perfect data, uh, kind of a big problem is, uh, multi-modal behavior in the data. Um, so, uh, this means that imagine that, you know, we are we want to like, uh, close this drawer, um, as we discussed in the first lecture, first of all, you know, the if you have like a redundancy a redundancy in your robot, uh, joints there are like multiple ways how you could be doing, um, kind of the same pose with the end effector, um, and on the other on the on the other side for the task itself, there are also um, very uh, infinite number of ways um, how you can reach the handle and and push it in, right? Um, and because there are like infinite numbers of ways how you can think about this, right? you can do like a side grasp, you can like actually grasp the handle or don't grasp it. Uh, you can do like a top down or you can do like, you know, 90° and"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "everything in between that you can think of, right? Or you can just like push it with the elbow. Um, so humans when you ask them to record this data will naturally like do all this to show all these different behaviors. Um, is is that clear? Does Do you understand? Yeah? Um, and so um, so we have this kind of stochasticity and also expert inconsistency, right? So there are like multiple ways to achieve the same goal. Um, and all are valid in principle cuz they all um, achieve the same like uh, success. Now, why is this a problem for behavioral cloning? Um, so if you're using our standard loss function that we introduced in the first algorithm uh, with um, mean squared error, the the math essentially forces the model to find the average of all the demonstrations, right? Um, so if you have like these two tracks, you know, like you're snowboarding or something, um, and um, you know, your expert shows you one mode where, you know, they go left around this tree"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "uh, and then um, another one shows you how to go right or across this around this tree um, and then the average of those will, you know, be like driving uh, straight into the tree. Um, and so uh, this will be a kind of a problem. So, what can we do about this? Um, so if the if standard regression um, to single point fails because um, the average uh, is inconsistent uh, with the expert data, we need a policy that can represent multiple uh, valid peaks of action or modes. Um so, kind of one way or our first idea how we can uh solve this is um you know, for example, using uh something like a mixture of Gaussian distributions. Um so, you represent this with a set of uh means, covariances, and weights. Uh and the nice thing is that um you can model multiple Gaussians or essentially any distribution that you could model with a mixture of Gaussians. Um one thing to note here is that you"}, {"t": 2100, "text": "typically need to predetermine um how many modes uh you want. Let's say there are 10. Um but, you know, if you want to like control like a humanoid, you know, maybe you want a thousand modes. Uh and you know, then things start to get quite tricky. Um what if we want to model uh uh what if we want to represent any dis- arbitrary complex distribution without this constraint? Um so, one powerful way to do this is through discretization. So, instead of predicting continuous values for an action, we can kind of turn the action space into grids uh of bins and then treat this as a classification problem. And so, discretization is a good way to uh represent multimodal distributions, uh such as, for example, for the drone flying case or the the the car. Um but, it's kind of difficult to um apply naively for higher dimensional spaces because the number you need um increases exponentially with higher dimensions. Um so, one um solution is um"}, {"t": 2163, "text": "uh doing uh autoregressive per-dimension discretization, uh which is then uh linear in the number of dimensions, uh and um you can then use kind of a a sequence model like a like a autoregressive transformer to deco- decompose this. Uh and this is essentially how, let's say, the first VLAs were were doing this. Um uh let's do the questions later um so we can get on time. Um, and, um, how this works is that then you autoregressively predict, um, every action dimension. And, um, with the with the chain rule then you can see that, uh, this ends up, you know, um, predicting, um, kind of the probability of the action, um, given the the state. Um, so the, uh, so this is very nice, but, um, one thing to consider here is that, you know, the these will this will kind of force you, uh, to use large"}, {"t": 2221, "text": "autoregressive models like LLMs or VLMs, um, to do that as a backbone. And, you know, this might be a bit, uh, more expensive computationally. Um, exactly. So, you will like kind of uh, sequentially go go and predict like each individual action dimension. So, if it's like your 7 degrees of freedom, uh, manipulator, then, you know, you will predict predict each, uh, dimension separately. Um, another popular way to handle multimodality comes from generative modeling, um, specially diffusion. Um, you've likely seen these in the context of generative art where a model turns, um, random noise into high-fidelity images. Um, uh, we will talk more in detail about this, uh, later in in week six, but this is kind of like a very, you know, like 1-minute version, uh, of how you can think about this. Um, so, um, the the process is twofold. Um, like you have, uh, the the forward process where"}, {"t": 2280, "text": "you can take a clean sample, um, like this, uh, image of a cat, and then, uh, you iteratively add Gaussian noise until all information is kind of lost and you're, uh, left with, uh, static noise. And then you have the generative backward process where, um, you train a neural network to denoise that, uh, step by step, uh, and learn how to recover, uh, of data from, uh, noise. And, um, so essentially, you, you know, you create a fake training set, um, uh, by creating images with various levels of noise, um, and then train this this denoising network to, uh, kind of predict, uh, cleaner versions of the image. Um, maybe one thing to note is that, um, actually, instead of predicting the full image, uh, during denoising, we actually predict the noise itself, and then subtractive subtractive from the current, um, sample to iteratively recover the the the, uh, clean signal. And so, both autoregressive discretization and, uh, diffusion use"}, {"t": 2340, "text": "sort of a iterative process where, um, where one is iterative over the action dimensions, and the other over the denoising process. Okay, and, um, kind of the last toolkit, um, to handle multimodality are latent variable models, uh, which can represent any distribution in principle, as long as the network is big enough. Um, in latent variable models, the, um, output of the network is still a Gaussian, but in addition to the image, it receives an additional input, uh, that is sampled from a prior, uh, distribution, um, let's say, like a zero mean, uh, variant Gaussian. Um, so this is kind of like a seed for the network. Um, um, and it will, um, kind of result in, um, the network predicting different modes, or different behaviors. So, obviously, you cannot just like input random numbers, uh, to get the target behavior. Um, so we need to train the model to kind of relate this input latent variables, uh, with the desired"}, {"t": 2400, "text": "mode, right? And so, you can think of this as, um, you know, uh, we could have these two modes, like like, uh, going left through the uh, through the tree, and and right, and then we kind of like condition our decoder on this latent to to kind of tell it like, oh, it's left or it's it's right, right? Um Um now, I want to address a common point of confusion, um the difference between the latent intent and the task specification. Um so, task conditioning is kind of this supervised signal where you tell the robot which specific behavior to execute like, um opening a drawer, uh and so it kind of defines the goal, um and the latent variable tells kind of the style or mode within that goal that you want it to represent. Um so, even for a fixed task ID, um the robot might still face infinite ways how to achieve it, right? So, as we just discussed with the uh drawer opening example."}, {"t": 2461, "text": "Okay. Uh So, imagine that now, you know, like we actually want to learn multiple tasks with our policy, right? And imagine that uh the task is to uh move the sliding door to the left, right? So, imagine this is our task ID, uh and we we are like modeling this distribution. Um So, one thing to note here uh is that in the real world, it's kind of difficult to define task success, right? So, in this example, um the robot moves the sliding door I don't know, around halfway, right? So, what does this tell us? Like, did it succeed or not? Like, if we make if we do a poll, like probably like some of you will say like, yeah, I think this is good enough. Others will say like, no, it should be like closer all the way, right? And this is like kind of an inherent problem,"}, {"t": 2521, "text": "um because there is a tendency to think that the behaviors we want for our agents are a set of discrete tasks, you know, like moving the sliding door, opening a door, uh uh slide sliding with uh door. Um but it's difficult to draw a boundary where a task begins and ends because uh you know, they're typically made up of smaller subtasks and skills and the world is like not discrete, but it's continuous, right? And so, you know, in principle, we would want our robots to learn the full spectrum of skills that are available in the uh environment. And so, this motivates kind of having goal-reaching as an objective where um goal-reaching we define a policy that ends up reaching any desirable goal state from any other initial state on the scene. Uh so, you can see here kind of the difference between trying to model like a single task uh to model like, you know, with a task ID like a one-hot encoding or like a"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "natural language could also be uh or having like goal-reaching. So, conditioning on two states, an initial state and a goal state. Um the nice thing about goal-reaching is that um the task because the tasks are continuous, the states are also continuous. Okay, so let's put everything together. Um I will kind of show a case study of how we can combine a latent variable model that handles multimodality with kind of trying to learn like task-agnostic control. Um so, one way to do so is using kind of undirected unstructured play data where users teleoperate a robot without doing any specific task. Um and so, you kind of like give them like, you know, let the teleop and they collect, you know, data. Um Um but the it's like very very multimodal. So, it's reset free. You don't have to like, you know, reset after opening the drawer. Um but also um"}, {"t": 2640, "text": "yeah, it's it's kind of like very scalable data because um uh essentially an operator engages in behavior that, uh, satisfies their own curiosity, um, which, if you think about it, is not unlike how, uh, children start learning. Right? Like, they're also kind of exploring and playing with the environment. So, how can we, uh, now use this latent variable models with goal conditioning to learn from this data? Um, so, we start by sampling a random window of play data, such as this one. So, this will be like one sequence. And then we encode the last frame of the sampled window through a goal encoder, uh, as we will use goal relabeling to learn control. So, this means that we sample random windows of, uh, frames and then ask the model to predict a series of actions associated, uh, or taken during that sequence to learn how to mimic that, how to reach, uh, um, that goal."}, {"t": 2700, "text": "Okay. Now, the trick here is that we address this inherent multimodality in the sort of free-form imitation data set, um, meaning that, you know, there might be many, many valid observations that, uh, connect the same initial and goal state by autoencoding contextual demonstrations through this kind of latent plan space, uh, with a sequence-to-sequence, uh, varia- conditional variational autoencoder. So, we have here this dis- posterior and, um, and this prior. And so, the posterior will take this sampled sequence, um, recognizing the exact behavior, uh, executed, and then the the prior or the the plan proposal or sampler, uh, will take the initial and and goal state, outputting a distribution over all possible, uh, behaviors that connect the initial to the goal state. And then we minimize the KL divergence between these two encoders, making the prior assign high likelihood to behavior that were actually executed during play. And then at test time, we will, uh, discard"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "the posterior and use this plan sampler to predict multimodal plans. And so, the trick here is that by feeding this short horizon goal image demonstrations into a simple maximum likelihood imitation objective, we can now learn kind of task-agnostic control in a fully self-supervised manner uh from this multimodal uh play data. And critically, um conditioning the policy or the decoder on the uh latent plan frees up the capacity um uh of the uh decoder um to learn uh unimodal behavior um cuz you are like this is this kind of like latent uh variable. Um maybe one last note, um can we do this also for language goals? Um cuz like for now, this was kind of using the the the goal images, right? From the feed um from the demonstrations. Uh we can also do this the same efficiently for language. Um the key insight here is that uh we can"}, {"t": 2821, "text": "kind of uh inter- interchange um uh visual goals with uh embeddings of uh natural language goals, um allowing to learn control mostly self-supervisedly um from unlabeled visual play data and reduce the burden of uh language annotations. Um I think I'm a little bit out of time to go into a greater detail of the math uh behind this. But um kind of um on a very high level, um in order to train this, uh the the thing is that um we would want to maximize the the marginal likelihood of our expert data, but there is kind of like one issue um because um our Z uh latent variable is continuous, um the marginalization becomes intractable. Um and so, we You just integrate over an infinite number of possible plans. And so, in order to"}, {"t": 2881, "text": "solve this issue, what you typically do is you use um a standard variational inference trick, where in instead of optimizing the likelihood directly, you optimize a surrogate objective, which is the variational lower bound. Um and so, you have like these two uh terms, um the reconstruction One of them uh tells you how well does the latent plan explain the expert behavior. And then the other one is the regularization uh that tells you how close is is the one is the uh is the Z to the prior distribution over your trajectories. Um And so, like when you then introduce goal conditioning, if you have like this context kind of variable containing SC and SG, um this then nicely aligns with these like three components we saw on this architecture"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "with the posterior, prior, and the action decoder. And and the nice thing about this this this this problem is that we have factorized this problem um in into this latent plans that frees up the capacity of the decoder for a learning only unimodal behavior. Um Side note, a lot of my PhD work also was using uh like learning from play data and using kind of this latent variable models. So, if you're interested more in this, um you can look at some of these papers. And to conclude today's lecture, we have talked about imitation learning um in its most simple form. We have explained how we can make the the visited by the expert and the states visited by the policy become closer with dagger and online interventions. And"}, {"t": 3002, "text": "then we have addressed some common pitfalls in imitation learning such as non-Markovian behaviors from the demonstrators. And we talked also about some possible things that you can do to alleviate them and also multimodal behavior. And a few like solutions or tools how you can try to alleviate them. With this thank you for your attention and yeah. Let's go with the papers."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Slide 1: Feedback -> Actions", "slide": "slide-1-feedback---actions-frame-2"}, {"t": 121, "title": "Slide 2: Recap: Mapping Observations to Actions", "slide": "slide-2-recap-mapping-observations-to-actions-frame-4"}, {"t": 121, "title": "Slide 3: Imitation Learning", "slide": "slide-3-imitation-learning-frame-5"}, {"t": 182, "title": "Slide 4: Behavioral Cloning", "slide": "slide-4-behavioral-cloning-frame-6"}, {"t": 301, "title": "Slide 10 [shown early] : Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "slide": "slide-10-shown-early-learning-to-fly-in-swiss-forests-frame-7"}, {"t": 360, "title": "Slide 5: What can go wrong?", "slide": "slide-5-what-can-go-wrong-frames-8-10"}, {"t": 540, "title": "Slide 6: Does it work?", "slide": "slide-6-does-it-work-frames-11-13"}, {"t": 540, "title": "Slide 8: Why did it work?", "slide": "slide-8-why-did-it-work-frame-14"}, {"t": 721, "title": "Slide 9: Why did it work? Data Augmentation!", "slide": "slide-9-why-did-it-work-data-augmentation-frame-15"}, {"t": 780, "title": "Slide 10: Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "slide": "slide-10-learning-to-fly-in-swiss-forests-frames-16-22"}, {"t": 901, "title": "Slide 11: Upper Bound of Behavioral Cloning", "slide": "slide-11-upper-bound-of-behavioral-cloning-frames-23-24"}, {"t": 1020, "title": "Slide 12: Addressing Compounding Error", "slide": "slide-12-addressing-compounding-error-frame-25"}, {"t": 1141, "title": "Slide 13: Aggregating Corrective Behavior", "slide": "slide-13-aggregating-corrective-behavior-frame-26"}, {"t": 1200, "title": "Slide 14: Aggregating Online Interventions", "slide": "slide-14-aggregating-online-interventions-frame-27"}, {"t": 1322, "title": "Slide 15: Case Study: Language Ambiguities", "slide": "slide-15-case-study-language-ambiguities-frame-28"}, {"t": 1322, "title": "Slide 16: Case Study: Language Ambiguities [method]", "slide": "slide-16-case-study-language-ambiguities-method-frames-29-31"}, {"t": 1322, "title": "Slide 17: Case Study: Language Ambiguities [demo]", "slide": "slide-17-case-study-language-ambiguities-demo-frames-32-38"}, {"t": 1504, "title": "Slide 18: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert?", "slide": "slide-18-why-might-we-still-fail-to-mimick-the-expert-frame-39"}, {"t": 1681, "title": "Slide 20: Adding History to Policy", "slide": "slide-20-adding-history-to-policy-frame-40"}, {"t": 1800, "title": "Slide 21: Causal Confusion", "slide": "slide-21-causal-confusion-frames-41-42"}, {"t": 1921, "title": "Slide 22: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert? [multimodality]", "slide": "slide-22-why-might-we-still-fail-to-mimick-the-expert-multimodality-frame-43"}, {"t": 2040, "title": "Slide 23: Multimodal Behavior", "slide": "slide-23-multimodal-behavior-frame-44"}, {"t": 2040, "title": "Slide 24: Mixture of Gaussian Distributions", "slide": "slide-24-mixture-of-gaussian-distributions-frame-45"}, {"t": 2100, "title": "Slide 25: Autoregressive Discretization", "slide": "slide-25-autoregressive-discretization-frame-46"}, {"t": 2221, "title": "Slide 26: Diffusion", "slide": "slide-26-diffusion-frame-47"}, {"t": 2340, "title": "Slide 27: Latent Variable Models", "slide": "slide-27-latent-variable-models-frame-48"}, {"t": 2400, "title": "Slide 28: Learning Multiple Tasks", "slide": "slide-28-learning-multiple-tasks-frame-49"}, {"t": 2461, "title": "Slide 29: Learning Multiple Tasks [success?]", "slide": "slide-29-learning-multiple-tasks-success-frames-50-51"}, {"t": 2521, "title": "Slide 30: How to Scale Control to “Any” Tasks?", "slide": "slide-30-how-to-scale-control-to-any-tasks-frame-52"}, {"t": 2521, "title": "Slide 31: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-31-untitled-transition-question-frame-53"}, {"t": 2580, "title": "Slide 32: Case Study: Play Data", "slide": "slide-32-case-study-play-data-frame-54"}, {"t": 2580, "title": "Slide 34: Case Study: Learning Policies Play Data", "slide": "slide-34-case-study-learning-policies-play-data-frames-55-56"}, {"t": 2640, "title": "Slide 36: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [plan prior and posterior]", "slide": "slide-36-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-plan-prior-and-posterior-frame-57"}, {"t": 2760, "title": "Slide 37: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [action decoder]", "slide": "slide-37-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-action-decoder-frame-58"}, {"t": 2760, "title": "Slide 38: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [language goals]", "slide": "slide-38-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-language-goals-frame-59"}, {"t": 2821, "title": "Slide 39: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models", "slide": "slide-39-the-math-behind-goal-conditioned-latent-variable-models-frame-60"}, {"t": 2881, "title": "Slide 40: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models [conditioned]", "slide": "slide-40-the-math-behind-goal-conditioned-latent-variable-models-conditioned-frame-61"}, {"t": 2941, "title": "Slide 41: My PhD: Natural Language + Learning from Play", "slide": "slide-41-my-phd-natural-language-learning-from-play-frame-62"}, {"t": 2941, "title": "Slide 42: Conclusion: Behavioral Cloning Failures", "slide": "slide-42-conclusion-behavioral-cloning-failures-frame-63"}], "toc_source": "estimated from slides (approx.)"}, "guests": [{"id": "qvTP6T5oq1w", "title": "Danfei Xu: Human Data as a Foundation for Robot Learning [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Danfei Xu", "duration": "36:48", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Prof. Danfei Xu (Georgia Tech & NVIDIA) explores the pivotal role of human data in developing robust robotic systems.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 53, "title": "The triangle of learning"}, {"t": 146, "title": "Limitations of teleoperation"}, {"t": 334, "title": "Leveraging human experience"}, {"t": 425, "title": "Egomimic scaling project"}, {"t": 640, "title": "Evaluating egomimic"}, {"t": 761, "title": "Zero-shot transfer challenges"}, {"t": 930, "title": "Egobridge for alignment"}, {"t": 1130, "title": "Mobile robot manipulation"}, {"t": 1325, "title": "Future research directions"}, {"t": 1747, "title": "The egoverse dataset"}, {"t": 1820, "title": "Q&A session"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "the robot learning and reasoning lab um uh where he advances robotics by combining machine learning, uh computer vision, and planning in order to build adaptable general-purpose robots. Uh he is also a researcher at Nvidia AI and earned his PhD in computer science from Stanford University uh with uh Fei-Fei Li and Silvio Savarese. And uh Dan Fei's work has earned significant recognition including uh a National Science Foundation Career Award and uh his research portfolio includes award-winning publications and innovations in imitation learning, long-horizon task reasoning, and uh robot learning systems. And with that uh we're very excited to hear uh about your work, Dan Fei. Take it away. All right. Thanks for your kind introduction. Hi everyone. Uh cool. All right. So um I know I think the lecture today is about imitation learning, right? I I was looking through"}, {"t": 60, "text": "the syllabus. Is that right? Oh, yeah. What's that? &gt;&gt; Could you confirm? Uh is the uh lecture to Was the lecture today about imitation learning? &gt;&gt; learning. Yes. Awesome. All right. So, that's a fitting topic. So Okay. So, but it's more a kind of contra- uh I want to say contrarian view, but more a kind of alternative view for imitation learning. So, hopefully um uh that connects to what you you've seen today. Um so, uh specifically the talk I'm going to give today is really examining a very simple hypothesis that we can scale robot capability uh with human experience data. And uh most robot learning today uh kind of still depends mostly on teleoperated demonstration data. And in contrast, uh LLMs and VLMs are successful because they can learn from naturally occurring human data crawled from the internet. So, the question is, can we scale robot uh can robot learning scale the same way from natural human behaviors?"}, {"t": 121, "text": "So, to think about this, let me start with a more conceptual view of robot learning. So, my view is that modern robot learning has three core couple components, data source, the learning algorithms and models, and the physical system. You cannot change one in in isolation. Progress really comes from co-designing all three of them all together. So, I call this the triangle of robot learning. And from this perspective, we can now kind of track a recent trend shift in the field, which is a growing focus on changing the data source, rather than only improving the models. The most dominant trend today is perhaps learning from teleoperated demonstrations. And we we can at the same time examining their their algorithms and hardware systems. So, just by manually, so for those of you who are not kind of have not done this kind of research before, just kind"}, {"t": 181, "text": "of giving some examples where just by manually control the robot with some devices like VR controllers, you can collect expert demonstration to train robot policies and to end from pixel fractions. And we've seen this both success in academia and industry, a lot of really impressive demos for solving tasks we thought were previously not possible, like such as doing laundry and making pizzas. So, we should have already covered this kind of topic today, but I'll give you a just more practical sense of how we actually tell our collective teleperson data and train policy model. I'm just giving example with an work I did with J and I did back in 2020. So, when human operate a robot using control device such as like a smartphone here, we capture two streams of data, what a robot senses and how it should act. This gives you the really the ground truth control signals for a robot given observation input. And importantly is that here is that we're not just"}, {"t": 240, "text": "kind of transferring the actual knowledge by perceiving and acting through the robot's body, we're training a model, a policy model, uh, as sort of by kind of transferring this kind of embodied sensorimotor knowledge that is not really existing, uh, in the today's internet data. So, that that's really kind of not really natural or the occurring human knowledge, but more more of a kind of deliberate demonstrative data. And when we when we revisit the triangle, collaboration creates really a tight coupling among all these three components. Data is generated only when humans act through a specific robot, and algorithm then learn from that robot specific data, and the resulting policies only work on this the same hardware configuration that you collect data on. And this this really prevents the system from scale, uh, to to collect more data, you need more robots, and to change the embodiments, you really need to recollect the data on that target robot. So, this leads to a really tight"}, {"t": 301, "text": "bottleneck, uh, or a very tight knowledge transfer bottleneck. Humans have enormous sensorimotor knowledge about physical world, but we only capture the knowledge when the day we deliberately act through robots, and this knowledge is really like, uh, kind of waste of human knowledge, right? We're we're we're kind of throwing away most of our natural, uh, knowledge about how we interact with this world. So, the question is, can we transfer the sensorimotor knowledge from humans to robots at scale similar to how it's happening in vision and natural language processing? And our proposal is to break this bottleneck by removing the data from the data collection data collection loop. So, instead of recording humans acting through robots, uh, we learn from humans acting acting naturally in the world. This kind of data contains rich sensory experience, can be collected with at much larger scale with our robots, and it really doesn't really uh require any intentional demonstrations. So, uh and so, the question then becomes"}, {"t": 360, "text": "whether we can turn this human experience into useful supervisions for robot policies. So, as I mentioned before, we cannot change one thing uh with this robot experience as a data source. We have to rethink how to capture and learn from the data, as well as the rest of the robot hardware systems. And this brings us to our first research milestone, uh which uh not sure uh have you have seen some of you have seen it. It's called Ego Mimics, Scaling Imitation Learning via Egocentric Video. Uh the core of this project is to capture embodied human data with an egocentric view with wearable devices, uh which means that we can collect this data anywhere. Uh unlike third-person views, egocentric data really capture how we humans perceive the world at an eye level. And specifically, we use the Project Aura glasses, which is kind of a pair of uh smart glasses, uh to capture this data. Uh it kind of gives you the essential sensory experiences I'm going to talk about a little later. And our work really is about turning these raw data stream into embodied"}, {"t": 422, "text": "human data that can be treated uh as equals with robot data. So, uh we So, we no longer treat kind of human data as human data, but more of human as kind of different different kind of robot. We have input and output. We just find to capture it and put it alongside the robot data to to train them up. And in order to do this, we have to bridge sensing, action, and kinematic gaps, and develop really this joint policy learning architecture that scales primarily with human data. Um so, let's first uh talk about how to align embodiments and viewpoints between human robots. Uh so, for embodiments, it's not really like a challenge uh as of 2026, but back then when we started this project in 2023, there weren't many kind of robots human-like robots that kind of mimics the embodiments of humans. So, we actually had to kind of think about how to design a lightweight and lean robots that are human-like. So, we kind of built our own robots with two six-arm and some of"}, {"t": 482, "text": "these devices. But one interesting thing is that we actually mimic the viewpoint of humans and I would actually even use the exact same sensing device as humans, which is this pair of eye glasses. So, we mount our glasses on the robot so they have a very similar camera hardware specification and viewpoint so that they can kind of minimize the inter-device inter-embodiment gaps. Among the many problems we had to solve to learn from the egocentric data, I want to kind of highlight one of very interesting one, which is the viewpoint shifts. When we work with robot like ours, the robot's viewpoint moves relative to like a fixed reference frame. Right? You can easily get action trajectories in the fixed kind of reference space frame. But with egocentric human data, like the ones I just showed you on the left, the chef cooking preparing dinner, we kind of our head naturally moves, we look around, check workspaces, focus on different tasks. This means that a hand"}, {"t": 540, "text": "position tracked relative to a moving reference frame. We need to really stabilize this reference frame to make human hand motion like usable as robot robot supervision action supervision for robot learning. And luckily, we were able to do this because Arya already gave us like a really really good slam system that can sort of track the head motion relative to hand or our hand relative to the head more of so it would actually turn us into a stable reference frame. So, in the first row, we have these kind of moving reference frame where head is everywhere, head is everywhere, but now we can actually transform this uh fixed world coordinate frame from SLAM map to transform all the trajectories to a fixed base frame. To This allows us to create these kind of very stable trajectory uh relative to a camera frame. Um so, with all of this kind of input output aligned, uh we actually were able to design a very simple uh kind of deep"}, {"t": 601, "text": "behavior cloning system. Uh you probably have already seen this in the previous lecture. So, it's really a supervised learning problem where you have input uh observations, let's say sensing and pre-perception, and output actions. And we basically put human pose human robot uh data together, did some normalization that, if you're interested in, you should read the paper. Uh but uh this is really kind of how we put together the system. And our hypothesis is that if you just train this policy with more human data, the robot performance would increase with just that same amount of robot data. So, uh the hypothesis is that we want to drive the robot performance uh primarily with human data. And we test this uh this kind of policy on a variety of different tasks, like a bagging grocery tasks, uh where like kind of uh you have to lift up bag handle and put all of these kind of groceries in in a bag. Uh and you can see that it kind of is a closed-loop policy that kind of corrects the the mistakes."}, {"t": 660, "text": "And folding clothes. And uh also making pour-over coffees uh for for our graduate students. I'll just let this this uh video finish playing. Um Um yeah. It's a very hard task. Succeeds once in a while. Um the but the true question is that does Ego motion scale with human data? And to check this, we actually did this kind of tiny scaling law uh experiments, where x-axis is hour data you collect either on human robots and y-axis is a score of performance. And you can see that with only a robot data, this blue line, you can see this kind of mild scaling curve. With more teleop data, a teleop robot data, the performance goes gets better. But if at at sort of once you have a certain amount of robot data, if you just add this human data into the training mix, you can dramatically see this dramatic jump in a"}, {"t": 720, "text": "robot performance. And this basically validates our hypothesis that human data is a viable way to drive up the robot performance. And one of the reasons is that we humans actually collect kind of demonstrate a lot faster than teleoperation. So some for some some tasks up to 10 times faster than teleoperation. And remember that also the nice thing is that we don't need a robot in the loop, so you can just provide us demonstration anywhere. Um so we also moved this robot to kind of variety part of campus to show the robustness of this. This these videos are on on the website, so if you're interested you can watch them. Uh so in interest of time I'm going to kind of skip and talk about sort of like what what kind of try robot learning framework has built, which is that we now have this human data source as the environment as the data source human experience as data source and we had to change our physical robot environment and also algorithms. But I can I can imagine that just"}, {"t": 781, "text": "co-training human robot data is not the most sophisticated algorithm out there. It's essentially still just behavior cloning. So the question is can we do better? And before that we have to check what's missing, right? So what we've seen in Google makes that it scales. So it scales with the robot robot policy performance with human data. What is missing really is that those tasks that human demonstrator has to exist in a robot data. So, in so to say, we have to pair collect this kind of paired and distributed virtual data from human robots in order for a robot to kind of improve its performance. Um and the dream of our our our kind of vision really is that we want to have this kind of zero-shot transfer, which is that we want a robot to be able to do something that only exists in human data. So, uh and clearly this co-training objective like other algorithms uh still cannot achieve that. So, what's missing?"}, {"t": 840, "text": "Um so, what what So, what is actually what we are hypothesizing, which is like ideal picture of like what how how should robot can do the zero-shot transfer, is to learn this kind of generalizable domain invariant latent space such that, let's say, you have human data of folding shirts and robot data of folding shirts, which are blue and orange dots, and you map them into this latent space, and your policy model basically decodes this into an action. And when a new batch of data arrives, folding shirts and folding shorts, which doesn't exist in robot data, uh but only in human data, uh your encoder in this domain invariant latent space should ideally map this to the same space so that the policy can still generate reasonable actions for this new task while you don't really have any robot data for. So, that's our ideal picture. But what happens in practice, as discovered by our student lead Ryan, is that it's actually not learning this"}, {"t": 901, "text": "invariant space. They are like pretty separate. Human robot data are are kind of they're very isolated. They're two two really just two clusters. So, the question uh Well, there are many reasons other ways that we can kind of uh enable this zero-shot transfer, but our specific hypothesis here really is that maybe if we can pull this these distribution together to form this late latent space through some learning algorithms, we could actually enable stronger human robot transfer. And this is where sort of our policy our work near experiment paper we call Eagle Bridge come in. Uh without getting too much details, this is really kind of a joint distribution alignment algorithm between human between human robot data. So we joint align these state action or observation action distribution between the two data sources via optimal transport. Uh so in case if you have not learned So optimal transport is sort of this like distribution matching algorithm and if you implement this"}, {"t": 961, "text": "like in a specific way it's differentiable so you can actually use this as a way to kind of pull distributions together without destroying their respective marginal distribution structure. So uh kind of just kind of skip over the equations here, but the takeaway here is that is a very general algorithm you can use a variety of ways to decide on how to pull these distributions together and by designing this cost function. Uh so the cost function we use is kind of matching their action shapes. So human robot should have roughly same similar shape of like sort of six shape of trajectories when they do similar things. We use that as a supervision to draw them together but you can use other things like language description and so other reliable weak supervision signals to pull these distributions together. And the algorithm itself is quite simple so it's just a additional absolute loss on top of our Eagle Bridge pipeline so it's kind of plug and play. Um so there instead of one loss you have two losses. And as a result we do see that on the left side Eagle Bridge in like largely"}, {"t": 1023, "text": "improved the alignment between human robots because it's actually a bit hard to see but you can see all of these orange dots behind all all these orange dots there actually blue dots behind them. So they're kind of more or less aligned and yeah so there parts of the data that do not align, but we observe that empirically as the the model does improve performance. And if you actually visualize the nearest neighbor points between human robot, they actually show that they have very similar behaviors. Like, if you visualize the the images a bit. So So So, we do see like improvements in performance. And also, we also see some early signs of solving tasks that only seem in robot data. For example, human data. For example, we designed this task where the robot has to basically uh so you start from beginning. So, human demonstrated on this this all of these regions where you kind of open a drawer and put something in"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "it. The robot robot data only covers the the the purple kind of quadrants of these. So, there's a only one region that that's does not cover by robot data, but only by human data. So, we can see that robot is actually able to solve this task with a significant boost in performance. So, most of the methods will not be able to do this in this kind of drawing. So, so so kind of just to kind of tie back to triangle. So, we were able to kind of improve the algorithm to to make sure that the whole cycle kind of are consistent with what we are trying to achieve here, which is learning from human data. But now, what we'll be seeing doing is kind of largely just focusing on table top manipulation data tasks. So, what if we want to change this for something like a humanoid, right? Like, where it can actually move around and do things in a room scale. So, we have to kind of adapt our algorithms and whole"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "system to to to to accommodate that. So, it's actually a very interesting topic because teleoperation teleoperated mobile manipulation has been there for a few years. Uh if you I'm I'm not sure if you guys seen us uh demo from his paper called Mobile Aloha, where they kind of tele the robot to do a variety of things and train a behavioral policy on top of that, which is actually quite impressive. Uh but at the same time, mobile demonstration are really really hard luck cuz you have actually have to move the robots around and uh it's also not very intuitive to do mobile uh tele robot demonstrations. But human data, on the other hand, do does contain all of the information we need to actually uh to to kind of learn from uh learn to behave in a mobile way. And specifically, if you look at uh the product Aria glasses, we could actually extract a lot of information from just one one task demonstration. For example, we can have the hands, actions, or slam map, and where the person is uh sort of"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "in in at any given time. So, we can reasonably expect to recover some sort of uh mobile manipulation policy from this data. All right. So, uh with that in mind, uh our student uh developed the algorithm called Emma, uh which kind of is very similar to Ego Mimic, but it's just co-trained from human-robot data. And we actually had to do a lot of full-stack design to impose the robots and algorithms so that we can mitigate the difference between uh the more kind of the the bigger gaps between human-robots when you start doing mobile manipulation. So, I won't go into too much details uh of these algorithms. If you're interested, you can search up the paper. Uh but at the end of the day, we were able to train a policy that does these kind of mobile manipulation tasks uh with uh through end-to-end learning. And we were also able to do these kind of long-range tasks, uh like grocery shopping, not just like bagging groceries. Um Uh with with just a end-to-end learning"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "pipeline. Uh you can see that the kind of the everything is driven by egocentric basically. Yeah. And we were able to see a kind of a drastic kind of boost in performance compared to just calibration data. So given the same amount of time, if you combine like six space manipulation teleoperation policy plus mobile human data, it vastly outperforms the mobile teleoperated demonstration data. And we see a very very clean scaling curve as you add more human data into the system, the policy performance increases. And after that, we started working on a few other things around this. And one of the most recent kind of ongoing work is to really deploy some of the things to a full-blown humanoids with dexterous hands and everything. So this is really our early kind of experiments on testing the replay of human"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "from human data to robots. Yeah. Okay. So I want to kind of end this talk. Uh How much time do I have? Uh you still have you still have time. All good. I can't hear you. Sorry. You still have time. Okay. Good. All right. Awesome. So Yeah. So I kind of want to reflect on what we built. Like there are many other works that happened in our lab that build around the kind of this triangle like better hardware, better algorithms, better human experience. So we like the in this talk we talked about like Eagle mimic which kind of first time that that established this triangle for human data. We improved the algorithm and we improved the the the the sort of the hardware and we also improved the the whole whole stack system. But there are many many other open questions out there to in order to better leverage human data for robot"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "learning. I would kind of summarize these into three categories. One is hardware systems, the other is all the data algorithms, and third is the whole research community. Um specifically, we're interested in how to better capture human experience context, how to design better hardwares for robots so that they're more human-like, what roles for human robot each play in a learning algorithm, and how to drive the collective progress for learning from human data for robot learning. Um so, the claim here is that we've already kind of captured certain aspect of human data human experience through these kind of wearable devices. Uh but, if you look at if you think more about this, first-person videos are not sensorimotor experience, and they're not decision-making context. So, there are a few kind of layers gap between first-person video all the way to things that are kind of decision-complete, so to say, for robot learning. Um first of all, sensorimotor experience"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "our sensorimotor experience is multimodal. It's not just first-person video, right? So, we we capture the videos effectively, but on the tactile side is for example, uh there we're nowhere near capturing the complete like force and tactile signals that we use daily to kind of do this kind of fine-grained contact rich manipulation yet. And on the other side and the context necessary for decision-making is also more than just instantaneous observation, right? It's not just mapping my current observation to that action. If you look at this first-person video again, the reason that this chef turned to the other counter to get the ingredient or to adjust the heat occasionally is not to only based on this instantaneous observation, but rather what he remembered from a few minutes ago that he had prepared these ingredients, and he had years of training on how to cook certain ingredients. We're still far from assimilating this kind of knowledge to our robots through"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "end-to-end learning. And not just we're not also not just care about like kind of robot kinematics. Can we Can we kind of make Sorry. So, the other side of things is that can we make robot hardware systems more human-like? For example, we designed this kind of very simple robots to show that if you have agile lean robots, you can learn from human more effectively. But what if you make human robots even more human? And in our work, recent work from Nvidia, we actually built this kind of a even more human-like upper body with 22 degree hands and better arms. We're able to do these kind of tasks that we were not able to do with with the tasks with the systems we had before. So, the question then is that if we make human robots more human-like, can we actually learn more effectively from from human data? Um and on the second on the data side,"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "kind of what we claim is that human embodiment is universal and has scalable data source. More human-like robots will make human data more useful for robot learning. So, if you kind of think about this in your head, what we're claiming really is that we have a lot of human data and we have some embodiment specific data to ground this this this this this this prior knowledge. So, the question is what roles should human and robot data each play in this in this whole setting? Um and most of you Some of you probably have seen kind of this analogy of like our data being kind of a pyramid where you have more scalable data sources on the basement Sorry, on the foundation also the less more scarce or more kind of high-quality data on the top. So, for a human robot data, our pyramid will look like this. But where we kind of fine-tune or pre-train human data with robot data. But I argue that just like how we we can actually flip this pyramid and say maybe"}, {"t": 1622, "text": "more robot data or high-quality robot data or better policy can actually enable us to learn better learn from this human data. So we may be able to kind of flip this pyramid and say it's only if we have more kind of better robot foundation model can we better learn from human data. So, you know, recent collaboration with collaboration with PI physical intelligence will kind of validate this hypothesis by saying that maybe a stronger foundation policy model can enable better human robot transfer. We saw that indeed if you actually pre-train your robot foundation model better like with more cross-modality robot data, we actually can see a bigger jump when you fine-tune it with robot with human data. So, and we also saw this sort of in this kind of Disney embedding map where with more with better VLA model foundation models, when you add in the human data, they actually"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "align better with the with the in-distribution robot data. So, if you recall the Eagle Bridge picture, now we're saying that if you have VLA better VLAs, you achieve a similar effect with human robot alignment. And finally, the question is can we drive collective progress? So, I recognize learning from human data is still kind of a I wouldn't say a niche field, but it's actually resource-intensive field. We we've seen a lot of kind of collective efforts on collecting a lot of robot data together. For example, Oyer I think was also part of some of these efforts. The question then becomes can we actually kind of drive this progress of the whole risk community by building some shared infrastructure from let's say data sets to policy learning algorithms to evaluation pipelines to the to to kind of to close the whole loop. And the hope is that if we kind of kind of open source all these data sets and infrastructure, we are able to do"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "better drive the performance and drive the kind of collective progress of the community. And I kind of just give you a very sneak peek of what we're going to release in a few weeks. It's called EgoVerse. It's exactly that. So we're able to kind of put together resources from many different institutions and also companies to build these data sets and all the infrastructures to deploy this these human-to-robot transfer algorithms on a variety of different embodiments. We're just going to show this is roughly what a data set looks like. It's roughly 1,300 hours of human data, but it's still increasing. So every day we we have companies kind of contribute their data some part of their data into internal policy among our academic partners. And we also have these kind of interfaces to browse through these data on a web portal. And we also across three different universities, we we cross-validated this data be useful for"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "for for robot learning. So for example, we were able to show like the exact same policy learning pipeline deployed on three completely different robots and achieve similar performance jump. Uh uh the by learning from human data. Okay, I think I'm slightly over time. So here are the three main papers discussed during this talk and here are the funding agencies. And if you're interested in learning more about the type of work we do, you're welcome to visit our web lab website or just email me if you have questions. And thank you. &gt;&gt; Cool. Thanks a lot for uh thanks a lot for the amazing talk. Um does anybody have any questions for Dunfei? Thank you very much. Hello. Thank you for a nice"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "presentation. I have a question about the quality of the data that you can get by human egocentric. You've talked in the beginning of the class. &gt;&gt; it's a it's a bit muff. Can you maybe hold your mic closer to you or a bit hard to hear. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; Um can you hear me better now? Yeah, yeah, better, yeah. Okay. So, the question I have is about Yeah, sorry. Go ahead. It's about the quality of the data that you can get uh using egocentric human data. Yeah. &gt;&gt; Because when you teleoperate, you get like high-quality data, I imagine. And like when you operate using hum- like humans, you don't get the exact same type. And I wanted to know what the bridge is and like how you're able to improve teleoperation data through humans. Yeah, I think that's a very like it's it's a very fundamental question is that what do we learn from human data? Um so, I"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "have like I have a very very long answer to this question, but let me just summarize my thoughts. Is that um right now, today, well, how we're using these human data is really just kind of adding that into a training mix uh and just use our intuition to bridge as many kind of gaps that we we see as possible. For example, humans move a lot faster in robot teleoperation. So, we need kind of align their align their speed together so so they can learn shared representation. They will move our head a lot, so maybe we will tell our demonstrator not move our head a lot so that I have a stable view. But so so that we're we're kind of treating this as a distributional alignment problem so that we can make humans more data more robot data like. But in the future, I think distributed around, we should actually make our robot more be able to generate more human-like behaviors such that I"}, {"t": 1982, "text": "can better leverage human data. So So I don't think that directly answers your question, but at least that that's kind of the for the time being where we have hacks. For in the long-term future, I think the future is bright in the sense that once we have better have better hardware, we can more systematically solve this problem. Well, thanks. We have more one more question here. Hi. How do you estimate forces from the video? We don't. Right now, we don't. We actually have There's a one paper that I did not talk about it's called mimic touch that we start to use tactile, not force. Very importantly, not force. But with fingers. Sorry. Okay. Is that the problem? It must be scary to deploy a robot without knowing forces. Yeah, force is force is hard to measure, right? Cuz force, if you think about it,"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "is is not really about your fingertip force, but the overall force you exert to the object. So So it's a kind of force transmission problem that like can you actually estimate how much overall force you exert on a given object in addition to your fingertip like sensing of tactile. So that's an incredibly hard problem that we will able to solve will have to solve, but now we don't actually have the hardware to be able to measure force or tactile uh, uh, in a high high enough of fidelity. Um, I have a question on top of that question. What do you think about event cameras to estimate force on tactile force? Event camera. So, event cameras are useful when you have a rapid drastic change of speed on different things, uh, but if you are talking about tactile force, I think they're mostly invisible from egocentric view. Uh, so if you think about like when you're holding something, uh, your your actually your your the contact you don't directly"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "like see the contact between fingertip and object because by definition it's occluded, right? So, uh, unless you put an event camera inside your fingertip like GelSight. That might be actually be something to look into. Uh, but again, I I think we don't have the right hardware for measuring force or tactile yet. At least like if you want to bridge the human-robot gap. Hello. Um, another question. Uh, thanks for the talk. Um, I imagine across these results like, um, every time you had a task it was a, um, model that was trained specifically for that task. Um, and you showed some of that distribution alignment type stuff with Um, but kind of just interested in your thoughts on like continual learning. So, Mhm. &gt;&gt; would this egocentric human data show like better performance in this like continual learning setting than kind of teleop? And what are your kind of predictions on that? Yeah. That's an excellent question. I I think this is more of like, uh, so my view about"}, {"t": 2162, "text": "continual learning is that human data is more suitable for studying this problem because you can imagine a setting where you want to adapt your home robots to a new home just by observing how humans like interact with their environment in their homes. Uh, than sort of having the human collect robot on robot teleop data to adapt the robot to that new home. So, I don't I I have a particular good idea about how to solve this problem from the sort of algorithm sense, but I think it's a more it's a very kind of suitable problem to just be studied in the human-to-robot transfer setting. Cool. Thank you very much. Let's thank Danfei again for the amazing talk. &gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 53, "title": "The triangle of learning"}, {"t": 146, "title": "Limitations of teleoperation"}, {"t": 334, "title": "Leveraging human experience"}, {"t": 425, "title": "Egomimic scaling project"}, {"t": 640, "title": "Evaluating egomimic"}, {"t": 761, "title": "Zero-shot transfer challenges"}, {"t": 930, "title": "Egobridge for alignment"}, {"t": 1130, "title": "Mobile robot manipulation"}, {"t": 1325, "title": "Future research directions"}, {"t": 1747, "title": "The egoverse dataset"}, {"t": 1820, "title": "Q&A session"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Georgia Tech"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 02.03.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (64 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week03_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/Ef4R5s1LqoQ (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_danfei_xu.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 3: Imitation Learning</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 02.03.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 3: Imitation Learning Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 02.03.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-feedback---actions-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Feedback -&gt; Actions", "title": "Slide 1: Feedback -> Actions", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Moved from 3 papers to 2 papers per lecture</li>\n<li>Extra paper slots on Thursdays in IFW A 32.1</li>\n<li>HW 2 released, due on March 12th</li>\n<li>We can see when you use AI in autograder</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Moved from 3 papers to 2 papers per lecture Extra paper slots on Thursdays in IFW A 32.1 HW 2 released, due on March 12th We can see when you use AI in autograder", "t": 0}, {"id": "title-shown-again-frame-3", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title <span class=\"own\">[shown again]</span>", "title": "Title [shown again]", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Same title slide as frame 1.]</span></p>", "text": "[Same title slide as frame 1.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-recap-mapping-observations-to-actions-frame-4", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: Recap: Mapping Observations to Actions", "title": "Slide 2: Recap: Mapping Observations to Actions", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: observation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> (snowy road through a car windscreen) -&gt; convolutional network -&gt; action distribution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> (Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2), with an arrow from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - Policy - Partially Observable</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - Policy - Fully Observable</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> - action: the decision taken by the agent at time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> - observation: what the agent observes at time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> - state: the “state” of the world at time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory: sequence of state and actions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_T, a_T)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s, a)</annotation></semantics></math> reward: how good is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s, a</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: observation o t (snowy road through a car windscreen) -> convolutional network -> action distribution a t (Left 0.0, Right 0.0, Straight 0.8, Backward 0.2), with an arrow from a t back to o t .] π θ ( a t ∣ o t ) - Policy - Partially Observable π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) - Policy - Fully Observable a t - action: the decision taken by the agent at time t o t - observation: what the agent observes at time t s t - state: the “state” of the world at time t τ trajectory: sequence of state and actions ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s T , a T ) r ( s , a ) reward: how good is s , a ?", "t": 121}, {"id": "slide-3-imitation-learning-frame-5", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Imitation Learning", "title": "Slide 3: Imitation Learning", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Data</strong>: Given trajectories collected by an “expert” “demonstrations” <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} = \\{(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_t)\\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a Dataset cylinder holding <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_t)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></li>\n<li><strong>Goal</strong>: Learn a policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that “imitates” the expert’s behavior</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: road image -&gt; <strong>Policy</strong> -&gt; the same road image with a forked arrow (choose a direction).]</span></p>", "text": "Data : Given trajectories collected by an “expert” “demonstrations” 𝒟 = { ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s t ) } [Figure: a Dataset cylinder holding ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s t ) ] Goal : Learn a policy π θ that “imitates” the expert’s behavior [Figure: road image -> Policy -> the same road image with a forked arrow (choose a direction).]", "t": 121}, {"id": "slide-4-behavioral-cloning-frame-6", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Behavioral Cloning", "title": "Slide 4: Behavioral Cloning", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Given expert demonstrations: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} = \\{(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_t)\\}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For deterministic policy, regress to expert’s actions: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_\\theta \\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}|} \\sum_{(\\mathbf{s}, \\mathbf{a}) \\in \\mathcal{D}} \\|\\mathbf{a} - \\hat{\\mathbf{a}}\\|^2</annotation></semantics></math> where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{a}} = \\pi_\\theta(\\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Deploy policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> on robot</li>\n</ol>", "text": "Given expert demonstrations: 𝒟 = { ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s t ) } For deterministic policy, regress to expert’s actions: min &#8289; θ 1 | 𝒟 | ∑ ( 𝒔 , 𝒂 ) ∈ 𝒟 ∥ 𝒂 − 𝒂 ̂ ∥ 2 where 𝒂 ̂ = π θ ( 𝒔 ) Deploy policy π θ on robot", "t": 182}, {"id": "slide-10-shown-early-learning-to-fly-in-swiss-forests-frame-7", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10 <span class=\"own\">[shown early]</span>: Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "title": "Slide 10 [shown early] : Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Same camera trick</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video frame of a quadcopter flying along a forest trail, with the title card “Quadcopter navigation in the forest - Trail following under the tree canopy”.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: this frame shows slide 10 between slides 4 and 5; the lecturer skipped ahead briefly. Slide 10 appears again in order at frame 16.]</span></p>", "text": "Same camera trick [Figure: video frame of a quadcopter flying along a forest trail, with the title card “Quadcopter navigation in the forest - Trail following under the tree canopy”.] [Note: this frame shows slide 10 between slides 4 and 5; the lecturer skipped ahead briefly. Slide 10 appears again in order at frame 16.]", "t": 301}, {"id": "slide-5-what-can-go-wrong-frames-8-10", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: What can go wrong?", "title": "Slide 5: What can go wrong?", "frames": [8, 10], "html": "<p><strong>Supervised Learning</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an ellipse <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> containing sample points <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1, x_2, x_3</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Inputs are independently, identically distributed (<strong>i.i.d.</strong>) and <strong>independent of predicted labels</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>Behavioral Cloning</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the expert’s states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4</annotation></semantics></math> along one path; the policy’s predicted actions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{a}_1, \\hat{a}_2, \\hat{a}_3</annotation></semantics></math> each deviate slightly, so the visited states drift further from the expert path at every step.]</span></p>\n<p>Small error in predicted action can lead to <strong>drift away</strong> from training data distribution!</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{expert} \\ne p_\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{expert}</annotation></semantics></math>: States visited by expert</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math>: States visited by the policy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Frame 9 is the earlier build showing only the expert path <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1 \\to s_4</annotation></semantics></math>; frames 8 and 10 show the full slide.]</span></p>", "text": "Supervised Learning [Figure: an ellipse p ( x ) containing sample points x 1 , x 2 , x 3 .] Inputs are independently, identically distributed ( i.i.d. ) and independent of predicted labels ! Behavioral Cloning [Figure: the expert’s states s 1 , s 2 , s 3 , s 4 along one path; the policy’s predicted actions a ̂ 1 , a ̂ 2 , a ̂ 3 each deviate slightly, so the visited states drift further from the expert path at every step.] Small error in predicted action can lead to drift away from training data distribution! p e x p e r t ≠ p π ( s ) p e x p e r t : States visited by expert p π ( s ) : States visited by the policy [Frame 9 is the earlier build showing only the expert path s 1 → s 4 ; frames 8 and 10 show the full slide.]", "t": 360}, {"id": "slide-6-does-it-work-frames-11-13", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Does it work?", "title": "Slide 6: Does it work?", "frames": [11, 13], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded NVIDIA video of a self-driving car learning to steer: a parking lot lined with traffic cones (subtitle “It began its driving lessons in a parking lot”), then a road narrowed by cones and barriers in a construction zone (subtitle “even leaving the road to stay safe.”).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>End to End Learning for Self-Driving Cars</em>, Bojarski, et. al., 2016</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: embedded NVIDIA video of a self-driving car learning to steer: a parking lot lined with traffic cones (subtitle “It began its driving lessons in a parking lot”), then a road narrowed by cones and barriers in a construction zone (subtitle “even leaving the road to stay safe.”).] Reference: End to End Learning for Self-Driving Cars , Bojarski, et. al., 2016 [Note: slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8.]", "t": 540}, {"id": "slide-8-why-did-it-work-frame-14", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Why did it work?", "title": "Slide 8: Why did it work?", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: top view of a two-lane road with a car drifted against the edge line, captioned:]</span> What should the policy do here? Expert never “visited this state”!</p>", "text": "[Figure: top view of a two-lane road with a car drifted against the edge line, captioned:] What should the policy do here? Expert never “visited this state”!", "t": 540}, {"id": "slide-9-why-did-it-work-data-augmentation-frame-15", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Why did it work? Data Augmentation!", "title": "Slide 9: Why did it work? Data Augmentation!", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Add “fake” data that illustrates corrections with side-facing cameras</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">aug</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">center</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{aug}} = \\{(\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{center}}, \\mathbf{a}), (\\mathbf{o}_{left}, \\mathbf{a} + \\delta), (\\mathbf{o}_{right}, \\mathbf{a} - \\delta)\\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: top view of a car with three cameras on its front: the left-facing camera is paired with “Action Label: Steer Right”, the centre camera with “Action Label: Go Straight”, the right-facing camera with “Action Label: Steer Left”.]</span></p>", "text": "Add “fake” data that illustrates corrections with side-facing cameras 𝒟 aug = { ( 𝒐 center , 𝒂 ) , ( 𝒐 l e f t , 𝒂 + δ ) , ( 𝒐 r i g h t , 𝒂 − δ ) } [Figure: top view of a car with three cameras on its front: the left-facing camera is paired with “Action Label: Steer Right”, the centre camera with “Action Label: Go Straight”, the right-facing camera with “Action Label: Steer Left”.]", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-10-learning-to-fly-in-swiss-forests-frames-16-22", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "title": "Slide 10: Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "frames": [16, 22], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Same camera trick</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded explainer video playing across seven frames. Title card “Quadcopter navigation in the forest - Trail following under the tree canopy” (same as frame 7). “Main challenge: perceiving a real-world trail from images is extremely difficult”. Six forest photos sorted into “trail heading left”, “trail straight ahead”, “trail heading right”, asking “which direction is the trail heading to?”. “How we solved the problem”: trail image -&gt; neural network -&gt; steering wheel with left / straight / right arrows. “How the classifier works”: an input image passing through convolutional feature maps. “Training the classifier”: a cartoon hiker wearing a head-mounted camera.]</span></p>", "text": "Same camera trick [Figure: embedded explainer video playing across seven frames. Title card “Quadcopter navigation in the forest - Trail following under the tree canopy” (same as frame 7). “Main challenge: perceiving a real-world trail from images is extremely difficult”. Six forest photos sorted into “trail heading left”, “trail straight ahead”, “trail heading right”, asking “which direction is the trail heading to?”. “How we solved the problem”: trail image -> neural network -> steering wheel with left / straight / right arrows. “How the classifier works”: an input image passing through convolutional feature maps. “Training the classifier”: a cartoon hiker wearing a head-mounted camera.]", "t": 780}, {"id": "slide-11-upper-bound-of-behavioral-cloning-frames-23-24", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Upper Bound of Behavioral Cloning", "title": "Slide 11: Upper Bound of Behavioral Cloning", "frames": [23, 24], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram from slide 5, with a mistake probability <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> marked at each step and horizon <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math> at the end; a photo of a tightrope walker between two towers.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>: time horizon</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>: probability of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> making a mistake at any steps</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(\\mathbf{s}, \\mathbf{a}) = \\begin{cases} 0 &amp; \\text{if } \\mathbf{a} = \\pi^\\star(\\mathbf{s}) \\\\ 1 &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>∀</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">train</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\mathbf{a} \\ne \\pi^\\star(\\mathbf{s}) \\mid \\mathbf{s}) \\le \\epsilon, \\quad \\forall \\mathbf{s} \\in \\mathcal{D}_{\\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 24) adds:]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑶</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_t c(s_t, a_t)\\right] \\le \\epsilon T + (1 - \\epsilon)(\\epsilon(T - 1) + \\cdots) \\approx \\mathbf{O}(\\epsilon T^2)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Distribution shift causes the error to grow <strong>quadratically!</strong></p>\n<p>More analysis: “A Reduction of Imitation Learning and Structured Prediction to No-Regret Online Learning” by Ross et al., 2011.</p>", "text": "[Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram from slide 5, with a mistake probability ϵ marked at each step and horizon T at the end; a photo of a tightrope walker between two towers.] T : time horizon ϵ : probability of π θ making a mistake at any steps c ( 𝒔 , 𝒂 ) = { 0 if 𝒂 = π ⋆ ( 𝒔 ) 1 otherwise P ( 𝒂 ≠ π ⋆ ( 𝒔 ) ∣ 𝒔 ) ≤ ϵ , ∀ 𝒔 ∈ 𝒟 train [Second build (frame 24) adds:] 𝔼 [ ∑ t c ( s t , a t ) ] ≤ ϵ T + ( 1 − ϵ ) ( ϵ ( T − 1 ) + ⋯ ) ≈ 𝑶 ( ϵ T 2 ) Distribution shift causes the error to grow quadratically! More analysis: “A Reduction of Imitation Learning and Structured Prediction to No-Regret Online Learning” by Ross et al., 2011.", "t": 901}, {"id": "slide-12-addressing-compounding-error-frame-25", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Addressing Compounding Error", "title": "Slide 12: Addressing Compounding Error", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<p>Can we make?</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{expert} = p_\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{expert}</annotation></semantics></math>: States visited by expert</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math>: States visited by the policy</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Can we make? p e x p e r t = p π ( s ) p e x p e r t : States visited by expert p π ( s ) : States visited by the policy", "t": 1020}, {"id": "slide-13-aggregating-corrective-behavior-frame-26", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Aggregating Corrective Behavior", "title": "Slide 13: Aggregating Corrective Behavior", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<p><strong>Dagger: Dataset Aggregation</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Roll out policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and collect states: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s&#39;_1, \\hat{a}_1, \\dots, s&#39;_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Query/label expert action at visited states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">expert</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{a}^* \\sim \\pi_{\\text{expert}}(\\cdot \\mid \\mathbf{s}&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Aggregate</strong> corrections with existing data <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>←</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} \\leftarrow \\mathcal{D} \\cup \\{(\\mathbf{s}&#39;, \\mathbf{a}^*)\\}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Update policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>←</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta \\leftarrow \\arg\\min_\\theta \\mathcal{L}(\\pi_\\theta, \\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram, now with blue arrows from each state the policy visited pointing back toward the expert path (the expert’s corrective labels).]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Algorithm will converge <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(s) = p_{expert}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>✅ Achieves <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\epsilon T)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of BC <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\epsilon T^2)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>❌ Hindsight labeling &amp; expert querying difficult</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paradox BC works better if the data has more mistakes and recoveries!</p>", "text": "Dagger: Dataset Aggregation Roll out policy π θ and collect states: ( s 1 ′ , a ̂ 1 , … , s t ′ ) Query/label expert action at visited states 𝒂 * ∼ π expert ( ⋅ ∣ 𝒔 ′ ) Aggregate corrections with existing data 𝒟 ← 𝒟 ∪ { ( 𝒔 ′ , 𝒂 * ) } Update policy θ ← arg &#8289; min &#8289; θ ℒ ( π θ , 𝒟 ) [Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram, now with blue arrows from each state the policy visited pointing back toward the expert path (the expert’s corrective labels).] ✅ Algorithm will converge p π ( s ) = p e x p e r t ✅ Achieves O ( ϵ T ) instead of BC O ( ϵ T 2 ) ❌ Hindsight labeling & expert querying difficult Paradox BC works better if the data has more mistakes and recoveries!", "t": 1141}, {"id": "slide-14-aggregating-online-interventions-frame-27", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Aggregating Online Interventions", "title": "Slide 14: Aggregating Online Interventions", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<p><strong>Human Gated Dagger</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Roll out policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and collect states: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s&#39;_1, \\hat{a}_1, \\dots, s&#39;_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Expert intervenes at time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math> when policy makes mistake</li>\n<li>Expert provides partial demo <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf{s}&#39;_t, \\mathbf{a}^*_t, \\dots, \\mathbf{s}&#39;_T)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Aggregate</strong> new demos <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>←</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} \\leftarrow \\mathcal{D} \\cup \\{(\\mathbf{s}&#39;_i, \\mathbf{a}^*_i)\\}; i \\ge t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Update policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>←</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta \\leftarrow \\min_\\theta \\mathcal{L}(\\pi_\\theta, \\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram where, after two policy steps, a blue expert arrow takes over and steers to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_4</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Lets the human take control!</li>\n<li>❌ How to detect when an intervention is needed?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: step 5 should read <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\min_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, as in step 4 of slide 13.]</span></p>", "text": "Human Gated Dagger Roll out policy π θ and collect states: ( s 1 ′ , a ̂ 1 , … , s t ′ ) Expert intervenes at time t when policy makes mistake Expert provides partial demo ( 𝒔 t ′ , 𝒂 t * , … , 𝒔 T ′ ) Aggregate new demos 𝒟 ← 𝒟 ∪ { ( 𝒔 i ′ , 𝒂 i * ) } ; i ≥ t Update policy π θ ← min &#8289; θ ℒ ( π θ , 𝒟 ) [Figure: the drifting-trajectory diagram where, after two policy steps, a blue expert arrow takes over and steers to s 4 .] ✅ Lets the human take control! ❌ How to detect when an intervention is needed? [Note: step 5 should read arg &#8289; min &#8289; θ , as in step 4 of slide 13.]", "t": 1200}, {"id": "slide-15-case-study-language-ambiguities-frame-28", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Case Study: Language Ambiguities", "title": "Slide 15: Case Study: Language Ambiguities", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three panels of a two-armed PR2 robot at a table of household objects. Panel 1 dialogue: “Fetch the round yellow thing” / “Do you mean the lemon in the middle?” / “Yes”, with boxes around the candidate yellow objects. Panel 2: “Place it left of the object on the bottom”, with a heat map over the table showing where to place. Panel 3: the robot placing the object.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language</em>, <strong>Mees</strong> and Burgard, ISER 2020</p>", "text": "[Figure: three panels of a two-armed PR2 robot at a table of household objects. Panel 1 dialogue: “Fetch the round yellow thing” / “Do you mean the lemon in the middle?” / “Yes”, with boxes around the candidate yellow objects. Panel 2: “Place it left of the object on the bottom”, with a heat map over the table showing where to place. Panel 3: the robot placing the object.] Reference: Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language , Mees and Burgard, ISER 2020", "t": 1322}, {"id": "slide-16-case-study-language-ambiguities-method-frames-29-31", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Case Study: Language Ambiguities <span class=\"own\">[method]</span>", "title": "Slide 16: Case Study: Language Ambiguities [method]", "frames": [29, 31], "html": "<p>“Pick up the blue stuff”</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: camera image of a table with a black bowl, a blue mug, a blue bowl, a red cup, a can in a bowl and a toy car; the blue mug and black bowl have blue boxes, the blue bowl a green box. A robot icon asks: “Do you mean this blue bowl on the table?”]</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Train referential expression comprehension model with ranking loss</li>\n</ol>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{rank}} = \\sum_i \\Big[\\lambda_1 \\max\\big(0, m_1 + S(o_i \\mid r_j) - S(o_i \\mid r_i)\\big) + \\lambda_2 \\max\\big(0, m_1 + S(o_k \\mid r_i) - S(o_i \\mid r_i)\\big)\\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ol start=\"2\" type=\"1\">\n<li>Train referential expression generation model</li>\n</ol>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">gen</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{gen}} = -\\sum_i \\log P(r_i \\mid v_i)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">mmi</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{mmi}} = \\sum_i \\big[\\lambda_3 \\max\\big(0, m_2 + \\log P(r_i \\mid v_k) - \\log P(r_i \\mid v_i)\\big)\\big]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ol start=\"3\" type=\"1\">\n<li>If at test time there are multiple objects within margin <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math>, generate descriptions and ask user</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[The three numbered steps appear one per build.]</span></p>", "text": "“Pick up the blue stuff” [Figure: camera image of a table with a black bowl, a blue mug, a blue bowl, a red cup, a can in a bowl and a toy car; the blue mug and black bowl have blue boxes, the blue bowl a green box. A robot icon asks: “Do you mean this blue bowl on the table?”] Train referential expression comprehension model with ranking loss ℒ rank = ∑ i [ λ 1 max &#8289; ( 0 , m 1 + S ( o i ∣ r j ) − S ( o i ∣ r i ) ) + λ 2 max &#8289; ( 0 , m 1 + S ( o k ∣ r i ) − S ( o i ∣ r i ) ) ] Train referential expression generation model ℒ gen = − ∑ i log &#8289; P ( r i ∣ v i ) ℒ mmi = ∑ i [ λ 3 max &#8289; ( 0 , m 2 + log &#8289; P ( r i ∣ v k ) − log &#8289; P ( r i ∣ v i ) ) ] If at test time there are multiple objects within margin m , generate descriptions and ask user [The three numbered steps appear one per build.]", "t": 1322}, {"id": "slide-17-case-study-language-ambiguities-demo-frames-32-38", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Case Study: Language Ambiguities <span class=\"own\">[demo]</span>", "title": "Slide 17: Case Study: Language Ambiguities [demo]", "frames": [32, 38], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video (2x speed) playing across seven frames: the PR2 robot at two small tables of household objects follows spoken commands shown as subtitles: “Fetch the red thing” (picks up a red bowl), “Place it behind the colored dish” (places it), then for an ambiguous request the robot asks “I am not sure, do you mean the middle banana?”, picks the banana, and gets “Place it on top of the bottom object”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language</em>, <strong>Mees</strong> and Burgard, ISER 2020</p>", "text": "[Figure: embedded video (2x speed) playing across seven frames: the PR2 robot at two small tables of household objects follows spoken commands shown as subtitles: “Fetch the red thing” (picks up a red bowl), “Place it behind the colored dish” (places it), then for an ambiguous request the robot asks “I am not sure, do you mean the middle banana?”, picks the banana, and gets “Place it on top of the bottom object”.] Reference: Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language , Mees and Burgard, ISER 2020", "t": 1322}, {"id": "slide-18-why-might-we-still-fail-to-mimick-the-expert-frame-39", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert?", "title": "Slide 18: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert?", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<p><strong>Non-Markovian behavior</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - arrow: action depends only on current observation</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid o_1, \\dots, o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - arrow: Human behavior might be affected by past observations, emotions, privileged information etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 19 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 20.]</span></p>", "text": "Non-Markovian behavior π θ ( a t ∣ o t ) - arrow: action depends only on current observation π θ ( a t ∣ o 1 , … , o t ) - arrow: Human behavior might be affected by past observations, emotions, privileged information etc. [Note: slide 19 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 20.]", "t": 1504}, {"id": "slide-20-adding-history-to-policy-frame-40", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Adding History to Policy", "title": "Slide 20: Adding History to Policy", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Encode with some sequence to sequence architecture</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a video frame of a robot arm at a desk with drawers, captioned “1. ‘Open the drawer’” (2x) -&gt; <strong>Seq2Seq Model</strong> -&gt; robot arm icon.]</span></p>\n<p>❌ Adding history to the policy <strong>doesn’t always make things better</strong>!</p>", "text": "Encode with some sequence to sequence architecture [Figure: a video frame of a robot arm at a desk with drawers, captioned “1. ‘Open the drawer’” (2x) -> Seq2Seq Model -> robot arm icon.] ❌ Adding history to the policy doesn’t always make things better !", "t": 1681}, {"id": "slide-21-causal-confusion-frames-41-42", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Causal Confusion", "title": "Slide 21: Causal Confusion", "frames": [41, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Policy might infer <strong>spurious correlations</strong>!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video frame of the robot arm at the drawer, “1. ‘Open the drawer’” (2x).]</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Data: every time the robot opens the drawer, the gripper grasps the handle with 10 N</li>\n<li>Model learns: if history shows a gripper force spike of 10 N, pull to open</li>\n<li>Test time: gripper slips and only reads 2 N, the pull command is never triggered</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 42) adds:]</span> ❌ Policy thinks history of gripper sensor causes open drawer instead of the visual state!</p>\n<p>More analysis: “Causal Confusion in Imitation Learning” by de Haan et al., 2019.</p>", "text": "Policy might infer spurious correlations ! [Figure: video frame of the robot arm at the drawer, “1. ‘Open the drawer’” (2x).] Data: every time the robot opens the drawer, the gripper grasps the handle with 10 N Model learns: if history shows a gripper force spike of 10 N, pull to open Test time: gripper slips and only reads 2 N, the pull command is never triggered [Second build (frame 42) adds:] ❌ Policy thinks history of gripper sensor causes open drawer instead of the visual state! More analysis: “Causal Confusion in Imitation Learning” by de Haan et al., 2019.", "t": 1800}, {"id": "slide-22-why-might-we-still-fail-to-mimick-the-expert-multimodality-frame-43", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert? <span class=\"own\">[multimodality]</span>", "title": "Slide 22: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert? [multimodality]", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<p><strong>Multimodal behavior</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stochasticity:</strong> Many (<strong>infinite</strong>) ways to close a drawer with a 7-DoF robot!</li>\n<li><strong>Expert Inconsistency</strong>: Humans operators might use different ‘<strong>modes</strong>’ across trials</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three photos of the robot arm closing the same drawer with clearly different gripper poses.]</span></p>", "text": "Multimodal behavior Stochasticity: Many ( infinite ) ways to close a drawer with a 7-DoF robot! Expert Inconsistency : Humans operators might use different ‘ modes ’ across trials [Figure: three photos of the robot arm closing the same drawer with clearly different gripper poses.]", "t": 1921}, {"id": "slide-23-multimodal-behavior-frame-44", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Multimodal Behavior", "title": "Slide 23: Multimodal Behavior", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Deterministic MSE BC policy will fail due to “<strong>averaging</strong>” of <strong>modes</strong>!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two photos of a snowy slope with a lone tree. Left: ski tracks split and pass the tree on the left and on the right. Right: a skier who took the average of the two tracks and went straight into the tree.]</span></p>", "text": "Deterministic MSE BC policy will fail due to “ averaging ” of modes ! [Figure: two photos of a snowy slope with a lone tree. Left: ski tracks split and pass the tree on the left and on the right. Right: a skier who took the average of the two tracks and went straight into the tree.]", "t": 2040}, {"id": "slide-24-mixture-of-gaussian-distributions-frame-45", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Mixture of Gaussian Distributions", "title": "Slide 24: Mixture of Gaussian Distributions", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<ul>\n<li>More expressive than a single gaussian</li>\n<li>Need to predefine number of gaussians</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, two Gaussian components (red and blue) and their bimodal sum (black outline), labelled <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_1, \\mu_2, \\Sigma_1, \\Sigma_2</annotation></semantics></math>; right, a bar chart of the mixture weights <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1 = 0.65</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_2 = 0.35</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(\\mathbf{a} \\mid \\mathbf{o}) = \\sum_i w_i \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_i, \\Sigma_i)</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "More expressive than a single gaussian Need to predefine number of gaussians [Figure: left, two Gaussian components (red and blue) and their bimodal sum (black outline), labelled μ 1 , μ 2 , Σ 1 , Σ 2 ; right, a bar chart of the mixture weights w 1 = 0.65 and w 2 = 0.35 .] π ( 𝒂 ∣ 𝒐 ) = ∑ i w i 𝒩 ( μ i , Σ i )", "t": 2040}, {"id": "slide-25-autoregressive-discretization-frame-46", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Autoregressive Discretization", "title": "Slide 25: Autoregressive Discretization", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Discretization great for representing multimodal distributions</li>\n<li>Impractical to scale to higher dimensions (exponential)</li>\n<li>Solution: <strong>per-dimension discretization with sequence model</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the ski-slope image as input tokens -&gt; <strong>Autoregressive Transformer</strong> -&gt; output tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{t,0}, a_{t,1}, a_{t,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, one per action dimension.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒕</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\mathbf{a_t} \\mid \\mathbf{s_t}) = p(a_{t,0}, a_{t,1}, a_{t,2} \\mid \\mathbf{s_t}) = p(a_{t,2} \\mid \\mathbf{s_t}, a_{t,0}, a_{t,1})\\, p(a_{t,1} \\mid \\mathbf{s_t}, a_{t,0})\\, p(a_{t,0} \\mid \\mathbf{s_t})</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Discretization great for representing multimodal distributions Impractical to scale to higher dimensions (exponential) Solution: per-dimension discretization with sequence model [Figure: the ski-slope image as input tokens -> Autoregressive Transformer -> output tokens a t , 0 , a t , 1 , a t , 2 , one per action dimension.] p ( 𝒂 𝒕 ∣ 𝒔 𝒕 ) = p ( a t , 0 , a t , 1 , a t , 2 ∣ 𝒔 𝒕 ) = p ( a t , 2 ∣ 𝒔 𝒕 , a t , 0 , a t , 1 ) p ( a t , 1 ∣ 𝒔 𝒕 , a t , 0 ) p ( a t , 0 ∣ 𝒔 𝒕 )", "t": 2100}, {"id": "slide-26-diffusion-frame-47", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: Diffusion", "title": "Slide 26: Diffusion", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Models complex distributions over continuous variables</li>\n<li>We can replace images with robot actions, more on W6</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a cat photo <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math> getting noisier left to right until it is pure noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_T</annotation></semantics></math>. Top arrow, <strong>Forward Process</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi><mo mathvariant=\"bold\">+</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">noise</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x_{i+1}} = \\mathbf{x_i} + \\text{noise}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bottom arrow, right to left, <strong>Generative Backward Process</strong>.]</span></p>\n<p>learn <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_i) = x_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> - in practice <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">noise</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\mathbf{x_i}) = \\text{noise}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi><mo mathvariant=\"bold\">−</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒊</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x_{i-1}} = \\mathbf{x_i} - f(\\mathbf{x_i})</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Models complex distributions over continuous variables We can replace images with robot actions, more on W6 [Figure: a cat photo x 0 getting noisier left to right until it is pure noise x T . Top arrow, Forward Process : 𝒙 𝒊 + 𝟏 = 𝒙 𝒊 + noise . Bottom arrow, right to left, Generative Backward Process .] learn f ( x i ) = x i − 1 - in practice f ( 𝒙 𝒊 ) = noise , 𝒙 𝒊 − 𝟏 = 𝒙 𝒊 − f ( 𝒙 𝒊 )", "t": 2221}, {"id": "slide-27-latent-variable-models-frame-48", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Latent Variable Models", "title": "Slide 27: Latent Variable Models", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Output still gaussian, but receives additional input</li>\n<li>Can represent “any” distribution</li>\n<li>Popular: conditional VAEs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(\\mathbf{a} \\mid \\mathbf{o}, z) = f_\\theta(\\mathbf{o}, z)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Conditional Decoder</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the ski slope with two tracks around the tree, the left track labelled <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{left}} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and the right <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{right}} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>: the latent picks the mode.]</span></p>", "text": "Output still gaussian, but receives additional input Can represent “any” distribution Popular: conditional VAEs π ( 𝒂 ∣ 𝒐 , z ) = f θ ( 𝒐 , z ) [arrow at f θ ] Conditional Decoder [Figure: the ski slope with two tracks around the tree, the left track labelled z left ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) and the right z right ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) : the latent picks the mode.]", "t": 2340}, {"id": "slide-28-learning-multiple-tasks-frame-49", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Learning Multiple Tasks", "title": "Slide 28: Learning Multiple Tasks", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<p>Latent Variable Models are different from task conditioning</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>“one task”</th>\n<th>“multiple tasks”</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">task id</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, \\text{task id})</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single task Behavior Cloning (Pomerleau 1991)</td>\n<td>Task conditioned Behavior Cloning (Rahmatizadeh 2018)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot tidying toy blocks in a living room.]</span></p>", "text": "Latent Variable Models are different from task conditioning “one task” “multiple tasks” π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) π θ ( a t ∣ s t , task id ) Single task Behavior Cloning (Pomerleau 1991) Task conditioned Behavior Cloning (Rahmatizadeh 2018) [Figure: a robot tidying toy blocks in a living room.]", "t": 2400}, {"id": "slide-29-learning-multiple-tasks-success-frames-50-51", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Learning Multiple Tasks <span class=\"own\">[success?]</span>", "title": "Slide 29: Learning Multiple Tasks [success?]", "frames": [50, 51], "html": "<p>“Move the sliding door to the left” - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">task id</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, \\text{task id})</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video of a robot arm at a desk with a sliding door, a drawer and buttons, moving the sliding door.]</span></p>\n<p>Did it succeed? Where is the threshold, 50%?</p>", "text": "“Move the sliding door to the left” - π θ ( a t ∣ s t , task id ) [Figure: embedded video of a robot arm at a desk with a sliding door, a drawer and buttons, moving the sliding door.] Did it succeed? Where is the threshold, 50%?", "t": 2461}, {"id": "slide-30-how-to-scale-control-to-any-tasks-frame-52", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: How to Scale Control to “Any” Tasks?", "title": "Slide 30: How to Scale Control to “Any” Tasks?", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<p><strong>Tasks</strong> are often <strong>continuous</strong>, not discrete</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>“one task”</th>\n<th>“multiple tasks”</th>\n<th>“any tasks”</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">task id</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, \\text{task id})</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, s_g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single task Behavior Cloning (Pomerleau 1991)</td>\n<td>Task conditioned Behavior Cloning (Rahmatizadeh 2018)</td>\n<td>Goal state conditioned Behavior Cloning (Lynch 2019)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: under “any tasks”, a “current” and a “goal” image of a simulated desk, with a latent space in between where a dotted arrow goes from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_g</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Tasks are often continuous , not discrete “one task” “multiple tasks” “any tasks” π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) π θ ( a t ∣ s t , task id ) π θ ( a t ∣ s t , s g ) Single task Behavior Cloning (Pomerleau 1991) Task conditioned Behavior Cloning (Rahmatizadeh 2018) Goal state conditioned Behavior Cloning (Lynch 2019) [Figure: under “any tasks”, a “current” and a “goal” image of a simulated desk, with a latent space in between where a dotted arrow goes from s t to s g .]", "t": 2521}, {"id": "slide-31-untitled-transition-question-frame-53", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 31: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [53, 53], "html": "<p><strong>Let’s put everything together!</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How can we learn any task with a latent variable model that handles multimodality?</strong></p>", "text": "Let’s put everything together! How can we learn any task with a latent variable model that handles multimodality?", "t": 2521}, {"id": "slide-32-case-study-play-data-frame-54", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Case Study: Play Data", "title": "Slide 32: Case Study: Play Data", "frames": [54, 54], "html": "<ul>\n<li>No upfront tasks</li>\n<li>Scalable, reset-free data collection</li>\n<li>Rich and highly multimodal behaviors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video of a person teleoperating a robot arm at a desk full of objects (“play”).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 33 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 32 to 34.]</span></p>", "text": "No upfront tasks Scalable, reset-free data collection Rich and highly multimodal behaviors [Figure: video of a person teleoperating a robot arm at a desk full of objects (“play”).] [Note: slide 33 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 32 to 34.]", "t": 2580}, {"id": "slide-34-case-study-learning-policies-play-data-frames-55-56", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: Case Study: Learning Policies Play Data", "title": "Slide 34: Case Study: Learning Policies Play Data", "frames": [55, 56], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: first builds of the architecture diagram completed on slides 36-37: a <strong>Sequence</strong> of observation boxes taken from a play video; its last frame becomes the <strong>Goal state</strong>, which a <strong>Goal encoder</strong> turns into a goal feature vector; at the bottom, the initial state box next to the goal feature.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 35 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 34 to 36.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: first builds of the architecture diagram completed on slides 36-37: a Sequence of observation boxes taken from a play video; its last frame becomes the Goal state , which a Goal encoder turns into a goal feature vector; at the bottom, the initial state box next to the goal feature.] [Note: slide 35 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 34 to 36.]", "t": 2580}, {"id": "slide-36-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-plan-prior-and-posterior-frame-57", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data <span class=\"own\">[plan prior and posterior]</span>", "title": "Slide 36: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [plan prior and posterior]", "frames": [57, 57], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: architecture diagram.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sequence</strong> (whole play window) -&gt; <strong>Posterior (Plan Recognition)</strong> -&gt; a Gaussian over latent plans</li>\n<li><strong>Goal state</strong> -&gt; <strong>Goal encoder</strong> -&gt; <strong>Goal features</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Initial state</strong> + goal features -&gt; <strong>Prior (Plan Proposal)</strong> -&gt; a Gaussian over latent plans</li>\n<li><strong>KL loss</strong> between the posterior and the prior Gaussians</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: architecture diagram.] Sequence (whole play window) -> Posterior (Plan Recognition) -> a Gaussian over latent plans Goal state -> Goal encoder -> Goal features Initial state + goal features -> Prior (Plan Proposal) -> a Gaussian over latent plans KL loss between the posterior and the prior Gaussians", "t": 2640}, {"id": "slide-37-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-action-decoder-frame-58", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data <span class=\"own\">[action decoder]</span>", "title": "Slide 37: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [action decoder]", "frames": [58, 58], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the slide 36 diagram completed: arrows show the goal state is the last frame of the sequence and the initial state the first. A latent plan is sampled from the posterior (“Sample latent plan”); the <strong>Action decoder</strong> takes the latent plan, the goal features and the <strong>Current state</strong> and outputs an <strong>Action likelihood</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: the slide 36 diagram completed: arrows show the goal state is the last frame of the sequence and the initial state the first. A latent plan is sampled from the posterior (“Sample latent plan”); the Action decoder takes the latent plan, the goal features and the Current state and outputs an Action likelihood .]", "t": 2760}, {"id": "slide-38-case-study-learning-policies-from-play-data-language-goals-frame-59", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data <span class=\"own\">[language goals]</span>", "title": "Slide 38: Case Study: Learning Policies from Play Data [language goals]", "frames": [59, 59], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an “Unstructured data” cylinder that is almost all <strong>Visual</strong>, with a thin <strong>Language</strong> slice labelled 1%. Two encoders feed one <strong>Latent Goal</strong>: a <strong>Goal Image encoder</strong> (a goal image of the robot desk) and a <strong>Language encoder</strong> (the instruction “Push the pink block to the right”). The latent goal, a <strong>Latent Plan</strong> and the <strong>Current state</strong> (a camera image) go into the <strong>Action decoder</strong>, which outputs an <strong>Action likelihood</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: an “Unstructured data” cylinder that is almost all Visual , with a thin Language slice labelled 1%. Two encoders feed one Latent Goal : a Goal Image encoder (a goal image of the robot desk) and a Language encoder (the instruction “Push the pink block to the right”). The latent goal, a Latent Plan and the Current state (a camera image) go into the Action decoder , which outputs an Action likelihood .]", "t": 2760}, {"id": "slide-39-the-math-behind-goal-conditioned-latent-variable-models-frame-60", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models", "title": "Slide 39: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models", "frames": [60, 60], "html": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau, z)</annotation></semantics></math> - arrows: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math> = trajectories; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> = latent plans -&gt; when z continuous, marginalization becomes intractable</p>\n<p>Optimize surrogate objective; variational lower bound of marginal log-likelihood</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Regularization</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reconstruction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(\\tau) \\ge \\underbrace{-KL\\big(q_\\phi(z \\mid \\tau) \\,\\|\\, p_\\theta(z)\\big)}_{\\text{Regularization}} + \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z \\mid \\tau)}\\left[\\log p_\\theta(\\tau \\mid z)\\right]}_{\\text{Reconstruction}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regularization</strong>: are latent plan <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> close to our prior distribution over <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n<li><strong>Reconstruction</strong>: how well does latent plan <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> explain the expert behavior <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "p θ ( τ , z ) - arrows: τ = trajectories; z = latent plans -> when z continuous, marginalization becomes intractable Optimize surrogate objective; variational lower bound of marginal log-likelihood log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) ≥ − K L ( q ϕ ( z ∣ τ ) ∥ p θ ( z ) ) ⏟ Regularization + 𝔼 q ϕ ( z ∣ τ ) [ log p θ ( τ ∣ z ) ] ⏟ Reconstruction Regularization : are latent plan z close to our prior distribution over τ ? Reconstruction : how well does latent plan z explain the expert behavior τ ?", "t": 2821}, {"id": "slide-40-the-math-behind-goal-conditioned-latent-variable-models-conditioned-frame-61", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models <span class=\"own\">[conditioned]</span>", "title": "Slide 40: The Math Behind Goal-Conditioned Latent Variable Models [conditioned]", "frames": [61, 61], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: small copy of the slide 37 architecture diagram.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(\\tau \\mid c) \\ge -KL\\big(q_\\phi(z \\mid \\tau, c) \\,\\|\\, p_\\theta(z \\mid c)\\big) + \\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z \\mid \\tau, c)}\\left[\\log p_\\theta(\\tau \\mid z, c)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>←</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c \\leftarrow (s_c, s_g)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrows]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\phi(z \\mid \\tau, c)</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>Posterior - Plan Recognition</strong>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(z \\mid c)</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>Prior - Plan Proposal</strong>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau \\mid z, c)</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>Action Decoder</strong></li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: small copy of the slide 37 architecture diagram.] log &#8289; p θ ( τ ∣ c ) ≥ − K L ( q ϕ ( z ∣ τ , c ) ∥ p θ ( z ∣ c ) ) + 𝔼 q ϕ ( z ∣ τ , c ) [ log p θ ( τ ∣ z , c ) ] c ← ( s c , s g ) [arrows] q ϕ ( z ∣ τ , c ) = Posterior - Plan Recognition ; p θ ( z ∣ c ) = Prior - Plan Proposal ; p θ ( τ ∣ z , c ) = Action Decoder", "t": 2881}, {"id": "slide-41-my-phd-natural-language-learning-from-play-frame-62", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: My PhD: Natural Language + Learning from Play", "title": "Slide 41: My PhD: Natural Language + Learning from Play", "frames": [62, 62], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a thumbnail above each paper.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>CALVIN: A Benchmark for Language-conditioned Policy Learning for Long-horizon Robot Manipulation Tasks</em>, <strong>Mees</strong> et. al., RA-L 2022 - <strong>Best Paper Award RA-L 2022</strong></li>\n<li><em>What Matters in Language Conditioned Imitation Learning over Unstructured Data</em>, <strong>Mees, et al.</strong> RA-L 2022.</li>\n<li><em>Grounding Language with Visual Affordances over Unstructured Data</em>, <strong>Mees, et al.</strong> ICRA 2023. - <strong>Finalist Best Paper Award ICRA 2023</strong></li>\n<li><em>Affordance Learning from Play for Sample-Efficient Policy Learning</em>, Borja*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et. al., ICRA 2022</li>\n<li><em>Latent Plans for Task Agnostic Offline Reinforcement Learning</em>, Rosete*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et. al., CoRL 2022</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: a thumbnail above each paper.] CALVIN: A Benchmark for Language-conditioned Policy Learning for Long-horizon Robot Manipulation Tasks , Mees et. al., RA-L 2022 - Best Paper Award RA-L 2022 What Matters in Language Conditioned Imitation Learning over Unstructured Data , Mees, et al. RA-L 2022. Grounding Language with Visual Affordances over Unstructured Data , Mees, et al. ICRA 2023. - Finalist Best Paper Award ICRA 2023 Affordance Learning from Play for Sample-Efficient Policy Learning , Borja*, Mees* et. al., ICRA 2022 Latent Plans for Task Agnostic Offline Reinforcement Learning , Rosete*, Mees* et. al., CoRL 2022", "t": 2941}, {"id": "slide-42-conclusion-behavioral-cloning-failures-frame-63", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Conclusion: Behavioral Cloning Failures", "title": "Slide 42: Conclusion: Behavioral Cloning Failures", "frames": [63, 63], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distribution Shift</th>\n<th>Non-Markovian Behavior</th>\n<th>Multimodal Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dagger</td>\n<td>Privileged Observation</td>\n<td>Mixture of Gaussians</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human Gated Dagger -&gt; Detect when to intervene</td>\n<td>History &amp; Causal Confusion</td>\n<td>Autoregressive Discretization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Diffusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Latent Variable Models</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Distribution Shift Non-Markovian Behavior Multimodal Behavior Dagger Privileged Observation Mixture of Gaussians Human Gated Dagger -> Detect when to intervene History & Causal Confusion Autoregressive Discretization Diffusion Latent Variable Models", "t": 2941}, {"id": "end-frame-64", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [64, 64], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-10-shown-early-learning-to-fly-in-swiss-forests-frame-7", "label": "Slide 10 [shown early] : Learning to Fly in Swiss Forests", "html": "this frame shows slide 10 between slides 4 and 5; the lecturer skipped ahead briefly. Slide 10 appears again in order at frame 16."}, {"slide": "slide-6-does-it-work-frames-11-13", "label": "Slide 6: Does it work?", "html": "slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8."}, {"slide": "slide-14-aggregating-online-interventions-frame-27", "label": "Slide 14: Aggregating Online Interventions", "html": "step 5 should read <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\min_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, as in step 4 of slide 13."}, {"slide": "slide-18-why-might-we-still-fail-to-mimick-the-expert-frame-39", "label": "Slide 18: Why might we still fail to mimick the expert?", "html": "slide 19 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 20."}, {"slide": "slide-32-case-study-play-data-frame-54", "label": "Slide 32: Case Study: Play Data", "html": "slide 33 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 32 to 34."}, {"slide": "slide-34-case-study-learning-policies-play-data-frames-55-56", "label": "Slide 34: Case Study: Learning Policies Play Data", "html": "slide 35 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 34 to 36."}]}, {"key": "04", "dir": "week04_rl_1", "date": "Mon 9 Mar 2026", "topic": "Reinforcement Learning I", "summary": "Value-based RL from the ground up: sparse rewards are smooth in expectation, value functions and the Bellman equation, value iteration, policy evaluation and policy iteration (with live gridworld demos), Q-values, tabular Q-learning vs SARSA, then deep RL: DQN with replay and target networks, double DQN, and three ways to take an argmax over continuous actions (discretization, CEM as in QT-Opt, a learned actor as in DDPG).", "key_ideas": ["Bootstrapping: V(s) = r + gamma V(s') lets you evaluate without full rollouts", "Acting with V needs a dynamics model; acting with Q only needs an argmax", "Q-learning is off-policy (max over next actions), SARSA on-policy (the action actually taken) - SARSA learns the safer cliff path", "DQN stabilizers: replay buffer breaks correlations, frozen target network fixes the regression target", "Max over noisy estimates overestimates; double DQN decouples selection and evaluation"], "papers": [{"title": "Evolution Strategies as a Scalable Alternative...", "who": "Salimans et al., 2017", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03864"}, {"title": "Learning Synergies...", "who": "Zeng et al., 2018", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09956"}, {"title": "Precise and Dexterous...", "who": "Luo et al., 2024", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.21845"}], "homework": ["hw4_reinforcement_learning"], "lecture": {"id": "90raNpc11tQ", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 4: Reinforcement Learning I | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "52:33", "description": "Lecture 4 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to RL"}, {"t": 91, "title": "MDP and RL objectives"}, {"t": 216, "title": "Smoothing reward functions"}, {"t": 425, "title": "Value functions"}, {"t": 537, "title": "Bellman equations"}, {"t": 729, "title": "Optimal policies"}, {"t": 858, "title": "Value and policy iteration"}, {"t": 1063, "title": "Policy evaluation"}, {"t": 1294, "title": "Q-functions and model-free RL"}, {"t": 1505, "title": "Q-learning and SARSA"}, {"t": 1757, "title": "Off-policy vs on-policy"}, {"t": 1944, "title": "Deep Q-Networks (DQN)"}, {"t": 2229, "title": "Double DQN"}, {"t": 2351, "title": "Continuous action spaces"}, {"t": 2595, "title": "Cross-Entropy Method"}, {"t": 2800, "title": "DDPG and Actor-Critic"}, {"t": 3044, "title": "Taxonomy of RL methods"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Today, with reinforcement learning, this is where things are going to start getting interesting because we will be moving from um from a world where someone tells us what to do with imitation learning to uh to a world where the agents interact uh themselves to figure out exactly uh how to learn uh policies through trial and error. Um so, uh to recap where we left off in imitation learning, we had an expert give us demonstrations, uh some data set with state action trajectories, and our goal was to uh to find and learn a policy uh that mimics or copies this expert. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And so, we we had this example with self-driving cars uh where, you know, like we observe the road, we pass it through our policy, uh and then we get like some steering decision, for example. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; but the fundamental question uh or limitation here is that uh we are"}, {"t": 60, "text": "entirely dependent on the expert, on the quality of the demonstrations uh that the expert provides, and um imitation learning fundamentally doesn't have a notion of being better than the expert. Uh so, like we can never become better than this uh expert uh trajectory data set that um some unknown expert collected. And so, this is essentially the ceiling we're we'll try uh we're trying to break today uh with reinforcement learning. Um before we get into this, um kind of like a quick recap um for the uh MDP formulation, the Markov decision problem um since this is what um RL essentially uh builds upon. So, we have again this uh uh state space, um the action space, uh transition dynamics, and a reward function. And uh this essentially uh loops this uh um environment uh agent interaction. So, the robot observes some"}, {"t": 120, "text": "state and then it selects We will do questions later, sorry. Selects an action and then we will get a transition together with the reward of this. Um We also defined what we are that we are that we are optimizing in MDPs. Is not working? Oh. Again? Can you hear me now? Is this better? Sorry, what? Can you hear me better? Okay, perfect. All right, thanks for the tip. Um So, yeah, so we mentioned the accumulated return is the reward the agent receives. Um we"}, {"t": 181, "text": "Sorry. Trying to get this. All right. Um and our RL objective is the expected return under the trajectory distribution induced by our policy. And so, the optimal policy is the arc max of of J over here. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And so, um last time we didn't fully answer like how are we actually computing now this this policy and that's what kind of the agenda is for today. Um maybe before we start talking about RL algorithms, I guess like kind of like a funny question, um so if imagine you want to train this RL algorithm and we have this this toy example, right? Where we are driving uh the mountains on this narrow road um and we have let's say a reward of plus one for for staying on the road and a reward of minus one for the car uh"}, {"t": 240, "text": "falling off the cliff, right? And now, in principle, there appears to be um some sort of like discontinuity here in this uh uh between staying and falling on the road, right? And if we try to directly optimize this reward function, um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; let's say it will with with like the the state of the car or something, um then we can't really uh solve this directly with with like our favorite gradient descent solver because uh it's not a it's a non-smooth function, right? So, it's not continuous and it's not differentiable. And um as you can see here, it's like essentially like a Heaviside step function. Uh and so, the gradient is essentially uh zero everywhere except um um so, you don't get any learning signal for this uh except at the boundary where it where you jump like from minus one to to plus one where it's undefined. And um so, why can we like kind of still kind of learn these behaviors even if this uh reward function is like not"}, {"t": 300, "text": "differentiable, right? And so, ultimately in in RL, like um it's all about uh maximizing expectations. Um so, uh in principle, we often talk about like, you know, actions that get high rewards uh in um you know, like discrete actions or so, but um we are typically interested in um optimizing the expected value of those rewards. And here, the kind of the magic lies in in the fact that um the expected values uh can be perfectly continuous in the parameter space um of their distributions uh even if the underlying uh reward function it's uh highly discontinuous. Um and so, this allows us then to use um uh smooth optimization methods like like gradient descent uh to optimize these objectives seemingly uh non-differentiable such this like binary uh rewards for the car driving example. Um so uh in in our case, if we model, let's say the the event of falling"}, {"t": 362, "text": "uh from the road kind of as a Bernoulli variable, um then um you know, the actual although we will have this sharp discontinuity, the expected value of this uh reward with respect to our policy parameters will remain smooth. Uh and here is kind of the visual confirmation of everything I just said. Um so, the the red line is is still kind of the original reward function, the the non-smooth heavy steps function with zero graded gradients almost everywhere. And then the the blue dashed line is the expected expected value which is effectively a convolution of the reward function with the policy's density function. Um and so, kind of the the key maybe the key uh message here is that um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; marginalizing over a stochastic policy uh turns a non-smooth reward function"}, {"t": 422, "text": "into a smooth objective. All right. So, we have now our objective. Uh we have this expected discounted return and we know now that it's smooth in in policy parameters and we can now try to train on this. Great. Um and we know that this is essentially a scalar value that tells us how good our policy will be on average starting from the first [snorts] state. All right. So, it summarizes the kind of entire quality of your policy in this one number. Um but there is kind of like one kind of practical issue when we try to do this, that it's essentially uh if you know, if you look at this term, this means that we have to like expand this and and unroll this to the end of the whole trajectory before we know how good we are doing, right? So on this kind of like you know, it's maybe not ideal for the car because this"}, {"t": 481, "text": "means we would need to let the car fall down the cliff before we actually learn that this is like a bad thing to do. Um and so this is essentially very slow and um kind of like could be catastrophic in some cases, right? So what can we do about this, right? Is there a kind of a better way how we can go around this, right? Can we estimate this without unrolling each episode until the the end? Um and the answer is yes, we can do this. And the key here is essentially the the Markov property that we saw during the NDP lesson um lecture that essentially we can factor the whole trajectory distribution &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; without kind of like depending on on the past to to predict the the future. Um so this leads us directly to to value functions, right?"}, {"t": 541, "text": "So how in order to avoid unrolling the the whole trajectory before getting like this the signal of &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; what is good and bad. Um so the value function is kind of similar as the the the the J scalar from before. It's again kind of like a single number that summarizes how good it is to be in a certain state, but now it is &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; essentially now it's conditioned on on the state rather than average over all the different starting states. So, it's kind of like a local version that says how good is my policy from this specific state onwards. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and um the key idea of why we can use this value function is again the Markov property"}, {"t": 601, "text": "that we can say that the whole future depends only on the current state and not on the full history. Uh so, while essentially conditioning on the current state is sufficient to to summarize everything relevant about this uh the past. Um And now essentially um you can I want to draw your attention to to the last formula here that kind of explains the relationship between those two where the RL objective is essentially the value function averaged over the different starting states. Okay, so how do we compute this efficiently? Um the the insight for this is is pretty uh simple. So, any infinite sum of rewards [clears throat] can be split essentially uh into what we get right now in terms of rewards and"}, {"t": 661, "text": "then like a discounted version of what we will be getting starting tomorrow. And so with this kind of like algebraic algebraic manipulation, we turn an infinite horizon problem into a one-step recursive equation. And this going back to our car example means that um the value of being in a particular position on the mountain road equals the immediate reward I'm getting right now at this one step uh plus some gamma times the value of wherever I will end up next. And so, this means I don't need to simulate the the full rest of my drive. I just need to know how good the next state is, and then the value function handles everything essentially beyond that. Um so, and this is what we call also like bootstrapping. Uh so, we only need to do this kind of one-step look ahead and then use our"}, {"t": 722, "text": "current estimate of of V for for the rest. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; All right. So, now we have a way to evaluate how good a policy is. Um the the value function we computed via the Bellman equation. Um but you know, ultimately we don't want to just evaluate um a policy. We want to find the best one, right? We want to like improve like the policy to find the the optimal one. Um and so, the optimal value function is simply the maximum value achievable from state S over all possible policies. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, going back to the car example, the value is the optimal value function is the value of being at a particular position on the mountain road when driven by the best possible driver. Um so, it's essentially the the the um the best performance or ceiling any"}, {"t": 780, "text": "policy could achieve from that state onwards. Um and the optimal policy, on the other hand, is the one that achieves the ceiling or optimal performance from every starting state. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um and maybe like one insight looking into this formulas to make this tractable is that once we have the the optimal value function, we don't need to search over all policies to act optimally. We can just we just need like one greedy single step look ahead. So essentially [clears throat] to summarize this, the key inside is that this this optimal value function compresses this infinite horizon planning problem into a sequence of one step greedy decisions. So all the kind of long-term reasoning or like anything"}, {"t": 840, "text": "about what will happen many steps into the future is implicitly encoded in this value function. So once we have computed this, we can like act optimally &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; because it's like kind of like local computation. All right. So how do we actually compute the optimal value function in practice? Um like the first answer to this is algorithm called value iteration which is surprisingly simple. We we start by initializing all values to zero assuming every state is worth nothing. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And then we repeatedly apply the Bellman optimality update. Once we convert, we extract the optimal policy with one final greedy step. And like this is essentially there is a conversion proof that because"}, {"t": 903, "text": "this is a contractor contraction operation, it is bound to converge &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; because essentially when you apply when you apply this to two when the you apply the Bellman operator to two different value functions you make [clears throat] them closer than before. Um but maybe like more importantly like let's look at the limitations, right? Uh so one big limitation is that uh this assumes that we have a known uh dynamics model like a transition uh model uh in advance. Um so analytically, we all also need to sweep through all the states. Uh so it's quite heavy uh computationally, right? So uh for every state and every action, uh so which can become quite intractable for larger uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; state spaces. Um and in practice, like we usually use like, you know, some"}, {"t": 961, "text": "tabular representation for these policies. So like the state space uh should be like what discrete and like quite small. Uh so we can like enumerate it on a table. Um here is a small example I programmed or I let Claude program for me. Not going to lie. Um the [clears throat] setup is simple. The green cell is the the goal with a reward of plus one. Um the red cell um over there is is danger with minus one. Then the gray cells are the walls. And the agent is stochastic. 80% of the time uh it moves in the intended direction. Uh 10% uh the other [clears throat] times it drifts in in the either direction. And so you can see here like, you know, uh what we just discussed. You start with with uh total ignorance. Um and then um once you start applying"}, {"t": 1022, "text": "the the Bellman updates, uh the states uh closer to the goal uh start updating. Uh and you can see how it like kind of back back propagates. Um so you can see how the um like visually the information flows through the state backwards through the state state space uh one step at a time. Um, and then the arrows you see are like kind of like the uh optimal um actions for for each state. So, this is like dynamic programming in its purest form. Um, no path planning. No nothing hard-coded, just like repeated local Bellman updates. Okay. So, value iteration works uh by taking the max uh over actions at every step. Um, but this implicitly assumes we're acting optimally uh always, right? Um, but maybe we want to also ask a simpler"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "question uh first um in terms of how good is a specific policy that I already have. Um, and [clears throat] so this is uh policy uh policy evaluation. Um, so it's um it's very similar, if you look at this uh to value iteration, except that uh we replace the max with expectation under the policy. So, instead of picking the best action, we are averaging over whatever uh our policy tells us to take. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so um on its own, policy evaluation like doesn't improve anything, but it's kind of like the first step we need uh towards policy improvement um because we you can't improve a policy if you don't know how good it is. Um, and so this is essentially um what we're going to like look into next. Maybe just to clarify again before we move on to the policy iteration, um I tried to put the both both operations"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "or updates side by side uh so because it's easy to like uh conflate them, uh and they look essentially identical, but as I said, uh we um like uh change the max for for like the expectation here. Um, maybe like on on a side note, uh, also because of this this change also affects the complexity, because now we no longer, um, maximize over, uh, all actions since the policy is fixed. So, now it's going to be like state space, uh, squared. All right. Uh, and now we we can put these two things together um, for policy iteration. So, this is kind of our first way how we can improve, uh, our policies. Uh, so, um, we have our policy evaluation to measure how good our current policy is. Um, and then we have a greedy policy improvement step, um, from value iteration, uh, which tells us how to do better, uh, given a value"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "function, right? And so, here we are like essentially just alternating, uh, between both of them. Um, and, uh, yeah, I here I just kind of I just put the update rule for the policy evaluation, but this is kind of in reality a a loop until convergence. I just didn't put it there. And, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; yeah, so, should be a pretty, uh, self-explanatory. And, um, this is also essentially, um, guaranteed, um, to uh, to to converge. Uh, because um, yeah, like the space of deterministic policies in this case is is is finite. And so, each iteration should like strictly, uh, improve the policy, um, until we have reached the optimal policy. So, in practice, um, it converges pretty fast. Um, here is the same grid world example. Um, and now you"}, {"t": 1262, "text": "can see, uh, that we have like these two different phases between policy evaluation and and and policy improvement. Um and uh on the right you'll see also the the return plot um which [clears throat] should always kind of strictly go go up. Um uh because yeah, as I said before, like every update should like strictly be better. Oops, sorry. Okay, so now we have a kind of a uh set of tools to um for computing optimal policies, right? So, value iteration uh and then policy iteration or like with policy evaluation together. And um they are um beautiful and have like strong convergence guarantees. Um but there is kind of like uh uh a a strong kind of limitation that makes them or at one of of many that makes them difficult to apply for for uh"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "robotics or kind of more uh complex problems. Um And um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so uh essentially the biggest one is that we need to have a dynamics model at test time, right? Um so, this means um that um this expectation uh requires marginalizing um the value function over all uh transition uh distributions. And so, for every candidate action we consider at every time step during the deployment on the real robot, we would need to like kind of calculate all of this. And so, this would take like very very long time uh to compute all this before actually executing an action. So, for example, if we have like a high-frequency robot like uh it might be a little bit difficult. Right? And besides um in robotics like it's often difficult to assume that we have a dynamics model um because the world is messy and um it's like sometimes hard to model. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and as I said before, like even if you"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "have a a model uh querying it at every time step might be slow, especially if you have like a 7° of freedom like continuous action space for your manipulator. So you would need to evaluate infinitely many candidate actions. So and this is kind of the fundamental motivation for Q functions. The key idea is what if we could absorb the dynamics model into the value function itself during training so that a test time we never need to query this. Right? And so during training now the expectation over over P is um computed and the result is is stored in the over the transition sorry over the dynamics model is computed and then the um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the result is stored inside the Q values. Um so this Q SA already uh essentially contains or is baked in the answer to uh if I take the the action A"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "um here what's the best possible future return average over all the different ways that the environment could could respond. Um so the [clears throat] trade-off that we are making here with the Q functions is that it's slightly higher dimensional than the value function, right? So now it's not just conditional on the state but also on the action. Um but and it might be more difficult a bit to learn because of that, but uh for uh model-free robotics where we might not always have um access [clears throat] to a reliable dynamics model this is a price um typically worth paying. Um this is just to reiterate the big benefit um if we actually do like Q value iteration at test time um we um don't have to query the dynamics model. This is just a look-up um uh or an argmax over a fixed function. So we don't need to simulate uh forward because the dynamics model is already"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "encoded in the Q function itself. All right. So, um the Q func- the Q function eliminates the need for the dynamics model at test time, but there is still kind of the problem that we needed um uh for uh during uh training time, right? Um and as I said, like we might even not always have access to the dynamics model. Um so, how can we get rid of it uh entirely so we don't even need it during training? Um and so, Q-learning, this is essentially where Q-learning comes for from, uh and it has like a quite nice answer. We can replace the expectation uh over the dynamics model with a single sample from the actual experience. Uh so, we can, you know, like essentially query the environment and kind of experience like uh what is happening. So, um we can uh instead of calculate um an"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "average of all the possible uh future uh states, we just kind of take an action, uh observe a single sample, and um uh store this transition. And we can create essentially uh this this TD target, um which [clears throat] is kind of like a uh estimate an unbiased estimate of the true Q val- value. And so, um we essentially now, with this learning update, we can um notch our uh TD targets uh towards um the the true uh Q functions um by sampling from the environment. Um treating essentially the world kind of as its own simulator. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um Here is Q-learning on the same uh grid world. And uh maybe you'll you'll see um kind of a"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "a difference from the previous examples, right? So, in the previous examples, because we had this access to the dynamics model, um uh uh like the policies would converge very fast, right? Um now we don't have any dynamics model, and so we have to spend a a long time kind of like exploring and querying the uh like getting samples from the environment um to figure out like what's the best uh policy, right? And so, I I speed this up like quite a bit more than the previous uh demos uh to kind of show you, but um this is for example one trade-off we're making. Now, this is not as sample efficient as the previous uh algorithms that had access to a dynamics model. Okay. So, we have Q-learning, um which is a model-free uh algorithm that learns purely from experience. Um"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "but maybe like I want to ask one question that is kind of subtle, but kind of uh interesting, right? Uh so, we we know that it learns from experience, but does it actually uh matter which policy uh collects the experience we use to update our Q-values? Um so, think about this carefully. Like, is there any dependency on like which &gt;&gt; No, because here we try to act with respect to the optimal policy, right? So, it doesn't matter which behavioral policy collected &gt;&gt; Yes. Exactly. So, as you very correctly said, the the max operator is the key. Uh it selects the best possible action at the next state, regardless of which policy generated uh this transition. Uh and so this means Q-learning is off policy uh and so the data uh that uh we use for the updates can come from any behavior. Uh it can be a random agent, an old version of the policy, uh human"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "demonstrator, or any mixture of those. Uh and that's what makes it also very very powerful. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the learning target always evaluates the optimal action uh independent of how the data was collected. Um So, Q-learning is off policy because the the max operator in the TD target uh always evaluates the optimal action, right? Um and um here is also uh maybe like one variant of this um for on-policy, which is uh the SARSA algorithm, where instead of answering with the max, \"What is the best I could do next?\" uh we ask, \"What am I actually going to do next?\" Um so, it learns the value of the policy um it is currently executing, including all of its exploratory uh mistakes. Um So, it's kind of like a very"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "subtle change, right? Like it's essentially everything remains the same except for for this operation where we replace the max. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and so, this has like profound implications in the behavior um that uh this will learn, this on-policy, uh because the SARSA value um for example, if you have in the self-driving example like a nervous driver, it will uh correctly penalate penalize states where a nervous uh driver is more likely to do uh mistakes. Um while Q-learning will ignore this. Um let's compare both in this kind of like classic uh example cliff walking task, right? So, uh where both agents start in the left and the goal is on the right, and you know, there is like this uh this kind of like uh death uh uh cells, right? And so, you can see that um the"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "uh Q-learning finds the optimal policy, which is the shortest distance uh right across the the edge of this like death uh uh cells, while the SARSA le- learns a much more uh safer policy, where it's like not optimal, but it avoids going like on this ledge, so it doesn't like risk falling into one of these death uh cells. All right. So, this is kind of like uh what we have seen so far, right? So, we have covered some exact solution methods uh like value iteration, policy iteration, and Q-value iteration, uh where um you typically need uh dynamics model uh but for sure for trading uh for testing, you don't need to do it for Q-values, and it's typically like for tabular representations. And then we have started looking into"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "some uh value based methods um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; where we start uh working uh with this like model-free uh algorithms that that learn from interactions. But, still kind of like constrained, right? In in terms of like okay, like very small state space and on grid worlds. Uh maybe like not super applicable for robotics just yet, right? Um and so, now we kind of like think about how we can scale some of these things up, right? Um especially for image observations, right? Uh if we want to um maybe before we go all the way to to robot arms uh like play like video games like you know kind of Atari even then if you want to have like an image observation space this is like really really big right it's like 256 pixels and then sorry values and then like 84 by 84 um pixels for the image and then usually"}, {"t": 1981, "text": "you need to like stack some frames so you can kind of see also what is happening before so it's very very large and you cannot like store this it's astronomically large you cannot store this in kind of like a tabular fashion right and and so this brings us to kind of like one of the um one of the seminal papers that kind of was a breakthrough in deep reinforcement RL which is called DQN probably many of you have already heard of this so this is deep Q learning and core idea is is conceptually quite simple you know you take everything you know about Q learning and replace the table or the tabular representation with a deep convolutional neural network you input a stack of raw game frames pixels and then the output is like a vector of Q values one per discrete action and then you train it with your Q learning updates and that's it and why this is so so seminal is that"}, {"t": 2042, "text": "this is kind of this was the first time where like single algorithm with the same architecture and hyper parameters was able to learn all 49 different like Atari games directly from raw pixels with with no like kind of like handcrafting or game specific engineers also like as a side note this was kind of the year I I I I moved to Freiburg for to study where Martin Riedmiller was a professor and and he just left to to do his sabbatical at a at a small startup called DeepMind. Um And yeah, so that was like great times. Um Yeah, so yeah, maybe I will skip this a little bit for time, but this is kind of the the main algorithm we just covered. Um I want to go over two key innovations"}, {"t": 2100, "text": "that they made in this paper to make this work. Um the first one is the experience replay buffer. Um the idea is simple, instead of discarding each transition after using it only once, they stored it in a replay buffer, and then sampled mini batches from this for training. Um why was this important? Well, because the it helps break temporal correlations, right? If you remember the whole IID thing in the in the first or second lecture we covered, right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; If you know, you input it like consecutive transitions from the same trajectory, then you know, this is bad for learning. In in this way, they could break this temporal correlation, and how half this samples look approximately like IID to the optimizer. And then stabilizes the training."}, {"t": 2160, "text": "And also you can then like uh also replay of course like past transitions several times, and maybe like there are like some, you know, interesting corner cases that then you don't have to like relive, that you can really forget. Um the second challenge here from using like deep networks for Q-learning comes from the correlation between the estimated value and the target value for the estimation. Um so concretely uh um the uh Bellman equation states that the the current value um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um the the current value uh depends on the Q value of the next state um and the current parameterization parameterization of the network. And uh so this means that um an update for the for the current Q value will like essentially alter the um also the the values for the next state uh the next Q state causing instability in"}, {"t": 2220, "text": "learning um and increase uh possibility of divergence. How they solve this is um the trick is pretty simple. Um uh they maintain a separate copy of the uh network with the frozen parameters to compute the target. Uh this is why they call this delayed target network. So typically the second target network is updated either slowly via uh averaging or periodically after a number of fixed steps. Um here are the the results. Um I think the most important one is not just like the raw scores, but the generality that I mentioned before that it was kind of one the first algorithm that could kind of like uh out of the box without any hyper parameter tuning um like achieve like uh super uh very very good performance uh uh in this 47 games. Um"}, {"t": 2281, "text": "DQN works remarkably well, but it has also kind of like a systematic failure mode um which is the overestimation bias. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so the the the idea or the insight here is that like the Q values typically are not perfect, they have noise and errors and um the maximum of a set of noisy estimates is usually greater or actually always greater or equal to the maximum of the true values. And so, here the noise doesn't like the noise doesn't get average out, but it gets amplified. And there was a later paper called DQN, sorry, double DQN that fixes this by using a very simple insight that the bias comes from using the same network to both pick the best value sorry, the best action and evaluate it. And so, then their idea was to decouple them using the online network to select the target network um"}, {"t": 2341, "text": "um to select and then the target network to evaluate. So, you keep the same two networks from DQN, but you just like rewrote them. um Okay. Now, um DQN works very beautifully for discrete action spaces. But in robotics, we are usually because our robot manipulators and so on are continuous, we are interested in continuous action spaces, right? Um so, this kind of now is kind of like a fundamental challenge of how do you apply this in robotics? um because, you know, with this uh with with the arc max over there, we we cannot like enumerate like an infinite action space. Uh so, uh because there is no closed-form solution for this kind of continuous optimization problem, there are like three different ways uh that we will very briefly discuss how we"}, {"t": 2401, "text": "can solve them. Um the first one is discretization. So, we can chop the continuous action action space into a discrete grid and apply DQN directly. Um this is very simple, but um it's exponentially costly in in the dimensions. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, uh like with a 7° of freedom arm, even with a coarse discretization per joint like this will explode combinatorially. The second one is sampling. So, we could like also um do like Monte Carlo uh sampling, draw like N candidate actions, and evaluate uh the Q function for each of the actions, and return kind of the best. And the third one is um to learn the argmax with a second neural network. Um that's going to be a DDPG. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, instead of solving the optimization at test time, we try to uh move this into the training. Um so, each approach is kind of different, but um it tries to answer the same question. Like, uh how do you find"}, {"t": 2461, "text": "the the peak of a uh complex [clears throat] non-linear function over a continuous space. And so, the right choice also depends a little bit on on your specific action uh dimensionality and setup and so on. Um Okay, so here uh I will Here is one example for um spatial discretization, which is kind of quite smart, as it side-steps this um combinational uh explosion problem. Uh where for uh pick and place um in this paper, they um exploited um the geometric structure of the problem um by mapping um the actions to um to the 2D top-down uh observations of the camera um so that instead of a a separate action head, they use a fully convolutional neural network that takes kind of like a height map, so with RGBD um as input, and outputs a dense Q uh value"}, {"t": 2520, "text": "map, so one Q value per pixel uh per rotation angle, and then evaluate this at 16 discrete orientations. Um so, the argmax is over the the map. Uh And here is an example. This is working. Yeah. Uh here is the system in action. Um The cool thing is that it doesn't like learn to reach uh just or to grasp immediately. It uh actually first pushes objects that are cluttered uh to rearrange them into more um graspable configurations and only then attempts to grasp. So, it first singulates and then grasps. And um this pushing before uh grasping strategy was never programmed. Uh nobody told the robot that pushing is useful. So, it kind of discovered this entirely on its own purely from maximizing um the the the reward signal. And um so and this emerges through this"}, {"t": 2582, "text": "Bellman backup where the Q function um for grasping assigns low value to cluttered configurations because grasping is uh uh the grasping success is low there. Okay. So, spatial discretization works nice or beautifully when the action space uh has uh exploitable geometric structure. But what about the general case uh that we care more about when uh we have like let's say a 7° of freedom uh robot arm with like some joint configuration. Um the simplest approach is to use Monte Carlo sampling, right? So, we sample an actions uniformly from the action space, uh evaluate Q for each, and then return the best. Um and this works because um the Q uh Q of pi is a is a smooth neural network function of A. And if we uh sample densely enough, uh we'll always find something close to the true arcmax."}, {"t": 2642, "text": "Um but the uh so although it it kind of works because it's smooth as we said, it requires N forward passes per action selection. It doesn't really scale. And then also like the the quality depends entirely on on the number of samples and on luck. So, there is no guarantee of of finding like the the right arc max. The cross entropy method is a smarter version of the Monte Carlo sampling. So, instead of sampling blindly from the entire action space, we iteratively concentrate samples around the high Q values. So, the algorithm is is pretty simple. We We again sample some actions from a Gaussian. We then keep the some number of elite actions with the highest Q, refit the Gaussian to those, repeat a bunch of times and then return. Um"}, {"t": 2700, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; It And this works very very beautifully. It has kind of two limitations. One that it's unimodal. So, if it's you know, if like it has if it will kind of like find the the peak that you know, you sample closest to. It's not like a guarantee for the true arc max. And then the optimization cost is paid at test time. So, you have to like do like N times K forward passes at every single control step. Right? So, it it really um needs like a lot of time or it can take a a long time during optimization at test time. Um Here is a application of this this paper called QT opt. This was from Google and and Berkeley. This is a demonstration of exactly this"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "algorithm for robotics in a robotics farm. I think they had like around seven cookers or so from 2018. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And here kind of they they made this work for the first time by using Q-learning with the cross-entropy method and a huge replay buffer 580 K real grasps a lot from all these different robots allowing each expensive very expensive real world transition to contribute to to many gradient updates. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Okay. So up until now we've been using now this this kind of cross-entropy method, right? to to solve the arc max problem every time the robot needs to move. But we as we said like we have this n times K forward pass at test time this is just like kind of inefficient. Right?"}, {"t": 2821, "text": "Especially like for high frequency control. Now the third kind of like solution to this problem is DDPG. So deep deterministic policy gradients that tries to learn the arc max instead of instead of searching for it. So the idea is instead of sampling we we're going to introduce a second network called the actor whose entire job is to predict directly predict the action that maximizes the Q function. So now at test time we have collapsed this expensive iterative search into a single forward pass. And so now we have kind of these two networks the critic which is the standard Q network like we saw in DQN. It's still being trained with the TD loss to estimate [clears throat] the value of state action pairs. And then we have the actor which is the new part. Uh we train this specifically to output actions um"}, {"t": 2880, "text": "that the critic says have a high value. Uh so, it takes a state and directly outputs a uh continuous action. Um technically, we train like how we train the actor puts us in in policy gradient territory. Uh we will cover probably this in the next lecture, so I will not go um too much into the details for now. Think of this is just like the actor is trying to maximize the Q uh value and gradient descent does does the rest. Um Yes, maybe two small things uh before we finish um about how we uh update the actor. Um so, we ask the critic in which direction the action space increases uh the Q values, and then we move the actor's parameter in that direction. Um Mathematically, we do this by applying the chain rule uh to the expected return um by taking the gradient of the Q function with respect uh to the action, um and then evaluated at the current"}, {"t": 2940, "text": "actor's output and &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh multiplied by the gradient of the actor with respect to its weights. And then, maybe like one small uh issue is the exploration. So, with DQN, uh we had discrete action uh spaces, but here our uh action space is now continuous, right? And so, we can't do this uh greedy like uh uh like with some probability we pick a random action. Like that would be actually bad because we could thrash our robot if we sample like random joint configurations. Uh so, instead of doing that in DDPG, uh we do uh what it does is uh it adds a Gaussian noise directly to the actor's output at training time. Um so, you take whatever action it recommends and then perturb it a little bit with a uh slightly Gaussian noise to give you like smooth local exploration about the current actors policy. Um All right. So, here is uh how it looks like. Uh there are also"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "some applications for real robots of DDPG. Um it uh works now with high-dimensional observation outputs and continuous actions. We don't need cross-entropy method at test time. Um maybe like one one thing to note note is that it's a notoriously brittle algorithm. Uh it's very sensitive to hyper parameters and doesn't always converge. And as I said, this this noise parameter uh needs to be tuned manually and for each new environment. And so, if you pick it like uh too small, like you might not explore enough. And if you do a too large noise, then it's unstable. All right. To uh recap, um here is kind of like a taxonomy on some trade-offs of the methods uh we worked we we discussed today. Um so, starting from the left, we have the exact methods um that are kind of"}, {"t": 3060, "text": "clean and gives you like convergence guarantees, but are restricted to uh having known dynamics model and working only on kind of like tabular environments or very small state space environments. Um then we've moved to um Q-learning and SARSA um to um uh learning from experience uh rather from uh a known model. Um and we get convergence guarantees as long as we explore long enough. Um but we are still kind of stuck in in kind of small uh toy environments. And then uh at the end then we move to this deep RL with discrete actions like DQN and and double DQN. This is where things get start getting interesting uh where we um, have like our first neural networks, we can encode image observations. Um, we get a replay buffer and so forth. Um, but of course we have now no, uh, guarantees for convergence anymore."}, {"t": 3121, "text": "And we are still stuck with, uh, discrete action spaces. And then finally we've covered deep RL with continuous actions, uh, with DDPG, QT-Opt, um, and cross entropy methods, which can now, uh, kind of handle continuous actions, high dimensional state spaces, um, and we are still off-policy, uh, with a replay buffer. Uh, with this, uh, thank you for attention, um, and, uh, yeah, let's move on to the papers. &gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to RL"}, {"t": 91, "title": "MDP and RL objectives"}, {"t": 216, "title": "Smoothing reward functions"}, {"t": 425, "title": "Value functions"}, {"t": 537, "title": "Bellman equations"}, {"t": 729, "title": "Optimal policies"}, {"t": 858, "title": "Value and policy iteration"}, {"t": 1063, "title": "Policy evaluation"}, {"t": 1294, "title": "Q-functions and model-free RL"}, {"t": 1505, "title": "Q-learning and SARSA"}, {"t": 1757, "title": "Off-policy vs on-policy"}, {"t": 1944, "title": "Deep Q-Networks (DQN)"}, {"t": 2229, "title": "Double DQN"}, {"t": 2351, "title": "Continuous action spaces"}, {"t": 2595, "title": "Cross-Entropy Method"}, {"t": 2800, "title": "DDPG and Actor-Critic"}, {"t": 3044, "title": "Taxonomy of RL methods"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "fHHLmTu9sFk", "title": "Aviral Kumar: How to Replicate the LLM Recipe in Robot Learning [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Aviral Kumar", "duration": "36:05", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Prof. Aviral Kumar (Carnegie Mellon University & Google DeepMind) talks about the challenges and opportunities in replicating the LLM recipe in robot learning.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and motivation"}, {"t": 132, "title": "LLM recipe for robotics"}, {"t": 435, "title": "Robot mid-training via RaC"}, {"t": 966, "title": "Empirical results and scaling"}, {"t": 1333, "title": "Post-training via policy-agnostic RL"}, {"t": 1656, "title": "Discussion and Q&A"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "very excited to have you here today. So, Avil is an assistant professor in the computer science and machine learning departments at Carnegie Melon University uh and also a researcher at Google DeepMind where he works on reinforcement learning uh for uh Gemini foundation models. uh he finished his PhD uh at UC Berkeley uh advised by Sergey Levvin in uh 2023 and uh he's also most famous for creating the the CQL reinforcement learning algorithm. Uh his lab at CMU um the AI and reinforcement learning lab is interested in a broad range of topics ranging from core reinforcement learning algorithms to scaling or rail methods to foundation models to real robots. Uh and with that uh let's give a big applause for Avil. [applause] &gt;&gt; Thanks for the introduction and thanks for for having me. Um yeah, it's uh it's nice to see that there's a class on robot learning which is just so many guest lectures in in in the class. Um I I looked up the schedule so that's quite"}, {"t": 61, "text": "nice to to see. Um okay, so today uh I'll talk a little bit about some of the work we've been doing in my lab at CMU on on sort of um things involving robot learning. um but kind of thinking about this problem from um pro perhaps a slightly different angle to what people think about this right now. So there's you you probably know there's a lot of work on like how on scaling data scaling models and robot learning and so on. But uh in my lab we work a lot on LLMs as well. So we work on like LLMs, we work on fundamental RL algorithms and so on. And like I do feel like there's like a lot of missing pieces from the LLM recipe that we haven't figured out in robot learning or that have no analog in robot learning right now. So I figured I'll talk about some such work that we we have done which kind of tries to address this gap. So if you wanted to actually take uh the recipe of from NLP from vision of of scaling models and and data then what should you be doing um for robots differently? Uh that's going to be basically this talk. Um and I'm going to cover things sort of at a"}, {"t": 120, "text": "higher level than going into too much detail into any work that I'll talk about. Uh but the links to the papers are up there. So if you have any questions you can definitely check them out as well. Um okay so let's get started. So u the broad question which this talk starts with is you know can we replicate the LLM recipe for robots? And to answer this question right the the obvious thing to first ask is like well what's the recipe for LLMs that we have seen work really well. So at a high level like what most people think um makes LLM LLM work well is training on diverse data from the internet. sort of like diverse text data. You kind of get a model out of it and then you would post train or fine-tune this model on top of a limited uh data set uh that exists out there for your particular task. So you post train this general LM to follow instructions. You post train this general LM to solve hard math problems. You find you you postrain it to sort of do really good things on the web etc etc. Uh in the robot land this recipe has resulted in the following sort of kind of analogies uh from from"}, {"t": 182, "text": "all these places. So in robot land if you see what uh today's sort of uh fully datadriven recipes look like they look like training uh what's called a VA policy vision language action policy uh on top of a bunch of diverse robot data and that's what's called pre-training and then you would potentially fine-tune this policy uh with some task specific data limited number of task specific demonstrations uh for post- training so that's what this looks like that's what the current instantiation of this of this protocol in the case of robots Now, if you look at it this way, you know, on the surface, it all looks great, but there's actually a lot of nuances that we missed out on when we instantiated the LLM recipe for robots. So, I'm going to go into that in one by one. So, the first thing um that you know we we look at here is the fact that actually for LLMs as well, the recipe is a bit more nuanced. Um and and this nuance is coming up a lot more clearly these days when you see reasoning models. So, what is the recipe? The recipe looks like pre-training on some"}, {"t": 241, "text": "large text corpora as usual. But rather than going right away from pre-training to post- training, you would end up having the space of of what's called mid-training. Uh so you see all these LLMs have uh sort of these long chains of thought which you know are basically the kinds of pieces of text that you don't ever see on the internet but you imbue into the model from a using a phase that's called mid-training in the middle of pre-training and post- training. So you would end up running pre-training. You'll end up then imbuing the model with some uh amount of behaviors that probably are useful for problem solving downstream which is called mid-training. Uh and it's usually useful for like teaching the the model how to search through different potential solutions for a given prompt that you asked it. And after this process you'll end up running post- training. And this post training is also run via reinforcement learning or R. In the robot land there's no analog for this mid-training phase. Also the post- training phase right now is being done with pure fine-tuning on human demonstration data. Um beyond that you'll also be surprised that pre-training right now in my opinion uh"}, {"t": 301, "text": "the way we do it for robots is kind of not quite correct. Um and I'll not be talking about that. But today's talk I'll talk about mid-training and post- training a little bit more in detail. So just to sort of go into this a little bit more more more more concretely typically the goal of pre-training in the context of language has been to learn from arbitrary data on the internet. It's almost like I'm learning what it means to generate text on the internet. And this model is not technically representing what's what you can think of as an optimal policy or an optimal uh distribution over over next tokens condition on the prefix. Um but however in the case of robots actually if you look at what pre-training looks like it usually looks like training uh the the robot to solve multiple possible tasks multiple possible language instructions specify those tasks uh solve those multiple possible tasks uh in an optimal manner because all the data that we use for robots is an expert data set and this in the LLM context looks a lot more like what's called instruction fine-tuning or it's a part of post- training as opposed to"}, {"t": 360, "text": "pre-training. So this already sort of is very different for robots than for LLMs and there's a question mark as to whether the the the robot recipe can even work here. Um I have some thoughts on that and some of the work that's ongoing in my lab actually touches upon this question. I'll not be talking about this part a lot but what I talk about is the second two parts today uh in this talk. So the second part uh in mid training as I mentioned earlier um the goal of mid-training for LLMs is to imbue them with certain behaviors or skills that can help you improve autonomously during post training. Um so this is often referred to as the phase of improving exploration for robot uh for for for language but there's no such phase for robots and likewise the post- training part uh for LLMs is done by autonomous rollouts. So you let an LLM uh tackle a particular prompt or a problem autonomously. you often make simulators for this process and uh or and or or you just deploy an LM in the real world and you now learn from the feedback that you get in this case. So you kind of like learn to learn useful behaviors by actually interacting with the target environment that you care"}, {"t": 421, "text": "about. So what I'm going to do today uh you know is talk a little bit about what would this mid-training and post training look like for robots um in this in in this particular you know if you believe in this particular framing of the high level paradigm of how we train these models. So I'll talk a little bit about some work that we did on what you can call um mid training for robots. Um so it's uh this idea of like actually collecting data a bit differently so that you imbue the model with interesting skills or behaviors such that when you deploy the policy on the robot it can do really well. Um and when you do this you actually see a lot of benefits of of mid-training that we see in language models. So if you if you're familiar with this idea of test time scaling or thinking for longer you kind of see something similar for for robots when you when you do this. uh and you also get like a lot more data efficiency and a lot better generalization which are exactly the things that mid-training gives uh to you in in the language land. So I'll talk about that a bit and then I'll talk a little bit about how can you do post- training for robots in a in a uh in an efficient manner and here we'll"}, {"t": 480, "text": "talk about how you can deploy offthe-shelf generalist robot policies on a real robot and let them learn from their autonomous data. So we had this algorithm that's called policy agnostic RL which is a very simple method um which can help you like just improve your um your your pre-trained robot policy uh online uh in a sample efficient manner and we did get some one of the first results for fine-tuning of these general VAS in the real world using this work. So that's the sort of the rough outline for for today's talk. Um okay so let's start with this idea of mid training. Um and uh I'm going to talk a little bit about what exactly I mean by recovery and correction data that you see up here. If you're interested in the paper, the the paper um sort of citation is listed here. You can you can just search it up on archive and you you'll find it. Cool. Okay. So currently, as I mentioned a couple of times already, the current robot data collection protocol for training all of these large generalist policies has been to basically just ask humans to tell you"}, {"t": 541, "text": "operate the robot to show expert demonstrations on solving some tasks and you just like collect tons of data like this. So for instance, like this is a particular task from the Aloha Unleashed paper where they had this bmanual arm trying to like close Ziploc bags uh and and you kind of had to like put stuff inside of it and close the lid together. Um another task that we particularly look at in our work is um I guess you know this video will will come to it is this task from like of shirt handling where a human teleoperator here it's my student Jan he's like actually teleyoperating this bmanual setup to basically insert this hanger into a t-shirt. So that's one of the tasks we consider in in our work and here it's all fully teleyoperated. So this is just a demo that you would collect and you would train your policy on top of this sort of a demo um uh to just imitate all the actions that you see up here. Um so that's the that's the kind of current robot data collection protocol. Now this data collection protocol is pretty good but the problem here is that it's it's actually suboptimal for generalization."}, {"t": 600, "text": "So if you think about uh the analogy between this data collection protocol and LLMs you can think of this as of like expert data collection as just showing to you what the optimal solution for a given math problem is. However, if you wanted to generalize, if you want your robot to be robust enough to deal with different t-shirts with potentially different uh different uh sort of orientations of things with potentially its own failures that it might encounter when it goes and solves the task, you need the you need the policy to learn how to kind of recover from these behaviors and that notion of recovery we also see in LLMs like we see LLMs not directly get to an answer for a given question but all these reasoning models actually go and explore and search through different branches of solutions. to then come up with the right answer. And that's kind of the same thing that we do here. So we basically collect a lot of counterfactual data in some ways through this process of human interventions that scales uh recoveries and corrections. So I'll just show you a um you know show you a video. So what we"}, {"t": 660, "text": "do is we train a base policy on some expert demonstrations and now we deploy this base policy and collect some amount of recovery and correction data according to a particular protocol that we describe in this work and the idea is this recovery and correction data can be thought of as backtracking and and self-correction style data for LLMs and if you train the policy on top of this data it'll learn to be more robust to the particular target task. So just to show you an example uh let me play this video. Um so here right now if you see there's it's an autonomous policy that is trained on some limited amount of um like uh like demonstration data. So it's a pre-trained autonomous policy. Uh you can already see it's a bit slow and it's not as fluid as or not not as flexible as what humans would do and it tries to sort of solve the task but then you'll see that it puts the hanger in front of the t-shirt and now the human takes over. Um they potentially intervene here. they show um what the correction should be and terminate the intervention and just having this notion of like uh"}, {"t": 721, "text": "of like you know recovering from the out of distribution state that the policy was going towards. Let me just bring it back here. Um so just oh oops yeah so just recovering from the state from which uh the policy was making a mistake at so it's the state at which the hanger is in front of the t-shirt recovering from it and showing the correct rule uh which is recovering back and showing the correct thing is good enough uh for showing you how to backtrack. So what we do in this work is we collect a lot of this kind of data and we train on top of this data uh for this mid-training phase. So we have a pre-trained policy learned by imitation learning. We do mid training on top of this kind of recovery and correction style data. So just to show you uh sort of some some uh kind of illustration for what this is schematically doing and and what the main insight is. The main insight for enabling this thing to work is the fact that recovery u is actually easier than correctly solving the task for many tasks we care about. And the same thing is true for language models as well. So if you think about it, here's a"}, {"t": 781, "text": "schematic cartoon of a long horizon task like t-shirt hangar insertion where you started off with an initial state. The initial state can be anywhere across the scene. So it's a larger larger uh area disk here. And now as you solve a task, you're kind of slowly narrowing down this the volume of states you're you're going to you're going to visit. So this cone or the cone like or a funnel like shape basically tells you that you are kind of narrowing down the set of good states that your demos might possess. And here are potential subtasks. So subtask could mean grabbing a hanger from the from the shelf potentially inserting one side of the collar and the hanger potentially inserting the other side of the collar hanger and so on. So these are potential subtasks inside of this particular task. So what we talk about here is the fact that in general if your policy might end up going away from your good demonstration data like in this black line, you would want the the human to show you how to recover back to the indistribution manifold inside of this funnel and then correctly"}, {"t": 840, "text": "solve the task from there on. And here if recovering is easier because you're recovering to sort of a broader set of states then you can easily learn this recovery behavior uh from data that you collect from limited data that you have because it's a easy tole learn behavior in a way. So once you do this the policy now when you train the policy and you deploy it the policy might end up recovering a lot correcting itself a lot and eventually we'll end up solving the task a lot better. So the idea is to scale up these notions of recovery and correction. If you're more mathematically inclined, here is like a simple sort of model which we discussed in the paper and I'm not going to talk about it too much today because there I don't have too much time to talk about it. But if you're interested in the math here, you can actually do some very simple math to explain why this thing works. So I like to call this idea the the P to the K model. So the idea is the same. Let's say the robot attempts to complete a particular subtask with probability and it succeeds with probability P. then in the probability with with which it fails meaning 1 minus P if it can recover to a"}, {"t": 901, "text": "particular indistribution point um then you can potentially get the the robot to the ability to try again and trying again will now gives you uh trying again will give you the ability to reduce your compounding errors over a whole roll out so think of it as if your LLM or if the LLM analogy makes more sense to you think of it as your if your LLM goes and tries to attack a certain problem with a certain solution ution approach and it fails, it can go back and try something else and in in effect you're kind of reducing the total errors that you might get as opposed to just like cloning the correct answer where if you failed you would just like never recover at all. So this is a simple way to kind of amplify your model's uh ability to solve long horizon tasks by exploring in the right way by by recovering at the right times by continuing at the right times and so on and that's sort of the main main idea of this method. Uh and this also gives us data that covers some of the unseen regions of the state space and that's a good thing as well because it gives you coverage over behaviors that the policy"}, {"t": 960, "text": "might encounter but human teleoperators will never encounter. Okay. So um so just to sort of show you some more uh results here. Um so another thing that we find here is because of this recovery and retry behavior you kind of get something that is equivalent to test time scaling uh for for robots. So in in in typical language modeling test time scaling is usually referred to the process of using longer chains of thought for thinking for longer. So a lot of these open AI 01 03 models and basically at this point every single LLM is a thinking LLM where it would go and do a lot of computation before it spits out an answer for you. In this way, in the case of robots, this approach of like scaling recovery and correction data for mid training kind of gives you this ability to uh to kind of do something similar but not in some language space or not in some thought space but rather at a low-level action space. So you're basically um um you're you're you're basically showing the policy um some ability to sort of like"}, {"t": 1020, "text": "just do multiple actions over time to succeed better on the particular task. So as a result for it what we found was that our particular approach which is rack it used a simple flow matching transformer policy which is not too big but on within just 5 hours of data collection um you know where we scaled up like this recovery and correction style data we could get a very good success rate u which is comparable to sort of the the best prize result on such a task of shirt hanging from Aloha unleashed where they had a smaller policy but then they had way more data um like they had 89 hours of data for for training And this was just pure expert data. We didn't have that much data at all. It's like way more efficient like an order of magn more efficient overall. And also compared to sort of this result from bite dance where they had a 4B vision language action model like a lot of pre-training and so on they also still used a lot more data but again got a much lower success rate than ours. So it's a lot more data efficient and parameter efficient which is precisely what you see uh with LLMs which can reason as"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "well uh in a similar manner. And to just show you some uh examples here, what we found was that again as you so if you remember if if you're familiar with sort of the the OpenAI uh blog post on the when the 01 model came out in 20 2024, they had this plot which showed that performance improves as a function of your uh of your COT length or or of your test time compute is what they say. We had a similar plot that we could draw for robots now where the x-axis is the number of recoveries your policy does within the course of a rollout when it's deployed and the y-axis is success rate which again you see there's a linear uh linear fit uh on top of it. So it's quite intriguing that you can kind of draw similar analogies from like coot length and and and RL in in and language models the sort of robot learning policies and and again um sort of like but here the the length of a coot is replaced by number of recoveries in an episode that you that you run. So that's sort of the the main result here. Um so let me show you some some quick examples. So like um the first policy up"}, {"t": 1142, "text": "top here. So these are these are three uh this is a video. I'll I'll play this video in a second. This is uh these are three different types of policies that we compare. So first one is the rack policy or this recovery and correction style policy trained on recovery and correction style data. Uh the second one is this edgy dagger policy. So it's a it's a it's an approach for doing human interventions uh within the rollout but again it's not uh structured in the form of recovery and correction. So there's no backtracking and correction. It just shows you, it just lets the human intervene whenever and the full demos is just like expert data training a BC policy on top of it. And the three columns that you see here are like three different camera views. So for the left gripper, the right gripper and the the third person camera view here. So you'll see that the rack policy ends up making many retries and corrections until it succeeds. Uh the edgit tagger policy will end up making some sort of corrective attempts because it's trained on some kind of intervention data but doesn't quite generalize. And you'll see that the BC policy just is you know just fails overall. So I'll just play this"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "video here and you can see all the three different um different policies acting. So they are kind of trying to solve the same sort of task um putting the hanger inside of this t-shirt. Um and you'll see that they um and all at 1x speed. So nothing uh nothing nothing more than that. So you'll see that this uh policy ends up um generalizing a lot better and some annotations will come up as well um here. So um so the first side is probably okay for uh for most policies. So if you see the rack policy ends up retrying itself uh because it did the thing incorrectly um and you'll see it it eventually succeeds. So it can it is persistent in a way um and and that's a good thing. And the BC policy on the other hand the lowest row actually just gets stuck uh overall um yeah uh and then it doesn't know how to succeed. Whereas the rack policy whenever it starts to get into this like weird scenario because of this recovery and connection data it would like learn"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "to like kind of go back and retry and that's like a like a good thing overall. So the the the edg policy just failed and the imitation learning policy also just failed overall and this is not a cherrypicked example this we see many of these uh in our in our work. So actually I was going to if I had more time I would have shown you uh a few more videos but the website for this paper has a lot of videos on it um including many other tasks which again have this notion of like multiple possible like sort of fairly long horizon tasks that would require otherwise a lot of data for training. That's kind of the general flavor of tasks we study here. And in all of these cases we again find that the recovery and collection style behaviors actually help you a lot uh overall. So I just had this slide on looking at some videos but uh maybe in the interest of time I'll u I'll skip this but I I can come back to it uh once I I finish quickly uh after this. Uh cool. Okay. The second thing I wanted to just very quickly mention because I"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "probably just have two three minutes so is this work on post training uh with policy agnostic RL. Um it's uh a paper that was run by my student Max um here at CMU. And the idea was kind of something basically about just very standard motivation around post training that you know we'll want to ultimately um take these generalist policies and then fine-tune them very quickly on a on a on a real robot uh with real world interaction. And currently there's not a lot of work that tries to do this at scale with general policies. Like there's a lot of work including my own work from my PhD days which tries to do this for like single task policies but not for generalist policies at all. So the problem now with generalist policies is that a lot of the RL machinery that we have built over the last few years has it really stably only works with like gshian policies which have fewer parameters and this is actually even true to this state like there's a lot of work on on doing the RL with flow matching policies RL with diffusion"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "policies but a lot of that work actually is quite unstable if you actually run it on a robot. uh this work is a bit old so this is from like a year ago so back then none of those things even existed but even to this day a lot of the work that has come out on fine-tuning general policies tends to be quite quite fragile overall so what we wanted to do in this work was to improve uh any general policy class um including diffusion policies maybe transformer policies flow matching policies etc and build a stable recipe for doing so uh overall um so what we found was actually the fact that if you if If you go and ask the question of why is fine-tuning offthe-shelf policies hard, you'll realize that a lot of the times when you do RL, you care about improving some notion of a value function uh on top of uh actions that were coming from your policy. So you kind of want to train the policy to maximize this value function or Q function in some way or the other. In some ways this kind of a objective requires computing the gradient of the policy of the policies"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "predictions with respect to its own parameters. So grad five of pi of s uh is what you kind of care about computing. Uh computing this is actually quite problematic for basically many policies. So it's problematic for diffusion policies because you could go through that propagate through the diffusion process. For flow matching policies, the same thing for transformers, you cannot even compute this because often times the action is sampled. So there's no differentiable connection altogether. So you have to rely on reinforce style objectives to do so. So this is something that is quite hard for this is a very different very hard reward loss function for the policy to maximize. So what we did do in this work or the main idea in this work was to sort of go and and try to decompose the policy improvement process into two steps. Um the first step was to just run this gradient process on top of action particles themselves. So rather than updating the policies parameters with respect to the gradient of maximizing"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "the Q function, we just find how to modify the action vector itself if I wanted to maximize the Q value. So I call that the optimized actions. And the second step was to distill these optimized actions back to your policy using a standard maximum likelihood style loss. And that loss seems to work because most of the models out there most of the generative models or most of machine learning models out there are just trained to uh train with maximum likelihood. So distilling some notion of actions into a particular model is just nothing but maximizing likelihood which should be easy which should be possible at least for most policies out there. So all we do is we we we take so the actual algorithm is a bit more complicated but we take the base policy produce different actions run some sort of optimization nonparametrically on those actions and then just distill those or behavior clone those actions uh into the base policy and this allows us to be agnostic to the policy because all of the optimization or all of the RL is happening on actions and not on the policy itself. &gt;&gt; Mhm. So this is sort of a very simple"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "idea that actually has been around for some time. But the nice thing in that we realized in this work was that the very same simple instantiation of hyperparameters, algorithm design choices, etc. that we did for one possible policy class actually works well for other policy classes as well for different uh algorithms for online fine-tuning or online learning of the of the policy. So just one uh kind of interesting result and I'll stop after this was that we kind of got some very good results on uh on fine-tuning open VA. So this work was done like a year ago at this point. We are working on a on a version of this with like more modern policies and more modern value functions right now in our lab. But this work was done a year ago where tasks were still very simple. But then this was the first result I know where we could fine-tune you know the the state-of-the-art PLA model at that time which is open VLA without needing any kind of demonstrations just autonomously on the fly on a real robot and this was done in 40 minutes only and that was quite good because uh 40 minutes of online is quite easy with like a"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "terrible robot like a widow X on here that you see now robots are obviously much better and also like VAS are much better so this number can be probably reduced down to 10 minutes uh if you use the right approach so that sort of quite quite intriguing and we also had some results on single task policies uh which were slightly different from so open was a transformer policy this is this is diffusion policy so again the same approach uh sort of works for both of these classes of methods with the same hyperparameters so it's a very simple idea that works really well uh when you go to scale uh these policies um so yeah that's sort of this approach um that's basically what I had so the main takeaway if you want uh to take away from this talk should be that we should think about we should think very carefully about how can we how we take these generalist model training recipes and instantiate them for robots pre-training is still a big open question uh in my mind uh I don't think we are doing pre-training correctly right now but mid-training uh meaning data scaling in the right way and fine-tuning or post- trainining in the right way both can take you a long way"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "so thinking about what learning lessons can be transferred from one to the other can be quite helpful here uh yeah and I'll stop there I'll yeah happy to take any questions u thanks Thanks for listening to my very uh rushed true talk. Uh [applause] thanks for the amazing talk. Um any questions from the audience? Yes, I have one question here. &gt;&gt; Can you hear me? Yeah. Um thank you for for a good talk. Um two questions. The first one is you you mentioned a couple of times um that you have used that the current pre-training landscape for robotic foundation models uh isn't where it needs to be. Can you maybe elaborate on that at the high level uh and maybe point us towards some some works from your lab that that propose an alternative or or improved solution for those. Um and then if I if I can ask a second question um could you quickly"}, {"t": 1742, "text": "elaborate on um again on on the differences in interventions between your work in the mid training um versus a method like HG dagger and and repeat again why these led to to your superior results. &gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good question. So let me go into the edg dagger question first. Um yeah, I think I I didn't have time to really cover this much in much detail, but I can show you some um so yeah, I think this picture probably makes uh the easiest sense. So if you can can you see my m mouse pointer actually or um no uh &gt;&gt; is my mouse pointer visible on the screen? Um &gt;&gt; or maybe &gt;&gt; he doesn't know the answer. &gt;&gt; Yes. Yes, we can see it. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. Okay. Um yeah. So I guess maybe Oh, okay. Great. So I can also draw on the screen. That's nice. Okay. So all I was saying was so here you see like you know the the our approach kind of you know when once the policy is outside it you kind of recover and then you go and connect. So you are kind of going back and correcting itself. Edg dagger would just take you"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "from this state and like would just go and solve the task right away. Um I think that's sort of the the the typical notion of the of the phrase edgy dagger. Now I would also say that edgy dagger the way it's been described in literature doesn't actually mention precisely a very concrete recipe for how humans should intervene. But the typical notion has been well if you're going slightly out of distribution you should just like solve the task from right there. But what we do here is we kind of go back and try to solve the task from there on. So for example if you were um uh oh okay so if you were uh let me just go back here somewhere. Okay. So uh basically the idea here would be uh if you're if you're uh if you're if you're starting to make a mistake uh what edg would try to do is would just try to directly like if you're starting to make a mistake it'll just try try to directly go in uh directly insert the hanger into the t-shirt whereas what we want to do is we want to kind of go back to a previous state uh which is quite subtle here but actually very visible in other demonstrations where look like you know"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "rather than going directly into the t-shirt from here on we would kind of go and move the robot arm quite away from on the t-shirt and then try to insert it from there on. So basically this notion of recovery not being present in a g dagger is the big issue uh overall. That's the the main difference. I think some of the other tasks that we have actually show this example quite quite better uh overall um uh in uh on on the website but recovery not being present is the main thing. Uh and that gives you the gains because now if you just try to correct yourself uh then you might not know how to correct yourself right away but recovery is kind of strictly easier. Um so that's the main gap that that comes in. Um the other the the other question I think for pre-training we don't have a work out yet. We want to put something out in the next one or two months. But the rough idea being that I think that like when you think about LLM pre-training we think about just modeling the distribution of all possible text. We don't train blas on modeling the distribution of all possible actions that a robot could ever take. We just train it on the narrow slice of actions that are quote unquote optimal or expert like because human"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "teleoperators show them that. So maybe we should rethink what uh like really a a BLA policy should model when you when you train it uh with during pre-training or what kind of data should you be training it on. It shouldn't be like expert data or just modeling one mode of behavior. Maybe it should be multiple modes of behavior because that's what an LM models. But again this is sort of the rough intuition. We still have something in flight on that. I don't think that much of the community has been thinking about this but that's the graph inclusion. Um, &gt;&gt; hey, I have &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Um, &gt;&gt; yeah. Yeah, please go ahead. Yeah, &gt;&gt; sorry. Um, I have another quick question. You mentioned that it performs better than a dagger, and I'm just wondering why like is it because it learns the recovery method itself when um the action goes out of distribution like off like off policy intervention. And you mentioned back propagation like how exactly does it actually learn the recovery behavior from like you helping it like push it back to a previous state"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "like uh like as opposed to dagger. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So basically the the the rough thing is like it learns this recovery behavior. So it learns to recover as opposed to just like going back um going going to like trying to like solve a task from from some place that it has already reached. So it does learn the recovery behavior. And the second question I think I missed uh what the second part was &gt;&gt; um why exactly does this like you mentioned back propagation at some point like how exactly does is this better than basically learning how to um get to another state that like from like why exactly is the recovery behavior better than just like directly um finding uh like a policy that reaches a further state directly &gt;&gt; from a good question. I think the the reason why that happens is because the volume of states that you can recover to is often a lot higher for most natural robotic tasks than the volume of states that uh that you see in the see from from there on. So imagine this like for example like if a uh so I guess you can"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "actually show to this theoretically as well. So if you let's say go to an OD state uh trying to come back to the manifold of the of good states which is shown by this funnel is actually harder because there's very few states from there on that are still valid states for the task. But going back is sort of easier because there's many more states that you can go back to and you can continue from there on. So if you think about like uh like trying to imitate data which shows you how to correct yourself eventually you have a narrow space of states that you need to somehow hit. So any mistakes in the correction in in mimicking the correction part would be problematic for you. Whereas if you just need to learn how to recover, if there's many very many very many many many states from which you to which you can recover uh because you're going backwards in the task, you can actually like restart uh from there on and succeed uh a lot better because there's many more states that you can go back to which are within your like uh good demo states uh in this particular setting. So it's just a volume argument roughly. uh overall &gt;&gt; uh can you explain a little more about how the local and global optimization"}, {"t": 2100, "text": "part works in the fine-tuning for robots? &gt;&gt; Yes. Uh I can talk about that. I also unfortunately had to run. So maybe let's make this the last question and then I can I'm happy to always answer any questions over email or if you have any thoughts just feel free to email me. Uh unfortunately just had like a lot of uh I I have to go to somebody's false exams actually so I just want to not be super late to that. But um but yeah, I I think the rough idea for global optimization is you would just sample n different actions and you would now take uh compute arg max over the q function over those actions and filter the ones that attain high enough q values. So let's say you have n uh I guess here you have k actions you would retain m actions that have the highest q value and then local optimization is just take a gradient step on that action with respect to the q function. So you want a to move in the direction that maximizes Q of SA and you do this via gradient descent. That's the simple approach. &gt;&gt; Cool. Uh thank you so much I let's let's thank again for speak for just making it super uh tough in between things"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "[applause] but yeah &gt;&gt; see you soon. &gt;&gt; Yeah. See you. Bye."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and motivation"}, {"t": 132, "title": "LLM recipe for robotics"}, {"t": 435, "title": "Robot mid-training via RaC"}, {"t": 966, "title": "Empirical results and scaling"}, {"t": 1333, "title": "Post-training via policy-agnostic RL"}, {"t": 1656, "title": "Discussion and Q&A"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "CMU & Google DeepMind"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 09.03.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (77 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week04_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/90raNpc11tQ (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_aviral_kumar.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 4: Reinforcement Learning I</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 09.03.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 4: Reinforcement Learning I Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 09.03.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-recap-imitation-learning-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Recap: Imitation Learning", "title": "Slide 1: Recap: Imitation Learning", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Data</strong>: Given trajectories collected by an “expert” “demonstrations” <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} = \\{(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_t)\\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a Dataset cylinder holding <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_t)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span></li>\n<li><strong>Goal</strong>: Learn a policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that “imitates” the expert’s behavior -&gt; ❌ <strong>we can’t get better than the expert!</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: road image -&gt; <strong>Policy</strong> -&gt; the same road image with a forked arrow.]</span></p>", "text": "Data : Given trajectories collected by an “expert” “demonstrations” 𝒟 = { ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s t ) } [Figure: a Dataset cylinder holding ( s 1 , a 1 , … , s t ) ] Goal : Learn a policy π θ that “imitates” the expert’s behavior -> ❌ we can’t get better than the expert! [Figure: road image -> Policy -> the same road image with a forked arrow.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-recap-markov-decision-process-frame-3", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: Recap: Markov Decision Process", "title": "Slide 2: Recap: Markov Decision Process", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Define by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M = \\langle \\mathcal{S}, \\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{P}, \\mathcal{R} \\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: loop between the environment (globe) and the robot (arm): “state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math>” to the robot, “action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math>” back to the environment.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>: State space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>: Action space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t \\in \\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>: Transition probability, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} \\sim \\mathcal{P}(\\cdot|, s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>: Reward function, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r: \\mathcal{S} \\times \\mathcal{A} \\to \\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t = R(s_t, a_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Define by M = ⟨ 𝒮 , 𝒜 , 𝒫 , ℛ ⟩ [Figure: loop between the environment (globe) and the robot (arm): “state s t , reward r t ” to the robot, “action a t ” back to the environment.] 𝒮 : State space, s t ∈ 𝒮 𝒜 : Action space, a t ∈ 𝒜 𝒫 : Transition probability, s t + 1 ∼ 𝒫 ( ⋅ | , s t , a t ) ℛ : Reward function, r : 𝒮 × 𝒜 → ℝ , r t = R ( s t , a t , s t + 1 )", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-3-recap-the-learning-objective-frame-4", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Recap: The Learning Objective", "title": "Slide 3: Recap: The Learning Objective", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Accumulated reward an agent receives: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G = \\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} r_t</annotation></semantics></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G = \\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Expected Return: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} r_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Optimal Policy: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi J(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: trajectories fanning out from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> to end states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{T_1}, \\dots, s_{T_4}</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Accumulated reward an agent receives: G = ∑ t = 0 T − 1 r t or G = ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t Expected Return: J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 r t ] or J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t ] Optimal Policy: π * = arg &#8289; max &#8289; π J ( π ) [Figure: trajectories fanning out from s 0 to end states s T 1 , … , s T 4 .]", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-4-arent-sparse-rewards-non-smooth-frames-5-6", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth?", "title": "Slide 4: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth?", "frames": [5, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mi>+</mi><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">success</mtext></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s,a) = \\begin{cases} +1 &amp; \\text{if } s \\in S_{\\text{success}} \\\\ -1 &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a narrow road cut into a cliff above a misty drop; the road is marked +1 (green) and the drop -1 (red).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 6) adds a plot, “The Gradient Problem (Rewards: -1 or +1)”: Reward Value against State / Action Space from -4 to 4. The reward is a step: -1 for negative inputs, +1 for positive ones. Both flat parts are labelled “Gradient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla r = 0</annotation></semantics></math>”; the jump at 0 is labelled “Discontinuity: Gradient Undefined”.]</span></p>", "text": "r ( s , a ) = { + 1 if s ∈ S success − 1 otherwise [Figure: a narrow road cut into a cliff above a misty drop; the road is marked +1 (green) and the drop -1 (red).] [Second build (frame 6) adds a plot, “The Gradient Problem (Rewards: -1 or +1)”: Reward Value against State / Action Space from -4 to 4. The reward is a step: -1 for negative inputs, +1 for positive ones. Both flat parts are labelled “Gradient ∇ r = 0 ”; the jump at 0 is labelled “Discontinuity: Gradient Undefined”.]", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-5-arent-sparse-rewards-non-smooth-the-expectation-is-smooth-frame-7", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth? <span class=\"own\">[the expectation is smooth]</span>", "title": "Slide 5: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth? [the expectation is smooth]", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mi>+</mi><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">success</mtext></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s,a) = \\begin{cases} +1 &amp; \\text{if } s \\in S_{\\text{success}} \\\\ -1 &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same cliff-road photo.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">fall</mtext><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a = \\text{fall} \\mid s) = p_\\theta(s)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Bernoulli</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\mid s \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p_\\theta(s))</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">smooth!</mtext></munder><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta) = \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{(a \\sim \\pi_{(\\theta)})}[r(a)]}_{\\text{smooth!}} = p_{(\\theta)}(s) \\cdot (-1) + \\big(1 - p_{(\\theta)}(s)\\big) \\cdot (+1)</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "r ( s , a ) = { + 1 if s ∈ S success − 1 otherwise [Figure: the same cliff-road photo.] π θ ( a = fall ∣ s ) = p θ ( s ) a ∣ s ∼ Bernoulli ( p θ ( s ) ) J ( θ ) = 𝔼 ( a ∼ π ( θ ) ) [ r ( a ) ] ⏟ smooth! = p ( θ ) ( s ) ⋅ ( − 1 ) + ( 1 − p ( θ ) ( s ) ) ⋅ ( + 1 )", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-6-arent-sparse-rewards-non-smooth-landscape-frame-8", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth? <span class=\"own\">[landscape]</span>", "title": "Slide 6: Aren’t Sparse Rewards Non-Smooth? [landscape]", "frames": [8, 8], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the cliff-road photo and a plot, “Differentiable Landscape in Parameter Space”: Value against Policy Parameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> from -4 to 4. Red solid line, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> (Non-Smooth): a step from -1 to +1 at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Blue dashed line, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi_\\theta}[r(s,a)]</annotation></semantics></math> (Smooth!): an S-curve rising smoothly from -1 to +1 through 0.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: the cliff-road photo and a plot, “Differentiable Landscape in Parameter Space”: Value against Policy Parameter θ from -4 to 4. Red solid line, r ( s , a ) (Non-Smooth): a step from -1 to +1 at θ = 0 . Blue dashed line, 𝔼 a ∼ π θ [ r ( s , a ) ] (Smooth!): an S-curve rising smoothly from -1 to +1 through 0.]", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-7-dont-wait-for-the-cliff-frames-9-10", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Don’t Wait for the Cliff", "title": "Slide 7: Don’t Wait for the Cliff", "frames": [9, 10], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t\\right] = \\mathbb{E}_{s_0, a_0, s_1 \\dots}\\left[r_0 + \\gamma r_1 + \\gamma^2 r_2 + \\cdots\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the cliff-road photo (+1 on the road, -1 off the cliff).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 10) adds a brace under the sum:]</span> Can we estimate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> <strong>without unrolling</strong> each episode to the end?</p>", "text": "J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t ] = 𝔼 s 0 , a 0 , s 1 … [ r 0 + γ r 1 + γ 2 r 2 + ⋯ ] [Figure: the cliff-road photo (+1 on the road, -1 off the cliff).] [Second build (frame 10) adds a brace under the sum:] Can we estimate J ( π ) without unrolling each episode to the end?", "t": 481}, {"id": "slide-8-value-function-frame-11", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Value Function", "title": "Slide 8: Value Function", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Expected discounted return starting from state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math> and following policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> thereafter</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞</mo></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"infix\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^\\pi(s) = \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\gamma^k r_{t+k} \\;\\middle|\\; s_t = s, a_t \\sim \\pi(\\cdot \\mid s_t), s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a_t)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>RL objective is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> averaged over starting states</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{s_0 \\sim p(s_0)}\\left[V^\\pi(s_0)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Expected discounted return starting from state s and following policy π thereafter V π ( s ) = 𝔼 [ ∑ k = 0 ∞ γ k r t + k | s t = s , a t ∼ π ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) , s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a t ) ] RL objective is V averaged over starting states J ( π ) = 𝔼 s 0 ∼ p ( s 0 ) [ V π ( s 0 ) ]", "t": 541}, {"id": "slide-9-bootstrapping-recursive-shortcut-frame-12", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Bootstrapping: Recursive Shortcut", "title": "Slide 9: Bootstrapping: Recursive Shortcut", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<p>Decompose infinite sums of rewards:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><munder><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">this is </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>V</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">!</mtext></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t R(s_t, a_t) = R(s_0, a_0) + \\gamma \\underbrace{\\sum_{t \\ge 1} \\gamma^{t-1} R(s_t, a_t)}_{\\text{this is } V\\text{!}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bellman Expectation Equation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^\\pi(s) = \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi(\\cdot \\mid s),\\, s&#39; \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s, a)}\\left[r(s, a) + \\gamma V^\\pi(s&#39;)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace]</span> value now = immediate reward + discounted value of next state; <strong>allows value estimation without full rollouts</strong></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Decompose infinite sums of rewards: ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t R ( s t , a t ) = R ( s 0 , a 0 ) + γ ∑ t ≥ 1 γ t − 1 R ( s t , a t ) ⏟ this is V ! Bellman Expectation Equation V π ( s ) = 𝔼 a ∼ π ( ⋅ ∣ s ) , s ′ ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s , a ) [ r ( s , a ) + γ V π ( s ′ ) ] [brace] value now = immediate reward + discounted value of next state; allows value estimation without full rollouts", "t": 601}, {"id": "slide-10-optimal-policy-frame-13", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Optimal Policy", "title": "Slide 10: Optimal Policy", "frames": [13, 13], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: small environment / robot loop diagram (state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math> / action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math>).]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Optimal Value:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s) = \\max_\\pi V^\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Optimal Policy:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi J(\\pi) = \\arg\\max_\\pi \\mathbb{E}_{s_0 \\sim p(s_0)}\\left[V^\\pi(s_0)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^*(s) = \\arg\\max_a \\mathbb{E}_{s&#39; \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s, a)}\\left[r(s, a) + \\gamma V^*(s&#39;)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "[Figure: small environment / robot loop diagram (state s t , reward r t / action a t ).] Optimal Value: V * ( s ) = max π V π ( s ) Optimal Policy: π * = arg &#8289; max π J ( π ) = arg &#8289; max π 𝔼 s 0 ∼ p ( s 0 ) [ V π ( s 0 ) ] π * ( s ) = arg &#8289; max a 𝔼 s ′ ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s , a ) [ r ( s , a ) + γ V * ( s ′ ) ]", "t": 722}, {"id": "slide-11-value-iteration-frames-14-15", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Value Iteration", "title": "Slide 11: Value Iteration", "frames": [14, 15], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How can we compute the optimal policy in practice?</li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Initialize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>∀</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0(s_t) = 0 \\quad \\forall s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 0, 1, 2 \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> until <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_{s_t} |V_{k+1}(s_t) - V_k(s_t)| &lt; \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>: Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s_t) \\leftarrow \\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)} V_k(s_{t+1})\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Extract policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^*(s_t) = \\arg\\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)} V^*(s_{t+1})\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 15) adds:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction</li>\n<li>❌ Assumes dynamics model</li>\n<li>❌ Needs to sweep through all states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(|\\mathcal{S}|^2 |\\mathcal{A}|)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>❌ State space must be discrete &amp; small</li>\n</ul>", "text": "How can we compute the optimal policy in practice? Initialize V 0 ( s t ) = 0 ∀ s t ∈ 𝒮 For k = 0 , 1 , 2 … until max &#8289; s t | V k + 1 ( s t ) − V k ( s t ) | < ϵ : Update V k + 1 ( s t ) ← max &#8289; a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) V k ( s t + 1 ) ] Extract policy π * ( s t ) = arg &#8289; max &#8289; a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) V * ( s t + 1 ) ] [Second build (frame 15) adds:] ✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction ❌ Assumes dynamics model ❌ Needs to sweep through all states O ( | 𝒮 | 2 | 𝒜 | ) ❌ State space must be discrete & small", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-12-value-iteration---gridworld-frames-16-26", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Value Iteration - Gridworld", "title": "Slide 12: Value Iteration - Gridworld", "frames": [16, 26], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Value Iteration - Gridworld - watching <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s)</annotation></semantics></math> emerge”, run live across eleven frames (the lecturer plays it, resets it and plays it again). Update rule shown at the top: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s) \\leftarrow \\max_a \\mathbb{E}_{s&#39; \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s, a)}[r(s, a) + \\gamma \\cdot V_k(s&#39;)]</annotation></semantics></math>. A 5x5 grid with a Goal (+1) cell top right, a Danger (-1) cell below it and four wall cells. Status panel: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> (discount) 0.90, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (threshold) 0.001. Actions up / down / left / right, “Stochastic: 80% intended, 10% each perpendicular”. Every cell starts at 0.000 (iteration 0); one mid-run frame shows iteration 19 with max <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta V|</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.00348.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Converged state shown (iteration <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> = 22, max <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta V|</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.00059, “Converged! max <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta V| &lt; \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>”); each cell gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and the greedy action:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>col 1</th>\n<th>col 2</th>\n<th>col 3</th>\n<th>col 4</th>\n<th>col 5</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>row 1</td>\n<td>1.186 →</td>\n<td>1.384 →</td>\n<td>1.591 →</td>\n<td>1.851 →</td>\n<td>Goal +1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row 2</td>\n<td>1.029 ↑</td>\n<td>wall</td>\n<td>1.384 ↑</td>\n<td>wall</td>\n<td>Danger -1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row 3</td>\n<td>0.892 ↑</td>\n<td>wall</td>\n<td>1.186 ↑</td>\n<td>1.029 ←</td>\n<td>0.590 ←</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row 4</td>\n<td>0.780 ↑</td>\n<td>0.865 →</td>\n<td>1.013 ↑</td>\n<td>wall</td>\n<td>0.536 ↓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row 5</td>\n<td>0.680 ↑</td>\n<td>0.751 ↑</td>\n<td>0.853 ↑</td>\n<td>0.737 ←</td>\n<td>0.625 ←</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Value Iteration - Gridworld - watching V * ( s ) emerge”, run live across eleven frames (the lecturer plays it, resets it and plays it again). Update rule shown at the top: V k + 1 ( s ) ← max &#8289; a 𝔼 s ′ ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s , a ) [ r ( s , a ) + γ ⋅ V k ( s ′ ) ] . A 5x5 grid with a Goal (+1) cell top right, a Danger (-1) cell below it and four wall cells. Status panel: γ (discount) 0.90, ϵ (threshold) 0.001. Actions up / down / left / right, “Stochastic: 80% intended, 10% each perpendicular”. Every cell starts at 0.000 (iteration 0); one mid-run frame shows iteration 19 with max | Δ V | = 0.00348.] [Converged state shown (iteration k = 22, max | Δ V | = 0.00059, “Converged! max | Δ V | < ϵ ”); each cell gives V * ( s ) and the greedy action:] col 1 col 2 col 3 col 4 col 5 row 1 1.186 → 1.384 → 1.591 → 1.851 → Goal +1 row 2 1.029 ↑ wall 1.384 ↑ wall Danger -1 row 3 0.892 ↑ wall 1.186 ↑ 1.029 ← 0.590 ← row 4 0.780 ↑ 0.865 → 1.013 ↑ wall 0.536 ↓ row 5 0.680 ↑ 0.751 ↑ 0.853 ↑ 0.737 ← 0.625 ←", "t": 961}, {"id": "slide-13-policy-evaluation-frame-27", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Policy Evaluation", "title": "Slide 13: Policy Evaluation", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How good is our policy?</li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Initialize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>∀</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0(s_t) = 0 \\quad \\forall s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 0, 1, 2 \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> until <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_{s_t} |V_{k+1}(s_t) - V_k(s_t)| &lt; \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>: Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s_t) \\leftarrow \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi(\\cdot \\mid s_t)}\\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)}[V_k(s_{t+1})]\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction</li>\n<li>⚠️ Cheaper than value iteration <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(|\\mathcal{S}|^2)</annotation></semantics></math>, still sweep through all states</li>\n<li>❌ Assumes dynamics model</li>\n<li>❌ State space must be discrete &amp; small</li>\n</ul>", "text": "How good is our policy? Initialize V 0 ( s t ) = 0 ∀ s t ∈ 𝒮 For k = 0 , 1 , 2 … until max &#8289; s t | V k + 1 ( s t ) − V k ( s t ) | < ϵ : Update V k + 1 ( s t ) ← 𝔼 a ∼ π ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) [ V k ( s t + 1 ) ] ] ✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction ⚠️ Cheaper than value iteration O ( | 𝒮 | 2 ) , still sweep through all states ❌ Assumes dynamics model ❌ State space must be discrete & small", "t": 1022}, {"id": "slide-14-value-iteration-vs-policy-evaluation-frame-28", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Value Iteration vs Policy Evaluation", "title": "Slide 14: Value Iteration vs Policy Evaluation", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Value Iteration:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s_t) \\leftarrow \\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)} V_k(s_{t+1})\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Policy Evaluation:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s_t) \\leftarrow \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi(\\cdot \\mid s_t)}\\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)}[V_k(s_{t+1})]\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[The only difference, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_a</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi(\\cdot \\mid s_t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is highlighted in red.]</span></p>", "text": "Value Iteration: V k + 1 ( s t ) ← max a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) V k ( s t + 1 ) ] Policy Evaluation: V k + 1 ( s t ) ← 𝔼 a ∼ π ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) [ V k ( s t + 1 ) ] ] [The only difference, max &#8289; a versus 𝔼 a ∼ π ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) , is highlighted in red.]", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-15-policy-iteration-frame-29", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Policy Iteration", "title": "Slide 15: Policy Iteration", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Alternates between finding out how good we are and trying to be even better</li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Initialize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0(s_t)</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j = 0, 1, 2, \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> until <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{j+1} = \\pi_j</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Policy Evaluation</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s_t) \\leftarrow \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi_j(\\cdot \\mid s_t)}\\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)}[V_k(s_{t+1})]\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Policy Improvement</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{j+1}(s_t) = \\arg\\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)} V^{\\pi_j}(s_{t+1})\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at Policy Evaluation]</span> this just one step of eval iteration until convergence</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under Policy Improvement]</span> find best action according to one-step look-ahead</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Alternates between finding out how good we are and trying to be even better Initialize π 0 ( s t ) arbitrarily ∀ s t ∈ 𝒮 For j = 0 , 1 , 2 , … until π j + 1 = π j Policy Evaluation V k + 1 ( s t ) ← 𝔼 a ∼ π j ( ⋅ ∣ s t ) [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) [ V k ( s t + 1 ) ] ] Policy Improvement π j + 1 ( s t ) = arg &#8289; max &#8289; a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) V π j ( s t + 1 ) ] [arrow at Policy Evaluation] this just one step of eval iteration until convergence [brace under Policy Improvement] find best action according to one-step look-ahead", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-16-policy-iteration---gridworld-frames-30-32", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Policy Iteration - Gridworld", "title": "Slide 16: Policy Iteration - Gridworld", "frames": [30, 32], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Policy Iteration - Gridworld - evaluate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_j</annotation></semantics></math>, improve greedily, repeat”, run live across three frames, on the same 5x5 grid as slide 12 (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> 0.90, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> 0.001). Header formulas: Eval: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{k+1}(s) \\leftarrow \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi_j}[r(s,a) + \\gamma \\cdot \\mathbb{E}_{s&#39; \\sim P}[V_k(s&#39;)]]</annotation></semantics></math>; Improve: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{j+1}(s) = \\arg\\max_a [r(s,a) + \\gamma \\cdot \\mathbb{E}_{s&#39; \\sim P}[V^{\\pi_j}(s&#39;)]]</annotation></semantics></math>. Status shows outer step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math>, inner evaluation step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, phase (Policy Evaluation / Policy Improvement) and the number of policy changes. A side plot, “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi_j) = V^{\\pi_j}(s_0)</annotation></semantics></math> - Monotonically ↑ by improvement theorem”, rises as a staircase from about -0.1 at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> to about 0.65 by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=2</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Frame 30: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math> = 0, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> = 29, Evaluation of the initial arbitrary policy: most values around -0.095, a few positive cells near the goal.]</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Frame 31: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math> = 1, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> = 31, 15 policy changes; the top rows already match the optimal values.]</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Frame 32: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math> = 4, 0 policy changes, “Converged! <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{j+1} = \\pi_j</annotation></semantics></math>”. The values and arrows are identical to the value-iteration result on slide 12 (1.186, 1.384, 1.591, 1.851 along the top row, …).]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Policy Iteration - Gridworld - evaluate π j , improve greedily, repeat”, run live across three frames, on the same 5x5 grid as slide 12 ( γ 0.90, ϵ 0.001). Header formulas: Eval: V k + 1 ( s ) ← 𝔼 a ∼ π j [ r ( s , a ) + γ ⋅ 𝔼 s ′ ∼ P [ V k ( s ′ ) ] ] ; Improve: π j + 1 ( s ) = arg &#8289; max &#8289; a [ r ( s , a ) + γ ⋅ 𝔼 s ′ ∼ P [ V π j ( s ′ ) ] ] . Status shows outer step j , inner evaluation step k , phase (Policy Evaluation / Policy Improvement) and the number of policy changes. A side plot, “ J ( π j ) = V π j ( s 0 ) - Monotonically ↑ by improvement theorem”, rises as a staircase from about -0.1 at j = 0 to about 0.65 by j = 2 .] [Frame 30: j = 0, k = 29, Evaluation of the initial arbitrary policy: most values around -0.095, a few positive cells near the goal.] [Frame 31: j = 1, k = 31, 15 policy changes; the top rows already match the optimal values.] [Frame 32: j = 4, 0 policy changes, “Converged! π j + 1 = π j ”. The values and arrows are identical to the value-iteration result on slide 12 (1.186, 1.384, 1.591, 1.851 along the top row, …).]", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-17-limitations-of-vs-for-robotics-frame-33", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Limitations of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(s)</annotation></semantics></math> for Robotics", "title": "Slide 17: Limitations of V ( s ) for Robotics", "frames": [33, 33], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Acting with V(s), requires <strong>querying Dynamic Models at test time,</strong> for every state-action combination</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">need dynamics model at test time!</mtext></munder><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^*(s_t) = \\arg\\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\underbrace{\\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)} V^*(s_{t+1})}_{\\text{need dynamics model at test time!}}\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: illustration of a robot arm in state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_t</annotation></semantics></math> fanning out many candidate motions into a thought cloud, “Simulate all actions (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>) (requires dynamics model) to find highest value (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math>)”, then “Execute best action (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^*_t</annotation></semantics></math>)”. A timeline below: Sense -&gt; Planning (long) -&gt; Act, from Start to End.]</span></p>", "text": "Acting with V(s), requires querying Dynamic Models at test time, for every state-action combination π * ( s t ) = arg &#8289; max a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) V * ( s t + 1 ) ⏟ need dynamics model at test time! ] [Figure: illustration of a robot arm in state S t fanning out many candidate motions into a thought cloud, “Simulate all actions ( P ) (requires dynamics model) to find highest value ( V )”, then “Execute best action ( a t * )”. A timeline below: Sense -> Planning (long) -> Act, from Start to End.]", "t": 1320}, {"id": "slide-18-q-values-frame-34", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Q-Values", "title": "Slide 18: Q-Values", "frames": [34, 34], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s_t)</annotation></semantics></math>: Expected discounted return starting from state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, acting optimally afterwards</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s_t) = \\max_a \\left[r(s_t, a) + \\gamma \\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)}\\left[V^*(s_{t+1})\\right]\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q^*(s_t, a)</annotation></semantics></math>: Expected discounted return from state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, committing to action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> first, acting optimally afterwards</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">still needs dynamics model during training</mtext></munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></munder><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q^*(s_t, a) = r(s_t, a) + \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{s_{t+1} \\sim P(\\cdot \\mid s_t, a)}}_{\\text{still needs dynamics model during training}}\\left[\\gamma \\max_{a_{t+1}} Q^*(s_{t+1}, a_{t+1})\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[thinking-face emoji next to “<strong>training</strong>”]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^*(s_t) = \\max_a Q^*(s_t, a)</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "V * ( s t ) : Expected discounted return starting from state s t , acting optimally afterwards V * ( s t ) = max a [ r ( s t , a ) + γ 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) [ V * ( s t + 1 ) ] ] Q * ( s t , a ) : Expected discounted return from state s t , committing to action a first, acting optimally afterwards Q * ( s t , a ) = r ( s t , a ) + 𝔼 s t + 1 ∼ P ( ⋅ ∣ s t , a ) ⏟ still needs dynamics model during training [ γ max a t + 1 Q * ( s t + 1 , a t + 1 ) ] [thinking-face emoji next to “ training ”] V * ( s t ) = max a Q * ( s t , a )", "t": 1381}, {"id": "slide-19-q-value-iteration-test-time-frame-35", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Q-Value Iteration Test Time", "title": "Slide 19: Q-Value Iteration Test Time", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<ul>\n<li>No dynamics model needed during <strong>test time</strong>!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^*(s_t) = \\arg\\max_a Q^*(s_t, a)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same robot-arm illustration, now with a single arrow to a box “Pre-computed Q-function (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> direct lookup) - Find highest value (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math>)” and “Execute best action (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^*_t</annotation></semantics></math>)”; the timeline shrinks to Sense -&gt; Act with almost no planning.]</span></p>", "text": "No dynamics model needed during test time ! π * ( s t ) = arg &#8289; max a Q * ( s t , a ) [Figure: the same robot-arm illustration, now with a single arrow to a box “Pre-computed Q-function ( Q ( s , a ) direct lookup) - Find highest value ( V )” and “Execute best action ( a t * )”; the timeline shrinks to Sense -> Act with almost no planning.]", "t": 1440}, {"id": "slide-20-q-learning---removing-the-dynamics-model-frame-36", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Q-Learning - Removing the Dynamics Model", "title": "Slide 20: Q-Learning - Removing the Dynamics Model", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Dynamics model might be unknown, too complex or slow</li>\n<li>The Solution? Replace Expectation with Experience</li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Execute action and observe transition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>TD target <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> terminal</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old estimate</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><munder><munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TD target</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new experience sample</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s_t, a_t) \\leftarrow (1 - \\alpha) \\underbrace{Q(s_t, a_t)}_{\\text{old estimate}} + \\alpha \\underbrace{[\\text{TD target}]}_{\\text{new experience sample}}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> learning rate</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Dynamics model might be unknown, too complex or slow The Solution? Replace Expectation with Experience Execute action and observe transition ( s t , a t , r t , s t + 1 ) TD target = r ( s t , a t ) + γ max &#8289; a Q ( s t + 1 , a ) , r ( s t , a t ) if s t + 1 terminal Q ( s t , a t ) ← ( 1 − α ) Q ( s t , a t ) ⏟ old estimate + α [ TD target ] ⏟ new experience sample [arrow at α ] learning rate", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-21-q-learning---gridworld-frames-37-39", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Q-Learning - Gridworld", "title": "Slide 21: Q-Learning - Gridworld", "frames": [37, 39], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Q-Learning - Gridworld - learn <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> from experience, no dynamics model”, run live across three frames. Update rule: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>←</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s_t, a_t) \\leftarrow (1-\\alpha) \\cdot Q(s_t, a_t) + \\alpha \\cdot [r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\cdot \\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a)]</annotation></semantics></math>. Same 5x5 grid; each cell is split into four triangles holding the Q-value of each action, with an arrow for the best one and a white dot for the agent. Status: episode, step, total steps, TD error <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\delta|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (lr) 0.50, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> 0.90, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy. Hyperparameter sliders: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.50, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decay = 0.97. Buttons: Play / Pause, Step, Fast x50, Reset. A side plot, “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">best </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = Q(s_0, \\text{best } a)</annotation></semantics></math> - Improves as Q converges”, shows max <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s_0, a)</annotation></semantics></math> per episode.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Frames 37-38: episodes 166 and 188 (about 3,400-3,700 total steps), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy 0.050; the Q-values have spread from the goal (about 0.8-0.97 next to it) and the <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> curve has risen and plateaued around 0.3.]</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Frame 39: after a reset, episode 4, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy 0.885: almost all Q-values are still near -0.02, and only the cell next to the goal has learned a large value (0.50).]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: an interactive web demo, “Q-Learning - Gridworld - learn Q ( s , a ) from experience, no dynamics model”, run live across three frames. Update rule: Q ( s t , a t ) ← ( 1 − α ) ⋅ Q ( s t , a t ) + α ⋅ [ r ( s t , a t ) + γ ⋅ max &#8289; a Q ( s t + 1 , a ) ] . Same 5x5 grid; each cell is split into four triangles holding the Q-value of each action, with an arrow for the best one and a white dot for the agent. Status: episode, step, total steps, TD error | δ | , α (lr) 0.50, γ 0.90, ϵ -greedy. Hyperparameter sliders: α = 0.50, ϵ -decay = 0.97. Buttons: Play / Pause, Step, Fast x50, Reset. A side plot, “ J ( π ) = Q ( s 0 , best a ) - Improves as Q converges”, shows max Q ( s 0 , a ) per episode.] [Frames 37-38: episodes 166 and 188 (about 3,400-3,700 total steps), ϵ -greedy 0.050; the Q-values have spread from the goal (about 0.8-0.97 next to it) and the J ( π ) curve has risen and plateaued around 0.3.] [Frame 39: after a reset, episode 4, ϵ -greedy 0.885: almost all Q-values are still near -0.02, and only the cell next to the goal has learned a large value (0.50).]", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-22-off-policy-vs-on-policy-frames-40-41", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Off-Policy vs On-Policy", "title": "Slide 22: Off-Policy vs On-Policy", "frames": [40, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Does it matter <strong>which policy</strong> collects the transition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> used to update our TD target?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TD target</mtext><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">what happened</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><munder><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">max assumes future optimal action, regardless of past behavior</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{TD target} = \\underbrace{r(s_t, a_t)}_{\\text{what happened}} + \\gamma \\underbrace{\\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a)}_{\\text{max assumes future optimal action, regardless of past behavior}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 41) adds:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Off-Policy learns from <strong>“any”</strong> data, even sub-optimal behavior -&gt; reuse experience (Experience Replay Buffer)</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Does it matter which policy collects the transition ( s t , a t , r t , s t + 1 ) used to update our TD target? TD target = r ( s t , a t ) ⏟ what happened + γ max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ) ⏟ max assumes future optimal action, regardless of past behavior [Second build (frame 41) adds:] Off-Policy learns from “any” data, even sub-optimal behavior -> reuse experience (Experience Replay Buffer)", "t": 1680}, {"id": "slide-23-off-policy-vs-on-policy-q-learning-vs-sarsa-frame-42", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Off-Policy vs On-Policy <span class=\"own\">[Q-Learning vs SARSA]</span>", "title": "Slide 23: Off-Policy vs On-Policy [Q-Learning vs SARSA]", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Q-Learning Off Policy target:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TD target</mtext><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><munder><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">What is the best I could do next?</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{TD target} = r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\underbrace{\\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a)}_{\\text{What is the best I could do next?}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SARSA On-Policy target:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">TD target</mtext><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><munder><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">What am I actually going to do next?</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{TD target} = r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\underbrace{Q(s_{t+1}, a_{t+1})}_{\\text{What am I actually going to do next?}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Q-Learning Off Policy target: TD target = r ( s t , a t ) + γ max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ) ⏟ What is the best I could do next? SARSA On-Policy target: TD target = r ( s t , a t ) + γ Q ( s t + 1 , a t + 1 ) ⏟ What am I actually going to do next?", "t": 1741}, {"id": "slide-24-q-learning-vs-sarsa---gridworld-frames-43-49", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Q-Learning vs SARSA - Gridworld", "title": "Slide 24: Q-Learning vs SARSA - Gridworld", "frames": [43, 49], "html": "<ul>\n<li>SARSA learns “safer” behaviors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive “cliff walking” web demo run live across seven frames (training, convergence, reset). Two side-by-side 4x12 grids: <strong>Q-Learning (Off-Policy)</strong>, “TD target = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r + \\gamma \\cdot \\max_a Q(s&#39;, a)</annotation></semantics></math> - best I <em>could</em> do”, and <strong>SARSA (On-Policy)</strong>, “TD target = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r + \\gamma \\cdot Q(s&#39;, a_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> - what I <em>will</em> do”. Start S bottom left, goal bottom right, and the whole bottom row between them is a cliff of skull cells. Above the grids, a plot “Episode return - Q-learning vs SARSA during training” with the caption: “During training: SARSA is more cautious (avoids cliff-edge states). After convergence: Q-Learning scores higher (shorter path), SARSA scores lower (safer detour).” Over about 150 episodes both returns climb from around -120 toward about -15, with deep dips whenever an agent falls off the cliff.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Converged Q-Learning: “13 steps (risky)”, a path along the row directly above the cliff]</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Converged SARSA: “17 steps (safe)”, a detour along the top row, far from the cliff]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "SARSA learns “safer” behaviors [Figure: an interactive “cliff walking” web demo run live across seven frames (training, convergence, reset). Two side-by-side 4x12 grids: Q-Learning (Off-Policy) , “TD target = r + γ ⋅ max &#8289; a Q ( s ′ , a ) - best I could do”, and SARSA (On-Policy) , “TD target = r + γ ⋅ Q ( s ′ , a t + 1 ) - what I will do”. Start S bottom left, goal bottom right, and the whole bottom row between them is a cliff of skull cells. Above the grids, a plot “Episode return - Q-learning vs SARSA during training” with the caption: “During training: SARSA is more cautious (avoids cliff-edge states). After convergence: Q-Learning scores higher (shorter path), SARSA scores lower (safer detour).” Over about 150 episodes both returns climb from around -120 toward about -15, with deep dips whenever an agent falls off the cliff.] [Converged Q-Learning: “13 steps (risky)”, a path along the row directly above the cliff] [Converged SARSA: “17 steps (safe)”, a detour along the top row, far from the cliff]", "t": 1801}, {"id": "slide-25-rl-taxonomy-frame-50", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: RL Taxonomy", "title": "Slide 25: RL Taxonomy", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Exact Solution Methods</th>\n<th>Value-based Methods</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Value Iteration, Policy Iteration, Q-Value Iteration</td>\n<td>Q-Learning (off-policy), SARSA (on-policy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction</td>\n<td>✅ Converges to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q^*</annotation></semantics></math> (tabular, sufficient exploration)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ Sweep through all states</td>\n<td>✅ No dynamics model needed (model-free)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ Needs dynamics model</td>\n<td>✅ Learns from interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ State space must be discrete &amp; small</td>\n<td>❌ State space must be discrete &amp; small</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ Tabular representation</td>\n<td>❌ Tabular representation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Exact Solution Methods Value-based Methods Value Iteration, Policy Iteration, Q-Value Iteration Q-Learning (off-policy), SARSA (on-policy) ✅ Convergence proof via Bellman contraction ✅ Converges to Q * (tabular, sufficient exploration) ❌ Sweep through all states ✅ No dynamics model needed (model-free) ❌ Needs dynamics model ✅ Learns from interaction ❌ State space must be discrete & small ❌ State space must be discrete & small ❌ Tabular representation ❌ Tabular representation", "t": 1861}, {"id": "slide-26-the-curse-of-dimensionality-scaling-to-image-observations-frame-51", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: The Curse of Dimensionality: Scaling to Image Observations", "title": "Slide 26: The Curse of Dimensionality: Scaling to Image Observations", "frames": [51, 51], "html": "<p><strong>The Curse of Dimensionality: Scaling to Image Observations</strong></p>\n<p>Atari game observation space <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>256</mn><mrow><mn>84</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>84</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 256^{84 \\times 84 \\times 4}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "The Curse of Dimensionality: Scaling to Image Observations Atari game observation space = 256 84 × 84 × 4", "t": 1921}, {"id": "slide-27-case-study-deep-q-learning-dqn-frame-52", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Case Study: Deep Q-Learning (DQN)", "title": "Slide 27: Case Study: Deep Q-Learning (DQN)", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<ul>\n<li>First time a single algorithm learned to play 49 different games from raw pixels</li>\n<li>Launched Deep Reinforcement Learning in 2013</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: screenshot of the title, author list and abstract of <em>Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning</em> (Mnih, Kavukcuoglu, Silver, Graves, Antonoglou, Wierstra, Riedmiller; DeepMind Technologies). The abstract describes a convolutional network trained with a variant of Q-learning on raw pixels, applied to seven Atari 2600 games.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at Martin Riedmiller]</span> Personal note: Martin just had started his sabbatical at a small startup called DeepMind, when I joined Uni Freiburg</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the 49-game result is from the later Nature paper (Mnih et al., 2015); the 2013 paper in the screenshot covers seven games.]</span></p>", "text": "First time a single algorithm learned to play 49 different games from raw pixels Launched Deep Reinforcement Learning in 2013 [Figure: screenshot of the title, author list and abstract of Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning (Mnih, Kavukcuoglu, Silver, Graves, Antonoglou, Wierstra, Riedmiller; DeepMind Technologies). The abstract describes a convolutional network trained with a variant of Q-learning on raw pixels, applied to seven Atari 2600 games.] [arrow at Martin Riedmiller] Personal note: Martin just had started his sabbatical at a small startup called DeepMind, when I joined Uni Freiburg [Note: the 49-game result is from the later Nature paper (Mnih et al., 2015); the 2013 paper in the screenshot covers seven games.]", "t": 1981}, {"id": "slide-28-dqn-algorithm-frame-53", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: DQN Algorithm", "title": "Slide 28: DQN Algorithm", "frames": [53, 53], "html": "<p><strong>Algorithm 1: Deep Q-learning with Experience Replay</strong> (pseudocode from the paper, transcribed as a list)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initialize replay memory <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to capacity <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Initialize action-value function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with random weights</li>\n<li>for episode <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 1, M</annotation></semantics></math>:\n<ul>\n<li>Initialise sequence <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1 = \\{x_1\\}</annotation></semantics></math> and preprocessed sequence <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_1 = \\phi(s_1)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 1, T</annotation></semantics></math>:\n<ul>\n<li>With probability <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> select a random action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise select <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\max_a Q^*(\\phi(s_t), a; \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Execute action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> in emulator and observe reward <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math> and image <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} = s_t, a_t, x_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and preprocess <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_{t+1} = \\phi(s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Store transition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\phi_t, a_t, r_t, \\phi_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Sample random minibatch of transitions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\phi_j, a_j, r_j, \\phi_{j+1})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_j = r_j</annotation></semantics></math> for terminal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_j = r_j + \\gamma \\max_{a&#39;} Q(\\phi_{j+1}, a&#39;; \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> for non-terminal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Perform a gradient descent step on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(y_j - Q(\\phi_j, a_j; \\theta))^2</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the greedy action should be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_a</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_a</annotation></semantics></math>; this slip is in the original paper’s pseudocode.]</span></p>", "text": "Algorithm 1: Deep Q-learning with Experience Replay (pseudocode from the paper, transcribed as a list) Initialize replay memory 𝒟 to capacity N Initialize action-value function Q with random weights for episode = 1 , M : Initialise sequence s 1 = { x 1 } and preprocessed sequence ϕ 1 = ϕ ( s 1 ) for t = 1 , T : With probability ϵ select a random action a t , otherwise select a t = max &#8289; a Q * ( ϕ ( s t ) , a ; θ ) Execute action a t in emulator and observe reward r t and image x t + 1 Set s t + 1 = s t , a t , x t + 1 and preprocess ϕ t + 1 = ϕ ( s t + 1 ) Store transition ( ϕ t , a t , r t , ϕ t + 1 ) in 𝒟 Sample random minibatch of transitions ( ϕ j , a j , r j , ϕ j + 1 ) from 𝒟 Set y j = r j for terminal ϕ j + 1 ; y j = r j + γ max &#8289; a ′ Q ( ϕ j + 1 , a ′ ; θ ) for non-terminal ϕ j + 1 Perform a gradient descent step on ( y j − Q ( ϕ j , a j ; θ ) ) 2 [Note: the greedy action should be arg &#8289; max &#8289; a , not max &#8289; a ; this slip is in the original paper’s pseudocode.]", "t": 2042}, {"id": "slide-29-experience-replay-buffer-frame-54", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Experience Replay Buffer", "title": "Slide 29: Experience Replay Buffer", "frames": [54, 54], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Store transitions in buffer <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">replay</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{\\text{replay}} = \\{(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1})\\}_{t=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of discarding them</li>\n<li>Sample random minibatch each update -&gt; breaks temporal correlations (creates i.i.d. samples)</li>\n<li>Each transition <strong>reused many times</strong> -&gt; more sample efficient</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">replay</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{\\text{replay}}</annotation></semantics></math> cylinder holding <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s_1, a_1, r_1, \\dots, s_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; “Sample Minibatch” -&gt; <strong>Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (gradient step)</strong> -&gt; the environment (globe); a two-way arrow “Interact with Environment” links the environment back to the buffer.]</span></p>", "text": "Store transitions in buffer D replay = { ( s t , a t , r t , s t + 1 ) } t = 1 N instead of discarding them Sample random minibatch each update -> breaks temporal correlations (creates i.i.d. samples) Each transition reused many times -> more sample efficient [Figure: D replay cylinder holding ( s 1 , a 1 , r 1 , … , s t ) -> “Sample Minibatch” -> Update Q θ (gradient step) -> the environment (globe); a two-way arrow “Interact with Environment” links the environment back to the buffer.]", "t": 2100}, {"id": "slide-30-delayed-target-network-frame-55", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: Delayed Target Network", "title": "Slide 30: Delayed Target Network", "frames": [55, 55], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔻</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">replay</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><munder><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target moves every step</mtext></munder><mo>−</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">prediction moves every step</mtext></munder><msup><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}) \\sim \\mathbb{D}_{\\text{replay}}}\\left[\\Big(r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\underbrace{\\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a; \\theta)}_{\\text{target moves every step}} - \\underbrace{Q(s_t, a_t; \\theta)}_{\\text{prediction moves every step}}\\Big)^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow]</span> <strong>optimizer unstable &amp; diverges</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔻</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">replay</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜽</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msup><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}) \\sim \\mathbb{D}_{\\text{replay}}}\\left[\\Big(r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a; \\boldsymbol{\\theta}^-) - Q(s_t, a_t; \\theta)\\Big)^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^-</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> copy of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> frozen for some steps, then <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>←</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^- \\leftarrow \\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> updated every step</li>\n<li>Freezing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^-</annotation></semantics></math> makes the target fixed -&gt; stable supervised regression</li>\n</ul>", "text": "ℒ ( θ ) = 𝔼 ( s t , a t , r t , s t + 1 ) ∼ 𝔻 replay [ ( r ( s t , a t ) + γ max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ; θ ) ⏟ target moves every step − Q ( s t , a t ; θ ) ⏟ prediction moves every step ) 2 ] [arrow] optimizer unstable & diverges ℒ ( θ ) = 𝔼 ( s t , a t , r t , s t + 1 ) ∼ 𝔻 replay [ ( r ( s t , a t ) + γ max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ; 𝜽 − ) − Q ( s t , a t ; θ ) ) 2 ] [arrow at θ − ] copy of θ frozen for some steps, then θ − ← θ [arrow at θ ] updated every step Freezing θ − makes the target fixed -> stable supervised regression", "t": 2160}, {"id": "slide-31-dqn-frame-56", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: DQN", "title": "Slide 31: DQN", "frames": [56, 56], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Discrete action space, image state space, sparse &amp; dense rewards</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: screenshots of five Atari games.]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>B. Rider</th>\n<th>Breakout</th>\n<th>Enduro</th>\n<th>Pong</th>\n<th>Q*bert</th>\n<th>Seaquest</th>\n<th>S. Invaders</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Random</td>\n<td>354</td>\n<td>1.2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>-20.4</td>\n<td>157</td>\n<td>110</td>\n<td>179</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarsa [3]</td>\n<td>996</td>\n<td>5.2</td>\n<td>129</td>\n<td>-19</td>\n<td>614</td>\n<td>665</td>\n<td>271</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contingency [4]</td>\n<td>1743</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>159</td>\n<td>-17</td>\n<td>960</td>\n<td>723</td>\n<td>268</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DQN</strong></td>\n<td><strong>4092</strong></td>\n<td><strong>168</strong></td>\n<td><strong>470</strong></td>\n<td><strong>20</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1952</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1705</strong></td>\n<td><strong>581</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human</td>\n<td>7456</td>\n<td>31</td>\n<td>368</td>\n<td>-3</td>\n<td>18900</td>\n<td>28010</td>\n<td>3690</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Discrete action space, image state space, sparse & dense rewards [Figure: screenshots of five Atari games.] B. Rider Breakout Enduro Pong Q*bert Seaquest S. Invaders Random 354 1.2 0 -20.4 157 110 179 Sarsa [3] 996 5.2 129 -19 614 665 271 Contingency [4] 1743 6 159 -17 960 723 268 DQN 4092 168 470 20 1952 1705 581 Human 7456 31 368 -3 18900 28010 3690", "t": 2281}, {"id": "slide-32-the-overestimation-bias-frame-57", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: The Overestimation Bias", "title": "Slide 32: The Overestimation Bias", "frames": [57, 57], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Max is not symmetric, it amplifies positive noise leading to systematic overestimation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_j = r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma \\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a; \\theta^-)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^-</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> In DQN, same network selects and evaluates action</p></li>\n<li><p>Double DQN: same two networks as DQN, just rerouted</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>Q</mi><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">online network selects action</mtext></munder><mo>;</mo><munder><munder><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">target network evaluates it</mtext></munder><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_j = r(s_t, a_t) + \\gamma\\, Q\\Big(s_{t+1}, \\underbrace{\\arg\\max_a Q(s_{t+1}, a; \\theta)}_{\\text{online network selects action}}; \\underbrace{\\theta^-}_{\\text{target network evaluates it}}\\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Deep Reinforcement Learning with Double Q-learning</em>, Van Hasselt et al., 2015</p>", "text": "Max is not symmetric, it amplifies positive noise leading to systematic overestimation y j = r ( s t , a t ) + γ max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ; θ − ) [arrow at θ − ] In DQN, same network selects and evaluates action Double DQN: same two networks as DQN, just rerouted y j = r ( s t , a t ) + γ Q ( s t + 1 , arg &#8289; max a Q ( s t + 1 , a ; θ ) ⏟ online network selects action ; θ − ⏟ target network evaluates it ) Reference: Deep Reinforcement Learning with Double Q-learning , Van Hasselt et al., 2015", "t": 2281}, {"id": "slide-33-dqn---scaling-to-continuous-action-spaces-frame-58", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: DQN - Scaling to Continuous Action Spaces", "title": "Slide 33: DQN - Scaling to Continuous Action Spaces", "frames": [58, 58], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Robots have often continuous action spaces <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mi>⊤</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{a} = [\\tau_1, \\tau_2, \\tau_3, \\tau_4, \\tau_5, \\tau_6, \\tau_7]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^7</annotation></semantics></math> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a 7-joint robot arm]</span></li>\n<li>How do you compute <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_a Q(s_t, a, \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> over an infinite action space?\n<ul>\n<li>Discretization</li>\n<li>Sampling + CEM</li>\n<li>Learn arg max with second network (DDPG)</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Robots have often continuous action spaces 𝒂 = [ τ 1 , τ 2 , τ 3 , τ 4 , τ 5 , τ 6 , τ 7 ] ⊤ ∈ ℝ 7 [Figure: a 7-joint robot arm] How do you compute arg &#8289; max &#8289; a Q ( s t , a , θ ) over an infinite action space? Discretization Sampling + CEM Learn arg max with second network (DDPG)", "t": 2341}, {"id": "slide-34-case-study-spatial-discretization-frame-59", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: Case Study: Spatial Discretization", "title": "Slide 34: Case Study: Spatial Discretization", "frames": [59, 59], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: system diagram. Left, a photo of the setup with labels RGB-D camera, robot arm, gripper, objects (a pile of wooden blocks) and workspace. The camera view becomes a top-down heightmap <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math>, rotated into 16 orientations (x16). Each rotation goes through two fully convolutional networks, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">FCN</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">pushing</mtext></msub><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{FCN}_{\\text{pushing}}\\ \\phi_p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">FCN</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">grasping</mtext></msub><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{FCN}_{\\text{grasping}}\\ \\phi_g</annotation></semantics></math> (both DenseNet-121), which output pixel-wise Q-maps <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">push</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\text{push}}(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">grasp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\text{grasp}}(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking the max over every pixel and rotation gives the “best push” and the “best grasp”, drawn on the heightmap. Every pixel is a discrete action.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Synergies between Pushing and Grasping with Self-supervised Deep Reinforcement Learning</em>, Zeng, Song et al., 2018</p>", "text": "[Figure: system diagram. Left, a photo of the setup with labels RGB-D camera, robot arm, gripper, objects (a pile of wooden blocks) and workspace. The camera view becomes a top-down heightmap s t , rotated into 16 orientations (x16). Each rotation goes through two fully convolutional networks, FCN pushing ϕ p and FCN grasping ϕ g (both DenseNet-121), which output pixel-wise Q-maps Q push ( s t , a t ) and Q grasp ( s t , a t ) . Taking the max over every pixel and rotation gives the “best push” and the “best grasp”, drawn on the heightmap. Every pixel is a discrete action.] Reference: Learning Synergies between Pushing and Grasping with Self-supervised Deep Reinforcement Learning , Zeng, Song et al., 2018", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-35-case-study-spatial-discretization-video-frame-60", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: Case Study: Spatial Discretization <span class=\"own\">[video]</span>", "title": "Slide 35: Case Study: Spatial Discretization [video]", "frames": [60, 60], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video titled “Learning Pushing and Grasping”: a robot arm above a table with a cluster of colourful wooden blocks.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Synergies between Pushing and Grasping with Self-supervised Deep Reinforcement Learning</em>, Zeng, Song et al., 2018</p>", "text": "[Figure: video titled “Learning Pushing and Grasping”: a robot arm above a table with a cluster of colourful wooden blocks.] Reference: Learning Synergies between Pushing and Grasping with Self-supervised Deep Reinforcement Learning , Zeng, Song et al., 2018", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-36-monte-carlo-sampling-frames-61-62", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Monte Carlo Sampling", "title": "Slide 36: Monte Carlo Sampling", "frames": [61, 62], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How to compute <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_a Q(s_t, a, \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> over an infinite action space?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Uniform</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_{a \\in \\mathbb{R}^7} Q(s, a; \\theta) \\approx \\arg\\max_{a^{(i)} \\sim \\text{Uniform}(\\mathcal{A}),\\ i = 1 \\dots N} Q(s, a^{(i)}; \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: interactive demo, “Continuous action spaces - why sampling approximates arg max and how CEM refines it”, in Monte Carlo mode. A bumpy multimodal curve <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s, a, \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> over action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\in [0, 1]</annotation></semantics></math>, true argmax at 0.515 (dashed line). With only N = 1 sample the estimate lands on a low bump: estimated argmax 0.251 (error 26.4%) in one frame and 0.065 (error 44.9%) in the other, with the warning “Too few: N=1 is not enough. Increase samples.”]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 62) adds:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Works because <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s_t, a, \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math>, dense enough sampling always finds a point near the true peak</li>\n<li>❌ Requires <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes per action selection, scales poorly</li>\n<li>⚠️ Quality depends entirely on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> and luck, no guarantee of finding the true argmax</li>\n</ul>", "text": "How to compute arg &#8289; max &#8289; a Q ( s t , a , θ ) over an infinite action space? arg &#8289; max a ∈ ℝ 7 Q ( s , a ; θ ) ≈ arg &#8289; max a ( i ) ∼ Uniform ( 𝒜 ) , i = 1 … N Q ( s , a ( i ) ; θ ) [Figure: interactive demo, “Continuous action spaces - why sampling approximates arg max and how CEM refines it”, in Monte Carlo mode. A bumpy multimodal curve Q ( s , a , θ ) over action a ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] , true argmax at 0.515 (dashed line). With only N = 1 sample the estimate lands on a low bump: estimated argmax 0.251 (error 26.4%) in one frame and 0.065 (error 44.9%) in the other, with the warning “Too few: N=1 is not enough. Increase samples.”] [Second build (frame 62) adds:] ✅ Works because Q ( s t , a , θ ) is smooth in a , dense enough sampling always finds a point near the true peak ❌ Requires N forward passes per action selection, scales poorly ⚠️ Quality depends entirely on N and luck, no guarantee of finding the true argmax", "t": 2642}, {"id": "slide-37-cross-entropy-method-cem-frames-63-64", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: Cross-Entropy Method (CEM)", "title": "Slide 37: Cross-Entropy Method (CEM)", "frames": [63, 64], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sample <strong>smarter</strong> by iteratively concentrating on high <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Sample <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> actions from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^{(i)} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_k, \\sigma_k)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i = 1 \\dots N</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Keep top <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math> with highest <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">set</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">top </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{set}} = \\{a^{(j)} \\mid Q(s, a^{(j)}) \\in \\text{top } M\\}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Refit <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">mean</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">set</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{k+1} = \\text{mean}(\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{set}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">std</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">set</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{k+1} = \\text{std}(\\mathbb{E}_{\\text{set}})</annotation></semantics></math> to elite set &amp; repeat</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Return <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^* = \\mu_K</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same demo in CEM mode (samples N = 20, elite size M = 5). In one frame the sampling Gaussian has collapsed onto the highest peak and the estimate equals the true argmax, 0.515 (“Converged”); in the other, step 1, a wide Gaussian covers the middle of the range, its samples are dotted on the curve, the elite ones in green, and the current estimate is 0.613.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ No gradients needed - works as a black-box optimizer on any <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>❌ Finds only one mode, if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is multimodal, converges to whichever peak it lands first</li>\n<li>⚠️ Optimization at test time, pays <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N \\times K</annotation></semantics></math> forward passes every single control step</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Sample smarter by iteratively concentrating on high Q Sample N actions from a ( i ) ∼ 𝒩 ( μ k , σ k ) , i = 1 … N Keep top M with highest Q , 𝔼 set = { a ( j ) ∣ Q ( s , a ( j ) ) ∈ top M } Refit μ k + 1 = mean ( 𝔼 set ) , σ k + 1 = std ( 𝔼 set ) to elite set & repeat Return a * = μ K [Figure: the same demo in CEM mode (samples N = 20, elite size M = 5). In one frame the sampling Gaussian has collapsed onto the highest peak and the estimate equals the true argmax, 0.515 (“Converged”); in the other, step 1, a wide Gaussian covers the middle of the range, its samples are dotted on the curve, the elite ones in green, and the current estimate is 0.613.] ✅ No gradients needed - works as a black-box optimizer on any Q θ ❌ Finds only one mode, if Q θ is multimodal, converges to whichever peak it lands first ⚠️ Optimization at test time, pays N × K forward passes every single control step", "t": 2642}, {"id": "slide-38-case-study-qt-opt-frames-65-67", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: Case Study: QT-Opt", "title": "Slide 38: Case Study: QT-Opt", "frames": [65, 67], "html": "<ul>\n<li>First time Q-Learning + CEM scales to real robots with image obs -&gt; 96% grasp success on unseen objects\n<ul>\n<li>Off-policy Q-learning: replay buffer stores 580k real grasps</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video playing across three frames: a robot arm grasping objects from a bin, with a “Camera view” panel (overlay “Input: 472x472 Image and gripper aperture - Output: Gripper aperture and displacement”). The middle frame shows the system diagram. <strong>Training time</strong>: reward = grasp success, determined by subtracting pre and post-drop images; state, action, reward go to <strong>Distributed RL</strong>, which sends back learned weights. <strong>Inference time</strong>: the camera image (State: 472x472 image and gripper aperture) goes to the <strong>Critic Function</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">State</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Action</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(\\text{State}, \\text{Action})</annotation></semantics></math>; the <strong>Cross-Entropy Method</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Action</mtext></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">State</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Action</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_{\\text{Action}} Q(\\text{State}, \\text{Action})</annotation></semantics></math> sends action proposals to the critic and gets Q-values back; the chosen Action (gripper displacement and aperture) goes to the robot.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>QT-Opt: Scalable Deep Reinforcement Learning for Vision-Based Robotic Manipulation</em>, Kalashnikov et al., 2018</p>", "text": "First time Q-Learning + CEM scales to real robots with image obs -> 96% grasp success on unseen objects Off-policy Q-learning: replay buffer stores 580k real grasps [Figure: embedded video playing across three frames: a robot arm grasping objects from a bin, with a “Camera view” panel (overlay “Input: 472x472 Image and gripper aperture - Output: Gripper aperture and displacement”). The middle frame shows the system diagram. Training time : reward = grasp success, determined by subtracting pre and post-drop images; state, action, reward go to Distributed RL , which sends back learned weights. Inference time : the camera image (State: 472x472 image and gripper aperture) goes to the Critic Function Q ( State , Action ) ; the Cross-Entropy Method arg &#8289; max &#8289; Action Q ( State , Action ) sends action proposals to the critic and gets Q-values back; the chosen Action (gripper displacement and aperture) goes to the robot.] Reference: QT-Opt: Scalable Deep Reinforcement Learning for Vision-Based Robotic Manipulation , Kalashnikov et al., 2018", "t": 2760}, {"id": "slide-39-deep-deterministic-policy-gradient-ddpg-frame-68", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG)", "title": "Slide 39: Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG)", "frames": [68, 68], "html": "<ul>\n<li>CEM solves <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max</annotation></semantics></math> at test time, DDPG learns it during <strong>training</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> passes</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^* = \\arg\\max_{a^{(i)} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_k, \\sigma_k)} Q(s_t, a^{(i)}; \\theta) \\qquad [N \\times K \\text{ passes}]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Learn a network <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\phi</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mi>a</mi></munder><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> forward pass</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\phi(s_t) \\approx \\arg\\max_a Q(s_t, a; \\theta) \\qquad [1 \\text{ forward pass}]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two Networks:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Critic</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(s_t, a, \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; same as DQN, trained with TD loss</li>\n<li><strong>Actor</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\phi(s_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; new, trained to maximize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>", "text": "CEM solves arg &#8289; max at test time, DDPG learns it during training Instead of: a * = arg &#8289; max a ( i ) ∼ 𝒩 ( μ k , σ k ) Q ( s t , a ( i ) ; θ ) [ N × K passes ] Learn a network μ ϕ such that: μ ϕ ( s t ) ≈ arg &#8289; max a Q ( s t , a ; θ ) [ 1 forward pass ] Two Networks: Critic Q ( s t , a , θ ) -> same as DQN, trained with TD loss Actor μ ϕ ( s t ) -> new, trained to maximize Q", "t": 2821}, {"id": "slide-40-ddpg-updates-and-exploration-frame-69", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: DDPG <span class=\"own\">[updates and exploration]</span>", "title": "Slide 40: DDPG [updates and exploration]", "frames": [69, 69], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Actor update</strong>: ask critic which action is better, move <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\phi</annotation></semantics></math> in that direction</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msub><mo minsize=\"180%\" maxsize=\"180%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi J(\\phi) \\approx \\mathbb{E}_s\\left[\\nabla_a Q(s, a; \\theta)\\Big|_{a = \\mu_\\phi(s)} \\cdot \\nabla_\\phi \\mu_\\phi(s)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exploration</strong>: A deterministic actor never explores, but unlike DQN we can’t use <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy, we add gaussian noise</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\mu_\\phi(s_t) + \\epsilon, \\quad \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Continuous control with deep reinforcement learning</em>, Lillicrap et al., 2015</p>", "text": "Actor update : ask critic which action is better, move μ ϕ in that direction ∇ ϕ J ( ϕ ) ≈ 𝔼 s [ ∇ a Q ( s , a ; θ ) | a = μ ϕ ( s ) ⋅ ∇ ϕ μ ϕ ( s ) ] Exploration : A deterministic actor never explores, but unlike DQN we can’t use ϵ -greedy, we add gaussian noise a t = μ ϕ ( s t ) + ϵ , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , σ ) Reference: Continuous control with deep reinforcement learning , Lillicrap et al., 2015", "t": 2880}, {"id": "slide-41-ddpg-real-robot-door-opening-frames-70-75", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: DDPG <span class=\"own\">[real-robot door opening]</span>", "title": "Slide 41: DDPG [real-robot door opening]", "frames": [70, 75], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video playing across six frames. Robot arms learning to open a door handle: “Two Workers - 2.5 hours - Ep1 1.5x” (then Ep2), and “Single Worker - 2.5 hours 1.5x”. In between, a learning-curve plot: test reward (-0.3 to 0.4) against hours (0.5 to 4.0) for “1 worker” (blue) and “2 workers” (green). The 2-worker curve leaves the 1-worker curve at about 1.1 hours and reaches about 0.3 by 2.5 hours; the 1-worker curve stays near 0 until about 3 hours and ends near 0.1. Red circles mark the 1-worker curve at 2.5 hours and the point where the 2-worker curve takes off.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Added from frame 71 on:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Test time = 1 forward pass, runs at any control frequency</li>\n<li>✅ Handles high-dimensional continuous actions, no CEM needed at test time</li>\n<li>❌ Notoriously brittle, very sensitive to hyperparameters, often fails to converge</li>\n<li>❌ Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> must be tuned manually, too small: no exploration, too large: unstable</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Deep Reinforcement Learning for Robotic Manipulation with Asynchronous Off-Policy Updates</em>, Gu et al., 2016</p>", "text": "[Figure: embedded video playing across six frames. Robot arms learning to open a door handle: “Two Workers - 2.5 hours - Ep1 1.5x” (then Ep2), and “Single Worker - 2.5 hours 1.5x”. In between, a learning-curve plot: test reward (-0.3 to 0.4) against hours (0.5 to 4.0) for “1 worker” (blue) and “2 workers” (green). The 2-worker curve leaves the 1-worker curve at about 1.1 hours and reaches about 0.3 by 2.5 hours; the 1-worker curve stays near 0 until about 3 hours and ends near 0.1. Red circles mark the 1-worker curve at 2.5 hours and the point where the 2-worker curve takes off.] [Added from frame 71 on:] ✅ Test time = 1 forward pass, runs at any control frequency ✅ Handles high-dimensional continuous actions, no CEM needed at test time ❌ Notoriously brittle, very sensitive to hyperparameters, often fails to converge ❌ Noise σ must be tuned manually, too small: no exploration, too large: unstable Reference: Deep Reinforcement Learning for Robotic Manipulation with Asynchronous Off-Policy Updates , Gu et al., 2016", "t": 2940}, {"id": "slide-42-rl-algorithm-taxonomy-trade-offs-frame-76", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: RL Algorithm Taxonomy &amp; Trade-offs", "title": "Slide 42: RL Algorithm Taxonomy & Trade-offs", "frames": [76, 76], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Exact Methods</th>\n<th>Tabular RL</th>\n<th>Deep RL (Discrete)</th>\n<th>Deep RL (Continuous)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithms</td>\n<td>Value Iter., Policy Iter., Q-Val. Iter.</td>\n<td>Q-Learning, SARSA</td>\n<td>DQN, Double DQN</td>\n<td>DDPG, QT-Opt (CEM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needs Dynamics Model</td>\n<td>✓ Train+Test</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State Space</td>\n<td>Discrete, small</td>\n<td>Discrete, small</td>\n<td>High-dim images</td>\n<td>High-dim images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action Space</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Continuous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy (train time)</td>\n<td>Tabular</td>\n<td>Tabular</td>\n<td>Neural Net</td>\n<td>Actor + Critic NNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off-policy</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Q-Learn ✓, SARSA ✗</td>\n<td>✓ (Replay Buffer)</td>\n<td>✓ (Replay Buffer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Convergence</td>\n<td>✓ Guaranteed</td>\n<td>✓ Tabular+explore</td>\n<td>✗ No guarantee</td>\n<td>✗ Brittle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key Innovation</td>\n<td>Bellman contraction</td>\n<td>TD updates from experience</td>\n<td>Replay buffer + Target network</td>\n<td>Actor learns arg max_a Q</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[The same table opens Lecture 5 as its recap slide.]</span></p>", "text": "Exact Methods Tabular RL Deep RL (Discrete) Deep RL (Continuous) Algorithms Value Iter., Policy Iter., Q-Val. Iter. Q-Learning, SARSA DQN, Double DQN DDPG, QT-Opt (CEM) Needs Dynamics Model ✓ Train+Test ✗ Model-free ✗ Model-free ✗ Model-free State Space Discrete, small Discrete, small High-dim images High-dim images Action Space Discrete Discrete Discrete Continuous Policy (train time) Tabular Tabular Neural Net Actor + Critic NNs Off-policy N/A Q-Learn ✓, SARSA ✗ ✓ (Replay Buffer) ✓ (Replay Buffer) Convergence ✓ Guaranteed ✓ Tabular+explore ✗ No guarantee ✗ Brittle Key Innovation Bellman contraction TD updates from experience Replay buffer + Target network Actor learns arg max_a Q [The same table opens Lecture 5 as its recap slide.]", "t": 3001}, {"id": "end-frame-77", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [77, 77], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-27-case-study-deep-q-learning-dqn-frame-52", "label": "Slide 27: Case Study: Deep Q-Learning (DQN)", "html": "the 49-game result is from the later Nature paper (Mnih et al., 2015); the 2013 paper in the screenshot covers seven games."}, {"slide": "slide-28-dqn-algorithm-frame-53", "label": "Slide 28: DQN Algorithm", "html": "the greedy action should be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_a</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_a</annotation></semantics></math>; this slip is in the original paper’s pseudocode."}]}, {"key": "05", "dir": "week05_rl_2", "date": "Mon 16 Mar 2026", "topic": "Reinforcement Learning II", "summary": "Policy gradients: the log-derivative trick, REINFORCE, variance reduction with reward-to-go, baselines and the advantage function, off-policy updates via importance sampling, then the trust-region family (TRPO, PPO) and actor-critic methods ending with SAC and the reparameterization trick.", "key_ideas": ["Policy gradient = imitation gradient weighted by return", "Baselines do not bias the gradient; V(s) is the natural one, giving the advantage A = Q - V", "Importance weights let you reuse old data but explode over long horizons", "PPO clips the probability ratio instead of solving TRPO's KL-constrained problem", "SAC trains the critic fully off-policy from a replay buffer - the most sample-efficient of the lot"], "papers": [{"title": "End-to-End Training of Deep Visuomotor Policies", "who": "Levine et al., 2015", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.00702"}, {"title": "Eureka: Human-Level Reward Design...", "who": "Ma et al., 2023", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12931"}, {"title": "Latent Plans for Task Agnostic...", "who": "Rosete-Beas et al., 2022", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.08959"}], "homework": ["hw4_reinforcement_learning"], "lecture": {"id": "AdTGz8YnnlE", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 5: Reinforcement Learning II | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "52:23", "description": "Lecture 5 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction"}, {"t": 9, "title": "Review of RL fundamentals"}, {"t": 89, "title": "Policy gradient methods"}, {"t": 954, "title": "Improving policy gradients"}, {"t": 1539, "title": "Importance sampling"}, {"t": 2002, "title": "Entropy and policy constraints"}, {"t": 2262, "title": "Trust region methods"}, {"t": 2405, "title": "Proximal policy optimization"}, {"t": 2524, "title": "Actor-critic architectures"}, {"t": 2599, "title": "Soft actor-critic"}, {"t": 3020, "title": "Summary and conclusion"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Today we will continue talking about reinforcement learning. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; maybe first let's start with a bit of a recap uh from from last week, right? So last week we introduced first like some exact methods like value iteration, policy iteration, uh Q-iteration, very kind of like beautiful algorithms uh with guaranteed convergence by the Bellman contraction, but as we discussed then you know they assume that you have a dynamics model which we don't often in robotics. Uh then we move to like tabular RL like Q-learning and SARSA um to start learning from experience towards like model-free models. Uh and then we also discussed kind of how we can uh scale this up to robotics with continuous images and continuous action"}, {"t": 60, "text": "spaces with like starting from DQN and and then towards uh DDPG and uh [clears throat] cross-entropy methods and so forth, right? Uh and so essentially these were all like the key innovations there and this last bit that is like the deep RL part uh centered around different ways how to solve this uh like argmax in different ways. So everything we looked at last time was value-based, right? So the idea being um that we try to learn the worth of every possible action uh and then you know in in in a specific state and then we kind of try to do the argmax to extract the policy. Uh and we saw that you know it's a little bit difficult to scale this to continuous sorry to high-dimensional spaces, right? So the question we're going to ask today is like, what if we skip this and"}, {"t": 122, "text": "instead of learning a value function and deriving a policy from it, uh why not just, you know, since we care about the the policy itself, uh why not just like parameterize our policy and um you know, uh wiggle the the the neural networks of our net of our of our network uh to produce uh behaviors that will achieve high rewards directly. Right? Um so, trying to make um you know, uh our network produce actions that will lead to uh higher rewards in the future. So, um to wrap up our recap, um here is the math from also from last time to kind of like see where we are starting from. You know, this is the standard RL objective uh that you've seen in the last couple of lectures. Um we are just trying to reward uh to maximize the rewards um as usual. Um we use a discount factor uh to tell um to"}, {"t": 182, "text": "to trade off the uh rewards from today versus the ones from tomorrow. Um and then uh because the world is stochastic, we uh don't um uh we can't optimize with a single trajectory, but we optimize expected uh returns. Um all right. Uh and finally, our trajectory dis- distribution um uh to recap is is this one. So, it's the three things multiplied together, uh where you start, what is your initial state distribution, uh then at each time step, the probability of your policy uh uh that assigns to your actions uh taken, and plus the uh sorry, multiplied by your environment's transition probability. Okay. So, let's say okay, that we are starting to uh parameterize our policy, right? Uh and With theta and we want to first understand how good it is, right? Because we cannot just like"}, {"t": 240, "text": "inspect our network weights to to determine this. So and this diagram here like captures this intuition, right? So from the same [clears throat] start date, like some trajectories are good, some are like kind of middle and some are bad. And now obviously we want to our policy to produce a put more probability to the good ones, right? Now the the catch is here that we cannot calculate this expectation analytically because we don't know the true distribution over all robot movements in the real world. And so we kind of already prefaced this a little bit in the in the last lecture, but in practice what we use is something we call Monte Carlo sampling. So we we run our robot like n times using our current policy P theta. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; collect those trajectories and calculate a average reward. And the kind of like we are making like an"}, {"t": 300, "text": "assumption here that that is maybe like not obvious, but we are making the assumption that all these data that we collect are coming from our current policy. So this is really what we mean by this is like an on policy method. So we cannot use like past data to do this. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so exactly. So we evaluate this expectation which is a function of our policy by rolling out our policy multiple times, observe the reward and then average it to estimate how good our policy is. Okay. So we we can evaluate this now with this Monte Carlo samples, but uh of course the the main thing is we want to improve like our policy, right? How do we do this? So we need to estimate a gradient for this. So our goal is to maximize the expected sum of rewards J theta and the trajectories are being"}, {"t": 362, "text": "drawn from distribution pi theta tau. But there [clears throat] is this problem that we cannot like it's not straightforward to get a gradient on J theta directly because there is this sampling process in between, right? So the relationship between both is kind of like implicit. So this means that, you know, we cannot simply differentiate through the robot moving in the in the real world. As they depend on the dynamics of the world which we don't actually know. So what can we do about this? So we can do a little bit of math &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; where we can start by writing this expectation as an integral. So an integral of pi theta of tau times R of tau d tau. Now due to this integral being a linear operation we can now bring the [snorts] gradient inside"}, {"t": 421, "text": "the integral. And now, you know, you might think like well, this doesn't seem to help us that much yet, right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Doesn't seem so useful. But because we still have this high-dimensional integral that we really want to avoid to to try to compute directly as and we still have this kind of unknown probability inside. But now there is kind of like a elegant trick of what we can do with this to put rewrite this into a form that we can um have it a bit more useful. So as I said, right? The integral here is not yet an expectation because you can't estimate it by sampling trajectories as you have a density gradient not a density. So, [clears throat] as I mentioned there is this this policy gradient theorem that allows us to rewrite this into a more useful form and we will do like this kind of derivation a little bit"}, {"t": 482, "text": "here. So for this we use this what is called like the log derivative trick. So, if you remember the derivative of a log is one over and then with the chain rule we can get this very nice identity on the top right. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; And Yes, and so here if you look at this identity over here we can see that the pi theta of tau times log theta &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; of pi theta divided by theta tau you can cancel this out to get grad theta of pi theta tau. And so here essentially what we have done is just like we have multiplied and divided uh"}, {"t": 540, "text": "by pi theta tau. So essentially this means you're just multiplying by one. And now we you can substitute this uh grad theta of pi theta tau the blue part back into our original formula. And now this starts to to become kind of like a bit more useful. Um Because now what we can do is we can write this as an expectation of grad log pi theta of tau times r of tau and this is now something more useful because we can evaluate this expectation with samples, right? And we have um um here now kind of still this this term inside um that is not pi of a given s, right? So, we don't still have like the our kind of policy"}, {"t": 601, "text": "function. But we will get here in a second. Um &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Maybe looking at this um Yeah, maybe you might you might look at this kind of like first part of the derivation and you might be saying like, \"Well, okay, like we have now this expectation over here. But we are still kind of sampling, right? You know, like I just said like a slide ago that you know, we can't differentiate like get the gradient through through this sampling process, right? Because but the the why this is helping us for now is that the difference is that now theta appears inside the expectation. So, we can't still differentiate through the sampling, that's correct, but we will be able to differentiate with respect to theta. If you remember also like the slides from last week"}, {"t": 660, "text": "on the smoothness of of theta despite having non-differentiable reward functions, for example, with the car driving through the mountain road. Exactly. So, we can sample this term. Here it's kind of a bit more clear. And that term the second term is still not differentiable, but through the sampling, right? Okay. So, now we have this this term over here. This log p theta of of tau term. And maybe like here on the top I've I've written again like the original formula for the trajectory distribution we just covered in the recap, right? So what can we do about this? So you might remember that the logarithm of a product is the sum of its logarithms. So if we apply it to the formula on both sides,"}, {"t": 721, "text": "we can get that we can write now this log pi of tau as the sum as the sum of log P of tau of S zero plus the summation from T equals one to capital T so over all the horizon of the log probabilities under the policy of um plus the log so under the policy plus the the log of the transition probabilities. Now we can substitute this back to our policy gradient formula, right? And we can you know when we have like the gradient of this, right? And so if we look at the the first term, the log of S zero, this doesn't depend on theta. So we can cancel this out. And if we look at the transition probability, this also doesn't depend on theta. So the gradient of it we can also cancel out."}, {"t": 781, "text": "Right? Cool. So after this simplification, the only terms left are the log pi theta at T &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; log log pi theta at given ST terms. Which are the ones that we actually can evaluate. So I mean we also don't have the transition dynamics, right? So it's good that we can cancel it out. And so this essentially now gives the vanilla policy gradient uh formulation. And we can now sample trajectories from our policy, computer gradient for each of the log pi of actions, sum them, multiply with rewards, and then we can grade we can get a gradient to apply to improve our policy towards higher rewards. And so this full algorithm is called reinforce. This is the acronym given by Williams in 1992. And this is"}, {"t": 840, "text": "kind of our very first policy gradient method. And sometimes it's also referred to as the vanilla policy gradient. It's quite simple as you see. You start by initializing your policy, you evaluate an estimate of the gradients um by collecting samples with the formula we just derived. Then you apply the gradients to your weights and repeat the process by collecting fresh data. And here the N is the number of of samples that you collect. So obviously the the the higher N the better cuz you will get a more accurate estimation of your expectation. Um in a perfect world you you can have like infinite samples to have an exact equality and a perfect gradient, but of course that's not possible especially with robotics. Um So let's build some intuition for what the gradient is actually doing. Um the probably the easiest way is to"}, {"t": 901, "text": "compare it to how the gradient for the uh uh behavior cloning formula looks like, something we covered a few weeks earlier. So the behavior cloning gradient is just the sum of the log probabilities of the actions taken. So you're maximizing the um likelihood of whatever actions uh, appeared in your data set collected by the expert, right? And so, all actions get the same weight, um, no matter like if they were good or if they led to good or bad outcomes. Um, the policy gradient here, uh, on the top is is kind of the same thing it's doing the same thing, um, but with the difference that now, uh, each trajectory is weighted by the total return. Um, so, intuitively, um, you know, now good trajectories get pushed up, uh, and bad ones get pushed down. All right. Now, let's talk about, uh, causality. So, we have derived a uh, reinforce of our first kind of vanilla policy"}, {"t": 960, "text": "gradient, but, um, now we can see like, you know, where it starts to, uh, break down in the real world. Um, consider, um, this example for the robot on, uh, on the top, right? So, imagine the task is to, uh, reach towards the or grasp the cup, and, um, but it overshoots and gets a negative reward in the end. Uh, so, what does, uh, reinforce do? Um, so, it will penalize every single action in this trajectory, right? Including all the good ones in the beginning of the trajectory. Um, and that's wrong, right? Because, you know, the first part maybe like was good behavior, but then, you know, like the the second half was bad. Um, and so, essentially like the what the gradient will be telling is like, uh, to the robot never move in that direction cuz like it it led to a bad outcome, right? So, it will be really, really like difficult to learn this behavior, um, cuz you'll need like a lot a lot of samples. And so, essentially this this violates a"}, {"t": 1020, "text": "little bit the the causality, um, the principles of causality, um, in that in the physical world an action taken at, uh, time step t uh, can only affect rewards that happen either at the same time or in the future, but it cannot possibly, uh, be responsible for uh, reward that already happened in the past. &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; So, the first kind of uh fix that we can do to try to alleviate this issue is something called reward to go. Um where instead of multiplying the gradient of the action at time T by the sum of all rewards, we only multiply it by by [clears throat] the rewards from T until the end of the episode. Um And so, this way we're not changing the expected value of our gradient, but we are reducing significantly also the variance and kind of trying to address this overshooting"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "example. All right. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Okay, so with this we have fixed causality, but there is another problem of this reinforced method that is the the high variance that I kind of mentioned right now. Um so, imagine that now we have like these two trajectories where one is the same that overshoots, but we get like a reward of 99 and the other one is like a perfect trajectory and gets a reward of a 101. And so, um effectively like this um the signal between these two trajectories now um is like 101 - 99. It's that's two, right? And that's like the actual information content in like what's the difference between these two behaviors in terms of rewards."}, {"t": 1140, "text": "Um but in reinforced like we will be computing the gradient scaled by 99 and 101. So, like your optimizer will be seeing this this great like this massive gradient updates Um where the the true signal is essentially only 2% of like all all all of this. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so the rest is just noise making your training like very unstable. Um so um what we can do here Yeah, so sorry. Here here you can see the what I just mentioned. Okay, so we've identified a problem, right? In that just like with this vanilla formulation reinforce can't distinguish good from bad trajectories when the returns are large and similar. Um so the fix for this is quite simple. We can subtract a baseline B from the"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "reward to go to center our rewards. Um that's the only change to the formula really happening here. Everything else stays the same and &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; now we are waiting each action log probability by the reward to go minus B instead of the raw reward to go. Um and what happens now in our case is that essentially now we are subtracting B equals 100. Um so that would be our the average and now the reward for our first trajectory would be minus one and the one for the second one would be plus plus one. And now the gradient does exactly what we would like it to do which is that it suppresses the overshooting behavior on the one hand and reinforces the good the perfect reach behavior, right? So that's really like what ideally we want"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "to be doing. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; now you might be also asking like well this sounds a bit like cheating. Like, what are we doing here like introducing some random like constants into the formula? Um no. So, we are not We are not cheating here because um in principle, like if you are subtract a constant, B, this doesn't change the expected value of the gradient at all. Um so, it's kind of like zero bias operation, but it helps enormously to reduce the high variance problem. Um Okay, so we've been writing this this policy gradient with now this reward to go minus the baseline to reduce the variance. Um and we said that, you know, B could be like the mean reward, a constant that that we subtract to reduce the variance. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; but if we look at this more carefully,"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "like what are we actually computing? Right? So, it's kind of summing up all the rewards from time step T onwards, right? So, everything that's happening after the current action. And we are taking the expectation over all the trajectory sampled from our policy. And this is actually, if you remember, like the definition of a Q function. Right? Which is the expected sum of the future rewards uh um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; given that you are in a in a state ST, and then you take your the action AT, and follow your policy afterwards. So, essentially, um this is very interesting, the reward to go um is a Monte Carlo estimate of your Q function. Uh so, one noisy sample, essentially. Um So, what we've been actually computing all all the time is kind of like this noisy estimate of the Q function"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "uh minus the baseline, right? Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so now that we have realized that, if we combine this with our baseline idea, we get what is called the advantage function, A of P of SA. Um, so what you think about, um, like, how does this What is actually this doing? So, um, if Q pi is is the score that you expect if you take a specific action A, then V of pi is the score you expect on average from this state ST just by following your current policy. Um, so the what the advantage is is trying to answer is how much better is it to take action A from ST. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, and if you plug this back into the policy gradient formula, um, you can see that now the positive"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "advantage means that the actions was better than our average expectation, so it it will increase its probability. A negative advantage means that, um, it was worse than our average expectation, so we will decrease it. Okay, so here's our latest and most refined policy gradient formulation on the top. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and it has the causality and the baselines, but it has still kind of like one practical issue that we mentioned in the first kind of like, uh, reinforce algorithm slide. Um, right, that the trajectories are only valid if, uh, they are collected by the current, uh, policy, right? Um, so this is like not super practical for us roboticists, right? Because we already know that collecting data with"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "real robots is, you know, very costly and you have to like uh robots break, motors break, and so forth. And so, if, you know, like once you collect some data, like and you do like one gra- gradient update, you have to throw away your data and like collect again. Like this is super inefficient, right? It might be great for, you know, in simulation or where it's like easy to to collect a lot of data, but ideally uh we would like to um be more sample efficient and maybe even like do multiple gradient steps um on the same data, right? Um All right. So, the challenge is clear. Uh we want to avoid throwing away our data. Um we want to um make uh our policy gradient uh algorithm a little bit more off-policy. How can we do this? Uh for this, we can turn into uh important sampling, uh which is a technique used to estimate"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "expectations under one distribution uh when you only have access to samples uh from a different distribution. Um in our case, we want to compute uh our expectation over a target distribution p of x, uh but we are stuck with uh samples. So, this would be our new uh kind of uh policy. Um but in our case, um we only have access to uh samples from q of x, which is uh data collected with our old policy. Um So, to do this, um how we can write uh the expectation as an integral, we can use the same uh multiply by one trick from the previous derivation um to add the term um q of x uh divided by q of x uh inside the integral here on the the on the right. And um now by rearranging the terms there, you can uh put it into a more useful"}, {"t": 1622, "text": "um form, an expectation over a samples from Q. Um I don't have a pointer, but um and um this means that now we can simply uh weigh uh weigh our function uh f of x um by the uh ratio of um p of x uh divided by uh q of x. Um so this means that now we can our robot can use our old samples um provided we weight them by the probability uh of that sample under the new policy divided by uh its probability under the old uh policy. Um one thing uh to note here is that this thing only works if Q has a non-zero support uh to remain unbiased. Um because as you can see, if we divided by uh if like Q zero is like zero, uh then like this uh uh will not be uh defined. This division."}, {"t": 1681, "text": "And so also intuitively, like this important sampling will work best if both distributions are not too far away. Uh not like the case I plotted here where there is like they're uh very very different. Cool. Uh how does this look like uh in practice in terms of uh math? So uh this is kind of like our RL objective now with importance sampling. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; And um how does this look like in practice? So uh we can uh start by inserting our trajectory distribution formula using the chain rule. Uh and uh substitute it uh uh for for the old uh and new policy. Uh now we know that both policies will have the same uh initial state and the same uh uh transition probabilities. Uh so, we can cancel them out as they're operating in the same MDP."}, {"t": 1740, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Uh and so now uh we are left with essentially the the ratio uh of products uh of the policy probabilities and uh and this is as I said before quite useful because we don't always have the the dynamics of the world. Uh and so this way we can now evaluate this importance weights. Now then um the next question is how do we derive uh the gradient of this? Um because uh in our gradient we still seem to be sampling trajectories from the new policy uh and not the old one, right? So, here like we uh in the the gradient we still are like sampling the the new the new policy. Um so now we can use the derivation uh we did uh two slides ago for the expectation of the importance sampling uh and simply plug in those terms into the formula. Um and now um"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "the important uh trick here is that this now changes. Now we are we have an expectation over the old policy. So, this is the the data that we have, right? Um and so uh we expand now the uh importance weights with the product uh we derived in the previous slide. And um this is still like a little bit troublesome uh because it is exponential in in the uh in the horizon T. Um so, this means that if, you know, imagine uh that all our importance terms are less than one. So, if we you multiply many many numbers um like if T is very long and you have many uh small numbers and you multiply them, um then their their product will go uh exponentially uh fast uh to zero. Uh meaning that your variance will grow also uh infinite uh to go to infinity"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "exponentially fast. Um Exactly. So, this you know, this term is a little bit troublesome uh cuz it can be quite unstable with exploding gradients and so forth. Um in practice, we often consider expectations over time steps instead of uh over trajectories. Um So, this is uh what we do. Um and we often um when we decompose this joint distribution uh of pi s and a, uh we get the action ratio and the state ratio. And uh while we can easily calculate our action ratio from our network, um we cannot do this for the state ratio. Um because we would need to know uh the global dynamics uh of the environment again, which we don't have, right? Um So, in practice, this often is"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "approximated by one, uh which is uh um kind of an approximation that algorithms now like TRPO and PPO do uh by forcing the updates to be small. But in essence, um this gives us kind of our final form uh for um that you see in the bottom, um the important weights uh reduce uh reduces to just a ratio of action probabilities uh of the new policy and the uh old policy. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And again, it's just an approximation and it will only work if they are like close enough. Uh so, here is how the full algorithm look likes in practice. Um, uh in practice it's not too different from like the reinforce algorithm we just saw a few slides ago. But now the key difference is that instead of collecting and updating collecting data and updating once you now can collect once and perform multiple"}, {"t": 1982, "text": "gradient updates. Of course like the you know so you can't do this like indefinitely. You cannot like do multiple gradient updates before collecting again because this would push your distributions too far and then like the ratio would not work. So we established that the important sampling breaks down when the support set is zero right when the new policy by theta prime assigns probability to action state pairs that the old policy for example never took. So what can we do about this? One kind of easy solution is to add an entropy bonus to the policies gradient objective. So the idea is that we don't want our old policy to become too"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "deterministic so that it assigns zero probability to regions of the action space. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So we want to really to encourage the policy to kind of spread out to reduce the chance of um of hitting the the zero support in the first place. And so [snorts] that's essentially what we're doing here. So we can add like this kind of entropy term to the loss Uh, and uh, then we can um, have this temperature parameter that controls like how much weight we give to this entropy. Uh, so um, the larger it is, the more like spread out uh, it will be and um, if you go closer to uh, zero, it will be like kind of more greedy. Um, there are kind of two different ways how you can implement this depending on uh, the action space of your um, of your robot. Uh, if it's uh, discrete actions,"}, {"t": 2102, "text": "then you can use the kind of standard uh, Shannon entropy. Um, and for continuous actions, uh, you can uh, use a differential uh, entropy for a Gaussian policy. Uh, and here uh, D is uh, the dimensionality for uh, your action space. So, if it's like a 7 DOF, then it it will be a 7. Um, okay, we could talk more about this, but maybe let's move on for time. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Okay. So, we've talked a a little bit about um, uh, that um, the important sampling only works when the old and new policies are close. But, what happens if they're not, right? Uh, if we as I said, if we perform multiple uh, gradient uh, updates and then you know, the uh, it goes uh, from the same batch to but then the policy changes too much before we collect new data. Uh, and here is like kind of like uh, a specific uh, kind of"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "failure mode example uh, you can see uh, where um, imagine [clears throat] that you have some uh, action that was unlikely under the old policy. Uh, uh, but uh, it gets a high reward, so the new policy gives it like higher weight. Oh, sorry, higher probability. Um, and then uh, the importance weight uh, will essentially become very large like let's say 20 uh, in this case. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so this will be really bad. Sorry. Uh because now your gradient estimate will explode essentially if like the importance weight is like very large. So one solution um to this problem [clears throat] is to constrain uh the policy so it doesn't change too much uh during those multiple gradient steps that you're taking. Uh one way how you can implement this is by adding a KL divergence constraint."}, {"t": 2222, "text": "Uh so which measures how &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Sorry. [clears throat] How close um uh two probability distributions are. And um essentially like this will now penalize um uh the new policy if it uh tries to assign let's say zero uh uh a probability of zero to an action that the old policy took. Uh because the old policy uh data is kind of our ground truth data uh that ensures um that we have support for uh the new policies actions. And this is essentially um what uh the algorithm called trust region policy optimization uh TRPO implemented in 2015. Um the key benefit uh so it's like essentially the the we're maximizing the um our policy gradient formulation we derived before subject to uh the KL"}, {"t": 2281, "text": "divergence, right? And um uh I will not go too much into details. We could do like uh talk a lot about this, but um the key benefits from TRPO are um that uh it provides uh a theoretical guarantee um that each update will improve uh or not uh worsen uh the true performance uh of JP uh pi. Um but kind of the the downside here is that uh this KL constraint requires computing the official information matrix, uh which is a matrix of second derivatives with respect to the KL divergence uh uh of the KL divergence with respect to the policy parameters. Uh and this is computationally very heavy. Uh it's it's like uh n squared uh for the memory uh and then um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh uh n cubed uh for uh compute. And so, there are like some approximations with"}, {"t": 2341, "text": "uh conjugate gradients and so on. Um but in general, like it's kind of like very difficult uh to scale. Uh even like uh for deep networks, uh you need to even further approximations. Uh um So, it's like not that popular nowadays. But still, uh you know, you can see a really compelling also like uh robotics applications even here from ETH uh uh how, you know, TRPO was used to uh to train like a quadruped locomotion um simple simple to real. And you can see uh very nice uh zero-shot generalization examples. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Uh and this is now a quadruped that can uh navigate challenging terrain. Um uh step over obstacles. Um and also like um uh do locomotion over surfaces that uh it might not have seen during training."}, {"t": 2401, "text": "Um so, like it actually works. All right. So, TRPO has great guarantees on but we kind of like hate the computational cost. Uh so, the question is can we get that stability without this expensive like second-order math? Uh and kind of the uh a more popular algorithm or that because it's a bit more easier to implement is called proximal policy optimization. Um where the key idea is like what if we can just clip the importance ratio instead of constraining it with the KL divergence. Um so kind of like this is how how now the our policy gradient update looks like and essentially you can see like we are constraining this to be like inside some interval and we are taking the minimum um of of these two uh"}, {"t": 2462, "text": "clips. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um and because now this is like a simple first-order method like it scales linearly and works perfectly with a standard optimizers like Adam and this kind of like scalability um is exactly why PPO is is the backbone for many uh um for for training like large language models with like RRLHF or like GRPO and so forth. Um the trade-off that we're making here is that we don't have a monotonic improvement guarantee anymore but and it's still near on-policy. Uh so we still need quite a bit of samples to learn PPO. Uh so it's quite reliable and quite stable across many different domains and applications but we are it's still mostly uh uh"}, {"t": 2521, "text": "fundamentally near on-policy method. Um in robotics Oh, this is not playing. Yeah, in robotics one famous application of PPO was done by Open AI 2019 where they taught this kind of really Dexel Shadow hand that has like 24 degrees of freedom to solve a Rubik's Cube. Um, and this was again also like a sim to real kind of approach where they did a lot of domain randomization in simulation. Uh, but then, you know, you can see it's like it can work across many perturbations even like if it has like tied fingers. Um, uh, and yeah, maybe like one thing to note here is that so here PPO didn't receive like camera observations"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "as input like a state input, but essentially they had a more modular approach where they first had like kind of perception uh, network to extract like the position and orientation of the cube which was then fed to PPO. All right. Um, okay, so here is the full objective of PPO. And you can see a lot of things that we have covered today come together in like this single formula. So, there are like three terms, right? So, first is the clipped uh, uh, the the clipped surrogate objective we just derived for PPO. Um, this is an actor the actor loss that is updating our policy parameters and it's trying to maximize the advantage while keeping the importance ratio in a in a safe range. Uh, the second term is the value"}, {"t": 2640, "text": "function loss and this is essentially a critic and it's kind of being trained simultaneously to give us better estimates of the advantage function. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; remember like the advantage function requires the value of pi, right? Of the states. And so the better our critic is, the lower the variance of of our advantage estimates will be and the more stable our policy. And the third term is the entropy bonus we discussed earlier, keeping the kind of policy from collapsing to a deterministic solution and maintaining exploration. And so here like the the key thing here is that this is now what we call an actor critic formulation or architecture. And this is kind of very nice because now we are combining the the best of both worlds of like policy gradients approaches to give us like"}, {"t": 2700, "text": "you know like direct optimization for the behavior we care about. As well as the value the value based methods that give we're using to give us low variance baseline through our critic. And so as I said like this gives rise to this family of algorithms called actor critic methods, right? And the actor is the policy and the critic is the value function. And they are yeah, trained together. Um Yeah, maybe like one thing to note here is that the critic is learns via TD updates. So meaning that it bootstraps from its own estimates to next time step rather than waiting for the full trajectory return, which is a limitation that we had if we only used policy gradients for our actor, right? We would have to wait until the end of the episode. Um"}, {"t": 2762, "text": "And so this means that now this is quite much more sample efficient than using this Monte Carlo returns. Um at the cost of the small like uh bias that you're bootstrapping bias. Um and maybe for robotics uh also interesting is um that uh you can often uh train the critic by using privileged information. Uh because you don't use the critic at test time. So especially if you're using like a simulator environment or or so, you can use like additional in state information or privileged information for your critic uh to reduce the variance, right? To get you like better advantage estimates. Uh and then you know like a test time you throw the critical away, so it doesn't really matter. But it kind of help a lot your your your trading. Um Yeah. Um Okay, I need to wrap up soon. So Yeah. Okay, so the last the last family"}, {"t": 2820, "text": "of algorithms uh we will uh uh briefly touch upon today is the soft actor critic. Right? Because um this kind of like closes the the loop, right? Uh we have started like uh with very on-policy methods and we've been trying to get more and more off-policy to like be more sample efficient and use more uh previous data, right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Uh and so soft actor critic is kind of the answer to that. It's a fully off-policy actor critic algorithm that trains the critic uh on a replay buffer uh just like DQN um in the lecture that we covered in the lecture before. Um so the actor has uh kind of these two terms. Uh the exploration um uh bonus that we saw before and the exploitation uh which like just answers if I were to do this action today, like how many uh points would I get, right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um and then like um there is kind of this this this"}, {"t": 2881, "text": "uh push and pull between uh these two, right? Um and then uh the critic loss is kind of a standard uh TD um uh error. So, the square difference between uh what the critic currently thinks uh a past state action pair was worth and what it uh uh what it should think based on the actual uh reward received uh plus the discounted value of the next state. And um the crucially also here um there is also this uh entropy uh term. Uh that means that uh you know, we want um states uh that lead uh or we benefit we we push for states that lead to higher rewards uh and also give you many different ways uh to achieve it. Right? So, this will get like uh uh uh uh pushed. And the key idea here is that the actor pulls all states from the replay buffer,"}, {"t": 2942, "text": "but asks now the current policy and not the old one what action it would take uh here today. Right? So, the critic asks kind of as a learned simulator um estimating how much reward uh the new action would have gotten in in the old state, even though it never actually has seen this state action combination. So, that's really kind of the the key uh here. Um yeah. There is one remaining problem. I will not go into too much details. Um this is pertaining um to how to uh back propagate to the sampling uh process um where you can use like a standard reparameterization uh trick uh for Gaussians, for example, also very used in variational inference and VAEs and so on. Um, so, yes, but I'm getting out of time."}, {"t": 3000, "text": "Uh, and there are also some real-world demonstrations of um, uh, SAC on a real robot, uh, real robots from the original papers, uh, both from legal stacking, uh, quadruped manipulation, or also dexterous hands. And, um, yeah. To conclude, uh, today we started with reinforce. Uh, it's kind of a very direct way to optimize a policy. Uh, we saw, you know, it has some limitations, the vanilla, uh, version of it of reinforce, and we started to fix those by adding causality and and baselines to kind of fight the high variance problem. Uh, and then we moved on to, uh, advantage, uh, functions to make our, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; to make our, uh, rewards relative to each other rather than absolute. Uh, then we kind of hit the on-policy wall. Uh, we, uh, cuz we are still kind of requiring to collect new, uh, fresh"}, {"t": 3061, "text": "data, um, for every gradient step. So, we introduced important sampling, uh, to allow us to, uh, take multiple gradient steps, uh, on a single batch of data. Um, and we also explored, uh, adding entropy regularization, um, to ensure the robot, uh, never stops exploring, and we, uh, still have like, uh, non-zero support for important sampling. Uh, and then we also, uh, looked at different ways, um, to, uh, constrain the change between the policies, uh, with a KL divergence, uh, and also with PPO, the simplified clipping, um, to kind of like bound how much, uh, the policy can drift in a single update. And finally, uh, we, uh, reached, um, soft actor-critic. where we train critic fully off policy with a replay buffer similar to what we saw last lecture with DQN."}, {"t": 3120, "text": "And we we see that this kind of achieves the highest sample efficiency from all our methods that we talked about today and this is also why it's still today a very popular algorithm for also real robotics. Yes, with this thank you for your attention. &gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction"}, {"t": 9, "title": "Review of RL fundamentals"}, {"t": 89, "title": "Policy gradient methods"}, {"t": 954, "title": "Improving policy gradients"}, {"t": 1539, "title": "Importance sampling"}, {"t": 2002, "title": "Entropy and policy constraints"}, {"t": 2262, "title": "Trust region methods"}, {"t": 2405, "title": "Proximal policy optimization"}, {"t": 2524, "title": "Actor-critic architectures"}, {"t": 2599, "title": "Soft actor-critic"}, {"t": 3020, "title": "Summary and conclusion"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "CPmTpXA5azw", "title": "Andrew Wagenmaker: Robots That Learn From Experience [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Andrew Wagenmaker", "duration": "31:32", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Andrew Wagenmaker (Postdoc @ UC Berkeley) talks about how to move towards robots that learn from experience in the real world via reinforcement learning.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and motivation"}, {"t": 74, "title": "The challenge of interaction"}, {"t": 315, "title": "The need for efficient exploration"}, {"t": 606, "title": "Imitation learning overview"}, {"t": 794, "title": "Exploration via noise control"}, {"t": 1272, "title": "Optimizing pre-training"}, {"t": 1514, "title": "Uncertainty-aware learning"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Andrew is a post-doctoral researcher, uh in electrical engineering and computer sciences at UC Berkeley, uh working uh doing his post-doctoral scholar now with Sergey Levine. And he previously completed a PhD in computer science at the University of Washington under the supervision of Kevin Jamieson. And he also spent time at Microsoft where he was uh working with Dylan Foster. Uh his research focuses on learning in dynamic sequential and interactive environments. And much of his work centers on developing effective and scalable approaches to exploration with the goal of enabling efficient uh online policy improvement. And uh yes, today we are very excited to hear uh the latest from Andrew. Uh please give him a warm uh welcome. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Cool. Yeah, should I go ahead and get started already?"}, {"t": 62, "text": "Okay. Well, thanks for the invitation. Yeah, excited to present to you guys. Um happy to take questions throughout the talk. Um I'm not sure how this works in terms of uh hearing you guys, but if you have questions I'm sure all of you can handle that. Um so, I want to talk today a little bit about sort of how do we enable robots to learn through interaction, through experience as they interact with the world. And then moving beyond maybe behavioral cloning or other approaches we have to to robot learning. Um and so, kind of just to motivate this, I want to just think about um uh you know, a task that I think many of us probably have done in our day-to-day lives. Um if ironing a shirt, right? And so, I think probably many of us have ironed shirts. And how did we how did we learn to iron a shirt? And you know, it might look something like the following. When you try to learn to iron to to iron a shirt, you might watch some videos on this. You might sort of uh internalize something from these videos, and then you'll go and try to iron a shirt. But um I I I think for tasks like this, at least for myself, when I tried to learn"}, {"t": 120, "text": "to iron a shirt, you know, I didn't first get it I didn't get it right on the first try. When I first tried to iron a shirt, there's some complexity here. I have to lay out the shirt just right. You know, I can't I have to make sure I'm not ironing creases into it, all these sorts of things. And so, really to do a task like learning to iron a shirt, we have to do more than just watch an internet video on this. We actually had to like try to do it. We had to take actions in our environment and we had to observe how the environment responded and then sort of update our behavior accordingly. Right? And so, learning as humans, learning to do tasks in the real world like ironing shirts, it requires some amount of interaction and experience with the real world. Right? We can't just internalize a video and do it perfectly. We actually have to try it and see what happens. Um, and so, we might ask, well, how do we get a robot to do a task like ironing a shirt? And we could take, you know, maybe a similar starting point where we take some data source, maybe this is internet data, maybe this is a simulator modeling a shirt, maybe this is some, um, humans demonstrating on a robot how to iron a shirt. And we could take that data and we could use that data to train"}, {"t": 180, "text": "a robot in some way to to iron a shirt. And we could go deploy this on our robot. Um, and this is, you know, I you know, maybe a little bit of a simplification simplification, but a lot of what is going on in robot learning today sort of follows a paradigm that looks something like this, right? We'll train policies via behavioral cloning, we'll train policies in a simulation, we deploy them into the world and we hope that they work. Um, but, you know, we don't actually have robots in our homes ironing our shirts. Um, and so, so why is that? And well, there's maybe a lot of reasons for that, but I think one thing is that if we look at these sort of data, there's actually like shortcomings to each of these. So, for simulators, if we have an accurate simulated model of the world, this is great and we can often learn in simulation. Maybe Euler's talked about this at some point in the course so far. Um, but modeling a an object like a shirt is actually pretty complex. Like the dynamics of a shirt are actually very sophisticated and so, actually getting an accurate simulated model that is not trivial. For something like internet data we might take a lot of YouTube videos but we don't actually have grounding for this this this this this YouTube video"}, {"t": 240, "text": "in the physical world. Right? Seeing a human iron a shirt doesn't tell me the motor commands I need to send to my robot to get it to iron a shirt. Right. In human demonstrations I know Oyer's talked to someone about like behavioral cloning and imitation learning, you know, this is actually a very, you know, I think an approach that has led to a lot of success over the last few years but it's really expensive to actually collect enough human demonstrations and often we find that even as we collect more there's some amount of distribution shift between our demonstrations and the deployment on the robot is we don't actually still get perfect performance. And so sort of all of these ways we might take this like prior data or our prior information, use it to sort of train a robot policy and deploy this in the world somehow it's still not fully sufficient to actually get robots that are in the world doing like really complex tasks that the way that we might want. And so how do we get there? And I I want to I want to argue I'd like to I'd like to suggest that perhaps what we need to do is think about how, you know, we as humans learn to do complex tasks like iron a shirt and think about how do we actually enable robots to learn from experience, to learn through interaction, to not just take some data source that's fixed and deploy that but"}, {"t": 301, "text": "to actually learn as they try things in the real world. Okay. So a lot of my work focuses on enabling this, enabling robots to learn from experience. And so I want to talk today about some of the kind of recent work we've been doing along these directions. Okay. So what what what how do we enable robots to learn from experience and what makes this thing of learning from experience hard? Um and there's actually a lot of challenges here but I think the one I want to highlight today which I think is perhaps the most fundamental is the following. Like to enable learning from experience we need to collect the experience that distinguishes the correct behavior. Okay. We usually call this exploration. Basically what it means is we try things in the real world and we sort of learn from that experience, you know, to to do the right thing. We call that whole process reinforcement learning. Um to maybe it even a little bit more concrete if we let's think about this example of ironing a shirt. Um in in this sort of instructional video says the first thing you're supposed to do when you iron a shirt is you spray the shirt with a spray bottle. Okay? And so, what do we want our robot to do if we're trying to get it to learn to iron a shirt? Well, we would like it"}, {"t": 362, "text": "to be playing actions in the real world, to be interacting with the real world, to be collecting experience in the real world that tells it or that shows it that it should pick up the spray bottle, right? In other words, if I deploy this policy here, and it tries a few different things, and one of these happens to be the correct thing, then I can use the information that I collect from doing this correct thing to learn the correct behavior. I'll I'll I'll sort of make this more concrete shortly. But, we might contrast that with a policy that looks looks like the following. You know, if I were to play this policy in the real world, um and never actually does the thing that is correct. It never actually picks up the spray bottle and sprays that shirt. And so, if I roll this policy out in the real world, it's never actually going to collect experience that enables learning, right? And so, when we think about learning from experience, we think about learning from interaction. Really, the fundamental thing we need to do is we need to understand how do we actually get the robot to collect this experience to sort of induce these interactions that will allow for learning. Now, so so sort of in some sense, the goal of exploration, the goal of this"}, {"t": 421, "text": "this process is to collect the experience necessary to learn behavior. And of course, we want to do this as quickly as possible. Okay. So, conventionally in reinforcement learning, we've kind of thought about exploration in the following way. I will drop some agent into the world, and they don't really know anything about the world, and they'll try a bunch of things in the world until they find the right behavior, and then they'll update their behavior accordingly. So, if we go back to this example, let's say I I drop the agent into the world, and let's say that the agent, the robot, has moved to this state I've labeled here. Well, what could they do from this state? Well, well, in principle, they could take any possible action, right? They can move their robot, they can move their their arm in any possible direction. So, maybe they take one of these possible directions, and they move to a new state. Right? And now that they're in this state, they can take any possible direction. They move to this state, and so forth, right? And if I roll this out many times, you know, I might induce behaviors that look something like the following. Right? Now, it's kind of actually obvious if we think about this. If I just did this, even if I did this like a thousand times, or a million times, this"}, {"t": 481, "text": "is actually probably not going to do the right thing. Right? If I want my robot to say, \"Pick up the spray bottle and spray the shirt,\" just taking like random actions isn't going to actually enable my robot to do this, right? Um and so, I guess maybe the conclusion that you conventionally when we think about exploration, it doesn't actually scale to the physical world. So, learning, actually enabling robots to learn through interaction, enabling robots to learn from experience, conventionally, in terms of how we thought about this in the reinforcement learning community, often doesn't actually give us very scalable, efficient approaches. Okay. And so, I think a lot of the reason that we adopted these approaches like behavioral cloning, um or or learning from simulation, and stuff like that, is that these approaches we have to exploration, these approaches we have to collect useful experience in the real world are simply not efficient enough to actually enable us to learn. So, I want to contrast, you know, this sort of exploration, where maybe just randomly taking actions in the real world, with how a human might explore. How would a human explore? Well, let's say I'm at this state."}, {"t": 541, "text": "Um maybe I would take one of three actions, right? Sort of I know something about the world. So, I know maybe the things I should do in this state is I should either try to pick up the uh I should either pick up the iron, or I should pick up the spray bottle, or I should pick up the shirt. In other words, the sort of actions or the behaviors that I might induce are significantly more structured and directed than what a robot would induce. So, when I go to learn to iron a shirt, rather than doing these sort of random behaviors, taking these random actions, I'm taking very structured, refined actions, right? And when I go to iron a shirt, you know, maybe it took me some amount of interaction, but it took me like 10 attempts, and then I figured out how to do it, right? And so, when humans explore, when humans interact with the world, we're able to do this much more efficiently than kind of the way we've typically done this in reinforcement learning before. Right? If we compare this exploration to sort of conventional exploration, we see the exploration on the right, this is actually something that I can maybe efficiently run on my robot, while this conventional exploration is is simply too inefficient, too random, too undirected. Okay. And so, I want to think a little bit today about how do we get there?"}, {"t": 600, "text": "Like, how do we enable robots to explore and learn like humans? So, to answer this, I'm going to take a little bit of a a detour here. So, I know Oyer has talked a little bit about in the course learning from demonstrations. This is probably maybe review, but I'll just I'll just I'll just give you some review just just for completeness. So, a lot of robot learning right now, what we're doing is we're taking human demonstration data. So, that's we're taking tasks we care about, we're having humans operate robots on these tasks, they collect a big data set of states and actions of sort of, you know, behaviors induced by a human to get the robot to solve the task. Now, a lot of what we're then doing in in in robot learning is we're taking some generative model, and we're training this model to predict the action that the human produced at a given state. And we call this behavioral cloning or imitation learning, right? We we're trying to train a policy that kind of captures the behavior that the human induces with the hope that the human uh or or that this this policy will then will then capture the behavior of the human to be able to successfully"}, {"t": 660, "text": "replicate it on the robot, right? Autonomously complete the task that the human has demonstrated. Okay. So, one detail I want to go over, I'm not sure how much Oyer has talked about in this course, is sort of how do we parameterize this generative model. This is actually important to what we're going to get to. And so, very often in robot learning, what we're doing now is we parameterize this generative model as a diffusion or a flow model. So, what is a diffusion or flow model? We can just think about a diffusion model as some generative model that takes as input some noise, usually sampled from a standard Gaussian. It feeds this noise through an iterative denoising process that then transforms their denoised this noise into an action that is sort of trained to be trained to look like a demonstrator action. And there's a lot of details here I'm not going to go over at the moment, but I think the thing I want to take away from this is that these BC policies we're training sort of most commonly in robot learning we can actually just think of these as simply mappings that are sort of noise to demonstrator actions. And for various reasons this is turns out to like work quite well for behavioral cloning. And it becomes kind of the standard that"}, {"t": 720, "text": "I think we do in robot learning. Okay. So, how do we then how do we actually think about deploying these policies? Usually we would do something like the following. You know, I'm at some state, my robot is at some state, I sample a noise, I denoise it to an action, and then I play the action on my robot. I'm at some other state, I you know, I sample some noise, denoise the noise, and play the action on my robot, right? And if I do this many times, you know, I'll I'll induce sort of a variety of behaviors on my robot, and maybe sometimes it solves the task, sometimes it doesn't. And often what we observe with imitation learning is that the behavior policies we get from this are like sort of reasonable, but maybe not good enough, right? Maybe it succeeds 20% of the time, maybe it succeeds 50% of the time. But we would really want a policy to be succeed sort of 90% of the time, 100% of the time. Okay. And so, what I kind of want to ask what I want to think about then is sort of actually really like a like a you know, we can actually approach this question from multiple angles. First, we might ask, well, if I'm given a BC policy, can I use this as a starting point to enable learning from experience? Right? Can I use this as a sort of get"}, {"t": 780, "text": "this more structured human-like exploration that I showed you a few slides ago? And maybe the dual of this question is we could ask, if I'm given a BC policy, can I improve that to the point where it actually succeeds with sort of like reasonable 90% 100% success? Okay. So, how do we think about doing this? Well, if we have a BC policy that's been trained on a lot on sort of a reasonable set of human demonstrations, we might have a policy that looks something like the following. At this state, it might produce say three options. It might produce one action that goes to the spray bottle, one action that goes to the shirt, and one action that goes to the iron. In other words, because I trained this policy on a variety of human demonstrations, on a reasonable set of human behaviors, the the action it's been trained to produce, it's been trained to mimic the human and not clone the human at this state. And the human would produce this reasonable set of actions. So, the action produced by the robot or that the BC policy are this reasonable set of actions. Now, I also told you a few slides ago that we could uh think of this BC policy"}, {"t": 840, "text": "as simply a mapping from noise, from Gaussian noise, to demonstrator actions. Okay? And so, what we could actually think about doing is we could take this uniform exploration that we would have over action space. You know, conventionally in RL, we would just explore over action space. We could shift this exploration to the noise space by controlling this noise generation. And then we could do something like the following. We could We could maybe try some noise. We could explore some noise, and that would lead to an action. It'd be denoised to an action that goes to the spray bottle. Maybe some other noise would be denoised to the action that goes to the the iron, and some other noise would be denoised to the action that goes to the shirt. In other words, by sort of taking this uniform exploration over action space, shifting this uniform exploration over noise space, and sort of leveraging the properties of this BC policy, we can actually induce a sort of very structured action space output action space behaviors over sort of reasonable human actions. Okay. So, we can actually go one step further than this, though. So, let's say we do this. Let's say we place some noise. We denoise this into an action."}, {"t": 900, "text": "We play this action on our robot, and we get some feedback that that's the incorrect action. Well, what we can do is we explore this one noise. So, we can shift our exploration distribution now away from that noise. Maybe we sample some other noise. We get We play this action, and this action turns out to be the correct action. So, now what we do is we shift shift our noise to this to to our exploration to this noise, right? And we can do this actually using standard reinforcement learning train, right? And so in other words, by modifying the sampling process, we can now lock onto the correct output behavior. We can sort of direct our noise sampling to sample a noise that will lead to the correct output behavior. Okay. And so cumulatively, we propose this algorithm that does something like the following. It looks like the following. Okay, so we have three We can I mean there are three key components to this. We have the BC policy, and that's the initial policy we've trained here, which we can think about as mapping from noise to actions, right? Some noise W to an action A. I'm also going to introduce this noise"}, {"t": 960, "text": "policy, which is what I've controlling the noise generation that is producing some noise, and then it'll also induce what I'll call this noise critic. Um I think Oyer has talked to you a little you all about how sort of these RL algorithms work. So the training for this is actually very simple. So what we'll do is we'll just simply roll out this policy in the following way. At every step, we'll we'll train our value function using sort of standard value function training to sort of fit the value of every noise at every state. Then we'll train the noise policy to basically maximize this value function. In other words, it to choose a noise that gets sort of the maximal value. And then at every step, what we do is we'll generate some noise using our noise value our our noise policy. We'll feed that noise through our BC policy to produce some action, and then we'll play that action on a robot and we'll observe the state and we'll add that to our data set. Okay? And so what we've done here is we've taken this BC policy and we're kind of using this as a starting point, but we're refining this distribution the BC policy is playing via reinforcement"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "learning to sort of lock onto the correct behaviors and get this sort of very structured exploration, right? And so if we do this properly, we can actually direct the exploration to be this sort of human-like exploration, and then if I lock in on the correct behavior, we can induce this sort of human-like improvement. Okay. So does it So So So, we ran this in a variety variety of settings and we found that actually works quite well. So, what I'm showing you here is a BC policy that was just trained on some demonstrations and the the task here is pretty simple. We're just trying to pick up the mushroom and set it on the cloth, but the BC policy initially sort of doing reasonably well, but not maybe succeeding less than 50% of the time. Um And so, what I'm showing you here is when we start running our approach by doing this sort of exploration over noise space. And what I want to highlight here is that from the start the behaviors that are being induced by this, by trying these different noise vectors, is actually very structured and very sort of like human-like almost, right? It's not succeeding every time, but every time it's making a reasonable attempt at sort of exploring this space of output actions and sort of inducing these very structured output"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "behaviors. And I compare this to sort of the previous state of the art for real world robotic RL, which is running an algorithm that is instead of exploring over noise space is sort of exploring over action space, right? So, it's trying different actions. And the thing I want to highlight here is that from the start the exploration behavior here sort of actually fairly useless, right? It's trying a lot of things, but most of these actions is leaning into random things in free space and it's not at all interacting meaningfully with our target task. Right? And so, it's not actually collecting useful information over what we should actually do to solve this task. And so, as as both of these are running, they're both refining their action distributions. In our case, we're refining the noise distribution for the more previous state of the art, it's refining its its distribution in action space. And what we see is that actually for our approach after only 25 episodes of online interaction, it's already locked onto the correct behavior. So, after a very small amount of online interaction, we can already learn to do right there. One thing I'll just mention briefly"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "here, um is that the I'm not going to I don't I don't have time to go into a lot of detail on this, but um this approach actually also circumvents a lot of technical challenges with running RL on top of pre-trained behavioral cloning policies, especially diffusion policies. You can't actually directly run RL on them, um at least not using conventional algorithms, due to various technical challenges having to do with how they do their thing noisy process and their inability to write down likelihoods. But so this approach that we're also actually allows us to incorporate BC policies within this RL training loop in a way that really wasn't impos- possible before. Okay. And so you can run this on a lot of studies. We took it on various uh diffusion policies and VLAs, um generally found it works quite well. Um this it but the BC take up policy that succeeds something like less than 50% of the time and boosts this to something that succeeds, say, 90% 100% of the time in anywhere from like 30 minutes to 90 minutes of online training. Um Here's another example policy. So this is actually not a video I did, but some researchers at ByteDance took our approach and they deployed it sort of on their bimanual setup. Um"}, {"t": 1203, "text": "and they they were able to get their policy ba- basically they in in this example they tried to get they tried to train a policy simply be be good at demonstration as they do sort of very high precision task. Um and what they found is that trying to train a policy only on these sort of uh demonstrations they it succeeded like less than 50% of the time. It's like training on demonstrations and human demonstrations they weren't able to get a policy that sort of reliably succeeded at this very high precision task. But we taken our approach and their BC policy they were able to refine the distribution of actions produced by this policy through the via online interaction and able to get it able to get it to actually successfully succeed sort of close to 100% of the time. Right? And so I think this just shows that, you know, we can go beyond what BC gives us. We can go beyond what we can get from these just like pre-trained policies that were trained on demonstration and their simulation if we enable sort of policy through online interaction. Now, doing this it's really critical that we do this efficiently, right? And so this approach, by leveraging kind of"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "the structure in the BC policy, we're actually able to get this very efficient online exploration and to these capabilities, but it's actually very critical that we we do this properly to enable these capabilities. Okay. Um here's some more simulated results. Um well, you're How am I doing on time? I have like a second part, but I don't know if I I don't know if I have time to go over it. What What is the time? How much time do I have left? Uh you're set on time. I think you're Yeah, you can do like maybe like 5 more minutes and then questions or Okay, sure. I'll go quite quickly through this part. Okay. So, the second part I wanted to talk about was I said how do we even think about training these policies? So, in this first part of the talk or or this first part I just assumed, you know, we're given a policy that's trained via behavioral cloning on demonstrations, but we might then ask, \"Well, is that actually the best way to train a policy if I want to then use this policy for RL improvement?\" Um So, is BC pre-training So, so we sort of usually call this initial this initial phase pre-training. Is this the most effective initialization to enable learning from experience?"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "Um And to sort of answer this, I want to go back to this example I showed you at the start of this talk, right? Where if we have some two policies, say on the left is one policy and on the right is the other policy, what we want is we want this policy to be doing reasonable things, say going to the ironing board, but we want to make sure that the behaviors that are needed for improvement are actually in distribution for this policy. And so, if we prefer the policy, pre-trained policy A to pre-trained policy B because pre-trained policy A is actually interacting with the spray bottle, which is what we want, while pre-trained policy B is not. And so, if we roll out pre-trained policy A, we'll collect experience that allows us to learn, while pre-trained policy B does not collect this experience. And so, when I pre-train a policy, what I really want is that when I ensure that it collects the experience necessary for enabling improvement. Okay. And so, the first thing we might ask, \"Well, is BC if we if we just train a policy in the standard way we train policies, is that going to be sufficient to enable learning from experience?\" And what we see is that if we if we we look at this sort of BC process, let's"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "say take some demonstrator, If they collect some set of demonstrations, and then I train a policy on those demonstrations. And let's say this case that it just so happens that the BC policy hasn't actually or the demonstrator hasn't actually demonstrated the the behavior picking up the spray bottle. Maybe we just haven't collected enough demonstrations yet. What happens when I do BC pre-training is that I simply get a policy that doesn't express that behavior. And so if I just pre-train with BC, what I'm doing is I'm just directly fitting the demonstrations. So if I'm perfectly trying to fit these demonstrations, and so doing that doesn't necessarily give me a policy that is going to express the behavior needed for improvement. If the demonstrations don't collect don't sort of span the right behavior, I'm simply stuck. All right. So if I want to use my BC policy on my pre-trained policy as a starting point for RL, this sort of says that using BC is maybe not sufficient. And we show we can show formally that it actually can happen. I'm not going to sort of go into a lot of detail here. Um but basically BC is maybe actually not going to express the actions needed. How could we fix this? Well, one thing we might do, and this is often what we do in part, is that we take the BC"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "policy and we just add some noise to it. Right? At every step, with some probability I'll take the BC play the BC action, and with some probability I'll play another action. Maybe maybe a random action. And it might look something like the following. If I do this, what I see is I actually do get I can induce the right behavior. Now the issue here is we can actually show that there's a fundamental trade-off between the exploration we'll induce and the performance of this policy. Okay. So if I explore from this policy, I can express a lot of behaviors. I can do a lot of things, but I'll actually decrease the performance significantly. If I reduce the exploration, then I I will maintain the performance, but I won't actually be able to express a lot of new behaviors. And so why is this? Well, if we think about this first case, when we explore a lot, we're actually over-exploring in some sense. Basically, we know from our demonstrations that we should go to the to the ironing board. But by adding So by adding exploration in this early phase, we're sort of deviating from the behavior we know to be correct, and we're we're making the policy worse. In the second case, we're actually under"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "exploring. So, now I'm not exploring significantly. I'm getting to the iron in the board, but I'm not actually adding that much expressivity to enable learning again at this point after the iron. Okay. So, what do we do to get around this? Um I don't think I have a whole lot of time just to go into a lot of detail here, but the kind of key idea behind this is thinking about the this pre-training process rather than just in terms of BC from a more like Bayesian angle. And in particular, trying to understand how do we actually quantify our uncertainty about the demonstrator's behavior, and training a policy that adjusts its exploration proportional to the uncertainty in the demonstrator's behavior, right? And so, we can actually show formally I I don't have time to go into to sort of describe this in detail, but we can we can show formally that you can actually take this these demonstrations, and rather than just fitting them with BC, you fit sort of a an estimate a policy that estimates the uncertainty of the demonstrator's behavior, and then this will actually scale the exploration of this pre-trained policy proportional to to sort of how uncertain they are. And what this allows us to do, it allows us to"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "lock into the correct behavior when we know what the demonstrator will do while ensuring that we get sort of expressive performance when we don't know what the demonstrator will do. Okay. So, I'll just skip ahead to the result. We we took here a policy that was trained on demonstrations where the goal is to pick up the corn instead of in the palm. And I'm showing you on the left the BC policy, and on the left our right our policy, which is sort of trained in this way that sort of takes into account our uncertainty of the demonstrator. Now, it turns out that BC both of these kind of do roughly the same in in this case, both mostly fail. They sort of aren't able to actually successfully or reliably pick up the corn and set it in the palm. But, what we did is we we took an online RL approach, and we ran an online RL on top of these policies. And what we found is that we weren't able to actually effectively improve the BC policy. It sort of gets stuck below 50%. Well, our policy we're actually able to improve up to 75%. And so generally we see this to be true that if we train policies in this way, we can get a pre-trained policy that has the same performance as a BC policy,"}, {"t": 1622, "text": "but after online improvement we're able to get a much more effective policy than simply trying to fine-tune the BC policy. Okay? And so kind of the conclusion of this is that rather than, you know, when we're thinking about online RL, when we're thinking about training our policies to explore, we both need to think about how to actually get good algorithms that can sort of learn and explore online, but we also need to maybe investigate even how we're doing the pre-training in the first place, how we're leveraging this knowledge we have in the first place. And if we think about that and sort of do it use it correctly, we can actually get approaches that are able to even do even better at enabling learning from experience and sort of enabling you to use real-world capabilities that maybe we wouldn't otherwise have gotten to. Okay, I'm going to close there. I think I'm out of time, so I don't know if there's uh time for questions, but happy to take a few if the folks have any. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Yes, questions. Hey, I'm going to have a quick question. So like this is still on trajectory level, right? Like um and I'm just wondering how the performance compares against Pi 0.6 star where they kind of"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "um determine like a good action and a bad action within the same trajectory and to like supersede any human demonstrated trajectory. Um and if you think that that like making the policy better based on exploration on the same trajectory will be good enough for most tasks. And um also I'm wondering how exactly the value function is trained. Um is it just like the predicted future will like sparse reward or is it like or like what do you do you think there's like more value in using VLM as a base critic like they did in the Pi 0.6 star paper? Yeah, um so I guess that we haven't none of these have we compared against Pi 0.6 star, which I think all both of these papers came out before that paper. Um uh I think the On the question of critic, I think whether or not you want to use a VLM or something like that probably really depends on the goal is. If you want to do like single task, I think you want to improve on like a single task, I think using like a VLM critic is overkill. Um,"}, {"t": 1742, "text": "I think if you want to do sort of more of multi-task language condition critics, then probably something like a VLM makes sense. Um, and that was more kind of the regime that they were operating in for the Pi0.6 star paper. We're kind of more operating in a regime where we're just looking at a single task. So, I think it just depends a lot on the situation. I think we are doing um Uh, I I guess that maybe they don't fully understand this question on like trajectory level exploration or whatever. Um, like in some sense I would say this is still trajectory level and that well, I mean, we're just running sort of standard RL. Like the RL training we're running is actually not that different than what they're running in the Pi0.6 star paper in terms of like both of them are just training like value functions in some way. Um Uh Uh, we're kind of I guess maybe the key difference in terms of how we're thinking about this and these approaches is where we're not just training an advantage condition policy. So, what we're training is a standard policy, but then we're refining it with some other policy to produce sort of the right"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "actions. Whereas the Pi0.6 star paper, they're trying to train this like single policy that's conditioned on these advantages that is like going to these advantages that will lead it to the right behavior. Um, I think I think there's a lot of work that needs to be done trying to understand actually which of these approaches is the most effective when. I think there may be a regime where advantage conditioning is the right way to do it and maybe a regime where it's more like direct RL is the right way to do it. Um, and I think we don't actually have like a very good understanding at the moment uh of that question. Tiffany. Uh, thanks for the great talk. I had a question about the DS RL. Actually, it only works if the BC policy is actually steerable via the initial noise and sometimes this is not the case depending on how you train the policy. And I was wondering if you try to steer policies based on the state because usually policies are actually more more sensitive to this. Yeah, that's a good question. We haven't really tried that. I think it'd be interesting to look into something of that flavor. Definitely becomes a little bit tricky because if you you perturb the state too much, you know, you might"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "make the policy produce just like bad actions. Um so, I think there'd be things you'd have to be careful with there, but I think it would be interesting to look into and we haven't tried that. Thanks. Anyone else? Okay, then let's thank the speaker again. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Thanks a lot, Andrew. Yeah, thanks for the invite. I will talk to you later. Yes, see you. Bye."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and motivation"}, {"t": 74, "title": "The challenge of interaction"}, {"t": 315, "title": "The need for efficient exploration"}, {"t": 606, "title": "Imitation learning overview"}, {"t": 794, "title": "Exploration via noise control"}, {"t": 1272, "title": "Optimizing pre-training"}, {"t": 1514, "title": "Uncertainty-aware learning"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "UC Berkeley (postdoc)"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 16.03.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (48 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week05_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AdTGz8YnnlE (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 5: Reinforcement Learning II</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 16.03.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 5: Reinforcement Learning II Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 16.03.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-recap-rl-algorithm-taxonomy-trade-offs-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Recap: RL Algorithm Taxonomy &amp; Trade-offs", "title": "Slide 1: Recap: RL Algorithm Taxonomy & Trade-offs", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Exact Methods</th>\n<th>Tabular RL</th>\n<th>Deep RL (Discrete)</th>\n<th>Deep RL (Continuous)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithms</td>\n<td>Value Iter., Policy Iter., Q-Val. Iter.</td>\n<td>Q-Learning, SARSA</td>\n<td>DQN, Double DQN</td>\n<td>DDPG, QT-Opt (CEM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needs Dynamics Model</td>\n<td>✓ Train+Test</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n<td>✗ Model-free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State Space</td>\n<td>Discrete, small</td>\n<td>Discrete, small</td>\n<td>High-dim images</td>\n<td>High-dim images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action Space</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Discrete</td>\n<td>Continuous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy (train time)</td>\n<td>Tabular</td>\n<td>Tabular</td>\n<td>Neural Net</td>\n<td>Actor + Critic NNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off-policy</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>Q-Learn ✓, SARSA ✗</td>\n<td>✓ (Replay Buffer)</td>\n<td>✓ (Replay Buffer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Convergence</td>\n<td>✓ Guaranteed</td>\n<td>✓ Tabular+explore</td>\n<td>✗ No guarantee</td>\n<td>✗ Brittle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key Innovation</td>\n<td>Bellman contraction</td>\n<td>TD updates from experience</td>\n<td>Replay buffer + Target network</td>\n<td>Actor learns <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg\\max_a Q</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Exact Methods Tabular RL Deep RL (Discrete) Deep RL (Continuous) Algorithms Value Iter., Policy Iter., Q-Val. Iter. Q-Learning, SARSA DQN, Double DQN DDPG, QT-Opt (CEM) Needs Dynamics Model ✓ Train+Test ✗ Model-free ✗ Model-free ✗ Model-free State Space Discrete, small Discrete, small High-dim images High-dim images Action Space Discrete Discrete Discrete Continuous Policy (train time) Tabular Tabular Neural Net Actor + Critic NNs Off-policy N/A Q-Learn ✓, SARSA ✗ ✓ (Replay Buffer) ✓ (Replay Buffer) Convergence ✓ Guaranteed ✓ Tabular+explore ✗ No guarantee ✗ Brittle Key Innovation Bellman contraction TD updates from experience Replay buffer + Target network Actor learns arg &#8289; max &#8289; a Q", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-untitled-value-based-vs.-policy-gradients-frames-3-4", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: value-based vs. policy gradients]</span>", "title": "Slide 2: [untitled: value-based vs. policy gradients]", "frames": [3, 4], "html": "<p><strong>Value-Based Methods:</strong> learn what every possible actions is worth &amp; hope argmax will extract the best policy</p>\n<p><strong>Policy Gradients:</strong> what if we optimize our policy directly to make good actions more likely?</p>", "text": "Value-Based Methods: learn what every possible actions is worth & hope argmax will extract the best policy Policy Gradients: what if we optimize our policy directly to make good actions more likely?", "t": 60}, {"id": "slide-3-recap-the-learning-objective-frame-5", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Recap: The Learning Objective", "title": "Slide 3: Recap: The Learning Objective", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Accumulated reward an agent receives: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G = \\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Expected Return: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\pi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t \\ge 0} \\gamma^t r_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Optimal Policy: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi^* = \\arg\\max_\\pi J(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: several trajectories fanning out from a start state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> to end states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{T_1}, \\dots, s_{T_4}</annotation></semantics></math>; one highlighted as a solid path.]</span></p>", "text": "Accumulated reward an agent receives: G = ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t Expected Return: J ( π ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ ∑ t ≥ 0 γ t r t ] Optimal Policy: π * = arg &#8289; max &#8289; π J ( π ) [Figure: several trajectories fanning out from a start state s 0 to end states s T 1 , … , s T 4 ; one highlighted as a solid path.]", "t": 122}, {"id": "slide-4-recap-trajectory-probability-frame-6", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Recap: Trajectory Probability", "title": "Slide 4: Recap: Trajectory Probability", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Probability of a trajectory <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = (s_0, a_0, s_1, a_1, \\dots, s_T)</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>:</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(\\tau) = \\rho(s_0) \\prod_{t=0}^{T-1} \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[braces under the three factors]</span> <strong>Initial State Distribution</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(s_0)</annotation></semantics></math>), <strong>Policy</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>), <strong>Transition Function</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>)</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: same trajectory fan from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{T_1} \\dots s_{T_4}</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Probability of a trajectory τ = ( s 0 , a 0 , s 1 , a 1 , … , s T ) under policy π : p π ( τ ) = ρ ( s 0 ) ∏ t = 0 T − 1 π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) [braces under the three factors] Initial State Distribution ( ρ ( s 0 ) ), Policy ( π θ ), Transition Function ( P ) [Figure: same trajectory fan from s 0 to s T 1 … s T 4 .]", "t": 122}, {"id": "slide-5-how-good-is-the-policy-frame-7", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: How Good is the Policy?", "title": "Slide 5: How Good is the Policy?", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math> be the policy we optimize</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^* = \\arg\\max_\\theta \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_t, a_t)\\right]}_{J(\\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\pi(\\tau)}[R(\\tau)] \\approx \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_i R(\\tau_i) = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_i \\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} r_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <strong>Monte Carlo Sampling</strong>: Sum over samples from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: trajectories from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> ending in states labelled good (green check), fair (yellow “=”), bad, bad (red crosses).]</span></p>", "text": "Let π θ ( a ∣ s ) be the policy we optimize θ * = arg &#8289; max &#8289; θ 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s t , a t ) ] ⏟ J ( θ ) J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p π ( τ ) [ R ( τ ) ] ≈ 1 N ∑ i R ( τ i ) = 1 N ∑ i ∑ t = 0 T − 1 r i , t [arrow at ∑ i ] Monte Carlo Sampling : Sum over samples from π θ [Figure: trajectories from s 0 ending in states labelled good (green check), fair (yellow “=”), bad, bad (red crosses).]", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-6-how-to-get-the-gradient-frame-8", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: How to get the Gradient?", "title": "Slide 6: How to get the Gradient?", "frames": [8, 8], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math> be the policy we optimize</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta^* = \\arg\\max_\\theta \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_t, a_t)\\right]}_{J(\\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\tau) = \\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}[R(\\tau)]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\nabla_\\theta \\int p_\\theta(\\tau) R(\\tau)\\, d\\tau = \\int \\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau) R(\\tau)\\, d\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: small version of the good / fair / bad trajectory fan.]</span></p>", "text": "Let π θ ( a ∣ s ) be the policy we optimize θ * = arg &#8289; max &#8289; θ 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s t , a t ) ] ⏟ J ( θ ) R ( τ ) = ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s t , a t ) J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ R ( τ ) ] ∇ θ J ( θ ) = ∇ θ ∫ p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) d τ = ∫ ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) d τ [Figure: small version of the good / fair / bad trajectory fan.]", "t": 482}, {"id": "slide-7-how-to-get-the-gradient-log-derivative-trick-frames-9-10", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: How to get the Gradient? <span class=\"own\">[log-derivative trick]</span>", "title": "Slide 7: How to get the Gradient? [log-derivative trick]", "frames": [9, 10], "html": "<p>Chain Rule:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta f(\\mathbf{x}(\\theta)) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i} \\nabla_\\theta x_i(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta \\log(p_\\theta(\\tau)) = \\frac{1}{p_\\theta(\\tau)} \\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, \\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau) = p_\\theta(\\tau) \\frac{\\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)}{p_\\theta(\\tau)} = \\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace label]</span> <strong>Log Derivative Trick</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∇</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla \\log x = \\frac{\\nabla x}{x}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Policy Gradient Theorem:</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) &amp;= \\int \\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, R(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\\\\n&amp;= \\int p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, \\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, R(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, R(\\tau)\\right]\n\\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in the first line]</span> “Can’t sample this”</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the expectation]</span> “We can sample this!”</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> “Sampling still non-differentiable, but we can differentiate wrt to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>!”</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Chain Rule: ∇ θ f ( 𝒙 ( θ ) ) = ∑ i = 1 n ∂ f ∂ x i ∇ θ x i ( θ ) ∇ θ log &#8289; ( p θ ( τ ) ) = 1 p θ ( τ ) ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) p θ ( τ ) ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) = p θ ( τ ) ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) p θ ( τ ) = ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) [brace label] Log Derivative Trick ∇ log &#8289; x = ∇ x x Policy Gradient Theorem: ∇ θ J ( θ ) = ∫ ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) d τ = ∫ p θ ( τ ) ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) d τ = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∇ θ log p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) ] [arrow at ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) in the first line] “Can’t sample this” [arrow at the expectation] “We can sample this!” [arrow at ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) ] “Sampling still non-differentiable, but we can differentiate wrt to θ !”", "t": 482}, {"id": "slide-8-how-to-get-the-gradient-expanding-the-trajectory-log-probability-frame-11", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: How to get the Gradient? <span class=\"own\">[expanding the trajectory log-probability]</span>", "title": "Slide 8: How to get the Gradient? [expanding the trajectory log-probability]", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<p>Trajectory Distribution: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau) = \\rho(s_0) \\prod_{t=0}^{T-1} \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(\\tau) = \\log p(s_0) + \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t) + \\log p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Policy Gradient Theorem:</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, R(\\tau)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta\\left[\\log p(s_0) + \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t) + \\log p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p(s_0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> crossed out in red (they do not depend on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\right)\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_t, a_t)\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: as written on the slide, the log-sum runs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 1 \\dots T</annotation></semantics></math> and the transition term sits outside the sum; the consistent form is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\left[\\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t) + \\log P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which matches the final line.]</span></p>", "text": "Trajectory Distribution: p θ ( τ ) = ρ ( s 0 ) ∏ t = 0 T − 1 π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) = log &#8289; p ( s 0 ) + ∑ t = 1 T log &#8289; π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) + log &#8289; p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) Policy Gradient Theorem: ∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∇ θ log p θ ( τ ) R ( τ ) ] [brace under ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) ] ∇ θ [ log p ( s 0 ) + ∑ t = 1 T log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) + log p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) ] , with log &#8289; p ( s 0 ) and log &#8289; p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) crossed out in red (they do not depend on θ ) ∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ) ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s t , a t ) ) ] [Note: as written on the slide, the log-sum runs t = 1 … T and the transition term sits outside the sum; the consistent form is ∑ t = 0 T − 1 [ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) + log P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) ] , which matches the final line.]", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-9-reinforce-frame-12", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: REINFORCE", "title": "Slide 9: REINFORCE", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Initialize <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>While not converged do\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Collect samples <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\tau^i\\}</annotation></semantics></math> by rolling out policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Compute gradient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) \\approx \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\right)\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_{i,t}, a_{i,t})\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta + \\alpha \\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n<li>Return <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Reference: <em>Simple statistical gradient-following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning</em>, Williams, R. J. (1992)</p>", "text": "Initialize π θ While not converged do Collect samples { τ i } by rolling out policy π θ Compute gradient ∇ θ J ( θ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 0 N − 1 [ ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ) ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s i , t , a i , t ) ) ] Update θ ← θ + α ∇ θ J ( θ ) Return π θ Reference: Simple statistical gradient-following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning , Williams, R. J. (1992)", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-10-intuition-behind-gradient-frames-13-14", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Intuition Behind Gradient", "title": "Slide 10: Intuition Behind Gradient", "frames": [13, 14], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Policy Gradient:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J_{PG}(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\right)\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_{i,t}, a_{i,t})\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><span class=\"own\">[brace under the log-prob sum]</span> “Imitation gradient, weighted by reward” -&gt; ✅ Upweight good trajectories, ❌ Downweight bad ones</p></li>\n<li><p>BC Gradient:</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J_{BC}(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Policy Gradient: ∇ θ J P G ( θ ) = 1 N ∑ i = 0 N − 1 [ ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ) ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s i , t , a i , t ) ) ] [brace under the log-prob sum] “Imitation gradient, weighted by reward” -> ✅ Upweight good trajectories, ❌ Downweight bad ones BC Gradient: ∇ θ J B C ( θ ) = 1 N ∑ i = 0 N − 1 [ ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ) ]", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-11-causality-frames-15-16", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Causality", "title": "Slide 11: Causality", "frames": [15, 16], "html": "<p>arm moves correctly toward cup, but overshoots. What will REINFORCE do?</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot arm moving along a yellow arrow toward a cup, overshooting it; a coin with a minus sign marks the negative reward.]</span></p>\n<p>✅ first half of the trajectory was good! but REINFORCE <strong>penalizes all the actions</strong> in the trajectory</p>\n<p><strong>Causality</strong>: Policy behavior at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> does not affect reward at time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math> when <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t &lt; t&#39;</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t) \\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;})\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the inner sum]</span> “sum of future rewards”; brace: <strong>Reward-To-Go</strong></li>\n</ul>", "text": "arm moves correctly toward cup, but overshoots. What will REINFORCE do? [Figure: a robot arm moving along a yellow arrow toward a cup, overshooting it; a coin with a minus sign marks the negative reward.] ✅ first half of the trajectory was good! but REINFORCE penalizes all the actions in the trajectory Causality : Policy behavior at t ′ does not affect reward at time t when t < t ′ ∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ( ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ) ] [arrow at the inner sum] “sum of future rewards”; brace: Reward-To-Go", "t": 960}, {"id": "slide-12-high-variance-frames-17-18", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: High Variance", "title": "Slide 12: High Variance", "frames": [17, 18], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau^1</annotation></semantics></math>: arm moves vaguely toward cup but overshoots - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R = 99</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau^2</annotation></semantics></math>: arm reaches cup perfectly - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>101</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R = 101</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two robot-arm reaches; the first overshoots the cup (minus-sign coin), the second stops at the cup (green arrow).]</span></p>\n<p>REINFORCE: Both have great return, lets make both behaviors more likely!</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\theta \\approx \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s) \\cdot R(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mn>99</mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>←</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Huge gradient for your optimizer!</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\theta \\approx \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s) \\cdot 99 \\quad \\leftarrow \\text{Huge gradient for your optimizer!}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Effective signal is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>101</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>99</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">101 - 99 = 2</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; Signal-to-Noise: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/100</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>2% of the gradient is informative</strong></p>", "text": "τ 1 : arm moves vaguely toward cup but overshoots - R = 99 τ 2 : arm reaches cup perfectly - R = 101 [Figure: two robot-arm reaches; the first overshoots the cup (minus-sign coin), the second stops at the cup (green arrow).] REINFORCE: Both have great return, lets make both behaviors more likely! Δ θ ≈ ∇ θ log &#8289; π θ ( a ∣ s ) ⋅ R ( τ ) Δ θ ≈ ∇ θ log &#8289; π θ ( a ∣ s ) ⋅ 99 ← Huge gradient for your optimizer! Effective signal is 101 − 99 = 2 -> Signal-to-Noise: 2 / 100 = 2% of the gradient is informative", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-13-reducing-variances-with-baselines-frame-19", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Reducing Variances with Baselines", "title": "Slide 13: Reducing Variances with Baselines", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;}) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-b</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> substract a constant baseline, often mean reward: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} R(\\tau_i)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same two reaches, now scored <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>99</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R = 99 - 100 = -1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>101</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R = 101 - 100 = 1</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>by substracting average reward, we get negative gradients for below average behavior ✅</p>\n<p>Why can we do this?</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}[\\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, b] = \\int p_\\theta(\\tau) \\nabla_\\theta \\log p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, b\\, d\\tau = \\int \\nabla_\\theta p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, b\\, d\\tau = b \\nabla_\\theta \\int p_\\theta(\\tau)\\, d\\tau = b \\nabla_\\theta 1 = 0</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>✅ Subtracting a baseline does not change the gradient in expectation, it is unbiased.</p>", "text": "∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ( ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) − b ) ] [arrow at − b ] substract a constant baseline, often mean reward: b = 1 N ∑ i = 0 N − 1 R ( τ i ) [Figure: the same two reaches, now scored R = 99 − 100 = − 1 and R = 101 − 100 = 1 .] by substracting average reward, we get negative gradients for below average behavior ✅ Why can we do this? 𝔼 [ ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) b ] = ∫ p θ ( τ ) ∇ θ log &#8289; p θ ( τ ) b d τ = ∫ ∇ θ p θ ( τ ) b d τ = b ∇ θ ∫ p θ ( τ ) d τ = b ∇ θ 1 = 0 ✅ Subtracting a baseline does not change the gradient in expectation, it is unbiased.", "t": 1202}, {"id": "slide-14-advantage-function-the-ideal-baseline-frame-20", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Advantage Function <span class=\"own\">[the ideal baseline]</span>", "title": "Slide 14: Advantage Function [the ideal baseline]", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;}) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under the inner sum]</span> <strong>Reward-To-Go</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\;Q^\\pi(s_t, a_t) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_\\theta}\\left[\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;}) \\mid s_t, a_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p>What could be the ideal baseline?</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^\\pi(s) = \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid s)}\\left[Q^\\pi(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Accounts for the specific difficulty of each state, ensuring the robot is only rewarded for actions that outperform its own average expectation in that situation</p>", "text": "∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ( ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) − b ) ] [brace under the inner sum] Reward-To-Go Q π ( s t , a t ) = 𝔼 π θ [ ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ∣ s t , a t ] What could be the ideal baseline? V π ( s ) = 𝔼 a ∼ π θ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) [ Q π ( s , a ) ] Accounts for the specific difficulty of each state, ensuring the robot is only rewarded for actions that outperform its own average expectation in that situation", "t": 1321}, {"id": "slide-15-advantage-function-frame-21", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Advantage Function", "title": "Slide 15: Advantage Function", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<p>How much better it is to take a action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^\\pi(s, a) = Q^\\pi(s, a) - V^\\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, A^\\pi(s_t, a_t)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q^\\pi(s_t, a_t) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\pi_\\theta}\\left[\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;}) \\mid s_t, a_t\\right]</annotation></semantics></math> - expected return by taking action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> from state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^\\pi(s) = \\mathbb{E}_{a \\sim \\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid s)}\\left[Q^\\pi(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math> - expected return from state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>", "text": "How much better it is to take a action a in state s ? A π ( s , a ) = Q π ( s , a ) − V π ( s ) ∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) A π ( s t , a t ) ] Q π ( s t , a t ) = 𝔼 π θ [ ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ∣ s t , a t ] - expected return by taking action a from state s V π ( s ) = 𝔼 a ∼ π θ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) [ Q π ( s , a ) ] - expected return from state s", "t": 1321}, {"id": "slide-16-distribution-shift-sample-efficiency-frame-22", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Distribution Shift &amp; Sample Efficiency", "title": "Slide 16: Distribution Shift & Sample Efficiency", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p(\\tau)}\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T-1} R(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;}) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau \\sim p(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> assumes samples from current policy</li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Collect samples <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\tau^i\\}</annotation></semantics></math> by rolling out policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Compute gradient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta) \\approx \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\right)\\left(\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} R(s_{i,t}, a_{i,t})\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta + \\alpha \\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> &lt;- We need to <strong>throw away old data &amp; collect new data every gradient step!</strong> <span class=\"own\">[scream emoji]</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Vanilla policy gradient is On-Policy, unlike Off-Policy algorithms like DQN that can reuse past data</strong></p>", "text": "∇ θ J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p ( τ ) [ ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ( ∑ t ′ = t T − 1 R ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) − b ) ] [arrow at τ ∼ p ( τ ) ] assumes samples from current policy Collect samples { τ i } by rolling out policy π θ Compute gradient ∇ θ J ( θ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 0 N − 1 [ ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 ∇ θ log π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ) ( ∑ t = 0 T − 1 R ( s i , t , a i , t ) ) ] Update θ ← θ + α ∇ θ J ( θ ) <- We need to throw away old data & collect new data every gradient step! [scream emoji] Vanilla policy gradient is On-Policy, unlike Off-Policy algorithms like DQN that can reuse past data", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-17-policy-gradients-off-policy-version-importance-sampling-frame-23", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version <span class=\"own\">[importance sampling]</span>", "title": "Slide 17: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version [importance sampling]", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Can we use data from previous policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{p}(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Importance Sampling” plot over Trajectory Space (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>), x from -4 to 4, y = Probability Density. Solid blue bimodal curve: Target <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> - new policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> (peaks near <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±</mi><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 1.5</annotation></semantics></math>, density 0.4). Dashed orange unimodal curve: Proposal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> - old policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{p}(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> (peak at 0, density ~0.33). Orange dots: samples from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Green dots, sized by weight <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w = p(x)/q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>: large near the target’s peaks, tiny near 0.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0\"></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left; padding-left: 0\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}_{x \\sim p(x)}[f(x)] &amp;= \\int p(x) f(x)\\, dx \\\\\n&amp;= \\int \\frac{q(x)}{q(x)} p(x) f(x)\\, dx \\\\\n&amp;= \\int q(x) \\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} f(x)\\, dx \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathbb{E}_{x \\sim q(x)}\\left[\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} f(x)\\right]\n\\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> needs to have <strong>non-zero support</strong> where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x) &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math> to remain unbiased</p>", "text": "Can we use data from previous policy p ‾ ( τ ) ? [Figure: “Importance Sampling” plot over Trajectory Space ( τ ), x from -4 to 4, y = Probability Density. Solid blue bimodal curve: Target p ( x ) - new policy p θ ( τ ) (peaks near ± 1.5 , density 0.4). Dashed orange unimodal curve: Proposal q ( x ) - old policy p ‾ ( τ ) (peak at 0, density ~0.33). Orange dots: samples from q ( x ) . Green dots, sized by weight w = p ( x ) / q ( x ) : large near the target’s peaks, tiny near 0.] 𝔼 x ∼ p ( x ) [ f ( x ) ] = ∫ p ( x ) f ( x ) d x = ∫ q ( x ) q ( x ) p ( x ) f ( x ) d x = ∫ q ( x ) p ( x ) q ( x ) f ( x ) d x = 𝔼 x ∼ q ( x ) [ p ( x ) q ( x ) f ( x ) ] q needs to have non-zero support where p ( x ) > 0 to remain unbiased", "t": 1622}, {"id": "slide-18-policy-gradients-off-policy-version-trajectory-importance-weight-frames-24-26", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version <span class=\"own\">[trajectory importance weight]</span>", "title": "Slide 18: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version [trajectory importance weight]", "frames": [24, 26], "html": "<p>RL objective with importance sampling</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J(\\theta) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim \\bar{p}(\\tau)}\\left[\\frac{p_\\theta(\\tau)}{\\bar{p}(\\tau)} r(\\tau)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mover><mi>π</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mover><mi>π</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{p_\\theta(\\tau)}{\\bar{p}(\\tau)} = \\frac{p(s_1) \\prod_{t=1}^{T} \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)}{p(s_1) \\prod_{t=1}^{T} \\bar{\\pi}(a_t \\mid s_t)\\, p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)} = \\prod_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)}{\\bar{\\pi}(a_t \\mid s_t)}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_1)</annotation></semantics></math> and the transition terms <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> are crossed out: they cancel</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How do derive the gradient to update our new policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}</annotation></semantics></math> with samples from old <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_{\\theta&#39;}(\\tau)}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_t \\mid s_t)\\right)\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\theta&#39;}(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> new policy</li>\n</ul>", "text": "RL objective with importance sampling J ( θ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p ‾ ( τ ) [ p θ ( τ ) p ‾ ( τ ) r ( τ ) ] p θ ( τ ) p ‾ ( τ ) = p ( s 1 ) ∏ t = 1 T π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) p ( s 1 ) ∏ t = 1 T π ‾ ( a t ∣ s t ) p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) = ∏ t = 1 T π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) π ‾ ( a t ∣ s t ) p ( s 1 ) and the transition terms p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) are crossed out: they cancel How do derive the gradient to update our new policy π θ ′ with samples from old π θ ? ∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ′ ( τ ) [ ( ∑ t = 1 T ∇ θ ′ log π θ ′ ( a t ∣ s t ) ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ) − b ) ] [arrow at p θ ′ ( τ ) ] new policy", "t": 1681}, {"id": "slide-19-policy-gradients-off-policy-version-importance-weights-frames-27-29", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version <span class=\"own\">[importance weights]</span>", "title": "Slide 19: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version [importance weights]", "frames": [27, 29], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\frac{p_{\\theta&#39;}(\\tau)}{p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left(\\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_t \\mid s_t)\\right)\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrows]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> = old policy; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{p_{\\theta&#39;}(\\tau)}{p_\\theta(\\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> = importance weights</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim p_\\theta(\\tau)}\\left[\\left(\\prod_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_t \\mid s_t)}{\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)}\\right)\\left(\\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_t \\mid s_t)\\right)\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{t&#39;}, a_{t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the product]</span> substitute from previous slide. this product can lead to <strong>exploding/vanishing gradients</strong> for large T!</li>\n</ul>", "text": "∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ p θ ′ ( τ ) p θ ( τ ) ( ∑ t = 1 T ∇ θ ′ log π θ ′ ( a t ∣ s t ) ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ) − b ) ] [arrows] p θ ( τ ) = old policy; p θ ′ ( τ ) p θ ( τ ) = importance weights = 𝔼 τ ∼ p θ ( τ ) [ ( ∏ t = 1 T π θ ′ ( a t ∣ s t ) π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) ) ( ∑ t = 1 T ∇ θ ′ log π θ ′ ( a t ∣ s t ) ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s t ′ , a t ′ ) ) − b ) ] [arrow at the product] substitute from previous slide. this product can lead to exploding/vanishing gradients for large T!", "t": 1740}, {"id": "slide-20-policy-gradients-off-policy-version-per-timestep-form-frame-30", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version <span class=\"own\">[per-timestep form]</span>", "title": "Slide 20: Policy Gradients Off-Policy Version [per-timestep form]", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<p>In practice, we often consider expectations over timesteps instead of trajectories</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) \\approx \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(s_{i,t}, a_{i,t})}{\\pi_\\theta(s_{i,t}, a_{i,t})} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{i,t&#39;}, a_{i,t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the ratio]</span> more stable to compute, but hard to measure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(s, a)}{\\pi_\\theta(s, a)} = \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)}{\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(s)}{\\pi_\\theta(s)} \\approx \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)}{\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Often approximate with 1” (the state-marginal ratio <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(s)/\\pi_\\theta(s)</annotation></semantics></math>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Final form:</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) \\approx \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})}{\\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{i,t&#39;}, a_{i,t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "In practice, we often consider expectations over timesteps instead of trajectories ∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 T π θ ′ ( s i , t , a i , t ) π θ ( s i , t , a i , t ) ∇ θ ′ log &#8289; π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s i , t ′ , a i , t ′ ) ) − b ) [arrow at the ratio] more stable to compute, but hard to measure π θ ′ ( s , a ) π θ ( s , a ) = π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) π θ ( a ∣ s ) ⋅ π θ ′ ( s ) π θ ( s ) ≈ π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) π θ ( a ∣ s ) “Often approximate with 1” (the state-marginal ratio π θ ′ ( s ) / π θ ( s ) ) Final form: ∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 T π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ∇ θ ′ log &#8289; π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s i , t ′ , a i , t ′ ) ) − b )", "t": 1860}, {"id": "slide-21-off-policy-policy-gradients-algorithm-frame-31", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Off-Policy Policy Gradients Algorithm", "title": "Slide 21: Off-Policy Policy Gradients Algorithm", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<p>Take <strong>multiple gradient steps</strong> on the same batch</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Collect samples <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\tau^i\\}</annotation></semantics></math> by rolling out policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Compute gradient using <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\tau^i\\}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) \\approx \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})}{\\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{i,t&#39;}, a_{i,t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta \\leftarrow \\theta + \\alpha \\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a red outer loop arrow from step 3 back to step 1 (new rollouts), and a blue inner loop arrow from step 3 back to step 2 (several gradient steps on the same batch).]</span></p>", "text": "Take multiple gradient steps on the same batch Collect samples { τ i } by rolling out policy π θ Compute gradient using { τ i } samples ∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 T π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ∇ θ ′ log &#8289; π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s i , t ′ , a i , t ′ ) ) − b ) Update θ ← θ + α ∇ θ J ( θ ) [Figure: a red outer loop arrow from step 3 back to step 1 (new rollouts), and a blue inner loop arrow from step 3 back to step 2 (several gradient steps on the same batch).]", "t": 1920}, {"id": "slide-22-entropy-regularization-frames-32-34", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Entropy Regularization", "title": "Slide 22: Entropy Regularization", "frames": [32, 34], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What if the support set is zero, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x) &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math> but <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x) = 0</annotation></semantics></math>?\n<ul>\n<li>New policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns probability to action-state pairs that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> never took.\n<ul>\n<li>❌ Importance weight explodes, estimator gets biased!</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Solution</strong>: Add <strong>entropy bonus</strong> to avoid policy becoming too deterministic</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℍ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta&#39;) = L_{PG}(\\theta&#39;) + \\beta\\, \\mathbb{H}(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(\\cdot \\mid s))</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Temperature parameter</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Discrete Shannon Entropy</th>\n<th>Continuous Differential Entropy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℍ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{H}(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}) = -\\sum_{a \\in A} \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s) \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℍ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{H}(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}) = \\frac{d}{2}(1 + \\log(2\\pi)) + \\sum_{j=1}^{d} \\log \\sigma_j</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the continuous formula is the entropy of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Gaussian policy with diagonal covariance <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1^2, \\dots, \\sigma_d^2</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "What if the support set is zero, p ( x ) > 0 but q ( x ) = 0 ? New policy π θ ′ assigns probability to action-state pairs that π θ never took. ❌ Importance weight explodes, estimator gets biased! Solution : Add entropy bonus to avoid policy becoming too deterministic L ( θ ′ ) = L P G ( θ ′ ) + β ℍ ( π θ ′ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) ) [arrow at β ] Temperature parameter Discrete Shannon Entropy Continuous Differential Entropy ℍ ( π θ ′ ) = − ∑ a ∈ A π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) log &#8289; π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) ℍ ( π θ ′ ) = d 2 ( 1 + log &#8289; ( 2 π ) ) + ∑ j = 1 d log &#8289; σ j [Note: the continuous formula is the entropy of a d -dimensional Gaussian policy with diagonal covariance σ 1 2 , … , σ d 2 .]", "t": 1982}, {"id": "slide-23-policy-gradients-with-constraints-frames-35-36", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Policy Gradients with Constraints", "title": "Slide 23: Policy Gradients with Constraints", "frames": [35, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What if our policy changes a lot before sampling new data?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>∇</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} J(\\theta&#39;) \\approx \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})}{\\pi_\\theta(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})} \\nabla_{\\theta&#39;} \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a_{i,t} \\mid s_{i,t})\\left(\\left(\\sum_{t&#39;=t}^{T} r(s_{i,t&#39;}, a_{i,t&#39;})\\right) - b\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the ratio]</span> Unlikely action under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> gets high reward, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}</annotation></semantics></math> makes it much more likely and gradient explodes! <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.10</mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">new</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0.005</mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w = \\frac{0.10\\ (\\text{new})}{0.005\\ (\\text{old})} = 20</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Solution</strong>: constrain the policy to not change too much during gradient updates</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}_{s \\sim \\pi_\\theta}\\left[D_{KL}(\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid s)\\, \\|\\, \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(\\cdot \\mid s))\\right] \\le \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "What if our policy changes a lot before sampling new data? ∇ θ ′ J ( θ ′ ) ≈ 1 N ∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 T π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) π θ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ∇ θ ′ log &#8289; π θ ′ ( a i , t ∣ s i , t ) ( ( ∑ t ′ = t T r ( s i , t ′ , a i , t ′ ) ) − b ) [arrow at the ratio] Unlikely action under π θ gets high reward, π θ ′ makes it much more likely and gradient explodes! w = 0.10 ( new ) 0.005 ( old ) = 20 Solution : constrain the policy to not change too much during gradient updates 𝔼 s ∼ π θ [ D K L ( π θ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) ∥ π θ ′ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) ) ] ≤ ϵ", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-24-trust-region-policy-optimization-trpo-frames-37-38", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Trust Region Policy Optimization (TRPO)", "title": "Slide 24: Trust Region Policy Optimization (TRPO)", "frames": [37, 38], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Maximize advantage from old policy while not deviating too much</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">maximize</mtext><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{maximize}_{\\theta&#39;} \\quad L(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{s, a \\sim \\pi_\\theta}\\left[\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)}{\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)} A^{\\pi_\\theta}(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">subject to</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{subject to} \\quad \\mathbb{E}_{s \\sim \\pi_\\theta}\\left[D_{KL}(\\pi_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid s)\\, \\|\\, \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(\\cdot \\mid s))\\right] \\le \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Monotonic improvement guarantee</li>\n<li>❌ Requires the Fisher Information Matrix to solve the constraint optimization, is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(N^3)</annotation></semantics></math> number of trainable parameters</li>\n<li>⚠️ Conjugate gradient approximation reduces this to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(kn) \\approx O(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, but still ~20x more expensive than a standard gradient step</li>\n<li>❌ Scaling to deep networks requires additional approximations like K-FAC (Kronecker-Factored Approximate Curvature)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Trust region policy optimization</em>, Schulman, Levine, et al., (2015)</p>", "text": "Maximize advantage from old policy while not deviating too much maximize θ ′ L ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 s , a ∼ π θ [ π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) π θ ( a ∣ s ) A π θ ( s , a ) ] subject to 𝔼 s ∼ π θ [ D K L ( π θ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) ∥ π θ ′ ( ⋅ ∣ s ) ) ] ≤ ϵ ✅ Monotonic improvement guarantee ❌ Requires the Fisher Information Matrix to solve the constraint optimization, is O ( N 3 ) number of trainable parameters ⚠️ Conjugate gradient approximation reduces this to O ( k n ) ≈ O ( n ) , but still ~20x more expensive than a standard gradient step ❌ Scaling to deep networks requires additional approximations like K-FAC (Kronecker-Factored Approximate Curvature) Reference: Trust region policy optimization , Schulman, Levine, et al., (2015)", "t": 2222}, {"id": "slide-25-case-study-quadruped-locomotion-with-trpo-frame-39", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Case Study: Quadruped Locomotion with TRPO", "title": "Slide 25: Case Study: Quadruped Locomotion with TRPO", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video, “DARPA Subterranean Challenge Urban Circuit”, camera label BETA_ENTRANCE_CAM52: a legged quadruped robot walking through an underground concrete facility, with a person in a safety vest and a wheeled robot nearby.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Quadrupedal Locomotion over Challenging Terrain</em>, Lee, Hwangbo, et al., (2020)</p>", "text": "[Figure: embedded video, “DARPA Subterranean Challenge Urban Circuit”, camera label BETA_ENTRANCE_CAM52: a legged quadruped robot walking through an underground concrete facility, with a person in a safety vest and a wheeled robot nearby.] Reference: Learning Quadrupedal Locomotion over Challenging Terrain , Lee, Hwangbo, et al., (2020)", "t": 2341}, {"id": "slide-26-proximal-policy-optimization-ppo-frames-40-41", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)", "title": "Slide 26: Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)", "frames": [40, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What if just clip importance ration instead of expensive constraints to avoid getting too large?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">clip</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{s, a \\sim \\pi_\\theta}\\left[\\min\\left(\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)}{\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)} A^{\\pi_\\theta}(s, a),\\ \\text{clip}\\left(\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s)}{\\pi_\\theta(a \\mid s)}, 1 - \\epsilon, 1 + \\epsilon\\right) A^{\\pi_\\theta}(s, a)\\right)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace]</span> Force the importance sampling ratio to stay within a safe interval</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms</em>, Schulman, et al., (2017)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Pros and cons, second build (frame 41):]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Scales linearly <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> with the number of trainable parameters</li>\n<li>✅ Significantly easier to implement, works with standard optimizers like Adam</li>\n<li>✅ Foundation to train LLMs with RLHF or GRPO</li>\n<li>❌ No monotonic improvement guarantee</li>\n<li>❌ Near-on-policy, needs many samples</li>\n</ul>", "text": "What if just clip importance ration instead of expensive constraints to avoid getting too large? L ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 s , a ∼ π θ [ min ( π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) π θ ( a ∣ s ) A π θ ( s , a ) , clip ( π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) π θ ( a ∣ s ) , 1 − ϵ , 1 + ϵ ) A π θ ( s , a ) ) ] [brace] Force the importance sampling ratio to stay within a safe interval Reference: Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms , Schulman, et al., (2017) [Pros and cons, second build (frame 41):] ✅ Scales linearly O ( N ) with the number of trainable parameters ✅ Significantly easier to implement, works with standard optimizers like Adam ✅ Foundation to train LLMs with RLHF or GRPO ❌ No monotonic improvement guarantee ❌ Near-on-policy, needs many samples", "t": 2401}, {"id": "slide-27-case-study-openai-dexterous-hand-frame-42", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Case Study: OpenAI Dexterous Hand", "title": "Slide 27: Case Study: OpenAI Dexterous Hand", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>PPO sim2real achieves human-level dexterity on 24 DoF</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot hand wearing a black rubber glove (labelled “Rubber Glove”) holding a Rubik’s cube.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Solving Rubik’s Cube With a Robot Hand</em>, OpenAI et al., (2019)</p>", "text": "PPO sim2real achieves human-level dexterity on 24 DoF [Figure: a robot hand wearing a black rubber glove (labelled “Rubber Glove”) holding a Rubik’s cube.] Reference: Solving Rubik’s Cube With a Robot Hand , OpenAI et al., (2019)", "t": 2521}, {"id": "slide-28-full-ppo-objective-frame-43", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Full PPO Objective", "title": "Slide 28: Full PPO Objective", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>O</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{PPO}(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{s, a \\sim \\pi_\\theta}\\left[L^{CLIP}(\\theta&#39;) - c_1\\left(V_{\\theta&#39;}(s) - V^{targ}\\right)^2 + c_2 H(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;})(s)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[braces]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{CLIP}</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>Clipped Surrogate Objective</strong> (“Actor”); <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(V_{\\theta&#39;}(s) - V^{targ})^2</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>For Advantage</strong> (“Critic”); <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math> = <strong>Entropy Bonus</strong></li>\n<li>✅ Combines the best of value-based methods and policy gradients!</li>\n</ul>", "text": "L P P O ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 s , a ∼ π θ [ L C L I P ( θ ′ ) − c 1 ( V θ ′ ( s ) − V t a r g ) 2 + c 2 H ( π θ ′ ) ( s ) ] [braces] L C L I P = Clipped Surrogate Objective (“Actor”); ( V θ ′ ( s ) − V t a r g ) 2 = For Advantage (“Critic”); H = Entropy Bonus ✅ Combines the best of value-based methods and policy gradients!", "t": 2580}, {"id": "slide-29-actor-critic-methods-frame-44", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Actor Critic Methods", "title": "Slide 29: Actor Critic Methods", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Actor</strong>: uses Policy Gradients to predict continuous actions</li>\n<li><strong>Critic</strong>: uses Value learning to evaluate goodness of a state</li>\n<li><strong>Actor-Critic</strong>: ✅ Low variance from critic, continuous actions from actor, direct policy optimization\n<ul>\n<li>Learns from every step via TD updates, at the cost of small bootstrapping bias</li>\n<li>Optimal baseline to lower variance via advantage</li>\n<li>Robotics: often asymmetric information via privileged info for critic</li>\n<li>❌ Actor and Critic still near-on-policy, needs many samples</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Actor : uses Policy Gradients to predict continuous actions Critic : uses Value learning to evaluate goodness of a state Actor-Critic : ✅ Low variance from critic, continuous actions from actor, direct policy optimization Learns from every step via TD updates, at the cost of small bootstrapping bias Optimal baseline to lower variance via advantage Robotics: often asymmetric information via privileged info for critic ❌ Actor and Critic still near-on-policy, needs many samples", "t": 2700}, {"id": "slide-30-soft-actor-critic-frame-45", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: Soft Actor-Critic", "title": "Slide 30: Soft Actor-Critic", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Critic trained fully off-policy on a replay buffer!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔻</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{Actor}(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{s \\sim \\mathbb{D}_{replay},\\, a \\sim \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}}\\left[\\alpha H(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;})(s) - Q_\\phi(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha H</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <strong>Exploration</strong>: Entropy Bonus</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\phi(s, a)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <strong>Exploitation</strong>: if I were to do this action today, how many points would I get?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔻</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover><mi>ϕ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{Critic}(\\phi) = \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim \\mathbb{D}_{replay}}\\left[\\left(Q_\\phi(s, a) - \\left(r + \\gamma\\left(Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}(s&#39;, a&#39;) + \\alpha H(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;})(s&#39;)\\right)\\right)\\right)^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_\\phi(s, a)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> How many points did I think this past action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> in this past situation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math> was worth?</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> actual reward received</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover><mi>ϕ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> target network</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha H(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;})(s&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> this state leads to high reward AND gives you many different ways to succeed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the standard SAC actor loss, minimized, is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\alpha \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s) - Q_\\phi(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which in expectation is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\alpha H - Q</annotation></semantics></math>. The slide’s <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\alpha H - Q</annotation></semantics></math> has the entropy sign flipped relative to that if read as a loss to minimize.]</span></p>", "text": "Critic trained fully off-policy on a replay buffer! L A c t o r ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 s ∼ 𝔻 r e p l a y , a ∼ π θ ′ [ α H ( π θ ′ ) ( s ) − Q ϕ ( s , a ) ] [arrow at α H ] Exploration : Entropy Bonus [arrow at Q ϕ ( s , a ) ] Exploitation : if I were to do this action today, how many points would I get? L C r i t i c ( ϕ ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ 𝔻 r e p l a y [ ( Q ϕ ( s , a ) − ( r + γ ( Q ϕ ‾ ( s ′ , a ′ ) + α H ( π θ ′ ) ( s ′ ) ) ) ) 2 ] [arrow at Q ϕ ( s , a ) ] How many points did I think this past action a in this past situation s was worth? [arrow at r ] actual reward received [arrow at Q ϕ ‾ ] target network [arrow at α H ( π θ ′ ) ( s ′ ) ] this state leads to high reward AND gives you many different ways to succeed [Note: the standard SAC actor loss, minimized, is 𝔼 [ α log π θ ′ ( a ∣ s ) − Q ϕ ( s , a ) ] , which in expectation is − α H − Q . The slide’s + α H − Q has the entropy sign flipped relative to that if read as a loss to minimize.]", "t": 2820}, {"id": "slide-31-reparameterization-trick-frame-46", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: Reparameterization Trick", "title": "Slide 31: Reparameterization Trick", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Problem: Update Actor based on the Critic’s score.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>→</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Stochastic &amp;Non-differentiable</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_{\\theta&#39;}(s), \\sigma_{\\theta&#39;}(s)) \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad \\text{Stochastic \\&amp; Non-differentiable}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrows]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\theta&#39;}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> = Mean Action Dist.; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\theta&#39;}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> = Std Dev Action Dist.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">where</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = f_{\\theta&#39;}(s, \\epsilon) = \\mu_{\\theta&#39;}(s) + \\sigma_{\\theta&#39;}(s) \\odot \\epsilon \\quad \\text{where} \\quad \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> sampled from a fixed distribution without weights, so no gradients needed!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔻</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{Actor}(\\theta&#39;) = \\mathbb{E}_{s \\sim \\mathbb{D}_{replay},\\, \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\\left[\\alpha H(\\pi_{\\theta&#39;})(s) - Q_\\phi(s, f_{\\theta&#39;}(s, \\epsilon))\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33.]</span></p>", "text": "Problem: Update Actor based on the Critic’s score. a ∼ 𝒩 ( μ θ ′ ( s ) , σ θ ′ ( s ) ) → Stochastic &Non-differentiable [arrows] μ θ ′ ( s ) = Mean Action Dist.; σ θ ′ ( s ) = Std Dev Action Dist. a = f θ ′ ( s , ϵ ) = μ θ ′ ( s ) + σ θ ′ ( s ) ⊙ ϵ where ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) [arrow at ϵ ] sampled from a fixed distribution without weights, so no gradients needed! L A c t o r ( θ ′ ) = 𝔼 s ∼ 𝔻 r e p l a y , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) [ α H ( π θ ′ ) ( s ) − Q ϕ ( s , f θ ′ ( s , ϵ ) ) ] [Note: slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33.]", "t": 2881}, {"id": "slide-33-conclusion-frame-47", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Conclusion", "title": "Slide 33: Conclusion", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<ul>\n<li>REINFORCE, optimize policy directly\n<ul>\n<li>Causality + Baseline: reduce variance</li>\n<li>Advantage Function: V(s) as optimal baseline</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Importance Sampling: multiple gradient steps on batch</li>\n<li>Entropy Regularization: maintain support</li>\n<li>TRPO: bound how much policy changes via KL</li>\n<li>PPO: Clip ratio instead, still near-on-policy</li>\n<li>SAC: fully off-policy critic via replay buffer, most sample efficient</li>\n</ul>", "text": "REINFORCE, optimize policy directly Causality + Baseline: reduce variance Advantage Function: V(s) as optimal baseline Importance Sampling: multiple gradient steps on batch Entropy Regularization: maintain support TRPO: bound how much policy changes via KL PPO: Clip ratio instead, still near-on-policy SAC: fully off-policy critic via replay buffer, most sample efficient", "t": 3000}, {"id": "end-frame-48", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-8-how-to-get-the-gradient-expanding-the-trajectory-log-probability-frame-11", "label": "Slide 8: How to get the Gradient? [expanding the trajectory log-probability]", "html": "as written on the slide, the log-sum runs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 1 \\dots T</annotation></semantics></math> and the transition term sits outside the sum; the consistent form is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\left[\\log \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t) + \\log P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which matches the final line."}, {"slide": "slide-22-entropy-regularization-frames-32-34", "label": "Slide 22: Entropy Regularization", "html": "the continuous formula is the entropy of a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Gaussian policy with diagonal covariance <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1^2, \\dots, \\sigma_d^2</annotation></semantics></math>."}, {"slide": "slide-30-soft-actor-critic-frame-45", "label": "Slide 30: Soft Actor-Critic", "html": "the standard SAC actor loss, minimized, is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\alpha \\log \\pi_{\\theta&#39;}(a \\mid s) - Q_\\phi(s, a)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which in expectation is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\alpha H - Q</annotation></semantics></math>. The slide’s <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\alpha H - Q</annotation></semantics></math> has the entropy sign flipped relative to that if read as a loss to minimize."}, {"slide": "slide-31-reparameterization-trick-frame-46", "label": "Slide 31: Reparameterization Trick", "html": "slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33."}]}, {"key": "06", "dir": "week06_generative", "date": "Mon 23 Mar 2026", "topic": "Generative Models", "summary": "How to represent full action distributions: autoencoders, VAEs (ELBO, reparameterization), VQ-VAEs (codebook, straight-through estimator), diffusion (DDPM training and sampling, the closed-form noising shortcut, DDIM, classifier-free guidance) and flow matching / rectified flow, with robotics case studies: Diffusion Policy, DiT-Block Policy and pi0.", "key_ideas": ["VAE latents are sampleable because the KL term pins them to N(0, I)", "VQ-VAE swaps the Gaussian for a discrete codebook; a prior over code sequences generates new data", "Diffusion trains a noise predictor; x_i can be sampled from x_0 in one step", "DDIM is a deterministic, few-step version of DDPM sampling", "Flow matching regresses a constant velocity from noise to data and integrates it with Euler steps"], "papers": [{"title": "Planning with Diffusion...", "who": "Janner & Du et al., 2022", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09991"}, {"title": "Implicit Behavioral Cloning", "who": "Florence et al., 2021", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.00137"}, {"title": "Steering Your Diffusion Policy...", "who": "Wagenmaker et al., 2025", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.15799"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "qd6Ldsuu46I", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 6: Generative Models | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "51:04", "description": "Lecture 6 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to generative AI"}, {"t": 140, "title": "Autoencoders and VAEs"}, {"t": 401, "title": "Training variational models"}, {"t": 942, "title": "Vector quantized VAEs"}, {"t": 1443, "title": "Diffusion models"}, {"t": 2198, "title": "Conditional generation"}, {"t": 2411, "title": "Diffusion policies in robotics"}, {"t": 2620, "title": "Flow matching and conclusion"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, welcome everybody. Uh, today uh, we're going to talk uh, in lecture six about uh, generative models uh, for robotics. And uh, I'm very excited to talk about this um, very important topic for robot learning today. Um, let's start with a quick recap of why we care about generative models in the first place. Um, so the fundamental question or sorry, the fundamental issue in robotics uh, as we talked in the third lecture um, is that uh, the mapping from observations to actions is not a function. It's like really a distribution. And there are like &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; two uh, main reasons we uh, uh, covered why this is uh, to kind of motivate why this is, right? Uh, the first one is like the stochasticity, right? So, uh, for example, with the like this 7° of freedom arm, uh, we already mentioned that there are like infinite ways how we could uh, kind of reach the same uh, goal state. And the second one is uh, like the inconsistency of experts, for example,"}, {"t": 61, "text": "when they collect uh, demonstration where there might be um, you know, like uh, many different modes uh, how they collect um, demonstrations for the same task, right? Maybe one day they they they like operate the robot faster or they have like a different mood or one day they do a side grasp, the other day is like a top-down grasp. So, there are many ways how to like uh, achieve the uh, the same task and goal, right? And this essentially means that um, our policy for our policy, we would like to learn a distribution over actions. Um, and uh, the um, the the generative modeling part comes uh, handy uh, to help us solve this uh, modeling of the distributions. Um, if you remember, like we already covered that when you train like a deterministic policy with behavior cloning um, that um, you know, with uh, the mean squared error, like the math will kind of force us to look for the average um, of um, all the demonstrations, right? So, you might end"}, {"t": 120, "text": "up in the hitting the tree in the middle of the obstacle instead of going left or right. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um and so Um we So, we kind of agree we need to learn like if the full distribution, right? And the question is how do we actually represent this high-dimensional uh multimodal distribution, right? And so, we today we will cover like kind of three like three families of generative models that uh try to answer this question from different angles. Uh starting with the autoencoders that learn like a compact latent representation. Then diffusion models uh that rather than um encoding structure explicitly learn to iteratively denoise uh to clean samples and then we'll finish with flow models uh that learn a deterministic um invertible transformation that maps a sample distribution to a complex data distribution. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And we will also kind of cover how all these three are behind many of the advances in robot"}, {"t": 181, "text": "learning in the last couple of years. So, before we dive into the specific architectures, um kind of to um to uh make sure that we all share the same model of what we uh uh what we mean when we say like generative modeling is kind of the same underlying concept no matter like if it's a VAE or or a diffusion or flow or GANs or anything is that um uh generative model is a uh learned transformation uh from a simple tractable distribution into a complex data distribution uh P data. Um so, typically uh we will start with something easy like a standard Gaussian uh from noise uh and then we will want to map this into the target data distribution that we actually uh care about or model. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, it could be like images of cats uh like you see in the slide or uh for us um robotics this could be like robot action trajectories. Um so one way to handle this multi modality question we a little bit"}, {"t": 241, "text": "already like pre-faced in in the imitation learning lecture with the latent variable models, right? Which allows us to really index into different modes of the distribution like go left or go right via this latent Z where we can now say how to condition our policy on like going left or right. Um but we didn't really talk about much about how we can actually train to compress how we can learn to compress the experts action into this latent Z. So we can learn this mapping. And this is where the architecture of the auto encoder comes in and the idea behind is like pretty simple. You've probably seen this before. We have two networks an encoder F I that compresses the input X into a compact latent representation Z and then a decoder G theta that takes the latent code and then tries to"}, {"t": 300, "text": "reconstruct the original input back with for example L2 norm loss. And that's it like we don't need any additional labels or anything so it's pretty like self supervised. And [clears throat] the bottleneck here in the middle essentially forces the encoder to learn like this compressed of the data because the only way to achieve like a low reconstruction error is to preserve like the most important like features let's say, right? Of the data distribution. So this naive auto encoder can compress data that's true but it has some limitations. First of all the latent space has no structure so the encoder can place clusters anywhere it wants. There's nothing like forcing it to like enforce any structure in the in the loss or so. And the second one is the only regularizer is the size of the bottleneck itself, right?"}, {"t": 361, "text": "Um So, the dimension dimensionality of the of the Z bottleneck is what constrains how much information can pass through. Um but [clears throat] it doesn't say anything about how like this information information should be organized, right? Um and then third and kind of the probably like for us the biggest crucial limitation is that this is a deterministic model, right? So, each input maps to a single point. And so, there is really like no distribution really. And so, this means that we also can't like sample new data at test time if uh if it's a deterministic. And so, here is where the variational autoencoders kind of like come in to solve all three problems that we just mentioned. The key idea is that instead of encoding to a single point, we encode it to a distribution over Z. So, specifically the encoder in in like"}, {"t": 421, "text": "most popular case will um output the parameters of a Gaussian like a mean and a variance sigma. And then we sample Z from that Gaussian before passing it to the decoder. Um and then um kind of to enforce the the structure of the on in the space, we add a regularization term that forces this approximate posterior to stay close to our Gaussian prior. Um and so, mathematically our goal here is to maximize the log log likelihood of the data. Um so, log P of theta X. Um however, it's often um, as we discussed also in the third lecture, uh intractable to compute this directly. Uh, so in instead we maximize um, the evidence lower bound uh or the elbow. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, and so the reconstruction loss here is um, uh similar to um, to the knife out and"}, {"t": 482, "text": "quarter, just that now we have an expectation uh a set is not deterministic anymore, right? So now we are asking on average over all the sets I might sample given this X, um, how well [clears throat] does the decoder recover uh the original X. Uh, and then the the KL regularizer is uh what um, kind of like penalizes it from deviating too much from uh distribution. Maybe the the key message here is that um, or the key advantage is that now um, in principle at test time we can sample any Z uh from our uh P of uh Z and then decode it into a meaningful output. So now we really have a generative model. Um, but there is one problem that we have faced in the previous lecture, too, um, that this stochastic uh sampling step is not uh differential. Um, I didn't get to the chance to really talk about this um, uh during the policy gradient uh"}, {"t": 541, "text": "derivation, um, but here last week, but here it kind of appears again. Um, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so uh what we can do here is that instead of sampling uh Z directly from um, the distribution that the encoder predicts, we can uh rewrite it as a function as a deterministic function of um, the parameters um, and the and an external source of uh noise. Uh, and so we uh pull a random vector epsilon uh from a standard uh normal distribution, um, and like this has like no learnable parameters. Uh, and then we define uh Z um, equals uh the mean uh plus uh sigma and then like the element-wise product with epsilon. And you can see on the bottom like how now the computation graph pulls this kind of this randomness to the side. And so now now for the perspective from"}, {"t": 600, "text": "the perspective of the gradient now our mean and variances are just notes in a standard deterministic calculation and the gradient can flow. Okay, so we have seen like the nice graph on one formula, but why does this make sampling the sampling operation differentiable? And so there are core problem is that the computing the gradient of the loss with respect to the encoders parameters phi is difficult because the loss involves an expectation under a distribution that itself depends on phi. And so there are like two ways how you can handle this. The first one is what we saw last week, which is the score function estimator, which in RL we call we call reinforce. And here like you can use the same derivation we did last week with the log derivative trick"}, {"t": 660, "text": "to move the gradient inside. Um but one thing to notice here is that in this formulation the gradient only acts on the log of the Q term ignoring completely how the Z itself changes with phi. So this means that this will have very very high variance because the the gradient has no way to see into the function of Z itself. So basically the optima will be kind of guessing because it knows if a sample was good or bad, but it doesn't know how to shift the mu and sigma to make it better. And the second option is the re-parameterization re-parameterization trick that uh we just covered where by rewriting and substituting Z with the formula from the previous slide, we can see that P of epsilon is"}, {"t": 720, "text": "now completely independent of phi. So, we can move freely the gradient inside the integral again similar to last week cuz it's a linear operation. And using a Leibniz rule &gt;&gt; [cough] &gt;&gt; and once [clears throat] it's inside, we can convert this back into a expectation over epsilon. So, which in practice means that we can now sample one epsilon compute the gradient and that's it. We're done. Um Yeah. Okay. And um Yeah, so Mhm. Exactly. So, the key thing here kind of to remember is that now with this, we can kind of the gradient of the loss with respect to phi now equals to the gradient of F with respect to Z. So, this means that"}, {"t": 782, "text": "this is coming from the decoder and it tells us which direction like we should like move or update our Z. Um So, it kind of directly tells us like how our encode encoder parameters will influence our latent. Cool. So, after all this math, what does this actually buy us? So, if we look at this now, the latent space and compare it to the previous encoders, we have now a more continuous and structured latent space. And it's generative because now we can sample Z and like the decoder will produce meaningful outputs. And at [clears throat] test time we don't even need the encoder for generation. We can just simply sample from Z from our Z from our prior distribution and then use the decoder."}, {"t": 845, "text": "Um So, although variational autoencoders are powerful, they don't come with without limitations. Concretely, it has two kind of common failure modes. First, if you have like a powerful high-capacity decoder, in principle, it could also try to learn P of X directly. So, this means that it could learn to completely ignore the latent Z that you are passing to the decoder. So, in this case, your model will become like a unconditional Sorry, deterministic [clears throat] model once again. The second one is a bit more subtle. It's what's called the prior mismatch. So, imagine [clears throat] we have trained our model on these two modes of going either left or right, right? And now imagine that a test site this works beautifully. We have trained on"}, {"t": 901, "text": "this and now at test time we sample a set from our Gaussian prior that lands in in the middle in between left and right. Now, the [snorts] issue is that the decoder has never seen a latent in that region because we had no demonstrations, right? So, even though our math kind of forces our latent [clears throat] space to be continuous, the decoder will in that case like hallucinate you know something that will be completely wrong and this will be this is especially big issue in robotics where you don't want to be hallucinating like unsafe actions for example. One way to alleviate this issue is by replacing the continuous Gaussian latent set with a discrete codebook of K learn vectors in what are commonly known as vector quantized VAEs. So the intuition behind this is simple."}, {"t": 962, "text": "Imagine you have a dictionary or a codebook of K learn vectors. Now when the encoder looks at an image and produces a continuous vector, we don't pass that continuous vector to the decoder. Instead we perform a nearest neighbor look up. We find one of the vectors in our codebook that are closest to it and then pass that discrete code to the decoder. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; The encoder now essentially is forced to commit to one discrete code rather than producing a Gaussian distribution and this kind of eliminates this posterior collapse we just mentioned before. And so also there is no KL divergence to minimize and no way for the decoder to ignore Z because the Z is now kind of a hard discrete selection. Mathematically [clears throat] we are performing a quantization"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "with this arc of min look up operation. But again this is kind of a recurring pattern like there is no this is not per se differential operation. So concretely this means if we nudge a bit our latent uh let's say, like the Z to the left or right, um then uh in principle um the the closest neighbor uh will be still the same. Uh because um argmin is a piecewise constant function, uh so it means the uh effectively the gradient is like zero almost everywhere. So, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; how do we fix this? Um So, in practice, we used a uh a straight-through uh estimator, which uh in essence just says, \"Let's pretend the quantization step didn't happen uh during the backward pass and uh pass the gradients along.\" Um so, the high-level intuition of this is that if um Zq and uh Ze live in the same embedding space,"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "um then by by definition, they will be very close to each other. Uh so, the gradient that tells the decoder how to change uh Zq to reduce the loss is probably a a very good proxy uh for how the encoder should have changed Ze, so like the the latent produced by the encoder in the first place. So, this means that we essentially uh copy the gradient from the decoder's uh input directly back into the uh encoder's output. Um and um yes, and here um the SG in the formula stands for the stop gradient, uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; which acts as an identity in the forward pass, um uh but has uh zero gradient in the in the backward pass. And um yeah, and here you can see now how that kind of like um resolves uh in the backward pass to copy um the decoder's gradient directly to the encoder."}, {"t": 1140, "text": "Cool. Um so, how does the loss for training um the VQ-VAE uh look like? Um so, we have the standard reconstruction loss on the left, where the encoder and decoder are trained jointly uh via this straight through gradient estimator. Then the second term is the codebook loss, which moves the codebook vectors towards the encoder outputs to essentially learn a representative like kind of vocabulary of the data distribution. And then finally we have the commitment loss. So for this kind of proxy gradient that we just talked about with the straight through estimator, in order to for this to stay valid, we need to make sure that like the Z E and Z Q, so the encoder's latent and the quantized version actually stay close to each other. So without this penalty, the encoder could output values that are very far away from the actual"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "codebook vector. All right. So this training gives us codebook and a decoder. But the question is like okay, like how do we actually like sample, how do we generate like new data at test time, right? &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Cuz this doesn't seem to produce it. So in order to do that, to generate new data with VQ-VAEs, we have a second training stage. So the idea is pretty simple. So concretely once we have trained our VQ-VAE, we run this through our data set and collect the sequences of code indices that our encoder picked. So this means we're essentially translating our data set into a sequence of discrete tokens. Now the second training stage consists in training a prior model over the sequence code. Typically this could be let's say an auto regressive transformer. And so then this model will learn like given some initial first codes, what are"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "the next most likely tokens or or or codes, right? Um and then uh we finally get to the inference stage, right? Uh here we will now sample from our prior, um let's say like our auto-regressive transformer model, then do a lookup um uh from the indices to the vectors, and then use our decoder trained in the first stage uh to generate a clean sample of data, like an image uh or robot actions in our uh case. So, to wrap up the section on autoencoders, here is a visualization um of how the latent space evolves across these uh three formulations or the variants uh we discussed um in uh VQ-VAEs have fixed most of the issues uh we presented, like the posterior collapse uh or the prior mismatch. Um um they still have some issues, like uh codebook collapse, which means that, for example, it can be the case that many"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "codes uh get um go unused during training because the encoder converges to just uh using only a small subset of uh K vectors. Um this is why uh nowadays people use, for example, FSQ, uh finite uh finite uh scalar quantization as a more robust alternative, but we won't um we don't have time to go into the details of that. Um and some other limitations are like uh you need to know in advance uh the size of your uh uh codebook, for example. Um and even with discrete codes, um mean-seeking behavior at the output level uh remains if our reconstruction loss still uses MSE. Uh so, you can still get, like, blurry face images, for example. Um to summarize, uh VQ-VAEs uh turn continuous signals into discrete tokens, enabling transformers uh to reason over observations, uh actions, and behaviors in a unified uh token space."}, {"t": 1381, "text": "In robot learning, um you can find them, um, across, uh, many different applications. Um, like, uh, for example, they are very popular for, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; tokenizing images and videos, uh, as you can see, uh, on the on the left side. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh, in models such as, uh, Gaia, um, for uh, self-driving, uh, or Cosmos, uh, video models. Um, they're also quite popular for latent action learning, uh, where works like La Pa or or Genie use, uh, VQ-VAEs to quantizing to quantize the movement between, uh, frames of of games or egocentric human, uh, videos and to translate then these latent codes into robotics. Um, and my own work, uh, used it, uh, to, uh, quantize latent plans from play. Um, and finally, on the right side, um, you can also see, uh, VQ-VAEs uh, are also, um, used, um, sometimes to discretize, uh, continuous, uh, robotic actions, uh, for"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "VLAs and such. All right. [clears throat] Um, so, we have seen that, um, kind of like VQ-VAEs have still this limitation, um, of having like, uh, mean seeking behavior in outputs, uh, where we used the MSE loss, right? Um, so, rather than learning a compressed representation, um, of the latent space and decoding from the space, uh, diffusion model ask, uh, what if we learned, um, directly to model the full continuous distribution, uh, over X without any bottlenecks at all? So, on a high level, um, the diffusion, uh, has two processes, um, during the forward pass, we take a clean sample, um, like this image of the cat in on the, uh, of on your left. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and then iteratively add Gaussian noise"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "until all information um is lost. And then on the backward process, we train a neural network to denoise um that um um static step-by-step uh to learn to recover the clean image uh from that noise. Now, by framing um this problem this way, um we aren't asking the model um to guess like the image in one shot, but we are asking it to to we are asking it to make um small incremental corrections, which is a much easier learning problem than doing everything in one go. Okay. Um we'll look a bit into the details of how these things work now. Um so for the forward process, uh as I just said, like we are iteratively adding data. Uh if you look at the equation, uh we look we define the next state um as a weighted sum uh of the current state and some um additional noise,"}, {"t": 1561, "text": "random noise. Um the first uh term is uh in the equation, the signal decay. Uh at every step, this means we slightly shrink uh our current sample, pulling it a little closer to zero. And then the second uh term is the noise um scale. Uh the term uh beta beta i is what we call um the naise the noise schedule or the var uh variance schedule. Uh it's a hyperparameter that determines exactly uh how much noise noise we inject at every uh step. Um and notice we're using the reparameterization structure here again, uh writing the noisy sample as a deterministic function of the current state uh plus some fixed noise uh variable. Uh we will see the benefits of this in in couple of uh slides. Okay, what about the reverse process? In principle, the reverse distribution is uh intractable, right? Because um"}, {"t": 1622, "text": "infinite clean images could have produced um the noisy version. Right? There is no way to know like which one this is the correct one. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um so, here is a key insight that makes diffusion models actually trainable. Uh instead of asking the network to predict the um the previous image, so X uh I minus one, uh directly, which is uh a hard kind of ill-posed problem, uh we ask it to to predict uh something much simpler, um the noise that was added. Um specifically, we train a a net uh a neural network that takes the noisy image and a the time step as an input, and then predicts the uh the noise that was uh added at that step. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, and as I said, like why is this a better learning problem? Because um uh noise is just a sample from a standard Gaussian, and um so, it's like"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "a really like clean like uh easy regression target. Um and because we know exactly how much noise was added during uh the forward process, uh we can train this with a simple MSE uh loss between the predicted noise and the uh actual noise. Uh and as I as forementioned, like each um individual denoising step is uh a small kind of like local correction, uh and much easier than having to predict everything in one go. Um so, let's look at a training objective, and um you'll notice immediately that uh it's uh kind of the the same elbow objective um uh as uh in the variational autoencoder case, uh just that we extend it now uh across uh T uh steps. Um so there are three terms, the prior regularization um it ensures that after um T steps of uh noising um the uh"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "um the end result will look like a a Gaussian prior um and this is um kind of um satisfied by construction during the forward pass. So in practice we don't need to learn it. Um then the reconstruction term um we already had this before how well um can we recover? And then the the big sum in the middle is the uh denoising matching terms. So this asks like at each time step how well does our learned reverse process uh match the true uh reverse? Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; One thing to to note here is that um now because the forward and reverse distributions are Gaussians with the same uh variance their KL divergence um simplifies to the L2 distance between their means. And so with a bit of math um you can derive then um what they did in this paper, denoising diffusion probabilistic models um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; to this kind of uh more uh simple uh"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "term that uh you can see at the bottom. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um uh essentially like they realized that um you could throw away like complex weighting uh coefficients from the elbow objective and just minimize the uh mean squared error between uh the true noise and the predicted noise. All right, so now if you look at the formula to actually compute that loss, there is kind of like one small issue uh and that is that um the formula says that we will need to teleport from our clean data uh to some arbitrary uh sample noise state uh without iteratively uh doing all the previous steps. Uh let's say like 500 steps. Uh which would be a then also like OT complexity, right? Um so here in the slide you can see the derivation of how we can enable efficient training."}, {"t": 1860, "text": "Um we first for convenience define alpha the signal signal retention as 1 minus beta and then start by unrolling X1 and X2. Um then we substitute X1 into X2. Um and then we get this this mess of square roots on the right side of the slide. Now the key thing here is that um the two noise terms both come from independent Gaussian distributions. And we use the key property that the sum of two Gaussians is also Gaussian. So the two noise terms can collapse into a single noise sample epsilon. And then when you generalize this to I steps um the um the the signal coefficient becomes the product of all the alphas"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "and then this gives us the final form that allows us teleporting from X0 to any noisy XY in a single step enabling fast training. So now let's look at how we actually can generate So we'll do questions after if that's okay. Um how we actually generate samples with this trained diffusion model. Um and so the first one is like the DDPM sampling procedure. Um where we start sampling Gaussian noise um iterate from uh T uh down to one, um and then at each uh sampling uh sorry, on each denoising step, we uh apply um this this formula that you see uh on the slide. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the first one is we predict the predicted mean, uh so we uh take uh XY, uh subtract the predicted noise, scaled,"}, {"t": 1981, "text": "uh and then renormalize. And so this is kind of the deterministic uh denoising step, so moving towards uh where we think uh X0 will be. Uh and then the second step um is kind of a little bit interesting more interesting. This is um we add some stochastic um um Gaussian noise. And so like you might be wondering, okay, this doesn't sound like right at all, right? So we are um kind of like denoising, but like why are we adding like uh noise back in? And so this is a little bit subtle but important because if we run this process purely deterministically, all samples starting from uh different XTs would tend to collapse towards uh the same mean. And so um the injected noise kind of maintains the the sample diversity, uh ensuring that we explore the full distribution rather than always generating um the same average uh output. Um so"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "okay, and then kind of like in practice um so DDPM um can take a long time to denoise like an image. For example, it could be around like 1,000 steps. Uh so it's really slow and therefore also like difficult to apply uh in robotics where we care for example about our real-time control. Um DDIM solves this uh issue by decoupling the number of denoising iterations in training and in inference. Um the key insight is that uh instead of um kind of stepping through all the time steps, uh we define a small subset, uh maybe let's say just 20 steps uh instead of 1,000, and then jump in between. Um and then in each step um uh we use our uh denoising uh sorry, our trained noise predictor uh to do um something uh a bit more clever. So, we use uh the inverse of our teleportation formula from before to estimate what the clean image would look"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "like um based on our current noisy estimate uh state. Um and then once we have that estimate of the target, we re-noise it just enough um to reach the next step in our uh subset. So, for example, um uh jumping uh from like T100 to uh to 80. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and the key thing here is what is actually missing. Uh so, in DDIM, we don't have a noise term. Um so, unlike DDPM, which is uh which adds this uh noise term back at every step to keep things stochastic, DDIM is deterministic. And so, this allows us now to um uh essentially uh at inference time um to use uh much fewer uh denoising steps to produce a a clean image or or a robot action. Um one thing to notice is that everything we've covered so far is uh unconditional generation, right? So, we are sampling from our P(x) uh the the"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "marginal distribution of our data uh with no control whatsoever over what comes out at the end of this generation process. Uh and in practice, this is usually almost never what we want, right? So, in image generation, we want to condition, let's say, on a text prompt um or for our robots, we want um them to solve like a task. Um um there is a simple fix on how we can achieve this, right? So, we can uh just uh pass uh along like a conditioning information C um that can act as an additional input to the noise predictor and then we can keep like the entire training procedure the same. Um conditioning on C so on C sounds simple right but we can just pass it as an extra input but there is a subtle problem. Um the model again similar as in &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the VAEs can learn to ignore the condition and learn to denoise unconditionally anyway since that's often the easier"}, {"t": 2220, "text": "optimization path. Um the kind of in the literature the the first kind of solution to this was classifier guidance which required training a separate classifier like P condition given X at every noise level which is quite expensive. Um there is a nowadays the a different trick that's usually more popular called classifier free guidance um which solves this with a with a kind of a more elegant trick. Um so during training it means what they do sorry what they propose is that they randomly drop the condition information with some probability replacing the condition with like let's say a null token or something. Um and so essentially they are forcing the network to learn both the conditional and unconditional generation simultaneously. Um and then [clears throat] at inference"}, {"t": 2280, "text": "they kind of blend both predictions so you start from the unconditional prediction and then the guidance scale times the difference between the conditional and unconditional predictions. Um and that that difference is essentially the direction in noise space that the condition is is pushing us and this this this this W is the guidance scale which controls how hard we move in that direction. Um in image generation for example increasing this guidance scale a lot will give you like sharper more prompt consistent images but at the cost of less variety and and diversity. All right, so we've talked a lot about like kind of the math behind um diffusion. Um let's see how how this comes together in in robot learning. Uh in an actual robot system. And so the first kind of case study that does this is diffusion policy"}, {"t": 2342, "text": "uh from Chengji in 2023 who by the way will be today's guest speaker. Um the key insight is actually rather simple. Everything we just derived for image generation applies directly to action generation. So we just replace pixels with robot actions. Instead of denoising an image we denoise a sequence of robot actions AT. Um the training objective is exactly the same. So we use the DDPM loss for training, DDIM for faster inference with less denoising steps. And then crucially here the observations like the current camera image or the robot state get injected directly into the noise prediction network as a condition. Um the the best part is that unlike previous works the proposed approach also doesn't require any future state prediction. So it doesn't have to like"}, {"t": 2401, "text": "predict like future like images or anything. So it's quite quite more efficient. So the policy directly outputs the sequence of actions conditional on the current observations. Um uh maybe I'll go a bit more quick over this. The first thing to notice here is that the observation is computed only once before K denoising steps, right? So, we don't have to recompute this at every step. This is quite crucial for efficiency. And then for the noise prediction, they use a 1D temporal unit treating [clears throat] like action sequence like a 1D signal over time. And then they inject the observation conditioning via film conditioning. So, feature-wise linear modulation that learns to shift and scale the activations of the unit based on the observation embedding. And then they also"}, {"t": 2460, "text": "presented a transformer-based version which uses cross-attention to condition the action denoising on the observation tokens. One thing to notice here is that diffusion policies really handle multimodality quite well. And in general, they we've seen tremendous progress. Um They work really well in also in in real robotic systems, but they have like kind of one challenge that they are really like specialist models. They work very well to fit like a single task, but have proven to be a bit more difficult to scale to learn like more generalist or multitask policies. And especially the transformer-based version from the original diffusion policy is quite brittle and like sensitive to hyper-parameters. Um There are like a few kind of follow-ups that try to address some of some of these limitations."}, {"t": 2520, "text": "For example, we will talk more about this later when we talk about generalist policies, but for example, Octo which trains a high-capacity transformer network on 800,000 robot trajectories. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And then defines like this readout tokens that compresses the observations and tasks and then attaches a conditional diffusion decoding head that attends to this readout token that summarizes like the previous information. And then really can condition both with natural language or goal image observations, for example. Another interesting follow-up is the IT block policies where the key architectural innovation is replacing the cross attention for conditioning with adaptive layer normalization Ada LN zero which is borrowed from the diffusion"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "transformer literature for image generation. So here instead of having the observation tokens attend to action tokens through full cross attention, the conditioning information is injected directly uh modulating the scale and shift parameters of the layer normalization inside each transformer block. As you can see on the on the right. And this is kind of like more more more stable for training and like really scales and really key much better. And as you can see it it is also allows handling like really long horizon dexterous manipulation tasks such as sushi cutting. All right. So now we move off to the third and final framework flow matching. Until now we've been kind of destroying data with complex kind of noise schedules. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Flow matching instead asks why not just draw a straight line between the noise and the data."}, {"t": 2641, "text": "So instead of predicting the noise vector, here we learn a velocity field that transports samples along along continuous paths from noise to data. Um and the [clears throat] path that we choose here uh is uh the a straight-line formulation. Um so this means that at T0, um you have pure noise uh and then um at T1, you have a kind of clean data. Uh and the benefit is that we have no complex like noise schedule uh to tune. Um so it's just really like a straight uh deterministic path. Um the [clears throat] ground-truth velocity um is uh the point a vector that points directly uh from um the noise to the data. Uh and we train to our network to predict this velocity uh at any given time T. Uh so this means that at test time, we don't uh denoise in the probabilistic sense. Um uh we actually are integrating. Uh so we"}, {"t": 2702, "text": "we start with with noise and then use um uh an ODE solver uh like uh for example like a Euler um uh step to follow uh our learned velocity uh towards the data. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And um yeah, maybe one thing to to notice is also the relationship uh between these. Um so for example, if you have a linear schedule, um then flow matching and um uh diffusion are actually equivalent. Um Uh so diffusion predicts the the noise uh and flow matching predicts the the velocity. Um but they are related by a a simple reparameterization. Um in general, flow matching straight lines are uh much easier to learn um and they require fewer integration steps. Uh and as I said, they don't have like"}, {"t": 2761, "text": "very complex uh noise schedule uh hyper parameters, and this is why it's kind of like been replacing diffusion as the go-to framework in both image generation and robot learning. Um So, flow matching already gives us straighter paths than diffusion, but there is still kind of like one subtle problem when you randomly pair noise samples with data samples, the straight lines between them might cross each other. And when the trajectories cross, then the velocity field has to represent conflicting directions at the same point in space, which forces the velocity Sorry, the learned field to curve. Um and so, this means that this will then like kind of slow down like integration. Um So, rectified flow kind of is one solution to this uh uh problem. Um"}, {"t": 2822, "text": "and they show how to do this with kind of iterative procedure where you train a flow matching model once, then use it to generate coupled noise data pairs. Um So, you sample noise, you learn run the learned flow to get the corresponding data point. Now you have your pair of like X0 and your epsilon, and they are now kind of naturally attached by this flow vector, right? And now what you do is you retrain on these coupled pairs. Um because the pairs are now matched by the flow itself, the trajectories kind of untangle, and the crossings get eliminated. And paths become really straighter, as you can see in this visualization that kind of shows the path between uh original flow matching and the rectified flow."}, {"t": 2881, "text": "All right. Uh in robotics, um Pi Zero uh from Physical Intelligence was the first model to as far as I know to adopt uh flow matching. Uh concretely, they fine-tuned a VLM to produce uh actions via flow matching. Uh so, this was kind of the as far as I know the first flow matching VLA, uh which now has become kind of like the standard for our default for most VLAs. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; one nice technical detail here is that um instead [clears throat] of sampling uh the flow time uniformly during training, uh they propose uh sampling um this from a shifted beta distribution uh that focuses on the noisier uh harder time steps. So, where action prediction uh should be uh more difficult and where the model needs to predict more. Uh we will talk more about uh VLAs and generative policies um in lecture nine. Um all right. So, to to conclude uh today we have covered um three families"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "of generative models, uh each answering a different question about how to represent uh complex data distributions. Um so, [clears throat] we started with latent variable models, uh so autoencoders that gives us compressed representations via reconstruction. Uh we have we then saw how to add um kind of uh more uh probabilistic latent uh spaces via reparameteri- zation, uh and also with VQ-VAEs how to discretize them into a uh learned codebook. Then with diffusion models, uh we've seen uh with DDPM uh how to iteratively noise uh Gaussians uh into data. With DDIM, um how to make this faster and deterministic uh by discretizing a probabilistic flow um ODE. Uh and then with uh classifier-free guidance, uh how to steer generation by blending conditional and unconditional predictions. And then finally with flow matching,"}, {"t": 3000, "text": "we [clears throat] have seen how we can learn velocity field that transport noise along straight paths and rectified flow that straighten straightens those paths further for even fewer integrations steps. The [snorts] key takeaway for for robotics is that we started with this simple observation that robot like robot behavior and action prediction is inherently multimodal and with MSE regression and deterministic policies this will fail. And everything we've covered today from VAEs to diffusion models to flow matching is kind of the the the tools we we use in some of our latest and greatest robot policies. And [snorts] yeah, with that thank you for your attention. And yeah, let's switch to the papers."}, {"t": 3062, "text": "&gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to generative AI"}, {"t": 140, "title": "Autoencoders and VAEs"}, {"t": 401, "title": "Training variational models"}, {"t": 942, "title": "Vector quantized VAEs"}, {"t": 1443, "title": "Diffusion models"}, {"t": 2198, "title": "Conditional generation"}, {"t": 2411, "title": "Diffusion policies in robotics"}, {"t": 2620, "title": "Flow matching and conclusion"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "tvFvIEOBKfM", "title": "Cheng Chi: Robotics Beyond Algorithms [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Cheng Chi", "duration": "33:56", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Cheng Chi (Co-Founder & CTO of Sunday Robotic) talks about how practical \"tips and tricks\" of building real-world robotic systems, the kind of hard-earned lessons and insights that are difficult to learn in academia.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to Sunday Robotics"}, {"t": 151, "title": "Bridging academia and startups"}, {"t": 280, "title": "Hardware development challenges"}, {"t": 456, "title": "Managing supply chains"}, {"t": 680, "title": "Data acquisition and testing"}, {"t": 856, "title": "Software for hardware quality"}, {"t": 1003, "title": "Scaling data operations"}, {"t": 1290, "title": "The full-stack approach"}, {"t": 1421, "title": "Q&A and concluding remarks"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Hello, nice to meet you. Uh my name is Chong. Uh I am right now CTO and co-founder of Sunday Robotics. Uh and I have a little few slides about myself. Um so, you know, I started as an undergrad in University of Michigan. That's the first time I, you know, set foot in the United States. Uh and I'm very fortunate to be advised by Dimitri Berenson for my undergrad. That's the first time I had research experience uh and you know, understand what it's like for academia. Um and then after that, I did my PhD uh with uh my lovely uh advisor Peter Allen uh we started out in Columbia University in uh New York City. And then I moved with her to Stanford uh towards the end of my PhD. Uh and so, you know, during my PhD, there are two works that I really enjoyed working on. One is called diffusion policy uh or you I think told me that you guys might have covered similar material. Uh you know, there's a lot of works similar to diffusion policy now. Uh for example, I think there's people using flow matching, people use diffusion for, you know, video and"}, {"t": 60, "text": "action generation, people use diffusion for uh you know, like for uh locomotion. Uh turns out to be uh used by quite a few people. Uh and then uh my last work at uh during my PhD was called universal manipulation interface. Uh I would say this is much more uh hardware project than a software project. Uh really and the code base is the same as the diffusion policy. But uh you know, the project the the work's about this kind of open-source lightweight uh gripper that allows you to collect data without needing a robot. And I would say uh my my life after PhD is much more about scaling this uh universal manipulation interface project than uh scaling the diffusion policy project. Uh and yeah, right now also after do after my PhD, I actually I met uh Tony initially at Twitter after uh the uh diffusion policy announcement. Uh and then and then again, we got reunited at Stanford uh because Tony was also at Stanford. Uh and then we started this company called uh Sunday Robotics."}, {"t": 123, "text": "Uh today, I'm going to talk about uh like this lecture is called uh robotics beyond algorithms uh where I'll talk a lot about a lot of aspects that I learned after my PhD graduation. A lot of them are quite surprising to me uh and it'll be less so about, you know, generative modeling or traditional what I learned uh during my during my my student times. Uh but I wish I I knew uh before coming to the startup. Yeah, what would look like uh look at a modern robotics company and robot robotic system, uh by modern I mean, you know, after I graduate my PhD, uh pretty much everybody everything is focused around the policy. for different companies, uh it might be different uh so, from manipulation company will be uh you know, like a visual motor policy uh or in academia as well. And then for works that relate to locomotion, there's often like a blind uh RL policy."}, {"t": 180, "text": "Uh and then uh still, you know, we are even though people keep saying about end-to-end, there was there was never actually end-to-end. Uh there's always some software uh between uh the sensor uh and the uh and the policy and the actuators. Uh so, like there's a layer of robotic software often uses various kind of classical robotic algorithms uh from the simplest to like PID control uh to something more complicated like SLAM. Uh and then I would say uh these two areas are usually what I like during my undergrad during my PhD, these are what I what I understand that these are called robotics. And fundamentally, these are really a robotic algorithms. Uh I I think they're very popular because I'm in a CS department and these algorithms are they're very fun, they're very interesting uh and they're also very easy to teach because fundamentally, if you implement them, it's just software. Uh so, you just uh clone clone a repo and run some scripts. Uh it's very easy to design homeworks around. Um but after uh"}, {"t": 241, "text": "Oh, okay. Yeah, so like, you know, these are how I learned during uh uh for robotics about these aspects. Uh so, I learned these uh during classes. Um so, these are two of my uh uh favorite uh classes, modern robotics by Kevin Lynch and interactive robotics by uh Peter Ross Hendrick. Um these are, you know, when when I was in during my in uh in my undergrad and early days of PhD, I took all these classes and tell I taught me about math, theory, and algorithms about robotics. Uh and then during my PhD, uh uh robot robot learning became very popular and then I I start reading papers and these are how I learned about robot learning. Um but I would say uh after graduation, I realized that these are only actually a small part of robotics and there's like many other things uh especially hardware and data that's I would say very very critical to get robotic system work. And I would say it's actually a a very big differentiator between different works or between different teams. Uh so, first I want to talk about"}, {"t": 301, "text": "hardware. Uh and I want to use an example of like building a very simple component, you know, everybody pretty much everybody know uh needs to use at some point if you want to do robotics. Uh it's a camera. Uh so, you know, if I want to build a camera, uh like when I started a project, uh it will be like a kind of there's an analogy uh to when you're starting a software project. So, uh in software, you're probably let's say you don't want to work on some generative model uh project, then you go uh Google search uh some uh repositories that you're interested uh you download the repository. And for hardware, really it's, you know, rarely you want to start fully from scratch. Uh so, what you do is that uh you either find open-source hardware out there or you go on Amazon, buy something similar and you take it apart and see how it's made up. Um and often a very important step uh during this process to find reusable components. For example, uh you know, for the software project, uh also also"}, {"t": 361, "text": "often find a file called requirement.txt or it's called something something.conda.yaml file and you'll see a lot of dependencies. So, you install numpy packages, pytorch, opencv. These are almost, you know, uh like in every single project. Uh and in the in the camera hardware example, we see that there's important components. There's a lens, uh there's a sensor, there's ISP. You know, these are have system models and then we know that if you want to build a build a like a camera like this, you need these components. And in the software land, uh so, like when when you put all these components together, uh you know, in the software land, we call this you know, what I call requirement.txt. Really this is a it's a build instruction uh or it's uh it's like a software dependency list. Uh in hardware, the corresponding concept is called BOM, bill of materials. So, on the right, I have a screenshot uh for part of the diffusion policy project. It really is like what are the parts you need to buy, what the quantity, the link to buy, and how much does it cost. Uh and you know, once you have this uh"}, {"t": 420, "text": "the this uh like this list, uh you need to build your development environment. Uh so, in a software project, what I do is that you pip install. Uh and so, really that that is automatically fetches all the packages uh to your local environment so you can start finally start writing code. And in hardware, this is actually much more complicated uh because, you know, these components might come from different vendors. Uh like the easy thing is you can buy on Amazon, but sometimes, you know, things are out of stock. Uh so, like this is really it's called this process called procurement. Um you know, this is like you really really bare-bone prerequisite before you start any hardware project. And I would say, you know, this is kind of like how things end at at least for me uh when uh for UMI. Uh the reason uh is that and then everything is every working quite smoothly uh because all of my uh material bill of materials are on Amazon. So, uh it it is something called a retail channel. Uh that's that's something you can you can just, you know, quickly can buy. And really it it's retail, I think it's pretty powerful abstraction. It creates an illusion of low latency. So, you can get"}, {"t": 481, "text": "exactly what what you want very quickly. And the reason what you and the entire business of retail is this building this cash and physical caching layer. So, at where uh you know, the customers are, you have these shelves and or virtual shelves where you put stuff on. Uh and then uh and then when people want it, they just grab it from from this local cash. But really this is an illusion of how the physical world actually works. Um if you want to change anything about any of these components or if you want to order large quantity at a time, uh you either quickly exhaust these cash uh in in the retail channels or you will need to make your your supplier or your supplier's suppliers change something. Uh so, camera module is made of lens, PCB, and cable. Each of component have their own supplier. So, you know, like lens uh supplier buy glasses, buy housing. PCB supplier get like a design file for you and then like buy sensor, buy ISP, put it together. Really just everybody is in the business of buying stuff and putting"}, {"t": 540, "text": "things together and selling it. And this is what what we call supply chain. Uh and I I I think it's more appropriate to call it a supply tree. And you know, like as things go forward, uh the timeline like as like it's actually as like, you know, the more complex uh the product you work on, the timeline complexity blows up really really quickly. Uh so, imagine that if nobody's like everybody's operating on this like Amazon model like model where you talk to your supplier, uh you know, they buy uh 10 lenses and then they realize they're all stuck for the lenses that work for the glasses. Uh and then they need to go to their supplier and then supplier need to like into now okay, realize okay, now I'm out of stock, need to buy all those things. Uh it's going to take like many many months for a complex product to complete. Uh and and that's why uh you need something called supply chain management. Uh you know, this is really like a really important job, a full-time job. A person to not only talk to your supplier, but your supplier's supplier, supplier's supplier, and then"}, {"t": 600, "text": "to inform them, \"Okay, all I need is new part or I need this new product, and I need a lot of them.\" So, you you give all of them heads-up so that they can start ramping up all their production, uh and then they're all uh in sync in terms of schedule such that eventually you can get the part you want at quality. Uh so, to me, I think analogy is kind of like a UV. Uh instead of where pip you only fetch the the first layer of dependencies, you look at all of our dependency tree and make sure everything's uh consistent. Uh and the reason I spend so much time talking about supply chain is really this is something I learned the most about hardware. Is that uh supply chain uh for hardware sometimes either equally important or even more important because it dictates uh whether you can make a buy versus build decision. This is very similar decision if you're building hardware software as well. So, let's Can you hear me? Hello. Any question? No, no, all good, all good. We're good. All good. All right, all right. Cool. Yeah, so this is about buy and build decision. Uh"}, {"t": 661, "text": "so, if you know, if if you if you have a good supply chain management, then you really can optimize what you need to build or what what what what you need to buy. Uh and a common mistake I see uh you know, researchers going to hardware is that they try to build everything themselves, whereas many things are available out there. Uh and then now let's say we have all the parts. Now we need to actually build the glove. And then all right, this is uh this is what we call skill capture glove. It's you know, our data collection. Uh so, it's derived from UMi. Uh and then this is the 1,000th glove we made. We painted it gold. Uh and really uh the first step you need to do is assembly. Uh so, you know, you take all these part all these parts, you put them together manually. Uh for UMi, I have a video tells teach you how to do that. And then here, really you need something called standard operating procedure or SOP. Uh and then uh here like this is our uh a lovely technician, Chris. And then here is a piece of paper in front of him. Uh this is part of our SOP. And there's"}, {"t": 721, "text": "in practice many many uh like many many pages, and then it's divided across different work stations. And like what you usually see is our business build something. Uh you know, if you try to build anything really, uh you will quickly realize you have some issue. Uh often is about uh consistency. So, if you want to measure anything, you build camera or you build like a angle sensor, uh like you you measure some some reading, and then by build putting things together, things are almost never consistent uh across different units. And so, the first thing to solve that problem is to first you need to be identify uh each glove or each uh each product you built. Uh so, here uh you know, we print a label that have like a QR code for a serial number of of this thing. Uh and then inside database, we actually track uh all of the components. And then once you can identify uh a glove from another, uh the next step we do is calibration and QA. So, this these are also done together uh in software. So, here we really just plug in uh the"}, {"t": 780, "text": "glove into computer, you perform a bunch of actions, and that records how like the the min and max range uh of of of each joint on the glove. Uh and then uh use that, you can you can calibrate you know, like the reading of the sensor. And also you can use this as QA. So, let's say you're moving the finger, but the reading on the computer is not moving, you know something's wrong. Uh or even the calibration range is super off compared to other gloves, then you know that something some some mistake has been made. Uh then you need to uh either go send the glove back to our manufacturing or you need to reject it. And again, like when I when I talk about these things, it sounds very simple, but when you're making, you know, hundreds of gloves, thousands of gloves, that should become a very very challenging task. You know, like when when human is doing any kind of work, you make mistakes. And then, you know, I I can I can see if you build thousands thousands of them, the probability of mistake is extremely high. Um And really here I think uh like like Henry is our founding software engineer. He built a critical tool uh which is called this is really"}, {"t": 840, "text": "to track all the data about glove uh in the in the software system. Uh and then and then there's a step after that where uh pack and ship. So, you print a shipping label, you put everything in a box, and then you ship it out. So, this entire process is called FATP. It's called final assembly, test, and pack up. And this is probably like when you hear about manufacturing, this company called called Foxconn, uh you know, things like that, they do this kind of work for Apple. Uh these are really when people think about factory, this is what's happening in the factory. It's called FATP. And I really I think like for for uh for this aspect, uh the the biggest thing I learned is the power of software. Um so, you know, when you when you're building very complex hardware, there's a lot of moving parts. There's a lot of people involved. There are there's a lot of components. And in our case, the quality of this hardware we build directly impacts the quality of data that that we can get. And therefore, it is very very critical for us to be able"}, {"t": 900, "text": "to like have a visibility to every aspect of of this process. Uh and that's why, you know, uh we we we uh Henry built like actually he he he proposed to build a software for us uh like because he you know, he really he go to the uh eats dinner on the same table with people who put the glove together and then buy all the parts. And he also goes to gym together with them. He really is in constant collaboration uh with people actually doing the work. So, he he quickly realized that there's a huge bottleneck uh that's like slowing down our production is just that people that the information are scattered around. People need to install these like random Python scripts here and there, and that really uh drives down efficiency. Uh so, what we found that what in the end we were able to do is uh through the software, we're able to track every single component uh at of the glove and who built the glove, when when was the glove built, how was the glove calibrated, and then we can track it to every single piece of data that we collect. Uh and then so, eventually and"}, {"t": 961, "text": "this this information is available all the way to our training data loader. Therefore, researcher, if they think something sucks about a specific kind of glove, uh they can just change change a uh data loading config, and that will all be handled. So, we found that this is actually very critical and uh in the end a very impactful work. Um I think the my takeaway here uh is just that uh many times uh there exists a software solution for a hardware problem and vice versa. And often that like this the solution and the problem space may not exist in the same in the same domain. And that's why we need people who understand the entire cycle or like who understand multiple aspects to find optimal solution. Yeah. So, the next aspect we'll talk about is data collection. You know, this will go less in depth, uh but I really want to tell a few stories. Uh so, you know, when we started uh really so I I during my PhD as uh reading the about researchers collecting data. So, these are my my co-author, uh"}, {"t": 1022, "text": "Shu and Jinjia uh from both from Curran's lab. Uh so, we we take this UMi gripper, we go everywhere around Stanford or in the area, we collect data, and then train the model. Um and this is like I would say the easiest mode data collection because uh you know, everybody is researcher. Everybody can understand what it takes to train a model, and everybody understands like uh have good intuition about how they collect data. The data the quality of data directly impacts the uh the the end policy you want. Uh but uh things get a lot more complicated when you need to scale data collection operation. Uh so, at Sunday, we have three generations of data collection owner. Uh so, the first uh owner was as my co-founder, Tony. Uh he started out as a PhD student at uh at Stanford, and he's now CEO of company. He started out as CEO of company. Uh and he really go like, you know, hand hand hand in hand hand by hand like, you know, recruited the first 20 member developers. Uh these"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "like are terminology for data collection uh data collector. And so, uh it's it's about, you know, knocking on doors, about posting uh random posts on Craigslist, trying to find uh like people to apply. And then after that, uh Camilla take over. Uh Camilla, she already started uh originally started as a campaign manager like for a political campaign in local uh mayor for for Honolulu uh in Hawaii. And then she uh become our head of operations and took over the data operations from Tony. And then she really scaled our data collection operation from 20 people to 200 people. Uh and during this process, uh you know, we need to find we need to like things completely changed. We need to have a formalized process of recruiting, formalized process of qualification, and then, you know, like a streamlined process of shipping uh items out. Um but at this layer, uh she still can directly interact and understand like every every person who collect data. Uh but eventually we uh like we realized"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "that that model uh cannot scale beyond this number. This is like for one person, this is about as much people you can keep a relationship as possible. So, Perry is our our our our right now current owner of our uh of our data. So, he actually started out as a data labeler at Tesla uh for Tesla autopilot. And then he grew all the way to leading all of the data labeling operation inside Tesla. Uh and then now he's our head of data. And he's really growing from this 200 people that Camilla left, and now to thousands of people that collect data for us every day. Um and one thing I really learned the most about uh about data operations is that uh so, when when when I started out, you know, I'm I'm a technical person. So, I uh I want to I I want to write good code, write good user interface. I also maybe there's some like AI generated instruction that can help people collect data more. But what I really learned is that uh data"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "data collection is an operation. And operations is really all the way about people. So if you if you understand people and you can build a good communication, you can build good trust, then really you don't need much fancy software. Just a Slack channel and then some spreadsheet will be good enough for like let's say a 200 people. Uh but you know if you if you don't get the people part right, people don't feel like they're they're valued, they're respected, and they don't they don't clearly understand exactly what you need and exactly what you don't want, then actually there's no amount of software, there's no amount of technical work that you can you can fix that hole. Uh and one thing we found really cool is that you know once people are really motivated, they understand like they they really want to contribute to actually make the home robot reality, uh people started going all the way to help us. So when we started at Sunday, actually in the beginning we're doing some kind of like Android phone-based tracking solution and then it was like"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "really not robust against white walls. So one of our memory memory developer, he invented this method, he put sticky notes on the on the wall by trial by trial and error and realized that these can add features to the wall and can really help stabilize the system. And like really just like you know being able to fully engage with the community of memory memory developers really really help us early on when we started when when the technology technology is not not mature. Yeah, so in the end data is about people. As like there's something about technology, but people is the most important aspect. Uh I would say like the reason I I put put together these slides is that you know in my in my the way I I grew up and the way I learned robotics is that like my my my my vision about robotics all about algorithm. It's how can I make the algorithms better, how we get you know different paradigm of learning different paradigm of data that that is able to"}, {"t": 1322, "text": "you know make robot from not working to working, which I think is very important. But I will also say that the world is actually quite big compared to academia. There's many in order to make something work actually work in the you know in the real world to build an actual product that some people I actually use. Uh there's a long way to go and a lot to learn beyond just what what I learned in academia to what I actually what what I need to do on a daily basis. And and that's what I think is like very very fun, very interesting. And I wish I knew these things before I started the company and like I wish that there's a lecture during my undergrad that teach me all these things. And that's what I'm doing right now. And really during this process, even half this idea during my PhD is that I think being able to operate operate across a stack is really really important. Uh so in academia our education system is usually often structured in a way that's like a vertically divided. So you know you are"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "a mechanical engineer student. You are a software like computer science PhD student. You're an electrical engineer PhD student. Or in robotics you have you are slam person, you are you are learning person. But really what I found is that to actually get robots to work, it's robot robotics really in the intersection of every single engineering discipline. And many times a problem in one area requires a solution in a different area. And therefore being able to have a visibility and intuition across the entire stack from hardware to software to learning to data really help you to find a good solution quickly and help you resolve issues quickly. Uh and that's really the people we found. So I'm extremely fortunate to work with I think now like close to 70 people at Sunday Robotics. Uh we really focus on doing doing our hiring and recruiting process on people who understand who understand multiple discipline at once. And what I found is that there's there's a commonality among them. It's often"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "about passion and curiosity. So if someone only like interested in like diving deep into one aspect or they just want to follow the flow, you know, like whatever school teach me, now I'm a mechanical engineer, I'll keep doing the same thing, nobody's like the our education system is does not provide enough guidance on how do you actually acquire all these breadth of knowledge. And what we found is that people who are really really passionate about what they're doing and really curious about how things work, they will naturally uh you know, kind of diverge from what they're doing as their main job to to to see okay, you know, the like they'll take apart like a clock, they'll take apart like a robot and then they will really understand how things work underneath. And we found that these kind of like a breadth of understanding is extremely critical to get robotics to work. And yeah, toward the end, so if you think you found a like-minded people, you also you know, enjoyed this kind of full stack moment or learn"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "you aspire to become like a full stack roboticist, you want to understand all everything, please check out our website. Yeah, that's it. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Hi, thanks for the talk. I'm pretty curious about your your approach to locomotion because a lot of companies like Figure and others are going for legged humanoids and you guys have this this rolling base approach. But again, like Sunday Robotics is targeting the home. Um you know, is your is your plan to to move forward like yeah, what's your approach to to locomotion for you know, houses that have multiple floors, things like that. Is it kind of like a perfect concept for now or is it something that you guys are planning on working on? Yeah, I think this is a very good question. Uh I would say the the way we think about like a problem and a big companies actually very different. Like a big company, they need to address the entirety market of human otherwise it's not worth it for them. But as a startup,"}, {"t": 1562, "text": "it's very important for us to focus on a small like a small subset like a niche category of people and really satisfy their their need. And we believe there's enough people where there's only one floor and and then they will they will happily buy a robot. And so for our very for our very first product, we'll like you know, we're definitely going with like the wheel-based approach. I think that's good enough. The robot is passively stable in any state. If you cut power, it's not going to fall, it's going to not going to hurt anybody. And I think that's a really difficult aspect to guarantee for a you know, for a bipedal robot. So I would say you know, eventually I think that's very something very very interested in exploring. But like like I think before there's a consensus what does safety even mean for a humanoid in the home, I will be very cautious about that approach. Thanks for a a great talk and a very unique one on on the full stack today. I had a question on on the the learning aspect and the data aspect that you"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "spoke a little bit about at the end. Um you talked about the the work that Perry and his his team are doing in in data labeling and data collection. Do you feel like human demonstration trajectories created by that team are sufficiently scalable for the the learning that you're trying to do with with your robots or are you guys also exploring some of the the recent directions that have been seen in in video models and world models that try to learn from more internet scale data as well? Yeah, so I can't say too much about our future research direction, but I would say even within the organization, how we collect data actually changes over time quite significantly. And then the kind of data we collect changes significantly. Really like everything is a variable. The only thing constant is the people. It's it's the same people collecting data and people have the same need, same thing desire and they're motivated by the same thing. And that's why I emphasize so much. Like once you I think you you build in a good good intuition about how people operate, I think it's become a lot easier."}, {"t": 1683, "text": "Um I'm a really big fan of this method using the glove for extracting data. Um and what I'm really interested in is also when you said okay, it's not just a question of software, it's also hardware and supply chain. How did you go about thinking okay, this is these are enough degrees of freedom, these are enough sensors. For example, do you want to integrate tactile feedback to improve the policy? How many cameras do you have on your glove? Like that's a system-based question. How did you guys go about that when you designed the glove and the manipulator itself? Yeah, very good question. I would say like there's a couple philosophies we follow. Number one, like we follow like you know, simplicity is really the name of the game here. So like hardware is not like software. Like every complexity you introduce like exponentially grow up in terms of probability of that causing a failure. So really we we think that having the bare minimum in terms of"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "complexity is key to actually ship something that's reliable. Um and so in terms of like when we started prototyping, it's really about our engineering intuition. So the Tony and I, we have the same we did a lot of robotic during our PhD. We understand roughly what kind of geometry, what kind of degree of freedom is enough for the task we care about and and for what sensors we use. And then after that, it's all about iteration. So we have like I think for the glove we iterated probably like 100 150 times of like various changes versions of of CAD. And then you print a glove, you try it, you try on different things. It's really you know, it's a brand new thing, nobody has done it before. So you just have to try a lot of things. And that's why I think having hardware and software and learning in the same like under the same roof so important. It's allow you to have a very, very tight feedback loop on your entire system, not just from each component. Uh when you're getting that much data from that many users, how do you maintain the data quality and like"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "filter it? And have you ever gotten weird data from users? Yeah, that's a that's you know, very common problem. Uh I would say, look, filtering is definitely very important. But I would say it's actually much more important uh for people to like number one get clearly communicated of what you want. And to make sure our people like what they're trying to do is aligned with what what you're trying to do. If everybody all they're trying to do is to game the system, there's actually very like there are there's a million edge cases you wouldn't thought of. But if you they understand that okay, I want this to I want I to use the data to help the robot to work. And these are the thing these are the aspect that a robot will likely care about. And uh these like these are like the impact of the data. I think people often quickly align with what we want to do. Do you have time for one more question? Or do you have to go? &gt;&gt; I have time. Hi. Hey. Um what's your relationship with data collectors? Like do you pay them? And like beyond that, um how does [laughter] how do you or Perry like motivate them to collect like"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "more or more quality data for you? Like how do you nudge them? Yeah, yeah. So right now I will pay them. Uh and then I think that's very important you know you know getting other people's time uh like you you have to pay them uh I would say. Uh I think there are uh there are many attempts uh I think in industry of uh just collecting data in a in a structured way. I think those those data are still very helpful. Uh but often uh what you find is that the the the distribution you want uh from the data is not exactly the same as the distribution in the real world. Sometimes pretty much you you want to do important sampling for aspects you care about. Uh in those cases uh you to instruct some people instruct people in a very detailed way. And what's the second half of the question? How about the motivation beyond payment? Any motivation beyond paying them? What's the second part? &gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say uh just uh you know, there are people who is you know, stay at home mom. There are people who are like actually like you know, very like old people that like they just"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "want to help their children for next generation. Well, I think it's just that uh like just like I feel like a lot of people like they want like they understand that there's a wave of AI robotics that's happening. A lot of them really want to be part of it. But you know, they they they don't actually have like a like a entry for them to you know, they're not like you guys you're where you this nice lecture hall, you can take classes, you can code. And then and we can understand how to how to help them. Like really there are many people interested. Uh but we need to explain to them what's the clear connection between the work they're doing. And like what actually how does that kind of impact other people in the other in the world. And then we found that helping them build a connection to be very important. Um Do you have time for one last question? Or do you need to go? Uh yeah, I have time. Thank you. Um I assume that uh changing the last uh in the last year of your PhD changing the university have had positive impact"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "to attracting more investors. And probably it was the goal that um you change it. And I was wondering how was it possible if it may if I may ask and um um yeah &gt;&gt; Oh yeah, well so so I was that's actually quite different. So uh that's my my my my advisor changed university, not me changing the university. And then she took me with her. Uh so I mean, I think you like I think PhD and undergrad are quite different. I think for undergrad there are a lot of transfer opportunities. Uh and I would say for PhD it's a much more about the a specific advisor you're working with than the university. Uh so you when you see a faculty move you know, across the uh different uh places, they often take their students with them. Thank you so much. Yeah, cool. Uh I think we are done. Uh let's thank again Cheng for his very insightful talk. &gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to Sunday Robotics"}, {"t": 151, "title": "Bridging academia and startups"}, {"t": 280, "title": "Hardware development challenges"}, {"t": 456, "title": "Managing supply chains"}, {"t": 680, "title": "Data acquisition and testing"}, {"t": 856, "title": "Software for hardware quality"}, {"t": 1003, "title": "Scaling data operations"}, {"t": 1290, "title": "The full-stack approach"}, {"t": 1421, "title": "Q&A and concluding remarks"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Sunday Robotics (co-founder)"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 23.03.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (38 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week06_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/qd6Ldsuu46I (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_cheng_chi.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 6: Generative Models</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 23.03.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 6: Generative Models Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 23.03.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-recap-why-generative-modeling-for-robotics-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Recap: Why Generative Modeling for Robotics?", "title": "Slide 1: Recap: Why Generative Modeling for Robotics?", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<p><strong>Stochasticity:</strong> Many (<strong>infinite</strong>) ways to close a drawer with a 7-DoF robot!</p>\n<p><strong>Expert Inconsistency</strong>: Humans operators might use different ‘<strong>modes</strong>’ across trials</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three photos of a robot arm closing the same drawer with clearly different gripper poses.]</span></p>", "text": "Stochasticity: Many ( infinite ) ways to close a drawer with a 7-DoF robot! Expert Inconsistency : Humans operators might use different ‘ modes ’ across trials [Figure: three photos of a robot arm closing the same drawer with clearly different gripper poses.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-recap-multimodal-behavior-frame-3", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: Recap: Multimodal Behavior", "title": "Slide 2: Recap: Multimodal Behavior", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Deterministic MSE BC policy will fail due to “<strong>averaging</strong>” of <strong>modes</strong>!</li>\n<li><strong>Goal</strong>: learn the <strong>full distribution</strong>!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two photos of a snowy slope with a lone tree. Left: ski tracks split and pass the tree on both sides. Right: a skier who took the average of the two tracks and went straight into the tree.]</span></p>", "text": "Deterministic MSE BC policy will fail due to “ averaging ” of modes ! Goal : learn the full distribution ! [Figure: two photos of a snowy slope with a lone tree. Left: ski tracks split and pass the tree on both sides. Right: a skier who took the average of the two tracks and went straight into the tree.]", "t": 61}, {"id": "slide-3-generative-modeling-frame-4", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Generative Modeling", "title": "Slide 3: Generative Modeling", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Goal</strong>: Learn complex, <strong>full distributions</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">in robotics </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x) \\rightarrow \\text{in robotics } \\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>How to Encode a Distribution</th>\n<th>How to Sample from a Distribution</th>\n<th>How to Transport through a Distribution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>VAEs</td>\n<td>Diffusion Models</td>\n<td>Flow Models</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Goal : Learn complex, full distributions p θ ( x ) → in robotics π θ ( a t ∣ s t ) How to Encode a Distribution How to Sample from a Distribution How to Transport through a Distribution VAEs Diffusion Models Flow Models", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-4-generative-modeling-noise-to-data-frame-5", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Generative Modeling <span class=\"own\">[noise to data]</span>", "title": "Slide 4: Generative Modeling [noise to data]", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>A generative model converts samples from an initial distribution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)</annotation></semantics></math> to a data distribution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{data}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a square of random colour noise -&gt; <strong>Generative Model</strong> (trapezoid) -&gt; a photo of a cat.]</span></p>", "text": "A generative model converts samples from an initial distribution p ( z ) to a data distribution p d a t a ( x ) [Figure: a square of random colour noise -> Generative Model (trapezoid) -> a photo of a cat.]", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-5-latent-variable-models-frame-6", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Latent Variable Models", "title": "Slide 5: Latent Variable Models", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Output still gaussian, but receives additional input</li>\n<li>Can represent “any” distribution</li>\n<li>Popular: conditional VAEs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(\\mathbf{a} \\mid \\mathbf{o}, z) = f_\\theta(\\mathbf{o}, z)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Conditional Decoder</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the ski slope with two tracks around the tree, the left track labelled <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{left}} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and the right <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{right}} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Output still gaussian, but receives additional input Can represent “any” distribution Popular: conditional VAEs π ( 𝒂 ∣ 𝒐 , z ) = f θ ( 𝒐 , z ) [arrow at f θ ] Conditional Decoder [Figure: the ski slope with two tracks around the tree, the left track labelled z left ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) and the right z right ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) .]", "t": 241}, {"id": "slide-6-autoencoder-frame-7", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Autoencoder", "title": "Slide 6: Autoencoder", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Compress input <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math> into a compact latent representation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math>, then reconstruct <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&#39;</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: graph <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\to z \\to x&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> above a network diagram: <strong>Encoder</strong> -&gt; <strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> Latent</strong> -&gt; <strong>Decoder</strong>, with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z = f_\\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> under the encoder and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&#39; = g_\\theta(z)</annotation></semantics></math> under the decoder.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reconstruction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L} = \\underbrace{\\left\\| x - g_\\theta\\big(f_\\phi(x)\\big) \\right\\|^2}_{\\text{Reconstruction}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Compress input x into a compact latent representation z , then reconstruct x ′ [Figure: graph x → z → x ′ above a network diagram: Encoder -> z Latent -> Decoder , with z = f ϕ ( x ) under the encoder and x ′ = g θ ( z ) under the decoder.] ℒ = ∥ x − g θ ( f ϕ ( x ) ) ∥ 2 ⏟ Reconstruction", "t": 241}, {"id": "slide-7-issues-with-autoencoders-frame-8", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Issues with Autoencoders", "title": "Slide 7: Issues with Autoencoders", "frames": [8, 8], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Latent space has no structure</li>\n<li>Bottleneck (size) is the only regularizer</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic, can’t sample new data!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a 2D latent space (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_2</annotation></semantics></math> axes) with three separate clusters labelled class A, class B and class C; the space between them is marked “? empty ? can’t sample here”.]</span></p>", "text": "Latent space has no structure Bottleneck (size) is the only regularizer z is deterministic, can’t sample new data! [Figure: a 2D latent space ( z 1 , z 2 axes) with three separate clusters labelled class A, class B and class C; the space between them is marked “? empty ? can’t sample here”.]", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-8-variational-autoencoders-vaes-frame-9", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)", "title": "Slide 8: Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)", "frames": [9, 9], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Constrain <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> to follow a <strong>distribution</strong>, often <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z) = \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li>Forces latent space to be continuous, structured &amp; sampleable</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>Encoder</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\phi(z \\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>Sample</strong> -&gt; <strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> Latent</strong> -&gt; <strong>Decoder</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x \\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Regularization</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reconstruction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(x) \\ge \\underbrace{-KL\\big(q_\\phi(z \\mid x) \\,\\|\\, p(z)\\big)}_{\\text{Regularization}} + \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z \\mid x)}\\left[\\log p_\\theta(x \\mid z)\\right]}_{\\text{Reconstruction}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regularization</strong>: are latents <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> close to our prior <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n<li><strong>Reconstruction</strong>: how well does latent <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> explain <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Constrain z to follow a distribution , often p ( z ) = 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) Forces latent space to be continuous, structured & sampleable [Figure: Encoder q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) outputs μ and σ 2 -> Sample -> z Latent -> Decoder p θ ( x ∣ z ) .] log &#8289; p θ ( x ) ≥ − K L ( q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) ∥ p ( z ) ) ⏟ Regularization + 𝔼 q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) [ log p θ ( x ∣ z ) ] ⏟ Reconstruction Regularization : are latents z close to our prior p ( z ) ? Reconstruction : how well does latent z explain x ?", "t": 361}, {"id": "slide-9-reparameterization-trick-frame-10", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Reparameterization Trick", "title": "Slide 9: Reparameterization Trick", "frames": [10, 10], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Problem: Sampling is non-differentiable</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two pipelines. Top, “✗ Naive sampling - gradients cannot flow”: Encoder (outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu, \\sigma^2</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; Sample z (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</annotation></semantics></math>, dashed box) -&gt; Decoder (outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; Loss <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the backward arrow marked “✗ gradient blocked here” at the sampling box. Bottom, “✓ Reparameterised - gradients flow through <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>”: Encoder (outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu, \\sigma^2</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\sqrt{\\sigma^2}</annotation></semantics></math>); <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⊙</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\odot</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math>, not learned) and added to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z = \\mu + \\sigma \\odot \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; Decoder -&gt; Loss, with “✓ gradients flow to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>”.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">where</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z = \\mu + \\sigma \\odot \\epsilon \\quad \\text{where} \\quad \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Problem: Sampling is non-differentiable [Figure: two pipelines. Top, “✗ Naive sampling - gradients cannot flow”: Encoder (outputs μ , σ 2 ) -> Sample z ( z ∼ 𝒩 ( μ , σ 2 ) , dashed box) -> Decoder (outputs x ′ ) -> Loss ℒ , with the backward arrow marked “✗ gradient blocked here” at the sampling box. Bottom, “✓ Reparameterised - gradients flow through μ and σ ”: Encoder (outputs μ , σ 2 ) -> μ and σ ( = σ 2 ); σ is multiplied ( ⊙ ) by ϵ ( ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) , not learned) and added to μ : z = μ + σ ⊙ ϵ -> Decoder -> Loss, with “✓ gradients flow to μ and σ ”.] z = μ + σ ⊙ ϵ where ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I )", "t": 600}, {"id": "slide-10-derivation-frames-11-12", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Derivation", "title": "Slide 10: Derivation", "frames": [11, 12], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi \\mathcal{L} = \\nabla_\\phi \\int q_\\phi(z \\mid x) f(z)\\, dz</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Option 1) Score Function Estimator - REINFORCE</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi \\mathcal{L} = \\mathbb{E}_{z \\sim q_\\phi(z \\mid x)}\\left[f(z) \\nabla_\\phi \\log q_\\phi(z \\mid x)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log q_\\phi</annotation></semantics></math> only; High variance, as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\phi} = 0</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Option 2 is added in the second build (frame 12):]</span></p>\n<p><strong>Option 2) Reparameterization</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Comment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z = \\mu_\\phi(x) + \\sigma_\\phi(x) \\odot \\epsilon, \\quad \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Reparam. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\to \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi \\mathcal{L} = \\nabla_\\phi \\int p(\\epsilon) f\\big(\\mu_\\phi(x) + \\sigma_\\phi(x) \\odot \\epsilon\\big)\\, d\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Leibniz/linearity, move grad. inside integral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊙</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi \\mathcal{L} = \\mathbb{E}_{\\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)}\\left[\\nabla_\\phi f\\big(\\mu_\\phi(x) + \\sigma_\\phi(x) \\odot \\epsilon\\big)\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi</annotation></semantics></math> acts on f directly -&gt; low variance <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\phi} \\ne 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_\\phi \\mathcal{L} \\approx \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\mu_\\phi} = 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\sigma_\\phi} = \\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Chain Rule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "∇ ϕ ℒ = ∇ ϕ ∫ q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) f ( z ) d z Option 1) Score Function Estimator - REINFORCE ∇ ϕ ℒ = 𝔼 z ∼ q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) [ f ( z ) ∇ ϕ log q ϕ ( z ∣ x ) ] [arrow] ∇ ϕ acts on log &#8289; q ϕ only; High variance, as ∂ z ∂ ϕ = 0 [Option 2 is added in the second build (frame 12):] Option 2) Reparameterization Step Comment z = μ ϕ ( x ) + σ ϕ ( x ) ⊙ ϵ , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) Reparam. z → ϵ , p ( ϵ ) independent of ϕ ∇ ϕ ℒ = ∇ ϕ ∫ p ( ϵ ) f ( μ ϕ ( x ) + σ ϕ ( x ) ⊙ ϵ ) d ϵ Leibniz/linearity, move grad. inside integral ∇ ϕ ℒ = 𝔼 ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) [ ∇ ϕ f ( μ ϕ ( x ) + σ ϕ ( x ) ⊙ ϵ ) ] ∇ ϕ acts on f directly -> low variance ∂ z ∂ ϕ ≠ 0 ∇ ϕ ℒ ≈ ∂ f ∂ z ⋅ ∂ z ∂ ϕ , where ∂ z ∂ μ ϕ = 1 , ∂ z ∂ σ ϕ = ϵ Chain Rule", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-11-variational-autoencoders-vaes-latent-space-frame-13", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) <span class=\"own\">[latent space]</span>", "title": "Slide 11: Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) [latent space]", "frames": [13, 13], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Latent space is continuous &amp; structured</li>\n<li>Generative! Any z ~ p(z) decodes meaningfully</li>\n<li>Smooth! Nearby z ’s produce similar outputs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “VAE latent space”: the class A, B and C clusters now sit inside one dashed circle labelled <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z) = \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math>, and a point “sample <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\sim p(z)</annotation></semantics></math>” drawn from the middle of the circle.]</span></p>", "text": "Latent space is continuous & structured Generative! Any z ~ p(z) decodes meaningfully Smooth! Nearby z ’s produce similar outputs [Figure: “VAE latent space”: the class A, B and C clusters now sit inside one dashed circle labelled p ( z ) = 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) , and a point “sample z ∼ p ( z ) ” drawn from the middle of the circle.]", "t": 782}, {"id": "slide-12-issues-with-variational-autoencoders-frame-14", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Issues with Variational Autoencoders", "title": "Slide 12: Issues with Variational Autoencoders", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Posterior Collapse</strong>: powerful decoder can learn to model <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> directly, while ignoring <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Prior Mismatch</strong>: sampling <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math> at test time can land in regions the decoder was never trained on</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Test time: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I}) \\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> lands in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{left}}</annotation></semantics></math> (✓), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{right}}</annotation></semantics></math> (✓), or gap (✗)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the ski slope with the two tracks around the tree.]</span></p>\n<p>Training time: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">left</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{left}} \\sim q_\\phi(z \\mid x_{\\text{left}}) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_{\\text{left}}, \\sigma^2_{\\text{left}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">right</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\text{right}} \\sim q_\\phi(z \\mid x_{\\text{right}}) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_{\\text{right}}, \\sigma^2_{\\text{right}})</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Posterior Collapse : powerful decoder can learn to model p ( x ) directly, while ignoring z Prior Mismatch : sampling z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) at test time can land in regions the decoder was never trained on Test time: z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) → lands in z left (✓), z right (✓), or gap (✗) [Figure: the ski slope with the two tracks around the tree.] Training time: z left ∼ q ϕ ( z ∣ x left ) = 𝒩 ( μ left , σ left 2 ) and z right ∼ q ϕ ( z ∣ x right ) = 𝒩 ( μ right , σ right 2 )", "t": 845}, {"id": "slide-13-vector-quantized-vae-vq-vae-frame-15", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Vector Quantized VAE (VQ-VAE)", "title": "Slide 13: Vector Quantized VAE (VQ-VAE)", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Replace the continuous Gaussian latent <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> with a <strong>discrete codebook</strong> of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math> learned vectors</li>\n<li>Force to “commit” to a discrete code</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the VQ-VAE architecture from the paper. Encoder: an image of a dog -&gt; CNN -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_e(x)</annotation></semantics></math> (feature grid). Each feature vector is replaced by its nearest entry of the “Embedding Space” codebook <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_1, e_2, e_3, \\dots, e_K</annotation></semantics></math>, giving a grid of code indices <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(z \\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. 1, 3, 2, 53) and the quantized grid <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Decoder: CNN -&gt; reconstructed image <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x \\mid z_q)</annotation></semantics></math>. A red arrow <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_z L</annotation></semantics></math> shows the gradient copied from the decoder input back to the encoder output. Right inset: in embedding space, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_e(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled toward its nearest code <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_q(x) \\sim q(z \\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Neural Discrete Representation Learning</em>, van den Ord, Vinyals, et al., (2017)</p>", "text": "Replace the continuous Gaussian latent z with a discrete codebook of K learned vectors Force to “commit” to a discrete code [Figure: the VQ-VAE architecture from the paper. Encoder: an image of a dog -> CNN -> z e ( x ) (feature grid). Each feature vector is replaced by its nearest entry of the “Embedding Space” codebook e 1 , e 2 , e 3 , … , e K , giving a grid of code indices q ( z ∣ x ) (e.g. 1, 3, 2, 53) and the quantized grid z q ( x ) . Decoder: CNN -> reconstructed image p ( x ∣ z q ) . A red arrow ∇ z L shows the gradient copied from the decoder input back to the encoder output. Right inset: in embedding space, z e ( x ) is pulled toward its nearest code e 2 , and z q ( x ) ∼ q ( z ∣ x ) .] Reference: Neural Discrete Representation Learning , van den Ord, Vinyals, et al., (2017)", "t": 901}, {"id": "slide-14-vq-vae-frame-16", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: VQ-VAE", "title": "Slide 14: VQ-VAE", "frames": [16, 16], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What’s the nearest codebook vector <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{C} = \\{e_1, e_2, \\dots, e_K\\}</annotation></semantics></math>?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">where</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_q(x) = e_k \\quad \\text{where} \\quad k = \\arg\\min_j \\left\\| z_e(x) - e_j \\right\\|_2</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the argmin]</span> <strong>Not Differentiable!</strong> updating <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_e(x)</annotation></semantics></math> will still point to same neighbor</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Straight-Through Estimator</strong>: copy the gradients from the decoder input (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_q</annotation></semantics></math>) directly to the encoder output (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_e</annotation></semantics></math>), bypassing the argmin</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>∇</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>∇</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z_q}{\\partial z_e} \\approx \\mathbf{I} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\nabla_{z_e} \\mathcal{L} \\approx \\nabla_{z_q} \\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sg</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_q = z_e + \\text{sg}[z_q - z_e]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sg</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>∂</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial z_e} = \\frac{\\partial z_e}{\\partial z_e} + \\frac{\\partial\\, \\text{sg}[z_q - z_e]}{\\partial z_e} = 1 + 0 = 1</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the reference author is van den Oord; the slide spells it “van den Ord”.]</span></p>", "text": "What’s the nearest codebook vector 𝒞 = { e 1 , e 2 , … , e K } ? z q ( x ) = e k where k = arg &#8289; min j ∥ z e ( x ) − e j ∥ 2 [arrow at the argmin] Not Differentiable! updating z e ( x ) will still point to same neighbor Straight-Through Estimator : copy the gradients from the decoder input ( z q ) directly to the encoder output ( z e ), bypassing the argmin ∂ z q ∂ z e ≈ 𝑰 ⇒ ∇ z e ℒ ≈ ∇ z q ℒ z q = z e + sg [ z q − z e ] ∂ z ∂ z e = ∂ z e ∂ z e + ∂ sg [ z q − z e ] ∂ z e = 1 + 0 = 1 [Note: the reference author is van den Oord; the slide spells it “van den Ord”.]", "t": 1021}, {"id": "slide-15-vq-vae-training-frame-17", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: VQ-VAE: Training", "title": "Slide 15: VQ-VAE: Training", "frames": [17, 17], "html": "<p><strong>Full Loss</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reconstruction</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sg</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Codebook</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>β</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">sg</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Commitment</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L} = \\underbrace{\\left\\| x - D(z_q) \\right\\|_2^2}_{\\text{Reconstruction}} + \\underbrace{\\left\\| \\text{sg}(z_e) - e_k \\right\\|_2^2}_{\\text{Codebook}} + \\underbrace{\\beta \\left\\| z_e - \\text{sg}(e_k) \\right\\|_2^2}_{\\text{Commitment}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reconstruction</strong>: Did the decoder recover x from the quantised code? Trains encoder &amp; decoder jointly via STE</li>\n<li><strong>Codebook</strong>: Moves the codebook vectors <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_k</annotation></semantics></math> toward the encoder outputs to learn a representative vocabulary of the data</li>\n<li><strong>Commitment</strong>: Forces the encoder to stay close to its chosen codebook entry, preventing the latent space from growing arbitrarily</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training gives us the codebook <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and decoder <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>, but <strong>how do we sample new data at test time?</strong></p>", "text": "Full Loss ℒ = ∥ x − D ( z q ) ∥ 2 2 ⏟ Reconstruction + ∥ sg ( z e ) − e k ∥ 2 2 ⏟ Codebook + β ∥ z e − sg ( e k ) ∥ 2 2 ⏟ Commitment Reconstruction : Did the decoder recover x from the quantised code? Trains encoder & decoder jointly via STE Codebook : Moves the codebook vectors e k toward the encoder outputs to learn a representative vocabulary of the data Commitment : Forces the encoder to stay close to its chosen codebook entry, preventing the latent space from growing arbitrarily Training gives us the codebook 𝒞 and decoder D , but how do we sample new data at test time?", "t": 1140}, {"id": "slide-16-vq-vae-how-to-generate-new-data-frame-18", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: VQ-VAE: How to Generate New Data?", "title": "Slide 16: VQ-VAE: How to Generate New Data?", "frames": [18, 18], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Training Stage: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">encoder</mtext></mover><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi></mrow></mover><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\xrightarrow{\\text{encoder}} z_e \\xrightarrow{\\arg\\min} e_k \\in \\mathcal{C} = \\{e_1, e_2, \\dots, e_K\\}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Collect code indices per training sample <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D} = \\{k^{(1)}, k^{(2)}, \\dots, k^{(N)}\\}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Learn a categorical prior over the sequence of codes: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(k_1, \\dots, k_n) = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} p(k_i \\mid k_1, \\dots, k_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> - i.e. autoregressive transformer…</li>\n<li>Inference time:\n<ul>\n<li><ol type=\"I\">\n<li>Sample Index: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i \\sim p(k_i \\mid k_1, \\dots, k_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n<li><ol start=\"2\" type=\"I\">\n<li>Lookup Vector: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Lookup</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_{k_i} = \\text{Lookup}(\\mathcal{C}, k_i)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n<li><ol start=\"3\" type=\"I\">\n<li>Decode: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{x} = D(e_{k_1}, \\dots, e_{k_n})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>", "text": "Training Stage: x → encoder z e → arg &#8289; min e k ∈ 𝒞 = { e 1 , e 2 , … , e K } Collect code indices per training sample 𝒟 = { k ( 1 ) , k ( 2 ) , … , k ( N ) } Learn a categorical prior over the sequence of codes: p ( k 1 , … , k n ) = ∏ i = 1 n p ( k i ∣ k 1 , … , k i − 1 ) - i.e. autoregressive transformer… Inference time: Sample Index: k i ∼ p ( k i ∣ k 1 , … , k i − 1 ) Lookup Vector: e k i = Lookup ( 𝒞 , k i ) Decode: x ̂ = D ( e k 1 , … , e k n )", "t": 1202}, {"id": "slide-17-vq-vae-latent-spaces-compared-frame-19", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: VQ-VAE <span class=\"own\">[latent spaces compared]</span>", "title": "Slide 17: VQ-VAE [latent spaces compared]", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three 2D latent-space panels.]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Autoencoder</th>\n<th>VAE</th>\n<th>VQ-VAE</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>scattered · gaps · no structure</td>\n<td>compact · continuous · sampleable</td>\n<td>discrete · explicit · no gaps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[clusters A, B, C far apart, the space between them marked “gap ✗”]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[clusters inside a dashed <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math> circle, with a “sample” point in the middle]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[four codebook vectors <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_1 \\dots e_4</annotation></semantics></math> drawn as diamonds, each with its encoder outputs snapped around it]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no structure -&gt; gaps</td>\n<td>structured but continuous</td>\n<td>discrete -&gt; no gaps possible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Fixes posterior collapse</li>\n<li>✅ No prior mismatch</li>\n<li>✅ Discrete and controllable, good compression</li>\n<li>❌ Codebook collapse -&gt; Finite Scalar Quantization (FSQ)</li>\n<li>❌ Need to determine <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math> in advance</li>\n<li>❌ Reconstruction still uses MSE, mean-seeking in outputs remains</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three 2D latent-space panels.] Autoencoder VAE VQ-VAE scattered · gaps · no structure compact · continuous · sampleable discrete · explicit · no gaps [clusters A, B, C far apart, the space between them marked “gap ✗”] [clusters inside a dashed 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) circle, with a “sample” point in the middle] [four codebook vectors e 1 … e 4 drawn as diamonds, each with its encoder outputs snapped around it] no structure -> gaps structured but continuous discrete -> no gaps possible ✅ Fixes posterior collapse ✅ No prior mismatch ✅ Discrete and controllable, good compression ❌ Codebook collapse -> Finite Scalar Quantization (FSQ) ❌ Need to determine K in advance ❌ Reconstruction still uses MSE, mean-seeking in outputs remains [Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]", "t": 1261}, {"id": "slide-19-diffusion-frame-20", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Diffusion", "title": "Slide 19: Diffusion", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<ul>\n<li>VQ-VAEs still have mean-seeking in outputs due to MSE</li>\n<li>Diffusion learns complex distributions over continuous variables</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a cat photo <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math> getting noisier left to right until it is pure noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_T</annotation></semantics></math>; top arrow <strong>Forward Process</strong>, bottom arrow (right to left) <strong>Generative Backward Process</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "VQ-VAEs still have mean-seeking in outputs due to MSE Diffusion learns complex distributions over continuous variables [Figure: a cat photo x 0 getting noisier left to right until it is pure noise x T ; top arrow Forward Process , bottom arrow (right to left) Generative Backward Process .]", "t": 1381}, {"id": "slide-20-diffusion-forward-process-frame-21", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Diffusion: Forward Process", "title": "Slide 20: Diffusion: Forward Process", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Iteratively add gaussian noise</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Signal Decay</mtext></munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Noise Scale</mtext></munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i+1} = \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1 - \\beta_{i+1}}}_{\\text{Signal Decay}}\\, x_i + \\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\beta_{i+1}}}_{\\text{Noise Scale}}\\, \\epsilon_i \\qquad \\epsilon_i \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_i</annotation></semantics></math>: noise schedule</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_i</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Reparametrization Trick!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the cat image getting noisier left to right.]</span></p>", "text": "Iteratively add gaussian noise x i + 1 = 1 − β i + 1 ⏟ Signal Decay x i + β i + 1 ⏟ Noise Scale ϵ i ϵ i ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) β i : noise schedule [arrow at ϵ i ] Reparametrization Trick! [Figure: the cat image getting noisier left to right.]", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-21-diffusion-backward-process-frame-22", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Diffusion: Backward Process", "title": "Slide 21: Diffusion: Backward Process", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Learn <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x_{i-1} \\mid x_i)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is intractable (infinite clean images could have produced the noisy version)</li>\n<li><strong>Solution:</strong> instead of predicting the previous image, predict the added noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i-1} \\approx x_i - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i)</annotation></semantics></math>!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: pure noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_T</annotation></semantics></math> denoised left to right into the cat image <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>", "text": "Learn p ( x i − 1 ∣ x i ) , which is intractable (infinite clean images could have produced the noisy version) Solution: instead of predicting the previous image, predict the added noise x i − 1 ≈ x i − ϵ θ ( x i , i ) ! [Figure: pure noise x T denoised left to right into the cat image x 0 .]", "t": 1622}, {"id": "slide-22-diffusion-objective-frame-23", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Diffusion: Objective", "title": "Slide 22: Diffusion: Objective", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Same ELBO as VAE, but <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math> denoising steps instead of one, and q is fixed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Prior Regularization</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><munder><munder><mrow><mi>−</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Denoising Matching</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Reconstruction</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(x_0) \\ge \\underbrace{-D_{KL}\\big(q(x_T \\mid x_0) \\,\\|\\, p(x_T)\\big)}_{\\text{Prior Regularization}} + \\sum_{i=2}^{T} \\underbrace{-D_{KL}\\big(q(x_{i-1} \\mid x_i, x_0) \\,\\|\\, p_\\theta(x_{i-1} \\mid x_i)\\big)}_{\\text{Denoising Matching}} + \\underbrace{\\log p_\\theta(x_0 \\mid x_1)}_{\\text{Reconstruction}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>KL between two Gaussians has closed form</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><msub><mover><mi>μ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{KL} \\propto \\left\\| \\tilde{\\mu}_i - \\mu_\\theta(x_i, i) \\right\\|^2 \\propto \\left\\| \\epsilon - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i) \\right\\|^2</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">simple</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{simple}} = \\mathbb{E}_{i \\sim U(1, T),\\, \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})}\\left[\\left\\| \\epsilon - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i) \\right\\|^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models</em>, Ho et al., (2020)</p>", "text": "Same ELBO as VAE, but T denoising steps instead of one, and q is fixed log &#8289; p θ ( x 0 ) ≥ − D K L ( q ( x T ∣ x 0 ) ∥ p ( x T ) ) ⏟ Prior Regularization + ∑ i = 2 T − D K L ( q ( x i − 1 ∣ x i , x 0 ) ∥ p θ ( x i − 1 ∣ x i ) ) ⏟ Denoising Matching + log &#8289; p θ ( x 0 ∣ x 1 ) ⏟ Reconstruction KL between two Gaussians has closed form D K L ∝ ∥ μ ̃ i − μ θ ( x i , i ) ∥ 2 ∝ ∥ ϵ − ϵ θ ( x i , i ) ∥ 2 ℒ simple = 𝔼 i ∼ U ( 1 , T ) , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) [ ∥ ϵ − ϵ θ ( x i , i ) ∥ 2 ] Reference: Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models , Ho et al., (2020)", "t": 1680}, {"id": "slide-23-diffusion-efficient-training-frame-24", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Diffusion: Efficient Training", "title": "Slide 23: Diffusion: Efficient Training", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">simple</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{simple}} = \\mathbb{E}_{i \\sim U(1, T),\\, \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})}\\left[\\left\\| \\epsilon - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i) \\right\\|^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i \\sim U(1, T)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Requires “teleporting” instead of iterative forward process O(T)</li>\n<li><strong>Signal Retention</strong> for algebraic convenience: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_i = 1 - \\beta_i</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Forward Process</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i+1} = \\sqrt{1 - \\beta_{i+1}}\\, x_i + \\sqrt{\\beta_{i+1}}\\, \\epsilon_i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑰</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_i \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expand</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1 = \\sqrt{\\alpha_1}\\, x_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_1}\\, \\epsilon_0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2 = \\sqrt{\\alpha_2}\\, x_1 + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_2}\\, \\epsilon_1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Substitute</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msqrt><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2 = \\sqrt{\\alpha_2}\\big(\\sqrt{\\alpha_1}\\, x_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_1}\\, \\epsilon_0\\big) + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_2}\\, \\epsilon_1 = \\sqrt{\\alpha_1 \\alpha_2}\\, x_0 + \\sqrt{\\alpha_2 (1 - \\alpha_1)}\\, \\epsilon_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_2}\\, \\epsilon_1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[noise variances add up]</span></td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_2 (1 - \\alpha_1) + (1 - \\alpha_2) = 1 - \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gaussian Sum</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>I</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma_a^2 I) + \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma_b^2 I) = \\mathcal{N}\\big(0, (\\sigma_a^2 + \\sigma_b^2) I\\big)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2 = \\sqrt{\\alpha_1 \\alpha_2}\\, x_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\alpha_1 \\alpha_2}\\, \\bar{\\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generalizing</td>\n<td>Signal Coefficient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\sqrt{\\alpha_i \\alpha_{i-1} \\dots \\alpha_1} = \\sqrt{\\prod_{j=1}^{i} \\alpha_j} = \\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><menclose notation=\"box\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boxed{x_i = \\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_i}\\, x_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}_i}\\, \\bar{\\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Allows “teleporting” from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math> to any noisy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math> in a single step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>!</p>", "text": "ℒ simple = 𝔼 i ∼ U ( 1 , T ) , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) [ ∥ ϵ − ϵ θ ( x i , i ) ∥ 2 ] [arrow at i ∼ U ( 1 , T ) ] Requires “teleporting” instead of iterative forward process O(T) Signal Retention for algebraic convenience: α i = 1 − β i Step Forward Process x i + 1 = 1 − β i + 1 x i + β i + 1 ϵ i , ϵ i ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 𝑰 ) Expand x 1 = α 1 x 0 + 1 − α 1 ϵ 0 , x 2 = α 2 x 1 + 1 − α 2 ϵ 1 Substitute x 2 = α 2 ( α 1 x 0 + 1 − α 1 ϵ 0 ) + 1 − α 2 ϵ 1 = α 1 α 2 x 0 + α 2 ( 1 − α 1 ) ϵ 0 + 1 − α 2 ϵ 1 [noise variances add up] α 2 ( 1 − α 1 ) + ( 1 − α 2 ) = 1 − α 1 α 2 Gaussian Sum 𝒩 ( 0 , σ a 2 I ) + 𝒩 ( 0 , σ b 2 I ) = 𝒩 ( 0 , ( σ a 2 + σ b 2 ) I ) -> x 2 = α 1 α 2 x 0 + 1 − α 1 α 2 ϵ ‾ Generalizing Signal Coefficient = α i α i − 1 … α 1 = ∏ j = 1 i α j = α ‾ i x i = α ‾ i x 0 + 1 − α ‾ i ϵ ‾ Allows “teleporting” from x 0 to any noisy x i in a single step O ( 1 ) !", "t": 1860}, {"id": "slide-24-diffusion-ddpm-sampling-frame-25", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Diffusion: DDPM Sampling", "title": "Slide 24: Diffusion: DDPM Sampling", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Sample Gaussian Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_T \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i = T, \\dots, 1</annotation></semantics></math>:\n<ul>\n<li>I. Sample <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i &gt; 1</annotation></semantics></math>, else <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z = 0</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><ol start=\"2\" type=\"I\">\n<li>Denoise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Predicted Mean</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Stochastic (Langevin) Term</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i-1} = \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\alpha_i}}\\left(x_i - \\frac{1 - \\alpha_i}{\\sqrt{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}_i}}\\, \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i)\\right)}_{\\text{Predicted Mean}} + \\underbrace{\\sigma_i z}_{\\text{Stochastic (Langevin) Term}}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Return <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at step 2]</span> <strong>Very slow! Image generation T=1000!</strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_i</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Typically <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\beta_i}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Stochastic (Langevin) Term</strong>: We add noise during denoising to increase sample diversity and avoid collapsing into a mean distribution</li>\n<li>DDPM is a discrete version of a Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE)</li>\n<li>DDPM denoising = score step (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla \\log(p)</annotation></semantics></math>) towards higher density + Langevin noise</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Sample Gaussian Noise x T ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) For i = T , … , 1 : I. Sample z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) if i > 1 , else z = 0 Denoise x i − 1 = 1 α i ( x i − 1 − α i 1 − α ‾ i ϵ θ ( x i , i ) ) ⏟ Predicted Mean + σ i z ⏟ Stochastic (Langevin) Term Return x 0 [arrow at step 2] Very slow! Image generation T=1000! [arrow at σ i ] Typically σ i = β i Stochastic (Langevin) Term : We add noise during denoising to increase sample diversity and avoid collapsing into a mean distribution DDPM is a discrete version of a Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE) DDPM denoising = score step ( ∇ log &#8289; ( p ) ) towards higher density + Langevin noise", "t": 1920}, {"id": "slide-25-diffusion-ddim-sampling-frame-26", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Diffusion: DDIM Sampling", "title": "Slide 25: Diffusion: DDIM Sampling", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<p>Reference: <em>Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models</em>, Song et al., (2020)</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Sample Gaussian Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_T \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Define subset of timesteps <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = \\{t_1, t_2, \\dots, t_K\\}</annotation></semantics></math> (only 20 steps instead of 1000)</li>\n<li>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i = K, K-1, \\dots, 1</annotation></semantics></math>: (where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{\\alpha}_i</annotation></semantics></math> denote <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t_i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{\\alpha}_{t_i}</annotation></semantics></math>)\n<ul>\n<li>I. Denoise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{x}_0 = \\frac{x_i - \\sqrt{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}_i}\\, \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i)}{\\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_i}}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><ol start=\"2\" type=\"I\">\n<li>Deterministic Re-Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i-1} = \\sqrt{\\bar{\\alpha}_{i-1}}\\, \\hat{x}_0 + \\sqrt{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}_{i-1}}\\, \\epsilon_\\theta(x_i, i)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Return <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><p><span class=\"own\">[arrow at step I]</span> Inverse of “teleportation” formula, can jump to any <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{i-1} \\in \\tau</annotation></semantics></math> directly</p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>α</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar{\\alpha}_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily the previous timestep, this enables skipping timesteps</p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the end of step II]</span> No noise term!</p></li>\n<li><p>Decouples number of denoising iterations in training &amp; inference</p></li>\n<li><p>DDPM is markovian, DDIM defines a non-Markovian forward process that results in the exact same marginal distributions as DDPM</p></li>\n<li><p>DDIM discretizes the probability flow ODE</p></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Reference: Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models , Song et al., (2020) Sample Gaussian Noise x T ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) Define subset of timesteps τ = { t 1 , t 2 , … , t K } (only 20 steps instead of 1000) For i = K , K − 1 , … , 1 : (where x i , α ‾ i denote x t i and α ‾ t i ) I. Denoise x ̂ 0 = x i − 1 − α ‾ i ϵ θ ( x i , i ) α ‾ i Deterministic Re-Noise x i − 1 = α ‾ i − 1 x ̂ 0 + 1 − α ‾ i − 1 ϵ θ ( x i , i ) Return x 0 [arrow at step I] Inverse of “teleportation” formula, can jump to any x i − 1 ∈ τ directly [arrow at α ‾ i − 1 ] α i − 1 from τ , not necessarily the previous timestep, this enables skipping timesteps [arrow at the end of step II] No noise term! Decouples number of denoising iterations in training & inference DDPM is markovian, DDIM defines a non-Markovian forward process that results in the exact same marginal distributions as DDPM DDIM discretizes the probability flow ODE", "t": 2040}, {"id": "slide-26-untitled-conditional-generation-frame-27", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: conditional generation]</span>", "title": "Slide 26: [untitled: conditional generation]", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<p><strong>Everything covered so far is unconditional generation from noise!</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What about conditional generation?</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a noise square and the prompt “A photorealistic image of an astronaut riding a horse” -&gt; <strong>Generative Model</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒄</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t, \\mathbf{c})</annotation></semantics></math> (the prompt enters as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒄</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; an image of an astronaut riding a horse in a desert.]</span></p>", "text": "Everything covered so far is unconditional generation from noise! What about conditional generation? [Figure: a noise square and the prompt “A photorealistic image of an astronaut riding a horse” -> Generative Model ϵ θ ( x t , t , 𝒄 ) (the prompt enters as 𝒄 ) -> an image of an astronaut riding a horse in a desert.]", "t": 2220}, {"id": "slide-27-classifier-free-guidance-cfg-frame-28", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG)", "title": "Slide 27: Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG)", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Problems</strong>: Model might ignore conditioning, classifier guidance required training separate classifier <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒄</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\mathbf{c} \\mid x_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><strong>Solution</strong>: train a single model for conditional &amp; unconditional generation by randomly dropping the condition during training</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Unconditional</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><munder><munder><mrow><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∅</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Guidance=Conditional-Unconditional</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\epsilon} = \\underbrace{\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t, \\emptyset)}_{\\text{Unconditional}} + w \\underbrace{\\big(\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t, c) - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t, \\emptyset)\\big)}_{\\text{Guidance=Conditional-Unconditional}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> <strong>Guidance Scale</strong> condition fidelity vs diversity trade-off</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Classifier-Free Diffusion Guidance</em>, Ho &amp; Salimans, (2022)</p>", "text": "Problems : Model might ignore conditioning, classifier guidance required training separate classifier p ( 𝒄 ∣ x t ) Solution : train a single model for conditional & unconditional generation by randomly dropping the condition during training ϵ ̂ = ϵ θ ( x t , t , ∅ ) ⏟ Unconditional + w ( ϵ θ ( x t , t , c ) − ϵ θ ( x t , t , ∅ ) ) ⏟ Guidance=Conditional-Unconditional [arrow at w ] Guidance Scale condition fidelity vs diversity trade-off Reference: Classifier-Free Diffusion Guidance , Ho & Salimans, (2022)", "t": 2220}, {"id": "slide-28-case-study-diffusion-policy-frame-29", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Case Study: Diffusion Policy", "title": "Slide 28: Case Study: Diffusion Policy", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Key</strong>: Denoises robot action sequences instead of images</li>\n<li>DDPM training, DDIM inference, injects observations into noise prediction network (no future state pred required)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">MSE</mtext><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L} = \\text{MSE}\\big(\\epsilon^k, \\epsilon_\\theta(O_t, A_t^0 + \\epsilon^k, k)\\big) \\qquad A_t^{k-1} = \\alpha\\big(A_t^k - \\gamma\\, \\epsilon_\\theta(O_t, A_t^k, k) + \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2 I)\\big)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot arm pushing a red T-shaped block on a table, with many coloured dots showing sampled action trajectories.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion</em>, Cheng Chi et al., (2023)</p>", "text": "Key : Denoises robot action sequences instead of images DDPM training, DDIM inference, injects observations into noise prediction network (no future state pred required) ℒ = MSE ( ϵ k , ϵ θ ( O t , A t 0 + ϵ k , k ) ) A t k − 1 = α ( A t k − γ ϵ θ ( O t , A t k , k ) + 𝒩 ( 0 , σ 2 I ) ) [Figure: a robot arm pushing a red T-shaped block on a table, with many coloured dots showing sampled action trajectories.] Reference: Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion , Cheng Chi et al., (2023)", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-29-case-study-diffusion-policy-architecture-frame-30", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Case Study: Diffusion Policy <span class=\"own\">[architecture]</span>", "title": "Slide 29: Case Study: Diffusion Policy [architecture]", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_t</annotation></semantics></math> computed <strong>once</strong> before <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math> denoising steps</li>\n<li>1D temporal U-Net with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_t</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM)</li>\n<li>Handles multimodality in demonstrations!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the paper’s overview figure. Input: image observation sequence plus robot pose <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑶</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{O}_t</annotation></semantics></math>. Output: action sequence, shown as the denoising steps <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_t^K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_t^3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_t^0</annotation></semantics></math> converging from scattered dots to one trajectory. (a) Diffusion Policy General Formulation: observations <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_{t-2}, o_{t-1}, o_t</annotation></semantics></math> go into Diffusion Policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑶</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_\\theta(\\mathbf{O}, \\mathbf{A}, k)</annotation></semantics></math>, which outputs an action sequence <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t \\dots a_{t+3}</annotation></semantics></math> over a prediction horizon <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_p</annotation></semantics></math>; the window then slides to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑶</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{O}_{t+4}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{A}_{t+4}</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) CNN-based: Conv1D blocks on the action embedding, each modulated by FiLM (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\cdot x + b</annotation></semantics></math> from linear layers on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑶</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{O}_t</annotation></semantics></math>), repeated <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>×</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\times K</annotation></semantics></math>, outputting <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla E(\\mathbf{A}_t)</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) Transformer-based: action embeddings cross-attend to observation embeddings, repeated <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>×</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\times K</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion</em>, Cheng Chi et al., (2023)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32.]</span></p>", "text": "O t computed once before K denoising steps 1D temporal U-Net with O t conditioned via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) Handles multimodality in demonstrations! [Figure: the paper’s overview figure. Input: image observation sequence plus robot pose 𝑶 t . Output: action sequence, shown as the denoising steps A t K , A t 3 , A t 0 converging from scattered dots to one trajectory. (a) Diffusion Policy General Formulation: observations o t − 2 , o t − 1 , o t go into Diffusion Policy ε θ ( 𝑶 , 𝑨 , k ) , which outputs an action sequence a t … a t + 3 over a prediction horizon T p ; the window then slides to 𝑶 t + 4 / 𝑨 t + 4 . (b) CNN-based: Conv1D blocks on the action embedding, each modulated by FiLM ( a ⋅ x + b from linear layers on 𝑶 t ), repeated × K , outputting ∇ E ( 𝑨 t ) . (c) Transformer-based: action embeddings cross-attend to observation embeddings, repeated × K .] Reference: Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion , Cheng Chi et al., (2023) [Note: slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32.]", "t": 2401}, {"id": "slide-32-scaling-diffusion-in-robotics-dit-block-policy-frames-31-33", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Scaling Diffusion in Robotics: DiT Block Policy", "title": "Slide 32: Scaling Diffusion in Robotics: DiT Block Policy", "frames": [31, 33], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Replaces cross-attention with adaptive Layer-Norm</li>\n<li>Scales with Model &amp; Data size</li>\n<li>Stable Training</li>\n<li>Long horizon bimanual dexterous tasks (1500+ timesteps)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, an embedded video of the two-armed sushi-cutting workstation playing across three frames (the arms pick up a red knife and cut the roll). Right, the “DiT-Block Policy” diagram: the goal “Cut Sushi” (encoded with DistilBERT) and three camera views (global, left wrist, right wrist), each through a ResNet + FiLM, feed a <strong>Self Attention Encoder</strong> (repeated N x); a <strong>Diffusion Decoder</strong> (repeated N x) takes the noisy actions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^K</annotation></semantics></math> and step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. An inset shows the adaLN-Zero block: Layer Norm -&gt; Scale, Shift (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_1, \\beta_1</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; Multi-Head Self-Attention -&gt; Scale (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_1</annotation></semantics></math>), then Layer Norm -&gt; Scale, Shift (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_2, \\beta_2</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; MLP -&gt; Scale (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_2</annotation></semantics></math>), with the scale and shift values produced by an MLP from the mean encoder output and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>The Ingredients for Robotic Diffusion Transformers</em>, Dasari, <strong>Mees</strong> et al., ICRA, 2025</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: week 2 cites the same paper as ICRA 2024; ICRA 2025, as here, is correct.]</span></p>", "text": "Replaces cross-attention with adaptive Layer-Norm Scales with Model & Data size Stable Training Long horizon bimanual dexterous tasks (1500+ timesteps) [Figure: left, an embedded video of the two-armed sushi-cutting workstation playing across three frames (the arms pick up a red knife and cut the roll). Right, the “DiT-Block Policy” diagram: the goal “Cut Sushi” (encoded with DistilBERT) and three camera views (global, left wrist, right wrist), each through a ResNet + FiLM, feed a Self Attention Encoder (repeated N x); a Diffusion Decoder (repeated N x) takes the noisy actions x K and step K and outputs ϵ . An inset shows the adaLN-Zero block: Layer Norm -> Scale, Shift ( γ 1 , β 1 ) -> Multi-Head Self-Attention -> Scale ( α 1 ), then Layer Norm -> Scale, Shift ( γ 2 , β 2 ) -> MLP -> Scale ( α 2 ), with the scale and shift values produced by an MLP from the mean encoder output and K .] Reference: The Ingredients for Robotic Diffusion Transformers , Dasari, Mees et al., ICRA, 2025 [Note: week 2 cites the same paper as ICRA 2024; ICRA 2025, as here, is correct.]", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-33-flow-matching-frame-34", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Flow Matching", "title": "Slide 33: Flow Matching", "frames": [34, 34], "html": "<p>Reference: <em>Flow Matching for Generative Modeling</em>, Lipman et al., (2023)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn a velocity field <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\theta(x_t, t)</annotation></semantics></math> that transports samples from noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)</annotation></semantics></math> to data <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0 \\sim p(x_0)</annotation></semantics></math> along continuous paths</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t = (1 - t)\\epsilon + t x_0, \\quad \\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I), \\quad t \\in [0, 1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1 - t)\\epsilon + t x_0</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Straight line from noise to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math>, no schedule</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Training:</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><munder><munder><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Predicted velocity</mtext></munder><mo>−</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Ground-truth velocity</mtext></munder><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L} = \\mathbb{E}_{t, x_0, \\epsilon}\\left[\\left\\| \\underbrace{v_\\theta(x_t, t)}_{\\text{Predicted velocity}} - \\underbrace{(x_0 - \\epsilon)}_{\\text{Ground-truth velocity}} \\right\\|^2\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x_0 - \\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Ground-truth velocity from noise to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, independent of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inference:</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{t + \\Delta t} = x_t + v_\\theta(x_t, t) \\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Integrate learned velocity with Euler ODE</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Diffusion, predict noise</mtext></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mover><mo>⇔</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">With linear schedule</mtext></mover><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><munder><munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Flow Matching, predict velocity</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left\\| \\epsilon - \\epsilon_\\theta(x_t, t) \\right\\|^2\\right]}_{\\text{Diffusion, predict noise}} \\;\\overset{\\text{With linear schedule}}{\\Longleftrightarrow}\\; \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left\\| v_\\theta(x_t, t) - (x_0 - \\epsilon) \\right\\|^2\\right]}_{\\text{Flow Matching, predict velocity}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Reference: Flow Matching for Generative Modeling , Lipman et al., (2023) Learn a velocity field v θ ( x t , t ) that transports samples from noise ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) to data x 0 ∼ p ( x 0 ) along continuous paths x t = ( 1 − t ) ϵ + t x 0 , ϵ ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , I ) , t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] [brace under ( 1 − t ) ϵ + t x 0 ] Straight line from noise to x 0 , no schedule Training: ℒ = 𝔼 t , x 0 , ϵ [ ∥ v θ ( x t , t ) ⏟ Predicted velocity − ( x 0 − ϵ ) ⏟ Ground-truth velocity ∥ 2 ] [brace under ( x 0 − ϵ ) ] Ground-truth velocity from noise to x 0 is constant, independent of t Inference: x t + Δ t = x t + v θ ( x t , t ) Δ t Integrate learned velocity with Euler ODE 𝔼 [ ∥ ϵ − ϵ θ ( x t , t ) ∥ 2 ] ⏟ Diffusion, predict noise ⇔ With linear schedule 𝔼 [ ∥ v θ ( x t , t ) − ( x 0 − ϵ ) ∥ 2 ] ⏟ Flow Matching, predict velocity", "t": 2641}, {"id": "slide-34-rectified-flow-frame-35", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: Rectified Flow", "title": "Slide 34: Rectified Flow", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Retrains Flow Matching on noise-data pairs generated by its own flow, untangling trajectory crossings &amp; straightening paths</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two animated sampling panels with “Sampling Duration” progress bars. <strong>Flow Matching - Curved Paths Flow Slow</strong>: sample trajectories (orange) bend and cross on their way from noise to the data points (blue). <strong>Rectified Flow - Straight Paths Flow Fast</strong>: the trajectories are nearly straight lines into the same data points.]</span></p>\n<p>Source: Alec Helbling</p>", "text": "Retrains Flow Matching on noise-data pairs generated by its own flow, untangling trajectory crossings & straightening paths [Figure: two animated sampling panels with “Sampling Duration” progress bars. Flow Matching - Curved Paths Flow Slow : sample trajectories (orange) bend and cross on their way from noise to the data points (blue). Rectified Flow - Straight Paths Flow Fast : the trajectories are nearly straight lines into the same data points.] Source: Alec Helbling", "t": 2761}, {"id": "slide-35-case-study-pi_0-frame-36", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: Case Study: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math>", "title": "Slide 35: Case Study: π 0", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Finetunes a VLM to produce actions via Flow Matching</li>\n<li>Samples flow-time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math> from a shifted Beta distribution to focus on noisier (harder action prediction) parts of flow</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math> overview. Training data: the “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> dataset” (robot photos), “Internet pre-training” and “OXE”. Three camera images each pass through a ViT, and together with the instruction “fold shirt” go into the <strong>pre-trained VLM</strong> (SigLIP (400M) + Gemma (2.6B)). An <strong>action expert</strong> (300M) takes the robot state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_t</annotation></semantics></math> and noise and outputs an action chunk <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t, a_{t+1}, \\dots, a_{t+H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Target robots: 14 DoF Bimanual Manipulators, 18 DoF Mobile Manipulators, 7 and 8 DoF Single Arm Manipulators.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math>: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control</em>, Physical Intelligence (2024)</p>", "text": "Finetunes a VLM to produce actions via Flow Matching Samples flow-time τ from a shifted Beta distribution to focus on noisier (harder action prediction) parts of flow [Figure: the π 0 overview. Training data: the “ π dataset” (robot photos), “Internet pre-training” and “OXE”. Three camera images each pass through a ViT, and together with the instruction “fold shirt” go into the pre-trained VLM (SigLIP (400M) + Gemma (2.6B)). An action expert (300M) takes the robot state q t and noise and outputs an action chunk a t , a t + 1 , … , a t + H . Target robots: 14 DoF Bimanual Manipulators, 18 DoF Mobile Manipulators, 7 and 8 DoF Single Arm Manipulators.] Reference: π 0 : A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control , Physical Intelligence (2024)", "t": 2822}, {"id": "slide-36-conclusion-frame-37", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Conclusion", "title": "Slide 36: Conclusion", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Latent Variable Models</th>\n<th>Diffusion Models</th>\n<th>Flow Models</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AE</strong>: compressed representation via reconstruction</td>\n<td><strong>DDPM</strong>: Iteratively denoise Gaussian noise to generate samples</td>\n<td><strong>Flow Matching</strong>: Learns velocity field to transport noise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VAE</strong>: constrain latent to follow a distribution via reparametrization trick</td>\n<td><strong>DDIM</strong>: Faster deterministic sampling via non-Markovian diffusion process</td>\n<td><strong>Rectified Flow</strong>: Straighten ODE trajectories via iterative reflow for faster, fewer-step sampling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VQ-VAE</strong>: Discretize latent space using learned codebook vectors</td>\n<td><strong>CFG</strong>: Steer generation by blending conditional &amp; unconditional scores</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Diffusion (DDPM)</mtext><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Stochastic SDE</mtext></munder><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mover><munder><munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">DDIM</mtext><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Deterministic ODE</mtext></munder><mover><mo>↔</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Linear Schedule</mtext></mover><munder><munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Flow Matching</mtext><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">same ODE, simpler Euler solver</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\underbrace{\\text{Diffusion (DDPM)}}_{\\text{Stochastic SDE}} \\xrightarrow{\\sigma = 0} \\underbrace{\\text{DDIM}}_{\\text{Deterministic ODE}} \\overset{\\text{Linear Schedule}}{\\longleftrightarrow} \\underbrace{\\text{Flow Matching}}_{\\text{same ODE, simpler Euler solver}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "text": "Latent Variable Models Diffusion Models Flow Models AE : compressed representation via reconstruction DDPM : Iteratively denoise Gaussian noise to generate samples Flow Matching : Learns velocity field to transport noise VAE : constrain latent to follow a distribution via reparametrization trick DDIM : Faster deterministic sampling via non-Markovian diffusion process Rectified Flow : Straighten ODE trajectories via iterative reflow for faster, fewer-step sampling VQ-VAE : Discretize latent space using learned codebook vectors CFG : Steer generation by blending conditional & unconditional scores Diffusion (DDPM) ⏟ Stochastic SDE → σ = 0 DDIM ⏟ Deterministic ODE ↔ Linear Schedule Flow Matching ⏟ same ODE, simpler Euler solver", "t": 2941}, {"id": "end-frame-38", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-14-vq-vae-frame-16", "label": "Slide 14: VQ-VAE", "html": "the reference author is van den Oord; the slide spells it “van den Ord”."}, {"slide": "slide-17-vq-vae-latent-spaces-compared-frame-19", "label": "Slide 17: VQ-VAE [latent spaces compared]", "html": "slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19."}, {"slide": "slide-29-case-study-diffusion-policy-architecture-frame-30", "label": "Slide 29: Case Study: Diffusion Policy [architecture]", "html": "slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32."}, {"slide": "slide-32-scaling-diffusion-in-robotics-dit-block-policy-frames-31-33", "label": "Slide 32: Scaling Diffusion in Robotics: DiT Block Policy", "html": "week 2 cites the same paper as ICRA 2024; ICRA 2025, as here, is correct."}]}, {"key": "07", "dir": "week07_sequence_modeling", "date": "Mon 30 Mar 2026", "topic": "Sequence Modeling & Transformers", "summary": "Why robots need history, then the transformer stack: attention (with a live step-through), positional encodings, encoder-decoder vs decoder-only, teacher forcing, BPE tokenization, scaling laws. Extends to vision (ViT patches, CLIP, LLaVA early fusion, Flamingo late fusion, native multimodal models) and finally to actions: per-dimension binning (RT-2, OpenVLA), action chunking (ACT) and FAST compression.", "key_ideas": ["Attention gives O(1) path length between any two tokens; RNNs need O(n)", "Compute-optimal training: scale parameters and tokens together, ~20 tokens per parameter", "VLMs differ mainly in where vision enters: prepended tokens, cross-attention, or joint training from scratch", "Naive binning breaks at high control frequency - adjacent tokens become nearly identical", "FAST = DCT + BPE on action chunks, like JPEG for actions"], "papers": [{"title": "Decision Transformer: Reinforcement Learning via Sequence Modeling", "who": "Chen et al., 2021", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.01345"}, {"title": "Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation (ALOHA)", "who": "Zhao et al., 2023", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13705"}, {"title": "Humanoid Locomotion as Next Token Prediction", "who": "Radosavovic et al., 2024", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.19469"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "imSTfMJjp7M", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 7: Sequence Modeling & Transformers | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "55:12", "description": "Lecture 7 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Limitations of reactive models"}, {"t": 136, "title": "Sequence modeling fundamentals"}, {"t": 289, "title": "Transformer architecture"}, {"t": 446, "title": "Attention and encodings"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Original transformer design"}, {"t": 825, "title": "Language tokenization"}, {"t": 1068, "title": "Scaling and engineering"}, {"t": 1224, "title": "Scaling laws and emergence"}, {"t": 1406, "title": "Compute-optimal scaling"}, {"t": 1645, "title": "Visual tokenization"}, {"t": 1839, "title": "Multimodal fusion techniques"}, {"t": 2127, "title": "Native multimodal models"}, {"t": 2312, "title": "Action tokenization"}, {"t": 2569, "title": "Action chunking techniques"}, {"t": 2797, "title": "Efficient action tokenization"}, {"t": 3127, "title": "Conclusion and summary"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Let's start. So, today is the seventh lecture things are going to start getting more interesting cuz we're going to talk a bit about sequence modeling and transformers. And also specially how they are related to robotics. So, as a quick recap we've been looking mostly so far at reactive policies. Where we've taken like a single observation OT feed it through our policy to get an action. And in principle like this this is like works in in in theory but it breaks very easily in the real world because one snapshot usually doesn't kind of cover all the information about the state. So, there is kind of like one two two issues right? So, like one of them is like memory with this kind of reactive policies we might not know"}, {"t": 60, "text": "what we have done like you know a few seconds ago how what decisions we have made or how the world was looking like which might be relevant when we are like driving an icy road with an autonomous car. Or from just like a single image observation it might not be possible to understand how pedestrians are moving you know in the scene so that we don't run them over if you know it's just like a static image it's very difficult to estimate any velocities or motion. And there's also another issue of action smoothness right? So, our kind of one step prediction models so far usually have been predicting just a single action for the next time step right? And this can often in the real world lead to kind of like jerky trajectory that are like non smooth cuz like every like every decision is kind of like different and so you can like get list this kind of jerky um"}, {"t": 121, "text": "oscillating um behavior sometimes. And so um today we're going to talk about um some fixes we can do to address these issues, uh which involve um sequence modeling on transformers. And that's what we're going to cover today. Um and so uh what it means is that um as I just said, right? Like just having a a single frame is usually like not not uh sufficient cuz in practice in robotics we are not operating in an MDP, but in like a partial partially observable um MDT MDP. Um and so to really understand the state, we kind of need to reason uh over the history or the the trajectory, uh so like a sequence of observations and and actions. Um and um and you can see also here, right? Like this is the kind of the trajectory probability formula we've been using uh through the lecture. Um and you see that how the policy we are um"}, {"t": 181, "text": "uh modeling this is just this kind of like one-step uh policy uh model like single-step and reactive. Um and this is kind of like a fundamental mismatch between um like the world is really like sequential, but like our policy like is isn't. Um so the natural question then is like um what if we just model the whole trajectory directly instead of just like the the next uh action. Uh so how do we how can we model these sequences, right? Uh and the most general way to think about this is um through the lens of auto-regressive models. Um and the the key insight here is um that um and this is just kind of the the pro it comes from like uh the property of the probability, um the chain rule, right? Um so it tells us that for um any joint distribution, uh no matter how complex or high-dimensional, we can decompose this into product of conditional distributions."}, {"t": 241, "text": "And so, this means that we can get the probability of the entire sequence just as the probability of the first element times the probability of the second element and so forth autoregressively. Um Now, the the key the key question we're going to like cover in in this lecture is how do we actually model these these conditional conditionals. Because if your sequence is like very high-dimensional, you know, it might be a high-resolution video or like a complex robot trajectory, then this will become like very long, very very like difficult, and we can't just use like a big lookup table. So, the first logical tool that was kind of like invented to handle these conditionals are recurrent neural networks or RNNs. The idea"}, {"t": 300, "text": "is very simple. You have like a hidden state that summarizes everything you've been so you've seen so far, and then you update it recurrently as new inputs arrive, which is great as now you can handle variable length inputs. In practice, they have three kind of serious issues or failure modes. The first one is long-range dependencies. So, to get information from step one to step 100, you have to propagate the gradients through all 99 intermediate steps, and then those gradients typically explode or vanish. And LSTMs kind of helped this, but didn't fully solve it. Then, you have the parallelization issue. It's RNNs are very difficult to parallelize due to their autoregressive nature. And the third one is the bottleneck problem. So, all your history gets compressed into a fixed-size um uh vector, um no matter how long your sequence is, right? So, uh there is kind"}, {"t": 361, "text": "of um uh a fundamental um information bottleneck that you can't hack your way around out of. And so, RNNs RNNs get you uh part of the way there, um but uh the limitations uh are, you know, um difficult uh in practice to to scale. Um and by now, most of you are are probably familiar with this um um with the Transformers paradigms, but I will be give like a brief recap um so everybody's on the same page. Um so, instead of uh recurrence um in Transformers, uh we use attention. And the key property of attention is uh that it gets you uh O(1) uh uh uh hop path length between any two uh positions in the sequence. Um looking at the um diagram, this means that the the the last word can attend to the first word um uh directly in a in a single step, right? With a direct connection, without having to propagate through all the intermediate um"}, {"t": 421, "text": "words. Um and so, this simultaneously uh solves all three problems we just described for RNNs. Um so, uh long-range dependencies are now trivial. Uh training can be fully parallelized because uh there is no recurrence, and um there is no fixed-size bottleneck uh because every token has direct access to every other uh token. Okay, so how does it actually work? Um so, now you have a sequence of tokens, and for each uh uh for each token, you want to compute a new representation that incorporates um uh information from the rest of sequence. And so, the the question that I um the attention operation tries to solve is which part of the sequence should you be looking at? Um and so, essentially, this is what what is being learned through through attention, right? Um So, you compute that relevance as the the"}, {"t": 480, "text": "dot product between a query vector and a set of key vectors. Then you normalize with a softmax, so it sums up to one, and then you just take a weighted average of the value vectors, and that's that's pretty much it. Um and now, what what the nice thing about this is that you can think of this as kind of a soft differentiable dictionary lookup, where instead of committing to a single to to one entry as in a regular dictionary, you're simultaneously retrieving all values and blending them together weighted by how much each key matched your query. Um and so, because all of these operations are fully differentiable, now the model doesn't just learn like the representation, but also what is kind of the optimal search strategy. Um Now, there is one subtle important issue that in attention one needs to address"}, {"t": 542, "text": "because um because the attention is computing this dot product between the tokens, it's fundamentally a set operation, and it treats all the inputs without any notion of order. So, this means that the robot picked up the spoon and the spoon picked up the robot would produce identical attention patterns if you don't do something about this. And the fix is to add positional encodings, so you inject order information directly into the token representations. Um and there are two main ways how you can do this. The original approach from the attention is all you need paper is to just add a fixed vector to each token embedding before it enters the transformer. Um, and it's pretty simple. It works well. Um, and it's absolute positional encodings. Um, but there is kind of one uh limitation that uh if um at test time"}, {"t": 600, "text": "now you have longer sequences than the ones you trained or you trained with um, your um, like like that this will fail, right? Cuz it's now out of distribution. Um, the second one is uh relative uh positional encodings, which solve this issue a bit more elegantly. So, instead of encoding the absolute position in the sequence, um, you encode the distance between the tokens. Um, for example, it could be a bias that um, you add directly to the attention score. So, this means that um, our example, um, this uh the word spoon attending uh to robot um, will know that it's just like four steps away regardless of of where in the sequence they are. Um, and crucially like this uh generalizes then to to um, kind of unseen lengths. Um, and many of the modern uh foundation models um, build upon um, kind of similar extensions based on uh relative distances. All right."}, {"t": 660, "text": "Uh, now let's look uh at how the original transformer um, put all this together. Um, so this model was designed for machine transla- language translation. Uh, so you have a source sentence um, and then you want to produce a target sentence in a different language. Um, and then this kind of breaks down into first understanding the uh source sentence and then generating the target. Uh, and hence they have this encoder-decoder split. Um, the encoder uses uh uh bidirec- bidirectional uh self-attention. So, every token can see every other token, past and future. Um and this kind of makes sense because you have um the full input available, and you want to get your richest possible representation um of each token. And now for a decoder, uh and this is very important, it uses uh causal uh self-attention, where each token can only attend to itself um uh and the tokens that came uh before it. Um and then we have um this this kind of like"}, {"t": 720, "text": "triangular uh mask there um on the right that is just setting uh future attention uh scores to negative infinity uh before softmax um so that they zero out. And this is what makes auto-regressive generation possible. At each step, you only can uh can condition on what you have already generated. Um and then in the middle you can see the cross-attention uh um where uh in the middle of the decoder, where uh where is where kind of two the encoder and decoder uh talk to each other. So, the the decoder kind of um ask um given what I've generated so far, uh what from the source is relevant for me right now? Um and so, this means that the queries come from the decoder, and the keys and values uh will come from the encoder. Cool. Uh how do we train this? Um well, uh very simple, we use something called teacher forcing. The idea is that during training, we don't care if the model"}, {"t": 781, "text": "makes a mistake at a certain time step. So, instead of feeding the model its own potentially wrong prediction, uh we always feed it the ground truth um of the preceding tokens, and ask it to maximize the likelihood of uh the next correct token via cross-entropy loss. Um and uh kind of the the nice thing about about this whole whole operation is that we just need one forward pass uh to compute all T all sequence losses with Transformer, whereas with the RNNs we would need to do one forward pass for for every for every token. All right, so before we can apply any of this to robotics, we need to ask a more basic question. What what is even a token? How do you discretize your input into units that this Transformer can operate on, right? And so starting with language cuz that's"}, {"t": 841, "text": "where Transformer originated, we have two kind of like obvious extremes, right? So the first one is characters to use characters, right? This would be a tiny vocabulary with sequences would be very large and the model would need to learn like very hard um to to model all of them, right? And then you have words on the other extreme where sequences would be like short and each token would be meaningful, but then you know, you would have the issue of like what do you do if you get an unseen word, right? Like you might not be able to represent it with your pre-trained vocabulary. And so neither extreme like works well and ideally you will you want something in the middle. So units that are larger than characters but smaller than words. And it can handle unseen vocabulary kind of gracefully"}, {"t": 900, "text": "and keep sequence lengths manageable. Because the the attention operation is quadratic, right? And so that's what sub word sub word tokenization gives you. Um So if you want the something in between, typically there is we use this algorithm called byte pair encoding or BPE, uh is uh essentially a bottom-up clustering approach Uh uh for for text. Um so, the logic is very simple. Uh as you see in the uh pseudocode here, you start with the most built uh basic building blocks, um all unique characters in your data set, and then you iteratively look for the most frequent pairs of adjacent tokens and merge them into a uh single new token. Uh and you repeat this K times um until you've built a vocabulary uh of the size you want. And what happens in practice is that common subwords then get uh merged early because they appear everywhere, uh and then this keeps our sequence length"}, {"t": 961, "text": "short, uh which is again critical um for cuz a transformer's memory usage is quadratic. Um and the advantage of this is also generalization because uh now if you uh see a completely new word um at test time, you can uh probably break it down until like the um uh until one common like uh subword unit. Um into a sequence of kind of like uh known subword unit. Um here is a concrete example uh for one sentence. Um the robots melted the robots unbearably cheesy fondue. Um and here in this animation, you can see how BPE works uh in this single sentence uh and how um like the subword uh tokens get merged. Um and then the the result is that we were we go from 54 characters compressed down to uh 34 tokens."}, {"t": 1022, "text": "So, like a 37% reduction, so that's great. Um one key detail um that you should know about BPE is that the compression factor scales with your uh corpus size. So, the more data you train your BPE tokenizer on, the more common patterns will get merged and the shorter your sequences will become and therefore more efficient your transformer. Um and so modern LLMs trains on, you know, kind of internet scale data. Uh you know, maybe with a vocabulary of like a 100k tokens, you'll get like three or four times uh kind of like compression rates compared to to to this thing. Um All right. So, how did we get from the original transformer to scaling these two large language models? Um I want to briefly touch up on a few engineering decisions that made them work at scale. The first"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "is um um architectural simplification. Uh so, modern LLMs like GPT drop the encoder entirely and use decoder-only models. It turns out that if you train a a decoder uh on next next token prediction, um it's simpler to scale and with enough data a causal decoder can just learn to represent the context just as well as the bidirectional encoder. Um then the second one is extending BPE to the byte level. So, instead of operating on characters, you operate on raw bytes. Um which means your base vocabulary um covers literally every possible input. And I know I just told you in the previous slide that BPE generalizes to unseen words and that's true. But with byte level BPE, you can also generalize to let's say emojis that we couldn't cover with the pseudo algorithm I just showed you"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "before. Um and then there are two kind of engineering tricks or innovations that made training on longer sequences more practical. One of them is Rope, Rotary Positional Encodings, which is kind of an elegant solution to the um positional encoding problem, where instead of adding the position vectors to the embeddings, it rotate it rotates the query and key vectors by an angle proportional to their positions. Um Um so that um the dot product uh um depends only on the relative distance between the the tokens. Uh and and not their absolute positions. And then um Flash Attention solves the memory bottleneck. Um so um the standard attention uh requires materializing the full um n times n attention matrix, uh which is uh n squared uh in memory consumption."}, {"t": 1202, "text": "And then for very long sequences, this becomes like a limiting factor. And then with uh Flash Attention, this uh tiles the computation into fast uh SRAM, so that you never actually uh store that matrix, getting your memory consumption uh down to O of n with the same result. So, let's take a step back and see how people scaled up um Transformers and what scale bought us. Um back in 2018, uh with GPT-1, we were kind of still very much in the pre-training plus fine-tuning regime. Uh so, you train on a billion tokens, but the model wasn't actually useful for any specific task until you did like supervised fine-tuning um on some narrow task uh data set. Um then you move to uh GPT-2 in uh 2019, so a year later. Uh and then we um scaled the model uh with 10 times the parameters uh and 10"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "times the tokens. And then we started to see zero-shot generalization, um where the model starts being able to perform tasks that it was uh not explicitly told to do uh because it has learned enough um kind of structure uh of the world through text, right? And then in GPT-3 in 2020 um with uh 175 billion parameters and 300 billion tokens um you have like this phenomenon with where in-context learning uh emerged, right? Uh so you give the model a few examples in the prompt and then it figures out like the pattern um of your um context uh pairs and um is able to apply this to new inputs uh without any gradient updates. Um and the surprising thing is that this kind of emerges uh or it it it just appeared as a consequence of scale. Um and this connects directly to um what uh Rich Sutton uh called the bitter"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "lesson in 2019 where the argument is quite simple um that he made. So it basically states that if you look at the history of AI over the past 70 years then the methods that ultimately win are not the ones um with the most clever domain knowledge or the most carefully hand-engineered features, but these are the ones that actually scale with compute. So like no matter if in chess or in in speech recognition or go um in every single case usually like um handcrafted approaches were competitive for a while, but then ultimately like more general methods uh with more compute eventually overtook them. Um and then this plot from the GPT-3 paper is kind of like a perfect illustration for this where uh you can see um that for uh this model with like a uh just 1.3 billion parameters is like essentially flatlining um no matter how many examples uh you put in the prompt um but then once you scale it to 175"}, {"t": 1382, "text": "billion parameters, um, the model becomes a few-shot learner. Uh, and again like this is, you know, uh, not because somebody uh, engineered it to do it so, but it just emerged uh, through scale. Um, and if this is true in language, um, the question, you know, that the field is uh, trying to answer today is also uh, what emerges when you scale up robot data, right? Um, so if scale is what matters, the next question is, uh, how do you scale optimally? Uh, right? And so for a few years, um, especially after uh, with GPT, the first GPT models, um, the community seemed to think that uh, just making the models bigger uh, was the primary approach. Uh, and this is what um, the Chinchilla paper uh, from Hoffman et al. Um, worked out uh, very precisely um, that this is not the case. Um, and the key finding here is that um, given a fixed compute budget, uh, you should scale your model size and your"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "data uh, in equal proportion. Um, so now if you look at the uh, left plot over here, uh, you can see um, the envelope for various model sizes across different compute budgets, uh, and at low compute budgets, the optimal choice is actually a smaller model. Right? So a 75 million parameter model trained on to its optimal point sits on the envelope uh, at low flops, uh, while a 10 B model uh, trained with the same compute budget would still be undertrained and sitting above the envelope. Um, and now in the center and and right plots, uh, where we plot the scaling uh, model size and training uh, against compute, uh, we can see for example that GPT-3 was actually undertrained, meaning that it was too big um, for the amount of tokens it was trained uh, on. Compared to the optimal scaling uh, frontier, which is um, this dashed green line"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "that was derived in in the Hoffman Chinchilla paper. The paper also found out that you need roughly around 20 tokens per parameter for compute optimality. All right. Um So this slide shows you something useful in practice that falls out of this scaling laws. And this is that you can actually predict how a larger model will perform before you train it. So the idea is simple, right? So before you compute your entire you commit your entire compute budget to one big expensive run to train your next GPT-6 model, you first do a bunch of cheap small runs at different model sizes and compute budget and then you can kind of like extrapolate what the performance of your bigger bigger model will be. And you can see that by kind of like"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "following the line the line of this isoflop plot. And then the the right plot here we plot the training tokens against the model size for some different models and you can see how well the first GPT models were all under trained, right? According to to the optimal frontier. And then kind of Llama 1, 2 and Chinchilla kind of like were more on the on the they were on the more optimal compute frontier. And then you see um the Llama 3 is like way above the frontier, right? So it it's trained on more tokens than the compute optimal um uh, formula would suggest. And the question is like, why did they do this, right? Um, because um, the motivation behind this is that"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "um, the compute optimal, um, to be compute optimal is the right strategy if you only care about the training costs, but if you're going to going to serve a model, um, to millions of users, then you actually want a smaller model that's cheaper to run at inference, uh, even if that means, uh, overtraining it slightly. And so, that's why they made this this kind of deliberate choice. All right. Uh, so we've established that, um, transformers are incredible general-purpose sequence models. Um, they, uh, we have LLMs, they they scale predictively, um, they have emerging capabilities, uh, at scale. Uh, but for a robot, language is only a half the story because, um, for a robot, uh, it has to perceive, um, the world, uh, to do decision-making, right? So, the next natural question is, uh, how can we extend our transformers and LLMs uh, to handle visual observations? So, um, maybe you already remember this"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "from one homework assignment, but very, like, uh, kind of recap uh, about image tokenization. Uh, the kind of the key insight from the vision, uh, transformer paper, uh, sorry, from, uh, Alexey, uh, who I overlapped with, uh, in Freiburg, um, when he was a post doc there, by the way. And, uh, Lucas Beyer, uh, who will come later in the course to give, uh, in-person guest lecture, uh, is quite simple. Um, so the idea is that you treat image patches, uh, as tokens. And so, you take your image, like, uh, 224 by 224, um, split it into your grid of 16 by 16 patches, um, that gives you 196 patches, um, and then you flatten each patch uh, each of these into a vector, project this linearly, and then you add a positional encoding to tell the transform transformer where in the image each patch patch came from."}, {"t": 1740, "text": "And you're done. Um, so now you have 196 tokens that all look exactly like text tokens to the transformer. And um, so essentially this is kind of similar to the philosophy of the BPE we saw for language, right? We kind of try to find the right unit, turn it into a vector, and then we let the transformer figure out the rest. And so for language, kind of sub words was this unit, and for images it's patches. And because now the interface is identical, you can start mixing and matching these text and visual tokens in the same sequence. So, um, so now we are at this kind of elegant situation where everything is a token, but this kind of raises the question if we are just concatenate this this this kind of image and text tokens in the sequence together,"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "then the transformer has no idea which one came from an image and which one came from a text and how they relate, right? And so for example, ideally we would want our model to be able to relate text tokens to um, let's say the word apple with the visual tokens corresponding to let's say the bounding box of an apple in the image, so that we can later ask our robot to pick up an apple and with a language instruction that it kind of kind of understand what we are telling it to do, right? So, so how can we teach the model to align these two modalities? One of the first answers to this question came from OpenAI's CLIP model. CLIP trains two separate encoders, hence the two towers, a VIT for images and a transformer for text. And then learns to align them in a shared embedding space using contrastive learning. Um and so it's very simple. You have a"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "batch of N image-text pairs scraped from the internet and then you encode um uh all of them into a N by N matrix where you compute the cosine similarities. Um and then you have a kind of like a temperature parameter to control the entropy or the sharpness of your distribution. Um and um yeah, and this way with this contrastive alignment you get now visual visually um visual representations that semantically are aligned with language. So now the image of a apple and the word apple will end up nearby in the embedding space of this model. Which is exactly what we wanted. All right, sorry. So"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "now this is another approach to aligning vision and language but with a different goal. So CLIP was about learning a shared embedding space between the two modalities. And now LLaVA, sorry, asks a different question. It asks, how do we inject visual information directly into a pre-trained LLM so that it can now reason over images in the same way it reasons over text. Cuz with CLIP we didn't have like an LLM, right? Um and so the answer here is to do early fusion. So you take your image, you run it through a frozen clip uh vision encoder, um so you get your uh patch embeddings. You pass those now through a uh small trainable MLP connection layer um um to fit them into the right dimensionality, and then you just prepend them um to your text token sequence. And now the LLM runs the standard self-attention over the entire unified"}, {"t": 1981, "text": "sequence uh with no architectural changes uh whatsoever. Um and so um here the key design choice is what is uh frozen and what is trained. So, the CLIP encoder stays frozen all the time, um so you leverage all the pre-training um and you're not touching this. And then the MLP connector uh learns how to project the visual embeddings into the LLM's token space. And then the LLM uh gets fine-tuned uh on visual instruction data uh teaching the model how to follow um instructions in uh that are grounded in images. Then there is a third way um um that world uh was uh um that comes from the Flamingo paper um called late fusion via gated cross-attention. Um and this is kind of contrasting to the Lava's early fusion approach. Um the key distinction is how and where uh vision gets injected into the"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "language model. So, in Lava, the image uh tokens are prepended uh to the text sequence, and then everything goes through the self uh to the same uh self-attention. Um Flamingo says uh what if instead of keeping the LLM's uh self-attention uh operating purely on text, um uh can we inject visual information at every layer uh via gated cross-attention? And so, concretely, what they uh proposed here is they um keep the vision encoder frozen, and then they uh kind of compressed its output with a perceiver resampler into a fixed size set of keys and values. And then at each layer of the LLM, there is a new cross-attention block inserted where the text tokens act as queries and attend to those visual keys and values. And kind of the elegant part of the whole approach is the tanh gate cuz it's initialized to zero. So this means that at the start of the"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "training, the cross-attention contributes exactly nothing. So the model behaves identically to the original LLM. And then as the training proceeds, this gate starts to gradually open up and the visual information flows in. So this is a very principled way to preserve the pre-trained language abilities while slowly learning to incorporate vision. Okay, so LaMDA and Flamingo share the same fundamental assumption. You start with a pre-trained language model and then you somehow add vision to it, right? But there is a third approach that asks, what if we just throw away all of that and train everything jointly from scratch? And that's the idea behind native multimodal models. So instead of adapting language a language model to handle images, you train a single unified transformer on all modalities"}, {"t": 2161, "text": "simultaneously. So images, audio, video, text, it doesn't matter. You interleave them in any order in any combination. And so the input now is completely flexible. You can have image image tokens followed by text, audio, sorry, audio followed by video or any arbitrary mix and combination. And so the The doesn't have uh let's say primary modality um uh with others bolted on. It just sees a sequence of tokens and learns to reason over uh all of them jointly. Um the appeal is obvious um um because uh in principle, this is the bitter lesson kind of like scale to the extreme um because there is no architectural bias toward anyone modality, uh but obviously is the most expensive one to train. Um so most of the current VLMs that you"}, {"t": 2221, "text": "know are related to one of these three paradigms we just covered. Um so early fusion on the left, uh which is basically a pre-trained LLM plus a vision adapter, uh is also the most popular one um in open-source community with Lava, PolyGemma, um Gemma 3, and so forth. Um the recipe is the same. You just uh swap in a better vision encoder, better base LLM, and iterate. Um then uh late uh fusion in the middle is a bit of a smaller family, um and then natively multimodal on the right is where the big proprietary models uh are converging, uh like Gemini, GPT-4.0, GPT-5. Uh and also increasingly um some of the um bigger open-source frontiers like Lama 4 or Qwen 3.5. Um the trade-off here is essentially flexibility versus uh preservation Sorry, uh versus scalability. Um so early fusion allows uh the richest"}, {"t": 2280, "text": "cross-modal interaction uh because the images and text uh mix freely through uh self-attention, but then you have to like fine-tune the LLM and risk degrading its capabilities. Um late fusion uh preserves the pre-trained LLM perfectly, but constrains how vision and language can interact uh since they only uh uh communicate through this um cross-attention pathways. Um, and then native multimodal uh, removes all of the constraints, um, but you pay the cost of training everything from scratch. All right. So, we went from transformers, um, for better language translation to LLMs as general reasoners to VLMs on native multimodal models that can perceive and reason about the world. And every step in this progression was kind of the same play. Uh, we find the right the right way to tokenize a new modality, feed it into the same architecture, and scale it. Uh,"}, {"t": 2340, "text": "text and images, uh, then audio and video, right? So, now the question is obvious, uh, can we do the same thing for, uh, robot actions? Can we tokenize, uh, actions, throw them into the same sequence as observations and language, uh, and train a model that can reason over all of them jointly? And this means that we can view robotics, uh, as a multimodal sequence prediction problem where we have language tokens, image tokens, um, and instead of, uh, kind of answer text tokens, uh, action tokens now that can command, uh, the robot's motors. Um, and so, basically, we can now run the same next, uh, word, uh, kind of prediction architecture, uh, and we can changing anything fundamental, but just adding like new, uh, robot action tokens. And, uh, by using now the attention mechanism, right? Um, we can now, uh, we"}, {"t": 2402, "text": "are asking, um, what from my entire history is relevant, uh, to what I should be doing right now, right? So, with the attention, we are kind of like also, um, um, looking, um, to solve one of these like, uh, memory issues that we, uh, uh, were mentioning in the beginning. So, how can we tokenize, uh, robot actions? Uh, because actions are continuous, um, and they're not discrete, right? So, the first way how we can, uh, go around this is to discretize them. The first approach used, um, by models like RT-2 and Open VLA, uh, used a simple, uh, per dimension per time step binning scheme. Um, so the first, that's pretty simple. First, um, you do quantile normalization, uh, so for each action dimension, you compute the first and 99th, uh, percentile of the action distribution, uh, across your training data, and then you clip to those bounds."}, {"t": 2460, "text": "Uh, and then by normalizing into the range minus one to one, uh, using the quantile bounds, um, the, the discretization is identical and comparable across every robot and every dimension. RT-2 originally used, uh, min-max normalization, but that doesn't work really well with, uh, outliers, as, uh, this can result in having to fit the majority of your distribution into, let's say, the first three bins, uh, and then waste the remaining 253 bins, uh, to represent this one outlier. Um, so yeah, so we discretize then each action dimension, uh, we have, uh, with the quantiles, and we have normalized this into minus one and one, um, and then into, uh, we discretize them into 256 bins. Uh, and then we finally, uh, inject this into our LLM vocabulary. Right? So, now for each, uh, action dimension, uh, we have like this, uh,"}, {"t": 2520, "text": "256 uh, bins. And how do we connect this with our LLM? Um, one common approach, uh, is what you can do is you can override, um, the uh, least frequent tokens in the vocabulary. So, let's say if you have 256 action tokens, the least uh, 256 frequent uh, tokens in in your vocabulary, um, these are often some I don't know, weird Chinese symbols or characters. Um, and you can override those now with your new action tokens to connect um, your LLM with your action tokens. And at this point now, the model is just can do next token prediction with the same uh, cross-entropy loss and the same architecture um, as the LLM. Um, so remember right at the uh, start of the lecture, we identified these two problems with the reactive policies, right? So, the first one was like memory"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "uh, where like a single observation can capture the full state of the world. Um, and then the second was action smoothness, um, making independent decisions at every time step produces this kind of jittery movements, right? Or incoherent behavior. Um, and so we uh, kind of addressed memory through the sequence modeling problem in the transformers. Uh, and action chunking is the direct answer to the smoothness problem. Um, so the key idea here is that instead of predicting just the next step, we ask the model uh, to predict a trajectory of future actions, so the next K actions. Um, so the most basic version um, you can see at the top of the diagram, where we query the model once, um, it gives us a chunk, let's say the next four time steps, um, of the trajectory. We execute those, uh, which gives us a more fluid motion, uh, since uh, all four actions were planned uh, at the same time or at once instead of sequentially with a one-step model. And after that, we re-query the model to"}, {"t": 2640, "text": "give us the prediction of the next four actions, right? And so forth. Now, um, if we extend this to longer horizon chunks, um, let's say to accommodate higher frequency control cuz you're half a humanoid or something, uh, let's say we have now a hundred, uh, like a action chunk of a hundred, um, that we are predicting. Um, there is one failure mode where, um, if we like execute all a hundred actions before we, uh, re-query the model, like in the meantime, like in this two seconds, the the robot is blind, right? It it doesn't it's not updating anything, and you know, bad things could happen in between, right? Uh, that we didn't account for. Um, and so, one solution for this is to do temporal ensembling, uh, which you can see at the bottom, um, where you re-query your model at every time step and maintain multiple overlapping predictions for every time step."}, {"t": 2700, "text": "Um, so for any, uh, given time step, now you can combine predictions for multiple queries with, uh, exponentially decaying weights. And now this gives you the smoothness benefits of chunking while staying reactive to new observations at every step. And ACT is, um, the paper, uh, from Tony Zhao, uh, Cheng's, uh, co-founder at Sunday Robotics, uh, if you remember the guest lecture from, uh, last week. Uh, and in this paper, um, they introduced action chunking from for Dexter's bimanual control. The setup is, uh, a bimanual Lo Ha robot, um, doing 50 hertz, uh, control, and the model predicts, um, chunks of a hundred actions at a time. Um, so as the robot is doing joint control with, uh, 14 degrees of freedom, um, the model is predicting, um, 1,400 values at every forward pass. And, uh, this work showed, uh, that this, uh, action chunking works really well when you're doing,"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "uh, really dexterous tasks like this battery insertion, which requires very precise coordinated two-arm motions with very little tolerance for any jitter or unsmooth behaviors. Where like a reactive policy making independent one-step decisions at 50 hertz would struggle quite a bit with doing these tasks, but by having this policy that is planning like kind of smooth like 2-second chunks of trajectories, you can see that it can execute it much more fluidly. All right. So, we have established that larger action chunks are helpful to do smoother high-frequency dexterous control. But ACT was not using any LLM and it was it was not predicting 1,400 values or tokens auto-regressively. Um cuz like once you once you want to do this with a VLA,"}, {"t": 2822, "text": "this starts to break down very quickly. Especially the higher frequency you go um because the tokens start to look very similar to each other. Um so, basically the higher the frequency you go, the less difference is there between one step to the next, right? Like it's such high frequency that you know, you look at one frame and the next one and there's like almost no movement happening in between, right? And um the learning signal for an auto-regressive model comes from the new information each token provides. And so, when the tokens are highly correlated, the marginal information content of the next token approaches basically zero. Um so, and now this means that the model this is very important can achieve a low prediction error by simply learning to copy the previous action token, which prevents it from learning the actual dexter skill."}, {"t": 2880, "text": "Um and so on on here on the far you can see how uh performance um starts to degrade uh once we train VLAs uh auto-regressively uh at higher uh control frequencies like 10 Hz or 20 Hz with this uh naive um binning uh tokenization scheme that we just explained. Um so the obvious solution Oh, is to do uh some sort of compression. Right? So, instead of um tokenizing every individual action, you want to find a more compact representation like the sub-words in the original BPE tokenization that captures the meaningful structure of the motion. Right? So, we want to find this equivalent uh kind of representation of the sub-words uh for BPE or the patches for images for our robot trajectories. Right? So, um units that are larger than than than a single time step, but like"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "smaller um than an entire trajectory. And so, how can we best compress um data? Right? Um we we just covered in LLMs uh we can use BPE uh which works well for discrete domains, but as we uh just said like we have continuous uh data streams uh for robotics. Uh we could use like maybe like VQ-VAEs, um but then this is one more model and you know, more complexity to train. And um the key idea here uh what you can do um to get more efficient uh compression um um is actually borrowed from signal processing um which is to use the discrete cosine transform. Um so, if you ever wondered how um for example, JPEG compression works, this is essentially it. Um so uh robot action chunks are smooth trajectories and smooth signals have most of their energy in high frequency"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "Sorry, in the low frequency domains. So, when you apply DCT to each action dimension, you are moving well, moving from the time domain to the frequency domain. This gives you a sparse frequency matrix where most high frequencies are near zero. You You condense scale and round these coefficients, flatten the matrix with the low frequency components first, and then you can run BPE on the actual resulting integer sequence. And so, what BPE will do is it will squash the zeros and merge the frequent coefficients combinations, giving you now a compact sequence of dense tokens that you can plug directly into your existing vocabulary. So, what are the results when we train a"}, {"t": 3060, "text": "VLA with this approach? Looking at the left plot, we can see that our performance now doesn't break after 5 Hz for an auto regressive VLA. We can actually keep improving performance, and the compression is doing exactly what it should, right? It's preserving the information that matters and disregarding redundant high-frequency correlations. And on the on the right side, you can see what this compression is is buying us at test time. Here, we adapted Pi Zero to do Sorry, we adapted Pi Pi Zero to do auto regression using this fast organization scheme we just introduced on top of the PolyGenna backbone and compared it against the the Pi zero from physical intelligence that is using flow matching instead of auto regression for action generation. And you can see that our model converges five times faster to the same performance."}, {"t": 3121, "text": "This was a collaboration I did with physical intelligence during my time at Berkeley. And one of the coolest results here is that we trained a very on the droid data set that could do zero shot language tasks in the wild. So we tested it in unseen kitchens across Berkeley, Stanford, and University of Washington. And it worked really well. And also we were able to do now complex long horizon by manual manipulation tasks like laundry folding at 50 hertz. Essentially now pushing along both axis of general generality and dexterity, which typically are in tension, right? Because either you build like kind of specialist policies like ACT that are very precise but narrow or generalist policies like open VLA that are broad but usually not dexterous"}, {"t": 3181, "text": "enough for fine manipulation. And this kind of work kind of make advances across both. And also an output of this work is a good off-the-shelf tokenizer that's available on hugging face that is useful for like training VLAs. If you want to check it out. All right. So to conclude today, we started with a simple observation. Reactive policies are fundamentally limited because one snapshot of the world can't capture the full state, right? And independent per step decisions produce jittery behavior. And the fix we addressed in this lecture is to reformulate robotics as a sequence modeling problem. Um and then we went around building this this toolkit, right? Transformers gives us a scalable architecture for sequence modeling with"}, {"t": 3240, "text": "01 access to any parts of our history. Uh scaling those Transformers gives us LLMs with emergent capabilities. Um we have seen how to extend them to vision uh through early fusion, late fusion, or native multimodal training. Um this gives gives us VLMs that can jointly reason over images and language. And then the final step extending all of that to robot actions, which turns out to be non-trivial because naive tokenization breaks at high control frequency. Uh and you need compression-based approaches like FAST to make autoregressive action generation actually work um at the frequencies the the robots require. Um the central insight running through all the lecture is really the bitter lesson applied to robotics. Uh find the right representation, use a general scalable"}, {"t": 3300, "text": "architecture, and then let the data compute do the work. Um with this, thank you for your attention."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Limitations of reactive models"}, {"t": 136, "title": "Sequence modeling fundamentals"}, {"t": 289, "title": "Transformer architecture"}, {"t": 446, "title": "Attention and encodings"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Original transformer design"}, {"t": 825, "title": "Language tokenization"}, {"t": 1068, "title": "Scaling and engineering"}, {"t": 1224, "title": "Scaling laws and emergence"}, {"t": 1406, "title": "Compute-optimal scaling"}, {"t": 1645, "title": "Visual tokenization"}, {"t": 1839, "title": "Multimodal fusion techniques"}, {"t": 2127, "title": "Native multimodal models"}, {"t": 2312, "title": "Action tokenization"}, {"t": 2569, "title": "Action chunking techniques"}, {"t": 2797, "title": "Efficient action tokenization"}, {"t": 3127, "title": "Conclusion and summary"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "VS7Ulaugevg", "title": "Ted Xiao: Three Eras of Robot Learning [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Ted Xiao", "duration": "36:36", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Ted Xiao (Founding Member of Technical Staff at Project Prometheus), who's been at the forefront of scaling robotics at \nGoogle DeepMind,  talks about the three eras of robot learning & how the field has evolved and where it's headed.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 184, "title": "Era of existence proofs"}, {"t": 654, "title": "Era of foundation models"}, {"t": 1281, "title": "Era of scaling"}, {"t": 1716, "title": "Q&A and future outlook"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Um, super excited uh, to welcome Ted today uh, for our uh, guest spotlight. Um, Ted Xiao is currently a founding member of the technical staff at Project Prometheus. Um, previously uh, he was a staff research scientist. Um, and also a technical lead at Google DeepMind on the robotics uh, team and also the Gemini multimodal team. Uh, his research agenda focuses on scaling physical AGI in the real world with a particular focus on approaches that can leverage internet-scale foundation models and methods that can improve with more experience. And uh, with this uh, we are very excited uh, to hear from you, Ted. Please give him a warm uh, welcome. Thanks for having me uh, Oier and uh, really uh, excited to to help kind of share some uh, perspectives or uh, showcase a lot of the progress that I've"}, {"t": 60, "text": "seen over uh, the last decade or so in robot learning um, and I think they a lot of today will be historical, but I think a lot of it will also uh, be directly relevant. Uh, the past tells us what the future will often hold and the technology trends I think that have just only accelerated and compounded are kind of uh, the whole reason I would say many times behind the entire hype and the progress happening today. Um, to kind of set the stage for a kind of like where a lot of the really exciting work in robot learning happens, uh, I think you can take a look back in 2015-2016 when really awesome breakthroughs uh, you know, um, from from DeepMind like a DQN or AlphaGo were happening. Um, the those are the seminal works on the left I think that inspired uh, kind of the initial um, that's in real-world robot learning which was still kind of a very much so unproven crazy idea. Um, but the essential kind of founding thesis was"}, {"t": 120, "text": "that we see all this immense progress in you applying learning-based methods to decision-making settings mostly in in video games or or or board games like Go or Atari, um, but on the other hand we actually did already have at that point uh, you know, electromechanical systems of robots that you know, were still pale in comparison to what we have today, but you know, they were able to you know, have this like quite famous that demo from a few decades ago at Stanford where they were able to remote teleop this mobile manipulation robot to actually go and clean up this living room or fetch you a beer from the kitchen. Uh, this is the technology for this the hardware had already existed for quite a bit now. It was just the intelligence that was the bottleneck. The the the generality of uh, the the actual performance a system that could operate in many conditions completely autonomously. And so the idea was like if the hardware is like you know, almost ready or it's it's it's definitely close to there, um, but the intelligence is not, but then you do have these intelligence breakthroughs in the digital domain, can you kind of"}, {"t": 180, "text": "explore the intersection of these two? And that was kind of the founding thesis of uh, the Google Brain team and and quite a few other organizations at that time and I think that kicked off the first era that I'll talk about today which is the era of existence proofs starting from around 2015-16-17 up until I would say about 21. Um, and and for each of these eras I'll maybe highlight some of the works that I felt really defined kind of the the vibes during this time. Um, some of the technical work kind of the thought behind it and the kind of results and and and the questions that were evolving during that time. Um, most of the work I'm talking about uh, today is just is from uh, Google DeepMind or Google Brain uh, just because that's where I had the most intimacy uh, of the you know, understanding what was going on, but of course during this time there's tons of interesting work happening all around the world in so many different labs and different um, you know, groups, um, but and uh, I'll I'll touch upon some of those at the end. So, circa 2016, right? DQN, AlphaGo, uh,"}, {"t": 243, "text": "Google Brain just had the idea of like what if we just bought you know, half a dozen a dozen uh, you know, very expensive robots. Every academic lab only had maybe one. What if we just had uh, you know, quite a few of them, put them in a room, have them collecting real-world experience for picking up various objects and bins and then we just try to figure out kind of what this environment looks like, what this MVP looks like, uh, what uh, and figure out the operations behind it and because we're just going to be investing a ton of resources into this, let's see if we can just scale things up and get things to learn. Um, this was you know, maybe you've seen some of these images uh, you know, that look like the left and the middle. These arm farms where you just have a bunch of robots uh, a bunch of Kukas together uh, picking up data uh, picking up different objects, collecting large-scale data sets um, mostly through some kind of autonomous exploration policy or some kind of off-policy learning on-policy learning. Um, that started working a bit. There were definitely things that worked well. Some"}, {"t": 300, "text": "things that were a bit trickier, uh, but it worked well enough that you know, ambition to scale to multitask learning a single policy that could actually understand and execute different tasks different skills in the real world became a very strong goal. Um, and that later then you know, spread beyond just the single bin to a single table to maybe a whole lab, right? Still a kind of structured environment, but you know, we can mock out this kitchen-like setting you see on the right um, and that was like the era of where we were really scaling up these offline data sets. Um, it's to touch upon some of the work from this era, right? We can we can start from this first one of like the initial first arm farms, right? Where can we even do end-to-end robot learning in the real world? Is this crazy idea something that's even possible? Um, here it was a lot of stuff like setting up these robots, putting them in a room, instrumenting them, uh, making sure that the deployment data collection and eval would work properly, taking the kind of methods that work well um, let's say DQN, right? Uh, you Q-learning is pretty"}, {"t": 360, "text": "powerful. Learning value functions and value-based RL was really interesting, but with robotics things got trickier with high-dimensional continuous action spaces. So, how do you do that? Um, you're not you're not you're not doing tabular Q-learning anymore. How do you learn over a continuous value function over a continuous state and action space? What happens when your observations are now higher-resolution pixels? How do you do that? Um, so the one of the the foundations of this works of what was called QT-Opt um, which was basically how we can apply the idea of Q-learning to robotics where we ran out of this you know, arm farm had more than 500,000 grasps and structures which was quite a bit at that time um, and then and then training with off-policy and and and as well as on-policy online rollouts, figuring out how to do that. Um, we also explored ways of evaluation. So, this RL CycleGAN work was a was kind of like an OG um, real-to-sim real and real-to-sim evaluation protocol where we would we would have a digital twin of grasping various objects in sim and this is still"}, {"t": 421, "text": "the era of spice of of GANs. So, style transferred and turned the sim images into realistic images uh, so that we could then kind of eval in a realistic-looking simulation. So, to the policy whether it was evaluating in the real world or in sim, ideally it was pretty agnostic and the sim evaluations could be a good signal. Um, we also were looking at you know, the full stack of not only do we treat this um, robotics domain as uh, quasi-static where you can pause anytime, the real world is is dynamic. So, if you want fast reactive uh, dynamic-looking robot trajectories, you also need to think about how to do kind of policy execution and learning concurrently with uh, um, your your inference time. Uh, the the next brief kind of batch of results was uh, when we were really thinking about okay, great. This QT-Opt thing, this real-world off-policy RL seems promising. How do we scale that up? Um, how do we get better performance? How do we get learn many tasks? This was definitely not easy um,"}, {"t": 481, "text": "and we were starting to hit I would say the bottlenecks of kind of what's challenging about these like real-world large-scale RL uh, flywheels. Um, and and so we started exploring a variety of different methods especially betting larger on imitation learning. With BC-Zero we collected a quite a large teleoperated data set uh, on on this uh, you know, one-arm mobile manipulation robot um, from Google X um, from Everyday Robotics uh, and then trained a language-conditioned behavior cloning policy. We also looked at how these kinds of imitation learning methods could play well with RL, right? Get the best of both worlds. Maybe you you start out with uh, you know, bootstrapping from expert data imitation learning and you transition to this like online self-improvement uh, you know, a reward function-based RL regime. How do you do that? Um, we of course also just took the thing that kind of worked QT-Opt and scaled it up to multiple tasks MT-Opt multiple tasks QT-Opt. Um, and finally I think we also started exploring uh, more novel algorithms uh, of like how you could scale up the generality or diversity of the data"}, {"t": 541, "text": "distributions of training over. Um, learning from play was one of these works where we just thought okay, what if instead of collecting this very real task-specific RL rollout or this you know, expert giving uh, you know, trajectories of of teleop, maybe you can just have experts just play around in an environment for you know, a very very long period of time. Then you use this very unstructured ungoal-oriented data to actually extract useful general [clears throat] um, you know, task-specific skills from that. Um, and and so these are all kind of I would say just just the next batch of works that took the foundations that were established in this first era arm farms and then scaled them up a bit more, explored different ways to apply them. And I think at the end of this uh, kind of you know, a section where we were okay, these all kind of work, can we scale them one step further? A lot of folks we had this like really big idea of like okay, what if instead of like these incremental scaling, we just started over from scratch. We have this new robot at the beginning of COVID, uh"}, {"t": 600, "text": "can we just invest now a year, year and a half on this really, you know, interesting bet of building out a large contracting team, having a much larger fleet of robots, and then they're just all collecting demonstrations in the same environment, um where our goal is not to solve one task or 10 tasks or 20 tasks, but hundreds or thousands of tasks. Um and and that's what we did. We set up the operations, we scaled up this data collection effort, and to my knowledge, this is one of the largest uh expert demo collection uh kind of projects, um and it was happening for over a year and a half on on many robots in the environments you see here. These are now all learned policies, but uh you know, going from, you know, this very wide uh large-scale diverse distribution of of training uh into this like more open-world evaluation setting was, you know, kind of a big lightbulb moment for for many on the team. Um and I think around when that data set started getting ready with the operations started getting the risk, that's what I would call like the"}, {"t": 660, "text": "beginning of the era of foundation models. Because while we were doing all this stuff, right? Like like the the the the the QT-Opt, the the BC-Zero, all of these explorations, at the same time our neighbors and our peers in the language modeling world started to, I think a lot of the breakthroughs there really started to scale, right? Uh this was, you know, GPT-3, 3.5, InstructGPT, it was uh you know, the era of Palm of of um LaMDA, it was the era of ChatGPT. Um and and you could see when you did train on an internet scale of different tasks and data distributions on Wikipedia and Reddit, and when you did kind of treat everything as, uh you know, if if you can ask for it, maybe the language model has a chance of doing it, um depending depending on prompting. All of these trends kind of were like really really picking up steam, and and so I think in the robotics world, we started to think about it seems like this new magical alien technology of foundation models and and and internet scale, you know, next token prediction seemed to be really taking off. How can we leverage"}, {"t": 721, "text": "those models themselves or even ideas of these models that drive these models to create foundation models for robotics? Um and I'll and I'll talk about some of the highlights here. Um I think if you just look um at what makes foundation model work, we could break these into different components. Uh so there's quite a certain design principles of treating everything as tokens, or there's the certain idea that like you can leverage pre-trained representations or pre-trained models entirely because the capabilities they showcase are general, all right? You can leverage foundation models as components, a part of a larger system. And there's also the idea that, you know, we went from online R farms, which were, you know, pretty powerful, the idea of autonomous self-improvement, but in practice were very difficult because of uh you know, the fact that on-policy online data collection would was very costly in terms of uh robot time and and and and human kind of maintenance um of managing these data distributions. If you could switch to this more offline learning setting that the rest of ML and supervised learning was kind of leading into, uh could we do that? So that was a"}, {"t": 782, "text": "bet on collecting large-scale behavioral cloning expert demonstrations, where it didn't really matter that the the expert demo was not collected by your policy, the fact that it was just, you know, the golden standard oracle behavior uh was quite powerful, and uh leveraging like uh economies of scale, so to speak. So if you combine these components, right? The design principles, the large offline robot data sets, and leveraging existing foundation models, which operate mainly over language as a substrate, you get this kind of like potential recipe for how you could create a robot foundation model um kind of in-house or from scratch. Um this was Robot Transformer 1, RT-1. Uh this was where we took different performance components, uh a very strong uh vision encoder, uh Vision Net, um and with film cross-attention, uh token learner, uh transformer, um uh backbone to kind of get to this tokens in tokens in out world, where we have RGB images, we have a language instruction, and then we generate uh"}, {"t": 841, "text": "different robot actions represented as discretized tokens. Um uh and and uh all of these design decisions, you have tokenized input outputs, you have decoder-only uh architecture, you have an image tokenizer, and and and you use kind of some kind of more intelligent like quantization almost or or or or or compression of of action tokens. These ideas, when you put them together that were inspired by kind of all the progress happening in broader ML, you combine them into one model, we got this like first general-purpose uh robot transformer, which could do hundreds of tasks, um you know, using imitation learning at like, you know, pretty reasonable performance, not perfect maybe between 60s and 80s, um but it was very interesting because it was like the first attempt at, you know, just taking from first principles a lot of the kind of uh bleeding-edge trends, scaling up to 35 million parameters, running at 3 Hz, um and this was kind of like a first, I would say, existence proof and a a a a a"}, {"t": 900, "text": "showcase of what these kind of concepts could give you. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um but at the same time, right now that we had RT-1, this like general backbone, we're also thinking of, \"Great, we had to do a lot ourselves, that we had to adapt these models, you had to rip them from pre-trained models and combine them like, you know, Frankenstein in a lab.\" It also seemed that these models by themselves were extremely powerful. For example, language models, right? They were already starting to show that they could they would hallucinate a lot, but they would still sometimes directionally get common sense reasoning down, they would have some physical intuition, it wasn't perfect, but was there a way that we could leverage that for, you know, robotics? Um one idea was that, \"Okay, great, they can give you like a plan of like, please put an apple on a table.\" Great, break it down into a plan, what would you first do? What's the order of operations that you should be doing? Um you could send out that that that kind of prompt to an LLM and look at what its completions were being and look at, you know, the the kind of uh log probs that would give you, and that's kind of what you see the numbers on the on the left. Um what's important though then is actually grounding that in what your"}, {"t": 960, "text": "robot policy, RT-1 in this case, could actually do. Um and here we we we we trained a value function, a Q function, um on top of uh um our our RT-1 uh that would tell you kind of like for each of these instructions in my current state, uh how likely is it that I can actually accomplish it. And this was a nice way then to kind of uh importance way or or kind of combine what the language model thought was important based on their log probs with with the robot policy that was actually doable, feasible, um via its kind of internal value function. And and by combining these then, you could then get plans that were more grounded. Like maybe a language model would say, you know, picking up the apple's good, but, you know, the robot policy knows that's not possible right now, I first even navigate and go find an apple. And so by combining these two worlds, uh we kind of got the first collision of uh using language models just completely off the shelf and putting that into a uh combined robotics system uh for planning long-horizon tasks, breaking them down into intermediate subgoals that RT-1"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "could then complete. Um there were other ways to use um foundation models as well. Uh one way was data augmentation. Uh RT-1, the data set that we collected, uh was by these days standards tiny, it was uh 87,000 trajectories collected over a year and a half, um but the instruction diversity was actually not quite large. Um and so the idea was, could we use a VLM for synthetic generation to relabel uh kind of these episodes as different tasks that could have been feasible? Can we relabel them, create a much larger data set, and then uh now we can retrain RT-1 on this much more or rich language language distribution. This is very similar to these days how uh models like image generation models or or video models like VIO are trained, a lot of synthetic captioning that kind of uses data to distill in and arbitrage what the VLM has learned, but what your data set did not contain. Um and then, you know, beyond just taking LLMs for sake and or VLMs for dial, uh"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "we also started thinking about, \"What if instead of expressing these foundation model benefits in arbitrage via data, what if we just made them the backbone itself?\" Um and and so this was kind of a project called RT-2, where we explored a regime of, you know, vision-language-action modeling. If you take a VLM, and which is trained on on tasks like you see on the middle, like, you know, captioning what is happening in this image or uh different language tasks, um could you also just treat robot action prediction as a visual question answering task? Given an image and given this certain kind of, you know, what should the robot do to, you know, whatever, um close the drawer, uh then can you just respond in language tokens that can get then get decoded to actions? Um and and could you then scale this up the same way that VLM backbones have exhibited very powerful scaling laws. Um we tried this on on various VLMs, Poly X and PaLM-E, uh and we and we mixed kind of, you know, 50/50 with internet data, with robotics data on the same RT-1 data set, and we found that this VLA paradigm was actually quite"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "powerful, and you were able to transfer a lot of concepts from this internet distribution around what new objects are, around common sense reasoning, around maybe recognizing even written text, a lot of things that you we wouldn't necessarily have expected because we never collected these kinds of information in our robot data set, but because they were in this internet distribution, and we were always co-fine-tuning, um this had a very good transfer properties when we were measuring generalization of VLAs to many new kind of out-of-distribution scenarios. Um and another project here was the Open Cross Modal Agent project, which which was I I view as a seminal existence proof of whether or not you could one coordinate, like, you know, a institutions around the world, more than 30 research labs, kind of combine all the data that's been collected for various projects, combine that data now, and then see if you could learn something new from it. Make Could you Could you expose it, clean that data, make it easy to kind of, you know, train on, and and and and could you start to see then, uh, you know, this kind of"}, {"t": 1200, "text": "motion uh knowledge transferring between different robot morphologies, even though the data was collected by different people for different use cases on different robots around the world? Um, during this time, too, I would say, uh, um, and I I think this is one of the the last ones of the era of foundation models is now that you do have these very strong existence proofs, RT1, RT2. You have large-scale datasets, and you have foundation models really maturing very fast. Uh, right? Um, new new new models are dropping every week uh that were very smart. Could you use these better for robot robotics field? RT2 is just kind of existence proof. If you did it this one way of just, you know, casting robot actions to strings essentially with some smart tricks, like could could this ever work? And the answer was yes, this could work. So, now, you know, I think a new interesting research area was, are there better ways to combine robotics and express them for VLMs or VLA training? Are there better ways of representing robot actions and physical motion? Um, and we There was quite a few that we explored um at"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "Google DeepMind ranging from object-centric segmentation masks all the way to hierarchical language where you break down a longer horizon instruction into shorter horizon substeps um looking at actual affordances, grasp angles and grasp angles and key points, or even trajectories um which were you know, just from an egocentric view. But, I think uh you know, all of this work was happening at immense velocity from 2021 all the way maybe to 2024 or early 2025, I think clearly in the last year and a year or two, um, there's been tremendous amounts of investments and energy and excitement in the into the idea that maybe these existence proofs that we saw, maybe the initial early foundation models, VLAs, this technology is is actually enough to generate real value, to actually put uh crazy amounts behind, and actually go and try to commercialize. Or or if you want to do more fundamental research, you need to scale this research needs to happen at order of magnitude or more higher. Um,"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "and and I think this era of scale um kind of led to quite a few works up until the present day, um, as well as a few directions which which I'll be highlighting. Um, so the first of which is, you know, to cap off off some of the work that would happen most recently at Google DeepMind, um, after seeing the promises of RT2, of RT1, of this like this kitchen multi mobile manipulation kind of domain, uh, there was a huge transition on the team to, okay, these foundation models that we're using, Palm and Polly, were quite interesting, but, you know, Gemini has been in a league of its own as a very strong multimodal frontier model. Uh, right? Um, and and and in addition, uh, there was kind of this hardware trend that Aloha, this bimanual Aloha platform where you puppeteer uh was giving you much higher data throughput at much higher quality where you could learn much more of dexterous behaviors. Um, and so this is kind of showcasing a project called Gemini Robotics which was released around March of last year that combined kind of this like embodied reasoning, the world knowledge of Gemini 2.0, and"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "then taught it how to do immense amount of diverse robot actions on the Aloha bodyment um that showcased a state of the art in dexterity and be able to post-train to new bodyments like the um the uh Unitronics humanoid on the right or this um you know, Biomech Franka setup um as well as exhibit kind of like compositional generalization around reasoning at test time. Uh, this quickly followed a few months later with Gemini Robotics 1.5. Once again, it had two parts, the body reasoning part, the VLM, the through the reasoning part, as well as the actual VOA, the the action prediction part. And here the focus was on on on two things. One was on thinking, uh, so leveraging kind of language-based reasoning at test time uh to to to exhibit kind of chain of thought. As well as the second way was uh was called motion transfer, which was like um uh no longer needing to post-train to adapt to specific embodiments, rather than um training with all these embodiments, the Aloha, the Biomech Franka, and the the humanoid together at pre-training time with a shared action"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "space. Um, this is very powerful because that a single policy, a single neural network could then control all these uh robots simultaneously without any specific fine-tuning needed. But, then, you know, to to then step away from the work that was being done in Alphabet at Google DeepMind, a lot of things are being scaled down, and a lot of great work was happening across the industry and academia. Um, model generality and performance is getting scaled, robotics data is getting scaled, and I think research directions, the entropy in the research environment is also kind of unprecedented at the moment. When you look at model performance, I look to really awesome work coming out of folks like a pie or or or generalist or or Dyna, you can see learning from experience, adapting, post-training has been a very, you know, increasing trend. Um, I was very impressed by the pie 0.6 paper that was looking at, you know, how you could, you know, really learn these longer horizon, very dexterous, precise tasks like espresso making. Um, I I think uh that was really cool to see."}, {"t": 1500, "text": "Um, uh you know, for generalization is also another type of model performance that isn't just like how What's your end-end success rate, rather how robust are you to changing distributions? Um, I think here, uh, you know, the Google DeepMind team was spending a lot of time and on this and show and seeing very uh you know, very strong results on the ability of the Gemini Robotics uh VLAs to generalize to all these new situations. Um, and now that we do have so much energy in this space, right? A lot of uh players that are putting out really cool foundation models on many different types of robots, just comparing how the field is advancing in terms of our knowledge and our capabilities has become a challenge where a lot of these real-world rollouts have required thousands or even tens of thousands of trials just to validate and get statistically significant samples of performance or AB tests. Um, so there's also really cool work happening in the scalable evaluation space, whether it's happening in Sim with the with the top picture um or whether it's in these decentralized"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "distributed evals like uh RoboArena on the bottom. For robot data scaling, too, I think this is also one of my probably the biggest buzzwords these days. So, like there's no robot of uh you know, there's no internet of robot data. Can we scale that? Um, maybe in the last era, the era of foundation models, a lot of scaling was happening both in-house, right? Berkeley would collect a large dataset, Brace dataset, um and then maybe the Google DeepMind team would collect collect large datasets for uh the uh the one-armed mobile manipulator or for Aloha. But, these days, you know, there's a lot more embodiments. There's a lot more types of data distributions that people are targeting. How do we scale that up? And there's really big bets that are happening now. They're not necessarily mutually exclusive, but they are quite opinionated and different. You have this co-design of hardware, data collecting, and and the model out of players like, you know, Sunday Robotics or Generalist are really getting good at this like UMI-like scale-up um at at at at a global footprint, right? That you can just ship these devices off all around the world, and it's very easy for humans to put on these gloves and collect data. You also have, you know, maybe you skip"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "the glove entirely. You just collect hand data. You scrap You scrap um strap a GoPro or an iPhone on someone's head, and you collect uh you know, egocentric data or or or or or glasses. Um, and this is also, I think, a huge rising trend. And and finally, maybe a very important part of a data distribution at scale is the in in the wild data, deployment data. Um, and and to wrap it up, I think this is my last slide. Uh, I think the science is also getting scaled. There's so many interesting scientific questions, really big bets that are being played, right? What is the importance of world models for data or evals or even as a backbone, right? I'm really excited by world action models, a lot of the the immense progress happening on Dreamer 0 particularly is a very impressive paper. Um, robot-free data that I mentioned, this is getting scaled up immensely, but there's so many important research questions of like, what are the pros and cons of this data compared to expensive, high-quality teleop data? We see a lot of thinking models with RLVR and and and post-training and reasoning happening in language modeling that's driving things like ejected coding. How do those ideas"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "kind of percolate and project into robotics? And finally, I think a big elephant in the room here is a lot of work all the work I talked about today was about manipulation, dexterous, general-purpose manipulation, but it's clear that, you know, for locomotion, for balance, for whole-body control, there's a lot of what happened in Sim to Real in in in Sim to Real with RL that's seeing immense progress in Robots are dancing, they're doing kung fu, they're recovering, they're moving with very high amounts of extreme agility, and that's not happening via imitation learning. That's happening through a very other kind of learning paradigm. How do you unite what we're seeing in manipulation with what we're seeing in locomotion? With that though, I'm uh you know, I'm happy to to to end here. Um, I think that if I had to just summarize this 10-year journey that in sentence is, we I think as a field, we've made more progress than many of us would ever thought would be possible in our lifetimes, but now there's more open questions and uncertainties and open frontiers than ever. So, I think the the area of robotics research is both more critical and closer to fruition than ever, but there's also never been so"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "many like uh you know, composing opposing beliefs and opinions and and and open research ideas where we are really entering kind of a new era of of research. So, with that though, I'm happy to take any questions. Thanks. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Thanks so much for the great talk. Any questions from the audience? Okay, I see one in the back. What do you think the role of high-quality teleoperation data will look like in the future? Will it just be absolute end tuning, or do you think it will still be crucial in the foundational level of the models? Yeah, great question. Um, you know, a question about the the role of real world teleop data. You know, is it going to be fine tuning or else elsewhere? I think again, this is I would say this is a huge um open question, right? Of like um whether or not"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "currently, right? I would say over the last few years, real world teleop data has been the golden standard. It is the bread and butter of most good perform policies today. Where it's like because data quality has been so hard to quantify in robotics that the safe bet was just to max out data quality by doing it in house with your own operations team or your own students who are very close to the model can also figure out whether it is good. I think we are shifting now. I I I think we are now in a place where the burden of high quality data is starting to get externalized, right? To to to you know, distributions that are very different from your target test time deployment. Whether or not this is going to continue I suspect this will probably continue. I like Anuka from Nvidia has this Nvidia has a data pyramid where maybe you start with internet data and sim data and and maybe non-robotic video data. Then you finally the pyramid on top, you know, the cherry on top is the fine tuning data. I think this is a very likely way that the world will trend. The timelines"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "though are what I'm not sure of. If this pyramid takes a decade to solidify before we really understand how to collect very like, you know, sub-optimal crappy general data, but then we learn how to leverage it properly uh so that we only need this tiny cherry on top of real data, that would be great. We're going to get there. If it takes 10 years though, that means that in the meantime it's probably better to prioritize really high quality teleop data. Maybe you just turn that towards a distribution you want. I think there's players who are doing that, right? Who are really looking at the deployment distribution, the long tail data as the mode. They just want them they don't care that the their method their method isn't scalable. They just want the best data possible on this really rare data in human homes, in a workplace, on the assembly line, in the wild, right? On at the job site. So, I think that's definitely for sure going to be very powerful in the next year or two, but if this pyramid of, you know, general purpose sub-optimal data collection at huge diversity, if that research gets"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "unlocked in the next year or two, then I would say the role of teleop data kind of diminishes a lot. If that research takes longer, then I think teleop is still king. Thank you. Yep, we have a second question. Um do you think that video action models will be the next big thing like having uh the backbone learn the physics itself instead of just like putting everything on uh the robot data um and overloading it? And do you think that and what do you think about um working in latent space instead like uh recent V-JEPA like 2.1 and combining those? Yeah, great question. Yeah, yeah, yeah, video models world models video world action models are are are these the next big thing? Um I think in a nutshell, yes, absolutely. I I think I think the properties that change when you really leverage the priors that don't come from language, VLAs, VLMs, most of their capabilities come from language uh from text. But then when you switch then to"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "large scale video based pre-training, the priors you learn around time and space and, you know, intuitive physics are just so different and and often times beneficial. Things that are very hard for text based pre-training to do, maybe video pre-training gives you for free. Um I'm very impressed by all the results. It's very intuitive why this makes a lot of sense. I think every year almost for the past few years everyone someone has tried to do this. I think now is maybe finally the time where where where, you know, video models, the the infra, the the pre-trained policies, the knowledge of like what it takes to train great video models, all of that is kind of there now where maybe we're ready to consume this technology in robotics. That being said though, you know, I I I think um the question of whether or not this is the final thing. Like will will we just replace VLAs with video action models world action models and then we don't look back? Um I'm not sure, right? Or do we need something that's the best of both worlds? I I say that because if you look at the existence proof in our other domains, in knowledge work, in in"}, {"t": 2041, "text": "language models, and even in like native image generation like Dana Banana, a lot of that capability is driven by text. Text is, I think, extremely powerful because of the amount of priors that we we get for free. You're starting from, you know, very, you know, high amounts of reasoning when you kind of distill the internet and this really helps you kind of raise up all of the modalities. So, Dana Banana Pro will take a lot of time reasoning. Same with, I think, some of Open AI's native, you know, image media generation models, they will be actually thinking in text space even if they're predicting in a modality that looks like pixels or audio or something like that. So, if the same type of text based reasoning that allows us to prove new math proofs actually transfers somewhat to robotics, that I think you would still need a substantial amounts of exposure, I think, to text. Which current video models look more like, you know, they they understand the intuitive kind of high frequency details of"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "pixels, but, you know, VIO by itself before it's kind of fused with Gemini is not a super smart text reasoner. So, I think it really depend I think it's actually on a rising trend. I think we'll see really cool progress that we've never seen before, but is this the final recipe? I'm not really sure. I still like text for many reasons, but but of course I think getting the best of both worlds will be closer to the answer. Um what about mobile manipulation? Do you think we're going to have some kind of like VLM conditioned on more classical computer vision stuff like, I don't know, any kind of map of the environment or like what is the most promising direction would you say? Yeah, great question. I think, you know, breaking this into research versus like technology. I think for research direction we talk about foundation models, right? I think the ethos there is not to use you know, let's say a slam mapping or different vision techniques of, you know,"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "especially like localization or things like that. Let's not use them as a black box at test time. Let's just distill those capabilities into the model itself, right? If there was an algorithm, a system, a software stack that could do a very specialized to do certain capabilities, let's just get the foundation models to also do it itself. That's one view. I think it's more like curious about the science itself of like anything that humans were able to write or create as an engineered system or as a specialized, you know, model that we trained, then the general should be able to do that too under some kind of resourcing and compute and scale. But I think, you know, for the commercialization, for the real world deployment,"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 184, "title": "Era of existence proofs"}, {"t": 654, "title": "Era of foundation models"}, {"t": 1281, "title": "Era of scaling"}, {"t": 1716, "title": "Q&A and future outlook"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Prometheus (co-founder)"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 30.03.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (59 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week07_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_ted_xiao.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 7: Sequence Modeling &amp; Transformers</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 30.03.2026</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 1 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 2.]</span></p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 7: Sequence Modeling & Transformers Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 30.03.2026 [Note: slide 1 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 2.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-recap-limitations-of-reactive-policies-frame-2", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: Recap: Limitations of Reactive Policies", "title": "Slide 2: Recap: Limitations of Reactive Policies", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: observation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> (snowy road through a windscreen) -&gt; <strong>Policy</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> (the road with a forked arrow), with an arrow from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p><strong>One Snapshot Can’t Model Full State</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Memory</strong>: what did I do 10 seconds ago? How fast are pedestrians moving?</li>\n<li><strong>Action Smoothness</strong>: single step model might produce jerky movements</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: observation o t (snowy road through a windscreen) -> Policy π θ ( a t ∣ o t ) -> action a t (the road with a forked arrow), with an arrow from a t back to o t .] One Snapshot Can’t Model Full State Memory : what did I do 10 seconds ago? How fast are pedestrians moving? Action Smoothness : single step model might produce jerky movements", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-3-robotics-as-a-sequence-modeling-problem-frame-3", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Robotics as a Sequence Modeling Problem", "title": "Slide 3: Robotics as a Sequence Modeling Problem", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Robots usually operate in a POMDP, which require reasoning over history <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = (o_0, a_0, o_1, a_1, \\dots, o_T)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: token rows labelled <strong>Image</strong> (blue) and <strong>Action</strong> (purple) above a tabletop camera image and an “ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>” robot-arm picture.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\pi(\\tau) = \\rho(s_0) \\prod_{t=0}^{T-1} \\pi(a_t \\mid o_t)\\, P(o_{t+1} \\mid o_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(a_t \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Trajectory is a sequence, but the policy is single-step and reactive</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Robots usually operate in a POMDP, which require reasoning over history τ = ( o 0 , a 0 , o 1 , a 1 , … , o T ) [Figure: token rows labelled Image (blue) and Action (purple) above a tabletop camera image and an “ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ” robot-arm picture.] p π ( τ ) = ρ ( s 0 ) ∏ t = 0 T − 1 π ( a t ∣ o t ) P ( o t + 1 ∣ o t , a t ) [arrow at π ( a t ∣ o t ) ] Trajectory is a sequence, but the policy is single-step and reactive", "t": 121}, {"id": "slide-4-autoregressive-models-frame-4", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Autoregressive Models", "title": "Slide 4: Autoregressive Models", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Any joint distribution can be written as a product of conditionals (chain rule of probability)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x) = \\prod_{i=1}^{T} p_\\theta(x_i \\mid x_{1:i-1})</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the conditional]</span> How do we learn each conditional?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Any joint distribution can be written as a product of conditionals (chain rule of probability) p θ ( x ) = ∏ i = 1 T p θ ( x i ∣ x 1 : i − 1 ) [arrow at the conditional] How do we learn each conditional?", "t": 181}, {"id": "slide-5-modeling-sequences-with-rnns-frame-5", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Modeling Sequences with RNNs", "title": "Slide 5: Modeling Sequences with RNNs", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>SOTA until 2016 in NLP, process sequences sequentially</li>\n<li>Limitations:\n<ul>\n<li>Learn long-range dependencies, exploding/vanishing gradients</li>\n<li>Parallelize training due to recurrence</li>\n<li>Retain information, fixed-size <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> state compresses all history</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t = f(h_{t-1}, x_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive “RNN” panel, “O(n) - Steps to reach first word: 6”: hidden states <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_0 \\dots h_6</annotation></semantics></math> over the words “The robot picked up the spoon carefully”; “To reach ‘The’, information flows through 6 hidden states. For length n: O(n).”]</span></p>\n<p>References: <em>Long Short-Term Memory</em>, Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber (1997); <em>Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks</em>, Sutskever et al., (2014)</p>", "text": "SOTA until 2016 in NLP, process sequences sequentially Limitations: Learn long-range dependencies, exploding/vanishing gradients Parallelize training due to recurrence Retain information, fixed-size h t state compresses all history h t = f ( h t − 1 , x t ) [Figure: an interactive “RNN” panel, “O(n) - Steps to reach first word: 6”: hidden states h 0 … h 6 over the words “The robot picked up the spoon carefully”; “To reach ‘The’, information flows through 6 hidden states. For length n: O(n).”] References: Long Short-Term Memory , Hochreiter & Schmidhuber (1997); Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks , Sutskever et al., (2014)", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-6-transformer-frame-6", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Transformer", "title": "Slide 6: Transformer", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Replace recurrence with attention, giving direct <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, equal access to the entire history</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: interactive panel “Transformer vs RNN: path length to attend past words” over the tokens “The robot picked up the spoon (carefully)”. Transformer side, “O(1) - Any two tokens interact in a single step, regardless of distance”: “‘spoon’ reaches ‘The’ in 1 step - same cost regardless of distance”. RNN side, “O(n) - Steps to reach first word: 5”: “To reach ‘The’, information flows through 5 hidden states. For length n: O(n).”]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Attention Is All You Need</em>, Vaswani et al., (2017)</p>", "text": "Replace recurrence with attention, giving direct O ( 1 ) , equal access to the entire history [Figure: interactive panel “Transformer vs RNN: path length to attend past words” over the tokens “The robot picked up the spoon (carefully)”. Transformer side, “O(1) - Any two tokens interact in a single step, regardless of distance”: “‘spoon’ reaches ‘The’ in 1 step - same cost regardless of distance”. RNN side, “O(n) - Steps to reach first word: 5”: “To reach ‘The’, information flows through 5 hidden states. For length n: O(n).”] Reference: Attention Is All You Need , Vaswani et al., (2017)", "t": 361}, {"id": "slide-7-transformer-attention-mechanism-frames-7-26", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Transformer: Attention Mechanism", "title": "Slide 7: Transformer: Attention Mechanism", "frames": [7, 26], "html": "<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Attention</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compare current hidden state (query) to all past hidden states (keys)</li>\n<li>Construct attention distribution to figure out what parts of the history are relevant via a softmax</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive step-through on the sentence “The robot picked up the spoon”, with steps 1 - embed, 2 - project Q,K,V, 3 - raw scores, 4 - softmax, 5 - weighted sum. Step 2: “Q = eWQ, K = eWK, V = eWV. Every token creates three vectors via learned weight matrices: Query (what am I looking for?), Key (what do I advertise to others?), Value (what I will contribute if attended to).” The query is “spoon”; the other words get keys. Step 3: “score(q, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i</annotation></semantics></math>) = q · <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_i</annotation></semantics></math> / √d. The query vector for ‘spoon’ is dot-producted with every Key vector. A high score means that key matches what the query is looking for. These are raw unnormalized scores.” Raw scores (unnormalized - does not sum to 1): The 0.4, robot 2.2, picked 1.7, up 2.8, the 0.9, spoon 3.1. Step 4: “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">score</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">score</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_i = \\exp(\\text{score}_i) / \\sum_j \\exp(\\text{score}_j)</annotation></semantics></math>. Softmax normalizes the raw scores into probabilities summing to 1. High scores become dominant weights; low scores are suppressed. These are the final attention probabilities <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_i</annotation></semantics></math>.” Softmax weights (sum = 1.00): The 2.6%, robot 15.8%, picked 9.6%, up 28.8%, the 4.3%, spoon 38.9%. Step 5: “output = <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i \\alpha_i \\cdot v_i</annotation></semantics></math>. The output is a weighted blend of all Value vectors, weighted by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_i</annotation></semantics></math>. ‘spoon’ now carries a blended representation absorbing context from the entire sequence it attended to.” A bar shows output(“spoon”) as a mix of v(The) … v(spoon) in those proportions.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[The live demo cycles through steps 2-5 repeatedly across frames 7-26; all twenty frames show one of the states above.]</span></p>", "text": "Attention ( Q , K , V ) = softmax ( Q K T d k ) V Compare current hidden state (query) to all past hidden states (keys) Construct attention distribution to figure out what parts of the history are relevant via a softmax [Figure: an interactive step-through on the sentence “The robot picked up the spoon”, with steps 1 - embed, 2 - project Q,K,V, 3 - raw scores, 4 - softmax, 5 - weighted sum. Step 2: “Q = eWQ, K = eWK, V = eWV. Every token creates three vectors via learned weight matrices: Query (what am I looking for?), Key (what do I advertise to others?), Value (what I will contribute if attended to).” The query is “spoon”; the other words get keys. Step 3: “score(q, k i ) = q · k i / √d. The query vector for ‘spoon’ is dot-producted with every Key vector. A high score means that key matches what the query is looking for. These are raw unnormalized scores.” Raw scores (unnormalized - does not sum to 1): The 0.4, robot 2.2, picked 1.7, up 2.8, the 0.9, spoon 3.1. Step 4: “ α i = exp &#8289; ( score i ) / ∑ j exp &#8289; ( score j ) . Softmax normalizes the raw scores into probabilities summing to 1. High scores become dominant weights; low scores are suppressed. These are the final attention probabilities α i .” Softmax weights (sum = 1.00): The 2.6%, robot 15.8%, picked 9.6%, up 28.8%, the 4.3%, spoon 38.9%. Step 5: “output = ∑ i α i ⋅ v i . The output is a weighted blend of all Value vectors, weighted by α i . ‘spoon’ now carries a blended representation absorbing context from the entire sequence it attended to.” A bar shows output(“spoon”) as a mix of v(The) … v(spoon) in those proportions.] [The live demo cycles through steps 2-5 repeatedly across frames 7-26; all twenty frames show one of the states above.]", "t": 480}, {"id": "slide-8-positional-encodings-frame-27", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Positional Encodings", "title": "Slide 8: Positional Encodings", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Attention has no inherent notion of order</li>\n<li>Inject order information to tokens</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Absolute Positional Encoding</th>\n<th>Relative Positional Encoding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A fixed vector PE is added to each token embedding before the transformer sees it.</td>\n<td>A bias b(i-j) based on the distance between tokens is added to the attention score, not the embedding.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two worked examples on “The robot picked up the spoon”. Absolute: (1) token embeddings + (2) fixed position vectors PE(0) … PE(5) = (3) inputs to transformer e+PE(0) … e+PE(5); a red box “PE(6)? PE(7)? never seen during training”. Relative: (1) context tokens (keys), query = “spoon”; offset from query; (2) relative bias per token b(-5) … b(-1), added to attention score; (3) biased attention scores s+b(-5) … s+b(-1); a green box “s+b(-6), s+b(-7): offsets -6, -7 work fine ✓”.]</span></p>", "text": "Attention has no inherent notion of order Inject order information to tokens Absolute Positional Encoding Relative Positional Encoding A fixed vector PE is added to each token embedding before the transformer sees it. A bias b(i-j) based on the distance between tokens is added to the attention score, not the embedding. [Figure: two worked examples on “The robot picked up the spoon”. Absolute: (1) token embeddings + (2) fixed position vectors PE(0) … PE(5) = (3) inputs to transformer e+PE(0) … e+PE(5); a red box “PE(6)? PE(7)? never seen during training”. Relative: (1) context tokens (keys), query = “spoon”; offset from query; (2) relative bias per token b(-5) … b(-1), added to attention score; (3) biased attention scores s+b(-5) … s+b(-1); a green box “s+b(-6), s+b(-7): offsets -6, -7 work fine ✓”.]", "t": 542}, {"id": "slide-9-original-transformer-architecture-frame-28", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Original Transformer Architecture", "title": "Slide 9: Original Transformer Architecture", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the encoder-decoder transformer. <strong>Encoder</strong> (x N layers): Source embedding (“The robot picked…”) + PE -&gt; Multi-head self-attention (bidirectional - sees all tokens) -&gt; Add &amp; Norm -&gt; Feed-forward network (two linear layers + ReLU) -&gt; Add &amp; Norm -&gt; encoder output (K, V). <strong>Decoder</strong> (x N layers): Target embedding (“Il robot ha…” shifted right) + PE -&gt; Masked MH self-attention (causal - past output only) -&gt; Add &amp; Norm -&gt; Multi-head cross-attention (Q: decoder, K,V: encoder) -&gt; Add &amp; Norm -&gt; Feed-forward network -&gt; Add &amp; Norm -&gt; Linear + softmax (next token probability) -&gt; output prediction. A causal attention-mask matrix (query position 0-9 against key position 0-9) is filled on and below the diagonal: “can attend (score kept)”; above it “masked (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>∞</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty \\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> after softmax)”.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cross-attention</strong>: Same as self-attention, but Q comes from the decoder, K and V come from the encoder</li>\n<li><strong>Encoder - Bidirectional self-attention</strong>: Every token attends to every other token, past and future</li>\n<li><strong>Decoder - Causal self-attention</strong>: Each token only attends to itself and past tokens</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: the encoder-decoder transformer. Encoder (x N layers): Source embedding (“The robot picked…”) + PE -> Multi-head self-attention (bidirectional - sees all tokens) -> Add & Norm -> Feed-forward network (two linear layers + ReLU) -> Add & Norm -> encoder output (K, V). Decoder (x N layers): Target embedding (“Il robot ha…” shifted right) + PE -> Masked MH self-attention (causal - past output only) -> Add & Norm -> Multi-head cross-attention (Q: decoder, K,V: encoder) -> Add & Norm -> Feed-forward network -> Add & Norm -> Linear + softmax (next token probability) -> output prediction. A causal attention-mask matrix (query position 0-9 against key position 0-9) is filled on and below the diagonal: “can attend (score kept)”; above it “masked ( − ∞ → 0 after softmax)”.] Cross-attention : Same as self-attention, but Q comes from the decoder, K and V come from the encoder Encoder - Bidirectional self-attention : Every token attends to every other token, past and future Decoder - Causal self-attention : Each token only attends to itself and past tokens", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-10-training-frame-29", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Training", "title": "Slide 10: Training", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: teacher-forcing example. Input (ground truth, shifted right): ⟨s⟩, The, robot, picked, up -&gt; <strong>Decoder-only transformer + causal mask</strong> -&gt; predicted distribution (one step ahead) <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>…</mi><mover><mi>p</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{p}(x_1) \\dots \\hat{p}(x_5)</annotation></semantics></math> with p = 0.72, 0.61, 0.85, 0.54, 0.68 -&gt; target (ground truth): The, robot, picked, up, spoon. Per-token loss <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log p_\\theta(x_t \\mid x_1 \\dots x_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>: The 0.329, robot 0.494, picked 0.163, up 0.616, spoon 0.386; average 0.397 = L.]</span></p>\n<p><strong>Teacher Forcing</strong>: maximize the likelihood of the next correct token <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t</annotation></semantics></math> given the true preceding tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{1:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Cross-Entropy Loss</strong></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L = -\\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^{T} \\log p_\\theta(x_t \\mid x_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ 1x forward pass computes all T losses with Transformers</li>\n<li>❌ T forward passes with RNNs</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: teacher-forcing example. Input (ground truth, shifted right): ⟨s⟩, The, robot, picked, up -> Decoder-only transformer + causal mask -> predicted distribution (one step ahead) p ̂ ( x 1 ) … p ̂ ( x 5 ) with p = 0.72, 0.61, 0.85, 0.54, 0.68 -> target (ground truth): The, robot, picked, up, spoon. Per-token loss − log &#8289; p θ ( x t ∣ x 1 … x t − 1 ) : The 0.329, robot 0.494, picked 0.163, up 0.616, spoon 0.386; average 0.397 = L.] Teacher Forcing : maximize the likelihood of the next correct token x t given the true preceding tokens x 1 : t − 1 Cross-Entropy Loss L = − 1 T ∑ t = 1 T log &#8289; p θ ( x t ∣ x < t ) ✅ 1x forward pass computes all T losses with Transformers ❌ T forward passes with RNNs", "t": 720}, {"id": "slide-11-language-tokenization-frame-30", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Language Tokenization", "title": "Slide 11: Language Tokenization", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Characters</strong>: a=0, b=1, c=2,…\n<ul>\n<li>Small vocabulary</li>\n<li>Large number of tokens</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Words</strong>: cat=0, car=1, dog=2, …\n<ul>\n<li>Large vocabulary</li>\n<li>Small number of tokens</li>\n<li>What about new words?</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ideally we would like something in between</strong></p>", "text": "Characters : a=0, b=1, c=2,… Small vocabulary Large number of tokens Words : cat=0, car=1, dog=2, … Large vocabulary Small number of tokens What about new words? Ideally we would like something in between", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-12-byte-pair-encoding-frame-31", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Byte-Pair Encoding", "title": "Slide 12: Byte-Pair Encoding", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Tokenize based on groupings of characters, prioritizing by frequency</li>\n<li>Generalizes to novel combinations of characters</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>function BPE (strings C, number of merges k)\n  V &lt;- all unique characters in C                      # initial vocabulary is characters\n  for i = 1 to k do                                     # repeat k times\n    t_L, t_R &lt;- most frequent pair of adjacent tokens in C\n    t_NEW &lt;- t_L + t_R                                  # concatenate into new token\n    V &lt;- V + t_NEW                                      # add to vocabulary\n    replace each (t_L, t_R) in C with t_NEW             # update corpus\n  return vocab V</code></pre>\n<p>Reference: <em>Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units</em>, Sennrich et al., (2015)</p>", "text": "Tokenize based on groupings of characters, prioritizing by frequency Generalizes to novel combinations of characters function BPE (strings C, number of merges k) V <- all unique characters in C # initial vocabulary is characters for i = 1 to k do # repeat k times t_L, t_R <- most frequent pair of adjacent tokens in C t_NEW <- t_L + t_R # concatenate into new token V <- V + t_NEW # add to vocabulary replace each (t_L, t_R) in C with t_NEW # update corpus return vocab V Reference: Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units , Sennrich et al., (2015)", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-13-bpe-example-frames-32-36", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: BPE Example", "title": "Slide 13: BPE Example", "frames": [32, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>BPE compression factor scales with corpus size</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an interactive BPE demo on the input sentence “The robots melted the robot’s unbearably cheesy fondue”, stepping through 0 - start, 1 - “th”, 2 - “the”, 3 - “ro”, 4 - “rob”, 5 - “robo”, 6 - “robot”, 7 - result, 8 - summary. Step 0: “every character is its own token. ’_’ marks word boundaries. The sentence has 54 tokens - one per character including spaces”, with the top pair frequencies (‘h’+‘e’ 3x, ‘r’+‘o’ 3x, ‘t’+‘h’ 2x, …). Step 6: merge robo + t -&gt; robot (2x in corpus): “The full word is now a single token. Both ‘robots’ and ‘robot’s’ share this token - BPE naturally handles morphological variants”; vocabulary learned: th, the, ro, rob, robo, robot; token count 44 (-10 from start). Step 8, compression summary: characters (start) 54 tokens, after BPE merges 34 tokens; 37% fewer tokens; 0.63x compression ratio; 7 merge rules learned; key tokens: “the”, “robot” -&gt; 1 token, “unbearably” -&gt; 4 tokens, “fondue” -&gt; 4 tokens. “Real LLMs use 50k-100k vocab with millions of merges - common words become 1 token, rare domain words split into subwords.”]</span></p>", "text": "BPE compression factor scales with corpus size [Figure: an interactive BPE demo on the input sentence “The robots melted the robot’s unbearably cheesy fondue”, stepping through 0 - start, 1 - “th”, 2 - “the”, 3 - “ro”, 4 - “rob”, 5 - “robo”, 6 - “robot”, 7 - result, 8 - summary. Step 0: “every character is its own token. ’_’ marks word boundaries. The sentence has 54 tokens - one per character including spaces”, with the top pair frequencies (‘h’+‘e’ 3x, ‘r’+‘o’ 3x, ‘t’+‘h’ 2x, …). Step 6: merge robo + t -> robot (2x in corpus): “The full word is now a single token. Both ‘robots’ and ‘robot’s’ share this token - BPE naturally handles morphological variants”; vocabulary learned: th, the, ro, rob, robo, robot; token count 44 (-10 from start). Step 8, compression summary: characters (start) 54 tokens, after BPE merges 34 tokens; 37% fewer tokens; 0.63x compression ratio; 7 merge rules learned; key tokens: “the”, “robot” -> 1 token, “unbearably” -> 4 tokens, “fondue” -> 4 tokens. “Real LLMs use 50k-100k vocab with millions of merges - common words become 1 token, rare domain words split into subwords.”]", "t": 961}, {"id": "slide-14-large-language-models-frame-37", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Large Language Models", "title": "Slide 14: Large Language Models", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Decoder-only architectures -&gt; simpler to scale</li>\n<li>Extend BPE to work on byte-level -&gt; no unknown tokens</li>\n<li>Techniques for scaling:\n<ul>\n<li>Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE)\n<ul>\n<li>Rotates <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math> vectors by an angle proportional to their position, so that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q \\cdot K</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the relative distance between tokens</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>FlashAttention:\n<ul>\n<li>Attention with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math> memory instead of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math> by tiling the computation in fast SRAM, never materializing the full attention matrix</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>-&gt; RoPE + FlashAttention make training on longer sequences practical</p>", "text": "Decoder-only architectures -> simpler to scale Extend BPE to work on byte-level -> no unknown tokens Techniques for scaling: Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) Rotates Q and K vectors by an angle proportional to their position, so that Q ⋅ K depends only on the relative distance between tokens FlashAttention: Attention with O ( n ) memory instead of O ( n 2 ) by tiling the computation in fast SRAM, never materializing the full attention matrix -> RoPE + FlashAttention make training on longer sequences practical", "t": 1141}, {"id": "slide-15-scaling-llms-frame-38", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Scaling LLMs", "title": "Slide 15: Scaling LLMs", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three circles sized by model size.]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>GPT-1 (2018)</th>\n<th>GPT-2 (2019)</th>\n<th>GPT-3 (2020)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>117M params, 1B tokens</td>\n<td>1.5B params, 10B tokens</td>\n<td>175B params, 300B tokens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pretraining + fine-tuning</td>\n<td>Zero-shot generalization</td>\n<td>In-context learning emerges</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "[Figure: three circles sized by model size.] GPT-1 (2018) GPT-2 (2019) GPT-3 (2020) 117M params, 1B tokens 1.5B params, 10B tokens 175B params, 300B tokens Pretraining + fine-tuning Zero-shot generalization In-context learning emerges", "t": 1202}, {"id": "slide-16-the-bitter-lesson---sutton-2019-frame-39", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: The Bitter Lesson - Sutton (2019)", "title": "Slide 16: The Bitter Lesson - Sutton (2019)", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Methods that scale with compute ultimately beat hand-engineered solutions -&gt; In-context learning is an emergent property of scale</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a portrait of Richard Sutton, and the GPT-3 paper plot of Accuracy (%) against Number of Examples in Context (K), from zero-shot through one-shot to few-shot, for 175B, 13B and 1.3B parameter models, each with a “Natural Language Prompt” (solid) and “No Prompt” (dashed) curve. The 175B model climbs to about 65% accuracy; 13B reaches about 25%; 1.3B stays below about 5%.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Language Models are Few-Shot Learners</em>, Brown et al., OpenAI (2020)</p>", "text": "Methods that scale with compute ultimately beat hand-engineered solutions -> In-context learning is an emergent property of scale [Figure: a portrait of Richard Sutton, and the GPT-3 paper plot of Accuracy (%) against Number of Examples in Context (K), from zero-shot through one-shot to few-shot, for 175B, 13B and 1.3B parameter models, each with a “Natural Language Prompt” (solid) and “No Prompt” (dashed) curve. The 175B model climbs to about 65% accuracy; 13B reaches about 25%; 1.3B stays below about 5%.] Reference: Language Models are Few-Shot Learners , Brown et al., OpenAI (2020)", "t": 1260}, {"id": "slide-17-scaling-laws-frame-40", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Scaling Laws", "title": "Slide 17: Scaling Laws", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Given compute budget, scale data &amp; model size equally\n<ul>\n<li>Optimal model size <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^* \\propto C^{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, data <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^* \\propto C^{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, compute <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C \\approx 6 \\times N \\times D</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>~20 tokens per parameter for compute-optimality</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three log-scale plots against compute (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> FLOPs, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>17</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{17}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>24</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{24}</annotation></semantics></math>). (1) “training loss vs compute”: one curve per model size (75M, 125M, 250M, 500M, 1B, 2.5B, 5B, 10B), each flattening out, with their lower “envelope” highlighted. (2) “optimal model size <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^*</annotation></semantics></math> vs compute”: a dashed “optimal scaling frontier” line with GPT-1, GPT-2 and GPT-3 plotted near it (GPT-3 above it). (3) “optimal training tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^*</annotation></semantics></math> vs compute”: the same frontier, with GPT-3 well below it (too few tokens for its size).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models</em>, Hoffmann et al. (2022)</p>", "text": "Given compute budget, scale data & model size equally Optimal model size N * ∝ C 0.5 , data D * ∝ C 0.5 , compute C ≈ 6 × N × D ~20 tokens per parameter for compute-optimality [Figure: three log-scale plots against compute ( log &#8289; 10 FLOPs, 10 17 to 10 24 ). (1) “training loss vs compute”: one curve per model size (75M, 125M, 250M, 500M, 1B, 2.5B, 5B, 10B), each flattening out, with their lower “envelope” highlighted. (2) “optimal model size N * vs compute”: a dashed “optimal scaling frontier” line with GPT-1, GPT-2 and GPT-3 plotted near it (GPT-3 above it). (3) “optimal training tokens D * vs compute”: the same frontier, with GPT-3 well below it (too few tokens for its size).] Reference: Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models , Hoffmann et al. (2022)", "t": 1441}, {"id": "slide-18-scaling-laws-extrapolating-performance-frame-41", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Scaling Laws: Extrapolating Performance", "title": "Slide 18: Scaling Laws: Extrapolating Performance", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Fit power law on cheap runs -&gt; extrapolate to predict optimal N and final loss before committing compute</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two plots. Left, “isoFLOP curves - fit on small runs, predict large model performance”: validation loss against model size N for compute budgets <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>19</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 10^{19}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>21</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{21}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>23</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{23}</annotation></semantics></math>, each a U-shape with a marked minimum; a “power law fit (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^*</annotation></semantics></math> locus)” through the minima is extended (“extrapolation”) into a “never trained” region, with “Prediction at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>25</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 10^{25}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N^* \\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 282B params, loss <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 1.85”. Right, “tokens vs model size - compute-optimal to inference-optimal”: training tokens D against model size N with the “Chinchilla frontier (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^* = 20 \\times N^*</annotation></semantics></math>)”. GPT-1/2/3 lie below it, “undertrained” (GPT-3: “300B tokens, needs ~3.5T”); Chinchilla and LLaMA 1-2 sit on it, “compute-optimal”; LLaMA 3 (70B) sits far above it, “inference-optimal: deliberately over-trained”. The top right is shaded “extrapolation region”.]</span></p>", "text": "Fit power law on cheap runs -> extrapolate to predict optimal N and final loss before committing compute [Figure: two plots. Left, “isoFLOP curves - fit on small runs, predict large model performance”: validation loss against model size N for compute budgets C = 10 19 , 10 21 , 10 23 , each a U-shape with a marked minimum; a “power law fit ( N * locus)” through the minima is extended (“extrapolation”) into a “never trained” region, with “Prediction at C = 10 25 : N * ≈ 282B params, loss ≈ 1.85”. Right, “tokens vs model size - compute-optimal to inference-optimal”: training tokens D against model size N with the “Chinchilla frontier ( D * = 20 × N * )”. GPT-1/2/3 lie below it, “undertrained” (GPT-3: “300B tokens, needs ~3.5T”); Chinchilla and LLaMA 1-2 sit on it, “compute-optimal”; LLaMA 3 (70B) sits far above it, “inference-optimal: deliberately over-trained”. The top right is shaded “extrapolation region”.]", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-19-untitled-transition-question-frame-42", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 19: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<p><strong>How can we extend LLMs &amp; Transformers to Vision?</strong></p>", "text": "How can we extend LLMs & Transformers to Vision?", "t": 1560}, {"id": "slide-20-image-tokenization-frame-43", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: Image Tokenization", "title": "Slide 20: Image Tokenization", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Split image to patches, flatten &amp; add positional encodings</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: ViT patch pipeline. A 224x224 image is split into 14x14 = 196 patches (16x16 px each); one 16x16 patch = 768 values (16x16x3 RGB) is flattened (768-d), passed through a linear projection W (768 -&gt; 768 learned weights) to a patch embedding (d = 768 dims), plus a positional embedding that encodes patch position, giving a patch token (1 of 196 tokens) that goes into the transformer, “same as for text tokens”. Bottom: “x 196 patches -&gt; sequence of 197 tokens (196 patch + 1 [CLS])”: [CLS], <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>196</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_{196}</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>An Image is Worth 16x16 Words: Transformers for Image Recognition at Scale</em>, Dosovitskiy, Beyer et al. (2020)</p>", "text": "Split image to patches, flatten & add positional encodings [Figure: ViT patch pipeline. A 224x224 image is split into 14x14 = 196 patches (16x16 px each); one 16x16 patch = 768 values (16x16x3 RGB) is flattened (768-d), passed through a linear projection W (768 -> 768 learned weights) to a patch embedding (d = 768 dims), plus a positional embedding that encodes patch position, giving a patch token (1 of 196 tokens) that goes into the transformer, “same as for text tokens”. Bottom: “x 196 patches -> sequence of 197 tokens (196 patch + 1 [CLS])”: [CLS], p 1 , p 2 , … , p 196 .] Reference: An Image is Worth 16x16 Words: Transformers for Image Recognition at Scale , Dosovitskiy, Beyer et al. (2020)", "t": 1681}, {"id": "slide-21-untitled-transition-question-frame-44", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 21: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<p><strong>How does the Transformer know which text tokens correspond to which visual tokens?</strong></p>", "text": "How does the Transformer know which text tokens correspond to which visual tokens?", "t": 1740}, {"id": "slide-22-clip-two-tower-contrastive-alignment-frame-45", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: CLIP: Two-Tower Contrastive Alignment", "title": "Slide 22: CLIP: Two-Tower Contrastive Alignment", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: top, images 1 … N go through an <strong>image encoder</strong> (ViT) and an <strong>image proj.</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_i</annotation></semantics></math> to image embeddings; captions (“a dog running”, “green forest”, “city at night”, …) go through a <strong>text encoder</strong> (transformer) and a <strong>text proj.</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_t</annotation></semantics></math> to text embeddings; matching pairs are compared by cosine similarity. Bottom left, the CLIP paper figure: a text encoder (caption “Pepper the aussie pup”) and an image encoder fill an N x N matrix of products <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_i \\cdot T_j</annotation></semantics></math>, with the matching pairs on the diagonal highlighted.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">CLIP</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Image-to-Text Loss</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Text-to-Image Loss</mtext></munder><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CLIP}} = -\\frac{1}{2N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left[\\underbrace{\\log \\frac{e^{s_{ii}/\\tau}}{\\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{s_{ij}/\\tau}}}_{\\text{Image-to-Text Loss}} + \\underbrace{\\log \\frac{e^{s_{ii}/\\tau}}{\\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{s_{ji}/\\tau}}}_{\\text{Text-to-Image Loss}}\\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Learnable temperature, entropy of the resulting probability distribution</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision</em>, Radford et al. (2021)</p>", "text": "[Figure: top, images 1 … N go through an image encoder (ViT) and an image proj. W i to image embeddings; captions (“a dog running”, “green forest”, “city at night”, …) go through a text encoder (transformer) and a text proj. W t to text embeddings; matching pairs are compared by cosine similarity. Bottom left, the CLIP paper figure: a text encoder (caption “Pepper the aussie pup”) and an image encoder fill an N x N matrix of products I i ⋅ T j , with the matching pairs on the diagonal highlighted.] ℒ CLIP = − 1 2 N ∑ i = 1 N [ log &#8289; e s i i / τ ∑ j = 1 N e s i j / τ ⏟ Image-to-Text Loss + log &#8289; e s i i / τ ∑ j = 1 N e s j i / τ ⏟ Text-to-Image Loss ] [arrow at τ ] Learnable temperature, entropy of the resulting probability distribution Reference: Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision , Radford et al. (2021)", "t": 1861}, {"id": "slide-23-llava-early-fusion-frame-46", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Llava: Early-Fusion", "title": "Slide 23: Llava: Early-Fusion", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Prepends image tokens to the text sequence, LLM processes everything in single unified self-attention pass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: image input -&gt; <strong>CLIP vision encoder</strong> (always frozen; patch embeddings) -&gt; <strong>MLP connector</strong> (projection layer; trainable) -&gt; image tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_1, i_2, i_3, \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> (196), prepended to the text tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1 \\dots w_4</annotation></semantics></math> from the text prompt (“describe this”): a “unified token sequence into LLM”. <strong>LLM (LLaMA)</strong>: self-attention over all tokens jointly; standard autoregressive generation; no new attention mechanisms -&gt; text output “a cat sitting on a mat”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Visual Instruction Tuning</em>, Liu et al. (2023)</p>", "text": "Prepends image tokens to the text sequence, LLM processes everything in single unified self-attention pass [Figure: image input -> CLIP vision encoder (always frozen; patch embeddings) -> MLP connector (projection layer; trainable) -> image tokens i 1 , i 2 , i 3 , … (196), prepended to the text tokens w 1 … w 4 from the text prompt (“describe this”): a “unified token sequence into LLM”. LLM (LLaMA) : self-attention over all tokens jointly; standard autoregressive generation; no new attention mechanisms -> text output “a cat sitting on a mat”.] Reference: Visual Instruction Tuning , Liu et al. (2023)", "t": 1921}, {"id": "slide-24-flamingo-late-fusion-via-gated-cross-attention-frame-47", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Flamingo: Late-Fusion via Gated Cross-Attention", "title": "Slide 24: Flamingo: Late-Fusion via Gated Cross-Attention", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Vision is injected at every layer via cross-attention, the LM never sees image tokens directly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: image -&gt; <strong>vision encoder</strong> (frozen) -&gt; <strong>Perceiver resampler</strong> (compresses to fixed-size K, V) -&gt; visual features (K and V for x-attn). Text tokens only (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_1 \\dots w_5</annotation></semantics></math>) flow up a stack that alternates “frozen self-attention · Chinchilla LM” with “gated cross-attention: Q from text · K,V from vision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">x-attn</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\leftarrow x + \\tanh(\\alpha) \\cdot \\text{x-attn}(Q, K, V)</annotation></semantics></math>”, ending in text output “a cat sitting on a mat”. Side notes: “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> learned, init = 0: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tanh</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>←</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tanh(0) = 0 \\to x \\leftarrow x</annotation></semantics></math>, LM fully preserved”; “trained: cross-attention layers, Perceiver resampler, tanh gate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (one per layer)”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Flamingo: a Visual Language Model for Few-Shot Learning</em>, Alayrac et al. (2022)</p>", "text": "Vision is injected at every layer via cross-attention, the LM never sees image tokens directly [Figure: image -> vision encoder (frozen) -> Perceiver resampler (compresses to fixed-size K, V) -> visual features (K and V for x-attn). Text tokens only ( w 1 … w 5 ) flow up a stack that alternates “frozen self-attention · Chinchilla LM” with “gated cross-attention: Q from text · K,V from vision, x ← x + tanh &#8289; ( α ) ⋅ x-attn ( Q , K , V ) ”, ending in text output “a cat sitting on a mat”. Side notes: “ α learned, init = 0: tanh &#8289; ( 0 ) = 0 → x ← x , LM fully preserved”; “trained: cross-attention layers, Perceiver resampler, tanh gate α (one per layer)”.] Reference: Flamingo: a Visual Language Model for Few-Shot Learning , Alayrac et al. (2022)", "t": 1981}, {"id": "slide-25-native-multimodal-models-frame-48", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Native Multimodal Models", "title": "Slide 25: Native Multimodal Models", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Instead of adding vision to an existing LLM, train all modalities jointly from scratch</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “any combination of inputs”: image -&gt; vision enc., audio -&gt; audio enc., video -&gt; video enc., text -&gt; tokenizer (“each modality is optional”), merged into an “interleaved token sequence (any order, any mix)”. Examples: image caption task (img img img txt txt txt -&gt; “describe this image”); audio transcription (aud aud aud aud txt -&gt; “transcribe this”); video QA (vid txt vid txt vid txt, freely interleaved); multimodal reasoning (img txt aud img txt aud, any mix, any order). All go into a <strong>unified multimodal transformer</strong> (“trained from scratch - every weight has always seen every modality”) -&gt; text (+ image) output.]</span></p>", "text": "Instead of adding vision to an existing LLM, train all modalities jointly from scratch [Figure: “any combination of inputs”: image -> vision enc., audio -> audio enc., video -> video enc., text -> tokenizer (“each modality is optional”), merged into an “interleaved token sequence (any order, any mix)”. Examples: image caption task (img img img txt txt txt -> “describe this image”); audio transcription (aud aud aud aud txt -> “transcribe this”); video QA (vid txt vid txt vid txt, freely interleaved); multimodal reasoning (img txt aud img txt aud, any mix, any order). All go into a unified multimodal transformer (“trained from scratch - every weight has always seen every modality”) -> text (+ image) output.]", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-26-vlm-taxonomy-frame-49", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: VLM Taxonomy", "title": "Slide 26: VLM Taxonomy", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>early fusion: pre-trained LLM + vision adapter</th>\n<th>late fusion: pre-trained LLM + cross-attention</th>\n<th>natively multimodal: joint training from scratch</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>LLaVA · 2023 - CLIP ViT + MLP + LLaMA</td>\n<td>Flamingo · 2022 - gated cross-attn + Chinchilla LM</td>\n<td>Gemini 1 · 2023 - dense transformer · from scratch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PaliGemma 2 · 2024 - SigLIP + linear proj. + Gemma 2</td>\n<td>GPT-4V · 2023 - undisclosed · likely cross-attn</td>\n<td>Chameleon · 2024 - VQ-VAE: images as discrete tokens in shared vocabulary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>InternVL 2.5 · 2024 - InternViT-6B + MLP + LLM</td>\n<td>LLaMA 3.2 Vision · 2024 - cross-attention + LLaMA 3</td>\n<td>GPT-4o · 2024 - natively multimodal · undisclosed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DeepSeek-VL2 · Dec 2024 - SigLIP + MLP + DeepSeekMoE</td>\n<td>IDEFICS 2 · 2024 - open Flamingo reproduction</td>\n<td>Gemini 3 · 2025 - sparse MoE · 1M ctx · Deep Think</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gemma 3 · Mar 2025 - SigLIP + linear proj. + Gemma 3</td>\n<td>NVLM-X · NVIDIA 2024 - cross-attn + InternViT-6B</td>\n<td>LLaMA 4 · 2025 - MoE · 400B / 17B · 1M ctx</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phi-4-multimodal · Feb 2025 - SigLIP-2 + MoE-LoRA + Phi-4</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>GPT-5 · Aug 2025 - native MM · unified routing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qwen2.5-VL · 2025 - dyn-res ViT + MLP + Qwen2.5</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Qwen3.5 · Feb 2026 - joint pretraining · text + vision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qwen3-VL · 2025 - DeepStack + MRoPE + Qwen3</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mistral Large 3 · 2025 - sparse MoE 675B / 41B + vision</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "early fusion: pre-trained LLM + vision adapter late fusion: pre-trained LLM + cross-attention natively multimodal: joint training from scratch LLaVA · 2023 - CLIP ViT + MLP + LLaMA Flamingo · 2022 - gated cross-attn + Chinchilla LM Gemini 1 · 2023 - dense transformer · from scratch PaliGemma 2 · 2024 - SigLIP + linear proj. + Gemma 2 GPT-4V · 2023 - undisclosed · likely cross-attn Chameleon · 2024 - VQ-VAE: images as discrete tokens in shared vocabulary InternVL 2.5 · 2024 - InternViT-6B + MLP + LLM LLaMA 3.2 Vision · 2024 - cross-attention + LLaMA 3 GPT-4o · 2024 - natively multimodal · undisclosed DeepSeek-VL2 · Dec 2024 - SigLIP + MLP + DeepSeekMoE IDEFICS 2 · 2024 - open Flamingo reproduction Gemini 3 · 2025 - sparse MoE · 1M ctx · Deep Think Gemma 3 · Mar 2025 - SigLIP + linear proj. + Gemma 3 NVLM-X · NVIDIA 2024 - cross-attn + InternViT-6B LLaMA 4 · 2025 - MoE · 400B / 17B · 1M ctx Phi-4-multimodal · Feb 2025 - SigLIP-2 + MoE-LoRA + Phi-4 GPT-5 · Aug 2025 - native MM · unified routing Qwen2.5-VL · 2025 - dyn-res ViT + MLP + Qwen2.5 Qwen3.5 · Feb 2026 - joint pretraining · text + vision Qwen3-VL · 2025 - DeepStack + MRoPE + Qwen3 Mistral Large 3 · 2025 - sparse MoE 675B / 41B + vision", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-27-untitled-transition-question-frame-50", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 27: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<p><strong>Transformer → LLMs → VLMs → Native Multimodal models</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Can we extend it to robot actions?</strong></p>", "text": "Transformer → LLMs → VLMs → Native Multimodal models Can we extend it to robot actions?", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-28-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-51", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "title": "Slide 28: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "frames": [51, 51], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three groups of tokens, <strong>Language</strong> (green), <strong>Image</strong> (blue), <strong>Action</strong> (purple), above their sources: the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and “ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>” with a robot arm. Same figure as week 1, slide 33.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three groups of tokens, Language (green), Image (blue), Action (purple), above their sources: the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and “ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ” with a robot arm. Same figure as week 1, slide 33.]", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-29-robot-action-tokenization-for-vlas-frame-52", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Robot Action Tokenization for VLAs", "title": "Slide 29: Robot Action Tokenization for VLAs", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<p><strong>Per-Dimension, Per-Timestep Binning</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Quantile</strong> bounds prevent outliers from expanding the discretization range and wasting bin resolution</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>step 1 - quantile normalization per dimension <span class=\"own\">[Figure: histograms of one action dimension for robot A (small gripper), with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1 = -0.02</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>99</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{99} = +0.04</annotation></semantics></math>, and robot B (large arm), with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1 = -1.50</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>99</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>2.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{99} = +2.30</annotation></semantics></math>; values outside the quantiles are clipped, and both map to “normalised [-1, +1] - identical for every robot”.]</span></li>\n<li>step 2 - divide [-1, +1] uniformly into 256 bins · bin width = 2/256 ≈ 0.0078 <span class=\"own\">[bin 0 … 256 equal bins … bin 255]</span></li>\n<li>step 3 - N-dim action at timestep t -&gt; N discrete tokens <span class=\"own\">[Figure: action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^7</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; tokens 183, 107, 240, 018, 155, 072, 201 for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_1 \\dots a_7</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></li>\n<li>step 4 - inject action tokens into pre-trained language model vocabulary · train with next-token prediction <span class=\"own\">[existing vocabulary (kept) | 256 slots -&gt; action tokens [0…255]]</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p>References: <em>RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control</em>, Brohan et al. (2023); <em>OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model</em>, Kim et al. (2024)</p>", "text": "Per-Dimension, Per-Timestep Binning Quantile bounds prevent outliers from expanding the discretization range and wasting bin resolution step 1 - quantile normalization per dimension [Figure: histograms of one action dimension for robot A (small gripper), with Q 1 = − 0.02 and Q 99 = + 0.04 , and robot B (large arm), with Q 1 = − 1.50 and Q 99 = + 2.30 ; values outside the quantiles are clipped, and both map to “normalised [-1, +1] - identical for every robot”.] step 2 - divide [-1, +1] uniformly into 256 bins · bin width = 2/256 ≈ 0.0078 [bin 0 … 256 equal bins … bin 255] step 3 - N-dim action at timestep t -> N discrete tokens [Figure: action a t ∈ ℝ 7 -> tokens 183, 107, 240, 018, 155, 072, 201 for a 1 … a 7 .] step 4 - inject action tokens into pre-trained language model vocabulary · train with next-token prediction [existing vocabulary (kept) | 256 slots -> action tokens [0…255]] References: RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control , Brohan et al. (2023); OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model , Kim et al. (2024)", "t": 2402}, {"id": "slide-30-action-chunking-frame-53", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: Action Chunking", "title": "Slide 30: Action Chunking", "frames": [53, 53], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Predict k future actions for smoother behavior <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(a_{t:t+k} \\mid o_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two timelines. “action chunking - predict K actions at once, execute all K before re-querying the model”: query 1 returns <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_0 \\dots a_3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>…</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t \\dots t+3</annotation></semantics></math> (chunk 1 - execute all 4), then re-query, and query 2 returns <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_4 \\dots a_7</annotation></semantics></math> (chunk 2 - execute all 4). “action chunking + temporal ensemble - re-query every step · <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_i = \\exp(-m \\cdot i)</annotation></semantics></math> · i = 0 is oldest prediction”: queries at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t, t+1, t+2, t+3</annotation></semantics></math> each predict 4 actions, so 4 overlapping predictions exist for step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t+3</annotation></semantics></math>, combined as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{a}_3 = \\sum w_i \\cdot a_i / \\sum w_i</annotation></semantics></math>. A plot of the weight <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>0.01</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_i = e^{-0.01 i}</annotation></semantics></math> against i (age of prediction) from 0 (oldest, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_0 = 1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, highest weight) to 50 (newest, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>50</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.61</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{50} \\approx 0.61</annotation></semantics></math>, lowest weight).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware</em>, Zhao et al. (2023)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: by the slide’s own definition (i = 0 is the oldest prediction) the oldest prediction gets the highest weight; the plot labels agree. This matches the ACT paper, but reads the opposite way from the usual intuition that newer predictions should count more.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32.]</span></p>", "text": "Predict k future actions for smoother behavior π ( a t : t + k ∣ o t ) [Figure: two timelines. “action chunking - predict K actions at once, execute all K before re-querying the model”: query 1 returns a 0 … a 3 for t … t + 3 (chunk 1 - execute all 4), then re-query, and query 2 returns a 4 … a 7 (chunk 2 - execute all 4). “action chunking + temporal ensemble - re-query every step · w i = exp &#8289; ( − m ⋅ i ) · i = 0 is oldest prediction”: queries at t , t + 1 , t + 2 , t + 3 each predict 4 actions, so 4 overlapping predictions exist for step t + 3 , combined as a ̂ 3 = ∑ w i ⋅ a i / ∑ w i . A plot of the weight w i = e − 0.01 i against i (age of prediction) from 0 (oldest, w 0 = 1.0 , highest weight) to 50 (newest, w 50 ≈ 0.61 , lowest weight).] Reference: Learning Fine-Grained Bimanual Manipulation with Low-Cost Hardware , Zhao et al. (2023) [Note: by the slide’s own definition (i = 0 is the oldest prediction) the oldest prediction gets the highest weight; the plot labels agree. This matches the ACT paper, but reads the opposite way from the usual intuition that newer predictions should count more.] [Note: slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32.]", "t": 2700}, {"id": "slide-32-naïve-action-tokenization-frame-54", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Naïve Action Tokenization", "title": "Slide 32: Naïve Action Tokenization", "frames": [54, 54], "html": "<p>As frequency increases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Information per token decreases</li>\n<li>Correlation between token increases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>-&gt; Solution: <strong>compression</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: plot of task success score (-1 to 5.5) against data control frequency (2, 5, 10, 20 Hz) for “Naïve Binning Tokenization”: about 1.1 at 2 Hz, a peak of about 3.6 at 5 Hz, then 0.5 at 10 Hz and about -0.1 at 20 Hz, with error bars. Right: three tokens entering an <strong>Autoregressive Transformer</strong>, with an arrow showing the next token predicted from the previous ones.]</span></p>", "text": "As frequency increases: Information per token decreases Correlation between token increases -> Solution: compression [Figure: plot of task success score (-1 to 5.5) against data control frequency (2, 5, 10, 20 Hz) for “Naïve Binning Tokenization”: about 1.1 at 2 Hz, a peak of about 3.6 at 5 Hz, then 0.5 at 10 Hz and about -0.1 at 20 Hz, with error bars. Right: three tokens entering an Autoregressive Transformer , with an arrow showing the next token predicted from the previous ones.]", "t": 2822}, {"id": "slide-33-effective-action-tokenization-frame-55", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Effective Action Tokenization", "title": "Slide 33: Effective Action Tokenization", "frames": [55, 55], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How to compress continuous robot data?\n<ul>\n<li>Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) not suited for continuous data</li>\n<li>VQ learned compression complex to train</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Key idea: <strong>Discrete Cosine Transform</strong> (DCT) ≈ JPEG compression</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: five-step pipeline. (1) Normalized action chunk (first 2 dimensions displayed) -&gt; Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) -&gt; (2) Frequency components -&gt; Quantize -&gt; (3) Sparse frequency matrix (each dim = 1 row), mostly zeros, e.g. rows 124 12 -3 0 0 0 12 / -86 0 0 0 0 0 0 / 344 3 1 0 0 1 5 / … -&gt; Flatten -&gt; (4) Low-frequency components first: 124, -86, 344, -45, 178, 12, 0, 3, 0, 15, … -&gt; Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) -&gt; (5) Compressed action tokens: 978, 233, 19, 1022, 1.]</span></p>", "text": "How to compress continuous robot data? Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) not suited for continuous data VQ learned compression complex to train Key idea: Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) ≈ JPEG compression [Figure: five-step pipeline. (1) Normalized action chunk (first 2 dimensions displayed) -> Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) -> (2) Frequency components -> Quantize -> (3) Sparse frequency matrix (each dim = 1 row), mostly zeros, e.g. rows 124 12 -3 0 0 0 12 / -86 0 0 0 0 0 0 / 344 3 1 0 0 1 5 / … -> Flatten -> (4) Low-frequency components first: 124, -86, 344, -45, 178, 12, 0, 3, 0, 15, … -> Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) -> (5) Compressed action tokens: 978, 233, 19, 1022, 1.]", "t": 2880}, {"id": "slide-34-fast-efficient-action-tokenizer-for-vlas-frame-56", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenizer for VLAs", "title": "Slide 34: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenizer for VLAs", "frames": [56, 56], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Scales to higher frequencies and trains 5x faster</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, score against data control frequency (2-20 Hz): FAST rises from about 3 at 2 Hz to about 7 at 20 Hz, while Naïve Binning Tokenization peaks at about 3.5 at 5 Hz and falls to about 0 at 20 Hz. Right, evaluation score against train iteration (0-700k): “<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math> + FAST (ours)” reaches about 79 by 100k iterations; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math> reaches about 76 only at 700k, marked “5x faster VLA Training”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for Vision-Language-Action Models</em>, Pertsch*, Stachowicz*, Ichter, Driess, Nair, Vuong, <strong>Mees</strong> et al. RSS 2025. <strong>Finalist Best Conference Paper Award</strong></p>", "text": "Scales to higher frequencies and trains 5x faster [Figure: left, score against data control frequency (2-20 Hz): FAST rises from about 3 at 2 Hz to about 7 at 20 Hz, while Naïve Binning Tokenization peaks at about 3.5 at 5 Hz and falls to about 0 at 20 Hz. Right, evaluation score against train iteration (0-700k): “ π 0 + FAST (ours)” reaches about 79 by 100k iterations; π 0 reaches about 76 only at 700k, marked “5x faster VLA Training”.] Reference: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for Vision-Language-Action Models , Pertsch*, Stachowicz*, Ichter, Driess, Nair, Vuong, Mees et al. RSS 2025. Finalist Best Conference Paper Award", "t": 2880}, {"id": "slide-35-fast-efficient-action-tokenizer-for-vlas-results-frame-57", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenizer for VLAs <span class=\"own\">[results]</span>", "title": "Slide 35: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenizer for VLAs [results]", "frames": [57, 57], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Two arrows from the title:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Generality @ Berkeley, Stanford, UW</th>\n<th>Dexterity @ Physical Intelligence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a 3x3 grid of autonomous robot-arm rollouts in kitchens (4x speed)]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: dexterous tasks: a robot arm over a table of dishes, laundry folding, a bussing station (8x speed)]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Reference: <em>FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for Vision-Language-Action Models</em>, Pertsch*, Stachowicz*, Ichter, Driess, Nair, Vuong, <strong>Mees</strong> et al. RSS 2025. <strong>Finalist Best Conference Paper Award</strong></p>", "text": "[Two arrows from the title:] Generality @ Berkeley, Stanford, UW Dexterity @ Physical Intelligence [Figure: a 3x3 grid of autonomous robot-arm rollouts in kitchens (4x speed)] [Figure: dexterous tasks: a robot arm over a table of dishes, laundry folding, a bussing station (8x speed)] Reference: FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for Vision-Language-Action Models , Pertsch*, Stachowicz*, Ichter, Driess, Nair, Vuong, Mees et al. RSS 2025. Finalist Best Conference Paper Award", "t": 3121}, {"id": "slide-36-conclusion-frame-58", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Conclusion", "title": "Slide 36: Conclusion", "frames": [58, 58], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Robotics is a sequence modeling problem</li>\n<li>Transformers as a scalable architecture → LLMs</li>\n<li>Different architectures for multimodal inputs → VLMs, Native Multimodal models</li>\n<li>Naïve robot tokenization breaks at high frequency control</li>\n<li>Compression-based techniques like FAST popular</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Robotics is a sequence modeling problem Transformers as a scalable architecture → LLMs Different architectures for multimodal inputs → VLMs, Native Multimodal models Naïve robot tokenization breaks at high frequency control Compression-based techniques like FAST popular", "t": 3240}, {"id": "end-frame-59", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [59, 59], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "title-frame-1", "label": "Title", "html": "slide 1 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 2."}, {"slide": "slide-30-action-chunking-frame-53", "label": "Slide 30: Action Chunking", "html": "by the slide’s own definition (i = 0 is the oldest prediction) the oldest prediction gets the highest weight; the plot labels agree. This matches the ACT paper, but reads the opposite way from the usual intuition that newer predictions should count more."}, {"slide": "slide-30-action-chunking-frame-53", "label": "Slide 30: Action Chunking", "html": "slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 30 to 32."}]}, {"key": "08", "dir": "week08_world_models", "date": "Mon 13 Apr 2026", "topic": "World Models", "summary": "Starts with mid-term feedback and the five final-project briefs. Then world models P(s' | s, a, h): pixel-space models with visual MPC, latent models (Ha and Schmidhuber's V-M-C, the RSSM of PlaNet, Dreamer V1-V4), video tokenization and compression, video backbones for policies (mimic-video, DreamZero) and JEPA, which predicts in latent space with no decoder at all.", "key_ideas": ["A world model is a learned simulator: actions in, future states out", "RSSM keeps a deterministic memory h_t plus a stochastic z_t, and learns a prior so imagination does not drift", "Dreamer trains the policy entirely in imagination by backprop through the frozen model", "Video models already know physics from internet video, so policies on top need far less robot data", "JEPA avoids pixel prediction; the risk is collapse, prevented by EMA targets, frozen encoders or Gaussian regularization"], "papers": [{"title": "Learning Universal Policies via Text-Guided Video...", "who": "Du et al., 2023", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.00111"}, {"title": "Training Agents Inside Scalable World Models", "who": "Hafner et al., 2025", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.24527"}, {"title": "World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies", "who": "Ye et al., 2026", "url": "https://dreamzero0.github.io/DreamZero.pdf"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "cTTmUZlOF2s", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 8: World Models | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "1:11:48", "description": "Lecture 8 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Welcome everybody. Today we are going to talk about world models. Very exciting topic. But before that I wanted to give a quick recap from the midterm feedback that we received. Very excited for all of you um that it seems that things that were well received were like the main lectures and especially the the guest lectures. And I also put like some um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; some comments from the feedback that were kind of like repeated in in different words mostly through through the different feedback forms. But yeah, I think in general it seemed that yeah, people criticized a little bit that the paper presentations on Monday were a bit rushed and sometimes difficult to follow and so forth. And that people also would"}, {"t": 62, "text": "appreciate having a break. So it's not like you know, two hours straight off of lecture. So we will try to improve this. Oh yeah, and some people also said that more credits would be deserved for the workload. I originally requested more credits from ETH but yeah, that's the university didn't feel like more credits were deserved. So that's not my choice. But yeah, we will try to improve things because we agree with a lot of the feedback that you gave gave. And so we will move one additional paper presentation from the Monday slot to Thursdays in order to create a bit more time. Because also a lot of people said that they also wanted to have a little bit more uh time devoted to the main lectures. &gt;&gt; [clears throat]"}, {"t": 120, "text": "&gt;&gt; And so, we will try to achieve this starting today. Uh so, um I will either talk a bit longer or the same as before, I'm not sure. Let's see how it goes. Um and uh we'll try to make a break in between um like uh after the main lecture uh before starting the uh first paper. And um then with the guest lecture from today. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Before we start, I'll also briefly touch upon the project. Uh cuz that's [clears throat] um kind of now the very exciting things that we will start with uh starting this um this this next weeks, right? The group projects. Uh we already uh all of you um um participated [clears throat] in this amazing uh robot assembly exercise 2 weeks ago. And now we want to like um take all the things we've been learning so far in the course uh and train some policies for for these"}, {"t": 180, "text": "robots, right? Um we will have uh five projects and competitions. Um I will briefly uh touch upon them. Um but they're uh also like the TAs will provide also more uh details on the project. Um the key dates uh or the key information that um is important for you to know is &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh we will have a demo day on the main uh building on the 21st of May. Uh and for each of these five projects, um like there will be some like evaluation criteria on how points are uh will be assigned for each team. Um and then for each of the five main projects, uh the best team will um have the chance to do the demo of their project a week later on the um"}, {"t": 241, "text": "29th of May. Uh to also uh uh selected uh Robot Learning VIPs that will be visiting ETH on that day. Um cuz that's like a a workshop that also Mark and others are organizing in Switzerland uh on that week. And uh they will be inviting a lot of these people like Jan LeCun, Sharan, Jitendra, and so forth uh to ETH. Uh I can't promise that all of them, you know, will come or be there to see like your demo, um but the chances are very high. Um we're trying to uh trying to make that work. Um so that also would be a very nice reward. Um and finally uh I'm um very excited to announce that uh we got some support from uh Nvidia in terms of compute uh to support um all of your uh group projects. Um and so we have around um"}, {"t": 303, "text": "12,500 hours of H100 compute. Um and I think it's a um around 277 uh H100 uh compute hours uh per team. Um and um yeah, we will uh also give more explanation uh shortly on how you can access um the compute um uh with all the details and so forth. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; All right. Um so we will have we will we are trying to make kind of like one project per main paradigm that we are covering in in the lecture, right? Um and so the first project will be centered around training VLA models. And So, here, well, this is a little of a little bit verbose, but essentially,"}, {"t": 361, "text": "like the evaluation criteria, as you can see, start with being able to do kind of like pick and place simple like for one object, like let's say a plastic banana. Then, we will have kind of like a combination that's more difficult where the policy has to do some reasoning to understand the task instruction. Like put the banana into the 2 - 1 bowl left from the left or something along those lines. Right? So, that you actually also need to have some of the VLM understanding going on there. Yeah, then for the third criteria, we will do a version of the famous move Coca near Taylor Swift task from the RT-2 paper. But, we won't tell you what images your policy will see beforehand. So, that will be a lot of fun deciding"}, {"t": 420, "text": "the images, I think. And yes, and then there will be a bonus for efficiency. So, for the smallest model. Yeah. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; So, that's the first project. The second part The second project will be revolving around the world models. And here, we will be basically pushing objects. Cuz that's like a great way to kind of like learn dynamics of like how objects move when you apply certain actions to them, right? And so, yeah. In principle, the first task will be to push an object kind of in a straight line. This will be small objects cuz like the like SO-101s don't have like a huge range. So, we hope we can make that work in a meaningful manner. So, it will be a little bit of a miniature"}, {"t": 481, "text": "object. Um, then we will have a harder version where there will be an obstacle in the middle. So, you have to like kind of navigate around the obstacle to push your object to the goal. Um, and then there will be a harder task where you have to push an unknown object. And the bonus will be for the policy that is most efficient in terms of number of pushing steps that it takes to to push to the goal. Um, then there will be a third project more focused on &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; reinforcement learning. Um, and here we are taking inspiration from the singulation task that we also covered &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; in one of the papers we presented. Um, where essentially you start by learning a pick and place policy that picks up one wooden block and"}, {"t": 541, "text": "places them in one of the bowls laid out in front of the arm. And this could also be for example with BC. It doesn't have to be RL. Um, but like you don't have to somehow like bootstrap like your your policy. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and then you start like learning how to singulate like four blocks that are together. Um, and then also a more difficult task will be singulating kind of a more blocks than four kind of similar to how you see um, in in the video from from the paper. Uh, and then the bonus will be again for uh, the fastest single ating policy. The fourth project will um, revolve around keyboard typing. Um, this will be probably inspired a little bit more towards approaches like coders policies"}, {"t": 602, "text": "where you can um, use uh, like LLMs for prompting and you know, maybe use some uh, predefined motion primitives and so on. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and um, yeah. So, you will start by kind of learning how to move like your your arm to uh, four predefined points on a sheet of paper. Um, then you will move on to learning how to define how to press some defined keys on a laptop keyboard. Um, obviously like you will have to attach something to the gripper so you can actually press it, but it shouldn't be too difficult. Um, and then um, more difficult tasks will be typing words on an unseen keyboard. Uh, that's not like yours. Um, and um, but still probably like laptop like because it's like too big. The range is is not that"}, {"t": 660, "text": "large. And then bonus also for the um, fastest spelling of the words. And finally, of course, we had to have our cloth folding task too. Um, probably like um, you will want to get inspired by like the diffusion policies we've been covering um, um, in the lecture. Um, so here the milestones will be starting with like grabbing an edge, uh, making one fold, and then like subsequent folds. Um, and the bonus will be for um folding the full clothes and also for the fastest policy. And um those are the five projects we have. Um I believe Alexey will send an email with information on how to sign up for each of the projects. Is it right? And you will send it today. Okay, perfect. So you will uh you will receive information on how"}, {"t": 721, "text": "to sign up for the uh projects uh later today uh and also uh regarding the the compute you you should also find um uh receive information uh shortly. All right. So um let's talk about world models. And uh I want to start by kind of giving like uh an intuition um uh like an example of what they are. Um maybe you have um kind of heard this uh example before, but just to recap on like uh what world models are or why we are so interested in them is um you can see this example from baseball, right? Where in baseball you have like a fastball um that will arrive um to like the plate in let's say around 400 milliseconds."}, {"t": 780, "text": "And the uh visual signal that arrives uh from your eyes uh to your brain takes around 100 milliseconds. And so that leaves only like 300 milliseconds to like process all the scene, uh decide like on your swing, and execute it. Um and like in principle it's like not enough time to um decide uh how to act, right? Um uh but like batters like hit, you know, around [snorts] 160 km/h balls in baseball. Um and like how can they do this, right? Like how can they react so fast? And essentially this is because like we have this internal world model. Um where kind of or in subconsciously we predict where the ball is going to go. Uh and then we initiate a swing or the baseballer is initiate a swing based on that prediction. Uh not based on just the perception. If that makes sense,"}, {"t": 841, "text": "right? If it if it would be only on perception, they would not be able to hit this fast like the this fast fastballs. Um and so with a good world model um uh what you can do is essentially you can uh act ahead of the information that uh arrives uh from like the visual input. Uh and that is a concept that we are interested into bringing into machine learning. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; because if a an agent has a good world model, uh then it can simulate the consequences of its actions, uh plan ahead, and kind of behave more efficiently, right? Um so what distinguishes um a world model from a policy, right? Um a policy or or a VLA for example um the real we talked about um in the last lecture, um essentially asks a question like given my current"}, {"t": 900, "text": "state uh and my goal, what is like the action I should be taking, right? So it it maps the the state and goal directly to a sequence of actions. Um and the world model asks a different question. It asks um given my current state and an action, what will most likely happen next. Um so it essentially models the transition dynamics uh like outputting a distribution over the um next states uh P of ST + 1 given ST and AT. Um, and so this matters a lot because um like the regular policies or VLAs in principle are therefore a little bit like more blind to physical causality um because um it doesn't have necessarily an internal representation of why a certain action leads to a good outcome um or what the physical consequences of any uh given action would be. Right? It"}, {"t": 962, "text": "doesn't necessarily have this temporal uh model of the world. Um So why do we actually want world models in robotics? Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh kind of I already have said it before. Uh if you have learned this model of dynamics, um then you can use it to imagine the future. Um so instead of immediately committing to an action, uh you can run let's say like a number of rollouts uh through your world model in your head, um kind of score each one, and then only execute the best the best plan in the real world. Um and this is uh essentially uh very powerful because you can explore many hypothetical uh trajectories for the cost of a single real one in the uh the real world. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um Another way to think about this um is cuz like you can be like okay, this sounds a lot like a simulator, right?"}, {"t": 1020, "text": "Like uh you can take your MuJoCo or um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh whatever simulator, and uh why not use that? And so essentially the world model, you can think of it an analogy is to this a learned simulator, right? It's a data-driven um like alternative to, uh, hand-engineered physics engines uh, that we uh, have been using traditionally, right? Um, like a like a simulator with physics is usually, um, built by experts. Uh, it has to obey all the physical laws, rigid object dynamics, uh, uh, friction, collision geometry, and so forth. Uh, and it's like deterministic and fast, um, but only, um, it's accurate to the things that have been modeled explicitly there, right? Uh, and anything that's has not been modeled in the in the simulator, I don't know, maybe it's like, uh, deformable objects or, uh, biological tissue, um, then it's not going to like work,"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "right? It's sometimes difficult to model everything accurately, cuz we already seen in the lecture, there are like, uh, some things that are just like too difficult to model. Right? Um, and the world model uh, flips this. So, instead of hand-coding the physics, um, you kind of like learn a transition distribution from real interaction data, right? So, any data you can find on robot trajectories, uh, YouTube videos, etc. Um, like your world model will learn whatever, uh, dynamics are present in that data, uh, without you having or anyone having to explicitly, um, specify them. And so, in principle, therefore, it generalizes to anything that, you know, you could capture, let's say, on on video, for example. Um, um, but I also you are bound by the data that, uh, you have trained it on, right? So, uh, it might be the case that you need like lots and lots of data, uh, to learn, for example, physics,"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "right? Which for some things we already have closed forms for. So, that's kind of the trade-off. Um, I want to also give a kind of a um, a definition about this cuz like nowadays the term world model is kind of very hyped and everybody calls their approach to be a world model. Um in principle, as I said before, um it's uh it models a distribution over the next states conditioned um on the current state, the action taken, and the memory of the past. Um and um the memory isn't uh just like necessary for the partial observability, uh but also it gives you the for example the uh the temporal sorry, the context needed for uh like motion, right? Like if you had like just a single frame, uh it can tell you like where things are, but um the history tells you like where where they're going, right? Um and um yeah, so essentially uh I think uh important thing is that it it"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "in general it needs to be action conditioned, right? Uh like if you just have a video model um that generates uh video from from text, then strictly speaking this would not be a a world model. Um and so um that's like what makes it useful for planning and control um that, you know, your agent needs to be able to query uh if it if it if I take a give a certain action in this state, like what state will I end up with? &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um Okay, so let's look at an early uh concrete example of action conditioned world model uh working directly in pixel space. Um this is work from Chelsea Finn et al. from 2016. Um and here the uh key idea is that uh in instead of trying to predict the the full frame uh from scratch, which is a very hard"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "generative uh prop modeling problem, um we predict the flow uh field that wraps the uh current image uh the current image into the next one. Um so, the model learns um given the current um image and the action I'm I'm about to take, how will uh each pixel move? Um and so, um architecturally, it has like some convolutional LSTM um and like like the architectures that were kind of popular at the time. Um And um the important thing here is to know that um by using this flow-based prediction rather than uh predicting full uh pixels, um essentially, it makes it like more much more tractable to learn because it's a much easier problem to just model motion uh instead of like predicting like consistently like uh visual like uh"}, {"t": 1323, "text": "pixel objects, right? So, it's much more uh structured and like less likely to to hallucinate, for example. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um How how do you use this for robotics, actually? So, this is um the follow-up work called visual foresight. And um the setup uh is visual MPC, model predictive control, uh operating entirely in pixel space. Um so, here the user can specify goal uh in different ways, like um clicking on a certain pixel and kind of telling them the policy, I want to move this object from this pixel to that pixel. Uh or using a image classifier. Um and essentially, the planner then uses the cross-entropy method that we already saw some lectures ago uh to iteratively then sample and refine uh the action sequences. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um and here um"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "one notable thing is that all the training data is uh was collected autonomously. So, this is just like kind of um more or less random robot interaction. Um um and then um because also the model uh learns flow, um they also show that it generalizes better to unseen objects and so on because it's easier to generalize motion instead of like uh generalizing like object-specific uh tasks. Um this slide um summarizes this kind of full pixel action condition world model framework and its tradeoffs. Um as I fore-mentioned, the benefits are that it's uh fully self-supervised. Uh it doesn't require human labeling. Um and the motion objective uh helps with the generalization to unseen objects because it's object-agnostic by approach. Um but there are some uh limitations to working directly in pixel space. Um so,"}, {"t": 1442, "text": "for example here, they were using the L2 loss um which leads to blurry predictions video predictions uh because the model will uh average uh over uncertainty rather than uh committing to a sharp uh outcome as we also covered in previous lectures. Um and then if you roll this over uh like extended temporal horizon, like this will um drift away and the errors accumulate. Uh and then of course um so, the model learns uh um flow, but then the visual MPC like operates in pixel space, right? And so, um um evaluating the full image sequences uh for every candidate actions uh at test time is is computationally quite expensive. So, because of that, right? Um there is this kind of like other paradigm that asks, what if instead of doing this planning"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "with um, like uh, cross entropy method in pixel space, uh, what if we compress those observations into a compact latent latent representation, um, and then did all the dynamics and planning in the latent space? Right? The And the intuition is that most of the information, um, uh, in a raw image is like not necessarily relevant for control, right? Like there's like a lot of like, let's say, I don't know, background pixels that maybe like it's not really necessary to model or be able to reconstruct to, uh, take optimal, um, decisions to for manipulation, for example. Um, so here is the general structure of how latent action condition world models work. Um, so the obser- it's pretty simple. The observations get encoded into, uh, compact latent code. Um, the dynamic model operates in that, uh, entirely in that latent space. Uh,"}, {"t": 1562, "text": "and then it maintains a recurrent, um, hidden state, uh, HT for the memory, um, predicts the next latent state, uh, and also predicts an imagined reward. And then the policy takes, um, the, uh, like the memory and the, uh, latent as an input and then outputs the actions. Um, and crucially it does it all in in in this latent space without, um, doing like, uh, interactions in the real environment. Um, and so, yeah, once you have a good world model, you can then train the policy purely in imagination, uh, which is, of course, cheaper than collecting lots of, uh, real-world, uh, interaction. So this is the original or OG uh, latent action condition world model paper. Though, if you want to debate who exactly invented what,"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "you can ask Schmidhuber directly. The architecture has three components, a memory RNN M that takes the latent and integrates it integrates it over time. And then it has a small controller. Oh, sorry, it has a vision model that encodes the observations with a VAE. And then it has the controller C that takes both the latent and the H memory as an input. And outputs the actions. And so it's like three kind of the the the vision, the memory, and the controller kind of a separate modules. So, one handles the perception, the other the the temporal prediction, and the last one the control. &gt;&gt; [snorts] [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Yeah, as I said, the the vision model is just a VAE. It's the variation autoencoder that we already saw in the lecture."}, {"t": 1683, "text": "The reconstruction on the right looks reasonable. It's not perfect, but it kind of captures the structure of the scene. And in general, like for these approaches, we don't necessarily need like for these latent approaches, we don't necessarily need pixel-perfect reconstructions. We just need that the kind of latent variable Z contains enough information for the controller to act on. That's kind of the important bit. And for the memory model, they use an RNN with mixture density network. And this is uh kind of important because um, this way with the the kind of the mixture of Gaussians they can handle like multi-modality um, in [snorts] uh, in many environments cuz you might have like many uh, plausible next states depending on the"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "stochastic elements in your in your environment. Um, and as we al- also saw before a single Gaussian would like um, average them out. Um, and so essentially the the job of the memory model is um, uh, two things to track what um, uh, has been happening in the past via the the hidden states and uh, predicting what will come next uh, as a proper distribution over the latent states. Um, uh, in this paper the controller is is very simple. It's actually just like a single fully connected layer uh, mapping uh, the latent uh, and HT and the uh, the latent and the memory um, states to to an action. Um, and so here kind of the intuition behind this is that uh, the world model should be doing all the heavy lifting in terms of representation"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "so that the controller doesn't need to be expressive. Um, and um, in some later more modern uh, works that we will discuss later uh, you will kind of see like a reflection of of this idea. Um, uh, interestingly here um, this controller doesn't get trained with back propagation but uh, black box evolutionary uh, uh, optimization algorithm uh, which is essentially very very similar to the cross entropy method um, um, that we've seen before. Um, and here uh, the reason for using a gradient free method is basically that um, because the world model rollouts involves uh stochastic um sampling steps that makes end-to-end differentiation a bit more difficult. Um, they just like treat the world model as a black box and uh optimize the controller parameters directly against it."}, {"t": 1860, "text": "Here are um some results. Uh on the left you can see how the uh latent dream actually looks like uh during training. And so you can see it's like blurry and uh um kind of a bit abstract. Um, not like a clear reconstruction um of the environment, but good enough for the controller to learn from. Right? So the the car is like uh navigating mostly inside the tracks um in this imagined world. Um and on the right uh you can see a nice ablation of what happens if you remove for example the memory component of this approach. Uh so [clears throat] you can see that the car drives almost immediately out of tracks um because it can see where it is right now, but uh without the memory doesn't have like a sense for its velocity or or or the curvature and you know, it's not able to maybe like anticipate some some turns. Uh here are the two main environments"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "they evaluated on. So the the car racing task and the VizDoom, like the first-person shooting game. Um and um yeah, what's notable is for um it's actually for the for the uh shooter game um that it's actually quite complex, like both uh visually um like there are like many enemies moving around in the game. Um and it's like a much harder perception task than the car racing uh environment. Um and like the fact that the VAE can compress um data into the latent code that's useful for control um, and the RNN can then, um, um, predict forward in that space, uh, well enough for the controller to train is actually quite strong for like, uh, 2018. Um, so it's it's pretty impressive, actually. Okay. Um, so here is now a fundamental problem"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "with this approach or with this world models approach, um, that's that's worth kind of understanding, um, a bit more carefully. And that is, um, that without, uh, priors, um, it will start drifting very very easily. Um, so during training the VAE encodes real observations into the, um, ZT latent. Um, and the RNN is trained on those real, uh, latent codes, right? Um, but then during imagination phase, right, there are no, uh, real observations, and the RNN has to feed its own hallucinated, uh, latents back into itself as the next step. Um, so there is like this kind of fundamental like distribution, uh, mismatch, uh, here. And, um, and that's because the VAE was, let's say, like trained independently of of the RNN, for example. Right? It encodes [clears throat] the observations, but not the model's"}, {"t": 2041, "text": "beliefs. Um, and so in order to fix this, like, you would need to learn a a prior, um, of, um, the latent, um, ZT given, um, the the the memory, uh, uh, state HT. So, like a distribution over the current, uh, latent that's conditioned on the deterministic hidden state, uh, HT. Um, and so that's kind of the issue here, um, that, um, like the RNN receives hallucinated, uh, uh, inputs at test time. Um, the fix to this, um, is, uh, was proposed by the recurrent state space, uh, model, um, or RSSM, uh, that kind of directly addresses, um, this issue. Um, and here the design choice is, um, splitting the latent state into two parts, um, so there is a deterministic, uh, HT flow, uh, that goes through the"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "RNN and is never sampled. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so it kind of carries the memory, uh, across time. Um, and then the second part is to have a stochastic, uh, um, latent, um, ZT that, um, captures the uncertainty around the state, around the current state. And so, um, then during training, um, you uh, kind of infer, um, the latent ZT from the actual observation, um, so that's your posterior. And then during imagination, um, you can sample ZT from a learned prior. That's like the model that we just said that was missing from the Schmidhuber paper. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; that only needs, um, the hidden state HT. Uh, so it doesn't require the actual observation, right? And in order to, to train this prior, um, they proposed using the, uh, KL loss, um, that forces"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "the, this to stay close to the posterior during training, so that the imagined latents, um, then stay in the region, um, that the model actually understands, right? So it's close in latent space. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, yeah. Uh, here is how, um, this works, uh, during imagination. So during this closed-loop uh, inference, um at each step, the policy reads the current uh uh hidden state and and latent and outputs an action um and then the world model advances. So, the RNN updates the hidden state and the next uh latent is sampled uh from the prior conditioned on the new uh hidden state. Uh and so, no real observation is needed anywhere um in this loop. And then, the imagined reward is accumulated um over the rollout under policy um can optimize uh against this."}, {"t": 2222, "text": "&gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um Yeah, and here like you can see again like this deterministic path, right? Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, while the stochastic part of the ZT um is um sampled fresh uh at each step from the prior, um the kind of the RNN HT like remains deterministic, right? It doesn't get uh corrupted. Um so, yeah, this is what makes it kind of work in in in practice. Uh to make it like the world model stable enough that uh you can uh roll out uh kind of very long um behaviors and get a meaningful training signal. Um the this slide here is the step from um the Planet uh paper or where the RSSM was introduced to Dreamer uh V1. And uh motivation is quite simple."}, {"t": 2280, "text": "Um so, the paper we just uh covered uh still actually uses the cross-entropy method uh from scratch at every single time step. Um so, it samples like thousands of action sequences, rolls them out, and refeeds the the Gaussian and so forth. Um and that's quite expensive, right? To do this all at uh at test time. And so the the question um Dreamer uh V1 asks is, why not um instead of doing that um kind of amortize the planning into a learned policy? Um so here they uh run the the imagination rollouts during training, and then they back propagate through the differentiable um recurrent state space model dynamics. And train a actor-critic um to basically internalize what the cross-entropy method was doing at test time in the previous version."}, {"t": 2341, "text": "Um so this way at uh deployment time, you can now just run the policy, and you don't you don't have a planning loop that's needed anymore. Um and so yeah, and you still have the two training procedures where you first train the world model um on the let's say real data, um then you freeze the world model, um roll out imagined trajectories in latent space, and uh then train the actor-critic by back propagating through those uh frozen dynamics. Okay. Uh Daydreamer is a direct application of the Dreamer uh algorithm or paradigm um to real physical uh robots. Um so it's the same uh kind of algorithm, same hyperparameters, uh just running on actual uh robots. Um And the uh key number here is on the"}, {"t": 2400, "text": "quadruped, right? So 1 hour of real interaction is enough to learn uh walking on the quadruped uh from scratch. Um and so the world model basically um lets you train for the equivalent of days in imagination um from that one hour of real-world interaction data. Um and that's kind of a very um um very one of the appealing uh properties of this paradigm. Um the paper also has a couple of nice contributions um to make Dreamer work on real robots uh or hardwares like asynchronous asynchronous training and inference like where the policy runs at a uh control frequency in real time uh while the world model trains in the background. Um I recommend checking the paper for for more details. Um And so I think the best way to understand what we've been building so"}, {"t": 2461, "text": "far is maybe like the scene from uh Matrix uh the movie if you have watched this before. Uh this there is a scene where like uh Neo uh dreams about like different martial arts uh like you know practicing like kung fu and accumulates like years of experience worth while dreaming and then learns how to master uh kung fu essentially completely in imagination, right? And that's kind of the dream of world models. Um so don't spend hours like watching your robot like fail um in real world just build a a model um that's uh good enough train in imagination uh and then transfer. Um Um to summarize, I mean if you're interested in this, there's a whole series of uh Dreamer papers um kind of I summarize kind of the main key points um of each of them. Um so we started like with the"}, {"t": 2522, "text": "Schmidhuber paper um uh that was kind of the uh proof of concept um the first one I think uh agent that was purely trained in imagination. Then uh we innovation was the RSSM planet in 2019. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Then Dreamer V1 that replaced the cross-entropy method with the actor-critic policy. Then in Dreamer 2 and 3, there were like some more improvements to the training per se like categorical latents, KL balancing, and so forth. I think the Dreamer V3 was very impressive cuz it was the first one to obtain diamonds in the Minecraft from pixels. And this is like a very very long like over two two over 20,000"}, {"t": 2581, "text": "actions I think that the agent needs to take to do this. And then last year Dreamer V4 managed to kind of repeat this. But without any online interaction at all, purely offline. Dreamer V4 actually introduces two important changes worth understanding and that serve kind of as a nice transition to to the next algorithmic framework. The [clears throat] first one is that here in Dreamer V4 the dynamics model is pre-trained on unlabeled video without any actions required. And this is done by a flow matching learning a history of the latents. And then you can fine-tune this on with actions to get a action condition model."}, {"t": 2640, "text": "And so this decoupling is quite nice because it means that you can leverage more like videos and scalable uh data sources that don't you don't have necessary actions for. Uh and then the second one I think they replace the hidden state of the recurrent neural network um with a transformer's KV cache. Um this gives your model much more expressive and longer range memory than a fixed size RNN hidden state. Um And as I said like the they had very nice uh like results with the Minecraft diamonds offline. And um yeah and yeah Danny Jar they also uh uh showed that it works also in one of my papers the the sort paper the the data we collected here that was collected autonomously uh for a different project."}, {"t": 2702, "text": "So you can see that it can also model uh real robot videos. That's very nice. But there is a fundamental limitation um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; with everything we've covered so far. Um and that is that the latent spaces trained for this world models like dreamer are typically specific to a certain domain. Right? So it's either Minecraft or DM control or some particular robot you're training for. And so the latents are um like compact and useful but they are kind of like specific to whatever environment that you are like targeting. Uh and it's like difficult to transfer them. Um And so if we want a world model that understands the general visual world um you know like that we can learn like we"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "can throw it anything right? No matter if it's like internet videos, robot data, Minecraft, whatever. Um then like a small like domain-specific like model is not going to cut it. And this brings us to the next part of the lecture, which is about generative video models. Right? So here, instead of a compact latent world model trained on one single domain, um the question we are trying to answer is what if we scale this up to internet-scale video and learn a more general model of how the visual world actually works. Um and if you want to apply transformer-based generative models to video, uh before diving into any approaches, like we can also think about how we can tokenize this, right? Um we already covered like how to tokenize images before. Um you might just think like, \"Okay,"}, {"t": 2822, "text": "I can just take my VIT, run this through each frame, and you know, that's my video tokenized.\" Um but this runs immediately into a problem, um right? Uh and that is that it really explodes your context length. Um so a 256 by 256 image with like the 16 by 16 patches we saw before will give you around 256 tokens per frame. Um you know, that sounds like fine. Um but [clears throat] if you multiply this by, let's say, 200 frames for a 10-second clip at 20 FPS, now you have like over 50,000 tokens. Uh and that's kind of at the limit of the context length for GPT-4, for example. Uh and that's only for a 10-second like video clip, right? So the challenge is like how can we tokenize video in a way um that's temporally aware and compact enough um that we can fit it into a"}, {"t": 2880, "text": "reasonable context window. And we kind of need to solve this before we can &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um build generative video models um that operate at any uh useful scale. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, we could probably do a full lecture on video tokenization. But here is um kind of the main gist of Sorry. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; The main gist um of things to be aware of. Um the core problem, as you can guess, is that video is essentially very fundamentally extremely redundant. All right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, um consecutive frames are uh highly correlated. Uh and as we said, like a naive tokenizer will ignore this completely. So, there are like three main axes um uh along which you can compress video tokens. Uh and they are typically um independent"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "and composable. Um the first one is um spatial compression, right? That's kind of like very obvious. So, you can use like deeper encoders, uh use larger strides, um and kind of um use like a lot Sorry. Uh use a lot of the VQ-style encoders that we also covered uh in the past lectures uh to kind of get a more spatial compression. The second axis is temporal compression. Um this means like merging multiple frames into uh like a single token. Um you can do this uh non-causally, for example, with uh tuplets, uh treating a chunk of images as uh 3D uh as a single 3D patch, or um you can do this cause causally by aggregating past frames um into [clears throat] a single token"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "while decoding frame by frame. Um the causal version is for example the one that you really want if you want your model to work in streaming or control settings. And for example Dreamer V4 or for example the Cosmos models uh use some sort of causal video tokenizers. Um and the last one is doing adaptive compression. Um so you can think of this as um um compress compressing the videos depending on the complexity of the images. Um so this means that if you have like simple static frames where there is not a lot a lot a lot of things going on um that you will use less tokens to represent um those frames or those video snippets. While if um the video is very complex um"}, {"t": 3062, "text": "or I don't know maybe you are doing like your robot task with a pick and place or something um if it's like a dynamic scene that you assign or allocate more tokens to represent that cuz you might want finer details uh and so on. And um And yeah and you can combine um ideas from these different kind of like compression axis. So for example Cosmos has different tokenizers but for example one of them applies like eight fold &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh spatial compression that's like eight fold and also the same for temporal. Um, so this means that for instead of the over five 50,000 tokens um uh from like applying the naive frame by frame VIT, um like this will"}, {"t": 3120, "text": "compress the same 10-second clip into around uh 100 tokens. All right. Um, so when we scale to internet video, um we kind of hit like uh another question, which is um where do actions live? Um, in the action condition world models we've covered so far, the actions are um explicitly inputs to the model. Um, so you feed your observations and um the future actions in and you could get future uh states out. And this requires action label data, which is usually scarce at internet scale. Um So there are two kind of uh alternative paradigms. Um, so the first uh to this um which uh we can say that they're like video-based action prediction models. Um, the first one is"}, {"t": 3181, "text": "uh like a world action model or WAM. Um, so it's a single model um that jointly predicts video and actions uh end-to-end. Um And so um unlike uh the Dreamer-based uh models where actions are inputs that condition the world model, um here in in WAMs the actions are outputs. So um the model generates a future video uh and uh predicts the actions uh that produce it um at the same time together. Um So one also difference to to uh with respect to the Dreamer series is that here this typically gets initialized from a general pre-trained video backbone. So the world model knowledge comes also from this pre-trained backbone rather from just like pure robot interaction data."}, {"t": 3240, "text": "And Dream Zero is one example of this. The second [clears throat] the second kind of family is our video action models or VAMs. Where essentially you freeze a video backbone that's been pre-trained on internet video, extract the video features and now train a lightweight inverse dynamics model or IDM on top of it using only robot data for the IDM part. And so this is for example mimic video approach. And here again the video backbone is doing the heavy lifting of the dynamics and the action head just needs to learn the mapping from the visual features to the robot actions. Which requires much less data. And so yeah, so where the actions live essentially gives a little bit of trade-off"}, {"t": 3300, "text": "between the data requirements, scalability and how tightly the world model is &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; coupled to control which we will talk a little bit about. Um. At this point in the lecture you might be wondering like okay. Didn't we just say before that video is expensive? Why use video backbones at all? Because we said like it's very token hungry and it's computationally expensive, right? And. I think I kind of already hinted at this, but you can see uh again like what this buys you by looking at what has been until now kind of the state of the art, right? Of the VLAs that we've been covering um cuz like if you think about it like on how like the kind of the VLA paradigm works, right? Um you typically train you take a pre-trained VLM, right? Um that's"}, {"t": 3363, "text": "been pre-trained on some like I don't know like captioning visual question answering tasks. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and then you fine-tune this into a large with a large-scale robot teleoperation data. And so the VLM will bring like some semantic pre-trained knowledge like it knows what cutting a carrot means. Um but it's been trained on this static image text pairs. Uh and so hence like it doesn't have this um understanding of temporal dynamics, no sense of how actions change the state. Um or physical causality as we said before. And therefore like to um uh we need to collect lots of robot teleop data to then like adapt this model to understand all of this, right? Um all this like dynamics, like contact"}, {"t": 3422, "text": "physics, what happens when you grasp something. Uh none of that comes from the VLM pre-training. Uh and so you're paying a very high data cost to compensate for a representation that was never designed for control. And so instead if instead of that um we build on top of a pre-trained video backbone, we can now flip the recipe um where now instead of starting from a VLM, um, that's been trained on this static image text pairs and hoping that the robot data teaches its its dynamics, um, we can now start from this video model, um, that's been pre-trained on large-scale internet scale, uh, video data. Right? So, beyond, um, semantics now, uh, it knows what cutting card means, um, it also has now visual dynamics baked in uh, right from the get-go, from"}, {"t": 3482, "text": "the pre-trained model. And so, it understands how, um, things, uh, move and how actions, uh, cause changes in the visual world. And so, this means that now the the robotics fine-tuning task suddenly becomes much simpler because now you only need to learn control. Um, so the mapping from the visual features to the robot actions, you don't need to teach the model physics from scratch. Um, and so, you will need, uh, way less data, um, um, to get a good, uh, VLA, uh, cuz the post-training is much more efficient. And, um, this is exactly what, for example, we found out in this Mimic Video paper, um, that I co-authored also with, uh, our TAs Yonas and Liam, um, who were also interning with me at Microsoft. And, um, we, uh, coined this, uh, as video action models and found that it led to a 10% uh, boost in sample efficiency, uh,"}, {"t": 3540, "text": "compared to uh, pi is 0.5, uh, style VLA models. Um, this slide gets, uh, a a something, uh, fundamental about why the video backbone approach really works. Um, the intuition is that, as I said before, like this video models really learn like in both like image semantics, uh, physical dynamics, and um visual action plans all at the same time, right? And so, um if that is true, then as I said before, the decoders uh job should become much more simple, like almost trivial, right? Like you will just be translating a visual plan into um that's already implicit uh in the video representations into a low-dimensional robot trajectory. Um And so, uh we did this Oracle experiment where we trained action decoders on top of um"}, {"t": 3601, "text": "different video representations. Um uh and where we um saw, for example, that if we gave it uh ground truth uh uh latents extracted uh from future uh frames of like things that actually happened, um then um the success rate was near perfect uh regardless of whether the backbone was fine-tuned on uh robot data or not. Um so, this means that the pre-trained video model uh backbone alone already provides representations that are rich enough to uh decode perfectly actions with uh minimal robot data. And um the the gap between the uh Oracle performance um and um the predicted um the Sorry, the actions predicted um from uh the non-Oracle uh frames"}, {"t": 3661, "text": "um shows you exactly where the bottleneck is, right? So, it's the video prediction quality, um meaning not the action decoding part. Um so, this means that um the path to better robot performance in this uh family or in this paradigm um is very straightforward, like it's just to build better video models. So, as the video models become better and approximate the oracle, um also the downstream performance will improve. Okay, I don't have that much time, so I will go uh fast a bit faster. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, in in this architecture, uh we use the Cosmos Predict 2 as the video backbone, um which is this pre-trained uh uh generative model. And um rather than maybe one thing that's interesting that is that rather than"}, {"t": 3721, "text": "uh generating uh uh full video at inference time, we use a partial denoising strategy, um where we run the video backbone um forward only to an intermediate flow time, um and then extract the latent uh visual plan from that point uh to condition the action decoder. Uh and also um a key decision um uh choice was here um that the video and action components, cuz they're both trained with flow matching, they uh have independent flow schedules. Um And um yeah, there's some results. We already covered this. Um as I said, we don't have to predict um we don't have to reconstruct the video at test time, um but just for visualization, uh that's uh how it looks on the right."}, {"t": 3781, "text": "Um and then uh Dream Zero takes the uh world action model approach we described earlier, this joint video and action prediction, uh into a single model uh without a separate uh IDM uh decoder. Um so here this is a and we have today also as a guest speaker Scott Reed who is a co-author of this paper. Maybe you'll talk a bit about this, too. Um So here the architecture is a single causal DAT that takes the video latents, uh noisy action tokens, uh proprioception and language as input and then jointly denoises both the video and actions uh using a flow matching uh with again teacher forcing during training. Um so here there is no real separation between the world model and the policy cuz it's like one model predicting both at the same time."}, {"t": 3841, "text": "Um and then inference the model runs autoregressively. It generates chunks of future frames uh and the corresponding actions together um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; autoregressively. Uh and it uses uh again KV cache to make this a bit more efficient. Um uh that's actually one of the nice things of this kind of approach that uh unlike in pure video generation settings uh because this is now a closed-loop setting, this allows kind of setting ground truth observation uh to replace the generated frames in your KV cache after each action execution um which allows you to bypass the compounding errors that are usually inherent to to pure autoregressive video generation models. Uh and they also have like tons of cool engineering contributions to make this"}, {"t": 3901, "text": "also work at real time with a 14B parameter model. Um so yeah, that's also recommendable to to check out. Um and they also have quite nice results um showing strong generalization uh with humanoids uh and also nice uh few-shot adaptation capabilities. Uh not all tasks are perfect as you can see uh also cloth folding uh or um picking up a marker and drawing uh a circle on the book is still not perfect. But in general, lots of uh very tasks um uh like for example also the uh shoelace knitting uh it's it's pretty impressive. Um So here is a a kind of like a small comparison slide um between uh world action models and video action models"}, {"t": 3962, "text": "um and how they compare across different data um across different axes. Um action condition world uh models win on data in the sense that uh you can train it with any sorts of data like successes uh failures, play data. Um so significantly broader amount of data uh compared uh to to WAMs. Um while they uh they need like kind of more task um task labels [clears throat] for instructing um instruction conditioning. Uh so which means that if you use play data, you would need to have uh some hindsight relabeling at scale. Um then um WAMs uh have a bit of an advantage on uh cross-embodiment um because the video backbones uh are robot agnostic. Um and only the action decoder needs uh"}, {"t": 4021, "text": "robot specific data. Um and uh for for action condition world models, um, cross embodiment learning is kind of an open problem still. Uh, then RAMs have um better feature are better at um retaining pre-trained capabilities. Um, and um action condition models need to learn essentially action conditioning from scratch, which can destroy partially um the pre-trained knowledge of the of the model. And then, um sorry. And then the action conditioned uh world models kind of have the advantage that they can um at least today uh go beyond pure imitation by uh also doing RL in imagination, uh which we still haven't uh uh seen for the other paradigm."}, {"t": 4083, "text": "Um, yeah, to close, um everything we have seen so far, um so Dreamer, Dream Zero, Mimic Video, etc., um all the approaches that we've covered uh so far um typically have a decoder somewhere. Uh, so at some point in these approaches, and it it varies sometimes like where, um there is a decoder that maps back um um to the pixels um to reconstruct. Um, and there is a a kind of a a fourth uh family of algorithms that kind of asks the asks the question, um do we even need to predict uh the pixels at all? And that's kind of the question that um the Jepa framework tries to uh address, um where the idea is that um instead of predicting what the next frame looks like, um"}, {"t": 4141, "text": "you just predict what the next frame's embedding will look like. Uh, so you encode the current frame, um, predict the next latent given, uh, your action, then encode, um, the actual next frame, uh, with the same encoder, uh, and minimize the distance between the two, uh, latents. Um, so you don't have any reconstruction, no decoder, uh, no no pixel loss. Um, and the appeal is like obvious cuz now you, you know, predicting in this latent space is also cheaper, um, and you're not, you know, wasting any capacity and and trying to model all the pixels. Um, uh, but of course there is a catch in the sense that, um, if the encoder maps, uh, every frame to the same vector, then the prediction loss is trivially zero, um, and that is this kind of representation collapse, um, and this kind of central problem that this Jepa family, um,"}, {"t": 4201, "text": "um, has been trying to solve over the last years. This is kind of the this fundamental challenge to any, um, self-supervised, uh, method that doesn't reconstruct the the input. Um, and so there are then, uh, a few different, um, approaches that, uh, address this. Um, uh, like V Jepa 2 uses, um, an EMA target encoder, uh, so a a slow-moving copy of the encoder, um, that the predictor must, uh, kind of, uh, chase. Uh, or the Dino world model uses, uh, um, pre-trained encoder, um, but doesn't do then end-to-end learning, um, and then the most recent work, uh, the world model, uh, uses a Gaussian regularizer, um, that forces the latent distribution, uh, to spread out making, um, this kind of constant vector, uh, solutions, uh, not possible. Um, um"}, {"t": 4261, "text": "yeah, so to put everything together uh for today's lecture, uh we started with with this formula. Uh predicting the next state um given the the current state, actions, and the memory. Um and then um all the approaches we've covered today uh have like kind of provided kind of a different answer to the same question. And they differ in terms of like how you represent a state, uh where the actions live, and what kind of uh data we can use. Um and I guess I'm way over over time. Um so yeah, I'll leave you with that. Thank you."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Slide 1: Mid-term feedback", "slide": "slide-1-mid-term-feedback-frame-2"}, {"t": 62, "title": "Slide 2: Changes", "slide": "slide-2-changes-frame-3"}, {"t": 180, "title": "Slide 3: Projects: Key Information", "slide": "slide-3-projects-key-information-frame-4"}, {"t": 361, "title": "Slide 4: Project 1: Reasoning Pick&Place - VLA", "slide": "slide-4-project-1-reasoning-pickplace---vla-frame-5"}, {"t": 420, "title": "Slide 5: Project 2: Pushing - World Model", "slide": "slide-5-project-2-pushing---world-model-frame-6"}, {"t": 481, "title": "Slide 6: Project 3: Singulation - Reinforcement Learning", "slide": "slide-6-project-3-singulation---reinforcement-learning-frame-7"}, {"t": 541, "title": "Slide 7: Project 4: Keyboard Typing - Code as Policies", "slide": "slide-7-project-4-keyboard-typing---code-as-policies-frames-8-13"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Slide 8: Project 5: Cloth Folding - Diffusion Policy", "slide": "slide-8-project-5-cloth-folding---diffusion-policy-frame-14"}, {"t": 721, "title": "Slide 10: World Models", "slide": "slide-10-world-models-frame-16"}, {"t": 900, "title": "Slide 11: World Models vs Policies", "slide": "slide-11-world-models-vs-policies-frame-17"}, {"t": 900, "title": "Slide 12: Why World Models?", "slide": "slide-12-why-world-models-frame-18"}, {"t": 1020, "title": "Slide 13: World Models: Data-driven Simulators", "slide": "slide-13-world-models-data-driven-simulators-frame-19"}, {"t": 1140, "title": "Slide 14: World Model Definition", "slide": "slide-14-world-model-definition-frame-20"}, {"t": 1201, "title": "Slide 15: Pixel AC-WMs", "slide": "slide-15-pixel-ac-wms-frame-21"}, {"t": 1380, "title": "Slide 17: Pixel AC-WMs [training and Visual MPC]", "slide": "slide-17-pixel-ac-wms-training-and-visual-mpc-frame-22"}, {"t": 1501, "title": "Slide 18: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-18-untitled-transition-question-frame-23"}, {"t": 1501, "title": "Slide 19: Latent AC-WMs", "slide": "slide-19-latent-ac-wms-frame-24"}, {"t": 1562, "title": "Slide 20: The OG Latent AC-WM", "slide": "slide-20-the-og-latent-ac-wm-frame-25"}, {"t": 1562, "title": "Slide 21: The OG Latent AC-WM: Visual Model", "slide": "slide-21-the-og-latent-ac-wm-visual-model-frame-26"}, {"t": 1620, "title": "Slide 22: The OG Latent AC-WM: Memory Model", "slide": "slide-22-the-og-latent-ac-wm-memory-model-frame-27"}, {"t": 1741, "title": "Slide 23: The OG Latent AC-WM: Controller Model", "slide": "slide-23-the-og-latent-ac-wm-controller-model-frame-28"}, {"t": 1980, "title": "Slide 26: Why Imagination Without Priors Drifts", "slide": "slide-26-why-imagination-without-priors-drifts-frame-29"}, {"t": 2041, "title": "Slide 27: Training a Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM)", "slide": "slide-27-training-a-recurrent-state-space-model-rssm-frame-30"}, {"t": 2160, "title": "Slide 28: RSSM Closed-Loop Inference Imagination", "slide": "slide-28-rssm-closed-loop-inference-imagination-frame-31"}, {"t": 2280, "title": "Slide 29: Dreamer V1", "slide": "slide-29-dreamer-v1-frame-32"}, {"t": 2522, "title": "Slide 32: Dreamer Lineage", "slide": "slide-32-dreamer-lineage-frame-33"}, {"t": 2581, "title": "Slide 33: Dreamer V4: Decoupling Videos & Actions", "slide": "slide-33-dreamer-v4-decoupling-videos-actions-frames-34-35"}, {"t": 2702, "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-36"}, {"t": 2822, "title": "Slide 35: Naïve Video Tokenization", "slide": "slide-35-naïve-video-tokenization-frame-37"}, {"t": 2941, "title": "Slide 36: Video Tokenization Compression", "slide": "slide-36-video-tokenization-compression-frame-38"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 37: Video World Model: Where Do Actions Live?", "slide": "slide-37-video-world-model-where-do-actions-live-frame-39"}, {"t": 3300, "title": "Slide 38: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-38-untitled-transition-question-frame-40"}, {"t": 3300, "title": "Slide 39: VLAs: Poor Sample Efficiency", "slide": "slide-39-vlas-poor-sample-efficiency-frame-41"}, {"t": 3300, "title": "Slide 40: Video Backbones", "slide": "slide-40-video-backbones-frame-42"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 41: Video-Action Models", "slide": "slide-41-video-action-models-frame-43"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 42: Policy Performance Scales with Video Model", "slide": "slide-42-policy-performance-scales-with-video-model-frames-44-45"}, {"t": 3721, "title": "Slide 43: mimic-video", "slide": "slide-43-mimic-video-frames-46-47"}, {"t": 3781, "title": "Slide 46: Joint Video-Action Sampling", "slide": "slide-46-joint-video-action-sampling-frame-48"}, {"t": 3781, "title": "Slide 47: World Action Models: DreamZero", "slide": "slide-47-world-action-models-dreamzero-frame-49"}, {"t": 3901, "title": "Slide 49 [shown early] : WAMs vs VAMs", "slide": "slide-49-shown-early-wams-vs-vams-frame-50"}, {"t": 3901, "title": "Slide 48: World Action Models: DreamZero [results]", "slide": "slide-48-world-action-models-dreamzero-results-frame-51"}, {"t": 3962, "title": "Slide 49: WAMs vs VAMs", "slide": "slide-49-wams-vs-vams-frame-52"}, {"t": 4021, "title": "Slide 50: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-50-untitled-transition-question-frame-53"}, {"t": 4141, "title": "Slide 51: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA)", "slide": "slide-51-joint-embedding-predictive-architecture-jepa-frame-54"}, {"t": 4141, "title": "Slide 52: Conclusion", "slide": "slide-52-conclusion-frame-55"}], "toc_source": "estimated from slides (approx.)"}, "guests": [{"id": "fqkp_wkov6M", "title": "Scott Reed: What is the right Backbone for Embodied Agents? [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Scott Reed", "duration": "36:11", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Scott Reed (Founding Member of Generalist Embodied Agent Research GEAR lab at Nvidia), who's been at the forefront of scaling robotics at  Google DeepMind and Nvidia,  talks about VLAs, World Models and what is the right backbone for embodied agents.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 110, "title": "Defining the backbone"}, {"t": 241, "title": "Vision language action models"}, {"t": 507, "title": "Diffusion decoders and strengths"}, {"t": 797, "title": "World action models"}, {"t": 1248, "title": "Strengths and weaknesses of WHMs"}, {"t": 1385, "title": "Training from scratch"}, {"t": 1568, "title": "Conclusion"}, {"t": 1593, "title": "Q&A session"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "I'm very excited to have a Scott give a guest lecture today with us. So, Scott Reed, he's a founding member of the generalist embodied agent research lab at Nvidia. And before that, he studied generative models and generalist agents for 8 years at Google DeepMind. For example, he's also very well known for leading um the Gato paper, generalist &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; agent paper a few years ago. And he completed his PhD at the University of Michigan in 2016 under Honglak Lee. And yeah, we are very excited to hear about your talk on latest research, Scott. Please give him a warm welcome. All right, thanks thanks for the intro and thanks for having me here. I'm very"}, {"t": 60, "text": "excited to speak to you all. And that I'll begin. So, the talk I'll give is about backbones for robot learning and the major choices that we can make here and emerging paradigms. So, VLAs, world action models, work from scratch. What's the right backbone we should use for embodied agents? First, a little bit about me. So, I'm from Michigan. This is actually just really the intro blur, but yeah. So, at DeepMind 8 years doing work on generative models and generalist agents. And last 2 years on the gear team led by Jim Fan and Yuka. And there's a family and a new guy there just joined. And yeah, that's me. So, what do I mean by backbone? So, for this talk, I mean the architecture that encodes robot sensor data, camera, as appropriate, and also the"}, {"t": 120, "text": "language instructions. Encode all of those things into some shared representation that can be used for action prediction. So, this backbone, it determines not just how the policy runs at inference time, but how it's able to soak up knowledge during pre-training and transfer that to downstream tasks of the robot. And the major choices here that can be made is one just go from scratch, just train some visual architecture just on your robot data, which could make sense if you have a lot of it. Or if you just care about some narrow task on a particular embodiment. It's not very open-ended. Over the past 5 or 10 years, the dominant paradigm has actually been more to use pre-trained foundation models instead, or at least initialize from them. And the major choices here would of course be to use VLM like a vision language model, which might have been used for things like visual question answering."}, {"t": 181, "text": "Or to the emerging paradigm is to use video models. Or there's different names for them. People Some people are calling them world models, but basically the the lineage of these things is from video generation. And in turn that into a backbone. Of course, video diffusion models have been around for a long time now, a few years, but I think only recently have reached a stage of maturity and in terms of the quality and also in terms of the architecture that would support them being a backbone for robotics and other embodied agents. So, the agenda, I'll first give brief survey of VLAs and have some commentary on those, and then world action models, and then discuss train from scratch both the historical perspective and some more recent exciting developments there. And then we can conclude with discussing the future directions and have some Q&amp;A."}, {"t": 241, "text": "So, what are what are the key papers in VLAs? So, of course, we have robot transformer series like RT-2, OpenVLA, the Pi models, Pi-0, Pi-0.5, Pi-0.6, the group models we did at Nvidia focus more on humanoids. TRI's large behavior model, and then Gemini and many others. These are just the ones I'm most familiar with. And the key principles here, the good things about them are it puts everything into a a unified modality. They tend to support cross embodiment well. You can just directly fine-tune VLM to generate control tokens. There's a lot of diversity here in terms of how they represent actions. Some use discrete, some use continuous and do diffusion or flow matching. And the great thing about them is that they can leverage internet scale"}, {"t": 301, "text": "data for reasoning and grounding. And in terms of the generalization, they there is some evidence that they outperform from scratch imitation learning. By by training on multi-task and multi-embodiment, they actually are more data efficient and have higher top-end performance than training from scratch on a single task or single embodiment. So, what do the architectures look like? So, I'll just show OpenVLA first cuz that's the simplest one. And then people may be familiar with this. Basically, you have some pre-trained any vision encoder like Dino or CLIP or both to encode language and and vision. And the instructions are encoded with some pre-trained language model. And this whole thing might be frozen at least at first, and then you learn to detokenize that into actions. In this case, the actions are just discrete discretized with binning. And then they'll later be detokenized into the continuous"}, {"t": 361, "text": "values when you actually want to run the policy. So, this is just a very simple architecture that actually turns out to work quite well. There are fancier things you can do like the Pi models have both a discrete and continuous action prediction. So, they have this thing called fast tokenizer. They have separate braces to encode the tokens. So, that VLM can be co-trained with both language tasks like predicting subtasks, while at the same time predicting actions. This turns out to be a great thing for pre-training because it turns out that if if you had just tried to co-train directly predicting discrete language tokens and then also back propagating flow matching or diffusion gradients through your VLM, this this would destroy your VLM"}, {"t": 422, "text": "weights. And so, you would lose things you would lose properties such as language following. There might be ways to do it if you carefully train in stages, but there's always a risk of catastrophic forgetting when once you just naively try to co-train on VLM language prediction tasks and robot action prediction. So, by doing everything with discrete tokens, it open up a lot of options for how to do the pre-training and to end up with better representations in your VLM for control. However, there is still a benefit to having this like separate diffusion or flow matching action expert because in some cases when you decode your discrete action tokens, you may have decoding errors. This is one of the problems with that. And you can imagine that this could cause safety issues. Like what if you you know, if if your"}, {"t": 480, "text": "decoded action is somehow undefined and it's an error and it just generates some kind of default position target for your robot arm. You could imagine the arm just flinging out and doing something really dangerous. So, so for both safety reasons and probably also for performance reasons, it's still good to have this flow matching or diffusion action expert because you'll never have decoding errors there. And there's a lot of other VLAs with diffusion decoders. So, like Pi, all the group models. This is the TRI. They call it large behavior model. I think it's just an alternative name for VLA. I also like large behavior model as a name. And this one also takes pre-trained VIT like image and language. They have a simplified architecture here where actually they're just concatenating the VIT image tokens and the language tokens and going straight to the"}, {"t": 542, "text": "VIT. That actually works surprisingly well, at least on their suite of tasks. And the great thing about this paper, everyone should read, they do a really rigorous study of like not just wishful thinking, but really examining does multi-task pre-training VLA style actually improve downstream task performance? Does it improve data efficiency and top-end performance? And they find that it does pretty pretty significantly. But they had to do very very careful very careful analysis due to the difficulty of real robot evaluation. They didn't do things like double-blind evaluation. So, you take pairs of policies, have evaluators actually not know which one is which one, and compare them head-to-head in the same robot at the same time. And doing careful statistical analysis to make sure that the results were statistically significant. Um and yeah,"}, {"t": 600, "text": "the findings do back up a lot of the intuitions that people had about VLAs, but it's great that someone actually did this very rigorously. And I think there's a lot of great lessons in that paper. Um and so here's some of the cool demos that I find inspiring from uh VLAs VLA era. So, like high difference states, the busy the fine model with RL uh post training, making special drinks for like over an hour, almost an hour. Uh I found that really, really impressive. Um here's another one from Nvidia. Uh it's called Ego Scale. Um this was built on the uh root animal 5 architecture and they used um a lot of um actually human egocentric data with hand tracking. Um And uh that data in particular is what I think made this recipe work very well on. And um so V- so VLAs can control a manual parallel jaw gripper, they can control like humanoid hands really well. Um they can also control um humanoids when"}, {"t": 660, "text": "you combine them with a whole body controller. So, the VLAs are now finding applications in all kind of different robot form factors. And um yeah, starting to be mature as a model class. So, what are the strengths? Um you get semantic grounding from pre-training. Um new new task learning is data efficient compared to going from scratch. And there's supposed to be predictable improvement as you scale the model and the data, assuming you have you know, high quality data to scale. And there's some evidence of cross-task and cross-embodiment transfer, but um I would say it's uh far from solved. Um there are some weaknesses like the prediction and reasoning are happening in your uh action space, which might be language, might be um the continuous space where you're doing diffusion. Um that might not be the right place to do reasoning about physics or uh control actually. It may may [clears throat] not be the optimal way to do that. The action representation is typically bolted on in some way after"}, {"t": 722, "text": "pre-training. And there's always a risk of catastrophic forgetting during fine-tuning. Um this is especially in data islands. Um we try we do to the lack of lots of web-scale data for robotics, we have this intuition that we should just throw all the robot data from different embodiments together. Let's call it cross-embodiment. Um but it's likely that the the model just sees these as completely separate islands that um from which there's not much transferability or that the transferability is is limited due to the difference in form and correlational structure um in these subsets of data. Um and the evidence for like zero-shot distribution zero-shot generalization out of distribution or to new embodiments is is limited or non-existent in my opinion for for um contemporary VLAs anyways. So, what is the emerging class of policy uh models that um"}, {"t": 780, "text": "have many people excited? Uh world action models or video action models. Um so, some popular um instantiations that came out recently would be Dream Zero, Cosmos Policy. Um some older ones actually is the GR1, GR2 from um ByteDance. Um and probably a bunch more uh coming out that have come out in recent conferences. Um these are just ones I'm mainly familiar with. And uh what's the paradigm shift here? Um we want to predict the world state in pixels uh either before acting or jointly with acting. Um and so we can do prediction and reasoning in a much richer space that may be more suitable to control. Um also, I would highlight that the pre-training objective um may allow the policy to just soak up a lot more relevant information um during pre-training than just action prediction. We can just squeeze a lot more juice out of the data that way uh in my opinion"}, {"t": 841, "text": "than we could if we were just predicting um uh actions only. So, what do these architectures uh look like? So, here's uh the Dream Zero architecture. Um we have there's going to be some VAE to encode the uh video frames into latents, um concatenate them with action embeddings, um conditions upon on language, um and we have some causal a block causal uh DiT so that um th- this this allows us to have um caching key caching during inference so that we can have we can do efficient uh sampling of frames and action uh when we want to uh run the policy. Uh in Dream Zero, we actually have some joint uh flow matching uh of both action and uh future uh chunk of frames. Um Th- there so in actually in Dream Zero, you can choose to jointly diffuse uh both fully or and we have they have something called Dream Zero Flash where"}, {"t": 902, "text": "um you just do one step uh or two or two steps where you don't completely actually denoise the future vision chunk and you just focus on the the action chunk. And that actually turns out to be to perform just as well. Um so so um among the different video-based uh video model-based policies, um there there's a few different ways to get actions. Some of them will actually fully sample a roll out and then use some inverse dynamics model to recover the actions. Um some of them are doing uh planning. Um and some of them uh just use the video uh prediction pre-training um as a pre-training technique and they don't actually need to fully diffuse the future observations uh during inference time. And um I think there's an interesting analogy to reasoning models in in the VLAs like um we see something similar there where a lot of the benefit from doing reasoning"}, {"t": 960, "text": "is that you just did it during pre-training. You learned a better representation that way and you the best thing to do probably still is doing reasoning at inference time, but even if you don't, having done it during pre-training makes your model better. Um gives you better representations. And then something something similar is true I would say for videos for video models. Um another uh one that came out around the same time was Cosmos Policy. Um very similar idea, but with an interesting um difference is in addition to predicting um just actions a- and images, future images, they also diffused uh future proprio and uh value. Um so, I think this paradigm is actually quite flexible. Um and maybe this paper could even have a stronger claim to being a proper world model. Um because it's just modeling uh a more complete um set of things than in the world of uh robots. And the way that they their architecture looks is also quite interesting. The"}, {"t": 1022, "text": "design choice they made was to encode uh proprio to embed proprio and actions and value into the image embedding space and just tile it spatially. Um so, that way from the perspective of the diffusion models, everything is is uh vision. You just have some uh special types of frames uh for for action, proprio, and value in addition to the um robot camera data. And the model just figures it out um as you know, as tend to do when you give them enough data. Um and also um a very different paradigm that actually came earlier was autoregressive world action models like GR1 and GR2 from from ByteDance. And these are like interleaving models that interleave uh language, robot state, uh image, and just predict everything. And they also have some uh pretty pretty encouraging impressive results. Um uh long before um"}, {"t": 1081, "text": "other other teams came to this uh paradigm. And I I I I think this could be scaled and pushed um quite far as well. Actually, this is more of the kind of flavor of model that I uh worked on previously. Um of uh Gato, which is just one big tran- transformer language model trained on all the robot data. Just kind of have some way of canonically serializing all of your sensor data and camera data into a flat sequence of tokens and model it with a transformer. Uh they make some clever design choices here and push it way further in in robotics, but um it's kind of that school of thought of like one big transformer for everything um into into a flat series of tokens. Um I think that can also be pushed quite far. Um here's some uh web demos from uh Dream Zero that I found uh pretty impressive. Um the cool thing about these is um no no none of these um behaviors were in the robot training data. Um"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "although it may have been shown by uh humans in in the pre-training at some stage uh of the video model. So, it can do things like untying boxes, uh pulling carts, pull a straw out of a cup, shirt ironing. Um I don't know if if it's really actually ironing. It's it's not actually doing ironing, it's going through the motions. So, you can see how this can be pushed further uh and hopefully um become genuinely useful uh at some stage of um development. So, uh diving deeper into Dream Zero, um we can look at the what the generated videos look like. So, for here's like untying the box. Um it's obviously not pixel perfect, but it's generally uh showing um it's generally consistent with what the robot actually does. Um what we tend to observe with these models is that performance um in terms of the sample quality and the success rate scales with model size. So, when we use like the 14 billion parameter when model, it was way"}, {"t": 1200, "text": "better than the 2B. And both for the samples and the success rate. And doing some failure analysis, we see most failures stem from video generation errors rather than action prediction. So, whenever predict the video predicted, that's what the policy ended up doing. Um, and this suggests something exciting, which is that improvements to the video backbone can directly translate to better when performance. So, whenever we see holes in the in the policy and the errors, um, if we just kind of improve the video [clears throat] model there, um, we can expect that uh policy will improve. And also, it doesn't necessarily require that we have robot data to fill in those gaps. It could be uh human data. Um, so um this gives us one uh promising path forward in um in robot learning. So, the strengths of when uh I'd say that they naturally learn some kind of intuitive physics from pixels. And they give us a much more scalable source of pre-training data. If most of the juice is just"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "coming from predicting camera sensors, we just need to give a lot of data from cameras. Um, and it's embodiment agnostic uh in a sense due to the unified output space for prediction and reasoning. Um, there's less of a tendency towards data islands um because everything shares the same form of correlational structure that you're predicting. Um, but they have some weaknesses like they're very expensive to sample compared to VLAs. Um, modeling every pixel is probably overkill. I mean, we're not doing it in space, we're doing it in latent space, but it's still it's a type of latent space that was learned to compress pixels but still be able to reconstruct them. Um, right? So, you know, we're taking we're taking the latent space of video prediction models that ultimately have to produce aesthetically appealing high visual fidelity. Um, that that still may be overkill for robotics. And there's um a lot of papers showing that you care about just one domain like Minecraft, for example, you could train a much much more"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "compressed uh tokenizer or VAE for just for that space. Um, and train a sequence model on that which um generalizes well and is much uh more compact and run a much faster. Um, if we were to care about like really open domain robotics, um, it's not clear we can compress that much, but um I do think that's a promising area of like can we if we just start doing control and we don't ultimately care about generation, is there some modification we can make to these tokenizers or VAEs used by whens that just compress them a lot? And there's a lot of exciting research happening. I think the answer is yes, but uh we need some an approach that is that is robust in general uh and kind of plug and play. And I and then I think that they'll drive a further cycle of improvement in whens. Um, also context length is currently limited in most contained query approaches. Again, there's tons of cool research happening here. Um, I think it's just a matter of making it really simple um for people to use um to and um and scalable without without sacrificing"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "performance. Um, so how do we how do we how do we extend uh context length? So, the third paradigm is uh training things uh from scratch. Um, here's the main papers that I think about in the um robot policy from scratch genre. Um, going way back in time to like the DQN days, um, before the foundation model era, like everyone just trained some bespoke network on like one task. And of course, people had the idea what if we train one big network on all the data. So, um, I would say Gato, Octo, RT-1 uh are of this um concept. So, in Gato, um, we even took like text and images and the QA tasks along with Atari and robotics and just tokenized everything into one big transformer. And you can do that um and you can do that with robots as well, um, whether it's single embodiment multitask like RT-1 or or or many tasks like RTX um and Octo."}, {"t": 1442, "text": "Um, there is this tendency to learn data islands which limit the generalization. I would say there's some generalization in all of these, um, but but still limited by the data island uh phenomenon. And um we do tend to I think of this approach we we do tend to hit a limit of just how many um embodiments how much data high quality data you can get from a like a data consortium or just like in my case harvesting all the data I could from DeepMind sim agents and a few robots. Um, it's it's kind of bounded by like researcher time. Um, so what are the modern approaches to like from scratch? So, really exciting things happening with UMI, um, Universal Manipulator Interface, um, like uh Generalist AI and Sunday of course and other companies. Um, I just included just uh some for examples. Um, they're doing large scale human based pre-training. I think they said they have like 500K hours something crazy like that. Um, so with these sensorized humans, if you add sensors to the humans, you can um"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "gather really really high quality really high quality demonstrations that transfer directly to the robot. Another approach is just to not even have sensors but use cameras to um to do annotation of data of like humans um doing things in the wild. This is Ego4D from DeepMind. We only had 20K hours, um, but uh it could easily be scaled uh way more. And um with a small amount of like sensorized data like 50 hours is a viable amount as well. And like a tiny amount of robot data, uh we see pretty pretty impressive results. So, both of these kind of approaches are showing scaling law uh type of behaviors where where you just increase the human data, you you see a a a continuous decrease in the validation loss and you see a a a monotonic increase in the success rate. So, both of these papers that take a very different approach, but they're both saying um with human data not not scaling the data but scaling human data which scales much better. Um, from scratch can then actually work. And so, you can think about training a foundation model without without having"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "to uh chain yourself to either VLMs or video models or anything. You can do whatever architecture you want. The architecture may not even matter. Um, so concluding thoughts and somewhat tongue here. Uh what I'm really bullish on these days is uh world action models video action models for their um generalization properties and also scaling human data which could be sensorized or only partially sensorized. I think that putting these two together will take robot learning uh a really long way and I'm excited to see um great work everyone does on this. And thank you to everyone for having me here and I think now I can pause for some discussion. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Hey. Uh so, you mentioned that you think that predicting the latent space of a VAE designed to like for video generation might be overkill. Do you"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "think predicting the latent space of like a Jeppa model or something like that might be more efficient? Yes, I think I think so. I think so. Um, yeah, latent world models um are very promising direction. I don't know if I would like make a big bet on that on the right now because I think we still need to fig we we don't know um exactly what are the right features for robotics. We have this intuition, many of us have this intuition that pixels are overkill, but at least they are sufficient. They they um that there's probably a lot in there that we don't need, but but we we don't know exactly how like we don't know how to cut it down to the minimal subset of features that that are needed. So, yeah, I I think Jeppa and other latent uh world models are definitely the right direction. Um, but yeah, it's it's it's un- unclear that they've that they've solved it yet. I"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "think I think more research needs to be done there. Hi. Um, thank you for the presentation. Um, you mentioned that um at some point these video action models should get better with the quality of the video generation. Um, but maybe there's an alternative conclusion which is that the action conditioning doesn't work. How do we ensure that it's not the action conditioning but the quality of the video model that is the problem? Uh the action conditioning? Do you mean like for if you're doing some kind of roll out and you're doing planning with the with the world model or how how do you mean the action conditioning here? &gt;&gt; the the slide showed that the action prediction actually worked, but &gt;&gt; Mhm. the generated video was bad. And these were the failure cases. So, the problem could be either that the video the video model is bad or that the action is out of distribution or not well the video model is not well"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "conditioned on this action. Yes, yes. We need to kind of decompose these problems. There are cases Yeah, I think there could definitely be cases where the video is is perfect and the model is just unable to uh find the right action that explains that video. Um, but typically we saw um when working on this paper that uh most of the failures um we can correlate we can correlate the the the task failure with the low quality video. Like the video will actually show it the the the the robot tends to at least try to do the gross motor actions that follow um follow the video. But you can see with the quality of these videos if there's some kind of very very fine grained uh manipulation, Then it may not that that information may just not be in the video. So I think at the very, very, very fine-grained manipulation level, there is still the problem of just generating actions,"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "which which can be disentangled from the the video generation itself. I think as video models get higher and higher quality, they'll capture more and more fine-grained motions. And I would expect like the correlation between video failure and action failure to to be even tighter. Hello. I have two questions. So, do you think that simulation will be redundant and that world models will be enough to basically capture all of the world's physics and dynamics? And that also RL, so like online and offline RL on top will like with scaling not be necessary. So like you think it's just like scaling issue and the current architecture we have is enough to capture everything. And also do you think that video alone is sufficient? Like you said sensorized human data. By that do you just mean like Arya glasses like capturing where the hand is or do you also mean like tactile feedback? Do you think that tactile can like force"}, {"t": 1862, "text": "can be completely inferred from the video? Do you think we still need hardware to collect that kind of human data? Yeah, a lot of great questions there. I'll try to go through them one by one. So for sim, it will sim be redundant. I think not anytime soon. I I do think that like neural-based sim will be very helpful for manipulation and and may displace some of the role of like sim manipulation. Although I'm sure there's a lot of people who would disagree with me on that, but I think like contact with manipulation and deformables and this kind of thing like it might be where neural-based sim can shine. We can do like learning in that neural simulator. But for things like whole body control on humanoids or quadrupeds and this kind of thing, I think sims I doubt that sims are really going anywhere. I mean just the the efficiency of the human hand-crafted sims is just so amazingly high that and then the cost the expense of running"}, {"t": 1922, "text": "these world models is is so expensive that um I wouldn't expect them to overtake uh human-crafted sims for for those anytime soon. But who knows what what research will come out on that next but yeah, I I think there's a role for both sim and neural sim for for for some time. Other questions on like force sensing and tactile. Yes, I I think when it comes to sensorized humans um I do think we should collect at least some amount of those. I don't know like I I think okay, I think yeah, so let's keep your head this argument that for for language models. Do they have a world model? He says, well, I mean if you are training a model on enough of the entire corpus of English text or web text and you really want to predict the next token with just maximal accuracy, implicitly in the model you"}, {"t": 1981, "text": "should have some world model of like how to explain what's going to happen next, which would require some deep understanding maybe even of physics, maybe even of things that you would think would require embodied experience, but if you just drive down the perplexity, if you just drive down the the loss of this language model, a better model would sort of by construction have this world model. So I think the same thing is even more true for video prediction models like things all kind of forces that are not explicitly in in the pixels may be latent in the model if it's good enough model at predicting those things. There's all kind of correlates of those forces in the pixels that you're predicting. So if you want to do a good enough job, that video model must have those features in them and you just need to elicit those features. We need ways to probe and elicit those features. And so if that's true, I still think we"}, {"t": 2042, "text": "need a lot of sensorized Do we have to sensorize humans and collect data? But maybe only like maybe several orders of magnitude less than we need of the pixels. Like like the Ego4D like the way they did it is I think informative. Like they had 20k hours of just in the wild with camera on the head. They only needed 50 with five like five is tracking the wrist and Manus glove is tracking the fingers. And you can imagine like adding tactile as well. Like probably that's that's kind of what I would expect is like the tactile is is there, but only a small amount. And it's really the point of it is like to elicit those features that were already in the world model that were kind of learned from mostly from video. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; You talked about that you know, policy performance will now scale with video prediction performance for example and"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "then also with the scale of the data. Do you think like the future of like exciting robot policies will only be or we will only see exciting policies from companies in the future basically? Because there's so much capital involved in training all these nice video models and collecting all this data or what's the future going to be? No, I think that exciting things are going to happen from academia and big companies and small companies. I think that yeah, a lot of times great ideas come from having experiences and yeah, I think we we will we will see amazing stuff including really high fidelity cool interactive video models from labs and companies of all scales, I think. So GPU if you want to train backbones, that takes a lot of GPU hours. But there are also other problems that you might be able to solve in robot learning and"}, {"t": 2161, "text": "that require less GPU hours. So which problems would you look at if you are like more constrained as a researcher?"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and background"}, {"t": 110, "title": "Defining the backbone"}, {"t": 241, "title": "Vision language action models"}, {"t": 507, "title": "Diffusion decoders and strengths"}, {"t": 797, "title": "World action models"}, {"t": 1248, "title": "Strengths and weaknesses of WHMs"}, {"t": 1385, "title": "Training from scratch"}, {"t": 1568, "title": "Conclusion"}, {"t": 1593, "title": "Q&A session"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "NVIDIA GEAR Lab"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 13.04.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (56 frames grabbed from the lecture recording, 1280x720, no text layer), from github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich <code>lectures/week08_slides.pdf</code></li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/cTTmUZlOF2s (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_scott_reed.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 8: World Models</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 13.04.2026</p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 8: World Models Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 13.04.2026", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-mid-term-feedback-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Mid-term feedback", "title": "Slide 1: Mid-term feedback", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Well-received: main lectures, guest lectures &amp; homeworks</li>\n<li>Things to improve: paper presentations, more time for main lecture &amp; a break, more ECTS credits for workload</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two screenshots of anonymous survey answers. Question: “What is your favorite aspect of the course so far? (What should we keep doing?)” - the answer praises the speakers and the homeworks and calls the course practical. A second answer says the paper presentations are useful as a list of papers everybody in the field knows about, but doubts the presentations themselves because they are very short and hard to follow.]</span></p>", "text": "Well-received: main lectures, guest lectures & homeworks Things to improve: paper presentations, more time for main lecture & a break, more ECTS credits for workload [Figure: two screenshots of anonymous survey answers. Question: “What is your favorite aspect of the course so far? (What should we keep doing?)” - the answer praises the speakers and the homeworks and calls the course practical. A second answer says the paper presentations are useful as a list of papers everybody in the field knows about, but doubts the presentations themselves because they are very short and hard to follow.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-changes-frame-3", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: Changes", "title": "Slide 2: Changes", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<ul>\n<li>We will move one additional paper presentation from Monday’s to Thursdays\n<ul>\n<li>More time for main lecture &amp; a break</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>", "text": "We will move one additional paper presentation from Monday’s to Thursdays More time for main lecture & a break", "t": 62}, {"id": "slide-3-projects-key-information-frame-4", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Projects: Key Information", "title": "Slide 3: Projects: Key Information", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<ul>\n<li>5 Projects &amp; competitions, <strong>Demo day on May 21th</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Best team for per project will have potential chance to demo to Yann LeCun, Shuran Song, Jitendra Malik &amp; others on May 29th (https://vreai.ch)</strong></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>NVIDIA supports the course with 12500 H100 hours, ~277 H100 hours per team</li>\n</ul>", "text": "5 Projects & competitions, Demo day on May 21th Best team for per project will have potential chance to demo to Yann LeCun, Shuran Song, Jitendra Malik & others on May 29th (https://vreai.ch) NVIDIA supports the course with 12500 H100 hours, ~277 H100 hours per team", "t": 180}, {"id": "slide-4-project-1-reasoning-pickplace---vla-frame-5", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Project 1: Reasoning Pick&amp;Place - VLA", "title": "Slide 4: Project 1: Reasoning Pick&Place - VLA", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Eval 1(50pts): Pick up a plastic banana and place it into one of 3 bowls in front of the arm</li>\n<li>Eval 2 (50pts): Perform the same task, but the language instruction this time requires your VLA to reason about which bowl to put the banana into (i.e. like “put the banana into the 2 minus 1 bowl from the left”)</li>\n<li>Eval 3 (50pts): Place a coke can on a certain image (you won’t know the images beforehand)</li>\n<li>Bonus (50pts): We give bonus points for the smallest model in number of parameters</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot arm above a table with a coke can and three printed portrait photos, captioned “Move Coke Can near Taylor Swift”.]</span></p>", "text": "Eval 1(50pts): Pick up a plastic banana and place it into one of 3 bowls in front of the arm Eval 2 (50pts): Perform the same task, but the language instruction this time requires your VLA to reason about which bowl to put the banana into (i.e. like “put the banana into the 2 minus 1 bowl from the left”) Eval 3 (50pts): Place a coke can on a certain image (you won’t know the images beforehand) Bonus (50pts): We give bonus points for the smallest model in number of parameters [Figure: a robot arm above a table with a coke can and three printed portrait photos, captioned “Move Coke Can near Taylor Swift”.]", "t": 361}, {"id": "slide-5-project-2-pushing---world-model-frame-6", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Project 2: Pushing - World Model", "title": "Slide 5: Project 2: Pushing - World Model", "frames": [6, 6], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Eval 1 (50pts): Push a given smaller object (TBD which one) in a straight line</li>\n<li>Eval 2 (50pts): Push a given smaller object around an obstacle</li>\n<li>Eval 3 (50pts): Push a new unknown object in a straight line (25pts) and around an obstacle (25pts again)</li>\n<li>Bonus (50pts): Least amount of pushes needed in each of the three challenges (10 pts for least pushes for the first, 10 for the second, 30 for the last, will be divided amont the 10 groups competing).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot pusher above a felt-covered table with three small coloured cubes, captioned “Time Step = 5”.]</span></p>", "text": "Eval 1 (50pts): Push a given smaller object (TBD which one) in a straight line Eval 2 (50pts): Push a given smaller object around an obstacle Eval 3 (50pts): Push a new unknown object in a straight line (25pts) and around an obstacle (25pts again) Bonus (50pts): Least amount of pushes needed in each of the three challenges (10 pts for least pushes for the first, 10 for the second, 30 for the last, will be divided amont the 10 groups competing). [Figure: a robot pusher above a felt-covered table with three small coloured cubes, captioned “Time Step = 5”.]", "t": 420}, {"id": "slide-6-project-3-singulation---reinforcement-learning-frame-7", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Project 3: Singulation - Reinforcement Learning", "title": "Slide 6: Project 3: Singulation - Reinforcement Learning", "frames": [7, 7], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Eval 1 (50pts): Learn a pick an place policy that picks up one wooden block and placing them in one of three bowls laid out in front of the arm (can be just BC).</li>\n<li>Eval 2 (50pts): Singulate the combined four wooden blocks</li>\n<li>Eval 3 (50pts): Singulate more combined blocks.</li>\n<li>Bonus (50pts): Fastest singulating policy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot arm next to a cluster of colourful wooden blocks on a table.]</span></p>", "text": "Eval 1 (50pts): Learn a pick an place policy that picks up one wooden block and placing them in one of three bowls laid out in front of the arm (can be just BC). Eval 2 (50pts): Singulate the combined four wooden blocks Eval 3 (50pts): Singulate more combined blocks. Bonus (50pts): Fastest singulating policy [Figure: a robot arm next to a cluster of colourful wooden blocks on a table.]", "t": 481}, {"id": "slide-7-project-4-keyboard-typing---code-as-policies-frames-8-13", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Project 4: Keyboard Typing - Code as Policies", "title": "Slide 7: Project 4: Keyboard Typing - Code as Policies", "frames": [8, 13], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Design a system (VLM -&gt; keypoints + some classical keypoint grasping policy) that is able to work in unseen eval environment.</li>\n<li>Eval 1 (50pts): Move to four points drawned on a paper sheet.</li>\n<li>Eval 2 (50pts): Pressing defined keys on a laptop keyboard.</li>\n<li>Eval 3 (50pts): Typing words on an unseen laptop keyboard.</li>\n<li>Bonus (50pts): Fastest spelling of words on the keyboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a small yellow 3D-printed robot arm with an orange fingertip over a laptop keyboard, with lab power supplies and an oscilloscope behind it.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Frames 9-13 are pixel-identical repeats of frame 8; the slide stayed on screen.]</span></p>", "text": "Design a system (VLM -> keypoints + some classical keypoint grasping policy) that is able to work in unseen eval environment. Eval 1 (50pts): Move to four points drawned on a paper sheet. Eval 2 (50pts): Pressing defined keys on a laptop keyboard. Eval 3 (50pts): Typing words on an unseen laptop keyboard. Bonus (50pts): Fastest spelling of words on the keyboard. [Figure: a small yellow 3D-printed robot arm with an orange fingertip over a laptop keyboard, with lab power supplies and an oscilloscope behind it.] [Frames 9-13 are pixel-identical repeats of frame 8; the slide stayed on screen.]", "t": 541}, {"id": "slide-8-project-5-cloth-folding---diffusion-policy-frame-14", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Project 5: Cloth Folding - Diffusion Policy", "title": "Slide 8: Project 5: Cloth Folding - Diffusion Policy", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Milestone (50pts): Grab one edge.</li>\n<li>Milestone (50pts): Make one fold.</li>\n<li>Milestone (50pts): Second fold.</li>\n<li>Bonus (50pts): Fold the full cloth &amp; fastest policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a two-armed robot folding a cloth on a table next to a laundry basket, overlay “autonomous, 2x speed”.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 9 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 8 to 10.]</span></p>", "text": "Milestone (50pts): Grab one edge. Milestone (50pts): Make one fold. Milestone (50pts): Second fold. Bonus (50pts): Fold the full cloth & fastest policy. [Figure: a two-armed robot folding a cloth on a table next to a laundry basket, overlay “autonomous, 2x speed”.] [Note: slide 9 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 8 to 10.]", "t": 660}, {"id": "title-shown-again-frame-15", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title <span class=\"own\">[shown again]</span>", "title": "Title [shown again]", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Same title slide as frame 1, shown when the lecture proper starts.]</span></p>", "text": "[Same title slide as frame 1, shown when the lecture proper starts.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-10-world-models-frame-16", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: World Models", "title": "Slide 10: World Models", "frames": [16, 16], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Batters can hit 160km/h balls by anticipating the location</li>\n<li>Our world model allows to decide batting in shorter time than visuals reach our brain</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: illustration of a baseball batter swinging, labelled “Subconscious world model” (brain icon), “Vision delay (100ms)”, “Fastball arrival (400ms)”, “Anticipated location” and “Reflexive action”.]</span></p>", "text": "Batters can hit 160km/h balls by anticipating the location Our world model allows to decide batting in shorter time than visuals reach our brain [Figure: illustration of a baseball batter swinging, labelled “Subconscious world model” (brain icon), “Vision delay (100ms)”, “Fastball arrival (400ms)”, “Anticipated location” and “Reflexive action”.]", "t": 721}, {"id": "slide-11-world-models-vs-policies-frame-17", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: World Models vs Policies", "title": "Slide 11: World Models vs Policies", "frames": [17, 17], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Policies/VLAs are blind to physical causality &amp; temporal dynamics</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy / VLA</th>\n<th>World model</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Given <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> and goal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>, what action should I take?</td>\n<td>Given <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> and action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math>, what happens next?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>inputs: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> (state), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math> (language goal)</td>\n<td>inputs: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> (state), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> (action)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(a_t \\mid s_t, g)</annotation></semantics></math> - policy / VLA</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - world model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> - action to execute</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> - next state</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Policies/VLAs are blind to physical causality & temporal dynamics Policy / VLA World model Given s t and goal g , what action should I take? Given s t and action a t , what happens next? inputs: s t (state), g (language goal) inputs: s t (state), a t (action) π ( a t ∣ s t , g ) - policy / VLA p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) - world model a t - action to execute s t + 1 - next state", "t": 900}, {"id": "slide-12-why-world-models-frame-18", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Why World Models?", "title": "Slide 12: Why World Models?", "frames": [18, 18], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What if we could predict how different sequence of actions would affect the environment?\n<ul>\n<li>Unlock optimizing for the optimal trajectory</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>robot</strong> (obs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <strong>world model</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, learned from data -&gt; “N imagined rollouts”, one marked “best” -&gt; <strong>execute best plan</strong> (real world · 1 trial).]</span></p>", "text": "What if we could predict how different sequence of actions would affect the environment? Unlock optimizing for the optimal trajectory [Figure: robot (obs x t ) -> world model p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) , learned from data -> “N imagined rollouts”, one marked “best” -> execute best plan (real world · 1 trial).]", "t": 900}, {"id": "slide-13-world-models-data-driven-simulators-frame-19", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: World Models: Data-driven Simulators", "title": "Slide 13: World Models: Data-driven Simulators", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Function that predicts the consequences of actions, i.e. a learned simulator</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Traditional simulator</th>\n<th>World model</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>hand-engineered physics engine</strong>: rigid bodies · contact models · friction · collision geometry</td>\n<td><strong>real interaction data</strong>: robot trajectories · human video · internet-scale video</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>simulator</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1} = f(s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> <span class=\"own\">[deterministic, hand-coded]</span></td>\n<td><strong>world model</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> <span class=\"own\">[learned from data]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>predicted next state</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>: accurate only for modelled phenomena</td>\n<td><strong>predicted next state</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>: generalises to anything seen in data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>● fast, repeatable simulation; ✗ requires hand-engineered physics models</td>\n<td>● models anything capturable on video; ● no manual engineering required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Function that predicts the consequences of actions, i.e. a learned simulator Traditional simulator World model hand-engineered physics engine : rigid bodies · contact models · friction · collision geometry real interaction data : robot trajectories · human video · internet-scale video simulator : s t + 1 = f ( s t , a t ) [deterministic, hand-coded] world model : p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) [learned from data] predicted next state s t + 1 : accurate only for modelled phenomena predicted next state s t + 1 : generalises to anything seen in data ● fast, repeatable simulation; ✗ requires hand-engineered physics models ● models anything capturable on video; ● no manual engineering required", "t": 1020}, {"id": "slide-14-world-model-definition-frame-20", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: World Model Definition", "title": "Slide 14: World Model Definition", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Given the current state, an action, and a memory of the past - what happens next?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strictly, <em>video models</em> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">video</mtext><mo>∣</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">text</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\text{video} \\mid \\text{text})</annotation></semantics></math> <em>are not world models</em></li>\n</ul>", "text": "Given the current state, an action, and a memory of the past - what happens next? P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t , h t ) Strictly, video models P ( video ∣ text ) are not world models", "t": 1140}, {"id": "slide-15-pixel-ac-wms-frame-21", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Pixel AC-WMs", "title": "Slide 15: Pixel AC-WMs", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Instead of full video prediction, predict action conditioned flow field that warps <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>F</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>←</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{F}_{t+1 \\leftarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math> current image to next frame</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the CDNA video-prediction network from the paper. RGB input <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>I</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{I}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (64x64x3) -&gt; 5x5 conv 1 (stride 2, 32x32) -&gt; seven 5x5 conv LSTM layers (32 to 128 channels, down to 8x8 then deconvolved back to 32x32); the robot action and state (5 values each) are tiled to 8x8 and concatenated at the bottleneck. A fully connected branch outputs 10 5x5 CDNA kernels <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mover><mi>m</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{m}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> that are convolved with the input image to give 10 transformed images <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mover><mi>J</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{J}_t^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> (64x64x3); a 1x1 conv with channel softmax gives compositing masks <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> (64x64), and masked compositing produces the 64x64 RGB prediction <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>I</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{I}_t</annotation></semantics></math>.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Unsupervised Learning for Physical Interaction through Video Prediction</em>, Finn, Goodfellow, Levine (2016)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 16 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 15 to 17.]</span></p>", "text": "Instead of full video prediction, predict action conditioned flow field that warps F ̂ t + 1 ← t current image to next frame [Figure: the CDNA video-prediction network from the paper. RGB input I ̂ t − 1 (64x64x3) -> 5x5 conv 1 (stride 2, 32x32) -> seven 5x5 conv LSTM layers (32 to 128 channels, down to 8x8 then deconvolved back to 32x32); the robot action and state (5 values each) are tiled to 8x8 and concatenated at the bottleneck. A fully connected branch outputs 10 5x5 CDNA kernels m ̂ ( i ) that are convolved with the input image to give 10 transformed images J ̂ t ( i ) (64x64x3); a 1x1 conv with channel softmax gives compositing masks Ξ (64x64), and masked compositing produces the 64x64 RGB prediction I ̂ t .] Reference: Unsupervised Learning for Physical Interaction through Video Prediction , Finn, Goodfellow, Levine (2016) [Note: slide 16 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 15 to 17.]", "t": 1201}, {"id": "slide-17-pixel-ac-wms-training-and-visual-mpc-frame-22", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Pixel AC-WMs <span class=\"own\">[training and Visual MPC]</span>", "title": "Slide 17: Pixel AC-WMs [training and Visual MPC]", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two panels. <strong>Training</strong>: robot (autonomous interaction) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(o_t, a_t, o_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> (large-scale robot interaction dataset) -&gt; <strong>pixel pred. model</strong> (conv-LSTM, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_2</annotation></semantics></math> loss, pixel warp). “key insight: predict pixel motion, not pixel values: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>o</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>F</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>←</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{o}_{t+1} = \\hat{F}_{t+1 \\leftarrow t} \\circ o_t</annotation></semantics></math> - warp frame by predicted flow field”. <strong>Test time - Visual MPC</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> (current obs) -&gt; sample N action seqs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{a_{t:t+H}\\}</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; roll out pixel preds <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>o</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{o}_{t:t+H}</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; “cost function - choose one”: pixel distance <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|d_t - d_g\\|_2</annotation></semantics></math>, goal image (image registration), or classifier (few-shot success) -&gt; best <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^*</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; execute + replan. “optimiser: Cross-Entropy Method (CEM) - gradient-free; iteratively refit Gaussian to top-k sequences · replan every step”.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Self-supervised training on robot videos</li>\n<li>✅ Generalizes to unseen objects due to motion objective</li>\n<li>❌ Blurry predictions with L2 loss</li>\n<li>❌ Planning in pixel space computationally expensive</li>\n<li>❌ Prediction error accumulates</li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: two panels. Training : robot (autonomous interaction) -> ( o t , a t , o t + 1 ) (large-scale robot interaction dataset) -> pixel pred. model (conv-LSTM, ℓ 2 loss, pixel warp). “key insight: predict pixel motion, not pixel values: o ̂ t + 1 = F ̂ t + 1 ← t ∘ o t - warp frame by predicted flow field”. Test time - Visual MPC : o t (current obs) -> sample N action seqs { a t : t + H } -> roll out pixel preds o ̂ t : t + H -> “cost function - choose one”: pixel distance ∥ d t − d g ∥ 2 , goal image (image registration), or classifier (few-shot success) -> best a * -> execute + replan. “optimiser: Cross-Entropy Method (CEM) - gradient-free; iteratively refit Gaussian to top-k sequences · replan every step”.] ✅ Self-supervised training on robot videos ✅ Generalizes to unseen objects due to motion objective ❌ Blurry predictions with L2 loss ❌ Planning in pixel space computationally expensive ❌ Prediction error accumulates", "t": 1380}, {"id": "slide-18-untitled-transition-question-frame-23", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 18: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<p><strong>What if we compressed observations into a latent space and trained a policy entirely inside that learned world?</strong></p>", "text": "What if we compressed observations into a latent space and trained a policy entirely inside that learned world?", "t": 1501}, {"id": "slide-19-latent-ac-wms-frame-24", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: Latent AC-WMs", "title": "Slide 19: Latent AC-WMs", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<ul>\n<li>By learning a compact, latent world model a policy can be trained entirely in imagination</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Latent AC-WMs - general structure”. environment (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <strong>encoder</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">enc</mtext><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t = \\text{enc}_\\phi(o_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^d</annotation></semantics></math>, compact latent code) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>dynamics model</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{t+1} = f(h_t, z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_{t+1} \\mid z_t, a_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\hat{r}_t \\mid h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t, h_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>policy</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi(z_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, optimised on imagined rewards) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; environment (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_{t+1}, r_t</annotation></semantics></math>); <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> fed back to encoder. Box below: “training the policy in imagination: 1. fix encoder + dynamics model 2. roll out imagined <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_1, \\dots, z_H</annotation></semantics></math> 3. policy acts on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t, h_t</annotation></semantics></math> 4. optimise policy on imagined <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{r}_t</annotation></semantics></math> =&gt; policy improves without real environment interaction”.]</span></p>", "text": "By learning a compact, latent world model a policy can be trained entirely in imagination [Figure: “Latent AC-WMs - general structure”. environment ( o t ) -> encoder ( z t = enc ϕ ( o t ) , z t ∈ ℝ d , compact latent code) -> z t -> dynamics model ( h t + 1 = f ( h t , z t , a t ) ; p ( z t + 1 ∣ z t , a t , h t ) ; p ( r ̂ t ∣ h t , z t ) ) -> z t , h t -> policy ( a t = π ( z t , h t ) , optimised on imagined rewards) -> a t -> environment ( o t + 1 , r t ); o t + 1 fed back to encoder. Box below: “training the policy in imagination: 1. fix encoder + dynamics model 2. roll out imagined z 1 , … , z H 3. policy acts on z t , h t 4. optimise policy on imagined r ̂ t => policy improves without real environment interaction”.]", "t": 1501}, {"id": "slide-20-the-og-latent-ac-wm-frame-25", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: The OG Latent AC-WM", "title": "Slide 20: The OG Latent AC-WM", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the “World Models” agent diagram, unrolled over three time steps: each observation (a first-person game frame) goes through the Vision Model <strong>V</strong> to a latent z; the Memory RNN <strong>M</strong> carries h forward in time; the Controller <strong>C</strong> takes z and h and outputs an action a. Side text from the paper: “At each time step, our agent receives an observation from the environment. World Model: The Vision Model (V) encodes the high-dimensional observation into a low-dimensional latent vector. The Memory RNN (M) integrates the historical codes to create a representation that can predict future states. A small Controller (C) uses the representations from both V and M to select good actions. The agent performs actions that go back and affect the environment.”]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Models</em>, Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)</p>", "text": "[Figure: the “World Models” agent diagram, unrolled over three time steps: each observation (a first-person game frame) goes through the Vision Model V to a latent z; the Memory RNN M carries h forward in time; the Controller C takes z and h and outputs an action a. Side text from the paper: “At each time step, our agent receives an observation from the environment. World Model: The Vision Model (V) encodes the high-dimensional observation into a low-dimensional latent vector. The Memory RNN (M) integrates the historical codes to create a representation that can predict future states. A small Controller (C) uses the representations from both V and M to select good actions. The agent performs actions that go back and affect the environment.”] Reference: World Models , Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)", "t": 1562}, {"id": "slide-21-the-og-latent-ac-wm-visual-model-frame-26", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: The OG Latent AC-WM: Visual Model", "title": "Slide 21: The OG Latent AC-WM: Visual Model", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Learn abstract, compressed representation of each input frame</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Original Observed Frame (a first-person corridor with a fireball) -&gt; <strong>Encoder</strong> -&gt; z -&gt; <strong>Decoder</strong> -&gt; Reconstructed Frame (a blurrier version of the same frame).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Models</em>, Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)</p>", "text": "Learn abstract, compressed representation of each input frame [Figure: Original Observed Frame (a first-person corridor with a fireball) -> Encoder -> z -> Decoder -> Reconstructed Frame (a blurrier version of the same frame).] Reference: World Models , Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)", "t": 1562}, {"id": "slide-22-the-og-latent-ac-wm-memory-model-frame-27", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: The OG Latent AC-WM: Memory Model", "title": "Slide 22: The OG Latent AC-WM: Memory Model", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Compress what happens over time <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z_{t+1} \\mid a_t, z_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li>The MDN output models <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> as a mixture of Gaussians</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an RNN unrolled over three steps; each RNN cell takes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> and the previous <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>, passes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math> forward, and feeds an <strong>MDN</strong> head (with temperature <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>) that outputs the predicted next latent.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Models</em>, Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)</p>", "text": "Compress what happens over time P ( z t + 1 ∣ a t , z t , h t ) The MDN output models P ( z t + 1 ) as a mixture of Gaussians [Figure: an RNN unrolled over three steps; each RNN cell takes a , z and the previous h , passes h forward, and feeds an MDN head (with temperature τ ) that outputs the predicted next latent.] Reference: World Models , Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)", "t": 1620}, {"id": "slide-23-the-og-latent-ac-wm-controller-model-frame-28", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: The OG Latent AC-WM: Controller Model", "title": "Slide 23: The OG Latent AC-WM: Controller Model", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Policy conditions on both the current latent <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> and the memory <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math>, single FC layer <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi(z_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>\n<ul>\n<li>Trained with black-box evolutionary optimisation algorithm (CMA-ES)</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Sample N controllers from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_c(i) \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>For each candidate run dream rollout and collect imagined reward</li>\n<li>Select top rollouts</li>\n<li>Update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> toward the top rollouts</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Models</em>, Ha and Schmidhuber (2018)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 24 and 25 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 26.]</span></p>", "text": "Policy conditions on both the current latent z t and the memory h t , single FC layer a t = π ( z t , h t ) Trained with black-box evolutionary optimisation algorithm (CMA-ES) Sample N controllers from W c ( i ) ∼ 𝒩 ( μ , Σ ) For each candidate run dream rollout and collect imagined reward Select top rollouts Update μ and Σ toward the top rollouts Reference: World Models , Ha and Schmidhuber (2018) [Note: slides 24 and 25 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 26.]", "t": 1741}, {"id": "slide-26-why-imagination-without-priors-drifts-frame-29", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: Why Imagination Without Priors Drifts", "title": "Slide 26: Why Imagination Without Priors Drifts", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<ul>\n<li>RNN is trained on real <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> from VAE, not own predictions</li>\n<li>No principled way to generate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> alone, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two pipelines. “during training - real observation available”: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>VAE</strong> encoder -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>MDN-RNN</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t = f(h_{t-1}, z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <strong>MDN</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_{t+1} \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>controller</strong> (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi(z_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>). “in imagination - no real observation”: the VAE box is crossed out with a “?”; the MDN outputs a hallucinated <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde{z}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> that is fed back into the RNN at the next step and also drives the controller (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi(\\tilde{z}_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>).]</span></p>\n<p><strong>problem</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>VAE is trained separately from the RNN - it encodes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math>, not the model’s own belief</li>\n<li>There is no prior <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math> - without <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> the model has no way to form a belief</li>\n<li><strong>Sampling errors in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde{z}_t</annotation></semantics></math> compound across steps =&gt; imagination drifts from reality</strong></li>\n</ul>", "text": "RNN is trained on real z t from VAE, not own predictions No principled way to generate z t from h t alone, P ( z t ∣ h t ) [Figure: two pipelines. “during training - real observation available”: o t -> VAE encoder -> z t -> MDN-RNN ( h t = f ( h t − 1 , z t , a t ) ) -> MDN ( p ( z t + 1 ∣ h t ) ) -> z t + 1 -> controller ( a t = π ( z t , h t ) ). “in imagination - no real observation”: the VAE box is crossed out with a “?”; the MDN outputs a hallucinated z ̃ t + 1 that is fed back into the RNN at the next step and also drives the controller ( a t = π ( z ̃ t , h t ) ).] problem VAE is trained separately from the RNN - it encodes o t , not the model’s own belief There is no prior p ( z t ∣ h t ) - without o t the model has no way to form a belief Sampling errors in z ̃ t compound across steps => imagination drifts from reality", "t": 1980}, {"id": "slide-27-training-a-recurrent-state-space-model-rssm-frame-30", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Training a Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM)", "title": "Slide 27: Training a Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM)", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an RSSM unrolled over three steps. Top, “deterministic path - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> carries memory (never sampled)”: RNN cells <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t, h_{t+1}, h_{t+2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{t+1} = f(h_t, z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, each taking the action. Under each RNN cell a box: “training - posterior: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(z_t \\mid h_t, o_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{KL} = D_{KL}(q \\| p)</annotation></semantics></math>; imagination - prior: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>”. “stochastic path - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> captures uncertainty about current state”: the sampled <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> feeds the next RNN step and decodes to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>o</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{o}_t</annotation></semantics></math> reconstruction and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{r}_t</annotation></semantics></math> reward (train only), with the observation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> entering the posterior.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>fix 1 - exposure bias</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic - never sampled, never corrupted. Even if <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> drifts, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> remains a clean memory anchor. Errors cannot compound through <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> - reset at every step. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>o</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{o}_t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{r}_t</annotation></semantics></math> decoded from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>: Forces <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> to encode everything needed to reconstruct the world</li>\n<li><strong>fix 2 - missing prior</strong>: Prior <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math> is learned - no observation needed. In imagination, sample <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> from prior conditioned on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math>. The model has an internal compass - grounded in its own memory. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{KL}(q \\| p)</annotation></semantics></math> forces prior to match posterior. Imagination stays close to what training taught the model</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Latent Dynamics for Planning from Pixels</em>, Hafner et al. (2019)</p>", "text": "[Figure: an RSSM unrolled over three steps. Top, “deterministic path - h t carries memory (never sampled)”: RNN cells h t , h t + 1 , h t + 2 with h t + 1 = f ( h t , z t , a t ) , each taking the action. Under each RNN cell a box: “training - posterior: q ( z t ∣ h t , o t ) ; L K L = D K L ( q ∥ p ) ; imagination - prior: p ( z t ∣ h t ) ”. “stochastic path - z t captures uncertainty about current state”: the sampled z t feeds the next RNN step and decodes to o ̂ t reconstruction and r ̂ t reward (train only), with the observation o t entering the posterior.] fix 1 - exposure bias : h t is deterministic - never sampled, never corrupted. Even if z t drifts, h t remains a clean memory anchor. Errors cannot compound through h t - reset at every step. o ̂ t and r ̂ t decoded from ( h t , z t ) : Forces z t to encode everything needed to reconstruct the world fix 2 - missing prior : Prior p ( z t ∣ h t ) is learned - no observation needed. In imagination, sample z t from prior conditioned on h t . The model has an internal compass - grounded in its own memory. D K L ( q ∥ p ) forces prior to match posterior. Imagination stays close to what training taught the model Reference: Learning Latent Dynamics for Planning from Pixels , Hafner et al. (2019)", "t": 2041}, {"id": "slide-28-rssm-closed-loop-inference-imagination-frame-31", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: RSSM Closed-Loop Inference Imagination", "title": "Slide 28: RSSM Closed-Loop Inference Imagination", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same three-step RSSM, now with no observations. Deterministic path: RNN <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> (current state), <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{t+1} = f(h_t, z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, … Under each RNN cell, “prior - imagination: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>”; “stochastic path - <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t \\sim p(z_t \\mid h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, no observation needed”. A <strong>policy</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> box at each step reads <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi(h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which feeds the next RNN step; each step also decodes <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>o</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{o}_t</annotation></semantics></math> reconstruction and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{r}_t</annotation></semantics></math> reward.]</span></p>\n<p><strong>policy + world model - closed-loop imagination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At each step: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> reads <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> - no real robot needed</li>\n<li>World model advances: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{t+1} = f(h_t, z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t+1} \\sim p(z_{t+1} \\mid h_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{r}_t</annotation></semantics></math> accumulated over the rollout -&gt; policy optimised on imagined reward</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Learning Latent Dynamics for Planning from Pixels</em>, Hafner et al. (2019)</p>", "text": "[Figure: the same three-step RSSM, now with no observations. Deterministic path: RNN h t (current state), h t + 1 = f ( h t , z t , a t ) , … Under each RNN cell, “prior - imagination: p ( z t ∣ h t ) ”; “stochastic path - z t ∼ p ( z t ∣ h t ) , no observation needed”. A policy π box at each step reads h t and z t and outputs a t = π ( h t , z t ) , which feeds the next RNN step; each step also decodes o ̂ t reconstruction and r ̂ t reward.] policy + world model - closed-loop imagination At each step: π reads ( h t , z t ) and outputs a t - no real robot needed World model advances: h t + 1 = f ( h t , z t , a t ) , then z t + 1 ∼ p ( z t + 1 ∣ h t + 1 ) r ̂ t accumulated over the rollout -> policy optimised on imagined reward Reference: Learning Latent Dynamics for Planning from Pixels , Hafner et al. (2019)", "t": 2160}, {"id": "slide-29-dreamer-v1-frame-32", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Dreamer V1", "title": "Slide 29: Dreamer V1", "frames": [32, 32], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Why plan from scratch with CEM at every step when you could amortise that planning into a learned policy?</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>PlaNet (Hafner et al., 2019) - World model only, plan at test time with CEM</th>\n<th>Dreamer V1 (Hafner et al., 2020) - World model + actor-critic, policy trained by backprop</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RSSM (world model)</strong>: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{t+1} = f(h_t, z_t, a_t) \\cdot p(z_{t+1} \\mid h_{t+1}) \\cdot p(\\hat{r}_t \\mid h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; trained on real data · ELBO loss · frozen during policy training</td>\n<td><strong>RSSM (world model)</strong>: same; trained on real data · ELBO loss · frozen during policy training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[learned policy box crossed out: actions chosen at test time]</span> <strong>CEM planning (test time)</strong>: 1. sample N action sequences 2. roll out in RSSM imagination 3. keep top-k, refit Gaussian 4. execute best first action, replan</td>\n<td><strong>imagination rollout</strong>: roll out <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z_t, a_t, \\hat{r}_t)_{t=1}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> in latent space; no real environment needed; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-returns for policy gradient; world model frozen during this phase. <strong>actor-critic</strong>: actor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t = \\pi_\\psi(h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; critic <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\xi(h_t, z_t)</annotation></semantics></math>; trained by backprop through frozen RSSM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>training: collect real data -&gt; train RSSM -&gt; repeat</td>\n<td>1. collect real data -&gt; train RSSM (ELBO) 2. freeze RSSM -&gt; train actor-critic in imagination (backprop); alternate between phases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✅ Actions chosen without policy bias; ❌ Planning from scratch at every step CEM</td>\n<td>✅ Backprop through differentiable dynamics enables long-horizon planning; ❌ Policy can exploit world model errors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Reference: <em>Dream to Control: Learning Behaviors By Latent Imagination</em>, Hafner et al. (2020)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32.]</span></p>", "text": "Why plan from scratch with CEM at every step when you could amortise that planning into a learned policy? PlaNet (Hafner et al., 2019) - World model only, plan at test time with CEM Dreamer V1 (Hafner et al., 2020) - World model + actor-critic, policy trained by backprop RSSM (world model) : h t + 1 = f ( h t , z t , a t ) ⋅ p ( z t + 1 ∣ h t + 1 ) ⋅ p ( r ̂ t ∣ h t , z t ) ; trained on real data · ELBO loss · frozen during policy training RSSM (world model) : same; trained on real data · ELBO loss · frozen during policy training [learned policy box crossed out: actions chosen at test time] CEM planning (test time) : 1. sample N action sequences 2. roll out in RSSM imagination 3. keep top-k, refit Gaussian 4. execute best first action, replan imagination rollout : roll out ( z t , a t , r ̂ t ) t = 1 H in latent space; no real environment needed; λ -returns for policy gradient; world model frozen during this phase. actor-critic : actor a t = π ψ ( h t , z t ) ; critic V ξ ( h t , z t ) ; trained by backprop through frozen RSSM training: collect real data -> train RSSM -> repeat 1. collect real data -> train RSSM (ELBO) 2. freeze RSSM -> train actor-critic in imagination (backprop); alternate between phases ✅ Actions chosen without policy bias; ❌ Planning from scratch at every step CEM ✅ Backprop through differentiable dynamics enables long-horizon planning; ❌ Policy can exploit world model errors Reference: Dream to Control: Learning Behaviors By Latent Imagination , Hafner et al. (2020) [Note: slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32.]", "t": 2280}, {"id": "slide-32-dreamer-lineage-frame-33", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Dreamer Lineage", "title": "Slide 32: Dreamer Lineage", "frames": [33, 33], "html": "<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 14%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>World Models (2018)</th>\n<th>PlaNet (2019)</th>\n<th>Dreamer V1 (2020)</th>\n<th>Dreamer V2 (2021)</th>\n<th>Dreamer V3 (2023)</th>\n<th>Dreamer V4 (2025)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Key innovation</td>\n<td>V-M-C decomposition (VAE + MDN-RNN + CMA-ES)</td>\n<td>RSSM (deterministic <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_t</annotation></semantics></math> + stochastic <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>\n<td>Backprop through dynamics (actor-critic replaces CMA-ES)</td>\n<td>Categorical latents (+ KL balancing)</td>\n<td>Symlog + value normalisation (+ fixed hyperparams across all domains)</td>\n<td>Flow matching + transformer KV (+ unlabeled video pretraining)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Headline result</td>\n<td>First agent trained purely in imagination</td>\n<td>200x more sample-efficient than model-free methods</td>\n<td>Outperforms model-free RL on DMControl</td>\n<td>First world model to match DQN on Atari</td>\n<td>First to obtain diamonds in Minecraft from pixels</td>\n<td>Diamonds from offline data only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detail</td>\n<td>Car racing solved from pixels</td>\n<td>CEM planning in latent space</td>\n<td>Policy learned entirely in imagination</td>\n<td>Human-level on 45 of 55 Atari games</td>\n<td>~20,000 actions from raw pixels. Same hyperparams: Atari, robotics, DMControl</td>\n<td>20,000+ actions - no env. interaction. Real-time on single GPU · learns from video</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "World Models (2018) PlaNet (2019) Dreamer V1 (2020) Dreamer V2 (2021) Dreamer V3 (2023) Dreamer V4 (2025) Key innovation V-M-C decomposition (VAE + MDN-RNN + CMA-ES) RSSM (deterministic h t + stochastic z t ) Backprop through dynamics (actor-critic replaces CMA-ES) Categorical latents (+ KL balancing) Symlog + value normalisation (+ fixed hyperparams across all domains) Flow matching + transformer KV (+ unlabeled video pretraining) Headline result First agent trained purely in imagination 200x more sample-efficient than model-free methods Outperforms model-free RL on DMControl First world model to match DQN on Atari First to obtain diamonds in Minecraft from pixels Diamonds from offline data only Detail Car racing solved from pixels CEM planning in latent space Policy learned entirely in imagination Human-level on 45 of 55 Atari games ~20,000 actions from raw pixels. Same hyperparams: Atari, robotics, DMControl 20,000+ actions - no env. interaction. Real-time on single GPU · learns from video", "t": 2522}, {"id": "slide-33-dreamer-v4-decoupling-videos-actions-frames-34-35", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Dreamer V4: Decoupling Videos &amp; Actions", "title": "Slide 33: Dreamer V4: Decoupling Videos & Actions", "frames": [34, 35], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Pretrain dynamics model on <em>unlabeled videos</em> via shortcut forcing/flow matching <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_{t+1} \\mid z_{\\le t})</annotation></semantics></math>, finetune <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z_{t+1} \\mid z_{\\le t}, a_{\\le t})</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{\\le t}</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> Memory: Replace RNN hidden states with Transformer KV cache</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learns MineCraft Diamonds, 20000 decisions, <strong>purely offline</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded Minecraft gameplay video (crafting-table and furnace menus, timestamps “1 min” and “3 min”); right, “Works on offline dataset from my Soar paper”: three frames of a robot arm putting objects (a red block, a green sponge) into a bowl on a table.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Agents Inside of Scalable World Models</em>, Hafner et al. (2025)</p>", "text": "Pretrain dynamics model on unlabeled videos via shortcut forcing/flow matching p ( z t + 1 ∣ z ≤ t ) , finetune p ( z t + 1 ∣ z ≤ t , a ≤ t ) [arrow at z ≤ t ] Memory: Replace RNN hidden states with Transformer KV cache Learns MineCraft Diamonds, 20000 decisions, purely offline [Figure: embedded Minecraft gameplay video (crafting-table and furnace menus, timestamps “1 min” and “3 min”); right, “Works on offline dataset from my Soar paper”: three frames of a robot arm putting objects (a red block, a green sponge) into a bowl on a table.] Reference: Training Agents Inside of Scalable World Models , Hafner et al. (2025)", "t": 2581}, {"id": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-36", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<p>Dreamer’s latents are domain specific. What if we want to scale the videos to all of Internet?</p>\n<p>-&gt; <strong>Generative Video Models</strong></p>", "text": "Dreamer’s latents are domain specific. What if we want to scale the videos to all of Internet? -> Generative Video Models", "t": 2702}, {"id": "slide-35-naïve-video-tokenization-frame-37", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: Naïve Video Tokenization", "title": "Slide 35: Naïve Video Tokenization", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Per-frame ViT produces too many tokens</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: pipeline. One frame <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> (256x256 pixels) -&gt; <strong>ViT Encoder</strong> (per-frame, no temporal; 16x16 patches, each -&gt; d-dim vector) -&gt; tokens per frame: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>256</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mn>256</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{256}{16} \\times \\frac{256}{16} = 256</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per frame -&gt; x number of frames: 20 FPS x 10 seconds = 200 frames (short clip!) -&gt; <strong>context explosion</strong>: total context length <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>51</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">256 \\times 200 = 51{,}200</annotation></semantics></math> tokens for a 10-second clip. “GPT-4: 128K limit total!”]</span></p>", "text": "Per-frame ViT produces too many tokens [Figure: pipeline. One frame o t (256x256 pixels) -> ViT Encoder (per-frame, no temporal; 16x16 patches, each -> d-dim vector) -> tokens per frame: 256 16 × 256 16 = 256 tokens per frame -> x number of frames: 20 FPS x 10 seconds = 200 frames (short clip!) -> context explosion : total context length 256 × 200 = 51 , 200 tokens for a 10-second clip. “GPT-4: 128K limit total!”]", "t": 2822}, {"id": "slide-36-video-tokenization-compression-frame-38", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Video Tokenization Compression", "title": "Slide 36: Video Tokenization Compression", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Axis 1 - Spatial Compression</th>\n<th>Axis 2 - Temporal Compression</th>\n<th>Axis 3 - Adaptive Compression</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>deeper encoder -&gt; fewer tokens per frame</td>\n<td>merge frames -&gt; fewer time steps</td>\n<td>variable budget -&gt; complex frames get more tokens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[naive: 16 patches -&gt; 256 tokens/frame; compressed: 4 patches -&gt; 4 tokens/frame]</span></td>\n<td>[8 frames <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_{t+0} \\dots o_{t+3}</annotation></semantics></math> merged into 1 token <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math>]</td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[four frames with token budgets that grow with frame complexity: “token budget allocated proportionally to frame complexity”]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>mechanism</strong>: VQ-VAE / VQGAN encoder with stride; 8x spatial: 256x256 -&gt; 4x4 tokens. <strong>examples</strong>: VQGAN · SD-VAE · Cosmos CV8x8x8. causal? yes - no temporal dependency</td>\n<td><strong>Tubelets</strong>: 3D patch (t,h,w); full tube at once; non-causal (TimeSformer · ViViT; Cosmos (analysis)). <strong>Causal Aggregation</strong>: attend past only; frame-by-frame decode; causal (Dreamer V4 tokenizer; Cosmos world model)</td>\n<td><strong>mechanism</strong>: tail-drop masking during training; tokens ordered by information content. <strong>examples</strong>: ElasticTok (Yan et al., ICLR 2025). causal? yes - works per-frame with context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>256 -&gt; 4 tokens / frame: 64x spatial reduction</td>\n<td>200 -&gt; 25 time steps: 8x temporal reduction</td>\n<td>variable tokens per frame: 2-5x reduction on average</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Combined (Cosmos)</strong>: spatial 8x + temporal 8x = 512x compression -&gt; 51,200 tokens becomes 100 tokens for a 10-sec clip. <em>Axes are independent and composable · causality requirement determines which temporal method to use</em></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: as written, the numbers do not agree. The column footers give 64x spatial (256 -&gt; 4 tokens per frame) and 8x temporal; the combined line says 8x spatial x 8x temporal = 512x. 51,200 / 512 = 100 tokens is consistent only with the combined line.]</span></p>", "text": "Axis 1 - Spatial Compression Axis 2 - Temporal Compression Axis 3 - Adaptive Compression deeper encoder -> fewer tokens per frame merge frames -> fewer time steps variable budget -> complex frames get more tokens [naive: 16 patches -> 256 tokens/frame; compressed: 4 patches -> 4 tokens/frame] [8 frames o t + 0 … o t + 3 merged into 1 token z t ] [four frames with token budgets that grow with frame complexity: “token budget allocated proportionally to frame complexity”] mechanism : VQ-VAE / VQGAN encoder with stride; 8x spatial: 256x256 -> 4x4 tokens. examples : VQGAN · SD-VAE · Cosmos CV8x8x8. causal? yes - no temporal dependency Tubelets : 3D patch (t,h,w); full tube at once; non-causal (TimeSformer · ViViT; Cosmos (analysis)). Causal Aggregation : attend past only; frame-by-frame decode; causal (Dreamer V4 tokenizer; Cosmos world model) mechanism : tail-drop masking during training; tokens ordered by information content. examples : ElasticTok (Yan et al., ICLR 2025). causal? yes - works per-frame with context 256 -> 4 tokens / frame: 64x spatial reduction 200 -> 25 time steps: 8x temporal reduction variable tokens per frame: 2-5x reduction on average Combined (Cosmos) : spatial 8x + temporal 8x = 512x compression -> 51,200 tokens becomes 100 tokens for a 10-sec clip. Axes are independent and composable · causality requirement determines which temporal method to use [Note: as written, the numbers do not agree. The column footers give 64x spatial (256 -> 4 tokens per frame) and 8x temporal; the combined line says 8x spatial x 8x temporal = 512x. 51,200 / 512 = 100 tokens is consistent only with the combined line.]", "t": 2941}, {"id": "slide-37-video-world-model-where-do-actions-live-frame-39", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: Video World Model: Where Do Actions Live?", "title": "Slide 37: Video World Model: Where Do Actions Live?", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action-Conditioned World Models (AC-WMs)</th>\n<th>vs</th>\n<th>Video-Based Action Prediction Models</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>actions in · future states out</em></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><em>pretrain on video · actions predicted from generated frames</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[observations + future actions -&gt; <strong>World Model</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; future states; “[observations + actions] -&gt; [<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>]”]</span></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>WAM - World Action Model</strong>: <em>single model - joint video + action prediction</em>. <span class=\"own\">[image + text -&gt; <strong>Joint Model</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">text</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(o_{t:t+H}, a_{t:t+H} \\mid \\text{text}, o_{0:t}, q_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; video and actions]</span> example: DreamZero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>what it does</strong>: given current obs + planned actions, simulate what the world will look like; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> is an input - conditions future prediction. examples: Dreamer V1-V4 · DreamDojo · V-JEPA2</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>VAM - Video Action Model</strong>: <em>frozen video backbone · lightweight policy head</em>. <span class=\"own\">[image + text -&gt; <strong>Video Backbone</strong> (frozen, pretrained on internet video) -&gt; video features -&gt; <strong>IDM</strong> (robot data only) -&gt; actions]</span> example: mimic-video</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Action-Conditioned World Models (AC-WMs) vs Video-Based Action Prediction Models actions in · future states out pretrain on video · actions predicted from generated frames [observations + future actions -> World Model p ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t ) -> future states; “[observations + actions] -> [ s t + 1 ]”] WAM - World Action Model : single model - joint video + action prediction . [image + text -> Joint Model p ( o t : t + H , a t : t + H ∣ text , o 0 : t , q t ) -> video and actions] example: DreamZero what it does : given current obs + planned actions, simulate what the world will look like; a t is an input - conditions future prediction. examples: Dreamer V1-V4 · DreamDojo · V-JEPA2 VAM - Video Action Model : frozen video backbone · lightweight policy head . [image + text -> Video Backbone (frozen, pretrained on internet video) -> video features -> IDM (robot data only) -> actions] example: mimic-video", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-38-untitled-transition-question-frame-40", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 38: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<p>Didn’t we say video is expensive? <strong>Why use video backbones?</strong></p>", "text": "Didn’t we say video is expensive? Why use video backbones?", "t": 3300}, {"id": "slide-39-vlas-poor-sample-efficiency-frame-41", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: VLAs: Poor Sample Efficiency", "title": "Slide 39: VLAs: Poor Sample Efficiency", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>VLMs are often trained on static images &amp; text, blind to physical causality and temporal dynamics\n<ul>\n<li>Larg-scale teleop data necessary to convert a VLM to VLA</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Image-Text Pairs (a photo, “Person cutting carrots”) -&gt; <strong>VLA</strong> box: <strong>VLM</strong> (Semantics) -&gt; Large Scale Robotics Data -&gt; robot arm; “❌ Expensive Post-Training”; “Learn: Dynamics+Control”.]</span></p>\n<p>Figure designed by Oier Mees for: mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs</p>", "text": "VLMs are often trained on static images & text, blind to physical causality and temporal dynamics Larg-scale teleop data necessary to convert a VLM to VLA [Figure: Image-Text Pairs (a photo, “Person cutting carrots”) -> VLA box: VLM (Semantics) -> Large Scale Robotics Data -> robot arm; “❌ Expensive Post-Training”; “Learn: Dynamics+Control”.] Figure designed by Oier Mees for: mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs", "t": 3300}, {"id": "slide-40-video-backbones-frame-42", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: Video Backbones", "title": "Slide 40: Video Backbones", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Offload dynamics learning to scalable action-free video data</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Video-Text Pairs (a stack of video frames, “Person cutting carrots”) -&gt; <strong>Video Model</strong> (Semantics + Visual Dynamics) -&gt; Small Scale Robotics Data -&gt; robot arm; “✅ Efficient Post-Training”; “Learn: Control”.]</span></p>\n<p>Figure designed by Oier Mees for: mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs</p>", "text": "Offload dynamics learning to scalable action-free video data [Figure: Video-Text Pairs (a stack of video frames, “Person cutting carrots”) -> Video Model (Semantics + Visual Dynamics) -> Small Scale Robotics Data -> robot arm; “✅ Efficient Post-Training”; “Learn: Control”.] Figure designed by Oier Mees for: mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs", "t": 3300}, {"id": "slide-41-video-action-models-frame-43", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: Video-Action Models", "title": "Slide 41: Video-Action Models", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Key: leverage <strong>generative video models</strong> as backbone</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the two pipelines from slides 39 and 40 stacked: <strong>VLA</strong> (VLM, large-scale robotics data, expensive post-training, learn dynamics + control) above <strong>Video-Action Model (VAM)</strong> (video model, small-scale robotics data, efficient post-training, learn control) -&gt; “Dexterous &amp; Generalizable Manipulation” (robot photos). A plot of success rate against robot data quantity (2%, 10%, 50%, 100%): the Video-Action Model (ours) starts near 0.75 at 2% and reaches about 0.9; the Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA) rises from about 0.3 at 2% to about 0.85 at 100%; an arrow marks “10x Sample-Efficiency”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs</em>, Pai*, Achenbach*, Montesinos, Forrai, <strong>Mees*</strong>, Nava*. arxiv, 2025.</p>", "text": "Key: leverage generative video models as backbone [Figure: the two pipelines from slides 39 and 40 stacked: VLA (VLM, large-scale robotics data, expensive post-training, learn dynamics + control) above Video-Action Model (VAM) (video model, small-scale robotics data, efficient post-training, learn control) -> “Dexterous & Generalizable Manipulation” (robot photos). A plot of success rate against robot data quantity (2%, 10%, 50%, 100%): the Video-Action Model (ours) starts near 0.75 at 2% and reaches about 0.9; the Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA) rises from about 0.3 at 2% to about 0.85 at 100%; an arrow marks “10x Sample-Efficiency”.] Reference: mimic-video: Video-Action Models for Generalizable Robot Control Beyond VLAs , Pai*, Achenbach*, Montesinos, Forrai, Mees* , Nava*. arxiv, 2025.", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-42-policy-performance-scales-with-video-model-frames-44-45", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Policy Performance Scales with Video Model", "title": "Slide 42: Policy Performance Scales with Video Model", "frames": [44, 45], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: bar chart of Success Rate (0-1.0) by Action Decoder Input, for a Pretrained Video Model (grey) and a Finetuned Video Model (orange). With Predicted Video: pretrained about 0.03, finetuned about 0.48. With Expert Video: both 1.0. An arrow runs from the pretrained predicted-video bar through the finetuned one up to the expert-video bars.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>✅ Policy achieves perfect performance when conditioned on ground truth video</li>\n<li>✅ Finetuning a video model on robot data improves video prediction quality and thus policy performance</li>\n<li>✅ Policy performance scales with better video models</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Frame 44 shows only the Expert Video bars and the first point; frame 45 is the full build.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: bar chart of Success Rate (0-1.0) by Action Decoder Input, for a Pretrained Video Model (grey) and a Finetuned Video Model (orange). With Predicted Video: pretrained about 0.03, finetuned about 0.48. With Expert Video: both 1.0. An arrow runs from the pretrained predicted-video bar through the finetuned one up to the expert-video bars.] ✅ Policy achieves perfect performance when conditioned on ground truth video ✅ Finetuning a video model on robot data improves video prediction quality and thus policy performance ✅ Policy performance scales with better video models [Frame 44 shows only the Expert Video bars and the first point; frame 45 is the full build.]", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-43-mimic-video-frames-46-47", "num": 43, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 43: mimic-video", "title": "Slide 43: mimic-video", "frames": [46, 47], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the mimic-video architecture. A <strong>Language Model</strong> (with a Language Encoder for the instruction “put the package on the conveyor belt”) and a <strong>Video Model</strong> (inputs: Video Input, a camera frame of two robot arms at a conveyor belt; Partial Video Prediction; “Video Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_v</annotation></semantics></math>” schedule; “Repeat”). In the second build an <strong>Action Decoder</strong> (“Action Noise <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_a</annotation></semantics></math>”, “Repeat”) is added, outputting a row of robot actions.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>mimic-video first partially denoises future video via flow matching</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[second build, frame 47:]</span> Then, video model activations condition a light-weight flow matching action decoder</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 44 and 45 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 43 to 46.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: the mimic-video architecture. A Language Model (with a Language Encoder for the instruction “put the package on the conveyor belt”) and a Video Model (inputs: Video Input, a camera frame of two robot arms at a conveyor belt; Partial Video Prediction; “Video Noise τ v ” schedule; “Repeat”). In the second build an Action Decoder (“Action Noise τ a ”, “Repeat”) is added, outputting a row of robot actions.] mimic-video first partially denoises future video via flow matching [second build, frame 47:] Then, video model activations condition a light-weight flow matching action decoder [Note: slides 44 and 45 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 43 to 46.]", "t": 3721}, {"id": "slide-46-joint-video-action-sampling-frame-48", "num": 46, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 46: Joint Video-Action Sampling", "title": "Slide 46: Joint Video-Action Sampling", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Autonomous Policy Execution</th>\n<th>Video Generation (skipped in regular policy inference)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two robot arms with humanoid hands handing a package over a conveyor belt; overlay “real autonomous”]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the model’s generated video of the same scene, blurrier, with a person walking past; overlay “video generation”]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Training data: 512 episodes (2x speed)</p>", "text": "Autonomous Policy Execution Video Generation (skipped in regular policy inference) [Figure: two robot arms with humanoid hands handing a package over a conveyor belt; overlay “real autonomous”] [Figure: the model’s generated video of the same scene, blurrier, with a person walking past; overlay “video generation”] Training data: 512 episodes (2x speed)", "t": 3781}, {"id": "slide-47-world-action-models-dreamzero-frame-49", "num": 47, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 47: World Action Models: DreamZero", "title": "Slide 47: World Action Models: DreamZero", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Joint video-action prediction, no explicit IDM</li>\n<li>Autoregressive video chunk generation, faster KV cache inference with 14B model</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two panels around a <strong>Joint Video-Action DiT</strong> made of Causal DiT Blocks. <strong>Training: Joint Video-Action Flow Matching</strong>: video frames go through a VAE Encoder to latents and actions through an Action Encoder, both noised; proprioception and language (“Pack up the fruits into the bag”) go through a State/Text Encoder; the DiT is trained with joint flow matching (teacher forcing). <strong>Inference: Closed-Loop Real World Execution</strong>: past frames (VAE Encoder), proprioception and language feed the DiT, which uses a KV Cache and autoregressive flow sampling to output future frames (VAE Decoder) and a future action chunk (Action Decoder) for async real-world execution; the scene is updated with real observations.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies</em>, Ye et al. arxiv, 2026.</p>", "text": "Joint video-action prediction, no explicit IDM Autoregressive video chunk generation, faster KV cache inference with 14B model [Figure: two panels around a Joint Video-Action DiT made of Causal DiT Blocks. Training: Joint Video-Action Flow Matching : video frames go through a VAE Encoder to latents and actions through an Action Encoder, both noised; proprioception and language (“Pack up the fruits into the bag”) go through a State/Text Encoder; the DiT is trained with joint flow matching (teacher forcing). Inference: Closed-Loop Real World Execution : past frames (VAE Encoder), proprioception and language feed the DiT, which uses a KV Cache and autoregressive flow sampling to output future frames (VAE Decoder) and a future action chunk (Action Decoder) for async real-world execution; the scene is updated with real observations.] Reference: World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies , Ye et al. arxiv, 2026.", "t": 3781}, {"id": "slide-49-shown-early-wams-vs-vams-frame-50", "num": 49, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 49 <span class=\"own\">[shown early]</span>: WAMs vs VAMs", "title": "Slide 49 [shown early] : WAMs vs VAMs", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Same slide as frame 52; the lecturer showed it before going back to slide 48.]</span></p>", "text": "[Same slide as frame 52; the lecturer showed it before going back to slide 48.]", "t": 3901}, {"id": "slide-48-world-action-models-dreamzero-results-frame-51", "num": 48, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 48: World Action Models: DreamZero <span class=\"own\">[results]</span>", "title": "Slide 48: World Action Models: DreamZero [results]", "frames": [51, 51], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Strong generalization and few-shot adaptation capabilities</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: four video stills of a humanoid robot (autonomous, 4x or 8x speed), each with its instruction and a success mark:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick up the red apple on the table and place it on the gray towel. ✅</li>\n<li>Fold the bottom of the green short sleeve to the middle. Then, pull the shirt toward the edge of the table. Next, fold the top of the shirt down to the middle. Finally, grasp the collar and folds it down. ⚠️</li>\n<li>Untie the knot of the shoelace. ✅</li>\n<li>Pick up the marker and draw a circle on the book. ⚠️</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies</em>, Ye et al. arxiv, 2026.</p>", "text": "Strong generalization and few-shot adaptation capabilities [Figure: four video stills of a humanoid robot (autonomous, 4x or 8x speed), each with its instruction and a success mark:] Pick up the red apple on the table and place it on the gray towel. ✅ Fold the bottom of the green short sleeve to the middle. Then, pull the shirt toward the edge of the table. Next, fold the top of the shirt down to the middle. Finally, grasp the collar and folds it down. ⚠️ Untie the knot of the shoelace. ✅ Pick up the marker and draw a circle on the book. ⚠️ Reference: World Action Models are Zero-shot Policies , Ye et al. arxiv, 2026.", "t": 3901}, {"id": "slide-49-wams-vs-vams-frame-52", "num": 49, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 49: WAMs vs VAMs", "title": "Slide 49: WAMs vs VAMs", "frames": [52, 52], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>WAMs (DreamZero, mimic-video)</th>\n<th>AC-WMs (Dreamer, DreamDojo)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>− instruction-labeled only - hard to use play data or failure trajectories at scale</td>\n<td>+ all robot data: play, failures, policy rollouts - easier to scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-embodiment</td>\n<td>+ easy - inputs/outputs embodiment-agnostic; only action decoder needs robot data</td>\n<td>− open challenge - each robot has its own action space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-training preservation</td>\n<td>+ small gap from pre-trained video model; preserves general visual capabilities</td>\n<td>− action conditioning changes input distribution; may destroy pre-trained abilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beyond imitation</td>\n<td>− BC paradigm only - instructions -&gt; actions; no RL or counterfactual simulation</td>\n<td>+ RL in imagination + fine-grained gradient-based planning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Planning</td>\n<td>+ best-of-N via diverse text prompts; easy action proposal generation</td>\n<td>+ fine-grained gradient-based optimization of action sequences at inference time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the title says “WAMs vs VAMs”, but the table compares WAMs (with mimic-video, a VAM, listed under them) against AC-WMs.]</span></p>", "text": "Dimension WAMs (DreamZero, mimic-video) AC-WMs (Dreamer, DreamDojo) Data − instruction-labeled only - hard to use play data or failure trajectories at scale + all robot data: play, failures, policy rollouts - easier to scale Cross-embodiment + easy - inputs/outputs embodiment-agnostic; only action decoder needs robot data − open challenge - each robot has its own action space Pre-training preservation + small gap from pre-trained video model; preserves general visual capabilities − action conditioning changes input distribution; may destroy pre-trained abilities Beyond imitation − BC paradigm only - instructions -> actions; no RL or counterfactual simulation + RL in imagination + fine-grained gradient-based planning Planning + best-of-N via diverse text prompts; easy action proposal generation + fine-grained gradient-based optimization of action sequences at inference time [Note: the title says “WAMs vs VAMs”, but the table compares WAMs (with mimic-video, a VAM, listed under them) against AC-WMs.]", "t": 3962}, {"id": "slide-50-untitled-transition-question-frame-53", "num": 50, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 50: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 50: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [53, 53], "html": "<p>All approaches so far had a decoder to reconstruct or generate pixels at some point</p>\n<p><strong>Do you even need to predict pixels at all?</strong></p>", "text": "All approaches so far had a decoder to reconstruct or generate pixels at some point Do you even need to predict pixels at all?", "t": 4021}, {"id": "slide-51-joint-embedding-predictive-architecture-jepa-frame-54", "num": 51, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 51: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA)", "title": "Slide 51: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA)", "frames": [54, 54], "html": "<ul>\n<li>What if we don’t predict pixels or tokens at all?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “JEPA - Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture: predict in latent space - no decoder, no pixel reconstruction”. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>Encoder</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">enc</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t = \\text{enc}(o_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>Predictor</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">pred</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{z}_{t+1} = \\text{pred}(z_t, a_t)</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{z}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (predicted). <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <strong>Same encoder</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">enc</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t+1} = \\text{enc}(o_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (target). Loss <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><msub><mover><mi>z</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|\\hat{z}_{t+1} - z_{t+1}\\|^2</annotation></semantics></math>. Side boxes: “No Decoder: no pixel reconstruction”; “Collapse risk: enc maps all frames to same vector: MSE = 0 trivially”.]</span></p>\n<p><strong>How to prevent collapse - three strategies</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>EMA Target Encoder</th>\n<th>Frozen Pretrained Encoder</th>\n<th>Gaussian Regularisation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target enc = slow-moving copy (momentum update). Breaks gradient symmetry</td>\n<td>Encoder fixed (e.g. DINO). Collapse impossible - encoder never changes</td>\n<td>Force latent distribution to be Gaussian. Provably prevents collapse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e.g. I-JEPA, V-JEPA 2</td>\n<td>e.g. DINO-WM</td>\n<td>e.g. LeWorldModel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>not fully end-to-end / requires tuning</td>\n<td>no end-to-end learning</td>\n<td>fully end-to-end / one hyperparameter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "What if we don’t predict pixels or tokens at all? [Figure: “JEPA - Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture: predict in latent space - no decoder, no pixel reconstruction”. o t -> Encoder z t = enc ( o t ) -> Predictor z ̂ t + 1 = pred ( z t , a t ) -> z ̂ t + 1 (predicted). o t + 1 -> Same encoder z t + 1 = enc ( o t + 1 ) -> z t + 1 (target). Loss ∥ z ̂ t + 1 − z t + 1 ∥ 2 . Side boxes: “No Decoder: no pixel reconstruction”; “Collapse risk: enc maps all frames to same vector: MSE = 0 trivially”.] How to prevent collapse - three strategies EMA Target Encoder Frozen Pretrained Encoder Gaussian Regularisation Target enc = slow-moving copy (momentum update). Breaks gradient symmetry Encoder fixed (e.g. DINO). Collapse impossible - encoder never changes Force latent distribution to be Gaussian. Provably prevents collapse e.g. I-JEPA, V-JEPA 2 e.g. DINO-WM e.g. LeWorldModel not fully end-to-end / requires tuning no end-to-end learning fully end-to-end / one hyperparameter", "t": 4141}, {"id": "slide-52-conclusion-frame-55", "num": 52, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 52: Conclusion", "title": "Slide 52: Conclusion", "frames": [55, 55], "html": "<p>One master formula <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(s_{t+1} \\mid s_t, a_t, h_t)</annotation></semantics></math>, five different approaches</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%;\">\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 16%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_t</annotation></semantics></math> (state repr.)</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t</annotation></semantics></math> role</th>\n<th>Planning</th>\n<th>Data</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pixel AC-WMs</td>\n<td>pixels <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_t</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>input - conditions future frame</td>\n<td>visual MPC (CEM)</td>\n<td>robot data + action labels</td>\n<td>Finn 2016, Visual Foresight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latent AC-WMs</td>\n<td>compact latent <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>input - conditions latent dynamics</td>\n<td>RL in imagination, Dreamer-style</td>\n<td>robot data + action labels</td>\n<td>Dreamer V1-V4, DayDreamer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WAMs</td>\n<td>video tokens (full frames)</td>\n<td>output - jointly predicted</td>\n<td>generate video + extract actions</td>\n<td>internet video + robot fine-tune</td>\n<td>DreamZero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VAMs</td>\n<td>video tokens (frozen backbone)</td>\n<td>output - IDM reads from video</td>\n<td>generate latent video + IDM</td>\n<td>internet video (backbone) + robot data (IDM)</td>\n<td>mimic-video</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JEPA</td>\n<td>latent <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t</annotation></semantics></math> (no decoder ever)</td>\n<td>input - conditions latent predictor</td>\n<td>latent MPC, no pixels needed</td>\n<td>robot data + action labels</td>\n<td>LeWorldModel, V-JEPA 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Open questions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Will flexible conditioning win?</strong> one model on text OR actions - best of both worlds</li>\n<li><strong>Pixels or latents?</strong> does pixel prediction help cross-embodiment, or is it unnecessary cost?</li>\n<li><strong>Can JEPA scale?</strong> V-JEPA 2 shows promise - can latent WMs match generative ones at scale?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "One master formula P ( s t + 1 ∣ s t , a t , h t ) , five different approaches Family s t (state repr.) a t role Planning Data Example Pixel AC-WMs pixels o t input - conditions future frame visual MPC (CEM) robot data + action labels Finn 2016, Visual Foresight Latent AC-WMs compact latent z t input - conditions latent dynamics RL in imagination, Dreamer-style robot data + action labels Dreamer V1-V4, DayDreamer WAMs video tokens (full frames) output - jointly predicted generate video + extract actions internet video + robot fine-tune DreamZero VAMs video tokens (frozen backbone) output - IDM reads from video generate latent video + IDM internet video (backbone) + robot data (IDM) mimic-video JEPA latent z t (no decoder ever) input - conditions latent predictor latent MPC, no pixels needed robot data + action labels LeWorldModel, V-JEPA 2 Open questions Will flexible conditioning win? one model on text OR actions - best of both worlds Pixels or latents? does pixel prediction help cross-embodiment, or is it unnecessary cost? Can JEPA scale? V-JEPA 2 shows promise - can latent WMs match generative ones at scale?", "t": 4141}, {"id": "end-frame-56", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [56, 56], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "slide-8-project-5-cloth-folding---diffusion-policy-frame-14", "label": "Slide 8: Project 5: Cloth Folding - Diffusion Policy", "html": "slide 9 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 8 to 10."}, {"slide": "slide-15-pixel-ac-wms-frame-21", "label": "Slide 15: Pixel AC-WMs", "html": "slide 16 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 15 to 17."}, {"slide": "slide-23-the-og-latent-ac-wm-controller-model-frame-28", "label": "Slide 23: The OG Latent AC-WM: Controller Model", "html": "slides 24 and 25 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 26."}, {"slide": "slide-29-dreamer-v1-frame-32", "label": "Slide 29: Dreamer V1", "html": "slides 30 and 31 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 29 to 32."}, {"slide": "slide-36-video-tokenization-compression-frame-38", "label": "Slide 36: Video Tokenization Compression", "html": "as written, the numbers do not agree. The column footers give 64x spatial (256 -&gt; 4 tokens per frame) and 8x temporal; the combined line says 8x spatial x 8x temporal = 512x. 51,200 / 512 = 100 tokens is consistent only with the combined line."}, {"slide": "slide-43-mimic-video-frames-46-47", "label": "Slide 43: mimic-video", "html": "slides 44 and 45 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 43 to 46."}, {"slide": "slide-49-wams-vs-vams-frame-52", "label": "Slide 49: WAMs vs VAMs", "html": "the title says “WAMs vs VAMs”, but the table compares WAMs (with mimic-video, a VAM, listed under them) against AC-WMs."}]}, {"key": "09", "dir": "week09_generalist_policies", "date": "Mon 27 Apr 2026", "topic": "Generalist Robot Policies", "summary": "How to build a generalist policy: aggregate existing robot data (Open X-Embodiment), design for heterogeneous sensors and actions (Octo's tokenizers and readout tokens, CrossFormer across robots), start from internet-scale VLMs (VLAs), and evaluate cheaply in simulation that correlates with the real world (SIMPLER). Includes practical training tips: convergence metrics, batching mixed datasets, sequence packing, data loading.", "key_ideas": ["Pooling 60 datasets from 34 labs beat each lab's own specialist method on average", "Readout tokens let new action heads and observation types be attached at fine-tuning time", "A VLA reuses the VLM's vocabulary for 256 action bins", "Current recipe: next-token prediction on FAST actions + web data, plus a flow-matching action expert behind a stop-gradient", "Real-to-sim evaluation works when control dynamics (SysID) and visuals are matched"], "papers": [{"title": "Language Conditioned Imitation Learning...", "who": "Lynch et al., 2021", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.07648"}, {"title": "A Generalist Agent (Gato)", "who": "Reed et al., 2022", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.06175"}, {"title": "pi*0.6: a VLA That Learns From Experience", "who": "Physical Intelligence, 2025", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14759"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "dtofzDY9zuo", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 9: Generalist Robot Policies | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "1:03:28", "description": "Lecture 9 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, welcome everybody. Um very happy to um uh talk today about uh generalist uh robot policies. Um also, um this is uh a topic where I have also myself done a bit of research over the last years. Um so, very uh excited to share um some insights here and also uh hopefully um some lessons that uh we've learned along the way uh that might be useful for you uh now that you're uh training yourself like via laces and so forth for your uh group projects. So, um if you remember, we already have covered um some um very impactful work in uh robot learning um from uh the last few years uh like ACT or diffusion policies um um that really excel at um doing this like dexterous tasks and uh learning uh handling like multimodal behaviors really well. Um and um you can"}, {"t": 60, "text": "see like the the demos are really, really impressive, very dexterous, uh high-frequency control. Um however, uh these methods um as we as we have seen in the past, they are um really well suited um to fit like single-task policies. So, it's kind of like more specialist um um models. Um and um are a little bit harder um to kind of scale to uh train more generalist um multi-embodiment uh robot policies. Um like typically here, um you would need to retrain like your policy often for uh like new uh robot policy uh robot embodiments or new tasks and so forth. Um and so, what's the goal? Like um the North Star um of the robot learning community uh is really to build generalist robot policies that can control many robots uh with a single model in many"}, {"t": 120, "text": "environments and perform also many tasks while working with and alongside humans, right? And so this is kind of our quest towards, let's say, the the holy grail in robot learning and it comes with many questions such as where do we get the data from to train these models or how can we model like very large-scale, very heterogeneous data distributions, how can we generalize these policies to unstructured um environments in the real world and so forth, right? And so the our goal is really to enable kind of this future where um in which you can download your favorite robot brain online and then out-of-the-box your robot should start showing reasonable or ideally intelligent behavior in a useful manner for your particular robot, right?"}, {"t": 180, "text": "Um of course this is very much inspired from advances that we've seen in the NLP and computer vision communities that we already have kind of covered in the lecture. Um and [clears throat] as you know, the work to kind of like canonical workflow workflow to to develop these these models, these foundation models in NLP are that they train on like a large diverse data set containing not just a single task but many many different tasks, right? So it could be like code completion, language translation uh question answering and so forth. And then we have seen that typically these models outperform the behavior of kind of more um single like specialist models that are like more tailored towards doing one specific task. Right? And this is let's"}, {"t": 240, "text": "say this this this paradigm shift that we have seen where large models trained on diverse data sets will typically surpass more specialist models that we maybe like in the past years were developing for different tasks like object detection, language translation, image captioning and so forth. Right? And so this are so this are these foundation models like the LLMs nowadays and in robot learning our goal is to kind of we would like to see the the same benefits and behaviors for robotics too. Right? So let's talk about what are some of the key ingredients that we need in order to develop such generalist robot policies and what what are some of the key differences and challenges in applying the same recipes that have worked in NLP and vision to robotics. So to start with kind of the obvious the"}, {"t": 301, "text": "first obvious thing that you'll notice is that &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; in robotics we don't have the luxury of like really large scale robot data sets that are comparable in size to the ones that you will find in NLP and vision that are used for frontier modeling nowadays. Because robot data is is scarce and expensive and you know now that you probably have started collecting your first teleop demos with the with your SR101 like you can imagine like how hard it is to you know collect these demonstrations to reach the same magnitude of like a frontier model. Um And so additionally um uh, there is also a difference in that, um, in terms of how to model this data, right? So, um, robot data is is very, uh, multimodal, um, and very"}, {"t": 360, "text": "heterogeneous, right? Uh, so instead of just training on natural language or or even just like language and vision, um, we have like multiple sensing modalities with with, uh, with robots, um, that, um, we need for the robots to be able to interact, uh, with the real world because interacting is in the real world, as you know, is like a multi-sensory experience, right? Um, [snorts] and uh, so basically every each robot embodiment has different observation and action spaces. So, it's really very heterogeneous. Um, additionally, we also typically want to do, um, real-time control with our robots. Um, so we don't, um, want to send a query to ChatGPT and like wait a minute for its API to, uh, return a response that tells us how, uh, the next action for our robot should be, right? &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, so, um, and finally, uh, we also, uh, as we scale the the capabilities of"}, {"t": 421, "text": "our generalist robot policies uh, that can now not only do like a single task, but many tasks, maybe even with many different environments, um, you can think that the cost for evaluating these models like increases like dramatically, right? Um, so we need also ways of doing like more, um, scalable evaluations or better better ways of doing evaluations. And so we'll talk a little bit about, um, these three ingredients in today's lecture. So, let's start with the data. Where do we get this from? Because, um, uh, as as you know, like no matter, uh, what the embodiment is, um, like it's it's really expensive to teleoperate a robot um, uh, uh, and you know for each and each and every new task that you want to teach your model um and I can talk from experience here here on the left you can see how I'm like teleoperating to collect some data with"}, {"t": 480, "text": "the Franka robot in the setup I did for my PhD. And so it's really costly to collect data. And so the key idea that enabled the first versions of generous broad policies was to leverage existing sources of data. So in general there had been some previous efforts in the community to collect like some data sets but usually not in a way that enabled co-training between them. And so kind of the the key idea here is that in general like no matter what your research is about every robotic labs in the world collects some sort of data, right? Like maybe like your lab focuses on I don't know like touch sensing and you collect like some teleop data with like tactile sensors or something. And it's usually like very small scale data to support like your research and"}, {"t": 540, "text": "and your papers and that's why often like we don't think of putting it online or you know we think it might not be interesting for others, right? But it in essence there is a lot of existing robot data in the world. And so when I was wrapping up my PhD I was able to collaborate with Google on this project called the Open Ex Embodiment data set where we essentially um went and asked all our friends in different labs around the world if they had any available robot data and then we aggregated all of that put it into a common format that allowed co-training on it. Um, and that's basically what we did. Um, and we ended up with over a million of real robot episodes, which was substantially more than any previous like robot data set effort up to that point. Um, and this was not just like a single robot embodiment, but many different like over 20 different"}, {"t": 600, "text": "robot embodiments. Um, and as you can see like really like like a lot of labs around the world participated in this this effort. And regarding the results of this project, this was really the first time where we saw sparks that is of a single model trained on all these multiple robot data sets surpassing the best previous method that was tailored for a particular setup. Um, and so here these are kind of the main results and you can see I for example contributed continuous evaluations during the model development with the robot setup that I built during my PhD in Germany, which you can see on the top. And so um, this paper has been quite impactful because it allowed now algorithmic progress on the modeling side of generalist robot"}, {"t": 660, "text": "policies by making it possible to train on over a million of diverse cross embodied robot trajectories for the first time. And many of the works I will present today or I will will build up upon this data set or will use this data set as their foundation. Okay. Um, so I have talked about where we can for example get the data to train generalist robot policies. And now the second question is how can we model um this data? Um, right? Uh, what can we do with this data? Uh, because of course this is easier said than done, right? Uh, as I already mentioned um a few slides ago, um in general like robots are very very heterogeneous. They have uh different task specifications, different types of sensors, uh different numbers of cameras, like maybe one robot has a single camera, another has like three or four, um"}, {"t": 720, "text": "different very different action spaces, and figuring out how to share information and knowledge across robots and um uh uh that looks so different uh makes this a very uh challenging problem. So, let's start with a basic example. When we typically train um a robot policy, um the as you already have seen in the exercises, uh the model will take as an input um a current observation, uh an image, and a task uh description, um nowadays often in in natural language, right? And then the model will predict like a sequence of of end-effector uh poses, uh let's say, uh to achieve um the goal of this uh tasks, right? And now our premise is that uh we want to um uh uh fit uh like a generous robot policy uh on a very large diverse heterogeneous um uh data set. And now if we um uh squint a bit and we"}, {"t": 780, "text": "um we replace the robot image with a more regular internet image, and now we replace the instruction with um let's say uh a caption of um captioning the scene, right? Um, and then, you know, like the model answers like, \"Oh, like the picture shows the Statue of Liberty\" or something. Um, this starts to look quite familiar uh because essentially this is like a like a vision um uh and language model, right? And so, the key idea um that a lot of the generous robot policy works built upon is that you can view robotics as a similar multi-model sequencing modeling problem, right? Where you're trying to learn a powerful model that maps from images and text um to answers, right? But in robotics, in our case, the answers are not really text, right? But they will represent actions. Um And [clears throat]"}, {"t": 840, "text": "this has many benefits because um now because we have now language tokens, image tokens, and action tokens, we can leverage also many of the advances that have been made in NLP and vision in like general like multi-model sequence modeling. So, for from non-robotic domains into the robotic domain. Um So, as a first step towards developing such generous robot policies, we developed Octo, which was the kind of the first open-source generous policy. And this is in a nutshell a large transformer-based policy that was trained on 800,000 robot trajectories of the Open X Embodiment data set I just talked about. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um and here, similar to in the similar to the Gato paper that Scott Reed talked about last week,"}, {"t": 900, "text": "the the main idea is that you tokenize all this diverse heterogeneous input and action outputs and train this large transformer, right? Um So, how does it work? So, Octo is a fairly straightforward transformer model. So, it has a pre-trained language encoder that tokenizes your language inputs. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um Uh we used a T5 model here. Uh and then it has a observation tokenizer that patches files your image observations and runs them [clears throat] through a shallow CNN to produce your image tokens. So then you concatenate both tokens, observation and task tokens, and pass them through a transformer that in essence looks very similar"}, {"t": 960, "text": "to a vanilla vision transformer like a VIT. Um and &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; maybe one thing to note here is that the transformer in Octo is has a block-wise causal structure. So all the task tokens cannot attend to each other, but they can't look at the observation tokens. And &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; observation tokens from the first time steps can look at themselves and the tasks, but not at future time steps. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; All right. And then for the actions, we define these purple readout tokens, which are essentially placeholder tokens that summarize the embeddings that then go into these functional heads. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; One thing to note is that this model we train it with goal condition behavior cloning. So this means that um"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "it predicts a sequence of actions to reach a goal state given the current state. And as we see as we saw also previously, this is a very flexible formulation because it allows you to um your goal states uh in various ways, uh be it um uh goal images or natural language instructions. All right. So, we have now the task observations and the readout blocks. And then, uh because this is a sequential model, um you pass this um this this you run this over multiple time steps. And in our [clears throat] case, the uh functional um heads uh uh were diffusion decoding heads uh because we found also in in experiments that it really helps um handle multimodal behavior uh much better than than other ways. Um and"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "I also wanted to um talk about some key design decisions um that we made in this uh in this project. Um first of all, uh we tried to invest all our compute inside the transformer um as [clears throat] opposed to prior designs that um had like these larger ResNet style encoders for each image separately and then uh merge them uh in a late fusion style. Um originally, we thought that wow, we have like so much data, we can just train everything from from scratch. Um and maybe in hindsight, it was not the best idea, but at the time, like that's what we thought. Um and maybe some additional insights we had in this project was that although we didn't align the coordinate frames from all these different uh data sets, which kind of was a scalability choice, uh we did take care of aligning the gripper dimension of all the different data sets. Um this was because in the beginning, um"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "a lot of the failures were uh related to the gripper, like early grasping. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, we kind of then made sure that the semantics uh for the gripper uh were all the same. So, like one for opening and zero for closing. And here in the figure, you can see like some of the different options that different data sets had had applied for controlling their end effector gripper action. Right? So, it could be like absolute, relative, continuous, and so forth. And so, that's the one thing we kind of um uh tried to align across all data sets. All right. [clears throat] So, we divided the evaluation into zero-shot evals and fine-tuning evals across many kind of labs and institutions. And we used the same fine-tuning recipe to adapt Octo to new sensory and action"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "spaces. I won't go too much into the results, but in general, we found that Octo could control multiple robot embodiments out of the box and also outperformed previous models from Google trained on similar data. So, particularly like the RT1X model that was trained for the Open X Embodiment project. It outperformed this model and it also matched the RT2X, which was a much larger 55 billion parameter closed-source VLA model from Google, which was kind of like the first VLA. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; All right. Um This video shows I think the first successful grasp we ever saw from our Octo model on the Google robot. Which we were serving via remote server. &gt;&gt; Bam!"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "&gt;&gt; Woo! &gt;&gt; One success. Nice. &gt;&gt; Very nice. &gt;&gt; One success. &gt;&gt; All right, good to &gt;&gt; Yeah. Um by the way, on the left this is Quan. Uh this is our guest uh lecturer today. Uh at the time he was at Google. Um so he was helping us um do this uh um our evaluations. So um Yeah, it's also worth uh it was very cool um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; cuz robot evals are always very challenging and even more when you are not next to the robot, but you have to rely on people helping you. Um and uh one thing to note here is that actually um these evals uh um in Mountain View they were quite out of distribution for our model because um although [clears throat] um we had trained on their data, like the data that Google had collected, um that data had been collected in a different building. So they had moved buildings, uh which is, you know,"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "obviously like then quite out of out of distribution. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh and so we were excited that, you know, even like, you know, simple tasks were still working. Um so this was our Eureka moment. Okay, so this were zero-shot evaluations, um but one thing I want to highlight about generalist robot policies in general is the adaptability aspect because ideally these models should be useful uh for your robot even if the model did not pre-train on your model. Right? Like it should be like uh a good basis for um post-training it to your particular embodiment because like it could be a new robot or maybe for this particular task um you want to add some new sensing modalities. Maybe you need to do a um force torque uh sensing or you want to change the number of cameras uh for your particular task, right? Uh or maybe you want to do"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "different um like action uh spaces. Maybe now instead of like Cartesian control, you want to do like joint control, right? So, um how we did approach this in Octo is that um uh once you have a new input, like let's say force torque readings, you can slot in this new input with a few tokens, and then you fine-tune the model. And because um in this case, most of the parameters were inside the transformer itself, you get to retain most of the knowledge when when fine-tuning. Um and um yeah, I won't go into detail about the fine-tuning results, but basically what we found out when we were doing this experiments for different fine-tuning setups across different labs, was that um fine-tuning"}, {"t": 1441, "text": "from a generalist robot policy or robot foundation model RFM, uh worked much better than any other like state-of-the-art visual representation uh learning-based backbone. So, this is kind of like the key message, I think. And I think this applies also to many other current like state-of-the-art um uh models. And we actually, I think in terms of the adoption, we it got quite popular, and we actually saw we found [clears throat] that people or researchers across the world actually used Octo and fine-tuned fine-tuned fine-tuned it to their embodiment. So, here is like on the left a video I found it on Twitter uh from a researcher who fine-tuned it uh on an unseen uh spot robot."}, {"t": 1500, "text": "And then also uh on the right you can see like a Japanese company that used used it to find unit for their humanoid robot. All right. So so far we have talked about how you can train a generalist or policy from scratch on you know on a diverse data set containing a bunch of single arm manipulation tasks, right? But ideally like we want to expand this to multiple embodiments, right? Because how generalist is this really like if all it can do is just like control like a 7° of freedom arm, right? Like ideally it should work no matter what robot embodiment you have, right? And this is like I think really important because as you all know um this is also one of the cool things of robotics that they are all these different embodiments, right? Tailored for different tasks, right? So we have drones that can fly around, we have"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "quadrupeds, we have self-driving cars, humanoids, etc. So what I'm trying to say is that if we develop methods that can learn from all these different embodiments, then like we can scale also the data that we can consume, right? Like there are there is more data than we think out there for robotics, but we all it's kind of like challenge because of its heterogeneity to have methods that can like transfer the relevant bits and knowledge is across all these embodiments. Right. So you can imagine [clears throat] for example that like navigation data could transfer a behavior to drones by learning how to avoid obstacles for example because it's kind of a common behavior no matter if it's like a like a navigation robot like a Roomba or if it's a an indoor drone for example, right? &gt;&gt; [snorts]"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "&gt;&gt; Um and so ideally we would like to have a very scalable approach that can learn from any robot embodiment without any constraints, no manual like action space alignments or any kind of like yeah, constraints, right? We just want to like take our data pass it through our transformer and like have have the model figure out like how to combine all these things. Um and that's kind of what we did in this paper called CrossFormer where we added more embodiments such as by manual manipulation, navigation or locomotion to the data mix we originally had trained Octo on for a total of 900,000 robot trajectories and trained a big transformer but now with embodiment specific heads on it. So we had like one head for like controlling single arms, one for quadrupeds and so forth."}, {"t": 1680, "text": "Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so this model accepts variable observation inputs and predict the variable action outputs which is quite important in order to predict action chunks of different length because basically one of the challenges when you do this kind of extreme cross embodiment learning is that a lot of these embodiments have vastly different control frequencies, right? So it could be from like you know, like a Widow X is doing like 5 hertz control to like a bimanual dexterous manipulation robot that is doing like 50 hertz control, right? And so this means also the action chunk length that your model should predict is like vastly different. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So having these different heads is like one way how you can go uh, about this. Um, and yeah. Um, this way also you don't have to do any"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "action space alignment. Um, and uh, in order to uh, maximize transfer across embodiments, um, you try to maximize parameter sharing across embodiments. So for example, um, by sharing image encoders uh, weights for camera views of the same type. Um, yeah. And uh, here are rollouts from the cross-former model. Um, as you can see uh, like there the evaluations is like very very diverse. Uh, many different embodiments from uh, the uh, quadraped in the center um, to um, like the single arm manipulation with the Franka arms. Um, we [clears throat] have also have like outdoor and indoor navigation. And um, yeah, also like the the sushi cutting task that I already presented. We did it uh, here too, which is this like by"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "manual uh, dexterous super long horizon um, task. And so, uh, what I want to highlight here is that um, all rollouts that you see, all video snippets that you see were produced by this by the same model. It's like a single checkpoint that's controlling all this um, robot embodiments. Right? So it's not like we cherry-picked like oh, like this this this checkpoint works best for this robot, so let's do that. It's like all the same set of um, neural network weights. Uh, so I think that's pretty cool. And um, regarding quantitative results, um, we also found that this kind of cross-embodied policy uh model matched or outperformed kind of specialist policies that were uh tailored um for specific embodiments. All right. So, CrossFormer tried to answer if a generalist policy could"}, {"t": 1862, "text": "control multiple embodiments and outperform embodiment-specific specialist policies. Now, this work from TRI, on the other hand, tried to answer the question um does multitask pretraining help post training? This is kind of something that many in the community believe to be true, right? Um but in robotics, determining this is somewhat like not trivial. Um so, I recommend this reading this paper because they did a very rigorous study um and evaluate by evaluating this uh kind of diffusion transformer policies on around that were trained on around a thousand uh 1,700 hours of pretraining data including the OXE uh data sets. And then, they fine-tuned it into a um uh single by manual uh tasks on their Frankas"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "uh across both Sim and Eval. Um and so, they really went out of their way to kind of gain statistical significance um here uh by doing blind AB testing in the real world with the operators. So, the operators like would not know which policy they were scoring at the time and so forth. Right? And you can see like really impressive dexterous tasks like with like cutting this this apple um &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; which is like super long horizon. I think it goes like for 2 minutes or something. Uh very, very impressive. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and I want to highlight like a few key results here that they did. Um so [clears throat] uh they found out that yes, indeed, like pre-training &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Sorry. Helps and makes uh uh post-training to single tasks um up to three times or five times more um sample efficient."}, {"t": 1981, "text": "And that this is uh most beneficial at uh low post-training data regime. So, if you don't have a lot of data to fine-tune to a new task, then uh this is where you reap most of these benefits. Um but interestingly, um I think they also found that um things like how you normalize your data uh often impacted uh downstream performance much more than architectural or algorithmic changes. Um which again shows also like uh why robot learning is like also like so challenging cuz there there are like kind of so many different knobs that one can uh tune to uh improve performance, right? And yeah, sometimes even things so simple as the normalization um like is is really, really important. Okay. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, everything we have covered um so far today uh has been pretty much focused on training generalist robot policies uh from scratch"}, {"t": 2040, "text": "uh on robot data. Um but even as I said before, even if at the time, you know, um people thought like, \"Oh, this is like a lot of data, like um uh when we're doing Octo and so forth.\" Um the truth is that this still is uh kind of um like ORC-E um data set is is minuscule uh compared to LLMs uh and VLMs that are trained on much, much more data, right? So, the obviously the obvious question here to ask um by many is does internet-scale uh priors or do internet scale priors like VLMs also transfer to downstream robotics control? And how would we go about that, right? And so the first idea to explore how to do this are similar to the Lava architecture we saw a few weeks ago. If you recall here, the idea here this was like how to add vision to an LLM, right? This is at the context where we saw this when we're discussing VLMs. And here the idea was"}, {"t": 2102, "text": "to prepend image tokens to the text so that the LLM then runs standard self-attention over this unified sequence so that image tokens and text tokens are together without any modifications like to the attention mechanism or and so forth, right? And so the key question people ask is like, well, can we treat now robot actions the same way as we as Lava treats like image tokens for adding this into the LLM, right? Um And that's basically how the first VLMs got fine-tuned into VLAs. Right? So you have now this VLM architecture and what you do is you tokenize your robot actions and inject these into your LLM vocabulary. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and one common approach how to do this is that you can override like the last frequent tokens in your vocabulary um"}, {"t": 2163, "text": "with [clears throat] these new action tokens. And now your LLM can predict these new action tokens as part because it's part of its vocabulary and then you train this with next token prediction with the same cross-entropy loss. Um we already covered action tokenization uh, in the past lecture so I won't go into details again, but just recap, um, the first approaches um, like RT2 or OpenVLA, uh, used a very simple naive, um, per dimension, per time step uh, binning discretization, uh, approach. Okay, so this is kind of the high-level idea, um, but as as I said before, as some of you will, um, kind of train like yourself VLM, uh, VLAs for uh, your group projects, um, I thought it might be good to share a few tips and"}, {"t": 2220, "text": "tricks, uh, about how you might, uh, go about training them, right? Uh, and some questions that, uh, might arise, uh, when doing so. But I think like this a lot of these are also general to, um, other models that are not um, specially, uh, VLAs, but could also be for your world model or, um, other sort of like large-scale, uh, robot policy training. Um, so if you, um, look for example at this, um, kind of first, uh, VLAs, um, the first question could be, uh, when you, let's say you start, I don't know, training OpenVLA or something on your robot, um, is how do I know if my model has converged uh, when when training. Uh, and so for auto regressive VLAs, uh, we found a good a good rule of thumb um, is, um, to wait until your action token accuracy reaches around 95%. Um, so one thing to note here is that, um, this action token accuracy is"}, {"t": 2280, "text": "computed on teacher forcing, uh, and does not represent like the actual performance in real world rollouts, um, but basically, like before it reaches 95%, uh, you probably should not do any real world rollouts, uh, cuz they won't be very good. Um, um, so this measures like, um, if we picked kind of the exact action been correctly and this like discretize uh approach. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; but then you typically also will want to track let's say um the L1 and L2 action errors uh which measure how far off the predicted uh continuous so how far the predicted continuous actions are from the ground truth. Um so this means like once you have um detokenized uh your uh action tokens and unnormalized system uh back to continuous values, you can compare them with your ground truth actions uh and see uh how how well you're doing."}, {"t": 2340, "text": "Um another uh design choice one has to make uh when training generalist policies is how to batch heterogeneous data. This will be maybe less relevant for you because I assume you all we all will mostly be like fine-tuning on like your one robot. But when you do pre-training, this is kind of a more relevant like with more different embodiment sources, this becomes a more relevant question. Um because you might have as I said before, let's say one embodiment with a single third-person camera uh observation input and another one um where for example, you add two wrist cameras so for a total of three. Um and now therefore you can't just simply stack these into a batch. So the easiest way to handle this heterogeneity is of course um that you can pad to the longest number of the observation inputs and mask the attention appropriately. Um and this is for example uh what we did for OCTO and CrossFormer. Um and so this is very easy to implement, right? It's probably like the"}, {"t": 2401, "text": "first thing one uh can start with. However, you also um need to uh understand the trade-offs you're making here if you have very heterogeneous observations. Um because uh like now this can result in half your batch being full of zeros. Right? So, um like you will be wasting like a lot of flops because like like if you're padding all the time, then like yeah, it's a lot of zeros um uh you're pushing through your GPU. Um another different way how you could implement this in your data loading pipeline is to use sequence packing, um which is kind of a technique specifically tailored to um handle variable-length inputs with transformers. Um so, the key idea is that instead of padding um and waste this compute on zeros, you concatenate everything into a single long vector um so that you maximize your GPU throughput. Um and so, now here every um"}, {"t": 2461, "text": "sample uh uh contributes exactly the tokens that it actually has. Um and uh in order to keep track uh of where each sample um in this long vector starts and ends, um you will have to maintain like um kind of like a um this cumulative sequence length uh uh like variable. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; The um trade-off that you're making when you apply um when you use the sequence packing approach is kind of implementation complexity. Uh so, you'll need um block diagonal attention masks um so that [clears throat] tokens from different samples don't attend to each other. And uh also, you will need to reset your positional encodings um um at each uh sample boundary, uh which I visualize here. Um these kind of two concrete implementation uh challenges [clears throat] I just"}, {"t": 2520, "text": "mentioned. Um so, you have to be very careful um on which tokens are allowed to attend uh uh to which tokens. Uh so, like the tokens in each sample should only attend to to their own tokens, right? And then, like, be careful to reset um your uh positional uh encodings um at every uh sample boundary, so you don't kind of uh uh keep growing the the length of this. Um Now, also, let's talk about a practical um but often overlooked topic. Uh, how do you actually load data uh during training? Uh, cuz this matters actually more than you might think. Um so, there are kind of two main strategies. Um on the left, uh you can see um uh sequential reads with a shuffle buffer. Um this is what uh like, for example, we use for Octo or OpenVLA also uses. Um so, you um stream data sequentially"}, {"t": 2581, "text": "from shards into into a buffer, and then you sample um a batch from there. And um kind of the key inside here is that if your buffer if your shuffle buffer is too small, um your um &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; your batches uh might become correlated. Um as the degree of freedom um sorry, as as the degree of randomness is uh directly proportional to the size uh of your shuffle buffer. Um and so, this is, for example, some This is, for example, something we learned um the hard way uh when we started training Octo. Um so, in the beginning, uh we had a shuffle buffer of only uh 20,000 samples. Um and um combined with uh trajectory-level interleaving, this resulted in really poor um like, generalization performance. Um um and so, we solved this by um uh shuffling and interleaving from different trajectories uh uh sorry"}, {"t": 2641, "text": "sorry, by uh shuffling and interleaving frames from different trajectories uh before decoding uh the images um and uh this allowed us to grow our shuffle buffer up to 500,000 samples. And this was kind of like actually made a big impact in the performance of the model. So nothing else changed. It was the same like like data mixture and and everything. But this was quite important. And on the right you have you can you see like &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; you can also implement a data loading solution based on true random reads where you maintain a flat index over your data set and perform like random seeks to to build build each build each batch. And this will gives you kind of this will give you more exact unbiased randomness, but here having like fast IO will be very"}, {"t": 2700, "text": "important because you know you can't typically like load all like your data set into into memory. And yeah, so basically um there's there's kind of this trade-off, right? And there are solutions that try to combine best of both worlds. You can also like do like kind of chunk random reads instead of like loading just like images or something. But yeah, this like data loading once you start doing pre-training for kind of heterogeneous large data sets is becomes really also like key like a key architectural choice that will affect all that will affect also the performance of your policy like via for example randomness and so forth. Another practical question that comes up um when you're training general policies"}, {"t": 2761, "text": "is how do you handle the fact that different robots have different action spaces? Right? So, maybe like one data set with one embodiment has like seven degrees of freedom arm. In the next data set, you have like like by manual with 14 degrees of freedom. And so, there are kind of like two different approaches um that are more or less popular. Um one on the left is kind of the transformer style approach, where you have a shared transformer backbone that processes the observations and then routes this to separate embodiment specific action heads. Um which with the benefit that you can easily have variable length outputs to accommodate for the different control frequencies. Um but of course, the downside is that if you want to add a new embodiment, now you will need to add a new head and find your this probably."}, {"t": 2821, "text": "Um and on the right, you can see the kind of more Pi Zero style approach, where you still have a shared transformer backbone, but now this is followed by a single action expert that outputs into a kind of unified padded action space. And so here, what you do is you will typically pick the the maximum action dimension across all your embodiments and have [clears throat] every robot action zero pad to fit that. And so this is kind of more more simple, much more simple architecturally. &gt;&gt; [cough] [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Um though Yeah, here like maybe like one caveat would be that you will typically predict a single like a fixed length action chunk for all regardless of their control frequency."}, {"t": 2880, "text": "&gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Yeah, maybe I have a question for you now. How do you think that the VLA for both of these options will know then the model which embodiment it should produce actions for at test time? So maybe you can take 20 seconds to discuss with your neighbor. So the question is how does the model know at test time? Like if it's should produce actions let's say for a 7° of freedom arm or a 14° of freedom arm. And we have these two models with one model has embodiment specific heads and the other one is a single action expert expert with this padding."}, {"t": 2941, "text": "Where all embodiments get uh predicted. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So the question is like at test time you have one model and now you need to do inference like like how how how does it work? Like what do you do? Okay, so for the let's start with the cross former. How does it work for the multiple heads? Like does the model infer like which head you are controlling or do you need to tell and specify to the model? Yeah? Yeah. Yeah, so that's that's a good guess. So it's kind of a mixture of all your responses. So [clears throat] for the cross former style approach,"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "this is explicitly handled by the user. All right, so like you will specify which head to use as part of your prompt or configuration. So you're kind of telling it like all used the bimanual head or used the quadruped head and so forth. Right? So the routing is hard coded by the user. And the Pi zero style like single action expert approach, there's typically no explicit head selection. And so the model has to infer the embodiment implicitly from the observations itself. Right? And [clears throat] so this is like the camera views. Right? So different types of camera views give some information on like which embodiment this might be. Also the proprioceptive is also an input to the action head. So this will also give a very very strong hint on like what embodiment it should produce actions for. And often also the language instructions"}, {"t": 3061, "text": "too actually. Right? Because like like you cannot do the same tasks with all embodiments. Right? So like yeah, if you tell like I don't know like a a Roomba like cut the sushi like you know, it's probably not going to work. Right? So all these together give give like a strong hint to the model on like what embodiment it should be controlling. Um And [clears throat] so yeah, like the backbone should learn how to pick up on these cues and then route information accordingly home through the action expert. All right. So [clears throat] to close off the kind of this VLA part, I want to present some of the high level ideas on how a lot of the current state-of-the-art VLAs are being trained, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; mostly based on the Pi 05 and the knowledge insulation papers."}, {"t": 3120, "text": "Um, there like of course like some follow-ups and I don't want to um, kind of explain everything since like I think later Quan will also share more about the latest model. Uh, but I would say this is kind of like a pretty solid recipe that nowadays a lot of like different labs have adopted or found success in replicating. Um, and so first here the VLM backbone is trained with next token prediction on a mixture of fast tokenized robot actions and web data. Um, so co-training on web data helps to preserve um, kind of the capabilities of the VLM backbone and like avoids like catastrophic forgetting. Um, and the fast tokens are good for representation learning, too. And we already covered a few lectures ago how fast tokenization works."}, {"t": 3181, "text": "Uh, if you remember. Um, and then in the sec- the second part is the the action expert which is trained with like a flow matching head, right? This is like the single action expert that will predict like all the different embodiments. Um, so now you produce this continuous actions with the flow matching head. And besides uh, uh, not attending to the fast tokens, the action action expert also implements a stop gradient operation. So this means that the action expert reads from the VLM backbone, but can't update it, insulating it in order to preserve its VLM kind of like semantic capabilities. And this also helps like convergence, like fast training, and so forth. And you can find more details on"}, {"t": 3240, "text": "the knowledge installation paper from Danny Drees on the bottom right. All right. So far we have talked about data and the modeling side of generalist from policies. Last but not least, we'll also need to talk a little bit about evaluations because as I said before, this now it's getting very very expensive, right? And &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Has anyone of you already done any rollouts with your SO101? I don't know. Yes? Yeah? Did you like it? Yeah? &gt;&gt; I mean, not all &gt;&gt; Did it Did it work? &gt;&gt; Yeah, this morning the last one. &gt;&gt; Okay, awesome. Okay. Um so, good. So, now when you keep doing this for a month, maybe like when I ask you in a month you'll be like, oh, I can't see this robot anymore. It's like"}, {"t": 3301, "text": "so expensive, like so tiring to do like rollouts with it. Um because like there are like several things that are make it difficult, right? So, for example, if you have like a third-person camera mounted to your robot, and you train like you collect some data, train your policy on it, and then someone goes and bumps into the camera, and now your camera is in a different like pose or position. Maybe like you didn't cali- I don't know, like, you know, like it will be like out of distribution, like it doesn't work anymore. Maybe you have to like collect new data, find tune, I don't know. Um then also hardware failures. Um I think I saw some some people already had hardware failures. How many people had hardware failures? Oh my god. All right. Lots of hardware failures. So, that's another thing like maybe you collect some data, right? Um and let's say your policy has proprioceptive"}, {"t": 3360, "text": "input, right? Um and now you have to do some surgery to save your robot. You know, you put like new motors or something. Uh and now like your proprioception will be how will have different readings than the data that you collected and trained your policy on, right? So, there are like a lot of things that, you know, could go wrong. Like I'm sure they will be fantastic and uh you will all enjoy it doing evals. Um but yeah. And in general, it also like takes a a long time to do evals, right? Like like you know, it's like low frequency uh control. It's not very fast. It takes like a long time. You see like move it and like, \"Okay, move another centimeter.\" And you know, like you wait there for 2 minutes to see like if it grasp something or not. So, it just takes a long time. And so, one way around this um is to use simulation uh because &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; simulation makes research more"}, {"t": 3420, "text": "accessible uh to people who might not have your uh same robot set up on one hand. And also it makes it a bit more reproducible because now you don't have to like, I don't know, um you know, this risk of like bumping into cameras or your proprioception to have different readings and so forth. And um this is why during the development of Octo, um we started a parallel project which we coined uh Simpler. Um this is actually this was a collaboration actually with um both Quan and uh also Ted Xiao, who also gave a guest lecture uh, a few weeks ago. So, they were both at Google at the time. Um, &gt;&gt; [cough] &gt;&gt; and so, [clears throat] in this project, we uh, try to leverage Sim2Real um, for policies trained exclusively on real-world data. Um, and so, the hope here is really that if we can find a way to evaluate our real-world policies in simulation in a way that informs us about how they will perform in the uh,"}, {"t": 3480, "text": "real world, then uh, we can just like, you know, sit back and enjoy life and um, uh, while we run a large number of evals in simulation. Um, and Okay. So, uh, I will ver- on a very high level explain how the idea of this works. Um, so, the success rates in Sim2Real are correlated um, between um, simulation and real world, so that's great. Uh, and this holds for different models. Um, some from Google's, um, also the Octo. And importantly, this also holds for different um, distribution shifts. Uh, like textures, um, different camera poses and distractors. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, how does it work? So, the first step is um, um, that you need to mitigate the control gap between um, the simulated and the real-world environments um, in order to ensure that um, the policies uh, actions executed in simulation will yield the same result"}, {"t": 3541, "text": "uh, um, uh, compared to the real robot's end effector uh, when observed um, when executed on the real world. Um, and so, in order to do this um, like, you can do this by doing a a system identification procedure uh, on a small uh, number of samples collected uh, from the real on the real world. Right? Um, so, basically, um, this means like your in a nutshell, your low-level controller in the real world and in the simulation should produce like the same outputs, more or less. Um And now, the second step is to minimize the visual distribution shift. Um by trying to match the simulated visuals to those of the real world. Um here we did a very simple procedure. This is not like super realistic, like uh like you know, like Nvidia Isaac Lab um kind of style. Uh here we did a green screening as a"}, {"t": 3601, "text": "first step um to try to minimize this visual distribution shift. Um so, this [clears throat] means we uh segmented out interactive simulated assets and overlayed them into uh real-world backgrounds. And then, the second one is texture matching, where uh we projected um uh textures from real-world objects uh into simulation assets. Like, for example, the Coke can. Like, we take the texture of like a real-world Coke can and overlay it on top of like the simulated Coke can. Um and so, maybe one thing to notice how like what you get out of this is um so, simpler gives you correlated um uh um performance. So, it does not mean that so, um it does not mean that if in simulation [snorts] uh you get 50% success rate on picking [clears throat] up the Coke can, that in the real world you will get also si- 55"}, {"t": 3660, "text": "uh success rate. So, it's not absolute. It doesn't tell you anything about the absolute um rollout success rate performance of your robot in the real world. All it can do is give you pairwise comparison. So, if policy A uh performs uh better than policy B in this simpler framework, um this will also hold true um in the real world. Um and the paper also has like kind of more explanations on how you can measure this with like ranking and so on. Um so I invite you to check it out. Um there is uh some follow-up work um uh cool work um that tries to improve like correlations of such real-to-sim evaluation frameworks uh such as Polaris, uh Robot Arena Infinity, uh or RoboLab. Uh and maybe there are some some others that I I'm not aware of. Um and so I think this is uh um really"}, {"t": 3721, "text": "interesting um to enable uh like researchers and practitioners around the world um to do like research on generalist robot policies. So, uh it abstracts away to some degree the need of you having to have like all these different robot setups um to try to see if your generalist robot policy uh is actually generalist. All right. Uh so, to conclude today's lecture, um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; we talked about the three ingredients um that we have covered in order to uh develop generalist robot policies. Uh we have talked about how you can aggregate existing offline robot data and convert it into a common format um that enables co-training on them. Uh then we have covered how you can uh train generalist policies and cross-embodiment cross-embodiment generalist robot policies uh from scratch on this data uh followed by how [snorts] um to leverage internet-scale priors like VLMs"}, {"t": 3782, "text": "uh in order to train uh VLAs or vision-language-action models. And finally, we have uh also shown one way um the community is exploring uh for how to make uh evaluations of such generalist robot policies uh more scalable uh by leveraging correlated evaluations in simulation. And uh with this, thank you for attention."}], "toc": [{"t": 180, "title": "Slide 4: Foundation Models in NLP", "slide": "slide-4-foundation-models-in-nlp-frame-2"}, {"t": 180, "title": "Slide 5: Foundation Models > Specialist Models", "slide": "slide-5-foundation-models-specialist-models-frame-3"}, {"t": 240, "title": "Slide 6: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies", "slide": "slide-6-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-frames-4-7"}, {"t": 480, "title": "Slide 8: Key Idea: Leverage Existing Robot Datasets", "slide": "slide-8-key-idea-leverage-existing-robot-datasets-frames-8-9"}, {"t": 480, "title": "Slide 9: Large Robot Data: Open X-Embodiment", "slide": "slide-9-large-robot-data-open-x-embodiment-frame-10"}, {"t": 540, "title": "Slide 10: Sparks of a Generalist Robot Policy", "slide": "slide-10-sparks-of-a-generalist-robot-policy-frame-11"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Slide 12: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [Large Models]", "slide": "slide-12-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-large-models-frame-12"}, {"t": 660, "title": "[untitled: Generalist Robot Policy - plug-in inputs and outputs]", "slide": "untitled-generalist-robot-policy---plug-in-inputs-and-outputs-frames-13-16"}, {"t": 840, "title": "Slide 14: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "slide": "slide-14-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-17"}, {"t": 840, "title": "Slide 16: [untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]", "slide": "slide-16-untitled-vision-language-model-inputs-and-outputs-frame-18"}, {"t": 840, "title": "Slide 17: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "slide": "slide-17-key-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-19"}, {"t": 840, "title": "Slide 19: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture", "slide": "slide-19-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-frames-20-21"}, {"t": 840, "title": "Slide 20: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [transformer]", "slide": "slide-20-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-transformer-frame-22"}, {"t": 960, "title": "Slide 21: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [readout and action head]", "slide": "slide-21-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-readout-and-action-head-frame-23"}, {"t": 960, "title": "Slide 22: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [diffusion action head]", "slide": "slide-22-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-diffusion-action-head-frame-24"}, {"t": 1080, "title": "Slide 23: [octopus emoji] Octo Design Decisions", "slide": "slide-23-octopus-emoji-octo-design-decisions-frame-25"}, {"t": 1321, "title": "Slide 25: The Eureka Moment", "slide": "slide-25-the-eureka-moment-frames-26-28"}, {"t": 1321, "title": "Slide 26: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-26-untitled-transition-question-frame-29"}, {"t": 1380, "title": "Slide 27: [octopus emoji] Octo Finetuning", "slide": "slide-27-octopus-emoji-octo-finetuning-frame-30"}, {"t": 1380, "title": "Slide 28: Adoption of Octo", "slide": "slide-28-adoption-of-octo-frame-31"}, {"t": 1500, "title": "Slide 29: Cross-Embodiment Learning", "slide": "slide-29-cross-embodiment-learning-frame-32"}, {"t": 1560, "title": "Slide 30: One Policy for Manipulation, Navigation, Locomotion & Aviation", "slide": "slide-30-one-policy-for-manipulation-navigation-locomotion-aviation-frames-33-34"}, {"t": 1740, "title": "Slide 31: CrossFormer Model", "slide": "slide-31-crossformer-model-frame-35"}, {"t": 1740, "title": "Slide 33: Quantitative Results", "slide": "slide-33-quantitative-results-frame-36"}, {"t": 1981, "title": "Slide 35: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-35-untitled-transition-question-frame-37"}, {"t": 2102, "title": "Slide 36: Recap Llava", "slide": "slide-36-recap-llava-frame-38"}, {"t": 2102, "title": "Slide 37: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-37-untitled-transition-question-frame-39"}, {"t": 2102, "title": "Slide 38: VLM -> Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA)", "slide": "slide-38-vlm---vision-language-action-model-vla-frame-40"}, {"t": 2163, "title": "Slide 39: Recap: Robot Action Tokenization for VLAs", "slide": "slide-39-recap-robot-action-tokenization-for-vlas-frame-41"}, {"t": 2220, "title": "Slide 40: Tips & Tricks: Convergence", "slide": "slide-40-tips-tricks-convergence-frame-42"}, {"t": 2340, "title": "Slide 41: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [padding]", "slide": "slide-41-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-padding-frame-43"}, {"t": 2401, "title": "Slide 42: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [sequence packing]", "slide": "slide-42-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-sequence-packing-frame-44"}, {"t": 2461, "title": "Slide 43: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [masking and positions]", "slide": "slide-43-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-masking-and-positions-frame-45"}, {"t": 2641, "title": "Slide 44: Tips & Tricks: Dataloading", "slide": "slide-44-tips-tricks-dataloading-frame-46"}, {"t": 2761, "title": "Slide 45: Tips & Tricks: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "slide": "slide-45-tips-tricks-cross-embodiment-heads-frame-47"}, {"t": 2880, "title": "Slide 46: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-46-untitled-transition-question-frame-48"}, {"t": 2941, "title": "Slide 47: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "slide": "slide-47-cross-embodiment-heads-frame-49"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 48: Current VLA Recipe", "slide": "slide-48-current-vla-recipe-frame-50"}, {"t": 3181, "title": "Slide 49: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [Scalable Evaluation]", "slide": "slide-49-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-scalable-evaluation-frame-51"}, {"t": 3301, "title": "Slide 50: Evaluating Policies is Expensive", "slide": "slide-50-evaluating-policies-is-expensive-frames-52-53"}, {"t": 3420, "title": "Slide 51: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation", "slide": "slide-51-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-frames-54-55"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 52: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [real vs sim correlation]", "slide": "slide-52-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-real-vs-sim-correlation-frame-56"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 53: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [robustness and what matters]", "slide": "slide-53-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-robustness-and-what-matters-frames-57-59"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 54: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [visual matching]", "slide": "slide-54-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-visual-matching-frame-60"}, {"t": 3660, "title": "Slide 55: Follow Up Works on Real-to-Sim Evals", "slide": "slide-55-follow-up-works-on-real-to-sim-evals-frame-61"}, {"t": 3721, "title": "Slide 56: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [summary]", "slide": "slide-56-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-summary-frames-62-63"}], "toc_source": "estimated from slides (approx.)"}, "guests": [{"id": "pzolgvyWEFY", "title": "Quan Vuong: π0.7, A Generalist Model with Emergent Capabilities [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Quan Vuong", "duration": "36:59", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees,  Quan Vuong (Co-Founding Member of Physical Intelligence), who's has been a central figure in the push towards large-scale pre-training for robot learning first at Google DeepMind at now at Physical Intelligence,  talks about the latest findings at Physical Intelligence when developing their frontier models that show emergent capabilities.", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to PIO7"}, {"t": 69, "title": "Generalist model objectives"}, {"t": 223, "title": "PIO7 architecture and design"}, {"t": 440, "title": "Performance and comparisons"}, {"t": 634, "title": "Data quality experiments"}, {"t": 746, "title": "Cross-embodiment and planning"}, {"t": 847, "title": "Commitment to open research"}, {"t": 961, "title": "Real-world deployment case"}, {"t": 1270, "title": "Q&A and research discussion"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, it's my pleasure today, uh to uh welcome Quan Pham uh for our guest lecture. Uh he co-founded Physical Intelligence uh and focuses on large-scale pre-training uh for cross-embodiment uh robot models. He obtained his PhD at uh USC &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh UCSD, sorry. Uh during which he was advised by uh Henry Christensen and Howie Su. And before co-founding uh Physical Intelligence, Quan worked at Google DeepMind uh where he uh started and co-led uh RTX and Open X Embodiment project. Uh and with this, uh please give him a uh warm applause. Go ahead, Quan. &gt;&gt; Sounds good. Yeah, I can actually see the Zoom link and I'm not going to try to make it show up. So, you know, if you have anything uh feel free to interrupt me. So, I'll keep today really informal, um just to share what our recent progress is. And I think my goal today"}, {"t": 61, "text": "is really to interact with uh Roy community at ETH. So, if you have any questions, save them for the end. Um so, PILE-7, um this is a work that we released recently. And I would say that one of the main innovation in this work is to get a single model that can perform many tasks and perform many tasks at an extremely high level of performance. Um why is this important and why is this hard? Um so, first, why is this important? Um previously, when you wanted to get really high performance for any particular task in end-to-end learning, normally what you do is you take a pre-trained model that are somewhat reasonable in performance, and not great, and then you post-train that model um to that specific task. And the reason why getting a single model that can perform many tasks at"}, {"t": 121, "text": "high level performance is important is that it's much more scalable, right? You don't have You don't have to You don't need to worry about which model checkpoint, what is hyper parameter during test time I use for a particular task. Um the the fewer model that you have to manage, the easier it is uh for you to scale. And it's hard because um it is not obvious how to do it. It's a really open research question. And Pilot 7 uh by no means solved this question of how do we get the best model that can perform many tasks, uh but I think it's a meaningful uh improvement. And so, I think we're in the age of scaling for robotics. Um and when people talk about scaling, usually there are two main axes. There's data and there's model. And one challenge is that it's it's not obvious what do you scale, and then how do you scale it? Uh for example, if you"}, {"t": 181, "text": "take all of the data um that you might have in robotics and you train the model on it, most likely it won't be a great model. It won't be as good as a specialist model that has been really heavily tuned and optimized for a particular scenario. And you know, if you [clears throat] look at face value, what that means is uh more data doesn't equal uh uh more uh performing model. So, um the majority of the rest of the talk will focus on uh how Pilot 7 was able to produce a single checkpoint that can match specialist performance. So, if you look at the top right of this um slide here, what this is showing is a figure from one of our previous work called RL token. Um X axis is the episode length of episodes in during evaluation and Y axis"}, {"t": 242, "text": "are um success rates. Um what this is illustrating is that the green are demonstrating the speed of a human teleoperator controlling the robot. Um the slightly pink the base policy um is illustrating the speed of a policy trained on the teleoperation data, which is actually slower than the speed of the human teleoperator, um which is kind of interesting, right? Because you would expect that good imitation learned policy will at least reproduce the speed that you see in um your training data. And then the yellow um which RL token wanted to show was that if you perform online reinforcement learning, you can get the speed to be superhuman in this case, um where you can see that the yellow plots are to the left of the green plots. Um so one original question that you can ask here is first, how is it possible"}, {"t": 302, "text": "that um you have a data set, you take a policy, you train on that data set, and the policy is actually slower than the data set? And how is it that um how do you get a generalist policy that have the same speed as the specialist that were trained using reinforcement learning? So here comes PIO 7. PIO 7 in terms of the architecture of the actual um action drive generation component of the system has not changed significantly from our prior work. Um it's very similar to PIO 6, uh very similar to PIO 5. We use a language model and a pre-trained vision encoder to and then we kind of graph an action expert onto it to teach the model on how to speak robot's language, if you will. So, the model takes in a prompt, for example, clean the kitchen."}, {"t": 360, "text": "Um it might also additionally take in a subtask from which indicates a subtask that if you do it and if you finish, you know, the rest of the subtask, you will finish the task instruction. Um and additionally, there are metadata that indicates the speed and the quality of the episode that you're training on. One other innovation in Pilot 7 is the goal condition capability of the model that leads to a much better generalization that I'll also touch on later. Oh, oops. I can't go back to the Ah, here we are. Um and so, the what I just talked about are the action generation component of the system. There is also a high-level component and the way to think about the high-level component is it's not like a planner. You know, it is the the the high-level which are indicated either by the human or the"}, {"t": 420, "text": "high-level policy or the world model um here are not capable of generating low-level actions themselves, but they are capable of generating plans for um the low-level to figure out what actions to take to execute on those plans. So, to show you the kind of um tasks that the Pilot 7 model are capable of and these are tasks that stress test very different axes. Um there is laundry folding um which has to deal with infinite state space. There is really precise task of screwing um a tiny screw into a robot arm. On the right, there's a task of building a box that require um really precise two-hand coordination. And there's a long-horizon task of taking out the trash on the bottom. Um and the noteworthy thing here is that all of"}, {"t": 480, "text": "this video and all of this task were performed by a single checkpoint um from the PIO 7 pre-training run. These are the kind of figure that I won't go into the detail um but the main takeaway here is first, let's look at the um different policy that are presented here. So, there are there is PIO 7 uh which is the pre-trained model and there is what we call RL specialist and SFT specialist. And to abstract away detail of what you think about this specialist is that these were policies that were specifically tuned to get high performance on specific tasks. Um and you know, if you glance at the slide, you can kind of see that the yellow, which is PIO 7, is either the same or actually better than the specialist."}, {"t": 543, "text": "Um which is the part that I was trying to get across, which is um I think this is the first time where at least for PIO it's possible for us to get a single checkpoint that perform as well as policies that are specifically tuned for different tasks. Now, the even more interesting bit here is that in some of these plots, you see that the yellow is actually better than the specialist. Um which is interesting, right? Which is this is saying that when you have a specialist that have been specifically tuned for a particular scenario um that are already very high performing. Now, you if you can get a generalist to also perform well in those scenario, the generalist can actually perform better. And now, why is that the case? Um that is the case because in large-scale um robotic evaluation, really every evaluation is a generalization evaluation. Um where you try to have"}, {"t": 602, "text": "control experiments and you try to fix the scene as much as possible, but there's al- always going to be some factor that change. And because you have a generalist, um it's going to be generally more robust to subtle change in the environment, um and therefore can actually outperform the specialist. And we we really try our best to make it a apple-to-apple comparison here in terms of data that different model are allowed to see. Now, one of the interesting plot from the PILE 7 paper is this plot on the left here. Um the x-axis is the percentage of data used in training, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; where as you go towards the left of the x-axis, you use fewer data. Um but the data are higher quality. And if you go to the right ax- to the right of the x-axis, you use more data, but the data are"}, {"t": 660, "text": "lower quality. Um so, if when when you go across the x-axis to the right, um you are increasing the volume of data that you train on by adding lower quality data. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And there are two um policies that are being compared. There is PILE 7 without metadata, and we can see here that increasing the data volume actually leads to decrease performance when you go from 80% to 100%. Uh when you add the really low quality data into training, um which is a kind of experimental evidence that um illustrate the point that I made at the beginning, which is that more data is not necessarily better. Um now, the interesting The other interesting bit here is that the second policy that I'm being shown, PyTorch 7 with metadata, there is a huge jump in performance going from 80% to"}, {"t": 720, "text": "100%. Um and this is by adding lower quality data. Um and so this is showing a plot where by representing information to your policy properly, um you can actually benefit from lower quality data and benefit in a pretty dramatic way. Um now, in addition to all of the thing that I just mentioned about high-performing policies, um one other really interesting result from PyTorch 7 is cross-environment transfer. Now, how do we categorize cross-environment transfer here? Let's say you have um these two robot arms here, you collected data for folding a shirt, and this is the task that's hard. This is a task that can require up to like hundreds and hundreds of hours of data because it's a pretty precise manipulation task that also have to deal"}, {"t": 780, "text": "with um with a very large observation space. Um now, with PyTorch 7, it's possible for you to take the policy checkpoint that was trained on only shirt folding data from the robot on the left, and now run it on the robot on the right here, which is um a station of two UR5, and have the station of the two URF performing shirt folding as well. And we've never included any shirt folding data at all um from the biome URF station. So, this is generalization to an unseen task on a unseen robot and task combination. Um and one thing that's interesting to me about this result is that this is not a simple task. Um this is a very difficult task and we were very surprised um how how well the policy works"}, {"t": 840, "text": "in this case. Um this part I haven't touched on the high-level planning aspect of PIO 7. Um what these videos are showing is that if you have a human that's coaching the robot by giving language instruction, you can actually get the system to perform tasks that you haven't collected data in before. And also on completely unseen environment and seen objects. Like this is the task where the human is trying to coach the robot the FRIA sweet potato and the FRIA uh are just not in the training data um at all. Let me skip this slide. Um so at Pi uh we believe that robotic is very far from being solved and we really want"}, {"t": 901, "text": "to help propel the community forward whenever we can. Um and part of that effort is to create the infrastructure for everyone across the world to experiment with robotics. Um so we open sourced Pi 0 and Pi 0.5 and people are always very surprised when they ask me the question, is there any difference between the Pi 0 and Pi 05 model that is open source and the model that researcher uses at Pi? Um and actually there is no difference. Um the checkpoint that we open source for Pi 0 and Pi 05 are also the checkpoint that researcher at Pi uses for their daily work. Um one other thing that I wanted to show, which is in this slide, is this uh blog post that we did called the physical intelligence layer. Uh I'm showing the blog post here with the URL so that you can look it up yourself um"}, {"t": 960, "text": "later on as well. Um The The The reason why this blog post is interesting is you can ask the question um if Pi can build a really great uh robotic model and when Pi does so, how does Pi externalize it? Um that is to say, how does Pi make that intelligence available for the rest of the world to use? Um and as part of understand as part of an exercise to understand how Pi make that model available for the rest of the world to use, we work very closely with robotic company that want to deploy robots today to see if the model and the system that we're building and they're also building are good enough for real task today. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And the first video that Excuse Excuse me. The first video that you see when you go to this blog post is a video of a robot from Weave Robotics that are folding laundry in um a real laundromat in the mission, uh"}, {"t": 1020, "text": "which is where in San Francisco that uh Pi and Weave are in. Um and this is interesting. One reason is because they are completely unseen clothing items and this is performing laundry folding for a real customer order. Uh in the blog post um there are also example of various scenario where the robot has to be somewhat intelligent to recover from either error or to be able to fold a really complex item of clothing. For example, these jeans with really long um pants. Um it's pretty hard to fold because the two legs of the pant would get tangled together. So, that's one example. The other example that the blog post show um is this example of a robot packaging a task. Now, what the task here is um"}, {"t": 1082, "text": "well, I'm not sure how it works in uh where you guys are, but in the US if you order an item from Amazon, it sometimes come in this small soft pouch that the package uh package in. And the task here is that there is a uh tray containing, you know, various kind of items and the robot has to supposed to pick them and put it through this really small gap here uh into the packaging machine. And then when the package is sealed, the robot is supposed to pick up the package and put it over here to be ready to be shipped out. Um and this task is hard because one of the deformable items, like there are can be many different kind of items that the robot is packing. Um the second reason why it's hard is because uh this is not a task that's easily automatable using a classical stack. Uh because, you know, errors can happen. The pouch itself is a small deformable objects. One of the thing that I really love"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "about this video is yeah, um this is daytime, right? This is when uh in the morning when the robot is running. And if you scroll to the end of the video, you can see that it's night time. So, this is a robotic system that performing a real task, packaging real customer order in a real warehouse, and it's running a full day workload. And the majority of it are autonomous. So, I I think this example was pretty extraordinary to me because it didn't even require that much data to get this level of autonomy. And so, the the takeaway here is we want to figure out how do we externalize the intelligence, the model that we're building. And the one way to do so is to work with company that want to deploy robots today and scale the deployment and to work really closely with them. We work as if we're on the same team. It's very free flow of information to see if"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "the system that we built together with this company are good enough for deployment. And one of the really critical aspect in these deployment are the ability to make mistake and to remotely intervene when the system make a mistake so that the system can recover. And one of the key question is can you get to a level of autonomy where you don't need to remotely intervene as much and you're essentially break even from a financial perspective so that you can scale deployment. And I think, you know, for some of the task that we work on with partner company, we are essentially there. So, with that, let me end the presentation. I want to leave some time for questions and answer and discussion. I feel free to ask me anything that's on your mind. And if you want to learn more about us, please go to our website, fi.website, and take a look at our blog post. We put a lot of effort into making them"}, {"t": 1261, "text": "presentable. Thanks for listening. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Hey, so Sergey said that you can get quite far with like a gripper hand, but since you're now incorporating data from humans, is it maybe now time to get like a more dexterous robot? &gt;&gt; Yeah, so the problem with I think what you're referring to multi-finger hand is the following. Um it really depends on whether you want to build hardware or you want to study intelligence today. Because if you want to study intelligence today, then you would like to have scale. Uh we believe that scale is important for intelligence behavior to emerge. Um now, what do you need the scale? In terms of hardware, you need hardware that is cheap, that is reliable, and"}, {"t": 1322, "text": "that is easy to tally up. And if you look at multi-finger hands, you can get two out of three today and not all three. Like you can get a hand that is reliable and easy to tally up, but it's not going to be cheap. Like you know, for the price of one multi-finger hand, you can get maybe six, seven robotic system uh with uh two-finger gripper. Um or you can get a multi-finger hand that is cheap and easy to tally up, but it's not going to be reliable. Like it would break so often that um you know, you would wonder why am I doing this in the first place. Um yeah, and so that's one argument why um if you want to focus on the intelligent question, working using it, like studying that using multi-finger hands um is a little bit of a distraction today. Now, this is not to say that multi-finger hand is not important. Um I think it is, and you"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "know, when the hardware gets to the point where it satisfies those three criteria, then we'll consider working on it. And the other thing is we we probably worked on like thousands and thousands of tasks at Pi at this point, and um we work on really realistic tasks. And we haven't seen that many tasks where you actually need something more than, you know, a two-finger mechanism. &gt;&gt; Uh okay. Uh first, nice presentation. I just wanted to ask about the scaling of this VLA models. Right now, I see that in your paper you are getting some good cross-embodiment generalization results, and the hardware is reaching a point where it's actually very cheap. So, what is stopping you from building like big data generation farms with thousands of people like collecting multitask data, and then training the"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "model that you can just deploy in any home. Do you have like very obvious bottlenecks that you are trying to solve at this point, or you think that we reached this point actually? &gt;&gt; Uh I see. Great question. So, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; let me tackle the last thing in your question first, which is um deployment of robot into the homes. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Now, I want to build a home robot at some point, but I think it is incredibly hard to do, and may not be the right thing to do today. Because I think robots in the home in the limit uh is kind of like uh physical AI complete task. Like I think if that works, then we would have solved uh physical intelligence. Um and so that's the like science answer. There is also a economics answer, where consumer robotics is just really hard and selling"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "into the home in a way that like creating a product that's compelling, that is cheap, that is safe um it's just really hard. And because we believe that robotics today are bottlenecked by motor capability, we want to focus on the research question first. Um right. So, why not the home today? That's the first part. And then the earlier part of your question, why not collect lots of data and have data collection farm? Um we like we're scaling data collections. You know, there are many other company in the world that are scaling up robot data collection. Um the critical question is really what do you scale? Because there are many axes. For example, do you collect data on many different tasks and only a little bit of data per task? Do you or do you collect data on not so many tasks, but lots of data per task? Uh what about human"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "video? Uh do you collect lots of it, too? What about Yumi? Um what about the robot embodiment? Do you collect data on lots of robots or and not as much data per robot or do you collect data with only a few robot platform with lots of data per platform? Um How do you change out the objects? In what environment do you collect the data from? Um like I don't think these questions have an answer today and they really need to be experimentally validated as to what's useful. Um and so you know, we want to be good scientists and we'll study how to scale by running experiments. &gt;&gt; Hi. Thanks for the great presentation. I wanted to ask um regarding the difference between Pi 0.6 and 0.7. So, for 0.6, in the paper, you talk a lot about the problem with interventions during training and like how much human feedback you need. And you also very"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "clearly today highlighted like some of the benefits of 0.7, you know, outperforming some of the specializing specialized tasks and all of that stuff. But I wonder like regard like with regards to training, how much did you were you able to reduce the interventions and how much more efficient is it to train 0.7? Like beyond the performance benefits you showed. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Are you referring to the intervention during data collection or evaluation? &gt;&gt; Evaluation. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Um I don't remember the number off the top of my head. Um the number should be in the paper. And we generally measure uh throughput of the overall systems. Um there should be some intervention number in there as well. Uh my um kind of general summary of the result is that there are just tasks that Pyro 7"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "can perform at a high-level performance that Pyro 6 just can't do at all. Uh for example, there's um screwing a very tiny screw in the robot arms. Um yeah, Pyro 6 is really can't do that task at all uh unless you run specialist post training uh for Pyro 6. Um and there was a figure that I shown where the Pyro 7 model is actually outperforming the Pyro 6 specialist, especially in terms of throughput, because the model is more robust to environment change. And so it it it generalizes better, like maybe the lighting has changed or the object has changed or the presentation itself has changed. Um so that's in terms of intervention during evaluation. Now, you also mentioned the question of training efficiency. Um there's multiple ways to define training efficiency. Um one of the one that I think so lot about is um how much compute going through the"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "training run. Um I would say between prior 06 and prior 07, they're roughly comparable, actually. Um which is great because you get a much more performing model while not having to increase the scale of your compute significantly. I'm not sure that answer your question exactly, happy to answer follow up. &gt;&gt; Yes, that was great. &gt;&gt; Thanks a lot. &gt;&gt; I have one more question. &gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks a lot for your presentation. Um I just heard that P07 has a small word model for action for goal conditioning. And I think directly I'm thinking about Dreamer Zero as a full word action model. And so I'm I'm guessing what are the uh uh differences trade-offs. I [snorts] I guess it's not black and white. So, are you leaning on towards more word action model maybe in the future or because of inference costs, training costs, and data collection, it's not needed? And"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "also, have you reached a fundamental bottleneck with VLA's that would be solved with word models? Or maybe you think that fundamentally there's not a difference um between uh the the the different models. &gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good question. I don't think there is a definitive answer to your question today. Like I think all of the thing that you asked is still open research question. We're exploring some of it, the community exploring some of it. Um I think really what's crucial um when I think about this question is one, uh model capability. For example, you want long context length. and you want a model that's durable. Um you want a model that is high capacity enough to ingest really large data set. Um and I think VOAs or video model can can can do both, but the way that you train them might be quite different. Um so one is yeah, model capability. Um"}, {"t": 1863, "text": "Number two is uh inference time constraint um as you mentioned and the reason why I mentioned inference time constraint here is I actually think it's a very solvable problems. Like if we get to the point where you have a really large model that is intelligent enough to perform task at a very high performance level and then, you know, the only remaining um obstacle is to make the model inference faster. Like I think that's a very solvable problems. Um and so it'll be great if we get to that point. Now, we need to scale up the model. Uh right, like currently the model at single digit billions. I have no doubts this year um we and the community will have low double digit billions and then it's going to go to, you know, mid-range billion uh double digit"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "billion then it'll go to high-range double digit billions. Um and so the model size will continue to increase. Um and I think we'll see much more interesting emotional capability when model size increase. Whether that's a VOA or a video action model that will scale better, like we need to try to scale both up. Like I don't think I have seen enough result in the community to say that one is like obviously the better choice compared to the other. &gt;&gt; Thanks. Do you have time for one last question? One? &gt;&gt; Yeah, go for it. &gt;&gt; Hey, thanks for uh coming here and giving the talk. Uh I was wondering if you could elaborate on what you said before on why you don't want to do a home robot right now. Because in your public demos sometimes like you do go to homes and collect and data on homes and show that you can do some like home tasks to some reasonable success and I think even generalize to new kitchens sometimes."}, {"t": 1980, "text": "So is it that you don't think if you get a reasonable non-100% success rate on home tasks that consumers won't buy it or is it that you're concerned about safety and that you would want to wait until you have a really good model to make sure it doesn't break things or hurt people or why would you not focus on home robots? Because in my mind I would think that consumers would tolerate like a 60% success rate in a robot like loading a dishwasher and folding your laundry because it's so useful and it's fun and there's no like economic output tied to it as it would be in like commercial settings. &gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think the dream of home robot is very real. Like I would like very much to build a home robot at some point too. Now there's a few things in your question. The first things is why I show a lot of demo on home tasks. You know, why is that the case? Well, when we release Pi Zero I think the community thought of us as a laundry company because so many"}, {"t": 2042, "text": "um of the tasks were laundry related tasks. Now we pick home tasks for a very specific reason um is that it's a really good test bed for research and communicating that research. Um it's a good test bed for research because the full complexity of dealing with open-ended environments are there. Um it's a good a set of tasks for communicating the research because one the tasks are very relatable. Like you know, we know robotics, but if um um I were to show a very precise like assembly task to the layperson in the world, like they're not going to be able to really grasp how interesting or how impressive it is. Whereas if they see a laundry folding robot, you know, they kind of get a sense that it's hard and that this would be useful to them eventually, right? So, communicating or communication of the progress to the layperson, like you know, we we can look"}, {"t": 2100, "text": "at an assembly task and understand that it's hard because we know robotics, but that's not the case with the layperson. Um And regarding why not go for home robot immediately right now, I I think the model are not good enough. Um like you know, you might be okay with a 60% success rate dishwasher robot, but you know, someone else might not be. Or you know, someone else might care a lot more about um human-robot interactions. Or someone else might care a lot about oh, the robot should not take up too much space because um you know, they they don't want it to get in their way. So, the um I think diversity in human preference uh is one of the reason why consumer robotic is really hard. Um and we think the bottleneck actually to build a really good home robot eventually is model capability, and we will want to focus on that part of the neck first. Like I I don't think if you take the model today and you try to build a home robot with it, um you would be very"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "successful. I I think that multiple scientific breakthroughs need to happen for that to be true. Now, the the the strategy that we've had is very explicitly general purpose robotics, right? Um and you can think about how in language model, you don't have to solve language before the model are useful. Um and so, we want to be at a position where our surface area for success is really large. And in some of the tasks and some of the environment model are good enough today for deployment and we want to go after them while still building up model capability so that the surface areas for success is larger and it's increasing and eventually home robots will will be one of the thing that the model are good enough for. &gt;&gt; Okay, thanks. All right. I think that's the end for Quan. Let's thank him again, please &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; for amazing talk."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction to PIO7"}, {"t": 69, "title": "Generalist model objectives"}, {"t": 223, "title": "PIO7 architecture and design"}, {"t": 440, "title": "Performance and comparisons"}, {"t": 634, "title": "Data quality experiments"}, {"t": 746, "title": "Cross-embodiment and planning"}, {"t": 847, "title": "Commitment to open research"}, {"t": 961, "title": "Real-world deployment case"}, {"t": 1270, "title": "Q&A and research discussion"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Physical Intelligence (co-founder)"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 27.04.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (64 frames, 1280x720, no text layer), re-extracted on 2026-10-04 from the 720p YouTube recording with the slide repo’s own <code>lectures/extract_slides.py</code> (github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich). That repo’s <code>week09_slides.pdf</code> is only 640x360, so its frame numbering differs from this one.</li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_quan_vuong.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 10: Generalist Robot Policies</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 27.04.2026</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the slide says “Lecture 10”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 9 (week 9, Apr 27).]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 1-3 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 4.]</span></p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 10: Generalist Robot Policies Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 27.04.2026 [Note: the slide says “Lecture 10”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 9 (week 9, Apr 27).] [Note: slides 1-3 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 4.]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-4-foundation-models-in-nlp-frame-2", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Foundation Models in NLP", "title": "Slide 4: Foundation Models in NLP", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>Large Dataset</strong> -&gt; <strong>Foundation Model</strong> -&gt; arrows to Code completion, Question Answering, Translation, …]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: Large Dataset -> Foundation Model -> arrows to Code completion, Question Answering, Translation, …]", "t": 180}, {"id": "slide-5-foundation-models-specialist-models-frame-3", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Foundation Models &gt; Specialist Models", "title": "Slide 5: Foundation Models > Specialist Models", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<p>Left, specialist models:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Translation: Seq2Seq, GNMT… <span class=\"own\">[Figure: unrolled recurrent network]</span></li>\n<li>Object Detection: R-CNN, Fast-RCNN… <span class=\"own\">[Figure: R-CNN pipeline: 1. Input image, 2. Extract region proposals (~2k), 3. Compute CNN features, 4. Classify regions]</span></li>\n<li>Image Captioning: DenseCap, Show &amp; Tell.. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: CNN -&gt; region features -&gt; LSTM captioning pipeline]</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>-&gt; <span class=\"own\">[arrow]</span> <strong>Transformer</strong> <span class=\"own\">[Figure: one block with a row of <strong>Images</strong> tokens and a row of <strong>Language</strong> tokens]</span> Vision-Language Models: ViLBERT, Flamingo, BLIP, GPT-4V…</p>", "text": "Left, specialist models: Translation: Seq2Seq, GNMT… [Figure: unrolled recurrent network] Object Detection: R-CNN, Fast-RCNN… [Figure: R-CNN pipeline: 1. Input image, 2. Extract region proposals (~2k), 3. Compute CNN features, 4. Classify regions] Image Captioning: DenseCap, Show & Tell.. [Figure: CNN -> region features -> LSTM captioning pipeline] -> [arrow] Transformer [Figure: one block with a row of Images tokens and a row of Language tokens] Vision-Language Models: ViLBERT, Flamingo, BLIP, GPT-4V…", "t": 180}, {"id": "slide-6-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-frames-4-7", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies", "title": "Slide 6: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies", "frames": [4, 7], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Large Datasets</th>\n<th>Large Models</th>\n<th>Scalable Evaluation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Robot Data is <strong>Scarce</strong></td>\n<td>Robot Data is <strong>Multimodal &amp; Heterogeneous</strong></td>\n<td>Robot Evals are <strong>Tedious &amp; Expensive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collecting Data requires <strong>Human Supervision</strong></td>\n<td>Robots need <strong>High Frequency Control</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Difficult to reproduce</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: pipeline along the bottom: a dataset cylinder -&gt; <strong>Robot Foundation Model</strong> -&gt; collage of robots deployed in many environments. The three columns appear one per build; same slide as week 1, slide 35.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8.]</span></p>", "text": "Large Datasets Large Models Scalable Evaluation Robot Data is Scarce Robot Data is Multimodal & Heterogeneous Robot Evals are Tedious & Expensive Collecting Data requires Human Supervision Robots need High Frequency Control Difficult to reproduce [Figure: pipeline along the bottom: a dataset cylinder -> Robot Foundation Model -> collage of robots deployed in many environments. The three columns appear one per build; same slide as week 1, slide 35.] [Note: slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8.]", "t": 240}, {"id": "slide-8-key-idea-leverage-existing-robot-datasets-frames-8-9", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Key Idea: Leverage Existing Robot Datasets", "title": "Slide 8: Key Idea: Leverage Existing Robot Datasets", "frames": [8, 9], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a cluster of circles of different sizes, one per existing robot dataset; the large ones are labelled Fractal, Bridge V2, RoboTurk and RoboNet, the small ones are unlabelled.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 9) draws one large ellipse around all the circles and adds:]</span> <strong>Aggregate</strong> existing data into <strong>common format</strong></p>", "text": "[Figure: a cluster of circles of different sizes, one per existing robot dataset; the large ones are labelled Fractal, Bridge V2, RoboTurk and RoboNet, the small ones are unlabelled.] [Second build (frame 9) draws one large ellipse around all the circles and adds:] Aggregate existing data into common format", "t": 480}, {"id": "slide-9-large-robot-data-open-x-embodiment-frame-10", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Large Robot Data: Open X-Embodiment", "title": "Slide 9: Large Robot Data: Open X-Embodiment", "frames": [10, 10], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a banner of the logos of the contributing institutions, e.g. ASU, Carnegie Mellon, Columbia, DLR, ETH zürich, Flexiv, Google DeepMind, Google Research, Intrinsic, Illinois, NYU, Imperial College London, IIT, KAIST, Stanford, Berkeley, Universität Freiburg, UTN, UT Austin, Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems, QUT, Shanghai Jiao Tong, UC San Diego, USC, Toyota Research Institute.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[robot emoji]</span> <strong>1M+</strong> Real Robot Episodes</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arm emoji]</span> <strong>22</strong> Robot Embodiments</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[building emoji]</span> <strong>34</strong> Research Labs</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[picture emoji]</span> <strong>300+</strong> Scenes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models</em>, Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, …, <strong>Mees</strong> et al., ICRA, 2024. <strong>Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers)</strong></p>", "text": "[Figure: a banner of the logos of the contributing institutions, e.g. ASU, Carnegie Mellon, Columbia, DLR, ETH zürich, Flexiv, Google DeepMind, Google Research, Intrinsic, Illinois, NYU, Imperial College London, IIT, KAIST, Stanford, Berkeley, Universität Freiburg, UTN, UT Austin, Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems, QUT, Shanghai Jiao Tong, UC San Diego, USC, Toyota Research Institute.] [robot emoji] 1M+ Real Robot Episodes [arm emoji] 22 Robot Embodiments [building emoji] 34 Research Labs [picture emoji] 300+ Scenes Reference: Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models , Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, …, Mees et al., ICRA, 2024. Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers)", "t": 480}, {"id": "slide-10-sparks-of-a-generalist-robot-policy-frame-11", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Sparks of a Generalist Robot Policy", "title": "Slide 10: Sparks of a Generalist Robot Policy", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a video still of a robot arm at a desk with a drawer and buttons, captioned “4. ‘Place the purple block inside the drawer’” (2x), labelled “My PhD robot setup” with an arrow to the AiS column of the chart below.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: grouped bar chart of success rate (0-100) per evaluation, comparing the Original Method, RT-1 and RT-1-X:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evaluation (lab)</th>\n<th>Original Method</th>\n<th>RT-1</th>\n<th>RT-1-X</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Kitchen Manipulation (CLVR; original: MVP BC-RNN)</td>\n<td>43</td>\n<td>48</td>\n<td>63</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cable Routing (RAIL; original: Resnet + MLP)</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NYU Door Opening (CILVR; original: VINN)</td>\n<td>53</td>\n<td>65</td>\n<td>80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autolab UR5 (AUTOLAB)</td>\n<td>53</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Task-Agnostic Play (AiS; original: TACORL, HULC2)</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>68</td>\n<td>72</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mean (“Multi-robot &amp; Multi-dataset”)</td>\n<td>41</td>\n<td>44</td>\n<td>63</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the colour legend has four original methods (MVP BC-RNN, Resnet + MLP, VINN, TACORL/HULC2) for five tasks; the Autolab UR5 original method is not named.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models</em>, Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, …, <strong>Mees</strong> et al., ICRA, 2024. <strong>Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers)</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 11 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 10 to 12.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: a video still of a robot arm at a desk with a drawer and buttons, captioned “4. ‘Place the purple block inside the drawer’” (2x), labelled “My PhD robot setup” with an arrow to the AiS column of the chart below.] [Figure: grouped bar chart of success rate (0-100) per evaluation, comparing the Original Method, RT-1 and RT-1-X:] Evaluation (lab) Original Method RT-1 RT-1-X Kitchen Manipulation (CLVR; original: MVP BC-RNN) 43 48 63 Cable Routing (RAIL; original: Resnet + MLP) 24 18 56 NYU Door Opening (CILVR; original: VINN) 53 65 80 Autolab UR5 (AUTOLAB) 53 25 45 Task-Agnostic Play (AiS; original: TACORL, HULC2) 33 68 72 Mean (“Multi-robot & Multi-dataset”) 41 44 63 [Note: the colour legend has four original methods (MVP BC-RNN, Resnet + MLP, VINN, TACORL/HULC2) for five tasks; the Autolab UR5 original method is not named.] Reference: Open X-Embodiment: Robotic Learning Datasets and RT-X Models , Open X-Embodiment Collaboration, …, Mees et al., ICRA, 2024. Best Conference Paper Award (out of 1765 papers) [Note: slide 11 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 10 to 12.]", "t": 540}, {"id": "slide-12-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-large-models-frame-12", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies <span class=\"own\">[Large Models]</span>", "title": "Slide 12: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [Large Models]", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[The slide 6 table, with the first column now reading “Leverage <strong>Existing Robot Data</strong>” over a thumbnail of the Open X-Embodiment summary figure (1M Episodes from 311 Scenes, 34 Research Labs across 21 Institutions, 22 Embodiments, 527 Skills, 60 Datasets). The <strong>Large Models</strong> column is outlined: Robot Data is <strong>Multimodal &amp; Heterogeneous</strong>; Robots need <strong>High Frequency Control</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[The slide 6 table, with the first column now reading “Leverage Existing Robot Data ” over a thumbnail of the Open X-Embodiment summary figure (1M Episodes from 311 Scenes, 34 Research Labs across 21 Institutions, 22 Embodiments, 527 Skills, 60 Datasets). The Large Models column is outlined: Robot Data is Multimodal & Heterogeneous ; Robots need High Frequency Control .]", "t": 660}, {"id": "untitled-generalist-robot-policy---plug-in-inputs-and-outputs-frames-13-16", "num": null, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "<span class=\"own\">[untitled: Generalist Robot Policy - plug-in inputs and outputs]</span>", "title": "[untitled: Generalist Robot Policy - plug-in inputs and outputs]", "frames": [13, 16], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a jigsaw-puzzle diagram. A central piece, <strong>Generalist Robot Policy</strong>, has input slots on the left and an output slot on the right. Build 1 (frame 13): inputs <strong>Language Instruction</strong> (a person speaking) and <strong>Wrist &amp; 3rd Person Camera</strong>; output <strong>End-Effector Control</strong> (a gripper with arrows); a heading being typed in, “N”. Build 2 (frame 14), heading “<strong>New Observation</strong>”: the language piece is swapped for a <strong>Goal Image</strong>. Build 3 (frame 15), heading “<strong>New Action Space</strong>”: the camera piece is swapped for <strong>Proprio</strong> and the output for <strong>Joint Control</strong> (a robot arm with arrows at the joints). Build 4 (frame 16): a box overlays the diagram:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Robot Data is <strong>Heterogeneous</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diverse</strong> Sensors</li>\n<li><strong>Diverse</strong> Actuators</li>\n<li><strong>Diverse</strong> Control Frequencies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[These four frames show no slide number.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: a jigsaw-puzzle diagram. A central piece, Generalist Robot Policy , has input slots on the left and an output slot on the right. Build 1 (frame 13): inputs Language Instruction (a person speaking) and Wrist & 3rd Person Camera ; output End-Effector Control (a gripper with arrows); a heading being typed in, “N”. Build 2 (frame 14), heading “ New Observation ”: the language piece is swapped for a Goal Image . Build 3 (frame 15), heading “ New Action Space ”: the camera piece is swapped for Proprio and the output for Joint Control (a robot arm with arrows at the joints). Build 4 (frame 16): a box overlays the diagram:] Robot Data is Heterogeneous Diverse Sensors Diverse Actuators Diverse Control Frequencies [These four frames show no slide number.]", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-14-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-17", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: policy inputs and outputs]</span>", "title": "Slide 14: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "frames": [17, 17], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a camera image of a tabletop (towel, spoon, toy watermelon slice) and the instruction “Pick up the spoon” both feed a <strong>Policy</strong> box, which outputs ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> (picture of a robot arm). Same figure as week 1, slide 30.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 13 and 15 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 12 to 14 and from 14 to 16.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: a camera image of a tabletop (towel, spoon, toy watermelon slice) and the instruction “Pick up the spoon” both feed a Policy box, which outputs ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … (picture of a robot arm). Same figure as week 1, slide 30.] [Note: slides 13 and 15 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 12 to 14 and from 14 to 16.]", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-16-untitled-vision-language-model-inputs-and-outputs-frame-18", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]</span>", "title": "Slide 16: [untitled: vision-language model inputs and outputs]", "frames": [18, 18], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a photo of the Statue of Liberty and the New York skyline plus the prompt “Caption the scene” feed a <strong>Vision-Language Model</strong> box, which outputs “The picture shows the Statue of Liberty in NY”. Same figure as week 1, slide 32.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: a photo of the Statue of Liberty and the New York skyline plus the prompt “Caption the scene” feed a Vision-Language Model box, which outputs “The picture shows the Statue of Liberty in NY”. Same figure as week 1, slide 32.]", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-17-key-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-19", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "title": "Slide 17: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three groups of tokens, <strong>Language</strong> (green), <strong>Image</strong> (blue), <strong>Action</strong> (purple), above the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and “ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>”. Same figure as week 1, slide 33.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: three groups of tokens, Language (green), Image (blue), Action (purple), above the instruction “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop camera image, and “ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ”. Same figure as week 1, slide 33.] [Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-19-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-frames-20-21", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Architecture", "title": "Slide 19: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture", "frames": [20, 21], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two tokenizer panels from the Octo paper. <strong>Task Tokens</strong>: the instruction “Put the knife on the plate” -&gt; <strong>Language Encoder</strong> -&gt; tokens, plus position embeddings <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; task tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒯</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{T}_T</annotation></semantics></math>. <strong>Observation Tokens</strong>: a camera image cut into patches -&gt; <strong>CNN</strong> -&gt; tokens, plus position embeddings <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math> -&gt; observation tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒯</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{T}_o</annotation></semantics></math>. Frame 21 moves the panels to the left edge to make room for the next build.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy</em>, Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et al., RSS, 2024.</p>", "text": "[Figure: two tokenizer panels from the Octo paper. Task Tokens : the instruction “Put the knife on the plate” -> Language Encoder -> tokens, plus position embeddings p -> task tokens 𝒯 T . Observation Tokens : a camera image cut into patches -> CNN -> tokens, plus position embeddings p -> observation tokens 𝒯 o . Frame 21 moves the panels to the left edge to make room for the next build.] Reference: Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy , Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, Mees* et al., RSS, 2024.", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-20-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-transformer-frame-22", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Architecture <span class=\"own\">[transformer]</span>", "title": "Slide 20: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [transformer]", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the task and observation tokenizer panels on the left; the task tokens (green) and observation tokens (blue) enter the <strong>Octo Transformer</strong>, “~ (ViT)”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy</em>, Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et al., RSS, 2024.</p>", "text": "[Figure: the task and observation tokenizer panels on the left; the task tokens (green) and observation tokens (blue) enter the Octo Transformer , “~ (ViT)”.] Reference: Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy , Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, Mees* et al., RSS, 2024.", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-21-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-readout-and-action-head-frame-23", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Architecture <span class=\"own\">[readout and action head]</span>", "title": "Slide 21: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [readout and action head]", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the Octo Transformer now takes <strong>Task</strong>, <strong>Observation</strong> and a <strong>Readout</strong> token; the readout output goes into an <strong>Action Head</strong> -&gt; a.]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta(a_t \\mid s_t, s_g)</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_g</annotation></semantics></math>]</span> goal image, language</li>\n<li>Goal state conditioned BC</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy</em>, Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et al., RSS, 2024.</p>", "text": "[Figure: the Octo Transformer now takes Task , Observation and a Readout token; the readout output goes into an Action Head -> a.] π θ ( a t ∣ s t , s g ) [arrow at s g ] goal image, language Goal state conditioned BC Reference: Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy , Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, Mees* et al., RSS, 2024.", "t": 960}, {"id": "slide-22-octopus-emoji-octo-architecture-diffusion-action-head-frame-24", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Architecture <span class=\"own\">[diffusion action head]</span>", "title": "Slide 22: [octopus emoji] Octo Architecture [diffusion action head]", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the same transformer, with further faded blocks of observation and readout tokens to the right (later time steps); the readout token now feeds a <strong>Diffusion Action Head</strong> -&gt; a.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy</em>, Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et al., RSS, 2024.</p>", "text": "[Figure: the same transformer, with further faded blocks of observation and readout tokens to the right (later time steps); the readout token now feeds a Diffusion Action Head -> a.] Reference: Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy , Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, Mees* et al., RSS, 2024.", "t": 960}, {"id": "slide-23-octopus-emoji-octo-design-decisions-frame-25", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Design Decisions", "title": "Slide 23: [octopus emoji] Octo Design Decisions", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Train everything from scratch, most params in Transformer</li>\n<li>Align gripper action across OXE datasets</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Gripper action conventions in OXE - Typical motion (open -&gt; closed to grasp -&gt; open to release), four dataset conventions”, each with a small step plot:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Convention</th>\n<th>Datasets</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Absolute, +1 open / 0 closed</td>\n<td>Taco, Austin Sailor, Austin Sirius, NYU Franka Play</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Absolute, 0 open / 1 closed (inverted)</td>\n<td>Roboturk, Viola, Stanford Hydra, BC-Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relative deltas, +1 opening / -1 closing</td>\n<td>RT-1, Kuka, Jaco Play, Berkeley AutoLab UR5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous with transition ramps</td>\n<td>Bridge - intermediate values during opening/closing (orange points: analog values during transitions)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Octo aligns all four to a single convention. Target: absolute, +1 open / 0 closed</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 24 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 25.]</span></p>", "text": "Train everything from scratch, most params in Transformer Align gripper action across OXE datasets [Figure: “Gripper action conventions in OXE - Typical motion (open -> closed to grasp -> open to release), four dataset conventions”, each with a small step plot:] Convention Datasets Absolute, +1 open / 0 closed Taco, Austin Sailor, Austin Sirius, NYU Franka Play Absolute, 0 open / 1 closed (inverted) Roboturk, Viola, Stanford Hydra, BC-Z Relative deltas, +1 opening / -1 closing RT-1, Kuka, Jaco Play, Berkeley AutoLab UR5 Continuous with transition ramps Bridge - intermediate values during opening/closing (orange points: analog values during transitions) Octo aligns all four to a single convention. Target: absolute, +1 open / 0 closed [Note: slide 24 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 25.]", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-25-the-eureka-moment-frames-26-28", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: The Eureka Moment", "title": "Slide 25: The Eureka Moment", "frames": [26, 28], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an embedded phone video of a laptop showing a video call (“Octo on Meta Hacking”): one participant in a lab, a robot arm at a table with fruit (the robot running Octo), and Oier Mees with two colleagues reacting and giving a thumbs-up.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: an embedded phone video of a laptop showing a video call (“Octo on Meta Hacking”): one participant in a lab, a robot arm at a table with fruit (the robot running Octo), and Oier Mees with two colleagues reacting and giving a thumbs-up.]", "t": 1321}, {"id": "slide-26-untitled-transition-question-frame-29", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 26: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<p><strong>How To Adapt To New Obs/Action Spaces?</strong></p>", "text": "How To Adapt To New Obs/Action Spaces?", "t": 1321}, {"id": "slide-27-octopus-emoji-octo-finetuning-frame-30", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: <span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji]</span> Octo Finetuning", "title": "Slide 27: [octopus emoji] Octo Finetuning", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<p><strong>Adaptable</strong> pre-trained representation for <strong>finetuning</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the task and observation tokenizers on the left. Top, “Pre-Training”: the Octo Transformer over Task, Observation, Readout, Observation, Readout, Observation tokens, each readout feeding an Action Head -&gt; a. Bottom, “Finetuning”: the same transformer with new observation token blocks (“New Observation”) and a new readout (“New Action Space”) feeding a “New Action Head” -&gt; a; the original task tokens are faded.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy</em>, Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, <strong>Mees*</strong> et al., RSS, 2024.</p>", "text": "Adaptable pre-trained representation for finetuning [Figure: the task and observation tokenizers on the left. Top, “Pre-Training”: the Octo Transformer over Task, Observation, Readout, Observation, Readout, Observation tokens, each readout feeding an Action Head -> a. Bottom, “Finetuning”: the same transformer with new observation token blocks (“New Observation”) and a new readout (“New Action Space”) feeding a “New Action Head” -> a; the original task tokens are faded.] Reference: Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Policy , Octo Model Team, Ghosh*, Walke*, Pertsch*, Black*, Mees* et al., RSS, 2024.", "t": 1380}, {"id": "slide-28-adoption-of-octo-frame-31", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Adoption of Octo", "title": "Slide 28: Adoption of Octo", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Key: RFMs learn <strong>better representations</strong> for <strong>transfer</strong></li>\n<li>Researchers finetune to their robot with 50 demos</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"im\">from</span> octo <span class=\"im\">import</span> OctoModel</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>model <span class=\"op\">=</span> OctoModel.load_pretrained(<span class=\"st\">&quot;hf://rail-berkeley/octo-base&quot;</span>)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two community examples. Left, a Spot quadruped robot with an arm next to a vacuum cleaner, “Source: Peter Mitrano via Twitter”. Right, a humanoid upper body placing a green bell pepper on a plate (“Both videos are playing at 4x”), “Source: Tokyo Robotics”.]</span></p>", "text": "Key: RFMs learn better representations for transfer Researchers finetune to their robot with 50 demos from octo import OctoModel model = OctoModel.load_pretrained( &quot;hf://rail-berkeley/octo-base&quot; ) [Figure: two community examples. Left, a Spot quadruped robot with an arm next to a vacuum cleaner, “Source: Peter Mitrano via Twitter”. Right, a humanoid upper body placing a green bell pepper on a plate (“Both videos are playing at 4x”), “Source: Tokyo Robotics”.]", "t": 1380}, {"id": "slide-29-cross-embodiment-learning-frame-32", "num": 29, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 29: Cross-Embodiment Learning", "title": "Slide 29: Cross-Embodiment Learning", "frames": [32, 32], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How “generalist” is a single arm manipulation policy?</li>\n<li>Can we <strong>learn from more robot embodiments</strong>?</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer</strong> knowledge across embodiments</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: jigsaw diagram: <strong>Large Robot Dataset</strong> -&gt; <strong>Generalist Robot Policy</strong> -&gt; output pieces Quadrupeds, Single Arms, Navigation, Quadcopters, each with small robot icons.]</span></p>", "text": "How “generalist” is a single arm manipulation policy? Can we learn from more robot embodiments ? Transfer knowledge across embodiments [Figure: jigsaw diagram: Large Robot Dataset -> Generalist Robot Policy -> output pieces Quadrupeds, Single Arms, Navigation, Quadcopters, each with small robot icons.]", "t": 1500}, {"id": "slide-30-one-policy-for-manipulation-navigation-locomotion-aviation-frames-33-34", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: One Policy for Manipulation, Navigation, Locomotion &amp; Aviation", "title": "Slide 30: One Policy for Manipulation, Navigation, Locomotion & Aviation", "frames": [33, 34], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a collage of robot-data thumbnails under a box: [robot emoji] 900k Robot Trajectories - Navigation, Locomotion, Manipulation, Bimanual. Second build (frame 34): an arrow into the <strong>Cross-Embodied Transformer</strong> box - Flexible Observation Spaces; Flexible Action Spaces <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1400</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\in [2, \\dots, 1400]</annotation></semantics></math>; Control Freq. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Hz</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Hz</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\in [5\\text{Hz}, \\dots, 50\\text{Hz}]</annotation></semantics></math> - which fans out to Quadrupeds, Single Arms, Navigation, Bimanual Arms and Quadcopters (robot icons).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Scaling Cross-Embodied Learning: One Policy for Manipulation, Navigation, Locomotion and Aviation</em>, Doshi*, Walke*, <strong>Mees</strong>, Dasari and Levine. CoRL 2024. <strong>Oral, top %4 of 670 papers</strong>.</p>", "text": "[Figure: a collage of robot-data thumbnails under a box: [robot emoji] 900k Robot Trajectories - Navigation, Locomotion, Manipulation, Bimanual. Second build (frame 34): an arrow into the Cross-Embodied Transformer box - Flexible Observation Spaces; Flexible Action Spaces ∈ [ 2 , … , 1400 ] ; Control Freq. ∈ [ 5 Hz , … , 50 Hz ] - which fans out to Quadrupeds, Single Arms, Navigation, Bimanual Arms and Quadcopters (robot icons).] Reference: Scaling Cross-Embodied Learning: One Policy for Manipulation, Navigation, Locomotion and Aviation , Doshi*, Walke*, Mees , Dasari and Levine. CoRL 2024. Oral, top %4 of 670 papers .", "t": 1560}, {"id": "slide-31-crossformer-model-frame-35", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: CrossFormer Model", "title": "Slide 31: CrossFormer Model", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<ul>\n<li>No action space alignment required</li>\n<li>Can consume data from <em>any</em> robot embodiment</li>\n<li>Maximize parameter sharing across embodiments</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, “Observation Image Tokenization”: the instruction “Sweep the objects into the dustpan” goes through a Language Encoder and conditions (FiLM Conditioning) a <strong>ResNet</strong> that encodes the Current Image and Goal Image into tokens <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒯</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{T}_o</annotation></semantics></math> with position embeddings <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Right, the <strong>Cross-Embodied Transformer</strong> over Observation Tokens (Workspace Image, Navigation Image, Wrist Image, Quadruped Proprio, Bimanual Proprio) and Readout Tokens; separate readouts feed a Quadruped Action Head, Single Arm Action Head, Navigation Action Head and Bimanual Action Head, each with its robots.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33.]</span></p>", "text": "No action space alignment required Can consume data from any robot embodiment Maximize parameter sharing across embodiments [Figure: left, “Observation Image Tokenization”: the instruction “Sweep the objects into the dustpan” goes through a Language Encoder and conditions (FiLM Conditioning) a ResNet that encodes the Current Image and Goal Image into tokens 𝒯 o with position embeddings p . Right, the Cross-Embodied Transformer over Observation Tokens (Workspace Image, Navigation Image, Wrist Image, Quadruped Proprio, Bimanual Proprio) and Readout Tokens; separate readouts feed a Quadruped Action Head, Single Arm Action Head, Navigation Action Head and Bimanual Action Head, each with its robots.] [Note: slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33.]", "t": 1740}, {"id": "slide-33-quantitative-results-frame-36", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Quantitative Results", "title": "Slide 33: Quantitative Results", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<p>Key: matches and <strong>outperforms specialist policies</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: grouped bar chart of success rate (0-1) for the Best Prior Method, a Single-Robot Dataset policy and CrossFormer. Values read from the bars, approximate:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Best Prior Method</th>\n<th>Single-Robot Dataset</th>\n<th>CrossFormer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Average (highlighted)</td>\n<td>~0.52</td>\n<td>~0.62</td>\n<td>~0.70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WidowX</td>\n<td>~0.21</td>\n<td>~0.21</td>\n<td>~0.25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Franka</td>\n<td>~0.52</td>\n<td>~0.41</td>\n<td>~0.41</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ALOHA</td>\n<td>~0.70</td>\n<td>~0.50</td>\n<td>~0.80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LoCoBot</td>\n<td>~0.48</td>\n<td>~0.92</td>\n<td>~0.93</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Go1</td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[no bar]</span></td>\n<td>~1.0</td>\n<td>~1.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TELLO</td>\n<td>~0.68</td>\n<td>~0.68</td>\n<td>~0.82</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35.]</span></p>", "text": "Key: matches and outperforms specialist policies [Figure: grouped bar chart of success rate (0-1) for the Best Prior Method, a Single-Robot Dataset policy and CrossFormer. Values read from the bars, approximate:] Best Prior Method Single-Robot Dataset CrossFormer Average (highlighted) ~0.52 ~0.62 ~0.70 WidowX ~0.21 ~0.21 ~0.25 Franka ~0.52 ~0.41 ~0.41 ALOHA ~0.70 ~0.50 ~0.80 LoCoBot ~0.48 ~0.92 ~0.93 Go1 [no bar] ~1.0 ~1.0 TELLO ~0.68 ~0.68 ~0.82 [Note: slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35.]", "t": 1740}, {"id": "slide-35-untitled-transition-question-frame-37", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 35: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<p>Everything so far trains on robot data <strong>from scratch</strong></p>\n<p>Do <strong>internet-scale priors like VLMs transfer to control?</strong></p>", "text": "Everything so far trains on robot data from scratch Do internet-scale priors like VLMs transfer to control?", "t": 1981}, {"id": "slide-36-recap-llava-frame-38", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Recap Llava", "title": "Slide 36: Recap Llava", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Prepends image tokens to the text sequence, LLM processes everything in single unified self-attention pass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the LLaVA pipeline from week 7, slide 23: image input -&gt; <strong>CLIP vision encoder</strong> (always frozen; patch embeddings) -&gt; <strong>MLP connector</strong> (projection layer; trainable) -&gt; 196 image tokens prepended to the text tokens from the text prompt (“describe this”) -&gt; <strong>LLM (LLaMA)</strong>: self-attention over all tokens jointly, standard autoregressive generation, no new attention mechanisms -&gt; text output “a cat sitting on a mat”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Visual Instruction Tuning</em>, Liu et al. (2023)</p>", "text": "Prepends image tokens to the text sequence, LLM processes everything in single unified self-attention pass [Figure: the LLaVA pipeline from week 7, slide 23: image input -> CLIP vision encoder (always frozen; patch embeddings) -> MLP connector (projection layer; trainable) -> 196 image tokens prepended to the text tokens from the text prompt (“describe this”) -> LLM (LLaMA) : self-attention over all tokens jointly, standard autoregressive generation, no new attention mechanisms -> text output “a cat sitting on a mat”.] Reference: Visual Instruction Tuning , Liu et al. (2023)", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-37-untitled-transition-question-frame-39", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 37: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<p>LLaVA treats images as another token sequence the LLM can attend to</p>\n<p><strong>Can we treat robot actions the same way?</strong></p>", "text": "LLaVA treats images as another token sequence the LLM can attend to Can we treat robot actions the same way?", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-38-vlm---vision-language-action-model-vla-frame-40", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: VLM -&gt; Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA)", "title": "Slide 38: VLM -> Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA)", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pretrained VLM</th>\n<th>-&gt; Fine-tune on robot data -&gt;</th>\n<th>Fine-tuned into VLA</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Image (RGB pixels) -&gt; Vision (ViT / CLIP)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Image (Robot camera) -&gt; Vision (Same encoder)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text (Instruction) -&gt; Tokenizer (Subword IDs)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Text (Task command) -&gt; Tokenizer (Same vocab)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LLM transformer (Autoregressive decoder)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>LLM transformer (Same weights, fine-tuned)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language tokens (Vocab of ~32k IDs)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Action tokens (256 reused vocab IDs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output: natural language answer, e.g. “The cat is on the mat.”</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>“1 128 91 241 5 101 127” de-tokenize -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta y, \\Delta z, \\Delta\\theta, \\text{grip}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the example token string has 7 numbers but the de-tokenized action lists 5 values; a typical 7-DoF action would add <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> roll/pitch or similar.]</span></p>", "text": "Pretrained VLM -> Fine-tune on robot data -> Fine-tuned into VLA Image (RGB pixels) -> Vision (ViT / CLIP) Image (Robot camera) -> Vision (Same encoder) Text (Instruction) -> Tokenizer (Subword IDs) Text (Task command) -> Tokenizer (Same vocab) LLM transformer (Autoregressive decoder) LLM transformer (Same weights, fine-tuned) Language tokens (Vocab of ~32k IDs) Action tokens (256 reused vocab IDs) Output: natural language answer, e.g. “The cat is on the mat.” “1 128 91 241 5 101 127” de-tokenize -> [ Δ x , Δ y , Δ z , Δ θ , grip ] [Note: the example token string has 7 numbers but the de-tokenized action lists 5 values; a typical 7-DoF action would add Δ roll/pitch or similar.]", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-39-recap-robot-action-tokenization-for-vlas-frame-41", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: Recap: Robot Action Tokenization for VLAs", "title": "Slide 39: Recap: Robot Action Tokenization for VLAs", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Same content as week 7, slide 29 (per-dimension, per-timestep binning): quantile normalization per dimension for robot A (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1 = -0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>99</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{99} = +0.04</annotation></semantics></math>) and robot B (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1 = -1.50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>99</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>2.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{99} = +2.30</annotation></semantics></math>) to a shared <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>+</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1, +1]</annotation></semantics></math> range; 256 equal bins of width <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>256</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0078</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/256 \\approx 0.0078</annotation></semantics></math>; a 7-dim action <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^7</annotation></semantics></math> becomes 7 tokens (183, 107, 240, 018, 155, 072, 201); the 256 action tokens are injected into the language-model vocabulary and trained with next-token prediction.]</span></p>\n<p>References: <em>RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control</em>, Brohan et al. (2023); <em>OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model</em>, Kim et al. (2024)</p>", "text": "[Same content as week 7, slide 29 (per-dimension, per-timestep binning): quantile normalization per dimension for robot A ( Q 1 = − 0.02 , Q 99 = + 0.04 ) and robot B ( Q 1 = − 1.50 , Q 99 = + 2.30 ) to a shared [ − 1 , + 1 ] range; 256 equal bins of width 2 / 256 ≈ 0.0078 ; a 7-dim action a t ∈ ℝ 7 becomes 7 tokens (183, 107, 240, 018, 155, 072, 201); the 256 action tokens are injected into the language-model vocabulary and trained with next-token prediction.] References: RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control , Brohan et al. (2023); OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model , Kim et al. (2024)", "t": 2163}, {"id": "slide-40-tips-tricks-convergence-frame-42", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: Tips &amp; Tricks: Convergence", "title": "Slide 40: Tips & Tricks: Convergence", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>How do I know my VLA training has converged?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three training curves against training step (0-100k+). <strong>Action token accuracy</strong> rises from about 0.05 and flattens near the dashed “95% target” after about 75k steps. <strong>L1 action error</strong> falls from about 0.5 to about 0.09. <strong>L2 action error</strong> falls from about 0.85 to about 0.1.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[arrow at the accuracy plot]</span> Did we pick the exact action bin?</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under the two error plots]</span> How far off are the predicted continuous actions (after detokenization) from ground-truth actions?</li>\n</ul>", "text": "How do I know my VLA training has converged? [Figure: three training curves against training step (0-100k+). Action token accuracy rises from about 0.05 and flattens near the dashed “95% target” after about 75k steps. L1 action error falls from about 0.5 to about 0.09. L2 action error falls from about 0.85 to about 0.1.] [arrow at the accuracy plot] Did we pick the exact action bin? [brace under the two error plots] How far off are the predicted continuous actions (after detokenization) from ground-truth actions?", "t": 2220}, {"id": "slide-41-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-padding-frame-43", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: Tips &amp; Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets <span class=\"own\">[padding]</span>", "title": "Slide 41: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [padding]", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Datasets in OXE have different observation spaces</li>\n<li>Padding &amp; Masking:\n<ul>\n<li>Easiest to implement</li>\n<li>Half your batch will be full of zeros, wasted FLOPS</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Dataset A: 1 third-person camera; Dataset B: 1 third-person + 2 wrist cameras. A batch table with columns 3rd-person Cam, Wrist L Cam, Wrist R Cam, Lang + actions: samples 1-2 (dataset A) have “padding” in both wrist columns; samples 3-4 (dataset B) fill every column.]</span></p>", "text": "Datasets in OXE have different observation spaces Padding & Masking: Easiest to implement Half your batch will be full of zeros, wasted FLOPS [Figure: Dataset A: 1 third-person camera; Dataset B: 1 third-person + 2 wrist cameras. A batch table with columns 3rd-person Cam, Wrist L Cam, Wrist R Cam, Lang + actions: samples 1-2 (dataset A) have “padding” in both wrist columns; samples 3-4 (dataset B) fill every column.]", "t": 2340}, {"id": "slide-42-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-sequence-packing-frame-44", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Tips &amp; Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets <span class=\"own\">[sequence packing]</span>", "title": "Slide 42: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [sequence packing]", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Datasets in OXE have different observation spaces</li>\n<li>Sequence Packing:\n<ul>\n<li>Maximize GPU Throughput</li>\n<li>Impl Complexity: reset positional encodings, block-diagonal att.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: samples 1-2 (A) are “img, lang+act”; samples 3-4 (B) are “img, wrist L, wrist R, lang+act”. They are concatenated into one “Packed view (one sequence)”: S1, S2, S3, S4 back to back, with <code>cu_seqlens = [0, 8, 16, 32, 48]</code> “&lt;- prefix sums of sample lengths”.]</span></p>", "text": "Datasets in OXE have different observation spaces Sequence Packing: Maximize GPU Throughput Impl Complexity: reset positional encodings, block-diagonal att. [Figure: samples 1-2 (A) are “img, lang+act”; samples 3-4 (B) are “img, wrist L, wrist R, lang+act”. They are concatenated into one “Packed view (one sequence)”: S1, S2, S3, S4 back to back, with cu_seqlens = [0, 8, 16, 32, 48] “<- prefix sums of sample lengths”.]", "t": 2401}, {"id": "slide-43-tips-tricks-batching-heterogeneous-datasets-masking-and-positions-frame-45", "num": 43, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 43: Tips &amp; Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets <span class=\"own\">[masking and positions]</span>", "title": "Slide 43: Tips & Tricks: Batching Heterogeneous Datasets [masking and positions]", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Attention must be block-diagonal\n<ul>\n<li>Tokens in sample i can only attend to other tokens in sample i. Without this mask, sample 1 would attend to sample 3’s images.</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a queries x keys grid over S1-S4 where only the diagonal blocks S1↔︎S1, S2↔︎S2, S3↔︎S3, S4↔︎S4 are “Attention allowed”; everything else is “Masked out”.]</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Positional encodings reset at each boundary\n<ul>\n<li>Token 0 of every sample gets position 0 - not its absolute offset in the packed sequence. Otherwise sample 4 would see “position 24+” as out-of-distribution input.</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[Figure: position ids 0 1 | 0 1 | 0 1 2 3 | 0 1 2 3, “positions reset per sample”.]</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>", "text": "Attention must be block-diagonal Tokens in sample i can only attend to other tokens in sample i. Without this mask, sample 1 would attend to sample 3’s images. [Figure: a queries x keys grid over S1-S4 where only the diagonal blocks S1↔︎S1, S2↔︎S2, S3↔︎S3, S4↔︎S4 are “Attention allowed”; everything else is “Masked out”.] Positional encodings reset at each boundary Token 0 of every sample gets position 0 - not its absolute offset in the packed sequence. Otherwise sample 4 would see “position 24+” as out-of-distribution input. [Figure: position ids 0 1 | 0 1 | 0 1 2 3 | 0 1 2 3, “positions reset per sample”.]", "t": 2461}, {"id": "slide-44-tips-tricks-dataloading-frame-46", "num": 44, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 44: Tips &amp; Tricks: Dataloading", "title": "Slide 44: Tips & Tricks: Dataloading", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two loaders. “Sequential reads + shuffle buffer” (Octo, OpenVLA): shards 1-3 are streamed into a shuffle buffer that batches are drawn from; “too small -&gt; correlated batches”. “True random reads” (index-based samplers): random seeks into a flat index <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0 \\dots N)</annotation></semantics></math> to build a batch.]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Sequential + buffer</th>\n<th>True random reads</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I/O pattern</td>\n<td>sequential reads (advantage)</td>\n<td>random seeks (disadvantage) - seek-bound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>throughput</td>\n<td>very high (advantage)</td>\n<td>lower (disadvantage) - bottleneck at scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>randomness</td>\n<td>approximate (disadvantage)</td>\n<td>exact (advantage) - no buffer bias</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>memory</td>\n<td>large buffer in RAM (disadvantage)</td>\n<td>index only (advantage) - lightweight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>obs. history</td>\n<td>free - adjacent (advantage)</td>\n<td>extra seeks (disadvantage) - cost x window size</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "[Figure: two loaders. “Sequential reads + shuffle buffer” (Octo, OpenVLA): shards 1-3 are streamed into a shuffle buffer that batches are drawn from; “too small -> correlated batches”. “True random reads” (index-based samplers): random seeks into a flat index [ 0 … N ) to build a batch.] Sequential + buffer True random reads I/O pattern sequential reads (advantage) random seeks (disadvantage) - seek-bound throughput very high (advantage) lower (disadvantage) - bottleneck at scale randomness approximate (disadvantage) exact (advantage) - no buffer bias memory large buffer in RAM (disadvantage) index only (advantage) - lightweight obs. history free - adjacent (advantage) extra seeks (disadvantage) - cost x window size", "t": 2641}, {"id": "slide-45-tips-tricks-cross-embodiment-heads-frame-47", "num": 45, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 45: Tips &amp; Tricks: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "title": "Slide 45: Tips & Tricks: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CrossFormer-style</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math>-style</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Observations (images + language + proprio) -&gt; Shared transformer backbone -&gt; separate heads: Single-arm, Bimanual, Quadruped, Drone, each with its own action length</td>\n<td>Observations (images + language + proprio) -&gt; Shared transformer backbone -&gt; one <strong>Action expert</strong> with a <strong>padded action space</strong>: single-arm, bimanual, quadruped and drone actions all fill the front of one fixed-length vector, the rest padded</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "CrossFormer-style π 0 -style Observations (images + language + proprio) -> Shared transformer backbone -> separate heads: Single-arm, Bimanual, Quadruped, Drone, each with its own action length Observations (images + language + proprio) -> Shared transformer backbone -> one Action expert with a padded action space : single-arm, bimanual, quadruped and drone actions all fill the front of one fixed-length vector, the rest padded", "t": 2761}, {"id": "slide-46-untitled-transition-question-frame-48", "num": 46, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 46: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 46: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<p><strong>How does the VLA know which embodiment to produce actions for at test time?</strong></p>", "text": "How does the VLA know which embodiment to produce actions for at test time?", "t": 2880}, {"id": "slide-47-cross-embodiment-heads-frame-49", "num": 47, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 47: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "title": "Slide 47: Cross-Embodiment Heads", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CrossFormer-style</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math>-style</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>User specifies head in prompt</td>\n<td>Model infers embodiment from obs, proprio &amp; task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the CrossFormer-style diagram from slide 45: shared backbone, one head per embodiment]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_0</annotation></semantics></math>-style diagram from slide 45: shared backbone, one action expert with a padded action space]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "CrossFormer-style π 0 -style User specifies head in prompt Model infers embodiment from obs, proprio & task [Figure: the CrossFormer-style diagram from slide 45: shared backbone, one head per embodiment] [Figure: the π 0 -style diagram from slide 45: shared backbone, one action expert with a padded action space]", "t": 2941}, {"id": "slide-48-current-vla-recipe-frame-50", "num": 48, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 48: Current VLA Recipe", "title": "Slide 48: Current VLA Recipe", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Next token prediction for VLM: FAST robot actions + Web</li>\n<li>Single action expert: flow matching + stop gradient to VLM</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>VLM backbone</strong> over image tokens (Images: cameras + web), language tokens (NTP loss) and FAST action tokens (Robot actions (FAST), NTP loss). Its output goes, across a “stop gradient” barrier, into an <strong>Action expert</strong> trained with a <strong>flow matching loss</strong>, which outputs continuous actions (e.g. -1.7, 1.25, 3.14, 1.42). Legend: “stop gradient - action expert cannot update backbone”.]</span></p>\n<p>References: <em><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>: a Vision-Language-Action Model with Open-World Generalization Model</em>, Physical Intelligence (2025); <em>Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better</em>, Driess et al., Physical Intelligence (2025)</p>", "text": "Next token prediction for VLM: FAST robot actions + Web Single action expert: flow matching + stop gradient to VLM [Figure: VLM backbone over image tokens (Images: cameras + web), language tokens (NTP loss) and FAST action tokens (Robot actions (FAST), NTP loss). Its output goes, across a “stop gradient” barrier, into an Action expert trained with a flow matching loss , which outputs continuous actions (e.g. -1.7, 1.25, 3.14, 1.42). Legend: “stop gradient - action expert cannot update backbone”.] References: π 0.5 : a Vision-Language-Action Model with Open-World Generalization Model , Physical Intelligence (2025); Knowledge Insulating Vision-Language-Action Models: Train Fast, Run Fast, Generalize Better , Driess et al., Physical Intelligence (2025)", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-49-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-scalable-evaluation-frame-51", "num": 49, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 49: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies <span class=\"own\">[Scalable Evaluation]</span>", "title": "Slide 49: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [Scalable Evaluation]", "frames": [51, 51], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[The slide 12 table, with the <strong>Large Models</strong> column now reading “<strong>Cross-embodied</strong> Policies” over the Octo emoji and a robot collage. The <strong>Scalable Evaluation</strong> column still reads: Robot Evals are <strong>Tedious &amp; Expensive</strong>; <strong>Difficult to reproduce</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[The slide 12 table, with the Large Models column now reading “ Cross-embodied Policies” over the Octo emoji and a robot collage. The Scalable Evaluation column still reads: Robot Evals are Tedious & Expensive ; Difficult to reproduce .]", "t": 3181}, {"id": "slide-50-evaluating-policies-is-expensive-frames-52-53", "num": 50, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 50: Evaluating Policies is Expensive", "title": "Slide 50: Evaluating Policies is Expensive", "frames": [52, 53], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sim evals <strong>democratize research</strong></li>\n<li>Sim evals are <strong>more reproducable</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three video clips captioned “… people bump into cameras,” (a person’s arm knocking a camera next to a robot), “grippers get stuck,” (a gripper stuck on a toy eggplant in a toy sink) and “and real eval is slow and tedious.” (a robot at a toy sink, played at 100x). Frame 52 is an earlier build with only the first clip and no bullets.]</span></p>", "text": "Sim evals democratize research Sim evals are more reproducable [Figure: three video clips captioned “… people bump into cameras,” (a person’s arm knocking a camera next to a robot), “grippers get stuck,” (a gripper stuck on a toy eggplant in a toy sink) and “and real eval is slow and tedious.” (a robot at a toy sink, played at 100x). Frame 52 is an earlier build with only the first clip and no bullets.]", "t": 3301}, {"id": "slide-51-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-frames-54-55", "num": 51, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 51: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation", "title": "Slide 51: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation", "frames": [54, 55], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sim evals are <strong>more reproducable</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>… and make them <strong>SIMPLER</strong> (real -&gt; sim)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: simulated replicas of real evaluation scenes. Google Robot: a counter with a can, an apple and a sponge; a coke can; a cabinet with an open drawer; a cabinet with an apple on top. BridgeData V2: a carrot and a plate; a towel and a spoon; two cubes; a toy sink with an eggplant. Second build (frame 55): a large grid of simulated rollouts with the caption “SIMPLER enables effortless and reproducible eval at scale.”]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy</em>, Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, <strong>Mees†</strong> et al., CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "Sim evals are more reproducable … and make them SIMPLER (real -> sim) [Figure: simulated replicas of real evaluation scenes. Google Robot: a counter with a can, an apple and a sponge; a coke can; a cabinet with an open drawer; a cabinet with an apple on top. BridgeData V2: a carrot and a plate; a towel and a spoon; two cubes; a toy sink with an eggplant. Second build (frame 55): a large grid of simulated rollouts with the caption “SIMPLER enables effortless and reproducible eval at scale.”] Reference: Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy , Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, Mees† et al., CoRL, 2024.", "t": 3420}, {"id": "slide-52-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-real-vs-sim-correlation-frame-56", "num": 52, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 52: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation <span class=\"own\">[real vs sim correlation]</span>", "title": "Slide 52: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [real vs sim correlation]", "frames": [56, 56], "html": "<p><strong>OK, but how meaningful are SIMPLER results?</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: scatter plot of SIMPLER success rate against real success rate for RT-1, RT-1-X, RT-2-X and Octo; the points lie close to a dashed diagonal, with Octo points scattered a little more widely.]</span></p>\n<p>How correlated are real and SIMPLER performance measures?</p>\n<p><strong>Very!</strong></p>\n<p>SIMPLER is a reliable proxy for real robot evaluation.</p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy</em>, Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, <strong>Mees†</strong> et al., CoRL, 2024.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the paper’s title is “Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation”; the trailing word “Policy” in the slide’s citation looks like a slip.]</span></p>", "text": "OK, but how meaningful are SIMPLER results? [Figure: scatter plot of SIMPLER success rate against real success rate for RT-1, RT-1-X, RT-2-X and Octo; the points lie close to a dashed diagonal, with Octo points scattered a little more widely.] How correlated are real and SIMPLER performance measures? Very! SIMPLER is a reliable proxy for real robot evaluation. Reference: Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy , Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, Mees† et al., CoRL, 2024. [Note: the paper’s title is “Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation”; the trailing word “Policy” in the slide’s citation looks like a slip.]", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-53-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-robustness-and-what-matters-frames-57-59", "num": 53, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 53: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation <span class=\"own\">[robustness and what matters]</span>", "title": "Slide 53: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [robustness and what matters]", "frames": [57, 59], "html": "<p><strong>OK, but how meaningful are SIMPLER results?</strong></p>\n<p>SIMPLER can accurately characterize an RT-1 policy’s robustness to various distribution shifts.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distribution shift</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> Real success rate</th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> SIMPLER success rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera pose</td>\n<td>-0.38</td>\n<td>-0.39</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Table texture</td>\n<td>-0.17</td>\n<td>-0.19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background</td>\n<td>-0.17</td>\n<td>-0.12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distractors</td>\n<td>-0.08</td>\n<td>-0.06</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lighting</td>\n<td>-0.04</td>\n<td>-0.07</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Frames 58-59 replace the chart:]</span> <strong>So, what matters for good simulated evaluation?</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Accurate control dynamics via system identification (SysID)\n<ul>\n<li>Same open-loop action sequence: <span class=\"own\">[Figure: three stills of a robot arm and a coke can, captioned Real, Sim w/o SysID (the can ends up somewhere else), Sim after SysID (ours) (matches the real outcome).]</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Reference: <em>Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy</em>, Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, <strong>Mees†</strong> et al., CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "OK, but how meaningful are SIMPLER results? SIMPLER can accurately characterize an RT-1 policy’s robustness to various distribution shifts. Distribution shift Δ Real success rate Δ SIMPLER success rate Camera pose -0.38 -0.39 Table texture -0.17 -0.19 Background -0.17 -0.12 Distractors -0.08 -0.06 Lighting -0.04 -0.07 [Frames 58-59 replace the chart:] So, what matters for good simulated evaluation? Accurate control dynamics via system identification (SysID) Same open-loop action sequence: [Figure: three stills of a robot arm and a coke can, captioned Real, Sim w/o SysID (the can ends up somewhere else), Sim after SysID (ours) (matches the real outcome).] Reference: Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy , Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, Mees† et al., CoRL, 2024.", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-54-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-visual-matching-frame-60", "num": 54, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 54: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation <span class=\"own\">[visual matching]</span>", "title": "Slide 54: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [visual matching]", "frames": [60, 60], "html": "<p><strong>So, what matters for good simulated evaluation?</strong></p>\n<ol start=\"2\" type=\"1\">\n<li>Mitigating visual distribution shifts via “Visual Matching”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two stills of a robot arm over a counter with an orange and two cans, captioned Real and Sim + Green Screen + Texture Matching; the two look nearly identical.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy</em>, Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, <strong>Mees†</strong> et al., CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "So, what matters for good simulated evaluation? Mitigating visual distribution shifts via “Visual Matching” [Figure: two stills of a robot arm over a counter with an orange and two cans, captioned Real and Sim + Green Screen + Texture Matching; the two look nearly identical.] Reference: Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation Policy , Li*, Hsu*, Gu*, Pertsch†, Mees† et al., CoRL, 2024.", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-55-follow-up-works-on-real-to-sim-evals-frame-61", "num": 55, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 55: Follow Up Works on Real-to-Sim Evals", "title": "Slide 55: Follow Up Works on Real-to-Sim Evals", "frames": [61, 61], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: overview figures from three papers.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>PolaRiS: Scalable Real-to-Sim Evaluations for Generalist Robot Policies</em>, Jain et al., 2025. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: 1. Tools for Scalable Real-to-Sim Environment Generation (a short video of a real scene -&gt; PolaRiS Scene Builder -&gt; simulated evaluation environment); 2. Simulation Dataset for Bridging Real-to-Sim Gap (short sim data cotraining turns a generalist policy into a PolaRiS-ready policy); 3. Evaluation Environments with Strong Real-to-Sim Correlation (scatter of sim against real performance); 4. Hub for Environment Sharing.]</span></li>\n<li><em>RobotArena: Scalable Robot Benchmarking via Real-to-Sim Translation</em>, Jangir et al., 2025. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: robotic datasets (DROID, Bridge V2, RH20T) and user-recorded videos are turned into “Large Scale Simulated Environments - Automated Environment Creation from Real Video for VLA Evaluation” (RobotArena ∞), with automatic policy evaluation by VLM scores and human preference evaluation by pairwise comparison and policy ranking.]</span></li>\n<li><em>RoboLab: A High-Fidelity Simulation Benchmark for Analysis of Task Generalist Policies</em>, Yang et al., 2026. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: “RoboLab Benchmarking Framework”: policies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1 \\dots \\pi_N</annotation></semantics></math>; Scene Generation, Task Generation (e.g. “Put the __ in the blue bin”) and Environment Generation, each done by a user or an LLM.]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: overview figures from three papers.] PolaRiS: Scalable Real-to-Sim Evaluations for Generalist Robot Policies , Jain et al., 2025. [Figure: 1. Tools for Scalable Real-to-Sim Environment Generation (a short video of a real scene -> PolaRiS Scene Builder -> simulated evaluation environment); 2. Simulation Dataset for Bridging Real-to-Sim Gap (short sim data cotraining turns a generalist policy into a PolaRiS-ready policy); 3. Evaluation Environments with Strong Real-to-Sim Correlation (scatter of sim against real performance); 4. Hub for Environment Sharing.] RobotArena: Scalable Robot Benchmarking via Real-to-Sim Translation , Jangir et al., 2025. [Figure: robotic datasets (DROID, Bridge V2, RH20T) and user-recorded videos are turned into “Large Scale Simulated Environments - Automated Environment Creation from Real Video for VLA Evaluation” (RobotArena ∞), with automatic policy evaluation by VLM scores and human preference evaluation by pairwise comparison and policy ranking.] RoboLab: A High-Fidelity Simulation Benchmark for Analysis of Task Generalist Policies , Yang et al., 2026. [Figure: “RoboLab Benchmarking Framework”: policies π 1 … π N ; Scene Generation, Task Generation (e.g. “Put the __ in the blue bin”) and Environment Generation, each done by a user or an LLM.]", "t": 3660}, {"id": "slide-56-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-summary-frames-62-63", "num": 56, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 56: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies <span class=\"own\">[summary]</span>", "title": "Slide 56: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies [summary]", "frames": [62, 63], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Large Datasets</th>\n<th>Large Models</th>\n<th>Scalable Evaluation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Leverage <strong>Existing Robot Data</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Cross-embodied</strong> Policies with <strong>Internet-scale Priors</strong></td>\n<td>Leverage <strong>Real2Sim Evals</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[the Open X-Embodiment summary figure]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[octopus emoji, a robot collage, and a <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> symbol]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[the SIMPLER Google Robot / BridgeData V2 scenes; frame 63 swaps in the grid “SIMPLER enables effortless and reproducible eval at scale.”]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Large Datasets Large Models Scalable Evaluation Leverage Existing Robot Data Cross-embodied Policies with Internet-scale Priors Leverage Real2Sim Evals [the Open X-Embodiment summary figure] [octopus emoji, a robot collage, and a π symbol] [the SIMPLER Google Robot / BridgeData V2 scenes; frame 63 swaps in the grid “SIMPLER enables effortless and reproducible eval at scale.”]", "t": 3721}, {"id": "end-frame-64", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [64, 64], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "title-frame-1", "label": "Title", "html": "the slide says “Lecture 10”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 9 (week 9, Apr 27)."}, {"slide": "title-frame-1", "label": "Title", "html": "slides 1-3 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering starts at 4."}, {"slide": "slide-6-ingredients-for-generalist-robot-policies-frames-4-7", "label": "Slide 6: Ingredients for Generalist Robot Policies", "html": "slide 7 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 6 to 8."}, {"slide": "slide-10-sparks-of-a-generalist-robot-policy-frame-11", "label": "Slide 10: Sparks of a Generalist Robot Policy", "html": "the colour legend has four original methods (MVP BC-RNN, Resnet + MLP, VINN, TACORL/HULC2) for five tasks; the Autolab UR5 original method is not named."}, {"slide": "slide-10-sparks-of-a-generalist-robot-policy-frame-11", "label": "Slide 10: Sparks of a Generalist Robot Policy", "html": "slide 11 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 10 to 12."}, {"slide": "slide-14-untitled-policy-inputs-and-outputs-frame-17", "label": "Slide 14: [untitled: policy inputs and outputs]", "html": "slides 13 and 15 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 12 to 14 and from 14 to 16."}, {"slide": "slide-17-key-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-19", "label": "Slide 17: Key: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "html": "slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19."}, {"slide": "slide-23-octopus-emoji-octo-design-decisions-frame-25", "label": "Slide 23: [octopus emoji] Octo Design Decisions", "html": "slide 24 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 23 to 25."}, {"slide": "slide-31-crossformer-model-frame-35", "label": "Slide 31: CrossFormer Model", "html": "slide 32 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 31 to 33."}, {"slide": "slide-33-quantitative-results-frame-36", "label": "Slide 33: Quantitative Results", "html": "slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35."}, {"slide": "slide-38-vlm---vision-language-action-model-vla-frame-40", "label": "Slide 38: VLM -> Vision-Language-Action Model (VLA)", "html": "the example token string has 7 numbers but the de-tokenized action lists 5 values; a typical 7-DoF action would add <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> roll/pitch or similar."}, {"slide": "slide-52-evaluating-real-world-policies-in-simulation-real-vs-sim-correlation-frame-56", "label": "Slide 52: Evaluating Real-World Policies in Simulation [real vs sim correlation]", "html": "the paper’s title is “Evaluating Real-World Robot Manipulation Policies in Simulation”; the trailing word “Policy” in the slide’s citation looks like a slip."}]}, {"key": "10", "dir": "week10_reasoning", "date": "Mon 4 May 2026", "topic": "Embodied Reasoning & Test-time Scaling", "summary": "Embodied chain-of-thought: VLAs that generate a plan, subtask, movement and grounded boxes before acting, trained on reasoning data generated by foundation models. Reasoning helps but costs ~4 s per action, so ECoT-Lite keeps the benefit with reasoning pre-training and reasoning dropout. Then test-time compute in Go and language, chain-of-thought, RL-trained reasoning (GRPO, DeepSeek-R1) and verifiers for visual reasoning.", "key_ideas": ["Reasoning chains make policies interpretable and correctable by a human", "Three explanations for why reasoning helps: better representations, a curriculum, more compute per action", "Training on reasoning but dropping it at test time keeps most of the gain at full speed", "Test-time search can substitute for huge model scale (AlphaGo Zero: ~100,000x)", "GRPO needs no critic: rewards are normalized within a group of sampled answers"], "papers": [{"title": "In-Context Imitation Learning via Next-Token...", "who": "Fu et al., 2024", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.15980"}, {"title": "VOYAGER: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent...", "who": "Wang et al., 2023", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16291"}, {"title": "Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning", "who": "Chen et al., 2025", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.08243"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "CxhrjQuGEuE", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 10: Embodied Reasoning and Test-time Scaling | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "55:41", "description": "Lecture 10 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "The limitations of data"}, {"t": 305, "title": "Embodied chain of thought"}, {"t": 823, "title": "Efficient embodied reasoning"}, {"t": 1352, "title": "Reasoning architectures"}, {"t": 1567, "title": "Test-time compute scaling"}, {"t": 2298, "title": "Emergent reasoning with RL"}, {"t": 2799, "title": "Visual reasoning with RL"}, {"t": 3077, "title": "Summary and conclusions"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "Welcome everybody to lecture 11 of our robot learning course. Very excited today to talk about what I think is one of the frontiers nowadays in robot learning. So we will be talking about embodied reasoning and test time scaling. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; So I want to start with this kind of question, right? Like over the last few lectures we've covered how to train large models with on large data sets. We've covered VLAs and so forth. And so one of the questions you might be asking yourself is like well, it seems like you know, just we can keep collecting robot teleop data and that's all we need. Like we just scale this kind of recipe from LLMs and we're good to go. And this is true. I think it's not the full story"}, {"t": 60, "text": "and I want to be careful here. So I'm not saying that data doesn't matter. It absolutely does. But consider what data actually gives you, right? So it will give you like a better prior over the behaviors that you have, right? It some kind of like compresses the distribution of the demonstrations into your model weights. And so what is missing I think is intelligent reasoning. And what I mean by this is that essentially you know, we we've saw like some very impressive like VLAs and and so forth in the previous lectures and even if we succeed in building a robot capable of many tasks, this isn't going to be very useful if it's not also able to reason intelligently. So for pretty much all the models we've looked at um so far in the course,"}, {"t": 120, "text": "um like even the most capable um VLA or something, um if the if these models encounter uh an out-of-distribution situation, um it they typically don't have a mechanism to intelligently um work their way out of it. Like they [clears throat] will kind of like um fail, right? Uh what separates it a little bit from like the like the intelligence that maybe we as us humans uh might have. And um with respect to like this scaling the data angle, right? We we can make an analogy um looking at um LLMs, right? Um which are trained on magnitudes of more data than our um largest uh VLAs nowadays. So, um LLMs, even after training on all of the internet, um it turns out that it's this all this data is still not enough to solve all the problems we want LLMs to solve for us,"}, {"t": 180, "text": "right? Um and for robots where data is orders of magnitude more expensive to get, um it's almost certainly not going to be um enough, too. And so, the alternative um um in LLM in LLMs nowadays is to train specifically for reasoning. And the key intuition here is that reasoning is compositional. It um basically consists of reusable like reusable building blocks of like logic and knowledge that then you can recombine to solve not novel problems with existing knowledge. Um this means you don't have to have seen a certain situation before uh if you can reason your way through it uh from first principles. And um this is what we're going to talk to, uh we're going to talk about today. Um, how like for robotics we kind of want the same"}, {"t": 240, "text": "the same properties of endowing them with this intelligent reasoning abilities that allows them to generalize beyond the behaviors that they have seen in the training distribution. And as we've been formulating robotics as a multi-modal sequence modeling problem, right? So I'm consuming images, language, and action tokens. We've already been transferring many advances from LLMs and VLMs. Model architectures, pre-training objectives, scaling strategies, and so forth. So the natural question is can we also transfer some of the algorithmic improvements that have dramatically helped LLMs and VLMs to generalize such as reasoning, right? So In today's lecture we are basically going to ask, okay, instead of just scaling pre-training, can we make like will a robot"}, {"t": 300, "text": "that thinks harder make it act better? Right? And this brings us to the first paper I want to talk about which is embodied chain of thought reasoning. The core idea here is is simple. So instead of having policy or a VLA in this case map directly from like an image and an instruction to actions, we insert some intermediate reasoning steps. So &gt;&gt; [clears throat and cough] &gt;&gt; given like a concrete instruction, the model we will ask the model to first generate some sort of a plan. Like move like to the watermelon and so forth. Like uh identify what are like the next sub-task we uh the policy uh should do um and ground these also in the images. So, it's kind of like chain of thought, but like applied to the physical uh world. Um"}, {"t": 361, "text": "Oh yeah, and here is um an animation for this that kind of compares this to like the non-embodied chain of thought um version. So, um the first question is like where does the data to get this generate this reasonings come from, right? Uh because we can't ask a human annotator to label every trajectory in in like a large robot data set uh like with a plan, a sub-task description, uh like bonding boxes, gripper positions, etc. Like that isn't really scalable. Um so, the idea is is pretty simple um as probably you can guess it it [clears throat] boils down to using internet-scale foundation models to annotate uh your existing uh robot data set uh by generating semantically grounded reasonings um like these bonding boxes uh of like the object um your end effector is going to interact with next,"}, {"t": 421, "text": "um uh traces of how the gripper is going to move into the future, and so forth. And now um kind of to repeat the core idea of um this embodied chain of thought architecture is really to train um an auto-regressive um vision-language-action model that predicts these intermediate grounded reasoning steps, um so, the task, the plans, um the movements, and so on before predicting the uh action tokens. Um so, essentially this means we are now forcing our model to think carefully and look carefully um before acting, right? And so, the action tokens get conditioned on this intermediate auxiliary reasoning tokens. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Okay, what does this &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; bias? So we in this work we evaluated now this VLA"}, {"t": 480, "text": "in really challenging out of distribution scenarios and in the real world like like distractor objects, spatial relations and so forth. And so all kind of unseen objects and unseen instructions and scenarios. So everything was kind of out of out of distribution. And here I think this result is like pretty significant. So we found out that um applying this embodied chain of thought technique brought a 30% performance boost over open VLA which is the same model but without the reasoning. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; And it also outperformed a large a much larger model from Google RT2-X which is a 55 billion parameter VLA compared to to to our model here which just had 7B. So the key the key message here is"}, {"t": 541, "text": "really that without collecting any additional robot data for the same amount of robot teleop data you already have collected you can improve your your models generalization capabilities by around 30% by applying this embodied reasoning technique. And as a side benefit beyond generalization performance it also gives you better interpretability and interactiveness because now like we these, uh, reasoning steps are, like, human-readable, you can see, like, where it's predicting the bounding boxes and so forth. Uh, at test time, you can also see, um, like, if the robot is predicting a wrong plan and edit this reasoning chains online, uh, and because the action tokens are conditioned on this intermediate reasoning tokens, it"}, {"t": 602, "text": "will naturally, um, kind of, um, self-correct itself, uh, like, if a human intervenes and, like, edits the predicted, uh, reasoning chains online, um, during inference. So, that's pretty cool cuz it's it makes the the whole model also more interpretable. Like, if it fails, you can see, like, why it failed and so forth. And one of the, um, coolest experiments, I think, from from this paper is, um, that it actually also generalizes the reasonings to unseen robots. Uh, so, here, in this work, um, the the VLA model with reasoning was trained only on reasonings for a sing from a single data set, the WidowX kind of, like, this uh, cheap, like, black, uh, um, end effector robots that you saw. Um, and then we applied this, um, to different data sets from the OpenX Embodiment, um,"}, {"t": 661, "text": "different data sets, as you can see here, and ask it, uh, to produce these reasonings. Uh, and as you can see, despite, like, um, the model looking at a completely novel robot, a completely different camera, um, everything is new, um, it still produces very plausible, um, reasoning chains. Um, and [clears throat] why is this? Because a lot of the tasks, uh, reasoning tasks are actually quite similar to the VLM pre-training, uh, tasks, and therefore the VLM already kind of has this like world model. And so, this embodied chain of thought is kind of trying to like is kind of unlocking like the reasoning capabilities that are already inside the VLM thanks to its pre-training. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um and so, um yeah, this this kind of suggests that um embodied chain of thought like could be a uh principal way to transfer reasoning"}, {"t": 722, "text": "across different uh embodiments without having to collect uh demonstrations uh and annotate them for every new uh like robot morphology that that you might have. And um yeah, so to summarize, um the core contribution here um is to um establish a new way to train VLAs with with embodied reasoning, showing that you can improve generalization, uh interpretability, and interactiveness without any additional robot data. Um and it's been since then kind of adopted um both in academia and industry. Uh for example, Gemini Robotics VLA uh incorporates um this as a core component, uh which I think is kind of a uh useful uh signal cuz it suggests that um um the field is converging towards um treating reasoning as a first-class component in um in the robot policies."}, {"t": 781, "text": "Um and there are like a number of follow-up works exploring different angles um to embodied reasoning. Um like Malmo Act, uh for example, adds spatial reasoning with depth tokens. Um Clark et al um transferred uh action-free reasonings from uh from so reasonings um coming from human uh data um by decoupling the reasoning uh training from the action prediction Um so far and so I really encourage you to check these papers out cuz yeah I think they kind of give you a good picture of like where the field is kind of like heading towards in terms of embodied reasoning. Now there is an obvious problem with all of this. Reasoning is unfortunately slow. Because we are basically increasing the number of tokens generated at test time quite significantly."}, {"t": 840, "text": "So open VLA which is kind of the the base model we were using in this work. Runs like it's a 7B model so it runs about um four actions per second. And the embodied reasoning version of it that we developed because it [clears throat] it had to like produce like this this full like reasoning chain runs at four seconds per action. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; So it's quite different quite a difference. Like we managed to do some inference speed optimizations later on but the first ever rollouts that actually Will and Michal who were the lead authors of this work did [clears throat] on the real like WidowX robot were really painful like just to watch like watching them do these rollouts with like doing like one task was taking like so forever. It was like I don't know one"}, {"t": 900, "text": "hertz or I don't know what. It was really like yeah painful to watch. So the question is like well as a roboticist we care about like having fast control. We want you know have real time control and we don't want to like also spend hours and hours watching our robot like move like kind of uh uh, at a at a very, very slow speed, uh, like, a turtle, uh, speed. So, can we get the benefits of reasoning without paying, um, this kind of inference cost, right? And so, before we jump, um, to solutions for the inference problem, I think it's actually, uh, worth pausing, um, and asking a more fundamental question, like, why does embodied reasoning work at all? Right? Because, um, um, and I want to be like a bit careful here because I think, like, we can't there's kind of a tendency in the field where, um, especially in machine learning,"}, {"t": 960, "text": "where we we see that something works, right? Like, oh, like, 30% more generalization performance, whatever. Uh, we get excited about it and then we move on to the next thing. Um, but if we don't understand, like, why it works, um, we can't really know, like, when it will fail, uh, and we don't we can't know how to improve it. Um, so, we can't build on this in a principled way. So, in the same scientific rigor we apply when, um, experiments fail, uh, I believe we should also apply when, uh, things succeed. Um, and so, in the next few slides, um, which are adapted from from Will, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; I'll try to shed some light around, like, some different hypotheses, um, that sound plausible for why this could be, um, uh, working. And so, the first hypothesis is better representation learning. The idea is that, um, the reasoning traces somehow supply additional, uh, knowledge that shape the model's internal representations, um, like, you know, saying, \"Okay, like,"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "uh, indicating somehow, like, the, uh, particular object's location, uh, is relevant or a specific movement, uh, for this scenario matters,\" and so forth. Like, basically, um, forcing the, um, like, the the the model to build richer, more semantically grounded uh, representations for the for the scenes. And if this is if this turns out to be the primary explanation, then what actually matters is the supervision signal itself from the reasoning during the training, not necessarily generating the reasoning subtest time. Right? So, the model's representations would already be attending to the right features, even if we skipped the generation of the reasoning subtest time. Right? And so, this if this hypothesis would be kind of true, then you know, the consequence would be really appealing in that we could train with reasoning for better representation, but deployed it without"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "to recover the higher the low latency and the higher frequencies. Um another hypothesis that it sounds also plausible could be that the embodied reasoning provides an improved learning curriculum. So, the intuition here is that predicting continuous robot actions is extremely out of distribution for a VLM that's been pre-trained on like image visual question answering and so forth, right? So, directly taking a VLM and then fine-tuning it, you know, end to end to produce continuous robot actions is really hard. It's like, \"Wow, it's completely something different to what the pre-training has been doing.\" Um and so, it could be that embodied reasoning provides some sort of implicit curriculum. Right? So, the model can first learn kind of more intermediate straight more simple tasks like like mapping movement"}, {"t": 1142, "text": "descriptions to actions and then work its way up to the full prediction problem from like pixels to to actions, right? And so, um um the practical im- implications of this would be, if this hypothesis is true, that then we could use reasoning just purely as a training scaffold by providing, let's say, reasoning uh examples in context during training, but without any loss on them, and then remove it at inference time. Getting kind of the generalization benefits again, but without the the um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the inference cost. And then the third hypothesis, um is increased policy policy expressivity. Um the idea here is that just simply having um more tokens in context, um even if they might be semantically meaningless, increases the expressive"}, {"t": 1200, "text": "capacity of the transformer when predicting actions. And actually, um this is a very, very plausible hypothesis, cuz it's been very well uh documented in like LLM studies. Um so, I put here two um uh two references that also study this, um that show that um LLMs or or transformers can use like intermediate tokens as a scratch space for hidden computation, improving performance even when those tokens carry no semantic meaning. And so, this really raises um very important question, um in the sense of like, for embodied reasoning, how much of the benefit is coming from the semantic content of the reasoning, and how much is it just coming from the extra compute budget that the additional tokens uh provide? Because if it's mostly the latter, then"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "um the specific grounding of the uh reasoning matters uh less than we we we might think. And we could get the same benefits much more cheaply instead of like using foundation models to try to have very nice labels, right? And &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so in in this work E CoT light these three hypotheses are empirically tested on Libero 90 benchmark by isolating kind of each mechanism individually. And here are the results. Um So reasoning pre-training and and reasoning dropout, which I will cover in a bit. And both remove reasoning at test time perform almost as good as the full original embodied chain of thought. The reasoning scaffolding where the reasoning is provided as as an input context, but without loss"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "also does quite well. And the thinking tokens experiment with like the kind of like null tokens, although [clears throat] better than the standard VLA doesn't close the Oh, it's not better. Yeah, it's it's a bit worse than the standard VLA. So doesn't really help. So um yeah. So I will briefly explain kind of the reasoning pre-training and dropout that worked so well. It's quite simple to understand. So the reasoning pre-training um is pretty straightforward. So this means like you have two training stages. In the first one you just take your VLM and fine-tune it to only predict the the reasonings. So you don't have any action loss or no action prediction. Once you have"}, {"t": 1380, "text": "finished training this model, you start um you take that model and then you fine-tune it to predict continuous robot actions. Um uh but without the uh predicting the reasonings. So, you're really transferring just the representations um learned in the first stage, right? And then in the second one, you don't um predict uh reasonings at all, just predict the actions. Um so, that you you don't have the cost at inference of predicting the full reasonings, right? So, you decouple both. And then, the reasoning dropout um is a single uh joint training stage, like the original embodied chain of thought, where you jointly uh train the model to uh both predict the reasonings and actions. Um mhm &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; but um what you do here is that with some probability now during the training, you drop out the reasonings, so that the"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "model uh learns to also produce the action tokens uh not conditioned on the reasonings. Um so, this allows you now at test time to choose if you want to generate the reasonings before predicting the action tokens or if you want to turn it off. So, now it gives you this this option to turn it on and off the reasonings. And um here you can see the the the same results um for uh real-world uh experiments with the real robot. Um and you can see on the on the left uh plot that basically um like the original like the original embodied chain of thought, so um during inference [clears throat] generating the the full um reasonings gives you the the most um uh performance in terms of um generalization success rate, but at the cost of"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "lower inference frequency. And then these two variants, the reasoning pre-training and reasoning dropout, um are slightly worse in terms of generalization performance, but um basically give you the same uh frequency as the standard VLA. Um so which which of these two should you use? Um it depends really on your constraints. So as I said, the original one is probably our slowest for most real-time settings. Uh reasoning pre-training is especially good if um your reasoning and action data are not paired. So if you have like other sources of data that are not robot data, where you can where you want to transfer reasonings from, like from simulation or human data and so forth, then that's that's the way to go. And reasoning dropout is the most flexible um because you can turn on and off um"}, {"t": 1562, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; with a single model the the reasonings so uh depending on your latency budget. All right. So everything uh we've covered so far has used um a fixed compute budget um at test time. Um and the natural follow-up question is um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; what if we let the model think longer on harder problems? What if more computer at inference time translates to better decisions? Cuz like in the kind of original Embodied CHAI model thought like there was kind of a fixed uh number of of reasonings um that the model was trained on, and then um it was like, \"Okay, these are seven different like reasoning steps, so I will always um produce all seven.\" Right? And so um the idea here um is test time compute scaling, right? Uh, and this is kind of the second half of of the of the lecture. So, if we"}, {"t": 1620, "text": "like, can we improve the performance if we like think harder at test time? And you probably might be wondering, okay, isn't just that planning, right? Uh, it sounds like that's kind of what we know as as planning uh, from like 50 years ago. Uh, with like A star, uh, Monte Carlo tree search and so forth. Um, and uh, here I've kind of put like a a small comparison uh, where you can see how this compares [clears throat] like from uh, where you you start like um, kind of from uh, really hand specified kind of domains um, to uh, kind of doing this um, test time compute scaling in uh, implicit like in in like with neural networks in the weights, right? Um, so [clears throat] this code really goes from structured discrete domains all the way to open-ended uh, tasks like uh, language encoding. So, we are um,"}, {"t": 1682, "text": "kind of um, each step kind of trades off uh, explicit structure for generality. Um, the canonical example of test time compute scaling is AlphaGo. Um, if you remember from um, uh, a few years ago, right? Um, and here is a very interesting uh, the very interesting performance from the the the nature paper um, they wrote. Where you can see that the the raw policy uh, network without any search uh, sits around 3,000 Elo. Um, and when you add the Monte Carlo tree search uh, at test time you jump to over 5,000 Elo points well into superhuman uh territory. Um now um the rule of thumb is is quite striking in that um typically like or there is this rule of"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "thumb that um gaining around 120 points uh ELO points requires either doubling the model size or doubling um the test time search budget. Um so they are kind of inter- interchangeable. Um and so this means that in order to get from the 3,000 ELO points uh from the raw network performance um to the 5,200 points um that would require uh without the tree search, without Monte Carlo tree search, that would require um making [clears throat] the model roughly 100,000 times larger. Um these numbers are maybe are a bit inflated because AlphaGo was trained by playing against earlier checkpoints of itself, so probably the the the real number is is not that high. Um but still one maybe like interesting fact about this is that um as far as I know to this day um no one"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "has built a um like a model um that surpasses the human performance without uh test time compute search. So just like by making the the model bigger. Um so every every Go like agent uses um test time search. And so the takeaway from this is is is is really that test time um search like can give you a real massive benefit. Now of course like this this has been shown in in in many games and in in Go and in poker and in other um kind of like game settings uh you have like a perfect verifier and very well-defined search space. Uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the question is, does this hold also in language, where tasks might be more open-ended, right? And the answer is yes, but with a nuance. Um so, in this paper from Charlie Snell,"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "um they showed that um in a FLOPs-match comparison, compute-optimal test-time scaling uh beats a model that's roughly 14 times larger. And that's quite impressive result, because it says that for the same um the paper like this result says that for the same compute budget, um you're [clears throat] often better uh training a smaller model and letting it think longer at inference than training a larger uh model. Um how they did this here, um the mechanism they used is that they um kind of supervise um um the model's own fail attempts with a learned verifier. So, the model learned to produce better answers um given the previous wrong ones. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and then the like the the verifier picked the best one. And then um then the second like they at test time"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "they generated multiple answers um and uh selected the best one again with with the verifier. Uh so, really like the thinking here is a little bit kind of like a search over the model's own output uh distribution guided by a reward signal. Um but here in um this plot from the paper, I think is very interesting um in that the picture flips on harder questions. So, like you can see like the the green are easy questions, the blue are medium questions, and the orange are hard questions. And um, for hard questions, in some cases, you can lose up to 30 uh 33.7% versus just pre-training a a bigger model. And the intuition here is actually that test time uh compute amplifies, in essence, what the model already knows. So, if the right answer is isn't anywhere in the model's own"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "distribution, no amount of search will help you recover it. Um, so we've seen them empirically that test time compute helps, um, but as I said before, it's also like kind of um, good to try to understand why. Um, I also recommend checking out this paper uh from Li et al. that um gives a theoretical like a nice theoretical answer. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; the result here says that uh for any problem um solvable in T computational uh steps, um, it can be solved by a constant-sized transformer generating OT reasoning tokens. Um, so the intuition here is that each forward pass for a transformer has a fixed computational depth um bounded by the number of layers. And so, uh a single forward pass can only execute a constant number of sequential steps, but by generating intermediate tokens"}, {"t": 2041, "text": "auto-regressively, the model can, um, basically unroll additional computation at test time, right? As we've seen before. And, um, so without the reasoning tokens, solving a problem that requires, um, T um serial steps, right? Sequential steps, would need uh a model that's uh much larger, um, but [clears throat] uh with the reasoning tokens, a constant-size model can solve it by just thinking longer. And so, this is the formal kind of counterpart to what we saw earlier with the What was it? Third hypothesis with the thinking tokens. So Okay, so reasoning tokens help, but maybe like a bit of history where does the idea actually come from in practice? And the original chain of thought paper"}, {"t": 2102, "text": "is is is very very beautiful because it's very simple. Here instead of asking they showed like that instead of asking the model to directly output an answer that if you show it a few examples where the reasoning steps are written out explicitly by let's say a human, then the model learns how to do the same for new cases. Um but maybe one thing that is interesting from this paper is that there is a catch. And that's that this chain of thought prompting doesn't work well with small base models. And so this are like the this is kind of the figure from from the paper where they show this kind of the scaling scaling laws for for the chain of thought prompting. And um"}, {"t": 2161, "text": "&gt;&gt; [cough and clears throat] &gt;&gt; so you can see that in for small models it actually hurts performance and because yeah, like it doesn't kind of understand what's going on and somewhere around 100 billion marks it suddenly like breaks breaks free and surpasses sometimes even like the pri- the best prior like supervised method. Um So, the takeaway here is that um reasoning um emerges and it requires a base model uh that's kind of like good enough, like large and and capable. Like nowadays, you can also distill it into into a into smaller models, but in general, um you need like a strong base model um for the reasoning to emerge. And so, the original formulation is just really like few-shot in-context learning. Like you manually write out"}, {"t": 2220, "text": "some examples um of with reasoning traces, uh prepend them to the prompt, and um ask it to generalize to like a new case, right? Um And while this works like it of course has like some limitations in that, you know, it's going to be a prompt sensitive and brittle, like depending on how you, you know, what what examples you put in the prompt, like you will get different results. Um it might be even dramatically different results. Um and you you will need like a human to or like, you know, some manual labor to uh like select and put those um uh examples um in the in in the context. So, um Yeah, and so, it will probably like not generalize outside um the specific examples or domain um that you provide. So, the question um becomes can we train um"}, {"t": 2281, "text": "the model uh to reason rather than just trying to elicit the reasoning uh with human prompts, right? That's kind of like the the next uh question the um the community also looked at. And this slide um shows how uh training for, um, reasoning intrinsically looks like in practice. Um, so this is, uh, from like the DeepSeek, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; paper. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; And so, here you can see, uh, in comparison on on the on the top part, like the single chain of thought, um, this is, uh, kind of like the clean, structured, uh, chain of thought, uh, prompt, um, that like GPT-4, uh, will give you, for example. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um, that is kind of following some some prompted template. Uh, and then, um, in the on the bottom"}, {"t": 2341, "text": "part, the green part, this this is like kind of the the DeepSeek, um, kind of model, the R1, uh, which is very different, and you [clears throat] can see like if you if you can read like you can read like the thinking process, like it, um, like, uh, like it second-guesses itself, um, it tries to consider, like alternative, uh, interpretations, um, uh, it even wonders about leap years, um, so, yeah, it's like very interesting, very fascinating to to see like two models like thinking process, like how it's thinking. Um, and it might be also like a bit messy, like, um, the the thinking process, but um, but that's kind of like the point that, um, this is a model that's learned to reason through reinforcement learning, um, on verifiable outcomes, and not, um,"}, {"t": 2400, "text": "one that follows, uh, human-written example, right? So, also its own thinking process might not necessarily be, um, the same [clears throat] thinking processes that a human might might do. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, how do you actually train a model to reason? Um, so the answer is reinforcement learning. Um, and um, nowadays um, GRPO is is very um, popular for this. Um, and you might recognize the the algorithm because it's uh, very closely related uh, to PPO, uh, which we covered a few weeks ago, if you remember. And the one of the key challenges um, when applying uh, PPO to language models is that the critic, um, so the value function is quite expensive and difficult to train, uh, because you need like a separate network, um, to estimate the expected returns uh, for every possible token sequence."}, {"t": 2461, "text": "Um, so it's like quite um, quite messy. And um, so GRPO, so which uh, is group relative policy optimization, um, simplifies this by uh, removing the the need for a learned uh, value function. So, instead of a critic, um, estimating the expected returns, um, what um, this algorithm does is that you sample now uh, a group of rollouts for one particular question. And then you compute the advantages uh, relative um, to the group. Um, so this means that each advantage uh, is just its reward minus um, the group mean uh, divided by the the group's standard deviation. Um, &gt;&gt; [cough and clears throat] &gt;&gt; and so um, this leads to or this means that the model learns by uh, comparing its own uh, outputs against each other."}, {"t": 2520, "text": "And um, if you also look at the objective here, you will recognize many terms um, from the PPO algorithm we covered. Um, Um, the key thing here um, is what is missing uh, from the GRPO algorithm. So, there are no human, um, labels. Um, there are no uh, reward models, uh, and there are no value functions. Um, so here, by just using binary, um, verifiable answers for, for example, for math, um, the reasoning emerges, uh, because the only way to consistently get uh, higher rewards or um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um, to consistently get the right answer, uh, is to learn to think carefully, uh, about the problem. Now, an interesting question is also, um, why didn't this work before? Uh, because people have been working,"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "um, on reinforcement learning with large language models for years, um, and um, as I already hinted before, um, the answer that's kind of accepted, uh, I think more or less by the community nowadays is that, um, you need a base model that is strong enough to produce sometimes correct answers to hard problems. So, this means that if your base model is too weak to ever produce a uh, correct uh, rollout, um, then there is really nothing to reinforce. Um, and so what changed in, um, modern LLMs is that they finally kind of, um, in the last, uh, I don't know, like 1 and 1/2 years or so, um, they crossed [clears throat] the, um, the the capability where they can occasionally solve solve hard problems, uh, on their own. And then, once that happens, um, like RL can essentially take over, uh, and systematically"}, {"t": 2641, "text": "amplify, um, that capability. Um, this is one of the um more more interesting plots, I think, from the Deep Seek paper. Um because [clears throat] here you can see like how the um reasoning is um emerging during the training. Um so uh like here you can see like how it's discovering this reasoning behavior um purely from this um binary uh correctness um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh reward. Um so to like for this model, like um nobody like tells us to say the words like wait. Uh and nobody like really um like labeled these are like good reasoning traces or these are bad reasoning traces. Um so um what you can see here is that uh until during the first training stages, until like uh the 6,000 steps, the the model"}, {"t": 2702, "text": "never says wait. Um right? So, it never like says this word. Um and then uh it's just trying to get like the answers right. And then once at some point it discovers that um stopping to reconsider improves its reward and um the frequency of um like reflective language, like all like let's let's uh think about this again and so on uh like uh jumps dramatically. And um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so the model like finds this on its on its own um because it has a strong enough prior to explore reasoning strategies um and not just like try to answer patterns. And um this means that on a weak base model, um like RL never will get past like uh step zero. Uh cuz like if there are like no good rollouts to reinforce, um"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "like you can't discover anything. And I think this is like actually one of the most compelling demonstrations of what reinforcement learning can do that supervised learning fundamentally can't which is to discover like reasoning for example here strategies that nobody explicitly like specified. Okay, so we have seen that RL based reasoning works beautifully in language and math but as for what it is the kind of our natural next question might be like well that is also transferred to to vision. The answer is yes and well there is like many many papers in the last year that do like similar things. I think as far as I know visual RFT is pretty one of the first ones that that showed this and it's pretty much GRPO applied to vision tasks"}, {"t": 2821, "text": "but instead of like a math grader here the verifier they use is IOU metric so the intersection over Union between like a predicted bounding box on the ground truth. So this this means that the model still reasons inside the thing tasks so like the the door is on the right. Um. Like near the top and then predicts a bounding box and the model learns through this this approach that better reasoning leads to better localization which then gets reinforced. And the key insight here is that GRPO the GRPO framework is basically completely agnostic to like what the verifier is"}, {"t": 2882, "text": "as long as you have a verifiable reward you can apply RL reasoning to it. And similar to like IOU like there are many other like geometric &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; metrics that in the computer vision community are being used to kind of improve the grounding of like VLMs with with RL. Um There is however an important failure mode to be aware when applying GRPO especially to like multimodal settings like vision. And that is that in in kind of the the vanilla setting like GRPO only rewards the final answer. So the reasoning trace is completely invisible to the verifier. Right? And so in math this is mostly fine because"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "it's really it's it's it's hard to get the right answers in math through wrong reasoning. But in vision tasks hallucinated reasonings and correct answers are unfortunately not mutually exclusive. So this means that what I'm trying to say is that a model can describe a completely hallucinated visual scene in its think block and still predict the right bounding box either by luck or by pattern matching from the image directly. And So how do we fix this hallucination problem? And so this is actually a work we did at Microsoft called Argos that tries to uh, address this by directly by rewarding not just the final answer, uh, but also rewarding the the [clears throat] intermediate, uh, reasoning steps."}, {"t": 3001, "text": "Um, so, it's actually quite simple. Um, you, uh, have like you adaptively select different verifiers, uh, like different foundation models, for example, um, per sample to simultaneously evaluate, um, the final answers, um, accuracy, um, during RL as well as, uh, like spatiotemporal localization, um, of relevant objects, uh, both either in single images or across videos, um, and so, the reasoning quality itself. Um, so, this means that you can like essentially check if, you know, the bounding boxes are still being localized correctly like across like the video and things like that, um, to reward, um, that the reasoning it's generating is actually grounded in like and it's correct, right? And so,"}, {"t": 3061, "text": "um, this way the model like can no longer hallucinate its way to a correct answer, uh, because the intermediate reasoning is kind of being rewarded and checked, uh, at every step, including this this, uh, temporal grounding in the videos. And so, coming back now from language and vision to, um, embodied AI and robotics, uh, which is ultimately, uh, what we care about, um, what we observed in this work is that, um, test-time reasoning gives you large gains on high-level planning, uh, high-level planning tasks, um, like kind of like simulated, uh, um, kind of tasks, uh, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; like, uh, Alfred and so on. Um, on the hardest tasks, um, um, this approach, uh, can give you, um, three to four times, uh, better, uh, performance than, uh, supervised chain-of-thought, uh, models."}, {"t": 3121, "text": "Which basically means that a model, um, that has learned to reason, uh, through reinforcement learning, generalizes to hard problems in a way that a model that has merely, um, learned to imitate reasoning traces does not. Um, but so that's kind of for for high-level planning, like, like navigation and so forth. But there is still, um, like the continuous control, like low-level problem, right? So, for continuous action prediction, um, you still like can't afford to generate, uh, full reasoning chains at every time step, right? Um, so what we did here is to follow the, um, embodied chain-of-thought light recipe of internalizing the reasonings during, uh, VLA training and then dropping them, uh, at inference time. Um, so I think, um,"}, {"t": 3181, "text": "the emerging picture for robot learning is kind of this this two-level architecture where you might have this RL trained reasoning for high-level, uh, planning and task decomposition, and then maybe like a ECoT light style reasoning internalization for fast reactive control. All right. Um, to conclude, um, uh, let me leave you with the four ideas, um, that I want you to take away from today. Um, the first one is that test time compute is, um, a new axis, um, that we can scale on, and that in many settings it's actually more efficient um, to scale on than scaling pre-training. Um, the second one is that for continuous control, you don't need to pay the cost of reasoning at inference time. So, you can train with reasoning, internalize it, and then drop at"}, {"t": 3242, "text": "deployment. Um, and the representations the representations are what matter, not like necessarily the tokens at test time. Um, the third point is that RL can make reasoning emerge, not because you tell the model to do so, but because in many cases reasoning turns out to be the best strategy for maximizing reward. And um that's like a way more powerful regime than supervised imitation learning of human thought, for example. Um, and the fourth is that for multimodal agents, verifying the reasoning trace is just as important as verifying the the answer or the outcome. Um, if you only reward the outcomes, you"}, {"t": 3301, "text": "might get hallucinations. And so, these four ideas together, I think um uh, define what what is one of the most exciting research frontiers in AI right now and in robot learning. And the question of how to bring all of them together into a single robot policy that can reason, adapt, and improve, that's a very much an open problem yet. Um, and with this, thank you for your attention. &gt;&gt; Okay."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "The limitations of data"}, {"t": 305, "title": "Embodied chain of thought"}, {"t": 823, "title": "Efficient embodied reasoning"}, {"t": 1352, "title": "Reasoning architectures"}, {"t": 1567, "title": "Test-time compute scaling"}, {"t": 2298, "title": "Emergent reasoning with RL"}, {"t": 2799, "title": "Visual reasoning with RL"}, {"t": 3077, "title": "Summary and conclusions"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube"}, "guests": [{"id": "oBEkY6NeE_o", "title": "Archit Sharma: Scaling Test-Time Compute at the Frontier [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Archit Sharma", "duration": "32:19", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Archit Sharma (Google DeepMind), talks about the challenges and opportunities in scaling test-time compute.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and context"}, {"t": 102, "title": "Pre-training compute scaling"}, {"t": 163, "title": "Rise of reasoning models"}, {"t": 207, "title": "Inference-time scaling"}, {"t": 610, "title": "Emergent reasoning in RL"}, {"t": 716, "title": "Methods for scaling compute"}, {"t": 788, "title": "Reasoning system examples"}, {"t": 844, "title": "Gemini Deep Think"}, {"t": 937, "title": "Broad domain benefits"}, {"t": 1014, "title": "Achievements and milestones"}, {"t": 1101, "title": "Future research challenges"}, {"t": 1453, "title": "Q&A and discussion"}, {"t": 1929, "title": "Closing remarks"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "It's my pleasure to welcome Arkid Sharma today for our guest lecture. He's currently a research scientist at Google DeepMind where he focuses on post training and reasoning research. He's also creator of the direct preference optimization DPO algorithm which is really foundational algorithm for aligning large language models with human preferences. And most recently he led the development of the Gemini Deep Think model, the first AI model to achieve the gold medal on problems from the International Mathematical Olympiad. And yeah, with this please give him a very warm welcome. And the &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; stage is yours. All right, thank you for the introduction or and yeah, very excited to talk to you all about"}, {"t": 61, "text": "scaling test and compute. All right. Um and my slides are visible, right? Yes. Yeah, good. &gt;&gt; Cool. Great. So, um I hope like everybody is like very familiar with LLMs these days. Um there's few canonical ones that I've put up here. Um and these are really big and integral part of my life and hopefully like a lot of us like these tools have billions of users. They're generating like insane amount of revenue right now and they're commanding such high valuations. Um and these really are um giving us a peek into what AGI would look like. So, a large large part of the success of LLMs have resulted from the scaling of the competition that is going into it and put into the neural network machines that are they are. And up until 2024, a lot of the improvements in these"}, {"t": 121, "text": "systems could be attributed to scaling the compute via pre-training. Pre-training is this large state it goes through where um it scrapes a lot of the data from the internet and other sources, and it trains ever larger models. And all of these models are trained to predict the next token, so these become like really strong prediction machines, and it's a very scalable objective. And perhaps like maybe to a surprise of like a lot of the machine learning computing, scaling this really simple technique like allows the model to become a lot lot better in a very general way. So up until 2024, a lot of the gains in LLMs were a result of scaling the pre-training compute. Late in 2024 and early 2025, we started seeing the advent of these models which perhaps are like attributed to like re- are um All right, very okay. Um"}, {"t": 180, "text": "are basically the reasoning models. And the reasoning models the key idea is that like these models are not instantaneously giving you an answer. Um they can spend some time thinking about what is going on, what the question entails, going through the context, maybe going through some tool calls, understanding the context a little bit more, and allow got reasoning through them before giving an answer. And this really was a game changer um for many many different reasons. Up until now, the primary axis of compute which we were scaling up was the during pre-training compute, but now we could like actually increase the amount of test time compute that these models use. And perhaps like very importantly so, when we increase the amount of test and compute that these models use, the model performance goes up predictably. So, I have plotted a few benchmarks here from the Gemini 2.5 report. Um the first"}, {"t": 242, "text": "one is a benchmark called Amy 2025. This is an American um math competition with a final answer problem. It's a high school level problem which the LLMs were absolutely terrible at um pre-2024. But, as you can see that like the x-axis is the amount of time a model can spend thinking before an answer. And as this time increases, the absolute performance actually goes up by over 20 percentage points. And this is the same throughout all the benchmarks. Like Code Bench is a competitive coding programming benchmark, and GPQA Diamond is a science and knowledge benchmark. And all of these metrics go up as the model is allowed to think more and more about the problems. And up until like 2024, like a lot of the generational improvements were conditioned on pre-training. But, if you look at this report again,"}, {"t": 301, "text": "Gemini 1.5 went to Gemini 2.5 Pro, and I've highlighted some of the boxes, and these are like dramatically large improvements in performance. And these a lot of these benchmarks are um coding or science and knowledge or math. And these benchmarks have gone up quite dramatically, and a lot of these improvements can be attributed to inference time scaling here. Now, what is like inference time scaling and test time compute? A lot of the machine learning systems or at least based on the neural networks were based on like a fixed amount of forward computation. You get an input, you do a pass through some neural network, and then you do your prediction or you produce your output. And this is a fixed amount of compute that a model will spend before giving you an output. For a system which have like test time built into them, they usually can actually spend a lot more compute before giving you an output. And the amount of"}, {"t": 361, "text": "compute they spend, there's some kind of a search procedure either in the output space directly or something of that form which allows it to spend more compute than a single forward pass uh would have allowed the system to spend. And this ability to search and spend more compute on input is typically what is called like a test time compute system. The most prominent example of test time compute perhaps is AlphaGo. Um AlphaGo was a system built circa in the 2016 to 2018 kind of an era where um these are the first neural networks to beat humans at an exceptionally hard game of Go. This was supposed to be a benchmark where like humans were supposed to excel and the AI systems would never um be at a human level. And one of the key examples in this"}, {"t": 420, "text": "key things in this research was that when you have a model which is a raw parametric model with a single fixed forward pass, it reaches about an Elo rating of 3,000. So it is the leftmost bar in this figure. And when you have the same model and you add test time search on top of it, and note the search is capped to maximum of 5 seconds, when you add this uh search on top of it, the Elo jumps from 3,000 to 5,000. It's the same model just with test time search added on top. This was quite an insane result because a 100 Elo points is 200 like a 64% win rate and Elo gap is basically means that the 3,000 Elo player could never beat and uh the 5,000 Elo player. And by the time you're around 3,500, you're already absolutely superhuman. So, the AlphaGo was the model which beat the"}, {"t": 481, "text": "best Go player at the time. So, this is one of the most prominent use cases of test-time compute, but this is very, very much hand-crafted to work for games and things which have access to a simulator. How do we enable LLMs to use test-time compute? Um so, there's this idea which came up in large language models, which is you should can allow the model to have some kind of chain of thought-based reasoning before they answer. So, let's say um the one of the cool things about large language models is you can typically prompt them so that they start following instructions much better. So, for example, for standard prompting, let's say the model that is told that it's it's shown how to be solved a very, very trivial math problem. Roger has five tennis balls, he buys two more cans,"}, {"t": 540, "text": "blah blah blah, how many balls does he have now? The answer is 11. Now, I'm asked a similar math problem, and the model is like going to give you an answer directly. And unfortunately, when you force the models to give an answer directly, they're typically not very effective at this. The idea in chain of thought prompting was that instead of forcing the model to answer correctly, the model is typically encouraged to reason through the math or reason through the steps before giving an answer. So, Roger started with five balls, two cans at three tennis balls each is six tennis balls, 5 + 6 = 11. The answer is 11. So, it goes through these steps before answering the question. And now the next question when it sees it, it is going to reason before it reduces an answer. So, the cafeteria had 23 apples. They used 20 to make lunch. So, that's 23 - 20 = 3. 3 + 6 = 9. So, the answer is 9. And now the model is correct."}, {"t": 600, "text": "So, it kind of shows that when the language model starts reasoning in natural language, it tend to produce more accurate and correct information as responses. This idea was exploited in this paper called group relative policy optimization from deep seek. Um and it led to a really interesting phenomena where if you warm start these models to reason a little bit and you do a lot of reinforcement learning um with verifiable rewards, the reasoning sort of emerges over the course of RL. So, if you can look at this plot on the right side, this is the RL training curve and on the Y axis is the average length per response. And this model is trained on verifiable rewards. So, situations where you typically know the right answer to the problem or you can verify what the right answer is. So, think about math problems, think about problems with code where you have unit tests. So,"}, {"t": 662, "text": "when you train the models using RL on these verifiable domains, the response length naturally starts to grow, which tells that the model is now starting to reason in natural language. And on the left side, if you look at the blue curve, the model starts with an accuracy the training accuracy of like 25 15% and it grows to 75 80% um and this is all happening via RL, but the important thing is that the inference time computer is also being used more and more through training and through evaluation as well. So, yeah, one of the coolest things that happened in this work was that it showed that reasoning emerges almost naturally over the course of RL as long as you allow the model to use more influence computer. And this was a very fascinating phenomenon. It led to like um a large set of improvements both in open-source community, but also this was"}, {"t": 720, "text": "being used in the frontier labs at the time. So, the question then like as I think about it and many other people are starting to think about it like can we push test and compute more so for LLMs? Currently like this kind of a natural reasoning regime allows you to do an influence time increase the scale the influence time computer via like chain of thought and this isn't the only way to scale test of time test time computer. So, there are many other ways to scale test time computer. For example, you can allow the model to reason multiple times in parallel and you can allow the model to take its previous answers and improve it over with with multiple iterations. You can allow the model access to tools such as web search, code execution. You can allow it sandbox environments. You can allow like model to verify its own answer and there's a lot of techniques"}, {"t": 780, "text": "you can use to increase the amount of test and computer the model uses and improve its answer. So, this kind of scaling of test time computer led to many many different kind of reasoning systems. Gemini Deep Think, which I'll talk a little bit more about, is an example of such a system, but recently if you've been using like any coding agentic coding tools as well or if you have used deep research agents, these are all examples of what I think are scaling test and compute, but more through specific scaffold or harnesses around like an LLM system. So, things like Deep Think or ChatGPT Pro are more general reasoning systems which uh can answer a variety of queries, but use an extended amount of inference time compute. But, there's more specific harnesses as well. So, Codex, Gemini Deep Research, Cloud Code, Alethia is another example like which are maybe more targeted research agents, but still"}, {"t": 841, "text": "use an uh a large amount of test time compute. Now, Gemini Deep Think is a system I know a little bit more about, so I can talk a little bit about it. Um it's an amazing system where you can put an arbitrary amount of test time compute, and the model in principle should keep improving its response. Probably the most stark example of this was the performance of the model on this set of problems where it has to prove very hard math problems like basically IMO level. And what we observed was quite surprising is that if you look at the plot here, the x-axis is the amount of inference time being compute being used on the log scale, and y-axis is the accuracy of the proof. So, the proof is either correct or incorrect as just by an expert human. And I think the difference between the two curves is less important, but if we"}, {"t": 901, "text": "focus on the blue curve, um it's pretty clear that as we increase the inference time compute, the model is like about 40% accurate to about 90% accurate. And this is I want to clarify this is quite a lot of compute. The model can work for hours here before it produces an answer. And it just keeps improving over time. So, and a lot of machine learning researchers love seeing like things which can be scaled and predictably improve performance. And this is one such example. Maybe perhaps like the interesting bit about DeepMind is that it's not just restricted to the reasoning problems, not just maths, not just verifiable things. The benefits actually do extend to like non-verifiable domains as well. So, in this example, um the model is asked to produce um an HTML file where like it has like a"}, {"t": 961, "text": "whole voxel scene with cherry blossoms, trees, and like pagodas. And what I find fascinating is like 2.5 flash, which is like a small smaller model, to 2.5 Pro, which is a larger model, produces a fairly significant improvement in how the UI looks. But by scaling inference time computer that the model used, we see an even larger improvement on how much how many details are added. So, if you look at the last picture, um the cherry blossom trees have a lot more detail going on in them. The pagoda has so much more finer detail available to it. And all of this is like a function of increasing the test time computer here. So, the point being that like a general extended inference computer system produces like improvements across many, many domains. And overall like these systems are quite remarkable, as I said. DeepMind Deep Think was the first system which"}, {"t": 1022, "text": "achieved a gold medal standard at International Mathematical Olympiad, which was a goal or a milestone which was not expected to be reached in 2025. It's a very hard math problem competition. And yeah, um on the right was like a tweet, but Codeforces is a competitive programming platform where like Gemini DeepMind, the latest version of it, like got an Elo of approximately 3445. And like to kind of ground this, there was only like seven humans in the world who are like better at this than um than Gemini DeepMind. And if you follow along these days, like it's not just math competitions, new math problems, problems that researchers are facing are being solved or at least being helped substantially by these models. And these scaffoldings like got good now or can like work for days to complete"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "large large engineering projects. So, to kind of like put all of this in a nutshell, over the last year, year and a half, like test-time scaling has been one of the significant drivers of improvements of AI systems. And like they're resulting in systems which are now producing real output in the world. Okay, so hopefully like I was able to like kind of tell you like all the amazing progress that has happened with test-time scaling. So, I'll briefly go over like kind of what is going on right now and what am I thinking about or some of the like things that people at the Frontier Labs are thinking about. And yeah, with test-time scaling, there's still quite a lot of open questions just related to fundamental optimization. I talked about how inference time scaling results in very very predictable gains, predictable improvements across many domains. But the questions about"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "like how do we do this efficiently, how do we use our tokens efficiently, how do we use our tools efficiently is still quite like an open question. And it's something we can make a lot of progress on. One of the interesting things about inference time scaling is that it's and maybe this is like me not slightly immediately obvious, but a lot of the success in pre-training was driven by increasing the depth of the model. And inference time scaling is actually one way to look at it is like it is increasing the depth of competition as well. Because you're allowing the model to reason and produce more tokens before answering the question, it's doing more forward passes. So, in some like loose sense, it is increasing the depth of competition, too. The question then becomes like what is the most effective way to increase the depth? Is it to increase the inference time compute through tokens, or is there other ways to increase depth of computation as well? And finally, like as short results from agents which can do work for days or"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "hours or weeks, we can like quite be sure that these agents are going to start working longer and longer and doing harder and harder tasks. So, how can we make sure that these agents can do the work effectively? They're still making progress. How can we optimize the AI system so they're making progress even after a couple of weeks? How do we measure that they're making progress? How do we make sure that they're making progress safely? There's a lot we can do about this, and these are all open questions or things which are being worked on right now. And maybe to like, you know, it might seem like a weird thought. Like, do we really want agents to work for months? And I think like we can be sure that there's a lot of problems in this world which we would like uh these models to do. Something very simple and maybe Okay, I don't want to say simple, but like solving the Riemann hypothesis. It's very tangible that like this isn't There's a million-dollar prize that is associated with it. So, like and in"}, {"t": 1262, "text": "practice like the financial value of solving this might be even higher. So, like at the very least we can throw a million dollars of compute at solving something as a problem like this. Now, same with like drug research, cancer treatment, or anything which extends human life or improves the quality of human life. There's a lot of value and a lot of compute we can throw behind such problems. And, you know, companies traditionally have made products by scaling the number of engineers, but now if you're building a new revolutionary product, like, how much inference compute would you be willing to pay for to like build this like really cool product? I imagine a lot. But, I think it's also like useful for me to talk about that we have been scaling like inference compute, we are scaling how much work agents can do, and this is not going to solve every single problem um that we face, and it will make a tremendous amount of progress, it will"}, {"t": 1321, "text": "drive a large amount of innovation, but as an AI researcher, I think there's some still things missing which I believe could be like interesting to work on. So, one of the key things I think is knowledge discovery. Knowledge discovery The models can solve well-defined problems extremely well by working like a lot of time, a lot of inference compute. But, it's often hard for the models to figure out like what problems matter and what kind of impact they will have on the world. Um recently, like, that was proposed something called the Einstein test, which I think is an interesting thing where the model is asked to rediscover the general theory of relativity training on data only up to like early 20th century, which is an interesting test about can the model discovering something like so abstract and something so revolutionary? Um so, knowledge discovery is still pretty hard, and one other thing that I think is interesting and cool to work on is like how can the agents improve with"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "experience? Humans when they work jobs or go through school or go through research, they typically become better researchers, better engineers, better at what they're doing. But, agentic work it's even though it uses a lot of infant computer and typically gets better, it is it can still be expensive. If you're doing a thing repeatedly, like you don't want to go through the same loop over and over again. And maybe another way to see this is like some tool calls or some things can be extremely expensive and reusing them over and over is not the best or the most efficient way to use computer. So, we would like AI systems that can improve with experience, that form the right intuitions about the problems, that can shortcut rather than reason from first principle every single time they encounter the same problem. And this is something which would be like great to build AI systems for. And yeah, I think that's all I have to"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "say for today. Hope like hopefully like you see a little bit of into peek into the frontier labs and like how we're thinking about test time scaling. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Hi. Thank you for the nice talk. Uh most of these work on test time scaling is done on token space. What do you think uh the progress might be going forward in working on latent spaces for the this test time scaling? You think this has some future? Thank you. Yeah, that's a great question. So, this is maybe perhaps like I was also hinting to it is um token space is has a lot of benefits. Uh a lot of the language models are trained to predict tokens and think in natural language. So, scaling inference compute by scaling uh into the token space which is actually very natural for the language models. This is why like reasoning"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "emerges in things like GRPL. But, it's arguably not the most efficient space to think in. The continuous space offers so much more space to represent thoughts, things which are maybe even more abstract. And you know, our language models like the internal representations of them are in like continuous spaces. So, we can consider scaling them up. It's much harder to get a system of that form working, but people are working on it, yes. Yeah. Hi, thanks for the great talk. Um I wanted to ask so one of the benefits of or like side effects I guess of test time compute is a lot more data that gets generated from these conversations. And of course, as you kind of pointed out in your last point, um of course with experience you expect these models to get better, hopefully. Um And so I'm wondering like have you seen any evidence of that already? And do you"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "think um future models would just create massive amount of shortcuts and not have to work for hours, but instead you know, get trained on a lot of these um external or like new data that got generated. Um because I think three points that you mentioned uh related to like how to scale it is like paralyzed COT, right? Which I think you can definitely get shortcuts on. Or train like through training. Iterative self-refinement I also think you can you can improve further. And so the last thing that remains is like web search. So, I wonder if you've seen any evidence of that. Yeah, that's a good question. So, um I think like there's already signs of like memories being used. Um people can create memories. Or if you have like looked at skills, I think skill is a great example of like how like a lot of the agenda coding systems like humans can add skills or coding systems can remember remember things that"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "they've done before and that allows the models to like shortcut some of the things rather than rediscovering them. Overall I think like there's some argument to be made that these are improving with experience but it's still pretty manual overall. I think there is a lot of headroom or a lot of ways we can do that but I think yeah this is very early on. I would say there's a lot of things we can do even right now. Hey thanks for the for the talk. For for those of us that haven't spent time ourselves researching um the methods in which we've had um learned reasoning through verifiable problems. Can you tell us a little bit about the challenges associated with moving from simple examples of verifiable problems so simple math"}, {"t": 1680, "text": "problems, simple coding problems to scaling that to domains where today we're seeing models capable of working through very complex proofs and and what needed to happen to to really scale it up to that point? Yeah. There's a few set of problems. I think like first of all like it's like maybe not well appreciated but the building the infrastructure is actually quite incredibly hard for RL scaling. The models need to train very very stably. The models need to like not diverge in multiple different ways. The divergence can come through this is talked about in the open community as well so like um, training mismatch can often like exaggerate over the course of our training which can cause like a fairly bad divergence in performance. Um, and there's a lot of like funny quirky things that can come up like,"}, {"t": 1740, "text": "um, then people spend a lot of time optimizing something like the compute efficiency which allows the model to reach a certain amount of performance. So that, um, in like a certain number of tokens over the course of training. Um, the other thing which I think is important and which is partly solved by the use of verifiable problems, but is that these things are extremely data limited. So, verifiable problems are nice but um, you run out of them fairly quickly. So, you start with easy problems, but as you train and especially if these are like very strong pre-trained models, you run out of them very quickly because very easy problems stop carrying any significant signal for the learning for these models. So, when you start reaching like the problem like proofs or something of those form, um, math proofs are actually like not that easy to verify. So, um, depending on how you do it, these things"}, {"t": 1800, "text": "can still get hacked and you have to work around these issues as well. So, harder domains are harder to verify, there's fewer of them, so you have to be more efficient on learning of them. And then you have to also come up with like good methods that like allow you to scale infants compute in the ways we have talked about. Hey, so you talked about scaling test time compute to maybe even months of work, but from our own experience, like models just make bad decisions at some point after days and they never look back. So, is this because of like a jagged front line that models are good in one way but bad in other ways? Or how can like how can we scale it up even more to month of work? Yeah, that's a good question. So, um I think um Yeah, I think the models still make mistakes. The models will still get stuck in the wrong directions that"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "um Like I do so as well. Like sometimes I get stuck wrong on the wrong idea, and then I I do not recover from it till somebody pulls me out of it. So, this is part of the problem we want to solve. So, like when when I talk about agents working for months, you want to you don't want to like come back to it and it just hasn't solved or made progress on the problem. If it is stuck, like asking somebody else or other agents or other humans to like help it better understand the problem. So, yeah, there's still work to be done on this aspect. Um Part of it might be the jaggedness. These are really good at solving things. Part of it is like handling under specification. Often like the problems are not well specified and they misunderstand the problem. And then get stuck. So, there's a few things which need to improve for these models to be able to work effectively for on the order of months."}, {"t": 1920, "text": "Cool. Um I think that's it for from our side. Let's uh thank again Arkit for fantastic talk. &gt;&gt; [applause] &gt;&gt; Yeah. It was great being here. Thank you for inviting me. Yeah, thank you so much for taking the time. We'll see you soon, Arkit. All right. Bye. Bye."}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction and context"}, {"t": 102, "title": "Pre-training compute scaling"}, {"t": 163, "title": "Rise of reasoning models"}, {"t": 207, "title": "Inference-time scaling"}, {"t": 610, "title": "Emergent reasoning in RL"}, {"t": 716, "title": "Methods for scaling compute"}, {"t": 788, "title": "Reasoning system examples"}, {"t": 844, "title": "Gemini Deep Think"}, {"t": 937, "title": "Broad domain benefits"}, {"t": 1014, "title": "Achievements and milestones"}, {"t": 1101, "title": "Future research challenges"}, {"t": 1453, "title": "Q&A and discussion"}, {"t": 1929, "title": "Closing remarks"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Google DeepMind, Gemini Deep Think"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 04.05.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (56 frames, 1280x720, no text layer), re-extracted on 2026-10-04 from the 720p YouTube recording with the slide repo’s own <code>lectures/extract_slides.py</code> (github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich). That repo’s <code>week10_slides.pdf</code> is only 640x360, so its frame numbering differs from this one.</li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_archit_sharma.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 11: Embodied Reasoning and Test-time Scaling</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 04.05.2026</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the slide says “Lecture 11”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 10 (week 10, May 4).]</span></p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 11: Embodied Reasoning and Test-time Scaling Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 04.05.2026 [Note: the slide says “Lecture 11”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 10 (week 10, May 4).]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-untitled-transition-question-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 1: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<p><strong>Is Scaling Robot Data All We Need?</strong></p>", "text": "Is Scaling Robot Data All We Need?", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-2-what-is-missing-intelligent-reasoning-frames-3-4", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: What is Missing? Intelligent Reasoning", "title": "Slide 2: What is Missing? Intelligent Reasoning", "frames": [3, 4], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Training LLMs on all of Internet not enough</li>\n<li>LLMs train specifically for <strong>reasoning</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, a grid of many robot-task video stills, captioned “Robots capable of <strong>many tasks</strong>”. Right, the ChatGPT and DeepSeek logos, captioned “<strong>Intelligent Reasoning</strong>: Generalization, Interpretability, Interaction, Controllability”. The two bullets are added in the second build (frame 4).]</span></p>", "text": "Training LLMs on all of Internet not enough LLMs train specifically for reasoning [Figure: left, a grid of many robot-task video stills, captioned “Robots capable of many tasks ”. Right, the ChatGPT and DeepSeek logos, captioned “ Intelligent Reasoning : Generalization, Interpretability, Interaction, Controllability”. The two bullets are added in the second build (frame 4).]", "t": 60}, {"id": "slide-3-robotics-as-multimodal-sequence-modeling-frame-5", "num": 3, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 3: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "title": "Slide 3: Robotics as Multimodal Sequence Modeling", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Can we <strong>transfer</strong> algorithmic improvements like <strong>reasoning</strong>?</li>\n<li>Can a robot <strong>think harder</strong> to <strong>act better</strong>?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the Language / Image / Action token rows over “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop image and “ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>” (same figure as week 1, slide 33).]</span></p>", "text": "Can we transfer algorithmic improvements like reasoning ? Can a robot think harder to act better ? [Figure: the Language / Image / Action token rows over “Pick up the spoon”, the tabletop image and “ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ” (same figure as week 1, slide 33).]", "t": 240}, {"id": "slide-4-embodied-chain-of-thought-reasoning-frames-6-7", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning", "title": "Slide 4: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning", "frames": [6, 7], "html": "<p>Break down hard problems into intermediate reasonings</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, a camera image of a tabletop with a towel (blue box), a toy watermelon (red box) and the gripper position (red dot), with a green arrow from the gripper to the watermelon. Right, a chat: the user asks “Put the watermelon on the towel”; the robot answers with “Plan: 1. Move to watermelon, 2. Grasp it, …”, “Subtask: Move to watermelon”, “Move: Move backwards”, “Bboxes, Gripper Pos:”, then “ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math>”. Text beside it:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Standard policies directly map images to actions</li>\n<li>❌ They thus often fail on novel tasks</li>\n<li>We thus propose to add <em>embodied reasoning</em> to the policy</li>\n<li><em>Embodied reasoning</em> causes policies to “<strong>look</strong>” and “<strong>think</strong>” carefully</li>\n<li>✅ This yields better performance, interpretability, and generalization!</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Frame 7 is an earlier build without the last two lines.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</em>, Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, <strong>Mees</strong>, Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "Break down hard problems into intermediate reasonings [Figure: left, a camera image of a tabletop with a towel (blue box), a toy watermelon (red box) and the gripper position (red dot), with a green arrow from the gripper to the watermelon. Right, a chat: the user asks “Put the watermelon on the towel”; the robot answers with “Plan: 1. Move to watermelon, 2. Grasp it, …”, “Subtask: Move to watermelon”, “Move: Move backwards”, “Bboxes, Gripper Pos:”, then “ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … ”. Text beside it:] Standard policies directly map images to actions ❌ They thus often fail on novel tasks We thus propose to add embodied reasoning to the policy Embodied reasoning causes policies to “ look ” and “ think ” carefully ✅ This yields better performance, interpretability, and generalization! [Frame 7 is an earlier build without the last two lines.] Reference: Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning , Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, Mees , Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024.", "t": 240}, {"id": "slide-5-creating-multimodal-reasonings-at-scale-frames-8-9", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: Creating Multimodal Reasonings at Scale", "title": "Slide 5: Creating Multimodal Reasonings at Scale", "frames": [8, 9], "html": "<p>Maximally use existing data by <strong>distilling foundation models</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “How do we create robot reasoning data at scale? We use a suite of foundation models to generate reasoning data for training”. A robot trajectory from the dataset (instruction “Put the watermelon on the towel”, image frames, Proprio 1: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}]</annotation></semantics></math>) is processed by Gemini, OWLv2 + SAM, Prismatic VLM and Grounding DINO (-&gt; Bounding Boxes) to produce a REASONING record:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>TASK: Place the watermelon on the towel</li>\n<li>PLAN: 1. Move to watermelon 2. Firmly grasp it 3. …</li>\n<li>SUBTASK: The watermelon is the first object the robot needs to interact with -&gt; Move to the watermelon</li>\n<li>MOVE: The watermelon is behind the robot, so it needs to move backward -&gt; Move backward.</li>\n<li>GRIPPER POS: [156, 55]</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</em>, Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, <strong>Mees</strong>, Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build of slide 5 (frame 9): the trajectory and the reasoning record are now boxed together as the “Embodied Chain-of-Thought Dataset”, and the record gains “VISIBLE OBJS: Watermelon [126, 146, 141, 125], Towel [20, 59, 218, 198], …”.]</span></p>", "text": "Maximally use existing data by distilling foundation models [Figure: “How do we create robot reasoning data at scale? We use a suite of foundation models to generate reasoning data for training”. A robot trajectory from the dataset (instruction “Put the watermelon on the towel”, image frames, Proprio 1: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] ) is processed by Gemini, OWLv2 + SAM, Prismatic VLM and Grounding DINO (-> Bounding Boxes) to produce a REASONING record:] TASK: Place the watermelon on the towel PLAN: 1. Move to watermelon 2. Firmly grasp it 3. … SUBTASK: The watermelon is the first object the robot needs to interact with -> Move to the watermelon MOVE: The watermelon is behind the robot, so it needs to move backward -> Move backward. GRIPPER POS: [156, 55] Reference: Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning , Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, Mees , Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024. [Second build of slide 5 (frame 9): the trajectory and the reasoning record are now boxed together as the “Embodied Chain-of-Thought Dataset”, and the record gains “VISIBLE OBJS: Watermelon [126, 146, 141, 125], Towel [20, 59, 218, 198], …”.]", "t": 300}, {"id": "slide-6-embodied-chain-of-thought-architecture-frames-10-11", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Architecture", "title": "Slide 6: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Architecture", "frames": [10, 11], "html": "<p>“<strong>Think carefully</strong>” and “<strong>look carefully</strong>” before acting</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a timeline. <strong>Input</strong>: the tabletop image and “USER: ‘Put the watermelon on the towel’”. <strong>Generated Embodied Chain-of-Thought</strong> (added in frame 11), in order:]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Semantic Reasoning:\n<ul>\n<li>TASK: Place the watermelon on the towel</li>\n<li>PLAN: 1. Move to watermelon 2. Firmly grasp it 3. Move to towel 4. Place watermelon on towel</li>\n<li>SUBTASK REASONING: The watermelon is the first object the robot needs to interact with. The robot is not yet close to the watermelon, so the robot needs to move closer</li>\n<li>SUBTASK: Move to the watermelon</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Visual Reasoning:\n<ul>\n<li>MOVE REASONING: The watermelon is behind the robot, so it needs to move backward</li>\n<li>MOVE: Move backward</li>\n<li>GRIPPER POS: [156, 55]</li>\n<li>VISIBLE OBJECTS: Watermelon [126, 146, 141, 125], Towel [20, 59, 218, 198], Spoon [114, 93, 141, 125] …</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>[<strong>Robot Action</strong>: ACTION: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}] = \\dots</annotation></semantics></math> (robot arm). Frame 10 shows only Input -&gt; Robot Action.]</p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning</em>, Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, <strong>Mees</strong>, Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "“ Think carefully ” and “ look carefully ” before acting [Figure: a timeline. Input : the tabletop image and “USER: ‘Put the watermelon on the towel’”. Generated Embodied Chain-of-Thought (added in frame 11), in order:] Semantic Reasoning: TASK: Place the watermelon on the towel PLAN: 1. Move to watermelon 2. Firmly grasp it 3. Move to towel 4. Place watermelon on towel SUBTASK REASONING: The watermelon is the first object the robot needs to interact with. The robot is not yet close to the watermelon, so the robot needs to move closer SUBTASK: Move to the watermelon Visual Reasoning: MOVE REASONING: The watermelon is behind the robot, so it needs to move backward MOVE: Move backward GRIPPER POS: [156, 55] VISIBLE OBJECTS: Watermelon [126, 146, 141, 125], Towel [20, 59, 218, 198], Spoon [114, 93, 141, 125] … [ Robot Action : ACTION: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] = … (robot arm). Frame 10 shows only Input -> Robot Action.] Reference: Robotic Control via Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning , Zawalski*, Chen*, Pertsch, Mees , Finn, Levine. CoRL, 2024.", "t": 361}, {"id": "slide-7-generalization-evaluations-frame-12", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: Generalization Evaluations", "title": "Slide 7: Generalization Evaluations", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<p>Boosts performance 30% <strong>without extra robot data</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: horizontal bar chart, “Our Embodied Chain-of-Thought VLA is more performant than other generalist policies, especially on novel tasks”. Success rate, approximate: ECoT (Ours) ~65, RT-2-X ~47, OpenVLA ~37, Octo ~19. A red arrow labelled “30%” runs from the end of the OpenVLA bar to the ECoT level.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the arrow spans about 28 points of absolute success rate (37 -&gt; 65); “30%” is the absolute gap, not a relative improvement.]</span></p>", "text": "Boosts performance 30% without extra robot data [Figure: horizontal bar chart, “Our Embodied Chain-of-Thought VLA is more performant than other generalist policies, especially on novel tasks”. Success rate, approximate: ECoT (Ours) ~65, RT-2-X ~47, OpenVLA ~37, Octo ~19. A red arrow labelled “30%” runs from the end of the OpenVLA bar to the ECoT level.] [Note: the arrow spans about 28 points of absolute success rate (37 -> 65); “30%” is the absolute gap, not a relative improvement.]", "t": 480}, {"id": "slide-8-human-robot-interaction-frames-13-15", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: Human-Robot Interaction", "title": "Slide 8: Human-Robot Interaction", "frames": [13, 15], "html": "<ul>\n<li>ECoT makes policies more <strong>interpretable</strong></li>\n<li>Allows human to <strong>correct</strong> behaviors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a robot over a table with a yellow plush chick, a purple plush eggplant, a yellow cloth and a yellow sponge. “Lastly, human operators can correct robot behaviors by editing reasoning chains”. The user asks “Pick up any object that is <strong>not yellow</strong>”; the robot’s reasoning reads “Plan: … Subtask: Move to the yellow cloth. Move: Move left” (marked wrong): “Robot misbehaves due to <strong>incorrect reasoning</strong>”. The user adds “The eggplant near the arm is purple!”, and in the last build (frame 15) the corrected reasoning reads “Subtask: The eggplant is a non-yellow object and should be picked up. -&gt; Move to the eggplant. …” (marked correct): “ChatGPT’s corrected reasoning fixes robot behavior!”]</span></p>", "text": "ECoT makes policies more interpretable Allows human to correct behaviors [Figure: a robot over a table with a yellow plush chick, a purple plush eggplant, a yellow cloth and a yellow sponge. “Lastly, human operators can correct robot behaviors by editing reasoning chains”. The user asks “Pick up any object that is not yellow ”; the robot’s reasoning reads “Plan: … Subtask: Move to the yellow cloth. Move: Move left” (marked wrong): “Robot misbehaves due to incorrect reasoning ”. The user adds “The eggplant near the arm is purple!”, and in the last build (frame 15) the corrected reasoning reads “Subtask: The eggplant is a non-yellow object and should be picked up. -> Move to the eggplant. …” (marked correct): “ChatGPT’s corrected reasoning fixes robot behavior!”]", "t": 541}, {"id": "slide-9-reasonings-generalize-to-unseen-robots-frame-16", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: Reasonings Generalize to Unseen Robots", "title": "Slide 9: Reasonings Generalize to Unseen Robots", "frames": [16, 16], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: four scenes from robots not seen in training, each with labelled bounding boxes, a predicted gripper path, and a reasoning panel:]</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"own\">[white plate, black bowl, orange, blue sink]</span> TASK: Put the black bowl in the blue sink. PLAN: Move to the black bowl. Pick up the black bowl. Move to the sink. Drop the black bowl in the blue sink. SUBTASK: The black bowl is the first object to interact with -&gt; Move to the black bowl. MOVE: The black bowl is to the right of the robot -&gt; Move right.</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[a robot, red bottle, yellow banana]</span> TASK: Move to the banana. PLAN: Move to the banana, grasp the banana, move the banana to the plate, release the banana. SUBTASK: The banana is on the left side of the plate, so the robot needs to move left -&gt; Move to banana. MOVE: The robot needs to move left to get closer to the banana -&gt; Move left.</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[black robot arm, carrot, red bowl]</span> TASK: The task is to put the carrot in the red bowl. PLAN: 1. Move to the carrot. 2. Grasp the carrot. 3. Move to the red bowl. 4. Release the carrot. SUBTASK: The robot has grasped the carrot and needs to move it to the red bowl -&gt; Move to the red bowl. MOVE: The red bowl is to the right of the robot, so the robot needs to move right to reach it -&gt; Move right</li>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[yellow towel, grey sponge; 4x]</span> TASK: Put the grey sponge on the yellow towel. PLAN: Move to the sponge, pick up the sponge, move to the towel, put down the sponge. SUBTASK: The sponge is on the table, and it needs to be reached first -&gt; Move to the sponge. MOVE: The sponge is below the robot, so move down -&gt; Move down.</li>\n</ol>", "text": "[Figure: four scenes from robots not seen in training, each with labelled bounding boxes, a predicted gripper path, and a reasoning panel:] [white plate, black bowl, orange, blue sink] TASK: Put the black bowl in the blue sink. PLAN: Move to the black bowl. Pick up the black bowl. Move to the sink. Drop the black bowl in the blue sink. SUBTASK: The black bowl is the first object to interact with -> Move to the black bowl. MOVE: The black bowl is to the right of the robot -> Move right. [a robot, red bottle, yellow banana] TASK: Move to the banana. PLAN: Move to the banana, grasp the banana, move the banana to the plate, release the banana. SUBTASK: The banana is on the left side of the plate, so the robot needs to move left -> Move to banana. MOVE: The robot needs to move left to get closer to the banana -> Move left. [black robot arm, carrot, red bowl] TASK: The task is to put the carrot in the red bowl. PLAN: 1. Move to the carrot. 2. Grasp the carrot. 3. Move to the red bowl. 4. Release the carrot. SUBTASK: The robot has grasped the carrot and needs to move it to the red bowl -> Move to the red bowl. MOVE: The red bowl is to the right of the robot, so the robot needs to move right to reach it -> Move right [yellow towel, grey sponge; 4x] TASK: Put the grey sponge on the yellow towel. PLAN: Move to the sponge, pick up the sponge, move to the towel, put down the sponge. SUBTASK: The sponge is on the table, and it needs to be reached first -> Move to the sponge. MOVE: The sponge is below the robot, so move down -> Move down.", "t": 541}, {"id": "slide-10-impact-of-embodied-chain-of-thought-frames-17-18", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Impact of Embodied Chain-of-Thought", "title": "Slide 10: Impact of Embodied Chain-of-Thought", "frames": [17, 18], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Key: new standard to train VLAs with reasoning</li>\n<li>Improves generalization, interpretability &amp; interactiveness</li>\n<li>Adoption by industry and academia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: embedded video, captioned “Gemini Robotics VLA 1.5”: a two-armed robot over a table with green, orange and yellow plates and fruit, overlay “THINKING WHILE ACTING” (Autonomous 1x); then a researcher talking at the same table.]</span></p>", "text": "Key: new standard to train VLAs with reasoning Improves generalization, interpretability & interactiveness Adoption by industry and academia [Figure: embedded video, captioned “Gemini Robotics VLA 1.5”: a two-armed robot over a table with green, orange and yellow plates and fruit, overlay “THINKING WHILE ACTING” (Autonomous 1x); then a researcher talking at the same table.]", "t": 661}, {"id": "slide-11-follow-up-works-on-embodied-reasoning-frame-19", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Follow Up Works on Embodied Reasoning", "title": "Slide 11: Follow Up Works on Embodied Reasoning", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: overview figures from four papers.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>MolmoAct: Action Reasoning Models that can Reason in Space</em>, Lee et al., 2025. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: a Pre-Trained Action Reasoning Model takes camera images plus an instruction (“Pour water into the cup”) or a trajectory input, and outputs depth perception tokens, a visual reasoning trace (2D waypoints) and action tokens.]</span></li>\n<li><em>Action-Free Reasoning for Policy Generalization</em>, Clark et al., 2025. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: “RAD learns broad <strong>reasoning</strong> from human + robot data and <strong>action</strong> generation from just robot data!”: for “Pick up the controller” the model emits Task, Plan, Subtask Reason, Subtask, Move Reason, Move, Visible Objects and Gripper Pos before the robot action; action-free human videos supply reasoning only, robot data supplies reasoning + action.]</span></li>\n<li><em>CoT-VLA: Visual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Vision-Language-Action Models</em>, Zhao et al., 2025. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: pre-training data (visual Q&amp;A, captioning, text to image/video), robot demonstrations and action-less videos train a model with causal &amp; full attention that first predicts a future image (visual chain of thought), then actions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>…</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_1 \\dots a_n</annotation></semantics></math>, in a closed-loop control cycle.]</span></li>\n<li><em>Self-Supervised Bootstrapping of Action-Predictive Embodied Reasoning</em>, Ganai et al., 2026. <span class=\"own\">[Figure: reasoning primitives (Plan, Vis.Ob., Subtask, Ego) generated by a foundation model, combined with reasoning dropout, a prior VLA and a posterior VLA trained jointly; candidate reasonings are sampled, weighted by importance weights and used to train the reasoning VLA.]</span></li>\n</ul>", "text": "[Figure: overview figures from four papers.] MolmoAct: Action Reasoning Models that can Reason in Space , Lee et al., 2025. [Figure: a Pre-Trained Action Reasoning Model takes camera images plus an instruction (“Pour water into the cup”) or a trajectory input, and outputs depth perception tokens, a visual reasoning trace (2D waypoints) and action tokens.] Action-Free Reasoning for Policy Generalization , Clark et al., 2025. [Figure: “RAD learns broad reasoning from human + robot data and action generation from just robot data!”: for “Pick up the controller” the model emits Task, Plan, Subtask Reason, Subtask, Move Reason, Move, Visible Objects and Gripper Pos before the robot action; action-free human videos supply reasoning only, robot data supplies reasoning + action.] CoT-VLA: Visual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Vision-Language-Action Models , Zhao et al., 2025. [Figure: pre-training data (visual Q&A, captioning, text to image/video), robot demonstrations and action-less videos train a model with causal & full attention that first predicts a future image (visual chain of thought), then actions a 1 … a n , in a closed-loop control cycle.] Self-Supervised Bootstrapping of Action-Predictive Embodied Reasoning , Ganai et al., 2026. [Figure: reasoning primitives (Plan, Vis.Ob., Subtask, Ego) generated by a foundation model, combined with reasoning dropout, a prior VLA and a posterior VLA trained jointly; candidate reasonings are sampled, weighted by importance weights and used to train the reasoning VLA.]", "t": 781}, {"id": "slide-12-inference-cost-of-reasoning-frame-20", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Inference Cost of Reasoning", "title": "Slide 12: Inference Cost of Reasoning", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Autoregressive VLA Inference Speed</strong> <em>OpenVLA</em></td>\n<td>4 Actions/Second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning Inference Speed</strong></td>\n<td>4 <strong>Seconds/Action</strong> <span class=\"own\">[crying-face emoji]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Autoregressive VLA Inference Speed OpenVLA 4 Actions/Second Embodied Chain-of-Thought Reasoning Inference Speed 4 Seconds/Action [crying-face emoji]", "t": 840}, {"id": "slide-13-untitled-transition-question-frame-21", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 13: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<p>Can we get the benefits of reasoning <strong>without the inference costs</strong>?</p>", "text": "Can we get the benefits of reasoning without the inference costs ?", "t": 900}, {"id": "slide-14-why-does-embodied-reasoning-help-robot-policies-frame-22", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies?", "title": "Slide 14: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies?", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Better representation learning</li>\n<li>Improved learning “curriculum”</li>\n<li>Increased policy expressivity</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Slide footer, in square brackets on the slide:]</span> ECoT-Lite Slides adapted from William Chen</p>", "text": "Better representation learning Improved learning “curriculum” Increased policy expressivity [Slide footer, in square brackets on the slide:] ECoT-Lite Slides adapted from William Chen", "t": 1021}, {"id": "slide-15-why-does-embodied-reasoning-help-robot-policies-1.-representation-learning-frame-23", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [1. representation learning]", "title": "Slide 15: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [1. representation learning]", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<p><strong>1. Better representation learning</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION (robot arm icon) -&gt; network -&gt; two outputs: REASONING (dashed) and ACTION.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace under the outputs]</span> Train on both, but only choose <em>actions</em> at test time</li>\n</ul>", "text": "1. Better representation learning [Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION (robot arm icon) -> network -> two outputs: REASONING (dashed) and ACTION.] [brace under the outputs] Train on both, but only choose actions at test time", "t": 1021}, {"id": "slide-16-why-does-embodied-reasoning-help-robot-policies-2.-curriculum-frame-24", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [2. curriculum]", "title": "Slide 16: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [2. curriculum]", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<p><strong>2. Improved learning “curriculum”</strong></p>\n<p>Predicting continuous robot actions is very OOD for a VLM, is ECoT a better curriculum?</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION + REASONING (dashed, as input) -&gt; network -&gt; ACTION.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace at the reasoning input]</span> In-context reasoning <em>scaffolds</em> policy -&gt; Akin to teacher-student learning!</li>\n</ul>", "text": "2. Improved learning “curriculum” Predicting continuous robot actions is very OOD for a VLM, is ECoT a better curriculum? [Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION + REASONING (dashed, as input) -> network -> ACTION.] [brace at the reasoning input] In-context reasoning scaffolds policy -> Akin to teacher-student learning!", "t": 1080}, {"id": "slide-17-why-does-embodied-reasoning-help-robot-policies-3.-expressivity-frame-25", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [3. expressivity]", "title": "Slide 17: Why Does Embodied Reasoning Help Robot Policies? [3. expressivity]", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<p><strong>3. Increased policy expressivity</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION + <THINKING> (filler tokens, as input) -&gt; network -&gt; ACTION.]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"own\">[brace at <THINKING>]</span> More tokens in-context improves expressivity (even when meaningless)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>References: <em>Let’s Think Dot by Dot: Hidden Computation in Transformer Language Models</em>, Pfau et al, 2024; <em>Think before you speak: Training Language Models With Pause Tokens</em>, Goyal et al, 2024.</p>", "text": "3. Increased policy expressivity [Figure: INSTRUCTION + OBSERVATION + (filler tokens, as input) -> network -> ACTION.] [brace at ] More tokens in-context improves expressivity (even when meaningless) References: Let’s Think Dot by Dot: Hidden Computation in Transformer Language Models , Pfau et al, 2024; Think before you speak: Training Language Models With Pause Tokens , Goyal et al, 2024.", "t": 1142}, {"id": "slide-18-results-in-libero-90-frame-26", "num": 18, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 18: Results in LIBERO-90", "title": "Slide 18: Results in LIBERO-90", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, two simulated kitchen scenes and the LIBERO benchmark overview figure. Right, horizontal bar chart “LIBERO-90 Success Rate”, axis 70-90, values read from the bars, approximate: Embodied CoT ~91, Standard VLA ~82, Reasoning Pre-training ~87, Reasoning Dropout ~89, Reasoning “Scaffolding” ~84, Thinking Tokens ~79. Reasoning Pre-training and Reasoning Dropout are boxed together.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning</em>, Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, <strong>Mees</strong>, Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slides 19-23 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 24.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: left, two simulated kitchen scenes and the LIBERO benchmark overview figure. Right, horizontal bar chart “LIBERO-90 Success Rate”, axis 70-90, values read from the bars, approximate: Embodied CoT ~91, Standard VLA ~82, Reasoning Pre-training ~87, Reasoning Dropout ~89, Reasoning “Scaffolding” ~84, Thinking Tokens ~79. Reasoning Pre-training and Reasoning Dropout are boxed together.] Reference: Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning , Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, Mees , Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025. [Note: slides 19-23 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 24.]", "t": 1380}, {"id": "slide-24-embodied-chain-of-thought-lite-reasoning-pre-training-frames-27-28", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite <span class=\"own\">[reasoning pre-training]</span>", "title": "Slide 24: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite [reasoning pre-training]", "frames": [27, 28], "html": "<p><strong>Reasoning Pre-training</strong> (top right, smaller: Reasoning Dropout)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a simulated kitchen scene with three bowls and a plate, instruction “Put middle bowl on plate” -&gt; <strong>Policy</strong> -&gt; “Reasoning: Plan, Subtask, Move, Bounding Boxes, Gripper” (faded); an arrow labelled “Transfer” points down from the policy. Frame 28 (numbered slide 25) adds a second <strong>Policy</strong> below that receives the transferred weights and takes the same input; its output is not yet shown.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning</em>, Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, <strong>Mees</strong>, Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: frames 27 and 28 carry slide numbers 24 and 25; slides 26-29 do not appear in the captured frames.]</span></p>", "text": "Reasoning Pre-training (top right, smaller: Reasoning Dropout) [Figure: a simulated kitchen scene with three bowls and a plate, instruction “Put middle bowl on plate” -> Policy -> “Reasoning: Plan, Subtask, Move, Bounding Boxes, Gripper” (faded); an arrow labelled “Transfer” points down from the policy. Frame 28 (numbered slide 25) adds a second Policy below that receives the transferred weights and takes the same input; its output is not yet shown.] Reference: Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning , Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, Mees , Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025. [Note: frames 27 and 28 carry slide numbers 24 and 25; slides 26-29 do not appear in the captured frames.]", "t": 1440}, {"id": "slide-30-embodied-chain-of-thought-lite-pre-training-completed-frame-29", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite <span class=\"own\">[pre-training completed]</span>", "title": "Slide 30: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite [pre-training completed]", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<p><strong>Reasoning Dropout</strong> (top left, smaller: Reasoning Pre-training)</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the slide 24-25 diagram completed: the pre-trained policy (faded, now also labelled “Action: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}]</annotation></semantics></math>”) transfers to a second policy that maps the scene directly to “Action: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}]</annotation></semantics></math>”, with no reasoning at test time.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the heading emphasises Reasoning Dropout while the diagram still shows the completed reasoning pre-training pipeline; slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning</em>, Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, <strong>Mees</strong>, Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.</p>", "text": "Reasoning Dropout (top left, smaller: Reasoning Pre-training) [Figure: the slide 24-25 diagram completed: the pre-trained policy (faded, now also labelled “Action: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] ”) transfers to a second policy that maps the scene directly to “Action: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] ”, with no reasoning at test time.] [Note: the heading emphasises Reasoning Dropout while the diagram still shows the completed reasoning pre-training pipeline; slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames.] Reference: Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning , Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, Mees , Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.", "t": 1440}, {"id": "slide-32-embodied-chain-of-thought-lite-reasoning-dropout-frame-30", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite <span class=\"own\">[reasoning dropout]</span>", "title": "Slide 32: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite [reasoning dropout]", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<p><strong>Reasoning Dropout</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: scene + instruction “Put middle bowl on plate” -&gt; <strong>Policy</strong> -&gt; “Reasoning: Plan, Subtask, Move, Bounding Boxes, Gripper” (dashed box, tagged “Randomly Dropped”) -&gt; “Action: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Grip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\Delta x, \\Delta\\theta, \\Delta\\text{Grip}]</annotation></semantics></math>”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning</em>, Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, <strong>Mees</strong>, Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.</p>", "text": "Reasoning Dropout [Figure: scene + instruction “Put middle bowl on plate” -> Policy -> “Reasoning: Plan, Subtask, Move, Bounding Boxes, Gripper” (dashed box, tagged “Randomly Dropped”) -> “Action: [ Δ x , Δ θ , Δ Grip ] ”.] Reference: Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning , Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, Mees , Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.", "t": 1440}, {"id": "slide-33-results-in-real-world-bridge-widowx-setup-frame-31", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: Results in Real-World Bridge WidowX Setup", "title": "Slide 33: Results in Real-World Bridge WidowX Setup", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left, a sketch of Policy Performance against Inference Freq. (Hz): Embodied CoT sits top left (strong but slow), Standard VLA bottom right (fast but weaker); arrows from both lead to “ECoT-Lite Variants (Ours)” at top right. Caption: “<em>ECoT-lite</em> is faster than reasoning policies and stronger than non-reasoning policies”. Right, two bar charts, values read from the bars, approximate:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Bridge Success Rate</th>\n<th>Inference Rate (Hz)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Embodied CoT</td>\n<td>~78</td>\n<td>~0.4 (hatched extension to ~1.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard VLA</td>\n<td>~51</td>\n<td>~3.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasoning Pre-training</td>\n<td>~70</td>\n<td>~3.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasoning Dropout</td>\n<td>~61</td>\n<td>~3.5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>More Performant than Standard VLAs <em>without</em> sacrificing speed!</p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning</em>, Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, <strong>Mees</strong>, Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: left, a sketch of Policy Performance against Inference Freq. (Hz): Embodied CoT sits top left (strong but slow), Standard VLA bottom right (fast but weaker); arrows from both lead to “ECoT-Lite Variants (Ours)” at top right. Caption: “ ECoT-lite is faster than reasoning policies and stronger than non-reasoning policies”. Right, two bar charts, values read from the bars, approximate:] Bridge Success Rate Inference Rate (Hz) Embodied CoT ~78 ~0.4 (hatched extension to ~1.2) Standard VLA ~51 ~3.5 Reasoning Pre-training ~70 ~3.5 Reasoning Dropout ~61 ~3.5 More Performant than Standard VLAs without sacrificing speed! Reference: Training Strategies for Efficient Embodied Reasoning , Chen, Belkhale, Mirchandani, Mees , Driess, Pertsch, Levine. CoRL, 2025. [Note: slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35.]", "t": 1440}, {"id": "slide-35-untitled-transition-question-frame-32", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 35: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [32, 32], "html": "<p>Embodied Reasoning so far has used a <strong>fixed compute budget</strong></p>\n<p>Can we do better <strong>by letting it think longer at test time</strong>?</p>\n<p>-&gt; <strong>Test-Time Compute Scaling</strong></p>", "text": "Embodied Reasoning so far has used a fixed compute budget Can we do better by letting it think longer at test time ? -> Test-Time Compute Scaling", "t": 1562}, {"id": "slide-36-isnt-llm-test-time-compute-scaling-just-planning-frame-33", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: Isn’t LLM Test-Time Compute Scaling just Planning?", "title": "Slide 36: Isn’t LLM Test-Time Compute Scaling just Planning?", "frames": [33, 33], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Classical planning (A*, STRIPS, PDDL)</th>\n<th>Learned planning (Chess, Go, Atari)</th>\n<th>LLM reasoning (CoT, GRPO, R1)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>World model</td>\n<td>Hand-specified - Transition function</td>\n<td>Learned - Policy + value network</td>\n<td>Implicit in weights - No explicit model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search / thinking</td>\n<td>Explicit tree search - A*, BFS, DFS</td>\n<td>MCTS rollouts - Many sims per move</td>\n<td>Token generation - Sequential reasoning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verifier</td>\n<td>Goal test - Exact, hand-specified</td>\n<td>Win / loss - Exact, from game rules</td>\n<td>Rule-based reward - Math grader, unit test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Known domains - Structured, discrete</td>\n<td>Discrete games - Bounded action space</td>\n<td>Open-ended tasks - Language, code, robots</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[A “&lt;” sign sits between neighbouring cells of each row and an arrow runs left to right under the table: each column generalises the one before.]</span></p>", "text": "Classical planning (A*, STRIPS, PDDL) Learned planning (Chess, Go, Atari) LLM reasoning (CoT, GRPO, R1) World model Hand-specified - Transition function Learned - Policy + value network Implicit in weights - No explicit model Search / thinking Explicit tree search - A*, BFS, DFS MCTS rollouts - Many sims per move Token generation - Sequential reasoning Verifier Goal test - Exact, hand-specified Win / loss - Exact, from game rules Rule-based reward - Math grader, unit test Scope Known domains - Structured, discrete Discrete games - Bounded action space Open-ended tasks - Language, code, robots [A “<” sign sits between neighbouring cells of each row and an arrow runs left to right under the table: each column generalises the one before.]", "t": 1620}, {"id": "slide-37-scaling-test-time-compute-in-go-frame-34", "num": 37, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 37: Scaling Test-Time Compute in Go", "title": "Slide 37: Scaling Test-Time Compute in Go", "frames": [34, 34], "html": "<p>Rule of Thumb, increasing 120 Elo points requires either:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2x model size</li>\n<li>2x test-time search</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Improving the raw policy from 3000 to 5200 Elo points would require scaling the model by ~100,000x</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: bar chart of Elo rating: Raw Network ~3000 (arrow: “No test-time search”), AlphaGo Zero ~5200 (arrow: “Full AlphaGo Zero”), AlphaGo Master ~4850, AlphaGo Lee ~3700, AlphaGo Fan ~3150, Crazy Stone ~1900, Pachi ~1300, GnuGo ~400. A dotted line at about 3650 marks “Superhuman Performance”.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Slide footer, in square brackets on the slide:]</span> Slides adapted from Noam Brown</p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Mastering the Game of Go without Human Knowledge</em>, Silver, et al. Nature, 2017.</p>", "text": "Rule of Thumb, increasing 120 Elo points requires either: 2x model size 2x test-time search Improving the raw policy from 3000 to 5200 Elo points would require scaling the model by ~100,000x [Figure: bar chart of Elo rating: Raw Network ~3000 (arrow: “No test-time search”), AlphaGo Zero ~5200 (arrow: “Full AlphaGo Zero”), AlphaGo Master ~4850, AlphaGo Lee ~3700, AlphaGo Fan ~3150, Crazy Stone ~1900, Pachi ~1300, GnuGo ~400. A dotted line at about 3650 marks “Superhuman Performance”.] [Slide footer, in square brackets on the slide:] Slides adapted from Noam Brown Reference: Mastering the Game of Go without Human Knowledge , Silver, et al. Nature, 2017.", "t": 1682}, {"id": "slide-38-untitled-transition-question-frame-35", "num": 38, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 38: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 38: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<p>Does this hold too in <strong>language</strong>?</p>", "text": "Does this hold too in language ?", "t": 1801}, {"id": "slide-39-scaling-test-time-compute-in-language-frame-36", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: Scaling Test-Time Compute in Language", "title": "Slide 39: Scaling Test-Time Compute in Language", "frames": [36, 36], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Compute-optimal test-time scaling beats a ~14x larger model on easy/medium problems at equal FLOPs</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Comparing Test-time and Pretraining Compute in a FLOPs Matched Evauation”: relative improvement in accuracy from test-time compute (%) against the ratio of inference tokens to pretraining tokens:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ratio</th>\n<th>Easy Questions</th>\n<th>Medium Questions</th>\n<th>Hard Questions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;&lt;1</td>\n<td>+21.6%</td>\n<td>+27.8%</td>\n<td>+11.8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>~=1</td>\n<td>+16.7%</td>\n<td>+3.5%</td>\n<td>-11.9%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;&gt;1</td>\n<td>+5.4%</td>\n<td>-24.3%</td>\n<td>-37.2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>SFT on own failed attempts + learned verifier - model learns to produce better answer given previous wrong ones, verifier picks the best one</li>\n<li>Generate multiple answers at test time, select best with verifier</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally can be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters</em>, Snell et al, 2024.</p>", "text": "Compute-optimal test-time scaling beats a ~14x larger model on easy/medium problems at equal FLOPs [Figure: “Comparing Test-time and Pretraining Compute in a FLOPs Matched Evauation”: relative improvement in accuracy from test-time compute (%) against the ratio of inference tokens to pretraining tokens:] Ratio Easy Questions Medium Questions Hard Questions <<1 +21.6% +27.8% +11.8% ~=1 +16.7% +3.5% -11.9% >>1 +5.4% -24.3% -37.2% SFT on own failed attempts + learned verifier - model learns to produce better answer given previous wrong ones, verifier picks the best one Generate multiple answers at test time, select best with verifier Reference: Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally can be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters , Snell et al, 2024.", "t": 1861}, {"id": "slide-40-why-do-reasoning-tokens-help-frame-37", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: Why Do Reasoning Tokens Help?", "title": "Slide 40: Why Do Reasoning Tokens Help?", "frames": [37, 37], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Any problem solvable in T computational steps can be solved by a <strong>constant-size transformer</strong> generating <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning tokens</li>\n<li>Generating <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate tokens before the answer gives the model <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> effective computation steps</li>\n<li>Without reasoning tokens: needs a much deeper/larger model or can’t solve the problem at all</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>Chain of Thought Empowers Transformers to Solve Inherently Serial Problems</em>, Li et al, 2024.</p>", "text": "Any problem solvable in T computational steps can be solved by a constant-size transformer generating O ( T ) reasoning tokens Generating O ( T ) intermediate tokens before the answer gives the model O ( T ) effective computation steps Without reasoning tokens: needs a much deeper/larger model or can’t solve the problem at all Reference: Chain of Thought Empowers Transformers to Solve Inherently Serial Problems , Li et al, 2024.", "t": 1980}, {"id": "slide-41-chain-of-thought-with-llms-frame-38", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: Chain-of-Thought with LLMs", "title": "Slide 41: Chain-of-Thought with LLMs", "frames": [38, 38], "html": "<p>More tokens = more computation steps</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the well-known side-by-side example from the CoT paper. Standard Prompting: a one-shot arithmetic word problem with a bare answer, then a new word problem; the model output gives a wrong number (red cross). Chain-of-Thought Prompting: the same prompt, but the example answer spells out the intermediate arithmetic before the answer (highlighted); the model output also writes out its steps and reaches the correct answer (green tick).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models</em>, Wei, Wang et al, NeurIPS 2023.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the CoT paper appeared at NeurIPS 2022.]</span></p>", "text": "More tokens = more computation steps [Figure: the well-known side-by-side example from the CoT paper. Standard Prompting: a one-shot arithmetic word problem with a bare answer, then a new word problem; the model output gives a wrong number (red cross). Chain-of-Thought Prompting: the same prompt, but the example answer spells out the intermediate arithmetic before the answer (highlighted); the model output also writes out its steps and reaches the correct answer (green tick).] Reference: Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models , Wei, Wang et al, NeurIPS 2023. [Note: the CoT paper appeared at NeurIPS 2022.]", "t": 2041}, {"id": "slide-42-cot-emerges-in-large-models-frame-39", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: CoT Emerges in Large Models", "title": "Slide 42: CoT Emerges in Large Models", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a 3x3 grid from the CoT paper: solve rate (%) on GSM8K, SVAMP and MAWPS against model scale (# parameters in billions) for LaMDA (0.4-137B), GPT (0.4-175B) and PaLM (8-540B), comparing standard prompting, chain-of-thought prompting and the prior supervised best (dashed). Chain-of-thought helps little for small models and pulls clearly ahead only at the largest scales; on GSM8K, PaLM 540B with CoT (~57%) slightly beats the prior supervised best (~55%).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models</em>, Wei, Wang et al, NeurIPS 2023.</p>", "text": "[Figure: a 3x3 grid from the CoT paper: solve rate (%) on GSM8K, SVAMP and MAWPS against model scale (# parameters in billions) for LaMDA (0.4-137B), GPT (0.4-175B) and PaLM (8-540B), comparing standard prompting, chain-of-thought prompting and the prior supervised best (dashed). Chain-of-thought helps little for small models and pulls clearly ahead only at the largest scales; on GSM8K, PaLM 540B with CoT (~57%) slightly beats the prior supervised best (~55%).] Reference: Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models , Wei, Wang et al, NeurIPS 2023.", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-43-original-cot-prompting-few-shotin-context-learning-frame-40", "num": 43, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 43: Original CoT Prompting = Few-shot/In-Context Learning", "title": "Slide 43: Original CoT Prompting = Few-shot/In-Context Learning", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<pre class=\"text\"><code>Q: [problem 1]\nA: [step 1... step 2... therefore answer] ✓\nQ: [problem 2]\nA: [step 1... step 2... therefore answer] ✓\nQ: [new problem]\nA: ???</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>⚠️ Prompt-sensitive, brittle</li>\n<li>⚠️ Requires Human input</li>\n<li>⚠️ Doesn’t generalize beyond the examples</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Q: [problem 1] A: [step 1... step 2... therefore answer] ✓ Q: [problem 2] A: [step 1... step 2... therefore answer] ✓ Q: [new problem] A: ??? ⚠️ Prompt-sensitive, brittle ⚠️ Requires Human input ⚠️ Doesn’t generalize beyond the examples", "t": 2102}, {"id": "slide-44-untitled-transition-question-frame-41", "num": 44, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 44: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 44: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<p>Instead of eliciting reasoning with human prompts, can we <strong>train the model to reason intrinsically</strong>?</p>", "text": "Instead of eliciting reasoning with human prompts, can we train the model to reason intrinsically ?", "t": 2220}, {"id": "slide-45-untitled-llm-vs-reasoning-model-response-frame-42", "num": 45, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 45: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: LLM vs reasoning-model response]</span>", "title": "Slide 45: [untitled: LLM vs reasoning-model response]", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure from the cited paper, summarised rather than copied: one arithmetic word problem (pages written per year), answered two ways. Top, “Large Language Model (LLM) GPT-4o’s Response”, labelled “Single Chain of Thought”: a short numbered step-by-step calculation ending in the answer (624 pages, boxed). Bottom, “Large Reasoning Model (LRM) DeepSeek-R1’s Response”: a long <code>&lt;think&gt; ... &lt;/think&gt;</code> block labelled “Thinking Process”, full of self-checking phrases (underlined: “Wait”, “Let me check that”, “Alternatively”, “Is that right?”, “I need to double-check”, a digression about leap years), followed by a short “Answer” that reaches the same 624 pages (boxed).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>DeepSeek-R1 Thoughtology: Let’s think about LLM reasoning</em>, Marjanović et al, 2026.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: this frame shows no slide number; it sits between slides 44 and 46.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure from the cited paper, summarised rather than copied: one arithmetic word problem (pages written per year), answered two ways. Top, “Large Language Model (LLM) GPT-4o’s Response”, labelled “Single Chain of Thought”: a short numbered step-by-step calculation ending in the answer (624 pages, boxed). Bottom, “Large Reasoning Model (LRM) DeepSeek-R1’s Response”: a long <think> ... </think> block labelled “Thinking Process”, full of self-checking phrases (underlined: “Wait”, “Let me check that”, “Alternatively”, “Is that right?”, “I need to double-check”, a digression about leap years), followed by a short “Answer” that reaches the same 624 pages (boxed).] Reference: DeepSeek-R1 Thoughtology: Let’s think about LLM reasoning , Marjanović et al, 2026. [Note: this frame shows no slide number; it sits between slides 44 and 46.]", "t": 2461}, {"id": "slide-46-learning-to-reason-with-rl---grpo-frame-43", "num": 46, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 46: Learning to Reason with RL - GRPO", "title": "Slide 46: Learning to Reason with RL - GRPO", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sample answers, compare with each other, update toward good ones</li>\n<li>No reward model, no value function, no human labels</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: left to right. Prompt <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> (a train-speed word problem) -&gt; Policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (policy model) -&gt; <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts (chain-of-thought + answer): three reach the correct answer, two do not -&gt; Reward <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i</annotation></semantics></math> (binary; correctness, format): 1, 1, 1, 0, 0 -&gt; Advantage <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{A}_i = \\frac{r_i - \\mu_r}{\\sigma_r}</annotation></semantics></math>, green bars for the correct rollouts and red for the wrong ones; “No critic model - rewards relative to other samples in the group” -&gt; GRPO objective -&gt; Updated <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\theta&#39;}</annotation></semantics></math> (reinforce the good, suppress the bad).]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">clip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">KL</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{G} \\sum_{i=1}^{G} \\min\\big(\\rho_i \\hat{A}_i,\\ \\text{clip}(\\rho_i, 1 \\pm \\varepsilon)\\, \\hat{A}_i\\big) - \\beta\\, \\text{KL}(\\pi_\\theta \\,\\|\\, \\pi_{\\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">old</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_i = \\pi_\\theta / \\pi_{\\text{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>: importance ratio</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">clip</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{clip}(\\rho_i, 1 \\pm \\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>: trust region (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon \\approx 0.2</annotation></semantics></math>)</li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">KL</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta \\cdot \\text{KL}(\\pi_\\theta \\| \\pi_{\\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math>: drift penalty from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{\\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{A}_i</annotation></semantics></math>: group-relative advantage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reference: <em>DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models</em>, Shao et al, 2024.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the objective is written to be maximised; as a loss to minimise, the sign flips.]</span></p>", "text": "Sample answers, compare with each other, update toward good ones No reward model, no value function, no human labels [Figure: left to right. Prompt q (a train-speed word problem) -> Policy π θ (policy model) -> G rollouts (chain-of-thought + answer): three reach the correct answer, two do not -> Reward r i (binary; correctness, format): 1, 1, 1, 0, 0 -> Advantage A ̂ i = r i − μ r σ r , green bars for the correct rollouts and red for the wrong ones; “No critic model - rewards relative to other samples in the group” -> GRPO objective -> Updated π θ ′ (reinforce the good, suppress the bad).] ℒ = 1 G ∑ i = 1 G min &#8289; ( ρ i A ̂ i , clip ( ρ i , 1 ± ε ) A ̂ i ) − β KL ( π θ ∥ π ref ) ρ i = π θ / π old : importance ratio clip ( ρ i , 1 ± ε ) : trust region ( ε ≈ 0.2 ) β ⋅ KL ( π θ ∥ π ref ) : drift penalty from π ref A ̂ i : group-relative advantage Reference: DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models , Shao et al, 2024. [Note: the objective is written to be maximised; as a loss to minimise, the sign flips.]", "t": 2461}, {"id": "slide-47-untitled-transition-question-frames-44-45", "num": 47, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 47: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 47: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [44, 45], "html": "<p><strong>Why didn’t this work before?</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Second build (frame 45):]</span> <strong>Base LLM needs to be good enough to produce any correct rollouts to reinforce!</strong></p>", "text": "Why didn’t this work before? [Second build (frame 45):] Base LLM needs to be good enough to produce any correct rollouts to reinforce!", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-48-reasoning-behaviors-emerge-from-rl-frame-46", "num": 48, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 48: Reasoning Behaviors Emerge from RL", "title": "Slide 48: Reasoning Behaviors Emerge from RL", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two plots against training steps (0 to ~10,500). (a) The frequency of reflection words climbs from about 1,000-2,000 early on to 6,000-10,000 after about 6,000 steps, peaking near 14,300. (b) The frequency of the word “wait” stays near zero until about 4,000 steps, then rises, spiking to about 1,500 near 8,600 steps. Caption: “Figure 9 | Evolution of reasoning behaviors during training. (a) Frequency of representative reflective words during the training process; (b) Specific occurrence patterns of the word ‘wait’ throughout the training process.”]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning</em>, DeepSeek-AI, 2026.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: DeepSeek-R1 appeared on arXiv in January 2025 and in Nature in September 2025; the slide’s 2026 matches neither.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: two plots against training steps (0 to ~10,500). (a) The frequency of reflection words climbs from about 1,000-2,000 early on to 6,000-10,000 after about 6,000 steps, peaking near 14,300. (b) The frequency of the word “wait” stays near zero until about 4,000 steps, then rises, spiking to about 1,500 near 8,600 steps. Caption: “Figure 9 | Evolution of reasoning behaviors during training. (a) Frequency of representative reflective words during the training process; (b) Specific occurrence patterns of the word ‘wait’ throughout the training process.”] Reference: DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning , DeepSeek-AI, 2026. [Note: DeepSeek-R1 appeared on arXiv in January 2025 and in Nature in September 2025; the slide’s 2026 matches neither.]", "t": 2580}, {"id": "slide-49-untitled-transition-question-frame-47", "num": 49, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 49: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 49: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<p>Does this RL reasoning apply too in <strong>vision</strong>?</p>", "text": "Does this RL reasoning apply too in vision ?", "t": 2821}, {"id": "slide-50-visual-reinforcement-fine-tuning-frame-48", "num": 50, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 50: Visual Reinforcement Fine-Tuning", "title": "Slide 50: Visual Reinforcement Fine-Tuning", "frames": [48, 48], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Applies GRPO to enhance visual grounding of VLMs</li>\n<li>The verifier is IoU (geometric), not a math grader</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a policy model answers visual questions (“the part of the vehicle that can be opened?” about a caravan photo; “What spices is the flower in the image?” about a flower photo) with a <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> reasoning block and an answer (a bounding box on the caravan door; “columbine”). Several candidate boxes are scored by a verifiable reward and the policy is updated by policy-gradient optimization against a frozen reference model. Rewards:]</span></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if match</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">if </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\" style=\"text-align: left\"><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{IoU} = \\begin{cases} f(IoU), &amp; \\text{if match} \\\\ 0, &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases} \\qquad R_{cls} = \\begin{cases} 1, &amp; \\text{if } P_{cate} = GT_{cate} \\\\ 0, &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Visual-RFT: Visual Reinforcement Fine-Tuning</em>, Liu et al., 2025.</p>", "text": "Applies GRPO to enhance visual grounding of VLMs The verifier is IoU (geometric), not a math grader [Figure: a policy model answers visual questions (“the part of the vehicle that can be opened?” about a caravan photo; “What spices is the flower in the image?” about a flower photo) with a <think> reasoning block and an answer (a bounding box on the caravan door; “columbine”). Several candidate boxes are scored by a verifiable reward and the policy is updated by policy-gradient optimization against a frozen reference model. Rewards:] R I o U = { f ( I o U ) , if match 0 , otherwise R c l s = { 1 , if P c a t e = G T c a t e 0 , otherwise Reference: Visual-RFT: Visual Reinforcement Fine-Tuning , Liu et al., 2025.", "t": 2821}, {"id": "slide-51-untitled-failure-mode-of-answer-only-rewards-frame-49", "num": 51, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 51: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: failure mode of answer-only rewards]</span>", "title": "Slide 51: [untitled: failure mode of answer-only rewards]", "frames": [49, 49], "html": "<p><strong>Failure mode</strong>: Standard GRPO rewards the final answer only, the <strong>reasoning trace is invisible to the verifier</strong></p>\n<p>In vision, hallucinated reasoning &amp; correct answers aren’t mutually exclusive</p>", "text": "Failure mode : Standard GRPO rewards the final answer only, the reasoning trace is invisible to the verifier In vision, hallucinated reasoning & correct answers aren’t mutually exclusive", "t": 2882}, {"id": "slide-52-argos---agentic-reasoning-with-multimodal-rl-frame-50", "num": 52, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 52: ARGOS - Agentic Reasoning with Multimodal RL", "title": "Slide 52: ARGOS - Agentic Reasoning with Multimodal RL", "frames": [50, 50], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Key:</strong> aggregate multiple reward signal from intermediate reasoning steps, ie spatial &amp; temporal reasoning in videos</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an image question (distance between a lamp and a sofa; response “1.8 metres”) and a video question (which action the person failed to complete; response “the bottle cap is on … option C”) go to an <strong>Adaptive Verifier</strong>. For the image it scores a Spatial 2D Point (object: white lamp, pixel coordinate x=346, y=126), the reasoning quality (image + question + response) and the final answer (predicted 1.8, ground-truth 2.1). For the video it scores a Spatial 2D Point (dark bottle, x=195, y=199, frame 22, time 23.01 s), a Temporal Segment (event: the person is handling what appears to be an oil bottle, frames 22-23, 23.01-24.11 s) and the final answer (predicted C, ground-truth B). Teacher models and scoring functions: Grounding DINO, SAM-2, MOLMO-7B, GLM-4.5V, Pointing Hand Metric, String Match, Relative Accuracy, Language Model Score.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Multimodal Reinforcement Learning with Agentic Verifier for AI Agents</em>, Tan, Peng, Yang, Cheng, <strong>Mees</strong> et al. arxiv, 2025.</p>", "text": "Key: aggregate multiple reward signal from intermediate reasoning steps, ie spatial & temporal reasoning in videos [Figure: an image question (distance between a lamp and a sofa; response “1.8 metres”) and a video question (which action the person failed to complete; response “the bottle cap is on … option C”) go to an Adaptive Verifier . For the image it scores a Spatial 2D Point (object: white lamp, pixel coordinate x=346, y=126), the reasoning quality (image + question + response) and the final answer (predicted 1.8, ground-truth 2.1). For the video it scores a Spatial 2D Point (dark bottle, x=195, y=199, frame 22, time 23.01 s), a Temporal Segment (event: the person is handling what appears to be an oil bottle, frames 22-23, 23.01-24.11 s) and the final answer (predicted C, ground-truth B). Teacher models and scoring functions: Grounding DINO, SAM-2, MOLMO-7B, GLM-4.5V, Pointing Hand Metric, String Match, Relative Accuracy, Language Model Score.] Reference: Multimodal Reinforcement Learning with Agentic Verifier for AI Agents , Tan, Peng, Yang, Cheng, Mees et al. arxiv, 2025.", "t": 2882}, {"id": "slide-53-argos---embodied-ai-benchmarks-frames-51-54", "num": 53, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 53: ARGOS - Embodied AI Benchmarks", "title": "Slide 53: ARGOS - Embodied AI Benchmarks", "frames": [51, 54], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Test-time reasoning helps for high-level planning</li>\n<li>3-4x better than supervised CoT on the hardest tasks</li>\n<li>For continuous control, internalize reasoning during VLA training and drop it at inference - ECoT-Lite style</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: three embedded simulation videos playing across four frames. A robot arm with the instruction “Stack the maroon triangular prism and the olive triangular prism in sequence.” (“Environment Step 5: Task successfully completed!”); an indoor navigation agent with “Navigate to the Toaster in the room and be as close as possible to it.” (a planning panel, then “Environment Step 14-15: Task successfully completed!” in front of a toaster); a robot arm at a stove with a pan and a moka pot.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Multimodal Reinforcement Learning with Agentic Verifier for AI Agents</em>, Tan, Peng, Yang, Cheng, <strong>Mees</strong> et al. arxiv, 2025.</p>", "text": "Test-time reasoning helps for high-level planning 3-4x better than supervised CoT on the hardest tasks For continuous control, internalize reasoning during VLA training and drop it at inference - ECoT-Lite style [Figure: three embedded simulation videos playing across four frames. A robot arm with the instruction “Stack the maroon triangular prism and the olive triangular prism in sequence.” (“Environment Step 5: Task successfully completed!”); an indoor navigation agent with “Navigate to the Toaster in the room and be as close as possible to it.” (a planning panel, then “Environment Step 14-15: Task successfully completed!” in front of a toaster); a robot arm at a stove with a pan and a moka pot.] Reference: Multimodal Reinforcement Learning with Agentic Verifier for AI Agents , Tan, Peng, Yang, Cheng, Mees et al. arxiv, 2025.", "t": 3061}, {"id": "slide-54-conclusion-frame-55", "num": 54, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 54: Conclusion", "title": "Slide 54: Conclusion", "frames": [55, 55], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Scaling test-time compute is often more effective than scaling pre-training</li>\n<li>Reasoning SFT in VLAs improves representations for control - drop reasoning at inference</li>\n<li>RL can induce emergent reasoning in LLMs and VLMs</li>\n<li>For multimodal agents, verifying the reasoning trace, not just the final answer, is essential</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Scaling test-time compute is often more effective than scaling pre-training Reasoning SFT in VLAs improves representations for control - drop reasoning at inference RL can induce emergent reasoning in LLMs and VLMs For multimodal agents, verifying the reasoning trace, not just the final answer, is essential", "t": 3181}, {"id": "end-frame-56", "num": null, "kind": "end", "title_html": "End", "title": "End", "frames": [56, 56], "html": "<p>Thank you for your attention</p>", "text": "Thank you for your attention", "t": null}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "title-frame-1", "label": "Title", "html": "the slide says “Lecture 11”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 10 (week 10, May 4)."}, {"slide": "slide-7-generalization-evaluations-frame-12", "label": "Slide 7: Generalization Evaluations", "html": "the arrow spans about 28 points of absolute success rate (37 -&gt; 65); “30%” is the absolute gap, not a relative improvement."}, {"slide": "slide-18-results-in-libero-90-frame-26", "label": "Slide 18: Results in LIBERO-90", "html": "slides 19-23 do not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 18 to 24."}, {"slide": "slide-24-embodied-chain-of-thought-lite-reasoning-pre-training-frames-27-28", "label": "Slide 24: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite [reasoning pre-training]", "html": "frames 27 and 28 carry slide numbers 24 and 25; slides 26-29 do not appear in the captured frames."}, {"slide": "slide-30-embodied-chain-of-thought-lite-pre-training-completed-frame-29", "label": "Slide 30: Embodied Chain-of-Thought Lite [pre-training completed]", "html": "the heading emphasises Reasoning Dropout while the diagram still shows the completed reasoning pre-training pipeline; slide 31 does not appear in the captured frames."}, {"slide": "slide-33-results-in-real-world-bridge-widowx-setup-frame-31", "label": "Slide 33: Results in Real-World Bridge WidowX Setup", "html": "slide 34 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 33 to 35."}, {"slide": "slide-41-chain-of-thought-with-llms-frame-38", "label": "Slide 41: Chain-of-Thought with LLMs", "html": "the CoT paper appeared at NeurIPS 2022."}, {"slide": "slide-45-untitled-llm-vs-reasoning-model-response-frame-42", "label": "Slide 45: [untitled: LLM vs reasoning-model response]", "html": "this frame shows no slide number; it sits between slides 44 and 46."}, {"slide": "slide-46-learning-to-reason-with-rl---grpo-frame-43", "label": "Slide 46: Learning to Reason with RL - GRPO", "html": "the objective is written to be maximised; as a loss to minimise, the sign flips."}, {"slide": "slide-48-reasoning-behaviors-emerge-from-rl-frame-46", "label": "Slide 48: Reasoning Behaviors Emerge from RL", "html": "DeepSeek-R1 appeared on arXiv in January 2025 and in Nature in September 2025; the slide’s 2026 matches neither."}]}, {"key": "11", "dir": "week11_frontiers", "date": "Mon 11 May 2026", "topic": "Frontier & Open Problems", "summary": "A survey of what is still unsolved: hype vs real generalization, which backbone and data recipe to bet on, data collection interfaces, dexterity beyond vision (touch, audio), knowing when to reason or ask for help, scaling RL with a data flywheel, lifelong learning, rapid in-context adaptation, unified mobile manipulation and multimodal memory. Ends with advice on doing research and the lecturer's own PhD story.", "key_ideas": ["Data scalability trades off against hardware alignment: egocentric video scales best, teleop matches the robot best", "Adaptive test-time compute: react fast when certain, reason when uncertain, escalate when lost", "Introspection - knowing what you don't know - is an open problem", "Good research problem = important problem + plan + excitement + your own reviewer #2"], "papers": [{"title": "A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence", "who": "LeCun, 2022", "url": "https://openreview.net/pdf?id=BZ5a1r-kVsf"}, {"title": "The Bitter Lesson", "who": "Sutton, 2019", "url": "http://www.incompleteideas.net/IncIdeas/BitterLesson.html"}, {"title": "Intelligence without Representation", "who": "Brooks, 1991", "url": "https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/papers/representation.pdf"}], "homework": [], "lecture": {"id": "eL4lcy1KNzE", "title": "Robot Learning 2026 – Lecture 11: Frontier & Open Problems | ETH Zürich", "speaker": "Oier Mees", "duration": "1:02:05", "description": "Lecture 11 from the ETH Zürich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals  to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), taught by Oier Mees.\nCourse page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/\nLecturer: https://www.oiermees.com", "chapters": [], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, welcome everybody. To the 12th and last lecture from my side for the discourse on robot learning. Um before I start with today's topic on the frontier and open problems. I did made a slide but &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; just a quick announcement for the project. We will send you an email tonight with some information for the PCs where you will be able to test your policies. Um so, we basically we found a bunch of PCs at my work in Microsoft and we brought them to ETH. We put them in a room and this will be the PCs that you will also deploy your policies on in demo day. And so, we will give you information on how you can access them I think starting tomorrow like later tonight via email. Um so, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; yeah, that was a kind of a quick announcement before we dive deep into today's topic. Um"}, {"t": 61, "text": "which is I'm going to talk today a little bit about like frontier and and open problems in in robot learning. And then I will end a little bit with some tips also that I found helpful on how to research for example like if you're interested in tackling any of these problems. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, to begin with let's start with something you've probably seen if you follow robotics on social media. Um because pretty much every single week someone declares that robotics is is solved or or manipulation is solved. Um and yeah, so I don't know a few days ago like you know, Genesis AI dropped a a new announcement and they declared this is the we are approaching the end game for robotics. Um Uh Brett Adcock also tweeted um about having their chat GPT moment for robotics in their lab. uh 2 years ago. Um we're still waiting"}, {"t": 120, "text": "on that. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Uh and others uh promise like the the road map to physical HRI and so forth. And so um I'm not saying um this is not like criticizing or I'm not saying you shouldn't believe this announcement or anything, but like just like take them uh you might want to take them with a grain of salt um because uh essentially uh you know, how can you believe them is somebody else already uh declared to have solved robotics uh the week before, right? So uh what happens when you ask to uh a robot to actually generalize, right? Um and so on the left you can see uh like a Boston Dynamics humanoid and on the right a mobile manipulator from Stanford trying to perform uh cooking-related tasks in a kitchen uh environment. And uh as you can see uh for the the kitchen one uh you might uh uh will have to wait quite a long time until the robot can cook dinner for you. Um now these two videos are not"}, {"t": 182, "text": "absolutely the latest results. Um they're a few years old. Um but they're kind of representative of something important, which is um when [clears throat] you take the robot out of the lab and you know, out of the lab setting and put it in a new um you know, environment and ask it to generalize then uh like maybe with the new lighting, new objects, new camera viewpoints and so forth, like things things start to break uh very quickly. Um and so um although looking at like the very impressive and very cool like um demo videos on on social media, um you know, you might be inclined to to to believe that robotics is solved. Um if you take the same robot on the same demo and put like put it in the next room uh outside like its training distribution, it will probably like fail quite quite badly. Um so how do these uh cool uh like Twitter demos that supposedly uh are the chat GPT moment for robotics um or the end"}, {"t": 241, "text": "game uh work or how do they get made? Um so, I want to share with you the uh really not-so-secret recipe um on how you can do a a rockstar demo uh on real robots. Um so, first of all like just collect uh training data um on your own. Don't rely on anybody else to do so. Just collect as much training data as possible on your target uh platform. Um the second step is um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; train a behavior cloning policy uh and just keep collecting data until extrapolation becomes interpolation for your policy. Um and then the third, uh if possible, try to train and test on the same day uh to avoid any distributional shifts whatsoever in the environment, no changes in the lighting, I know what changes in on camera viewpoints, table heights, and nothing. Right? Um and so, it turns out um you can get a lot of money from VCs for following this very simple recipe."}, {"t": 302, "text": "Um So, but what is the actual problem we're trying to solve in robotics? And I like to think um about generalization as this kind of like um hierarchy of scopes where um like the most impressive demos you see are operating in this very narrow um kind of like between one object and one room uh kind of kind of um circle. Um and of course like you know, like there's more ambitious work trying to pull towards like you know, maybe like generalizing the one building uh kind of environment. Um but the real goal is like the world, right? Um and because uh humans generalize to the real world and um you don't need to have seen um, like every kitchen to cook in, uh, in order, uh, to cook in a new kitchen, right? Uh, you don't need to have picked up every possible, like, object or every possible mark in the world, uh, to understand how you need to"}, {"t": 360, "text": "grasp, like, uh, a new unseen mark, right? Um, and so, basically, like, you know, you you kind of stitch together your knowledge, um, reason about novel situations, and compose what you already know in novel ways to to be able to, um, do this sort of generalization, um, and, uh, that's not interpolation, right? Like, it's really like kind of this um, this missing, um, like, intelligent reasoning or whatever you want to call it, uh, thing that, um, is still very elusive for our robot policies. Like, we are not there yet. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, and so, this really goes a little bit beyond just collecting more data in order to, um, achieve these abilities, right? Um, and so, where we are today is that we have kind of, like, powerful generalist models, often in the form of, like, VLMs, for example, that are trained on internet-scale data and generalize broadly, um, but often lack this physical grounding, um, so, they can kind of describe, for example, an"}, {"t": 420, "text": "image with a glass on a table, but, um, they don't truly understand what will happen if, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; if they push, uh, the, um, this off the, um, off the edge and that it breaks apart, right? Um, and, um, this kind of also applies to generative video models, uh, where, I don't know, like, models like Sora can, um, synthesize really, um, very uh, realistic-looking, like, kind of physical interactions, right? Like, liquids pouring or or objects falling, um, but if you look closely, they they they do actually tend to violate, like, physical, uh, basic physics, um, so, they are very good at making it look like they um like like the world, but don't really like fully understand um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; like physics, for example. And so, um on the other hand, like we we we do have like specialists um kind of embodied models or policies um that can perform like uh heterogeneous um sorry,"}, {"t": 482, "text": "uh dexterous tasks uh and consume like more heterogeneous uh sensing modalities in order to better interact in the in the world. Um, but uh do tend to uh not generalize um kind of to broader uh in the wild tasks. And so, the our our question in robot learning is kind of like to um you know, to kind of find like the the the best of the both worlds, right? Um And so, what is the path forward? Um I believe that um in order to achieve like this this sort of like um true embodied uh intelligence, uh we we we need to move kind of to beyond like this this passive data um um uh um that like uh foundation models are trained on towards grounding them in the in the physical world um by by feeding them uh like richer embodied data um that is inherently multimodal, spatial uh and also temporal. Um and so, uh"}, {"t": 543, "text": "ideally, this would then help us um build agents that can not like describe the world, but actually um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; reason and and like more intelligently interact uh with with it. So, this brings us to the kind of first open problem. Uh what is the best uh backbone for robotics, right? And it seems we have um this kind of three uh camps in the community right now. And the first one says like, \"Oh, let's use a vision uh language model because VLMs already come uh with um all this like rich semantic knowledge uh about the world um and they can reason about scenes and so forth. Um so, we can fine-tune them on some robot data, uh and then you get like this, let's say, object-level um semantic generalization for free. Um the second camp says, uh let's use a generative video model um because they better understand temporal dynamics. Um They kind of better model um how the"}, {"t": 601, "text": "environment or the world uh evolves around uh across time. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and um their pre-training is a little bit more similar um to what a robot policy um needs to do, like essentially like reasoning over consequences of of the actions, right? Um and then there is the maybe third camp that maybe is a little bit more provocative that says like, \"Well, um the backbone actually doesn't matter that much if we have enough uh real-world uh robot data, then we can just train everything from scratch. Uh and forget about internet pre-training. Like, we just care about robotics. Let's just like collect robot data, train a big model on it. That's it.\" Um and uh well, it's interestingly um Scott Reed gave a um kind of like a guest lecture on exactly this topic um a few weeks ago. Uh so, if you missed it, uh it's on YouTube. Um you can find it on the course uh website."}, {"t": 660, "text": "And uh invite you to to watch it um if you're interested in this. Um And so, yeah, I think in general like each of these positions has like serious proponents. Um but the jury is kind of still out on on which one is the the best way to move forward. Um and this is closely related the the backbone uh question is very closely related also to the data question, right? What is the best data recipe for for robotics uh or for training a like kind of like more generalist robot policy? Right, so um there are like different approaches like for example on the left this could be like this three three tier um approach where you start with web data um to have like kind of good uh world knowledge and and grounding language grounding. And then you um uh scale uh with some like simulated uh interaction uh for kind of like scalable cheap like experience uh and then like kind of a a little real"}, {"t": 721, "text": "world robot data on top uh to kind of bridge the sim-to-real gap. Um the second uh proponent uh could be like stripping down the the simulation uh layer um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; because um well the argument um these companies is that well um you know like simulating um the entire world accurately is um very very challenging and needs a lot of engineering and you might rather um better invest the time uh actually collecting real world data instead of trying to figure how to um simulate um all all the physics and everything. Right? Um and here are a few of more data recipes that follow some of the uh background choices um we just discussed. So on the left this would be like [clears throat] a kind of uh like a data recipe that treats uh video as a first class citizen uh where you kind of start training maybe on general like YouTube like internet videos"}, {"t": 780, "text": "um then add some human egocentric uh videos for mid training uh and then end post training it um uh to predict robot actions. Um and um the intuition as a forementioned is that uh video is a far richer prior for embodied tasks than uh let's say visual question answering tasks uh could ever be. Um and then on the on the right um you can see like kind of the like the from scratch recipe of just training like all the layers like with real world data maybe like it varies a bit maybe with on the type of annotations and granularity but just like really going all in on the real world data. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, once you've decided on your data recipe, um, you also face kind of the next question of like how do you actually collect like real world data, right? Um, at scale cuz all of the like no matter what like in the end like you will need some like more or less like"}, {"t": 841, "text": "real world data. Um, and so what you're looking at here is kind of like a zoo of, um, data collection interfaces um, each making different trade-offs uh, between scalability um, alignment with the target embodiment uh, and um, the complexity of the tasks um, that you can demonstrate. Um, so for example, um, Yumi style grippers um, as you saw in the talk, um, from uh, Cheng um, are more scalable than teleoperation robots um, but now you need to do like some sort of cross embodiment learning. Um, puppeteering gives you higher quality data than um, VR teleop for example, but it's the most expensive one uh, to collect as you need also like a leader follower uh, arm setups um, and this means you will now also have more hardware to break. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and on this plot you can see a little bit the trade-offs between the um, data scalability and the hardware alignment."}, {"t": 901, "text": "Um, so on the uh, vertical on the Y axis you can see the how much data you can collect uh, ranging from thousands to uh, tens of millions of of hours and on the horizontal axis um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um, you can see how close how closely this data aligns with um, the actual hardware that you want to deploy on. And so, egocentric videos gives you tens of millions of hours of data, but it's human data, it's not robot data. So, there is some quite a big embodiment gap. Um While teleop gives you perfectly aligned data with the robot fully in the loop, but you are kind of a bit more capped in terms of like the data volume because it's like slower and expensive. And data wearables like let's say for example gloves sit somewhere in the middle."}, {"t": 961, "text": "Um so, yeah, basically solving these trade-offs or finding like your ways around it like for example, I don't know like by having a humanoid robot to better like leverage like egocentric videos or things like that is one of one of the open problems that community is looking at. So, what is a um what is kind of like the goal we are working towards? Um And in general, I think maybe one good framing framing is that ideally we would like to have like dextrous generalist policies. &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And the challenge is that um so far dexterity and generalization have required somewhat different ingredients for success. Um so, this means if you think about it like for example for dexterity you typically need high frequency"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "control. Um but this is like a little bit at odds with running very large foundation models, right? Cuz they're like more slow and computationally more expensive. Um for dextrous Um, dexterity you also typically need more heterogeneous sensing, for example, like touch sensing um, modalities that most foundation models, um, simply don't consume today. Um, and on the other hand, if you want if you care about pushing generalization, um, then, um, you need to leverage, uh, foundation models, right? Uh, and, uh, also for robotics, you also need to think about mobility because, um, uh, robots that can, uh, move across different, uh, environments, uh, and accumulate, uh, experience, um, across different, like, rooms and and buildings and so on, uh, they, uh, can learn to generalize better than, uh, the"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "same robots if they are just like bolted onto a table and like not moving a lot and just like kind of seeing the same scene, right? Um, so, the goal, um, uh, is, um, kind of like we want to be like at the intersection of this Venn diagram, um, where we want to have, like, systems that, um, are dexterous, um, but, um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and can handle, like, kind of the complexities of all the, uh, uh, real manipulation tasks, um, but that are, um, general general enough, um, to do so across, um, like, um, unstructured environments and like any kind of, like, unseen objects that we throw at them. And so, um, yeah, most systems are kind of like in one of two camps, but like that's one of the open problems, um, to try to develop, um, algorithms and systems that kind of, uh, go more towards this goal of, um, having like, uh, dexterous generalist policies that"}, {"t": 1140, "text": "generalize. Um, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, let me maybe illustrate a point about dexterity requiring more than vision, uh, with kind of like a personal video, um, from, uh, a PhD Oh, I think you don't Can you hear the sound? Sorry, I didn't check. Okay, so you you can't hear the sound, but um what what is happening here is so uh I spent quite a lot of time training like a model that would take like an RGB image as an input and predict 3D shapes and objects pose. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; um so that even though um like a robot the robot had like a depth camera cuz they were like partially occluded, um you could um kind of predict like the object like like for example the handle of the mug and so on like very accurately to better grasp it. Um and what you what you see here is that the"}, {"t": 1201, "text": "robot like misses the handle, um just goes for grasping the mug directly, and absolutely destroys it because it has no touch or or force feedback. So, it was kind of like doing a blind grasp that was like too powerful. Um and I think that our current VLAs are also probably still annihilating objects because we haven't made that much progress in this front. Um and I think our robots are still kind of blind most of the time when we're making contact with objects. And so, your first idea could be well, we uh if you remember we covered like natively in multimodal models earlier in the course, um the idea being that instead of bolting like additional modalities to like vision, we could like train a unified transformer that can from scratch across all modalities jointly, right? Um"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "&gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, we could throw in like touch, force, proprioception, all of this into it, and we solve the problem, right? Um but the issue is that um the moment [clears throat] you try to do that, um you uh basically run into like the what I would call the long tail of robot sensing. And um there are like a few issues with this, and um the first and foremost, as you probably can guess, is the data scarcity. So, many of the modalities that matter for dexterity, uh like touch, tactile uh feedback, force, um are usually not available at scale, um uh cuz the hardware is is expensive, it's fragile, um and also like not very standardized, so it's still kind of very researchy. Um and the second pairing, so even when um individual modalities exist, um for example, cross-modal paired data, where you have like all the modalities you wish kind of paired at the same time, um that is nearly nonexistent. Um"}, {"t": 1320, "text": "and third, the heterogeneity. Um so, sensors um vary a lot across different robot embodiments, which uh makes also transfer uh quite hard. Um so, and given the scarcity of this paired multimodal data, um there's also like an interesting question, um for example, um can we use language as a bridge um to reason across modalities um that we haven't seen uh before together? Uh because we have like kind of um uh disjoint amounts of data, right? Like, for example, we might have large amounts of vision language data, some [clears throat] audio data, uh and maybe like small um data of touch, um um so, but but no data to train all of this like simultaneously, right? At scale. So, um here's some initial work we did at Berkeley um on how you can give uh VLAs uh senses they were never trained on, like touch or audio, um by using language as a semantic uh uh of glue uh"}, {"t": 1381, "text": "to bridge all um sorry, not to bridge, to bind all these um heterogeneous modalities. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, I won't go into the details, but basically um this unlocks um like VLA's new cross-modal reasoning abilities um such as, for example, asking um to grasp the object that feels uh like a pineapple, but is brown, for example. Um and so, here here you can see like a a few different things that then this enables you to do, like multimodal prompting, um cross-modal uh like audio-visual prompting, um or even um uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; generation of object uh object descriptions uh up on interaction. So, for example, um uh on the in this case here, uh this is like kind of the scenario where the robot like grasps into a shopping bag, and it can't see anything with the camera, and it grasps like some random"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "object, and with um based on like the touch uh sensor, then like describes what it thinks it's feeling. Um so, uh yeah. So, similar to what a human could do, right? &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Um This slide uh connects directly with what we covered last week on reasoning with LLMs and VLAs. Um and I want to like kind of maybe touch upon briefly um uh on maybe like a a broader question for um uh for you guys on like when and how should a robot actually reason, right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, because now if you um think about, okay, like um your future robot butler um that is like, you know, assisting you at home, um reasoning will probably be um on like a spectrum um depending on like your policies' confidence, right? So, uh when the task you're um uh tasking your robot is in distribution, right? So, maybe it's like"}, {"t": 1501, "text": "a known object, a known task it already has done before. It's like it has a training data for this. Um, then we probably don't want to reason, right? We just like want to run our policy at the highest frequency as possible. Um, because [clears throat] well, we don't want to like waste computation. Um, but then when you detect that your policy is not completely sure um, about what to do because maybe it's like a new object or uh, maybe the lighting is different or something. Um, that's where test time compute adaptive test time compute becomes valuable, right? Uh, and basically with like the some of the techniques we saw last week we can try to think through like the task at hand by spending more tokens on it at test time uh, to solve it, right? Um, and then on the third column, this is like the"}, {"t": 1562, "text": "very out of distribution when [clears throat] the task is not or the situation is not solvable with test time compute or more thinking or more reasoning um, then the safest thing is to stop and ask a human for help, for example. Um, but this raises like like I think this makes kind of sense. But it raises an immediate question. How does a model actually know what it doesn't know? Because that's the whole like the whole adaptive reasoning framework from like the slide before. Um, so [clears throat] scaling up compute when like the policy is uncertain, escalating when it's out of distribution and so on. Um, actually assumes that we have a reliable way to detect when the model is uncertain in the first place. And it turns out that it's a very big"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "assumption for our current machine learning models. And so this is the fundamentally the problem of introspection. So knowing when a model does not know. And it remains really one of the is really one of the central problems in machine learning. And arguably it becomes even harder as we scale our models up. Because large models are often very good at pattern matching or sorry pattern completion when um while [clears throat] remaining poorly calibrated about their competence. Because usually because they have seen like so much data, right? Um like generally novel situation can still appear superficially familiar making out of distribution detection very very difficult. And in robotics it kind of is even harder because failure can arise along so many"}, {"t": 1683, "text": "different dimensions, right? It could be like objects, environments, embodiments, tasks. Um sensor noise and so forth, right? And so like a policy can be overconfident or appear confident while being wrong for entirely the different reasons, right? Um this connects to another limitation on where the field stands today. Um as as you have seen in the course most of the current robot learning models rely on imitation learning. Um and as we saw earlier in the course this means that um policy is bounded by uh the demonstrations in the data set. Now, um think about like this kind of toy example, right? Uh and uh how like this what this means in practice. So, um"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "for this toy example, this means that no matter how how large your model is, if your training data consists only of demonstrations going from state one to two and from um state uh two to three, then um the policy has no way of learning um that there might be a better path like going from uh one to three. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; or or that there might be even be like a different way of uh solving uh a certain task that is &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh not one that a human operator uh might demonstrate. So, how do we break through um through this imitation? And [clears throat] um as you have probably guessed and al- also we have seen in the lecture before, uh one of the possible answers could be reinforcement learning uh because um as you've seen the reinforcement learning is um uh well suited to to learn near optimal"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "policies from suboptimal data by stitching together trajectories. Um so, in in in this example, like for example with offline RL, it would actually be able to learn that um like there exists a better trajectory uh that was not demonstrated in in the uh data set. And um and we have seen how uh RL is really um fueling a lot of um new capabilities in other areas such as reasoning, agents, uh knowledge discovery, and so forth. Um but scaling reinforcement learning for real uh robots has been very very challenging. Uh and it still requires uh a lot of uh algorithmic uh innovation. So, not just more compute. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, I think really getting um RL to work uh at scale for robotics is is probably one of the highest impact things um that uh you could work on uh right now. Now, figuring out RL for robotics might"}, {"t": 1860, "text": "be also important to achieve, let's say um one of the holy grails of robot learning, which is the data flywheel. Uh like it is one of the kind of main goals of of like the community. Um I don't probably you've already heard this before. Um the idea is pretty simple. Um so, we know from the bitter lesson that more data uh usually translates to better models. Uh and so, better learning and models produce uh like more capable robots and uh more capable robots get deployed more widely. Uh and then, you know, with more deployments, we could generate even more training data. And then, like this cycle like compounds itself, and you get like kind of like more data, better models, and so forth. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Right? Um So, in order to get the flywheel spinning, um kind of like the the first idea um that we could have is, okay, like we can"}, {"t": 1920, "text": "uh co-train uh our models on both like the expert data, let's say um like your teleop data um that we have collected um and uh and then, uh the autonomous robots, so like the data that is being generated um at the deployment. Right? And then, we can just like we have more data, we train on this. Beautiful. Amazing. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; Uh it turns out this is a more challenging problem than one might think from um the outside or from the surface um because if you look at the at these two data distributions, um they look very, very differently. Um Um, so while the expert of data is, um, kind of, uh, optimal, usually higher frequency, like smooth, um, autonomous rollouts will, you know, often be like quite longer in in time. It will take longer to to do the same task often, um, because maybe the policy doesn't always,"}, {"t": 1980, "text": "um, choose the optimal trajectory. Uh, it can also be jerky, uh, and suboptimal. And so, if you like naively mix, um, both of them, let's say with imitation learning, um, that will not work really well. Um, the model will kind of get confused with, um, by the mismatch in kind of the quality, frequency, and the, um, kind of like just in general like the data distribution. Um, and so this is another area where, uh, new algorithms are are needed, um, because we need methods that can, um, learn from all the data, uh, uh, a robot experiences, no matter how good or bad it is. And, uh, kind of extract some useful signal from it. Um, and this [clears throat] is, for example, uh, an area where where reinforcement learning could be well suited to help. But, basically the, um, the goal is really to, uh, never try"}, {"t": 2042, "text": "to never throw away data. Uh, because real-world data is like very, uh, precious and expensive. Um, this is directly related to ano- another broader open problem, uh, which is lifelong learning, right? And so this means that, um, in a nutshell, a robot should improve over time, um, by accumulating and learning, uh, from all the experience and interaction, uh, in the physical world, um, similar to how intelligent, uh, beings uh do. However, um the dominant uh paradigm nowadays is that we train a model, um we dep- uh we freeze its weights, we deploy it, and then ask it to generalize to novel situations um that uh its pre-training data might not have uh prepared it for. And so, um besides the mixing uh and learning from mixed quality uh deployment data, um you know, you also"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "want to make sure um once you start um fine-tuning on on this data, that you don't have catastrophic forgetting, um like forgetting about the model's original capabilities, uh and so forth. And so, this is uh a very exciting problem um that will be a very, very impactful. Um there [clears throat] is also a more immediate version of of this. Um what do you do if your policy fails right now in the middle of deployment? Right? So, instead of like uh re- training your model, &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; you want um to uh your model to have the ability uh for rapid adaptation, uh to recover on the fly. And um imagine you unpack a robot uh at home for the first time, and you um ask it to bring you a Coke from the fridge, right? Um and then maybe it tries, and it struggles to uh grasp the ha- grasp the handle of the fridge to open it. Um maybe in that case, uh just giving it some language feedback uh might be enough. Right? Like you can maybe tell"}, {"t": 2161, "text": "the robot, like, \"Oh, like, you just need to grasp a bit more to the left.\" Um that might that might suffice, right? So, you put that kind of into the context of the model, and then it can like adapt, and and um uh successfully finish the task. Uh but maybe imagine it's more loss than that. Uh maybe it doesn't know how to open the fridge at all. So, the easiest way uh might be for you to actually go there and um do the demonstration yourself uh of opening the fridge and then put that into the context of your robot's model. Also, this is kind of like the vision for in context learning for robotics. So, this means instead of retraining your model every time it encounters something new, you want a model to have the ability for rapid adaptation by doing some sort of like few shot learning from whatever signal is available. Um Now, if you remember a few slides ago, I"}, {"t": 2220, "text": "said in order to improve generalization um robots need the ability to expose themselves to broader and more diverse data that they can learn from. And now, if we think about how mobile manipulation looks like nowadays, we see that it relies a lot on let's say the traditional slam stack to build like maps of the environment, localize themselves, plan the navigation, and so on. And then, only once the robot needs to interact with an object, there is kind of like a hard switch to then like move to a learn policy like a VLA. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; And so, there are several very interesting open research questions here where I think we will hopefully converge to a more principled approach that doesn't involve such a hard switch. Um So, of course, first we would like to have like a more unified model that can do whole body control so"}, {"t": 2282, "text": "that it can handle both the navigation and the manipulation part together. Um And then second, there is a big question mark on what is the best way to represent the environment to allow for long-range navigation in a in a big building, for example. Uh so, do we need like an explicit 3D map of the environment? Maybe a bird's-eye view projection. Uh, maybe a topological map is enough or scene graphs. Or maybe just someone walking through the um office like uh like a walk-through video um with their iPhone and then just put that kind of video into the context uh of the model. Maybe that's enough. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and how will such a model uh like a such a unified model uh then handle state estimation? Like uh will it be like implicit? Uh will it be explicit? Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; and uh if we put some sort of"}, {"t": 2341, "text": "environment uh representation into our model's context, uh how do we handle uh environment changes? Right? Cuz like the environment is not static. It it like continually like uh changes. Um we probably also want to be able to uh have some ability to adapt um to some sort of to some sort of like local navigation without a map. Um so these are all very exciting open questions um that I think are really uh fantastic to to explore right now. And maybe the last one, if we have um such mobile robots interacting with the environment over prolonged periods of time, then they should also of course be able to do um this sort of lifelong uh continual learning. Um and if you think about this, we stumble across uh uh another very interesting question which uh is about memory at scale. Right? Because like a robot generates a continuous streams of multimodal"}, {"t": 2400, "text": "experiences like video, depth, language, touch, audio, bounding boxes, uh etc. All of this is potentially useful, um but the question is like what do you store um and at what time scale, right? Um this can range from uh short horizon memories such as very dense videos, um to longer horizon memory, uh where you probably want to try compressing experience into like more semantic events uh that maybe like helps you also remember uh what you already have done and for like task progress and so on. Um but like then you have to kind of uh think about uh when to write to memory and what to forget, uh which is also kind of a interesting question. Um and then, you know, you can think about this all the way to to years, right? Like um how do you uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; uh how do you like store all of this? How can you retrieve? Uh how do you can you do like um"}, {"t": 2460, "text": "cross modal alignment and so forth? Um So, these are all interesting questions, um and I can share some initial work we have done towards addressing some of these. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; So, VL maps uh fuses VLM embeddings into 3D spatial maps, enabling uh zero-shot spatial navigation uh to open vocabulary targets without any training whatsoever. Uh so, it's really out of the box. Or uh Leela and um is um kind of like learns how to do uh mapless navigation uh entirely from uh videos, like YouTube videos. Um and like uh we showed that the uh the more YouTube data we train on, the better the performance gets uh for uh open vocabulary uh mapless navigation. We also have some preliminary results on how to use foundation models uh to enable autonomous improvement uh without human interventions,"}, {"t": 2520, "text": "uh for example, by um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; by proposing and and scoring um uh tasks based on environment affordances. And um regarding adaptation, also um, a few, for example, on the left, um, PAL is a non-parametric approach um, that allows generalist policies to adapt to new long-horizon tasks with uh, as little as five demonstrations, uh, while achieving um, the same performance um, that the full fine-tuning would uh, achieve uh, for the same model with around 100 demonstrations, if I remember correctly. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; And &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; so, in order to achieve this kind of embody embodied intelligence, um, uh, I think a lot of the problems uh, I just mentioned can be distilled into this this pillars uh, of embodied uh, reasoning, dexterous mobile manipulation, lifelong learning, and rapid adaptation. Um, and so, these are the things that I think that will actually um,"}, {"t": 2581, "text": "uh, that would that it that it uh, that it would I think will actually uh, will needed to be solved um, um, to make robotics work, um, like not just like for for like a demo video, but like in general, um, uh, so, maybe this is uh, my end game for robotics. All right. Um, so, I hope that after this course, um, you all have a better understanding um, about like the tools that are available um, in the modern modern robot learners' researchers' um, toolkit. And um, that if some of these problems excite you, uh, you can start advancing uh, the frontier yourself. Um, but knowing what the open problems uh, are is only like half the um, the battle. Um, the other half is uh, knowing how to do actually research in in robot learning or in general research in general. Um, so, um,"}, {"t": 2640, "text": "before finishing, I would like to share a few things um, that I have learned uh, about how to do research, uh, sometimes the hard way, um, that I think um, might be useful also for you. So, to start with some uncomfortable facts of research. First, most of the research is incremental, and that's not like a criticism. That's usually how science works. An analogy, for example, like today's self-driving cars like rest on around 40 years of of like research and accumulated work, right? From like the Alvin car in in 1986 to the DARPA challenge to end-to-end modern learning. Like nobody solved it in a single paper. Right? Second thing, most research ideas never"}, {"t": 2702, "text": "become papers. So, you might have like 10 ideas. Maybe you run like five experiments and get two interesting results. And then maybe you publish one paper. And that that would be like a good ratio, I think. But like the like the the fact is that most of the ideas you have like usually don't work, right? And that's like not like a failure, but that's kind of the process that how it goes. And also, the third point is that most of the papers don't stand the test of time. So, the field moves really fast and you know, something that might feel like a breakthrough today might be considered like a footnote in in 2 years, right? Um, and the fourth point I want to make is that So, in in in a world where where scale is important, simple ideas tend to have more impact"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "because they can be scaled more easily. Um so this means that if your idea requires um 17 different moving parts to work, um then maybe try to ask yourself if um there is a simpler version uh that gets you 80% uh of the way there. So how do you find a good research problem? Uh what what is like a good recipe for this? Um I think it it's it's simple and I will um um briefly go over it. Um so uh first you need like an important problem and then second you need a plan for how to tackle it. Uh you need like both, like the problem and the plan. Um just having one of one of the two doesn't get you all the way there, right? Um like an example, like you could say uh you know, let's cure cancer. Like that's a really important problem, right? Uh sounds very impactful. Um but in this case if you don't know how, then you know, maybe it's not the best idea to start working on that, right? Uh"}, {"t": 2823, "text": "conversely, let's say you have a new algorithm um that improves 1% on the liberal benchmark, which is a very narrow and saturated simulated robot benchmark, um that does not really like move the needle on anything that matters. Um so the sweet spot is uh to try um to think of like a a problem that is important and where you have like some angle of attack or like some plan on like how you want to like address this. Um and then maybe the the last point here is uh it's also good to to think about um you know, if your plan works exactly as you uh as you have thought and you're like successful, um how will this look like? Um what [clears throat] will it like unlock? Uh how does it help the community? Um like will something be now possible that wasn't um possible before. These are good things to uh think about."}, {"t": 2881, "text": "Um the next ingredient you need and honestly probably the most important one is excitement because a research takes a lot of time and effort and you know experiments don't work. There are deadlines. Sometimes paper rejections. Um and so you know it can be painful sometimes um and what will probably make you more likely to succeed is if you're also excited about the problem because if you kind of like find the problem interesting and exciting then the hard parts of getting there like feel worth it if that makes sense. Um and if if if if you're not excited then it just feels like work. Um All right. The fourth there is a fourth ingredient that I think is also quite crucial for making"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "being successful in research and you know hopefully you attack some of the problems we just mentioned before and this is trying to be your own reviewer number two. So you know when like you submit like a a paper to a conference you know this is the reviewer that's like very critical of your work. Right? And so what I mean by this is that um before you go on and embark in a long like you know multi-month like research journey of a a particular problem you should try to ask yourselves what is the most likely reason that your idea might fail. Right? And now if you can answer that honestly and you still believe that the idea is worth worth pursuing um then then you're good then go ahead and and proceed. Um and why I'm I'm saying you should try to do this is because this is um"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "a tool or a basically a way you have to protect your most valuable research resource which is your time. Right? &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; In general, there are two different styles of research and it's worth knowing which one you're which one you're approaching. Um there is the method-driven research which starts usually with an idea or a technique and then you kind of need to find the right problem for it in order to have an impact. Um today it could be something like oh there you know a new video diffusion model just was released. Uh let me try to apply it for robot manipulation uh somehow like I don't really know like what exactly what problem or how but like it's like a kind of like a cool new or shiny model I want to like use it for for something. Uh problem-driven research starts the other way around like you start with a concrete problem. Uh let's say uh for example how can I"}, {"t": 3062, "text": "make VLA generalized to a novel camera viewpoint? And then you go and find or invent you know a method that solves it. Um and so the problem is kind of your anchor and your method is like whatever like solves your problem best. Both styles are can produce uh great research. Um for a PhD I probably would recommend problem-driven is safer uh because if the method doesn't pan out then you can try another one and you will still be trying to solve an impactful problem. Um but if you never had like a impactful like a real problem to begin with if your method fails in the method-driven research approach then you don't have anything to fall back on. Um Okay, but how do we actually do research? Um here are some loose tips. I always recommend starting with something that you know that absolutely"}, {"t": 3120, "text": "should absolutely work and then go make make it incrementally harder. Right? So maybe start with some over fitting experiment before you try solving all the problems at once. Uh and also always make sure to visualize both your inputs and outputs to your models. It's really really important. Um &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; because if you start at full complexity and your experiment fails, then you have no idea what the problem is. So you need to know which parts work incrementally in order to push the boundary. And uh I also recommend always talking to people about your idea, colleagues or authors of papers you're building upon. Uh this can really save you weeks of effort and work. Um and once you have results, share them. So share your findings with the community, the people who care about the same problems as you do. Might be like your next collaborators, for"}, {"t": 3180, "text": "example. Try open sourcing your your data, your code as much as possible. This is the most direct way for your research to be useful for others other than yourself. And also try to invest some time in polishing your figures. Ideally, someone should be able to look at your figures and immediately understand what's going on without like reading the whole paper or captions. If they have to make an effort to understand your figures, then they will move on and not bother like reading like your paper or your blog or whatever. Um and when sharing your research, be also be mindful of your audience. Um so, in doubt, don't assume that your audience already knows all the same foundations as you do. Um And of course, one more thing on sharing, if nobody knows about your research, then it basically didn't happen. Uh so, a great"}, {"t": 3240, "text": "paper that nobody has read has like zero impact. So, like try to disseminate your findings, put your work on archive, post about it on social media, etc. Um but, you know, remember like I think this brings us kind of to the beginning of the of today's talk. Uh you know, try to find the line between like making people curious about your work uh and you know, not over hyping it by saying you have solved robotics uh or like in Brad Adcock's case, you know, where 2 years later after the tweet, we are still waiting for his chat GPT moment in robotics, right? Um I want to end today with a personal story that I think maybe illustrates a little bit some of the things that I just tried to um uh &gt;&gt; [clears throat] &gt;&gt; give you some suggestions on how to do like research, how to tackle them. Um so, early in my PhD, I was kind of drowning in the classic"}, {"t": 3301, "text": "robotics pipeline. Uh I had a thousand ROS nodes, disjoint models for perception, planning, and control that were accumulating errors across all this all this super complex pipeline. Um Not only that, like a week before a major deadline, the robot broke. Uh it wouldn't finish self-calibrating at startup, so it wouldn't allow me to run any of my code. It was a bad time. Um and as any good computer scientist, you know, like any decent computer scientist that you know, uh that would ask you in a such a situation, what do you do? Like you try turning it off and on again, right? So, that's what I did for actually a few days until um it somehow finished self-calibrating like I think the night of the deadline or something. Uh and then I had to pull an all-nighter"}, {"t": 3360, "text": "to run all my experiments for the deadline. And so, this traumatic very traumatizing experience turned into a bigger question for me. Uh can I ditch all of this? Can I like forget about all this Ross notes, all this super complex pipeline with a thousand models uh and just like accumulating all the errors um and do end-to-end learning? Um So, really uh like because of this experience, I asked myself, what would it take to have like a cool end of PhD demo where instead of all this Ross notes, I have like one model that can do like multiple manipulation tasks like end-to-end um that can be like commanded and instructed with language. Um and so, I wrote actually a proposal with some very loose ideas that got accepted and funded me for a few years. I then convinced my PhD advisor um uh to buy me a Franka robot. I'm very"}, {"t": 3421, "text": "grateful um for his help with this. And then I went on and and spent like 1 year setting this this robot up from from zero with like custom heavy like a metal table with holes um I wrote writing like the VR teleop code, mounting cameras, 3D 3D printing the mounts for the wrist camera, figuring out how to make the low-level controller smooth and whatnot. All of this was like a lot a lot of work. Uh so, I spent a year on this. Um and then once I had a working setup, I started doing a bit more research. Once I started doing research uh with it, I quickly realized that iterating in the real world would take me forever, but that I I wanted to graduate at some point. So, I started working on a simulation environment that used basically the same stack for teleop um as you can see on the left video with like the VR controller and so forth."}, {"t": 3480, "text": "And after spending some time on making the simulation nice, I realized that actually there wasn't any simulation like benchmark or dataset that allowed training like these long horizon language condition policies um uh that required some degree of generalization. So, I thought like well, maybe I can like try to polish a bit my sim and maybe like I'll make like a small paper out of it or something in case it's useful for others. And so, this kind of like byproduct or even like afterthought that I had become then the Calvin benchmark um which to this day is still one of my highest cited papers and also won a best award. Um and building all this infra to set up like visual motor control policies uh was hard work. Especially in a lab that primarily was focused on doing slam. Um but then this allowed me to iterate very quickly um on the problem that I actually wanted to solve."}, {"t": 3541, "text": "Um it took me about 5 years with this setup um to get to my cool end of PhD demo that wasn't and running any ROS and could do like over 25 like different manipulation tasks uh all grounded in language and generalize, you know, to unseen combinations of them and so forth. Um so, I was very happy. Um Oh, okay, we are at time. So, um so, this was my uh last lecture of the course. Uh, because next week we will have um, guest speakers only. Um, so I [clears throat] wanted to take a uh, brief um, a brief moment to say thank you. Um, uh, for all your patience because um, this being a new course uh, it has taken a lot of effort to put together. I really felt like um, we have been flying a plane while while uh, building it at the same time. And uh, also a lot of thanks for like the feedback you gave like uh, on the midterm. That was really useful um, and hopefully made the course better."}, {"t": 3601, "text": "Um, this was the first time I had uh, I have teach a full course um, and the first one where I had to like also design from scratch all the materials. Uh, and so although juggling also two full-time jobs uh, at the same time cuz I'm not a professor. I work at industry. I can say that teaching this course has been an absolute privilege for me. Um, and [clears throat] like seeing the students excited about uh, how their policies are training like uh, scheming like to share data across different teams uh, or asking our guest speakers insightful questions. I think that makes me like really really happy. And um, the goal of this course is really beyond teaching you like the content and the basics uh, really to inspire you that robot learning um, is a cool field to work on uh, and I hope that we were able to convey this. Um, and with this uh, I'm also I'm really looking forward to seeing um, all"}, {"t": 3660, "text": "your projects uh, with the real robots on the demo day. I think that's that's going to be a lot of fun. I can't wait for it. And uh, before we finish uh, and I pass on to Lucas who hopefully somewhere here. Um, uh, I there is one more group I want to thank um, I want to acknowledge um, the teaching assistants. Uh, because like this really would have not been possible uh, without them. So designing a new course from scratch while working two full-time jobs is hard, but the exercises, the project supervision, the robot setups, the TA session, that was all of them. So I would ask the TAs to stand up. Please stand up and give let's give them a big applause. &gt;&gt; [applause] [applause] &gt;&gt; Awesome. And with this we are done."}, {"t": 3721, "text": "&gt;&gt; [applause]"}], "toc": [{"t": 120, "title": "Slide 1: Every Week on Twitter: Robotics is Solved!", "slide": "slide-1-every-week-on-twitter-robotics-is-solved-frame-2"}, {"t": 120, "title": "Slide 2: How Autonomous Robots (Usually) Generalize", "slide": "slide-2-how-autonomous-robots-usually-generalize-frame-3"}, {"t": 302, "title": "Slide 4: [untitled: scales of generalization]", "slide": "slide-4-untitled-scales-of-generalization-frame-4"}, {"t": 420, "title": "Slide 5: The Quest for the Best of Both Worlds", "slide": "slide-5-the-quest-for-the-best-of-both-worlds-frame-5"}, {"t": 420, "title": "Slide 6: The Path to Embodied Intelligence", "slide": "slide-6-the-path-to-embodied-intelligence-frames-6-8"}, {"t": 543, "title": "Slide 7: What is the Best Backbone for Robotics?", "slide": "slide-7-what-is-the-best-backbone-for-robotics-frame-9"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Slide 8: What is the Best Data Recipe?", "slide": "slide-8-what-is-the-best-data-recipe-frame-10"}, {"t": 660, "title": "Slide 9: What is the Best Data Recipe? [video and robot-only pyramids]", "slide": "slide-9-what-is-the-best-data-recipe-video-and-robot-only-pyramids-frame-11"}, {"t": 841, "title": "Slide 10: Data Collection Interfaces", "slide": "slide-10-data-collection-interfaces-frame-12"}, {"t": 841, "title": "Slide 11: Data Scalability vs Hardware Alignment", "slide": "slide-11-data-scalability-vs-hardware-alignment-frame-13"}, {"t": 1021, "title": "Slide 12: Towards Dexterous Generalist Policies", "slide": "slide-12-towards-dexterous-generalist-policies-frame-14"}, {"t": 1140, "title": "Slide 13: Dexterity Requires More Than Vision", "slide": "slide-13-dexterity-requires-more-than-vision-frame-15"}, {"t": 1140, "title": "Slide 14: Recap: Native Multimodal Models", "slide": "slide-14-recap-native-multimodal-models-frame-16"}, {"t": 1260, "title": "Slide 15: The Long Tail of Robot Sensing", "slide": "slide-15-the-long-tail-of-robot-sensing-frame-17"}, {"t": 1260, "title": "Slide 16: Reason Across New Modalities?", "slide": "slide-16-reason-across-new-modalities-frame-18"}, {"t": 1320, "title": "Slide 17: Giving VLAs Senses They Were Never Trained On", "slide": "slide-17-giving-vlas-senses-they-were-never-trained-on-frame-19"}, {"t": 1501, "title": "Slide 19: When & How to Reason Intelligently?", "slide": "slide-19-when-how-to-reason-intelligently-frame-20"}, {"t": 1621, "title": "Slide 20: [untitled: introspection]", "slide": "slide-20-untitled-introspection-frame-21"}, {"t": 1683, "title": "Slide 21: Current Robot Models Rely on Imitation Learning", "slide": "slide-21-current-robot-models-rely-on-imitation-learning-frame-22"}, {"t": 1741, "title": "Slide 22: Can we Scale RL for Robotics?", "slide": "slide-22-can-we-scale-rl-for-robotics-frame-23"}, {"t": 1801, "title": "Slide 23: Robot Learning Data Flywheel", "slide": "slide-23-robot-learning-data-flywheel-frame-24"}, {"t": 1920, "title": "Slide 24: Co-Training Expert & Autonomous Data", "slide": "slide-24-co-training-expert-autonomous-data-frame-25"}, {"t": 1980, "title": "Slide 25: Lifelong Learning", "slide": "slide-25-lifelong-learning-frame-26"}, {"t": 2101, "title": "Slide 26: Rapid Adaptation: In-Context Learning", "slide": "slide-26-rapid-adaptation-in-context-learning-frame-27"}, {"t": 2220, "title": "Slide 27: Towards a Unified Model for Mobile Manipulation", "slide": "slide-27-towards-a-unified-model-for-mobile-manipulation-frame-28"}, {"t": 2341, "title": "Slide 28: Multimodal Memories Across Time & Space", "slide": "slide-28-multimodal-memories-across-time-space-frame-29"}, {"t": 2400, "title": "Slide 30: First Steps: Autonomous Improvement", "slide": "slide-30-first-steps-autonomous-improvement-frame-30"}, {"t": 2460, "title": "Slide 31: First Steps: Adaptation via Human Feedback", "slide": "slide-31-first-steps-adaptation-via-human-feedback-frame-31"}, {"t": 2520, "title": "Slide 32: Ingredients for Embodied Intelligence", "slide": "slide-32-ingredients-for-embodied-intelligence-frame-32"}, {"t": 2581, "title": "Slide 33: [untitled: transition]", "slide": "slide-33-untitled-transition-frame-33"}, {"t": 2581, "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "slide": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-34"}, {"t": 2640, "title": "Slide 35: The (Harsh) Reality of Research", "slide": "slide-35-the-harsh-reality-of-research-frame-35"}, {"t": 2881, "title": "Slide 36: The Recipe for Good Research Problems", "slide": "slide-36-the-recipe-for-good-research-problems-frames-36-38"}, {"t": 3001, "title": "Slide 39: Styles of Research", "slide": "slide-39-styles-of-research-frame-39"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 40: Debugging Your Research", "slide": "slide-40-debugging-your-research-frame-40"}, {"t": 3120, "title": "Slide 41: Share Your Research", "slide": "slide-41-share-your-research-frame-41"}, {"t": 3180, "title": "Slide 42: Share Your Research [social media]", "slide": "slide-42-share-your-research-social-media-frame-42"}, {"t": 3301, "title": "Slide 43: Personal Backstory", "slide": "slide-43-personal-backstory-frame-43"}, {"t": 3360, "title": "Slide 44: Personal Backstory [continued]", "slide": "slide-44-personal-backstory-continued-frame-44"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "[untitled, no slide number: closing message]", "slide": "untitled-no-slide-number-closing-message-frame-45"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 48: Huge Thanks to the Teaching Assistants", "slide": "slide-48-huge-thanks-to-the-teaching-assistants-frame-46"}, {"t": 3601, "title": "Slide 49: References", "slide": "slide-49-references-frame-47"}], "toc_source": "estimated from slides (approx.)"}, "guests": [{"id": "0XB7fNS_ONg", "title": "Lucas Beyer: Vision in the Age of LLMs [ETHZ Robot Learning 2026]", "speaker": "Lucas Beyer", "duration": "1:09:04", "description": "In this guest lecture for the ETH Zurich course \"Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models\" (Spring 2026), hosted and led by Oier Mees, Lucas Beyer (Meta Superinteligence Labs), talks about the frontier in multimodal foundation model training, ranging from pre-training to post-training.\nCourse Website: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/", "chapters": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction"}, {"t": 104, "title": "Vision and pre-training"}, {"t": 2060, "title": "Mid-training concepts"}, {"t": 2085, "title": "PaliGemma architecture"}, {"t": 2208, "title": "Training mixtures"}, {"t": 2345, "title": "Scaling and conversion"}, {"t": 2511, "title": "Perception and final recipe"}, {"t": 2727, "title": "Q&A and closing remarks"}], "paragraphs": [{"t": 0, "text": "So, it's a real pleasure today um uh to have uh Lucas Byer um have a give a guest lecture um to finish uh today's uh uh uh lecture on on on the course. Um Lucas uh grew up in Belgium uh wanting to make uh video games on their AI. Uh he went on to study mechanical engineering um uh in Aken in Germany where he also did a PhD in robotic perception and computer vision. Um I actually saw you also work on like the Spencer robot, right? Like Yep. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So &gt;&gt; and I also agree that to Russ is terrible. &gt;&gt; Yeah. We also wanted to ditch it. &gt;&gt; Yeah. So I know Okay. So I think that I guess that's the difference like he got traumatized by by robotics and Ross and then decided to pursue a career in vision while I went to do like robot learning like with end to end learning. Um anyways um yeah then he later um"}, {"t": 60, "text": "became part of Google brain um here in Zurich uh working on a multimodel uh team uh where he's done a lot of impactful uh work like the uh vision transformers uh we covered uh earlier in the lecture u or also like uh um like the cy cichlid models um and polygeemma um that drive a lot of the VAS uh nowadays or in the last few years um and Uh yeah, now he's at at Meta where he continues uh working um on doing research on multimodel AI. Um and uh yeah, with this uh let's uh thank him for being here and giving a big applause. Yeah. Hello and thanks for having me. Um good evening. So this lecture I will mostly talk about the the part that comes before. This is probably going to be the least robotics lecture in this"}, {"t": 120, "text": "robot learning course. Um but I think you've heard a lot in the past uh lectures about VAS. Um and a lot of them actually are based on uh or all of them are based on VLMs, vision language models. And a lot of them are actually based on a specific vision language model polymer uh which is open source model which we released a couple years ago. Uh and here I want to mostly trace you through the history of like when computer vision didn't work at all all the way to like the latest uh update which was polyma. So I want to tell you and explain to you how things work before you turn them into VAS uh and make them work on robotics. Um and I think over the last like I don't know 6 7 8 years we have made gigantic progress uh in vision uh in general and uh one example for this"}, {"t": 181, "text": "it's not quite vision but it's a nice example here you see the p uh you kind of see the background picture you kind of can guess this is Zurich right this was actually generated by older version of 03 imagen like precursor of current image generation chpt Um, I think I generated this sometime last summer and five, six years ago. This is actually who knows this picture. This is what we were super excited about. This was like the best generation from a model by far. It was a huge jump. We call it dog ball. Uh, and yeah, like we went from this five, six years ago to like this background just a year ago, which is insane. Um anyways uh actually as you just heard in the past I did robotics or I always did perception side of robotics and my personal long-term goal is still uh to that there exist hopefully before I retire uh a robot"}, {"t": 240, "text": "that any nontechnical person can teach to do basically anything uh they want and nontechnical is important part here like without programming please um that way it's useful to as many people as possible right uh And like maybe 8ish years ago in my opinion what was needed to get there uh was two things. uh one is a very robust and general perception such that you can teach it to do something like let's say to put the glass in your dishwasher with you show it with one glass or you teach it with one glass and it generalizes to all glasses for example in all dishwashers right uh and the other thing is that I thought was learning from a few examples you show it the thing a few times no matter how like tele op video whatever um and then it learns from that I think now I revised this a bit because uh language models happened and are really Good. So maybe we can even teach it from a few instructions uh only. But I think still now robust general perception is a key thing. Well and 8 years ago when I"}, {"t": 302, "text": "started this journey vision didn't work. Uh and so there was a lot of work to do and actually learning from examples was on pretty good track. So I went on to work on vision. Um and one example of a very good uh general robust vision system is the humans uh visual representation that we have in our heads. Uh to show you what I mean by this is here I can show all of you guys this I tell you okay this is class A. These five examples this is class B and this is class C. And I show you a new picture and by now you already know is it class A? Yeah class B. I shouldn't have said yeah class C. Uh yeah, but this is easy, right? But you also have seen millions of flowers in your life or hopefully otherwise it's a sad life. Um and another one that you have seen less examples of in your lifetime but probably still a few is like satellite pictures. Let's say these are all pictures of class A. These are all pictures of class B. These are all"}, {"t": 360, "text": "pictures of class C. What class is this? A B C. Very good. Uh you learn very quickly from just a few examples, right? But maybe you say okay you all have flown maybe or seen this on TV or whatever. What about this? I hope that maybe half of you have never seen this picture. Um here uh this is class A. This is class B. Uh and I show you this example. This may require you a little bit of thinking. 3 2 1. Who says class A? Okay. Who says class B? Ah almost all of you have very good visual representation. It's class A. Just three things. no matter what. Uh but this was slightly trickier but still like even if you have never seen this thing before like you were able to quickly learn right uh this is kind of what uh I mean and in our team back then we settled to kind of try to learn some things some model that is as good as this as you guys at this"}, {"t": 421, "text": "generalizing perception. So to make progress towards that we need to measure that and so first thing that we did ish set up a benchmark which we call visual task adaptation benchmark. Um so it's basically the setting that you've just seen. Um where we say okay do whatever with whatever data to get uh your model that you want to test whether it is a good general visual representation. Then we come here from this abstract landscape of all kind of reasonable perception tasks and we sample a bunch of tasks from all over this which just means like get a long list of very diverse tasks. Um and for each of these tasks we give you a few examples to train on uh or to learn from. Uh and then we have test examples and you basically need to come with some adaptation algorithm whatever you want. For example, fine-tuning as the most classical thing about you anything uh and basically learn for each"}, {"t": 480, "text": "task individually learn from the few examples and perform as well as possible on that task and then you get a score per task average of this and this is the score and we kind of want to make this like we do research like the the next few years are all research on doing this how to do this such that we get as good as possible score across the broad category of tasks and that way hopefully we and here climbed towards a general visual representation. Uh and then that's kind of what we did. Um and I would say that nowadays we are actually there. Uh we have this and the general recipe. I think most of you know this by now. It's like pre-train, mid train, fine-tune, RL tune, done. Uh and this is also the outline of the rest of the talkish. So I'm going to talk about pre-train, mid-train, finetune. And I'm probably going to skip RL uh tuning so you can not be late for dinner. Um so pre-training I think actually I"}, {"t": 540, "text": "yeah I don't even know how to ask. I think everybody is convinced now that you should just scale things right. Um but back then it was not clear at all and nobody was scaling things and it took a while for the whole community to get there. Um like in Five, six, seven, eight years ago, all the rage to try to learn general visual representations was unsupervised or self-supervised pre-training. So just learn on images or videos without annotations and just come up with some task for example reconstruction or future prediction in videos or whatever um that you can have as kind of a pre-text or pre-training task um and then learn your representation that way. hopefully it will be very general because it's not biased by humans like annotating this or creating task. Um and there were a lot of self-supervised uh methods. Um so we basically reimplemented a large subset of them and to see like which one is the best, which"}, {"t": 600, "text": "one generalizes best according to like this in this setting, right? Um and then we kind of found out that none of them is consistently the best. But in each of them making the model larger uh which is kind of what these tables show or these uh bars show like making the model larger is always better. Like these are different um methods and then within the method the bars from left to right are larger and larger models. Um and so this was kind of not what we hoped but it was kind of oh interesting of the tasks themselves like the ways to pre-train themselves are kind of all over the place but for each one making model larger larger larger seems to be better better uh but still like the self-supervised uh learning is kind of a mess. Let's ignore this part here and let's focus on that part and this is a problem that I think also robotics in general uh struggles with. Uh basically the there is almost no correlation between your pre-training task how good"}, {"t": 661, "text": "your model performs on the pre-training task versus your downstream task performance of the model like how well it performs on the thing you care about after the transfer. Um so here on these plots each of these bubbles are different methods to pre-training uh and different models uh that were pre-trained. And on the x- axis to the left, it means better at pre-training. And on the y-axis to the top, it means better downstream. And you can just see that the bubbles are all over the place. There's no clear trend. Uh except for like same color bubbles, they get to the top and right, which means keep getting better at both things, which same color means exact same setting, just make model larger. So again like making model larger seems to be kind of the only thing that is meaningful for pre-training here. Um and however this is and this I think is also maybe an insight that should translate to robotics. Um if given it is"}, {"t": 722, "text": "such a mess and no very strong correlation across methods from pre-training to downstream. Um so what do you do at the end of the day? You pre-train a bunch of models and you need to choose one that you actually use and deploy, right? Uh so you actually always in order to choose one need to somehow evaluate multiple of them and to evaluate you need to have some examples like either you have your validation examples or if you don't you sit down you look at what the robot or the model does and say good bad and while doing so you just write it down and boom you have your examples. Uh so at the end of the day you always have examples in reality. Uh and so with this in mind, we then thought, okay, then why not take some of these examples, just a few, and throw them into the pre-training and see if that makes the correlation better. And that's kind of what we did afterwards, which is just take any of these self-supervised label free pre-training methods and throw a few very few labelled examples into the pre-training. Um and then everything became so much"}, {"t": 781, "text": "better. Um, blue curve is without this uh like how good is it on downstream like training the this adaptation uh doing the transfer to a downstream task uh over time and like it really struggles to get anywhere good but the orange curve which is the model which was pre-trained with a few examples in the pre-training is just immediately so much better easier to transfer and so on right uh so this basically made us completely discard uh self-s supervisor unsupervised pre-training and decide okay uh this is it we need supervised pre-training uh and we need to scale things up and then this is basically I think this is one of my my second favorite plot in my career um here this is now going to fully supervised pre-training so just try to find however whatever a large data set with a lot of examples uh that are annotated like which in this case it was classification pre-training so image and"}, {"t": 841, "text": "class label for it like I don't know picture of this and the class is laptop um and here on the x-axis left to right is a bigger and bigger model on the y-axis is just how good is the model with some metric in this case it was imagine a top five um in few shot setting I think um but it doesn't matter higher is better uh and all of these pink crosses were all kinds of different uh recent papers back then which were all about scaling up models and kind of what I want to say here is like sure they get a little bit better but it's not really anything to call home about like going from 50 to 55 okay but it's not worth the making the model gigantically larger which makes everything more complicated more costly slower um and we kind of reproduce this with this orange curve which is making a reset larger and larger and larger uh pre-training on imageet which is a data"}, {"t": 900, "text": "set of a million images. Uh however, so it's kind of it's getting better but very slowly as you make the model much much larger. This is insane size actually back then. Um however going to this green curve and then to the blue blue curve the difference is larger and larger pre-training data and this is like uh I think 15 times larger this jump and this is another uh 20 times larger I think. Um, so overall couple of orders of magnitude more pre-training data and this changes everything like this dot here large model on the largest data is just so much better than everything scaled up and published before. Um and even more encouraging than this is there was a eval data set which was called objectnet which they take uh imageet classes and just put the objects into very weird scenarios like chair is a class and then they just take pictures of chairs in"}, {"t": 960, "text": "very weird locations orientations whatnot like here is a chair on the floor in the bathroom for example. So this is supposed to test really generalization to completely out there images. The first image is always what it looks like in imageet typically it's very canonical and then here in object net it's like what um and when we then eval these models there we see the exact same trend like here it's same scenario but evaluated on this very generalization out of distribution testing um and okay I swapped the colors of the lines I was sorry for that but other than that like it's same thing there is this is really really good gener ization when you start training large models on large data. Um so great that that was basically one one breakthrough that was like okay we should do large scale pre-training. Um then maybe now it's kind of obvious and"}, {"t": 1021, "text": "you're like yeah what took you so long to figure this out. Um just want to show a few reasons why it took the the whole community a while. This happened in parallel to it happening in the language also. This was roughly the times around GPT1 GPD2. Um and the same issues were there and in vision. Uh one thing is that it just requires insane patience uh insane compute and also a lot of attention to detail. Uh here is one way to show some part of this. The red dot is what everybody used to train on uh ResNet 50, a smallish medium model on imageet like 1 million example data set which was large back then. If you then just take this recipe and train it on 10 times larger data, you get worse results. This blue point uh and so this was there was actually I'm not sure papers but at least several blog posts"}, {"t": 1080, "text": "about this like as negative result. So people more or less concluded yeah maybe 1 million images is kind of enough to pre-train and if we do 10 times more things don't get better. Um so what it and this is true like we reproduce this. Uh but then if you also train like 10 times longer and another 10 times longer then things start getting better. So you actually need um are three things like you need to make the model larger and the data larger and the training duration longer and each of these orders of magnitude more otherwise you don't see the effect of like large scale pre-training being nice. If you do only two of them you see much less of an effect. If you only do one of them you basically don't see an effect. Uh this is what kind of made made it take a while to get to that point. Uh previously was my number two favorite plot. This is my number one favorite plot. Um, this was one of our training curves. Uh, where you see, don't mind this staircase thing. This was common"}, {"t": 1141, "text": "back then. Um, but gen like here, if you look at this, uh, um, x-axis is training time and y-axis is performance. And if you just zoom into this little box, it looks like this. And this was 8 GPU weeks of compute. So like if you were training on a GPUs for a week and kind of looks flat any reasonable person would say yeah okay let's stop this job use the GPUs for something else. Uh good thing we were not reasonable. If you zoom out to this box which is 8 GPU months you can clearly see no things are getting better a lot right? So just keep training. So that's the the thing about it needing a lot of patience. Uh and finally there is like I I say it as if it was easy and so on. uh but there is actually a lot of details to get right and a lot of them are not obvious at smaller scale. Here is uh one example with weight decay which is one of many training parameters um and just two different values for this parameter. If you just test them and have like two"}, {"t": 1202, "text": "preliminary runs and then you kill the one that does not look promising, you would kill the blue one even though it ends up outperforming the orange one at the end of training, right? Um so this needs basically a lot of experience of fiddling with these details uh to also work well. Um all right but then like this is convincing. Okay yeah we should pre-train on lots of data large models lots of patients large models lots of patients we can do but lots of data is a bit more difficult to get. Um and uh typically all kind of pre-training data up until then look kind of like this like this grid. Somebody comes up with a list of classes and then for each class somehow tries to get a lot of pictures uh usually by crawling web or image search engines or things like that. Actually one uh anecdote that I like a lot is envision this data set MS Coco actually let me put some numbers in"}, {"t": 1260, "text": "here. First number is number of images. Second number of classes. Uh this MS Koko is super widely used for over a decade in computer vision. Uh this data set it has 80 classes. Um does anybody by chance know where the classes come from? Yeah. Um so it's actually um the the senior professor on this project in the US asked his uh eight or nine or 10 yearear-old kid like what are things that you can think of and the kid like being an American kid was like microwave, pizza, frisbee, baseball and so these are the 80 classes and these are the things that computer vision community trained on like all uh All detectors that you know are largely trained on this data set and so really good do typical 8 to 10 year old North American things. Um and then that's it. Um and yeah but but so always you had to"}, {"t": 1323, "text": "come up with a list of classes and then sure there were more and more projects trying to come up with more and more list of classes and collecting data like public imageet inside Google inside meta. Um but in the end of the day always bound by the creativity of the people that come up with classes. Uh and really the other approach that kind of liberates us from this if we can figure out how to train on this is if we just flip this around and instead of listing classes finding images for them we list images. Like we just go through the web list all of images and then don't have classes for them. But what can we have for them on the web? An image is usually somehow surrounded by a text or like some text attached to it. Uh the most canonical example is add text in the image tag. Um and then you do get some garbage like this. This doesn't teach you anything. Thumbnail for version blah blah blah. Um but you also get stuff like this. Uh these are real examples by the way. Uh Frankfurt airport skyline."}, {"t": 1381, "text": "So nobody ever creating a data set would think of making this a class Frankfurt airport skyline, right? But here you have it. So you have the chance to learn from it if you figure out how. Um, and this is a huge breakthrough that clip did. But I actually want to ask who knows how clip training works. Oh, very few I or like halfish. I thought I could skip this, but I won't because this is important. I think this was like one gigantic breakthrough. Um, and it just enabled so many nice things. Um, so how it works, you have these image and free form text uh, pairs. You just take a whole bunch of them, let's say 30,000, put them in a mini batch. All of the images, you send them through an image encoder and you get out per image a vector of let's say thousand dimensional vector. You do the same for all of the texts that correspond to"}, {"t": 1440, "text": "these images. So your 32,000 texts, you send them through a text encoder and you get a vector out of it. Uh and now your training task is in the uh original clip formulation. It is for each like for each text you have the one image that it corresponds to, right? Uh let's say this one and then you have diagonal of ones and the rest of zeros like they don't correspond. So you can do classification task for this text classify which of these image vectors corresponds to it, right? And vice versa for this image vector classify which of these text vectors correspond to it. Right? And these are vectors. So classify is just dot product and then softmax and then train this. And this way you train image encoder and the text encoder that kind of need to learn what text describes what image and kind of match these things. Um later we made the cigip which is a simplification is just binary. So same setup same you build this matrix of text and images and then"}, {"t": 1500, "text": "just for each pair you say yes or no do they correspond? Uh so binary classification but it's again like just dotproduct of these two vectors and then sigmoid target 01 and that's it. Um and with this this like opens us to learn from this complete free form data and to learn all the concepts we can find on the web or we can find images for on the web. Um I'm going to skip on how to use this because it doesn't matter for the rest and it works extremely well. You just trust me. Um so I for me this was like a complete game changer and it was like yeah now we can actually learn everything not just these 80 classes from North American kitties. Um but then what people started doing with clip and I'm not going to name names so I censored the method name here but this is a very good representative um is that they started from large image"}, {"t": 1560, "text": "text random data and then showed look if we filter it down to this to that to that we get better and better and better performance uh and if you look at it what was this filtering well first filtering let's filter it to all the words that appear in English Wikipedia version Uh another thing Lion is one of these uh open versions of these large data sets. The full one has 5 billion images but then all papers use two billion version and 2 billion version is the English only version of it. Um and then it gets better like original clip model which was released is then often used to filter image text pairs like if original clip model um matches them gives them a high matching score then it's probably not random noise but something predictable right? So original clip model would give this a low score but this a high score if it knows about it. So use this to filter it and original clip model was trained on English only. Um and it gets worse like then they start doing stuff like okay let's only keep those images which kind"}, {"t": 1621, "text": "of resemble the images from the imageet data set. Uh and then yeah and the best model is the one that has all of these huristics for filtering. best model uh measured how measured on imageet well yay uh but then by doing so we're going hmageet in case you don't know is also very North American biased in what it has so by doing so we kind of I really didn't like this because we uh we now just had this capability of learning from everything or learning all the concepts we can find on the web but we kind of throw them in the garbage by just filtering to English only imagenet like stuff um and So we did a project to analyze this in detail and in like try to influence everybody who does image pre-training away from this filtering uh such that we get better general models. So we did an experiment uh where we pre pre-trained such a clip or cig model uh on pre-training data filter to English only versus keeping everything and a"}, {"t": 1681, "text": "very controlled experiment otherwise. Um and then the English only uh model given this picture would say this is the can tower in Toronto when in reality it's Mil tower in Thran and the model trained on without this English filter would say it correctly and same for this cathedral which the English only model would say is one in Montreal, Canada when in reality it's one in Brazil which also again the not trained on whole all data not just English method would get right. Uh, and uh, let's do one more thought experiment. Close your eyes. Good. Think of a toilet. Open your eyes. Uh, you probably all thought of something that looks like this. But in reality, these are also toilets. Uh, but you wouldn't really get these pictures if you filter to North American uh, data, right? Um, and there is a long list of such examples. Um, and there exists data sets analyzing this. So we"}, {"t": 1741, "text": "build a benchmark and showed that basically as models get better on imageet and the cocoa which is this here. Um these benchmarks are very correlated but then they get worse on and then we collected a whole set of benchmarks for like for measuring how good it is on basically across the globe things. Um all right and then like we slowly I hope kind of convince the community to stop doing the filtering um and then we can move on. Uh there is one ooh time uh there is one more thing um about clip and cig pre-training there is a fundamental issue in this pre-training that I just described um and this is my favorite example uh take this picture and this caption they are perfect this is super clean data like uh the picture perfectly described by the"}, {"t": 1801, "text": "caption uh now imagine this is in a mini batch with a lot of other picture caption images. And the task of the model to of the training as I explained before is for this image find out of all of the captions the matching one the one that corresponds to this image right and vice versa. Um however to solve this task if there is no other cat image in the mini batch then it just needs to learn to recognize a cat and the word cat and it can completely ignore the rest. Right? And same for dog. If there's no other dog image in the whole mini batch, then it just needs to learn to recognize dog and the word dog and that's it and nothing else. Um, and to learn about sitting uh the model there needs to be another image in the mini batch where there is a cat and the dog and they are not sitting otherwise it can always take the shortcut. And to learn left off there needs to be another image in the mini batch with a cat and a dog sitting but the other way around. And how likely is that from random web"}, {"t": 1861, "text": "data even if your mini batch is like 30,000 or even a million examples. Uh so this is the thing clip actually just doesn't learn really these relationships. Uh and then there were a bunch of benchmarks that measured this uh that were created and we kind of wanted to fix this in a scalable way because then what people started doing is actually kind of finding the counterpart and putting it into the mini batch and so on and then starting to enumerate things like left off, right off and so on where they won't try to fix it. We uh since um as said earlier you should try to go for the simple thing that scales. So we wanted to try to find a simple fix that scales and pre-trains but without this issue. And there is a really simple one which is just learning to caption like generate the caption for the image. So you generate word by word just like LLMs generate text um right and when you need to generate the word that comes after"}, {"t": 1921, "text": "sitting you need to generate left and give it higher probability not right or in front or behind and so on. Um so this is simple um I'm not going to go into the details but it sounds obvious in hindsight the question is why didn't people do it? In the original clip paper there was a figure showing this is horribly less efficient. um we were able to reproduce this picture but it was just an artifact of their exact setup like kind of an accident of history. Uh changed things and it's not horribly uh less efficient. Uh and doing so then on these benchmarks that specifically measure the deficiency of clip models it just completely nails them. However, and here's the next lesson that is more general than vision. And I if you take anything away from this whole lecture it's this. What does this benchmark look like? Let's have a look at the data. This is the key point. Look at the data. Uh here is an example from this benchmark. And uh given a picture that I'm not showing you here, you need to"}, {"t": 1981, "text": "choose which of these two sentences matches the picture. The sentences are the horse is eating the grass or the grass is eating the horse. Quiz time. Who thinks it is the horse is eating the grass? Okay. Who thinks it is the grass is eating the horse? you're funny once. Um, so of course it is the horses eating the grass. You didn't even need to see the picture, right? And this is the picture just for completeness. And the benchmark is full of these. It doesn't make sense. Obviously, you're going to nail this benchmark if you have any real world understanding. It maybe it shows that clip models, even the text part, has no real world understanding. But like just to show it, we trained a blind captioner that just learns to caption without looking at the image and it also completely needs it. Um so the key lesson look at the benchmarks actually because this is a very classical computer vision benchmark mistake that has happened almost every generation of PhDs like there are papers and"}, {"t": 2041, "text": "benchmarks that are broken like this like every 5 years. Um and luckily there were other people that also figured this out made the actually proper version of the benchmark and then uh also their captioning pre-training just completely nails it. Uh so good. Um and then just to say in future image encoder pre-training models then we always have a captioning loss because of this. Um so if you know SIGLIP or if you use SIGLPIP v2 is better and has a captioning loss. So use that instead. Um now I have an issue because I have just five minutes for mid training and finetuning. I think I will just skip fine tuning. Um and mid Yeah, I'll just go a little bit over time. People are hungry. Um, so mid training is actually a relatively recent term and people like this guy is established professor and they're like what is mid training? I think this"}, {"t": 2101, "text": "random person on the web uh I don't mean this as an insult if you are here or listening to this uh described it really well. uh it's the steps that come after the bulk of self-supervised pre-training and are for shaping core capabilities and before fine-tuning with RL for exact behaviors. So it's if there are core capabilities or I like to call them skills also of the model that you think will be really useful to doing all of your downstream task or a large fraction of your downstream task but do not necessarily just come out of the pre-training as you saw the pre-training with image text whether it's captioning or the matching clip style is kind of crude right it wouldn't for example learn locations like uh I don't know top left or 2 m away off or something or unlikely to learn this Um here and this is the most canonical example of mid training then that I promised you that I will get to uh of the VLM right this is"}, {"t": 2160, "text": "a polygeema and most uh VLMs look like this. You have an image encoder for example clip or cigip that takes an image uh sends it through and gets you a bunch of vectors out uh which are then you can just see as tokens. Uh then you concat them with uh tokens from a text which is typically some kind of question about the image um or some kind of task doesn't necessarily have to be a question and then they go into a language model and the language model spits out the answer um or yeah so this is for VLM and then VLA later instead of answer it's like actions um and this is mid training because it takes two pre-trained models stitches them together and then gives it new core skills that will be needed later that do not really come out of the pre-training. And then the important part is what is the training mixture of this? Uh and the training mixture should be a bunch of data that is reasonably large. Doesn't have to be as large as uh pre-training."}, {"t": 2222, "text": "Doesn't have to cover everything in the whole world, but needs to force the model to learn the skills that you uh want it to. Um, for example, I mean the simplest example here is to just caption the image. Um, but then for example, you could add OCR in it. Uh, which is like being able to read the image. Then you need to prepare data that has pictures and then the text that corresponds to it. Um, but then again it's just to give the skill to the model, not to make it necessarily useful to a user to do this skill. So you would just for example use have a ton of just random images that have some kind of text on it and the task is really just to spit out the whole text and that's it or just some random chunks of the text. This in itself is not useful to a user of the model but it it gives the model the skill of reading text. All right. And then later in the finetuning you would actually turn it into useful to the user uh kind of tasks."}, {"t": 2280, "text": "Um and uh yes and for this you will need a lot less data. Um right and here in the mixture there is some very special types of questions also that are about objects. Uh partially actually to make polygeema nice for like robotic style tasks. So it learns a lot about objects and locations and so on. Um so is object in the image which of object object object are in the image and this you can kind of semi-automatically create data sets for this if you have some existing images with some kind of metadata um or turn question and answers around given an answer generate the likely question that led to this answer for this image. Um and some detection some segmentation data and also some like caption what is in this specific area of the image. uh for example, which again itself is not a useful thing to a user, but it really forces the model to learn a skill that can be then repurposed in fine-tuning"}, {"t": 2342, "text": "for actually useful tasks. Um all right. Uh and then in mid-raining you can also use this or it's used a lot to scale up a few remaining axis that you were not able to scale up during pre-training. For example, for vision a very common thing is resolution. In mid- training, you would learn higher and higher resolution images as input where you see more and more details which is just not practical to do higher resolution pre-training. It's impossible compute wise. Uh or if you have video to extend to longer and longer uh context uh and if you were doing some kind of 3D to have 3D task to have more and more slices and so on. Um all right, but this is only text out. Although I think you have already seen how to get actions out with VA. So this is maybe a bit less interesting but for the classic vision part you can of course turn a lot of tasks into text output tasks and this is the nicest example I think for detection where you can really just split the x-axis into bins um like24 which is"}, {"t": 2402, "text": "almost pixel locations and then you can just output bounding boxes like this in a textual way right with the coordinates as text um and there is a general way to convert almost anything you want into text such that VLM can uh that you can learn to model it with the VLM which is the most common technique is called VQVE. I don't have the time to go into the details but if you're interested look up VQVE it's basically your autoenccoder which in the bottleneck learns a discrete dictionary. So any image like for example this segmentation mask image you can then turn to into a sequence of uh newly learned tokens. you can just add them to your vocab of your VLM LLM and then suddenly the VLM can output segmentation masks by outputting these segmentation tokens mixed into the text. Um, and yeah, I will skip the finetuning. Uh, it's really just a large mix of tons"}, {"t": 2460, "text": "of specialized uh tasks. And the nice thing is when doing the finetuning, you also get kind of skill transfer of these skills. So for example um you may have learned to do segmentation but on natural images like photos of animals and whatn not uh and in your finetuning you may have fine-tuned on uh let's say question answering on satellite images like is there a lake yes no in this section and the model can actually transfer some of these skills like for example after this finetuning on a wide mix of data the model can certainly learn to segment things in satellite images which do not exist as a specific task neither in mid-raining nor in fine-tuning here I can say for sure um yeah and then you can do array tuning to hopefully get even better performance but we're not going to go into that now um so kind of concluding I would slightly provocatively say yeah this recipe"}, {"t": 2521, "text": "somewhat solves uh perception now like in the we have gone from 8 years ago perception barely working at all to it kind of being solved Um however if you are somewhat looking at papers there is a bunch of papers that kind of say like nope not at all vision language models are actually blind and then they give you for example a task like this just everybody can do this like two lines with a kink and the question is how many intersections do these lines have possible answer 012 um and then they test all kind of frontier models it's a bit older already So slightly older front models but still on this end just showed that okay they get pretty bad accuracy because a human would get above 90% for sure. Um and then they have a whole bunch of these tasks and then so what am I even talking about? How can I claim perceptions kind of solved um the important part is that this recipe that I just went through this is what I mean by solved. You still"}, {"t": 2580, "text": "need to apply it for the things you care about. Um, and then all of these VLMs that they tested, they have a huge huge huge list of SFT uh data sets in the end. But obscure things like how many intersections do you have these lines or like similarish tasks are very likely not in there because it's just not a meaningful way to spend time to do the post training mix. Um, but the models can actually do this. You just need to quickly explain the task to them. To make this uh point, I took this task, I generated 128 examples of it and then I fine-tuned polygeemma on it and they get 95 96% accuracy already. And this was super simple, super cheap. Uh and since it's actually classification task, we don't even need the language model part of it, which can just fine-tune uh siglip on it and we get like 97% accuracy. So the VLMs are not really blind. They are more difficult of"}, {"t": 2641, "text": "understanding like if you give them completely new task. They kind of intrinsically can do the task because 128 examples are not enough to learn the new underlying skill of finding intersections. Uh but they are enough to learn to understand the task. What does the user actually want from me? Um so yeah. Um so this is the recipe. If you have any task just think of the task you want to solve. collect a couple hundred examples in an afternoon, fine-tune a large pre-trained model like a VLM on this and then it works and then if it's doesn't work well enough for you then arune it and then you can deploy it. There is a little caveat because it's a little bit of bold statement but I am careful to say perception task. Um so I really mean pure perception. Now the interesting things and those that you guys will encounter a lot if you go into robot learning is those that come after perception uh like for example adding some reasoning. So for example"}, {"t": 2701, "text": "this recipe is not enough to solve the task of here take a picture of my homework and just say solve my homework there if it's non-trivial homework. Um or explain why this is funny. Or if you add some planning like this recipe is also not enough to do planning. For example, robot make me a cappuccino. Sure, it understands where is the capuccino machine, what where is the cup and so on, but it this is not enough to plan the whole sequence. Um, and yeah, let's ignore the last point uh and get to the conclusion for today. So, we have this simple recipe now that the whole community has converged to and it's relatively simple. Uh, and if somebody else did the pre-training for you, it's pretty cheap. you can just take any of the good recent VLM models and fine-tune it on a small set of examples. You need to tune the learning rate, but then it's going to work um for perception tasks. Um so now this is I really like this because we can now move to actually making useful things not just papers and"}, {"t": 2760, "text": "we can move to the interesting tiers uh like planning, reasoning, uh actions. Um this is a little bit unsatisfying uh but it's not a blocker in that as mentioned before just giving the text of a new task explaining it in words uh is not enough. We do need to fine-tune a little bit on it to solve a new task. Um and this same recipe okay this is a bit older now because I made this slide last summer but this same recipe is starting to work in other domains. Language obviously you all know this but also in chemistry for example. I saw a paper doing exactly this recipe but through chemistry a chemistry sorry and working really well. Um so yeah then what is left to do I kind of claim that this recipe is good enough uh for perception uh and now we should move beyond that and I think you being here means you want to move beyond that uh in robotic learning because that's definitely a step beyond uh so yeah apply to new task to new domains and"}, {"t": 2821, "text": "work on the things that come afterwards like reasoning planning and that's it thank Do we have questions for us? Thanks. A quick question like is there any recommendation on the best papers or like uh downstream toss um better do the small scale pre-training not only like bit kind of transfer learning or the fine too thanks small scale pre-train itself &gt;&gt; small scale pre-training I think is almost the oxymoron no really um I think it really depends what you want to do or why you want to do it for but like small scale I would say is not pre-training take if it's in any way possible take something pre-trained and then fine tune it. Uh there is one thing if you want to try to analyze and understand pre-training"}, {"t": 2881, "text": "dynamics you can kind of do it at smaller scale um by it depends which dynamics you're interested in. Uh but by making synthetic data for some specific task uh you can just generate it and then you can kind of have infinite. Oh, you want &gt;&gt; quick follow up like then on the like small scale of data just as we saw on the like with the K kind of things regarding these kind of like parameter hyperparameters then on the small scale of data even if I'm just doing the finetuning process how to better choose the like the process when to stop and how to choose them &gt;&gt; yeah um for how to choose the hyperparameters even at smaller scale uh the the good thing if you use a reasonably probably well pre-trained model as starting point in fine tune is that type of parameters are less sensitive which doesn't mean they are not sensitive they still are um actually for this you can check the polygema paper like we do a very big study of how to fine-tune on different tasks and like"}, {"t": 2941, "text": "what parameters are sensitive what not we even have like a recommendation tune this one first this one next and so on and this is a kind of general recommendation not just for balma um but finetuning is relatively cheap. So I would say just sweep the hyperparameters and then it's very important to have the to have a setup that you can trust like to have a properly split training and validation set. Make sure they really don't overlap and so on. &gt;&gt; Hey Lucas, thank you for the wonderful talk. I had a question about image augmentations and how much that can help over actually collecting more and more new data. And a second follow-up would be how do you do data dduplication and just making sure your data set mixture is unique. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Um okay. So image augmentation it's always better to get more data than to do augmentation only in the case that you cannot get more data and do not have"}, {"t": 3001, "text": "enough then augmentation helps. Um we actually have a whole paper on this too which is called how to train your vit which does a study exactly like if you have uh 10 times less data but all kinds of augmentations which one help and if you have 100 times less data which help how much and so on but the TLDDR is augmentations can get you to roughly one order of magnitude more data but that's it. Uh and this is after very intensive tuning of the augmentations which is quite expensive. Uh so yeah if any way possible get more data instead. And the second part of the question was &gt;&gt; dduplication. &gt;&gt; Ah yeah dduplication. Um yes this I don't mention it because I feel like it goes without saying but in all of our works we do heavy dduplication. Uh there are multiple things for dduplication. One is uh if you have a held out set of tasks that you use for testing or evaluating like I"}, {"t": 3060, "text": "showed in the very beginning this task adaptation benchmark you should remove all images that come even close to this from your pre-training set. Uh and in all of our experiments we did that which is the only way we can actually trust our results somewhat uh because especially if the whole web is your pre-training set then your benchmarks are likely in your pre-training set and you're not measuring the generalization you think you are. Um the and then I think when we had maybe a graph order of magnitude wise for each 1 billion pre-training image maybe 10 million would be near duplicates uh of the downstream. So we remove them but then if you have 1 billion removing 10 million doesn't matter much um in terms of what you cover but it gives you the certainty that your learnings are actually real and not just the work you think. Um then uh and how to do this is a little bit difficult. We use the internally trained model specifically designed for that. I think there exist some open source models also for that."}, {"t": 3121, "text": "But otherwise just use a good image emer and you get the vector as image embedding and you can do nearest neighbor search and do some threshold uh and those that are near enough you consider them duplicates and remove and then tune it for high recall and rather remove 10 times too much than too little. Um yeah, &gt;&gt; yeah, thanks for the great talk. Two questions, one more specific, one more general on you told us a little bit about the limitations of no matter how you scale your batch size, you're always going to have this problem with with clip and and siglet. Um and going to to Gemma, it was my understanding that that the contrastive loss used there was only on vision. Was that problem avoided because nothing is frozen after it gets fed through the uh language model backbone? &gt;&gt; Yes, exactly. Thank you. I wanted to say it on this slide because it's very important but then I forgot uh because I was a bit in a hurry. Um but here uh"}, {"t": 3183, "text": "then this is a clip pre-trained model. So it has all the limitations that I just explained before. Um right and the reason why not the captioning pre-training model is just like big corp technicalities. Um but it is what it is. And so a very important part here was exactly to not freeze this one and a lot of VLMs that you find that are not polymer have this one frozen so they just cannot recover from the limitations of clip. Um and this was very important to us and we did a lot of studies on not freezing it and specifically when you start having things like uh question with relationships or things about locations like detection and so on. This becomes very important. Um and this is why like I I would not use VLM that say they froze the encoder for anything finetuning. &gt;&gt; Thanks. And the quick second one, uh, you you spoke to us at the end about your approach to to fine-tuning um,"}, {"t": 3242, "text": "retraining models off the shelf. Do you before you look to finetune a task first see if it's possible to do it zero shot or with in context learning and and take some other approaches that require less um, data collection even if it's only an afternoon. &gt;&gt; Mhm. Um, just because I forgot to finish this is the training mix has captioning. So this is how the learnings uh go into the vision model. Now for fine-tuning um I think in context learning is kind of tricky. I think there have been many papers going back and forth having whether this actually works at all or not. So I kind of just don't do this. Um just prompting yeah if it works uh then it's fine. But then it usually means that there was something similarish, not the same thing but similarish in the SFT mix in the first place. So you don't really need to do finetuning. But then yeah, if quantum doesn't work, then go to fine tuning. And yeah, data is good. It just if it"}, {"t": 3301, "text": "takes an afternoon, just go collect some data, sit down. It's annoying and lazy. Not lazy, um, boring, but just do it. &gt;&gt; Question. &gt;&gt; Hi. Yeah, thanks for the great talk. I think we all agree we could have listened to it for like two more hours. It's really interesting. Um I wanted to ask you kind of much more general question like if you have any opinions on JA like joint in relative architecture proposed by the main &gt;&gt; that's just for context. Anyways, um so yeah, I was wondering like if you have any thoughts, do you share his view that this is kind of the future and that &gt;&gt; I have already that I posted at some point but yeah no like my opinion is that exactly he did not introduce this like this is what the people have been doing forever in self supervision and then he just like gives a new name to it and maybe I didn't need a name because like there was no name for it uh But that's"}, {"t": 3360, "text": "it. I think uh everybody agrees that like this general direction makes sense and we should try doing it. Uh yeah, but I also kind of meant to ask if you share at least the the hype part of his view that it's much much um high higher priority over LMS uh in terms of reasoning capability etc. &gt;&gt; Yeah, not really. Um yeah, because this is recorded, let's stop here. How much time do you have more? &gt;&gt; For me, it's fine. I'm not hungry yet for anybody here. If you're happy to see, I will not take it personally. &gt;&gt; So, going back to what you started with, are there any plans to continue working on the robot that any nontechnical person can teach again? &gt;&gt; Oh, yeah. It would be good. I still have a few uh VMish things I am working on. I"}, {"t": 3421, "text": "want to see through and then after that yeah &gt;&gt; did you read the news that we acquired some negro startup learning startup? &gt;&gt; I guess &gt;&gt; um one one question like um so you have a claim that perception is soft. So I kind of challenging a little bit there. So there's uh for any like animals or any like uh kids they can like understand streaming videos like visual content just coming through all the time and um so understanding this streaming video and react acting vision actually like part of human was default perception behavior. I forgot the this kind of area is quite poorly in real first you basically cannot streaming understanding you have to like cap the"}, {"t": 3481, "text": "KB catch in some way to to somehow so so it's and the perception system also doesn't have the ability for example like focus on really matters everywhere like and filter out the non like matters tokens all those I think somehow can solve So maybe I kind of a little comment on that. Streaming video understanding. Yeah. Uh I think for the I think there is a few parts to this for the streaming video understanding part. I think it's basically a case of this uh that I think there is just not that many questions and types of tasks in uh in SFMix to but I think fundamentally they can do most understanding parts of video. Um but there is the other part that is doing this efficiently uh and online/ on the"}, {"t": 3540, "text": "fly. Um this yeah I didn't talk about architectures at all here. Um this is I would say not solved but getting very close. Um and uh yeah and I think this is not I see it more as a optimization issue. Uh like not as in like oh write more efficient cooler kernels maybe that will help too. Um but it's more about it's not about uh missing capability but it's about making the current capability but it's cheaper like cheaper compute or memory or things like that. I think this is a generally very interactable thing but I I will not comment on like architectural details. &gt;&gt; Okay you &gt;&gt; okay thank you very much for the talk as well. So you say that all of this is basically possible by leveraging web scale data right and web scale data is still much"}, {"t": 3600, "text": "much larger than what humans need to learn right do you see any way to reduce this dependency on large data for example for robotics or also other domains where this type of data isn't accessible &gt;&gt; um yes it kind of depends what you want to do um because I think If you want like my goal that I mentioned the robot to be able to learn anything then it needs to have like seen anything like a robot that has never seen a chair will not be able to learn anything about chairs unless we're going to continue learning which is a whole different question. Um but yeah so I think if you want to be general there is no way around it. Now if you don't want to be general let's say you want to make a robot that is in the factory in the supply chain then it should be robust to stuff like illumination changes like I don't know maybe the box is suddenly becoming a different box because supplier reasons or whatever um then I think this should"}, {"t": 3661, "text": "be very possible because then you don't need to have seen shares like they will not help you in your task at all so I think it heavily depends on how general you want the model to be. Thank you. Um hi uh thanks for the talk. Um I have one question is um is over there. Um do you think there is still a lot to do in term of infant speed because all these large models are very capable of doing great things for questioning and so on and for robotic control or even drones. Do you think there's still some work to be done in this area? &gt;&gt; Yeah, probably. Um, I think especially if you go to VLA and then you have like actions and like chunks and you want to predict I don't know 50 or so tokens into the future, this can be like too slow to make it happen in real time and there I have seen so I'm not expert on this but I've seen quite a few nice like papers and approaches for example I"}, {"t": 3720, "text": "think pi robotics had a few works on that um and uh that's one thing the other thing that I I wanted to put so much into this slides. Um I wanted to put in there but didn't is distillation. I think this is also like a super important actually. Who knows what is distillation in the context of burning most people. Okay. Yeah. Um I think there is also like uh how to say like with pre-training you can do it right, you can do it wrong. like there's a lot of details to be careful of but then if you get the details right um and they respect us about this um then I think discussion is extremely strong uh and so I think a very very good recipe or like let's say addendum to this recipe that I mentioned is like do everything scale up and so on to get as good as possible model but then when you actually need to use it nobody wants to use this gigantic model that you need 20 GPUs to run on and so on um then go and"}, {"t": 3782, "text": "distill it for or like first do the like pre-training to make it really good. Transfer RL tune on your task to make it good and robust on your task and then distill it into one that has the latency that you need for the task. Uh and this distillation step can be very expensive uh but because it needs very very long training usually. Um but then it can be very much worth it if it turns it from to a how to say from to a broker from a broker into hey we can actually do this and run this on the robot or in real life. Oh and then there is million engineering tricks and so on from the optimization side. &gt;&gt; Yeah. You show us that getting lunch to do tasks requires training on like a million different uh tasks in the train set and doing a simple unseen task requires a little bit of fine-tuning sometimes. Do you think this is evidence that frontier LMS have like a low fluid intelligence as France would say and"}, {"t": 3841, "text": "they're just memorizing the steps to solve a task and we need some like EBM approach where you separate the goal out of the model and put it in like some energy function is more sample efficient than just making more QA tasks. difficult to answer. I would say it's not necessarily clear that fining is like the most efficient way or so. Um and so I I wouldn't necessarily agree that okay energy based is the solution. Um but I do agree as I mentioned this is like the unsatisfying part of the current recipe, right? That it doesn't just understand what you want from free form text. Um and I think there is a lot of back and forth currently on how to fix this. Um where like there is a camp of people saying like not just do more"}, {"t": 3901, "text": "and more and more task and eventually we have enough that it actually has to generalize because it's just too much to memorize. Uh which is kind of uh happening in some uh regimes more in text not in image text. Um and then there's other camps that say like yeah no this doesn't work at all. There is also people exploring things like um automatically generating these few examples like based on the text prompt. You can imagine if the model is really good at language and at coding maybe from the task we have seen here like I just wrote little code to make this a few examples to then train on you could imagine maybe like a reasoning model that starts doing this and so on. But I I agree. I think this is the part that is annoyingly not solved yet or would be nice to solve in a nicer way. &gt;&gt; Uh thanks for the talk. I have more of a conceptual question. We're spending all"}, {"t": 3960, "text": "this uh compute and and amazing brains like yours on how to make models smarter. At some point it's going to cost also like the human brains. I don't think we're getting smarter as humans by outsourcing most of our cognition. Maybe we're already becoming a bit stupid. Like you think of maybe some software engineers that have spent years becoming extremely good at their craft and are just pressing some okay keys um at 347 is not that smart. So let's imagine your adult job unfortunately and your new task is to make human smarter. How would you approach it? What the &gt;&gt; the the human training after all these trainings? &gt;&gt; No, I agree. Like when I don't know like Codex is down, I was like ah I cannot work anymore. Yeah. No, I mean I don't know. I don't have a good idea. One thing that I like, but I think I'm just getting too"}, {"t": 4021, "text": "old to learn new stuff. Uh, but I think the the L&amp;Ms are just really nice to learn new stuff with. I imagine old me like 15 20 years ago, I would have loved to have a LM there instead of needing to Google everything, read like 20 territory tutorials and so on, but just be able to ask it questions. So yeah, that's kind of my but then also you you have the calculator like you don't care if you cannot multiply large numbers anymore in your head because you just don't need it. And could you outsource maybe like you don't want to start running something new today. Would you be keen to have an AI lead you this way? Do you think this is possible have an AI like your sprink on everything and knowing what are you smart on and not so smart yet and push this direction? &gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. I use a lot like this. &gt;&gt; Okay. Okay. Last question. Uh thank you for the talk. How do you feel about um image encoders that do"}, {"t": 4081, "text": "weight with text like Dino V3 or others? &gt;&gt; Yeah, I not a fan. I don't see why. Um like basically the text is there. Why not use it? And I think there is I think maybe DO is the only one that gets reasonable performance at all without text. There have been million of other attempts before in this for supervisit area and they are just always clearly worse. So I think Dino is a let's say I think it's a intellectually interesting concept like huh we should be able to be to learn uh like good general presentation without text and so it's nice and it's nice that they got this far but I think it's unnecessarily making it hard for us so okay with this let's thank Lucas again for a fantastic"}], "toc": [{"t": 0, "title": "Introduction"}, {"t": 104, "title": "Vision and pre-training"}, {"t": 2060, "title": "Mid-training concepts"}, {"t": 2085, "title": "PaliGemma architecture"}, {"t": 2208, "title": "Training mixtures"}, {"t": 2345, "title": "Scaling and conversion"}, {"t": 2511, "title": "Perception and final recipe"}, {"t": 2727, "title": "Q&A and closing remarks"}], "toc_source": "chapters from YouTube", "affiliation": "Meta Superintelligence Labs"}], "slides": {"about_html": "<ul>\n<li>Lecture: Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models, ETH Zurich, Spring 2026, Oier Mees, 11.05.2026</li>\n<li>Source: <code>slides.pdf</code> in this folder (47 frames, 1280x720, no text layer), re-extracted on 2026-10-04 from the 720p YouTube recording with the slide repo’s own <code>lectures/extract_slides.py</code> (github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich). That repo’s <code>week11_slides.pdf</code> is only 640x360, so its frame numbering differs from this one.</li>\n<li>Transcribed from the frame images by Claude (vision), not by an OCR engine. Text is verbatim; equations are LaTeX; pictures are described in <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>. Frames that are animation builds of the same slide are merged into one section, with the frame numbers listed. Everything in square brackets is the transcriber’s own wording, not slide text: <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[Figure: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> descriptions, <code>&lt;span class=\"own note\"&gt;[Note: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code> remarks, build and arrow annotations, and heading parts that are not on the slide (e.g. <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[untitled: ...]&lt;/span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;span class=\"own\"&gt;[continued]&lt;/span&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li>Video: https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE (transcript: <code>transcript_lecture.md</code>; guest talk: <code>transcript_guest_lucas_beyer.md</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />", "sections": [{"id": "title-frame-1", "num": null, "kind": "title", "title_html": "Title", "title": "Title", "frames": [1, 1], "html": "<p><strong>Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models</strong></p>\n<p>Lecture 12: Frontier &amp; Open Problems</p>\n<p>Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 11.05.2026</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: the slide says “Lecture 12”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 11 (week 11, May 11).]</span></p>", "text": "Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models Lecture 12: Frontier & Open Problems Oier Mees - ETH zurich, Microsoft - 11.05.2026 [Note: the slide says “Lecture 12”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 11 (week 11, May 11).]", "t": 0}, {"id": "slide-1-every-week-on-twitter-robotics-is-solved-frame-2", "num": 1, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 1: Every Week on Twitter: Robotics is Solved!", "title": "Slide 1: Every Week on Twitter: Robotics is Solved!", "frames": [2, 2], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: screenshots of three social-media posts, summarised: a venture investor quoting a robotics startup’s launch of a new “robotic brain” model (with a photo of a two-armed humanoid cooking at a stove) as “the endgame for robotics”; a humanoid-company founder announcing an imminent “ChatGPT moment” for robotics (Jan 7, 2024, 2.4M views); and an NVIDIA researcher promoting a talk on a “roadmap for solving Physical AGI” as a parallel to the LLM success story.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: screenshots of three social-media posts, summarised: a venture investor quoting a robotics startup’s launch of a new “robotic brain” model (with a photo of a two-armed humanoid cooking at a stove) as “the endgame for robotics”; a humanoid-company founder announcing an imminent “ChatGPT moment” for robotics (Jan 7, 2024, 2.4M views); and an NVIDIA researcher promoting a talk on a “roadmap for solving Physical AGI” as a parallel to the LLM success story.]", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-2-how-autonomous-robots-usually-generalize-frame-3", "num": 2, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 2: How Autonomous Robots (Usually) Generalize", "title": "Slide 2: How Autonomous Robots (Usually) Generalize", "frames": [3, 3], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two video stills: a humanoid robot fallen on the floor next to a shopping cart in a cafeteria, captioned “Boston Dynamics”; a two-armed mobile robot in a kitchen, captioned “Fu et. al., Stanford 2024”.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 3 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 2 to 4.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: two video stills: a humanoid robot fallen on the floor next to a shopping cart in a cafeteria, captioned “Boston Dynamics”; a two-armed mobile robot in a kitchen, captioned “Fu et. al., Stanford 2024”.] [Note: slide 3 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 2 to 4.]", "t": 120}, {"id": "slide-4-untitled-scales-of-generalization-frame-4", "num": 4, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 4: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: scales of generalization]</span>", "title": "Slide 4: [untitled: scales of generalization]", "frames": [4, 4], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: nested ellipses, from smallest to largest: <strong>1 object</strong> inside <strong>1 room</strong> inside <strong>1 building</strong> inside <strong>the world</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: nested ellipses, from smallest to largest: 1 object inside 1 room inside 1 building inside the world .]", "t": 302}, {"id": "slide-5-the-quest-for-the-best-of-both-worlds-frame-5", "num": 5, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 5: The Quest for the Best of Both Worlds", "title": "Slide 5: The Quest for the Best of Both Worlds", "frames": [5, 5], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Non-Embodied Foundation Models</th>\n<th>Specialist Embodied Models</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a <strong>Transformer</strong> over rows of Images and Language tokens]</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"own\">[Figure: robot arms preparing food at a table (4x)]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✅ Internet-scale Data</td>\n<td>✅ Physical Grounding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✅ Broad Generalization</td>\n<td>✅ Embodied Sensing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ Lacks Physical Grounding</td>\n<td>❌ Narrow, Task-specific Data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>❌ Non-Embodied Sensing</td>\n<td>❌ Poor Generalization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>", "text": "Non-Embodied Foundation Models Specialist Embodied Models [Figure: a Transformer over rows of Images and Language tokens] [Figure: robot arms preparing food at a table (4x)] ✅ Internet-scale Data ✅ Physical Grounding ✅ Broad Generalization ✅ Embodied Sensing ❌ Lacks Physical Grounding ❌ Narrow, Task-specific Data ❌ Non-Embodied Sensing ❌ Poor Generalization", "t": 420}, {"id": "slide-6-the-path-to-embodied-intelligence-frames-6-8", "num": 6, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 6: The Path to Embodied Intelligence", "title": "Slide 6: The Path to Embodied Intelligence", "frames": [6, 8], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Today’s foundation models lack physical grounding</li>\n<li>Embodied data inherently <strong>multimodal, spatial &amp; temporal</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: built up over three frames. <strong>Embodied Data</strong> (Multimodal, Temporal, Spatial, Physical) and <strong>Foundation Models</strong> are connected by arrows in both directions (a loop); Foundation Models -&gt; <strong>AI Agents for the Digital &amp; Physical World</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "Today’s foundation models lack physical grounding Embodied data inherently multimodal, spatial & temporal [Figure: built up over three frames. Embodied Data (Multimodal, Temporal, Spatial, Physical) and Foundation Models are connected by arrows in both directions (a loop); Foundation Models -> AI Agents for the Digital & Physical World .]", "t": 420}, {"id": "slide-7-what-is-the-best-backbone-for-robotics-frame-9", "num": 7, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 7: What is the Best Backbone for Robotics?", "title": "Slide 7: What is the Best Backbone for Robotics?", "frames": [9, 9], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Three options in boxes:]</span> Vision-Language Model | Generative Video Model | Doesn’t matter if we have enough data to train from <strong>scratch</strong>?</p>", "text": "[Three options in boxes:] Vision-Language Model | Generative Video Model | Doesn’t matter if we have enough data to train from scratch ?", "t": 543}, {"id": "slide-8-what-is-the-best-data-recipe-frame-10", "num": 8, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 8: What is the Best Data Recipe?", "title": "Slide 8: What is the Best Data Recipe?", "frames": [10, 10], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two data pyramids. Left: <strong>Web Data</strong> (Wikipedia, Common Crawl) at the base, <strong>Simulation Data</strong> (a simulated robot fleet) in the middle, <strong>Real-World Data</strong> (a person teleoperating a robot arm in VR) on top. Right: Web Data at the base and Real-World Data on top, with no simulation layer.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: two data pyramids. Left: Web Data (Wikipedia, Common Crawl) at the base, Simulation Data (a simulated robot fleet) in the middle, Real-World Data (a person teleoperating a robot arm in VR) on top. Right: Web Data at the base and Real-World Data on top, with no simulation layer.]", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-9-what-is-the-best-data-recipe-video-and-robot-only-pyramids-frame-11", "num": 9, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 9: What is the Best Data Recipe? <span class=\"own\">[video and robot-only pyramids]</span>", "title": "Slide 9: What is the Best Data Recipe? [video and robot-only pyramids]", "frames": [11, 11], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two more pyramids. Left: <strong>Internet Videos</strong> (YouTube) at the base, <strong>Human Videos</strong> (a collage of egocentric clips) in the middle, <strong>Real-World Data</strong> (VR teleoperation) on top. Right: every layer is labelled <strong>Real-World Data</strong>, each a collage of robot clips, the base the largest.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: two more pyramids. Left: Internet Videos (YouTube) at the base, Human Videos (a collage of egocentric clips) in the middle, Real-World Data (VR teleoperation) on top. Right: every layer is labelled Real-World Data , each a collage of robot clips, the base the largest.]", "t": 660}, {"id": "slide-10-data-collection-interfaces-frame-12", "num": 10, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 10: Data Collection Interfaces", "title": "Slide 10: Data Collection Interfaces", "frames": [12, 12], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: five photos:]</span> UMI-Style (a hand-held gripper used at a sink) - Gloves for Dexterous Hands (a robot hand next to a person wearing a sensor glove) - Static Bimanual Puppeteering (a person between two leader arms on a frame) - VR (a person in a VR headset teleoperating a robot arm) - Mobile Bimanual Puppeteering (a person pushing a mobile two-arm rig to clean a restroom, 10x speed)</p>", "text": "[Figure: five photos:] UMI-Style (a hand-held gripper used at a sink) - Gloves for Dexterous Hands (a robot hand next to a person wearing a sensor glove) - Static Bimanual Puppeteering (a person between two leader arms on a frame) - VR (a person in a VR headset teleoperating a robot arm) - Mobile Bimanual Puppeteering (a person pushing a mobile two-arm rig to clean a restroom, 10x speed)", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-11-data-scalability-vs-hardware-alignment-frame-13", "num": 11, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 11: Data Scalability vs Hardware Alignment", "title": "Slide 11: Data Scalability vs Hardware Alignment", "frames": [13, 13], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: plot of Data Scalability (log scale) against Hardware Alignment. Three points on a dashed line falling to the right: <strong>Egocentric Videos</strong> at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^7</annotation></semantics></math> hr, <strong>Data Wearables</strong> at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^5</annotation></semantics></math> hr, <strong>Teleop</strong> at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^3</annotation></semantics></math> hr. A vertical dashed line splits the x-axis into “Sensorized Human Data” (left) and “Robot in the loop” (right); Data Wearables sit almost on the boundary.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: plot of Data Scalability (log scale) against Hardware Alignment. Three points on a dashed line falling to the right: Egocentric Videos at 10 7 hr, Data Wearables at 10 5 hr, Teleop at 10 3 hr. A vertical dashed line splits the x-axis into “Sensorized Human Data” (left) and “Robot in the loop” (right); Data Wearables sit almost on the boundary.]", "t": 841}, {"id": "slide-12-towards-dexterous-generalist-policies-frame-14", "num": 12, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 12: Towards Dexterous Generalist Policies", "title": "Slide 12: Towards Dexterous Generalist Policies", "frames": [14, 14], "html": "<p>Dexterity and generalization require different ingredients</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: Venn diagram. <strong>Dexterity</strong>: High-Frequency Control; Heterogeneous Multimodal Sensing. <strong>Generalization</strong>: Foundation Models; Mobility. Overlap: <strong>Goal</strong>.]</span></p>", "text": "Dexterity and generalization require different ingredients [Figure: Venn diagram. Dexterity : High-Frequency Control; Heterogeneous Multimodal Sensing. Generalization : Foundation Models; Mobility. Overlap: Goal .]", "t": 1021}, {"id": "slide-13-dexterity-requires-more-than-vision-frame-15", "num": 13, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 13: Dexterity Requires More Than Vision", "title": "Slide 13: Dexterity Requires More Than Vision", "frames": [15, 15], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: video still of the PR2 robot at the Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg “TidyUpRobot” setup, grasping a blue cup near a cardboard box, with the overlay “Failure modes”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Self-supervised 3D Shape and Viewpoint Estimation from Single Images for Robotics</em>, <strong>Mees,</strong> Tatarchenko et al., IROS, 2019.</p>", "text": "[Figure: video still of the PR2 robot at the Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg “TidyUpRobot” setup, grasping a blue cup near a cardboard box, with the overlay “Failure modes”.] Reference: Self-supervised 3D Shape and Viewpoint Estimation from Single Images for Robotics , Mees, Tatarchenko et al., IROS, 2019.", "t": 1140}, {"id": "slide-14-recap-native-multimodal-models-frame-16", "num": 14, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 14: Recap: Native Multimodal Models", "title": "Slide 14: Recap: Native Multimodal Models", "frames": [16, 16], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Instead of adding vision to an existing LLM, train all modalities jointly from scratch</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: same diagram as week 7, slide 25: image, audio, video and text inputs (each optional) go through their encoders into an interleaved token sequence (any order, any mix), then a <strong>unified multimodal transformer</strong> “trained from scratch - every weight has always seen every modality”, giving text (+ image) output.]</span></p>", "text": "Instead of adding vision to an existing LLM, train all modalities jointly from scratch [Figure: same diagram as week 7, slide 25: image, audio, video and text inputs (each optional) go through their encoders into an interleaved token sequence (any order, any mix), then a unified multimodal transformer “trained from scratch - every weight has always seen every modality”, giving text (+ image) output.]", "t": 1140}, {"id": "slide-15-the-long-tail-of-robot-sensing-frame-17", "num": 15, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 15: The Long Tail of Robot Sensing", "title": "Slide 15: The Long Tail of Robot Sensing", "frames": [17, 17], "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Scarcity</strong>: many relevant sensing modalities (e.g. touch) not available at scale</li>\n<li><strong>Pairing</strong>: cross-modal paired data (e.g. RGB + depth+ tactile + force simultaneously) is nearly nonexistent</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneity</strong>: sensors vary across robot embodiments, making transfer hard even when data exists</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Scarcity : many relevant sensing modalities (e.g. touch) not available at scale Pairing : cross-modal paired data (e.g. RGB + depth+ tactile + force simultaneously) is nearly nonexistent Heterogeneity : sensors vary across robot embodiments, making transfer hard even when data exists", "t": 1260}, {"id": "slide-16-reason-across-new-modalities-frame-18", "num": 16, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 16: Reason Across New Modalities?", "title": "Slide 16: Reason Across New Modalities?", "frames": [18, 18], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: overlapping circles: <strong>Language</strong> in the centre, overlapping <strong>Vision</strong> (top), <strong>Audio</strong> (bottom left) and <strong>Touch</strong> (bottom right).]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: overlapping circles: Language in the centre, overlapping Vision (top), Audio (bottom left) and Touch (bottom right).]", "t": 1260}, {"id": "slide-17-giving-vlas-senses-they-were-never-trained-on-frame-19", "num": 17, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 17: Giving VLAs Senses They Were Never Trained On", "title": "Slide 17: Giving VLAs Senses They Were Never Trained On", "frames": [19, 19], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>Vision</strong> (camera images), <strong>Touch</strong> (a tactile sensor image) and <strong>Audio</strong> (a spectrogram) feed into <strong>FuSe Finetuning</strong> (“Generative+contrastive losses ground multimodal inputs in language”), which starts from a <strong>Pre-trained Generalist Policy</strong> (Octo or a VLA). <strong>Multi-Modal Language</strong> instructions also feed in, e.g. “Push the button that plays piano”, “Pick the object that feels squishy”.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Beyond Sight: Finetuning Generalist Robot Policies with Heterogeneous Sensors via Language Grounding</em>, Jones*, <strong>Mees*</strong>, Sferrazza*, Stachowicz, Abbeel, Levine. ICRA, 2025.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]</span></p>", "text": "[Figure: Vision (camera images), Touch (a tactile sensor image) and Audio (a spectrogram) feed into FuSe Finetuning (“Generative+contrastive losses ground multimodal inputs in language”), which starts from a Pre-trained Generalist Policy (Octo or a VLA). Multi-Modal Language instructions also feed in, e.g. “Push the button that plays piano”, “Pick the object that feels squishy”.] Reference: Beyond Sight: Finetuning Generalist Robot Policies with Heterogeneous Sensors via Language Grounding , Jones*, Mees* , Sferrazza*, Stachowicz, Abbeel, Levine. ICRA, 2025. [Note: slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19.]", "t": 1320}, {"id": "slide-19-when-how-to-reason-intelligently-frame-20", "num": 19, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 19: When &amp; How to Reason Intelligently?", "title": "Slide 19: When & How to Reason Intelligently?", "frames": [20, 20], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a “policy confidence” bar running from certain through uncertain to unknown, with three columns:]</span></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✓ no reasoning needed (certain)</th>\n<th>adaptive TTC (uncertain)</th>\n<th>escalate / stop (unknown)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>In-distribution task</strong> - Known objects, scene, goal</td>\n<td><strong>Near-distribution task</strong> - Novelty or low confidence</td>\n<td><strong>Out-of-distribution task</strong> - Novel goal or failure mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-&gt; <strong>Reactive policy</strong> - No reasoning tokens (~50 Hz control loop)</td>\n<td>-&gt; <strong>Uncertainty detection</strong> -&gt; <strong>Scale test-time compute</strong> - ↓ frequency, ↑ reasoning</td>\n<td>-&gt; <strong>Escalate / ask for help</strong> - Pause and defer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Below a dashed line, “test-time compute can take many forms”: <strong>More thinking tokens</strong> (CoT, GRPO, RL reasoning); <strong>Larger / specialist model</strong> (Foundation model fallback); <strong>Human in the loop</strong> (Teleop, correction, label).</p>", "text": "[Figure: a “policy confidence” bar running from certain through uncertain to unknown, with three columns:] ✓ no reasoning needed (certain) adaptive TTC (uncertain) escalate / stop (unknown) In-distribution task - Known objects, scene, goal Near-distribution task - Novelty or low confidence Out-of-distribution task - Novel goal or failure mode -> Reactive policy - No reasoning tokens (~50 Hz control loop) -> Uncertainty detection -> Scale test-time compute - ↓ frequency, ↑ reasoning -> Escalate / ask for help - Pause and defer Below a dashed line, “test-time compute can take many forms”: More thinking tokens (CoT, GRPO, RL reasoning); Larger / specialist model (Foundation model fallback); Human in the loop (Teleop, correction, label).", "t": 1501}, {"id": "slide-20-untitled-introspection-frame-21", "num": 20, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 20: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: introspection]</span>", "title": "Slide 20: [untitled: introspection]", "frames": [21, 21], "html": "<p><strong>How does a model know what it doesn’t know?</strong></p>\n<p>Generalization across many axes: objects, environments, embodiments, instructions, tasks…</p>\n<p><strong>Open Problem: Introspection</strong></p>", "text": "How does a model know what it doesn’t know? Generalization across many axes: objects, environments, embodiments, instructions, tasks… Open Problem: Introspection", "t": 1621}, {"id": "slide-21-current-robot-models-rely-on-imitation-learning-frame-22", "num": 21, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 21: Current Robot Models Rely on Imitation Learning", "title": "Slide 21: Current Robot Models Rely on Imitation Learning", "frames": [22, 22], "html": "<p>Policy bounded by the demonstrations in the dataset</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Dataset”: states 1, 2, 3; the demonstrated path goes 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3.]</span></p>", "text": "Policy bounded by the demonstrations in the dataset [Figure: “Dataset”: states 1, 2, 3; the demonstrated path goes 1 -> 2 -> 3.]", "t": 1683}, {"id": "slide-22-can-we-scale-rl-for-robotics-frame-23", "num": 22, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 22: Can we Scale RL for Robotics?", "title": "Slide 22: Can we Scale RL for Robotics?", "frames": [23, 23], "html": "<p>Imitation learning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Imitates seen behaviors in the data</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reinforcement learning (RL):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Learns near optimal policies from suboptimal data</li>\n<li>Stitches suboptimal trajectories</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: “Dataset” with the path 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3; “Offline-RL policy” going straight 1 -&gt; 3.]</span></p>", "text": "Imitation learning: Imitates seen behaviors in the data Reinforcement learning (RL): Learns near optimal policies from suboptimal data Stitches suboptimal trajectories [Figure: “Dataset” with the path 1 -> 2 -> 3; “Offline-RL policy” going straight 1 -> 3.]", "t": 1741}, {"id": "slide-23-robot-learning-data-flywheel-frame-24", "num": 23, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 23: Robot Learning Data Flywheel", "title": "Slide 23: Robot Learning Data Flywheel", "frames": [24, 24], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a cycle of four circles:]</span> <strong>Increased Deployments</strong> (improved reliability) -&gt; <strong>More Training Data</strong> (deployment data, suboptimal demos, human corrections) -&gt; <strong>Better Learning</strong> (BC, RL, fine-tuning) -&gt; <strong>More Capable Robots</strong> (multiple embodiments) -&gt; back to Increased Deployments</p>", "text": "[Figure: a cycle of four circles:] Increased Deployments (improved reliability) -> More Training Data (deployment data, suboptimal demos, human corrections) -> Better Learning (BC, RL, fine-tuning) -> More Capable Robots (multiple embodiments) -> back to Increased Deployments", "t": 1801}, {"id": "slide-24-co-training-expert-autonomous-data-frame-25", "num": 24, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 24: Co-Training Expert &amp; Autonomous Data", "title": "Slide 24: Co-Training Expert & Autonomous Data", "frames": [25, 25], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Expert trajectories - Human demos, teleoperation</th>\n<th></th>\n<th>Autonomous rollouts - Self-generated experience</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>✓ High quality</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>✓ Cheap and scalable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✓ On-manifold states</td>\n<td>-&gt; <strong>Distribution mismatch</strong> - state spaces don’t overlap &lt;-</td>\n<td>✓ Covers failure modes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✓ Short, smooth, dense</td>\n<td>-&gt; <strong>Temporal mismatch</strong> - frequency, length, smoothness &lt;-</td>\n<td>✗ Noisy, suboptimal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ Expensive, limited scale</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>✗ Off-manifold states</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ Bounded by human skill</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>✗ Longer, jerky, variable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both mismatches lead to: <strong>New algorithms required</strong> - weighting, alignment, joint learning</p>", "text": "Expert trajectories - Human demos, teleoperation Autonomous rollouts - Self-generated experience ✓ High quality ✓ Cheap and scalable ✓ On-manifold states -> Distribution mismatch - state spaces don’t overlap <- ✓ Covers failure modes ✓ Short, smooth, dense -> Temporal mismatch - frequency, length, smoothness <- ✗ Noisy, suboptimal ✗ Expensive, limited scale ✗ Off-manifold states ✗ Bounded by human skill ✗ Longer, jerky, variable Both mismatches lead to: New algorithms required - weighting, alignment, joint learning", "t": 1920}, {"id": "slide-25-lifelong-learning-frame-26", "num": 25, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 25: Lifelong Learning", "title": "Slide 25: Lifelong Learning", "frames": [26, 26], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: performance against time. During pre-training the curve is flat; at “deploy” it splits into <strong>lifelong learning</strong> (green, rising toward high) and <strong>reality today</strong> (dashed, slowly declining toward low); the vertical distance between them is labelled “gap”.]</span></p>\n<p>open problem: how do we get there?</p>", "text": "[Figure: performance against time. During pre-training the curve is flat; at “deploy” it splits into lifelong learning (green, rising toward high) and reality today (dashed, slowly declining toward low); the vertical distance between them is labelled “gap”.] open problem: how do we get there?", "t": 1980}, {"id": "slide-26-rapid-adaptation-in-context-learning-frame-27", "num": 26, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 26: Rapid Adaptation: In-Context Learning", "title": "Slide 26: Rapid Adaptation: In-Context Learning", "frames": [27, 27], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: <strong>Policy failure</strong> (novel task, environment…) branches into three remedies: <strong>Corrective instructions</strong> (language feedback), <strong>Robot demo in context</strong> (same embodiment), <strong>Cross-embodiment demo in context</strong> (human or novel robot); all three lead to <strong>Adapted policy</strong> (no retraining required).]</span></p>\n<p>open problem: few-shot, real-time adaptation</p>", "text": "[Figure: Policy failure (novel task, environment…) branches into three remedies: Corrective instructions (language feedback), Robot demo in context (same embodiment), Cross-embodiment demo in context (human or novel robot); all three lead to Adapted policy (no retraining required).] open problem: few-shot, real-time adaptation", "t": 2101}, {"id": "slide-27-towards-a-unified-model-for-mobile-manipulation-frame-28", "num": 27, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 27: Towards a Unified Model for Mobile Manipulation", "title": "Slide 27: Towards a Unified Model for Mobile Manipulation", "frames": [28, 28], "html": "<p><strong>today</strong>: SLAM + metric map -&gt; Localization stack -&gt; Nav planner -&gt; (hard handoff) -&gt; VLA (manipulation only); Maps are static - world changes, can’t precompute all</p>\n<p><strong>vision</strong> - which env. representation? Metric map, Gaussian splat, Topological map, Scene graph, BEV projection, Walkthrough video (?)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mapless adaptation - reason from current obs + memory</li>\n<li>⚠ context length bottleneck - can’t fit a city map</li>\n<li><strong>Unified model</strong> - implicit state estimation, loop closures, whole body control -&gt; <strong>Navigation</strong> (mobile base) and <strong>Manipulation</strong> (bimanual, dexterous)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Open points along the bottom: <strong>Replace SLAM</strong> (implicit localization) - <strong>Env. representation</strong> (map, video, implicit?) - <strong>Whole body control</strong> (nav + manipulation jointly)</p>", "text": "today : SLAM + metric map -> Localization stack -> Nav planner -> (hard handoff) -> VLA (manipulation only); Maps are static - world changes, can’t precompute all vision - which env. representation? Metric map, Gaussian splat, Topological map, Scene graph, BEV projection, Walkthrough video (?) Mapless adaptation - reason from current obs + memory ⚠ context length bottleneck - can’t fit a city map Unified model - implicit state estimation, loop closures, whole body control -> Navigation (mobile base) and Manipulation (bimanual, dexterous) Open points along the bottom: Replace SLAM (implicit localization) - Env. representation (map, video, implicit?) - Whole body control (nav + manipulation jointly)", "t": 2220}, {"id": "slide-28-multimodal-memories-across-time-space-frame-29", "num": 28, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 28: Multimodal Memories Across Time &amp; Space", "title": "Slide 28: Multimodal Memories Across Time & Space", "frames": [29, 29], "html": "<p>what gets stored? video, depth, language, touch, audio, proprio, bboxes, …</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Short-horizon memory (seconds)</th>\n<th>Long-horizon memory (minutes - hours)</th>\n<th>Lifelong memory (months - years)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>dense video, recent frames</td>\n<td>language, semantic events</td>\n<td>episodic + semantic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>occlusions, grasp correction</td>\n<td>task progress, steps done</td>\n<td>smarter over deployment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>high token cost</td>\n<td>loses spatial detail</td>\n<td>storage + retrieval at scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>doesn’t scale to minutes</td>\n<td>when to write, what to forget?</td>\n<td>staleness, consolidation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All three feed <strong>cross-modal alignment</strong> (joint reasoning + retrieval across all modalities and timescales) -&gt; <strong>Unified multi-scale memory</strong> (video + language + touch + …)</p>\n<p>open problem: store, align, retrieve, and forget across modalities, tasks, and a lifetime of deployment</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide 29 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 28 to 30.]</span></p>", "text": "what gets stored? video, depth, language, touch, audio, proprio, bboxes, … Short-horizon memory (seconds) Long-horizon memory (minutes - hours) Lifelong memory (months - years) dense video, recent frames language, semantic events episodic + semantic occlusions, grasp correction task progress, steps done smarter over deployment high token cost loses spatial detail storage + retrieval at scale doesn’t scale to minutes when to write, what to forget? staleness, consolidation All three feed cross-modal alignment (joint reasoning + retrieval across all modalities and timescales) -> Unified multi-scale memory (video + language + touch + …) open problem: store, align, retrieve, and forget across modalities, tasks, and a lifetime of deployment [Note: slide 29 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 28 to 30.]", "t": 2341}, {"id": "slide-30-first-steps-autonomous-improvement-frame-30", "num": 30, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 30: First Steps: Autonomous Improvement", "title": "Slide 30: First Steps: Autonomous Improvement", "frames": [30, 30], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Leverage foundation models to enable autonomous improvement without human interventions</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: an <strong>Offline Dataset</strong> and an <strong>Online Dataset</strong> train the <strong>Robot Policy</strong>, which is deployed on Robots 1-5 (kitchen scenes) for “Continuous Improvement”. A <strong>Task Proposals</strong> foundation model suggests tasks to the robots, and a <strong>Reward Detector</strong> foundation model scores their attempts, feeding “Autonomous Data Collection” back into the online dataset.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Autonomous Improvement of Instruction Following Skills via Foundation Models</em>, Zhou*, Atreya*, Lee, Walke, <strong>Mees</strong>, Levine. CoRL, 2024.</p>", "text": "Leverage foundation models to enable autonomous improvement without human interventions [Figure: an Offline Dataset and an Online Dataset train the Robot Policy , which is deployed on Robots 1-5 (kitchen scenes) for “Continuous Improvement”. A Task Proposals foundation model suggests tasks to the robots, and a Reward Detector foundation model scores their attempts, feeding “Autonomous Data Collection” back into the online dataset.] Reference: Autonomous Improvement of Instruction Following Skills via Foundation Models , Zhou*, Atreya*, Lee, Walke, Mees , Levine. CoRL, 2024.", "t": 2400}, {"id": "slide-31-first-steps-adaptation-via-human-feedback-frame-31", "num": 31, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 31: First Steps: Adaptation via Human Feedback", "title": "Slide 31: First Steps: Adaptation via Human Feedback", "frames": [31, 31], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Leverage <strong>verbal</strong> or <strong>visual cues from humans</strong> to <strong>adapt</strong> to new tasks</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure, left: PALO. For a new task (“put the turnip in the drawer”), “PALO: Policy Adaptation via Language Optimization” uses a VLM to search in language space for a task decomposition from <strong>only 5 demos</strong>, while “Policy Finetuning in parameter space” needs <strong>&gt; 100 Demostrations</strong> (crossed out). A plot of success rate against number of demonstrations: PALO is near 0.8 from about 10 demos; finetuning climbs slowly to about 0.65 at 60.]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Policy Adaptation via Language Optimization: Decomposing Tasks for Few-Shot Imitation</em>, Myers*, Zheng*, <strong>Mees</strong> et al. CoRL 2024.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure, right: the PR2 dialogue from week 3, slide 15 (“Fetch the round yellow thing” / “Do you mean the lemon in the middle?” / “Yes” / “Place it left of the object on the bottom”).]</span></p>\n<p>Reference: <em>Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language</em>, <strong>Mees,</strong> Burgard ISER 2021.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: week 3 cites the same paper as ISER 2020; this slide says ISER 2021. Both are defensible: it was presented at ISER 2020 and the Springer proceedings came out in 2021.]</span></p>", "text": "Leverage verbal or visual cues from humans to adapt to new tasks [Figure, left: PALO. For a new task (“put the turnip in the drawer”), “PALO: Policy Adaptation via Language Optimization” uses a VLM to search in language space for a task decomposition from only 5 demos , while “Policy Finetuning in parameter space” needs > 100 Demostrations (crossed out). A plot of success rate against number of demonstrations: PALO is near 0.8 from about 10 demos; finetuning climbs slowly to about 0.65 at 60.] Reference: Policy Adaptation via Language Optimization: Decomposing Tasks for Few-Shot Imitation , Myers*, Zheng*, Mees et al. CoRL 2024. [Figure, right: the PR2 dialogue from week 3, slide 15 (“Fetch the round yellow thing” / “Do you mean the lemon in the middle?” / “Yes” / “Place it left of the object on the bottom”).] Reference: Composing Pick-and-Place Tasks By Grounding Language , Mees, Burgard ISER 2021. [Note: week 3 cites the same paper as ISER 2020; this slide says ISER 2021. Both are defensible: it was presented at ISER 2020 and the Springer proceedings came out in 2021.]", "t": 2460}, {"id": "slide-32-ingredients-for-embodied-intelligence-frame-32", "num": 32, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 32: Ingredients for Embodied Intelligence", "title": "Slide 32: Ingredients for Embodied Intelligence", "frames": [32, 32], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Four boxes:]</span> Intelligent Embodied Reasoning - Dexterous Mobile Manipulation - Lifelong Learning - Rapid Adaptation</p>", "text": "[Four boxes:] Intelligent Embodied Reasoning - Dexterous Mobile Manipulation - Lifelong Learning - Rapid Adaptation", "t": 2520}, {"id": "slide-33-untitled-transition-frame-33", "num": 33, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 33: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition]</span>", "title": "Slide 33: [untitled: transition]", "frames": [33, 33], "html": "<p><strong>After this course, you have the tools to start advancing the frontier in robot learning!</strong></p>", "text": "After this course, you have the tools to start advancing the frontier in robot learning!", "t": 2581}, {"id": "slide-34-untitled-transition-question-frame-34", "num": 34, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 34: <span class=\"own\">[untitled: transition question]</span>", "title": "Slide 34: [untitled: transition question]", "frames": [34, 34], "html": "<p><strong>But, how to do research in robot learning?</strong></p>", "text": "But, how to do research in robot learning?", "t": 2581}, {"id": "slide-35-the-harsh-reality-of-research-frame-35", "num": 35, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 35: The (Harsh) Reality of Research", "title": "Slide 35: The (Harsh) Reality of Research", "frames": [35, 35], "html": "<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Most research is <strong>incremental</strong> - today’s self-driving cars rely on on 40 years of work: from Pomerleau’s ALVINN in 1986 to the DARPA Challenge to modern end-to-end learning</li>\n<li>Most research ideas never become papers.</li>\n<li>Most papers don’t stand the <strong>test of time</strong>.</li>\n<li>The most impactful ideas are often the simplest, because <strong>simple ideas can be scaled</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: ALVINN appeared at NIPS 1988, with proceedings published in 1989; the slide’s 1986 is earlier than either.]</span></p>", "text": "Most research is incremental - today’s self-driving cars rely on on 40 years of work: from Pomerleau’s ALVINN in 1986 to the DARPA Challenge to modern end-to-end learning Most research ideas never become papers. Most papers don’t stand the test of time . The most impactful ideas are often the simplest, because simple ideas can be scaled [Note: ALVINN appeared at NIPS 1988, with proceedings published in 1989; the slide’s 1986 is earlier than either.]", "t": 2640}, {"id": "slide-36-the-recipe-for-good-research-problems-frames-36-38", "num": 36, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 36: The Recipe for Good Research Problems", "title": "Slide 36: The Recipe for Good Research Problems", "frames": [36, 38], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Needed ingredients:\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>an important problem</li>\n<li>a plan for how to tackle it</li>\n<li><strong>excitement!</strong> - Research requires tons of time and effort. You will be more likely to succeed if you are excited!</li>\n<li>be your own reviewer #2 - What is the most likely reason your idea could fail? If you can answer that honestly and still believe in the idea, proceed</li>\n</ol></li>\n<li>Example ideas:\n<ul>\n<li>Cure cancer (important, but how?)</li>\n<li>Algorithm that improves 1% on Libero benchmark (missing problem)</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>If you are succesfull, <strong>how does it help the community</strong>?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Ingredients 3 and 4 are added one per build (frames 37-38), each replacing the “Example ideas” block with its explanation; the slide numbers run 36-38.]</span></p>", "text": "Needed ingredients: an important problem a plan for how to tackle it excitement! - Research requires tons of time and effort. You will be more likely to succeed if you are excited! be your own reviewer #2 - What is the most likely reason your idea could fail? If you can answer that honestly and still believe in the idea, proceed Example ideas: Cure cancer (important, but how?) Algorithm that improves 1% on Libero benchmark (missing problem) If you are succesfull, how does it help the community ? [Ingredients 3 and 4 are added one per build (frames 37-38), each replacing the “Example ideas” block with its explanation; the slide numbers run 36-38.]", "t": 2881}, {"id": "slide-39-styles-of-research-frame-39", "num": 39, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 39: Styles of Research", "title": "Slide 39: Styles of Research", "frames": [39, 39], "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method-driven</th>\n<th>Problem-driven</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You start with an idea, but need to find the problem for it</td>\n<td>You start with a problem, but need to find the method for it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Video diffusion models just dropped, let me apply it to robot manipulation… somehow”</td>\n<td>“How can I make my VLA work with a novel camera viewpoint?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Both can lead to impactful research, but problem-driven is safer for a PhD student</p>", "text": "Method-driven Problem-driven You start with an idea, but need to find the problem for it You start with a problem, but need to find the method for it “Video diffusion models just dropped, let me apply it to robot manipulation… somehow” “How can I make my VLA work with a novel camera viewpoint? Both can lead to impactful research, but problem-driven is safer for a PhD student", "t": 3001}, {"id": "slide-40-debugging-your-research-frame-40", "num": 40, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 40: Debugging Your Research", "title": "Slide 40: Debugging Your Research", "frames": [40, 40], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Start with something that <strong>should work</strong>, then make it incrementally harder</li>\n<li>Talk to colleagues, authors of papers you are building upon, advisors etc.</li>\n<li>Visualize your model’s data &amp; outputs to understand its behavior</li>\n<li>Revisit your initial assumptions after experiments</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Start with something that should work , then make it incrementally harder Talk to colleagues, authors of papers you are building upon, advisors etc. Visualize your model’s data & outputs to understand its behavior Revisit your initial assumptions after experiments", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-41-share-your-research-frame-41", "num": 41, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 41: Share Your Research", "title": "Slide 41: Share Your Research", "frames": [41, 41], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Sharing your findings is how you <strong>find your community</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Open-source</strong> code &amp; data: the most direct way for your research to be useful to others</li>\n<li>An image is worth a thousand words: polish your figures</li>\n<li><strong>Adapt</strong> to your <strong>audience</strong>: even experts may know nothing about your specific topic</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Sharing your findings is how you find your community Open-source code & data: the most direct way for your research to be useful to others An image is worth a thousand words: polish your figures Adapt to your audience : even experts may know nothing about your specific topic", "t": 3120}, {"id": "slide-42-share-your-research-social-media-frame-42", "num": 42, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 42: Share Your Research <span class=\"own\">[social media]</span>", "title": "Slide 42: Share Your Research [social media]", "frames": [42, 42], "html": "<ul>\n<li>If nobody knows about your research, it didn’t happen!</li>\n<li>Disseminate your research on social media\n<ul>\n<li>Find the line between making people curious about your work</li>\n<li>And overhyping it like this:</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: a large red cross next to the same “ChatGPT moment for robotics is happening tomorrow” post from slide 1 (Jan 7, 2024, 2.4M views).]</span></p>", "text": "If nobody knows about your research, it didn’t happen! Disseminate your research on social media Find the line between making people curious about your work And overhyping it like this: [Figure: a large red cross next to the same “ChatGPT moment for robotics is happening tomorrow” post from slide 1 (Jan 7, 2024, 2.4M views).]", "t": 3180}, {"id": "slide-43-personal-backstory-frame-43", "num": 43, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 43: Personal Backstory", "title": "Slide 43: Personal Backstory", "frames": [43, 43], "html": "<p>Early PhD: frustrated with thousand ROS nodes, disjoint models and errors accumulating through a complex pipeline</p>\n<p>Fun fact: robot broke a week before the deadline</p>\n<p>-&gt; <span class=\"own\">[arrow]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can I ditch ROS and learn everything (motor skills+ language) end2end for a cool end of PhD demo?</li>\n<li>What would it take?</li>\n<li>Wrote a proposal that funded me for the next PhD years</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: the three-panel PR2 language-grounding figure from week 3, slide 15.]</span></p>", "text": "Early PhD: frustrated with thousand ROS nodes, disjoint models and errors accumulating through a complex pipeline Fun fact: robot broke a week before the deadline -> [arrow] Can I ditch ROS and learn everything (motor skills+ language) end2end for a cool end of PhD demo? What would it take? Wrote a proposal that funded me for the next PhD years [Figure: the three-panel PR2 language-grounding figure from week 3, slide 15.]", "t": 3301}, {"id": "slide-44-personal-backstory-continued-frame-44", "num": 44, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 44: Personal Backstory <span class=\"own\">[continued]</span>", "title": "Slide 44: Personal Backstory [continued]", "frames": [44, 44], "html": "<p>Convinced my advisor to buy me a Franka robot, spent 1 year setting it up with a custom made table, VR control etc.</p>\n<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: two photos of a Franka robot arm at a custom wooden desk setup (the one used for the play-data work in week 3), one with a person operating it.]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"own note\">[Note: slide numbers 45-47 do not appear in the captured frames; the next frame shows no slide number, and the numbering resumes at 48.]</span></p>", "text": "Convinced my advisor to buy me a Franka robot, spent 1 year setting it up with a custom made table, VR control etc. [Figure: two photos of a Franka robot arm at a custom wooden desk setup (the one used for the play-data work in week 3), one with a person operating it.] [Note: slide numbers 45-47 do not appear in the captured frames; the next frame shows no slide number, and the numbering resumes at 48.]", "t": 3360}, {"id": "untitled-no-slide-number-closing-message-frame-45", "num": null, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "<span class=\"own\">[untitled, no slide number: closing message]</span>", "title": "[untitled, no slide number: closing message]", "frames": [45, 45], "html": "<p><strong>This was my last lecture of the course!</strong></p>\n<p>Thank you for your patience, this was a new course and your mid-term feedback was very helpful.</p>\n<p><strong>Teaching this has been an absolute privilege</strong>, even while juggling two full-time jobs.</p>\n<p>I am looking forward to seeing your projects on demo day!</p>", "text": "This was my last lecture of the course! Thank you for your patience, this was a new course and your mid-term feedback was very helpful. Teaching this has been an absolute privilege , even while juggling two full-time jobs. I am looking forward to seeing your projects on demo day!", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-48-huge-thanks-to-the-teaching-assistants-frame-46", "num": 48, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 48: Huge Thanks to the Teaching Assistants", "title": "Slide 48: Huge Thanks to the Teaching Assistants", "frames": [46, 46], "html": "<p><span class=\"own\">[Figure: group photo of the teaching assistants on a rooftop terrace, with the ETH Zurich main building dome and the lake in the background.]</span></p>\n<p>This course would not have been possible without them</p>", "text": "[Figure: group photo of the teaching assistants on a rooftop terrace, with the ETH Zurich main building dome and the lake in the background.] This course would not have been possible without them", "t": 3601}, {"id": "slide-49-references-frame-47", "num": 49, "kind": "slide", "title_html": "Slide 49: References", "title": "Slide 49: References", "frames": [47, 47], "html": "<ul>\n<li>Uni Freiburg, Deep Learning Lab</li>\n<li>UC Berkeley Deep RL</li>\n<li>Stanford University, Deep RL</li>\n<li>Cornell Robot Learning</li>\n</ul>", "text": "Uni Freiburg, Deep Learning Lab UC Berkeley Deep RL Stanford University, Deep RL Cornell Robot Learning", "t": 3601}]}, "notes": [{"slide": "title-frame-1", "label": "Title", "html": "the slide says “Lecture 12”, but the course page and the YouTube title list this as Lecture 11 (week 11, May 11)."}, {"slide": "slide-2-how-autonomous-robots-usually-generalize-frame-3", "label": "Slide 2: How Autonomous Robots (Usually) Generalize", "html": "slide 3 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 2 to 4."}, {"slide": "slide-17-giving-vlas-senses-they-were-never-trained-on-frame-19", "label": "Slide 17: Giving VLAs Senses They Were Never Trained On", "html": "slide 18 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 17 to 19."}, {"slide": "slide-28-multimodal-memories-across-time-space-frame-29", "label": "Slide 28: Multimodal Memories Across Time & Space", "html": "slide 29 does not appear in the captured frames; the numbering jumps from 28 to 30."}, {"slide": "slide-31-first-steps-adaptation-via-human-feedback-frame-31", "label": "Slide 31: First Steps: Adaptation via Human Feedback", "html": "week 3 cites the same paper as ISER 2020; this slide says ISER 2021. Both are defensible: it was presented at ISER 2020 and the Springer proceedings came out in 2021."}, {"slide": "slide-35-the-harsh-reality-of-research-frame-35", "label": "Slide 35: The (Harsh) Reality of Research", "html": "ALVINN appeared at NIPS 1988, with proceedings published in 1989; the slide’s 1986 is earlier than either."}, {"slide": "slide-44-personal-backstory-continued-frame-44", "label": "Slide 44: Personal Backstory [continued]", "html": "slide numbers 45-47 do not appear in the captured frames; the next frame shows no slide number, and the numbering resumes at 48."}]}, {"key": "12", "dir": null, "date": "Mon 18 May 2026", "topic": "Guest Lecture: Dieter Fox", "summary": "Guest lecture by Dieter Fox (University of Washington and AI2). No recording was uploaded, so there is nothing to browse for this week.", "key_ideas": [], "papers": [], "homework": [], "lecture": null, "guests": [], "slides": null, "notes": [], "placeholder": true}], "homework": [{"key": "hw1_pytorch_tutorial", "label": "HW1 - PyTorch & NumPy", "due": "March 5", "html": "<h1 id=\"homework-1-pytorch-tutorial\">Homework 1: Pytorch tutorial</h1>\n<p><strong>Due Date: 05.03.26 23:59 CET</strong> <strong>Needs to be solved individually. Gradescope checks for duplicate code.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"setup-uv-jupyter\">Setup (uv) + Jupyter</h2>\n<h3 id=\"create-a-virtual-environment\">1) Create a virtual environment</h3>\n<p>You may use any package manager. We demonstrate the setup with <code>uv</code>, an extremely fast Python package installer and manager: https://github.com/astral-sh/uv.</p>\n<p>From the repo root:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># Create a virtual environment with Python 3.12</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">uv</span> venv <span class=\"at\">--python</span> 3.12</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># Activate the environment</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># On macOS/Linux:</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"bu\">source</span> .venv/bin/activate</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># On Windows:</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># .venv\\Scripts\\activate</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"co\"># Install dependencies</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">uv</span> pip install torch torchvision jupyter</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>For an introduction on how to use jupyter notebooks, you may check this ressource: https://docs.jupyter.org/en/latest/</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2>\n<p>All exercises live in <code>src/</code>. Most of these exercises can be solved in very few/one line of code, you’re free to use all of torch unless otherwise specified. Usually functions shouldn’t change the dtype or device of a tensor. If you receive a dtype or device make sure to use it for your returned tensors.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-1-tensor-basics\">Exercise 1: Tensor basics</h3>\n<p>Start with <strong>Exercise 1</strong> here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>src/ex1_tensor_basics/ex1.ipynb</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Open the notebook, read the short explanations, and <strong>fill in all <code>TODO</code>s</strong> in the provided functions.<br />\nPlease <strong>do not change function names or signatures</strong>, since the autograder imports these functions directly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-pytorch-core\">Exercise 2: PyTorch core</h3>\n<p>Continue on with <strong>Exercise 2</strong> here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>src/ex2_pytorch_core/ex2.ipynb</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Again: Open the notebook, read the short explanations, and <strong>fill in all <code>TODO</code>s</strong> in the provided functions. Please <strong>do not change function names or signatures</strong>, since the autograder imports these functions directly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-3-neural-networks\">Exercise 3: Neural networks</h3>\n<p>Then complete <strong>Exercise 3</strong> here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>src/ex3_neural_networks/ex3.ipynb</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Again: Open the notebook, read the short explanations, and <strong>fill in all <code>TODO</code>s</strong> in the provided functions. Please <strong>do not change function names or signatures</strong>, since the autograder imports these functions directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Important</strong>: The MNIST classification exercise in ex3 has the additional deliverable of adding a plot of your training and test loss curves to your PowerPoint/slides that you’re presenting in your video submission (more details on the video under <strong>2. Assignment submission</strong>). Do not forget to do that! You’re welcome to use any plotting utility of your choice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-4-glu\">Exercise 4: GLU</h3>\n<p>Finally complete <strong>Exercise 4</strong> here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>src/ex4_glu/ex4.ipynb</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>For this exercise you will need to read the GLU paper that we provide in the exercise description in the notebook and we encourage you to have a look at the ViT paper that we also provide in that description. Then <strong>fill in all <code>TODO</code>s</strong> in the provided functions to complete this exercise. <strong>NOTE</strong>: In this exercise we additionally encourage you to think about reproducibility of your results and whether these results are statistically significant. We want you to touch on these points quickly in the video submission. Again, you’re welcome to use any plotting utility of your choice for plotting the results. Please <strong>do not change function names or signatures</strong>, since the autograder imports these functions directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Important</strong>: The GLU exercise has the additional deliverable of adding your results and discussion to the video submission that you will upload. Please read all instructions carefully to not forget about this.</p>\n<h2 id=\"submission\">Submission</h2>\n<h3 id=\"gradescope-registration\">1. Gradescope Registration</h3>\n<p>To submit your work, you must be enrolled in the course on Gradescope.</p>\n<p>You should’ve received an email with gradescope details. Please follow the instructions in the email.</p>\n<p>If you didn’t get an email for gradescope (e.g. you’re late enrolled to this course) write Alexey an email: agavryushin@ethz.ch</p>\n<h3 id=\"assignment-submission\">2. Assignment Submission</h3>\n<p>You will submit <strong>all deliverables</strong> (Code and Video) to the single assignment named <strong>“Homework 1”</strong>.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Navigate to the course page on Gradescope.</li>\n<li>Click on the assignment <strong>“Homework 1”</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drag and drop (or select) the following files from your computer:\n<ul>\n<li><code>ex1.ipynb</code></li>\n<li><code>ex2.ipynb</code></li>\n<li><code>ex3.ipynb</code></li>\n<li><code>ex4.ipynb</code></li>\n<li>Your video file (<code>.mp4</code>)</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Click <strong>Upload</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Autograding:</strong> A text box will appear showing the autograder’s progress on your code. Wait for it to finish to see your preliminary score for the coding exercises.</li>\n<li><strong>Manual Grading:</strong> The teaching assistants will manually review your video after the deadline. Your final score will be updated to include points for the presentation components. Coding problems and the video each count for 50 of the achievable 100 points for this homework.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"video-submission\">Video submission</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>ex3 and ex4 require you to show and discuss your results.</li>\n<li>To do this we want you to submit a short video of no longer than 1min of you talking about your results in ex3 and ex4 walking us through the results that you created.</li>\n<li>To show your results we encourage you to put plots that you created in ex3 and ex4 into a quick slide deck which you then walk us through on the short video. Please note we won’t grade whether the submission is pretty or not but only whether your reasoning is correct.</li>\n<li>Please make sure your results are visible while explaining (you may use any software to record your screen).</li>\n<li>Please submit the video in <code>.mp4</code> format</li>\n<li>You may focus your time in the video on ex4 and only briefly show that ex3 was completed correctly at the beginning.</li>\n<li>We use the video to additionally grade your understanding of ex4. We look for correct reasoning instead of a clear yes or no answer. Please make sure to find the right arguments for your conclusions.</li>\n<li>If you import utilities for plotting or other things please remove them before submitting to the autograder.</li>\n</ul>\n", "source": "https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/tree/main/hw1_pytorch_tutorial"}, {"key": "hw2_robot_control_mdps", "label": "HW2 - Robot Control & MDPs", "due": "March 12", "html": "<h1 id=\"homework-2-robot-control-and-mdp\">Homework 2: Robot Control and MDP</h1>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Due Date: 12.03.26 23:59 CET</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Needs to be solved individually. Gradescope checks for duplicate code.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Video deliverables have to be uploaded in a video format covering all video requirements of 3 exercises, less than 5:30 min in total, 4:30 min without bonus question</strong></li>\n<li><strong>In case your total video size surpasses 100MB, please upload multiple videos of smaller size to Gradescope.</strong> # Installation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Follow the instructions in the <a href=\"https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/blob/main/hw2_robot_control_mdps/Installation_Guide.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Installation_Guide.md</a> to install the required packages and dependencies.</p>\n<p>The setup was tested on different Linux distributions and Windows, which are addressed in the installation guide. Should you be a user of MacOS, we highly recommend using the computers with Linux distributions readily available in CAB H 56 and CAB H 57. The exercises are tested on CPU setup, so there is no need for GPU access.</p>\n<h1 id=\"general-guidelines-of-answering-theoretical-questions-in-videos\">General Guidelines of Answering Theoretical Questions in Videos</h1>\n<p>The purpose of the theoretical questions is to check your understanding of the topics. Therefore, the answers should be kept succinct, and we will give full scores as long as your answers are correct. There is no need to elaborate the answers extensively and try to cover all scenarios. Exceeding the video limits will reduce your score.</p>\n<h1 id=\"exercise-1-design-keypoints-in-workspace-and-implement-inverse-kinematics\">Exercise 1: Design Keypoints in Workspace and Implement Inverse Kinematics</h1>\n<p>In this exercise, you will design a set of keypoints from the Lemniscate of Bernoulli (infinity sign) in the 3D workspace, and implement Inverse Kinematics to obtain the robot’s joint space in order to let its end-effector track these keypoints.</p>\n<h2 id=\"todos\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation\">Code Implementation</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex1.py</code>: 1. <strong>Generate 2D Lemniscate Keypoints:</strong> Implement the <code>get_lemniscate_keypoint(t, a)</code> function. This function returns either a single keypoint or an array of keypoints on the 2D Lemniscate curve, depending on the time step t. 2. <strong>Generate 3D Keypoint Set:</strong> Build a complete set of 3D keypoints on the Lemniscate curve in <code>build_keypoints(count, width, x_offset, z_offset)</code>, using the previously implemented <code>get_lemniscate_keypoint(t, a)</code>. 3. <strong>Implement Inverse Kinematics:</strong> Implement inverse kinematics using the Damped Least Squares method. Complete all <code>TODOs</code> in <code>ik_track</code> function.</p>\n<p>After you are done, you can test the results of the robot tracking the keypoints using inverse kinematics by running the following:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/inverse_kinematics.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Note that the tracking is done by purely teleporting the joint positions to the output from IK; there is still no control involved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions\">Theoretical questions</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>If you increase the width of the Lemniscate (increasing a), what issue can happen with the robot performing IK?</li>\n<li>What can happen if you change the dt parameter in IK?</li>\n<li>We implemented a simple numerical IK solver. What are the advantages and disadvantages compared to an analytical IK solver?</li>\n<li>What are the limits of our IK solver compared to state-of-the-art IK solvers?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The theoretical questions require only short and direct answers. Each question is expected to have a 1-sentence answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"examples-of-off-the-shelve-ik-solvers\">Examples of off-the-shelve IK solvers</h2>\n<p>https://github.com/stack-of-tasks/pinocchio</p>\n<p>https://github.com/kevinzakka/mink</p>\n<h2 id=\"deliverables\">Deliverables</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Video:</strong> Video (.mp4) of the robot tracking the generated keypoints. <strong>The video length must be less than 2 minutes</strong> including robot motion and theoretical questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your code with filled in TODOs in <code>exercises/ex1.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical questions</strong>. The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h1 id=\"exercise-2-trajectory-generation-and-pid-control\">Exercise 2: Trajectory Generation and PID Control</h1>\n<p>In this exercise, you will learn how to generate waypoint trajectories while controlling the intermediate robot movement. First, you learn how to generate waypoints ensuring smooth movement and subsequently define a control law to follow the trajectory.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-control-pipeline\">The Control Pipeline</h3>\n<p>Your task will be to fill in parts of a pipeline consisting of waypoints generation, Inverse Kinematics (IK) computation, and PID control. The pipeline has the following structure:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Waypoint Generation:</strong> Based on a set of target keypoints, a trajectory of intermediate waypoints is generated using quintic splines time scaling, presented during the lecture.</li>\n<li><strong>Inverse Kinematics (IK):</strong> Implemented in the previous exercise, to map waypoints from work space to joint space.</li>\n<li><strong>PID Control:</strong> The difference between the desired joint angles and the current joint angles (the tracking error) is fed into a PID controller. The controller calculates the appropriate control signal.</li>\n<li><strong>MuJoCo Simulation:</strong> The generated control signal is applied directly to the motors as joint torques via <code>data.ctrl[:]</code>. The physics engine is stepped to simulate the robot’s movement toward the target waypoint.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The whole system can be visualised as follows:</p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│            Trajectory &amp; IK Generation            │\n│  ╭────────────────────────────────────────────╮  │\n│  │ 1. Generate Quintic Spline Waypoints       │  │\n│  │ 2. Solve Inverse Kinematics (target_qpos)  │  │\n│  ╰─────────────────────┬──────────────────────╯  │\n╰────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────╯\n                         │\n      Target Joint Pos   │  Current Joint Pos\n                         ▼\n╭────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────╮\n│               PID Controller                     │\n│                                                  │\n│  ╭──────────────────╮     ╭───────────────────╮  │\n│  │ Compute Error    │     │ Calculate signal  │  │\n│  │(target - current)│     │(Kp*P + Ki*I + Kd*D)│ │\n│  ╰─────────▲────────╯     ╰─────────┬─────────╯  │\n│            │                        │            │\n│            │   ╭────────────────╮   │            │\n│            ╰───┤ Apply Control  │◄──╯            │\n│                │ (data.ctrl[:]) │                │\n│                ╰────────┬───────╯                │\n│                         ▼                        │\n│                ╭────────────────╮                │\n│                │  MuJoCo Sim    │                │\n│                │  (mj_step)     │                │\n│                ╰────────────────╯                │\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────╯</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"todos-1\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation-1\">Code Implementation</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex2.py</code>: 1. <strong>Generate Waypoints:</strong> Implement the <code>generate_quintic_spline_waypoints(start, end, num_points)</code> function. This function takes a normalized time <code>s</code> (from 0 to 1) and returns a smoothed scaling factor using the polynomial. You can find the polynomial needed for this exercise in the second lecture slides. 2. <strong>PID Control:</strong> Implement the calculation of the Proportional (P), Integral (I) and Derivative (D) terms in <code>pid_control(...)</code>. The resulting control signal will be computed according to the formula of PID that can be found in the second lecture slides.</p>\n<p>We already tuned the default gains for you,</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre class=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>KP <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fl\">150.0</span>, KI <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0</span>, KD <span class=\"op\">=</span> <span class=\"fl\">0.01</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>You can test the quintic spline waypoint generation even before implementing the PID controller. For testing the quintic spline waypoint generation, run the following:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb4\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb4-1\"><a href=\"#cb4-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/quintic_spline.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>You can see the robot does not actually move under a control law but merely gets teleported to a new position, as we have not yet defined the PID controller.</p>\n<p>After implementing the PID controller, you can test the whole system by running the following:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb5\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb5-1\"><a href=\"#cb5-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/pid_control.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>A viewer window should pop up showing the robot smoothly moving between several predefined keypoints.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions-1\">Theoretical questions</h3>\n<p>To get a feeling for the choice of the PID gains, you will analyze how their choice influences the behavior of the waypoint tracking. Test different settings of the gains to be able to answer the following: 1. If you keep increasing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_P</annotation></semantics></math>, what issue arises when tracking the waypoints? 2. How does <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_D</annotation></semantics></math> mitigate the effect you saw above when increasing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_P</annotation></semantics></math>? 3. In what scenarios is a non-zero <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_I</annotation></semantics></math> needed for the controller to perform well?</p>\n<p>There is no need to show these behavior changes in the video and you can just write down your answers in the video. Or say them out loud. The theoretical questions require only short and direct answers. Each question is expected to have a 1-sentence answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deliverables-1\">Deliverables</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Video:</strong> Video (.mp4) of the robot moving between the waypoints. <strong>The video length must be less than 2 minutes</strong> including robot motion and theoretical questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your code with filled in TODOs in <code>exercises/ex2.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical questions</strong>. The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h1 id=\"exercise-3-training-a-policy-for-waypoints-tracking\">Exercise 3: Training a Policy for Waypoints Tracking</h1>\n<p>In this exercise, we will get to the simulation of Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) and train a reinforcement learning (RL) policy to track randomly chosen waypoints. Compared to the previous exercise, the pipeline also changed and has the following structure:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Observation:</strong> The environment constructs an observation vector containing the current environment states (robot &amp; target). This is sent to the RL agent.</li>\n<li><strong>Action Prediction:</strong> The RL agent predicts a normalized action in the range <code>[-1, 1]</code> at a control frequency of <strong>10 Hz</strong> (every 0.1 seconds).</li>\n<li><strong>Action Processing:</strong> The environment receives the normalized action and converts it into joint position targets within the robot’s physical limits.</li>\n<li><strong>Mujoco Simulation Step:</strong> These target positions are fed into <code>data.ctrl</code> and are converted to target joint torques internally by MuJoCo (<code>data.ctrl</code>). The Mujoco simulator steps every 0.002s (500 Hz), and it steps 50 times (<code>ctrl_decimation = 50</code>) before the environment requires a new action from the RL controller (10 Hz).</li>\n<li><strong>Reward Calculation:</strong> After each control step (or 50 simulation steps), the new end-effector tracking error is measured, the reward is computed, and new states are returned to the RL agent to repeat the cycle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The whole system can be visualised as follows:</p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n│                  PPO Agent                       │\n│  ╭────────────────────────────────────────────╮  │\n│  │             Policy Network                 │  │\n│  ╰─────────────────────┬──────────────────────╯  │\n╰────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────╯\n                         │\n        Observation      │  Action [-1, 1]\n   (Joints, EE, Target)  │  (10 Hz)\n                         ▼\n╭────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────╮\n│               RL Environment                     │\n│                                                  │\n│  ╭──────────────────╮     ╭───────────────────╮  │\n│  │ Get Observation  │     │ Process Action    │  │\n│  │ (State Vector)   │     │ (Scale to Limits) │  │\n│  ╰─────────▲────────╯     ╰─────────┬─────────╯  │\n│            │                        │            │\n│  ╭─────────┴────────╮     ╭─────────▼─────────╮  │\n│  │ Calculate Reward │     │ Set Action Targets│  │\n│  │ (Dist to Target) │     │ (data.ctrl[:] = a)│  │\n│  ╰─────────▲────────╯     ╰─────────┬─────────╯  │\n│            │                        │            │\n│            │   ╭────────────────╮   │            │\n│            ╰───┤  Control Loop  |◄──╯            |\n|                |    (10 Hz)     │                │\n│                ╰────────┬───────╯                │\n│                         ▼                        │\n│                ╭────────────────╮                │\n│                │     MuJoCo     │                │\n│                │    (500 Hz)    │                │\n│                ╰────────────────╯                │\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────╯</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"todos-2\">TODOs</h2>\n<p>You will now fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex3.py</code>: 1. <strong>Reset Robot:</strong> Implement a function to reset the robot to its default joint positions with some random noise. 2. <strong>Reset Target Position:</strong> Sample and compute a new random 3D target position relative to the robot’s base. 3. <strong>Process Action:</strong> Convert normalized policy actions (in the range <code>[-1, 1]</code>) to target joint positions within given ranges. 4. <strong>Compute Reward:</strong> Calculate the RL reward based on the distance (error) between the end-effector and the target. 5. <strong>Get Observation:</strong> Extract the observation vector from the environment states. Note that you need to perform some coordinate transformations to convert some states from the world frame to the base frame.</p>\n<p>While this is not necessary for the completion of the exercises, if you are interested in the representation of rotation in 3D space used in robotics and the reasoning behind the dimensionality of parameters such as join positions <code>qpos</code> and other parameters in MuJoCo, check out https://eater.net/quaternions.</p>\n<p>Now that you have implemented the environment blocks, let us train the policy with <code>scripts/train.py</code>. Please refer to <code>scripts/train.py</code> for the training arguments; there is no need to modify any arguments besides <code>--max_iterations</code> and <code>--save_checkpt_freq</code>. While the script is running, you can open a new terminal and track the training progress and rewards in real time, running the following from the hw2_so100_tutorial folder:</p>\n<p><code>tensorboard --logdir=logs --port=6006</code></p>\n<p>Then open the TensorBoard at the provided link (http://localhost:6006) in your browser.</p>\n<p>The checkpoints are automatically saved. For evaluation, choose a specific checkpoint in <code>scripts/evaluate_rand_targets.py</code>. For example, run:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb7\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb7-1\"><a href=\"#cb7-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/evaluate_rand_targets.py <span class=\"at\">--load_run</span><span class=\"op\">=</span>1 <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span><span class=\"op\">=</span>500</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>The simulation window will pop up. The robot will perform tracking for 10 episodes (each episode is set to 2 seconds). The final ee_tracking_error will be printed on our terminal, including an average value at the end of the 10 episodes. <strong>Include these print outputs in your video.</strong> The trained policy gets a full score if <strong>Average final EE tracking error &lt; 0.05</strong>. If your policy converges properly, this should be easily achieved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions-bonus\">Theoretical questions (Bonus)</h3>\n<p>Observe the robot’s performance when deploying your trained policy. You can run the following code and compare the performance of your RL policy with the previously implemented PID controller:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb8\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb8-1\"><a href=\"#cb8-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/evaluate_trajectory.py <span class=\"at\">--load_run</span><span class=\"op\">=</span>1 <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span><span class=\"op\">=</span>500</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>What difference can you observe when the robot is tracking the keypoints on the Lemniscate curve? To improve the performance of the RL policy, what changes can you make in the functions in ex3? Modify these functions (you can also change their arguments, and make corresponding changes in <code>env/so100_tracking_env.py</code>). Train another RL policy with your new environments and show the performance in the video, and explain how your changes impact the robot’s performance. You can also make changes to the PPO hyperparameters (gamma, ent_coef, etc.).</p>\n<h2 id=\"deliverables-2\">Deliverables</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Video:</strong> Video (.mp4) of the robot moving between the random targets, including the error printouts on your terminal <strong>(&lt; 30 s)</strong>. If you completed the bonus question, include your answers to the theoretical questions, and performance of the new policy <strong>(&lt; 1 min)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your code with filled in TODOs in <code>exercises/ex3.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonus question</strong>. To get bonus points, the video must include your answers to the theoretical questions, and the code must include your modifications.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"important-concepts\">Important Concepts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>np.clip(a, a_min, a_max)</code>: Clips the values of an array to be within a given range (useful for restricting joint positions).</li>\n<li>In-place Array Modification: When modifying arrays as attributes of a data object (like <code>mujoco.MjData</code>), use slicing (e.g., <code>[:]</code>) to modify the array in-place so the physics engine detects the changes, rather than overwriting the entire array reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"further-resources\">Further Resources</h2>\n<p>In this exercise, we do not expose the inner workings of the full environment classes or the reinforcement learning algorithm. You will become familiar with the applied RL algorithm, Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), in the following lectures alongside other continuous action space and policy gradient methods. However, it is highly recommended that you look through the entire code base to understand how to work with Mujoco, gymnasium, and the PPO algorithm (from stable-baselines3), in order to prepare yourself for future exercises.</p>\n<p>Should you be interested in exploring the setup in depth, here are some further resources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Theoretical background of PPO:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/algorithms/ppo.html\">OpenAI Spinning Up: PPO</a></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Documentation for the used implementation of PPO:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stable-baselines3.readthedocs.io/en/master/modules/ppo.html\">Stable-Baselines3: PPO</a></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Physics Engine Documentation:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://mujoco.readthedocs.io/\">MuJoCo Documentation</a></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><strong>Advanced Environments:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>A more advanced and scalable environment is NVIDIA Isaac Lab, which those working on projects in the laboratories at ETH are probably familiar with: <a href=\"https://isaac-sim.github.io/IsaacLab/\">NVIDIA Isaac Lab Documentation</a></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Further technical details were also not revealed, such as the fact that we are spawning multiple environments in parallel to take advantage of multiprocessing. Fortunately, for the sake of this exercise, the implemented functions act in separate threads, so you do not need to take this parallelism into account; you can assume the functions are operating in a single environment.</p>\n", "source": "https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/tree/main/hw2_robot_control_mdps"}, {"key": "hw2_robot_control_mdps_Installation_Guide", "label": "HW2 - Installation guide", "due": "", "html": "<h1 id=\"hw2-so-100-tutorial-installation-guide-linux-windows\">HW2 SO-100 Tutorial — Installation Guide (Linux &amp; Windows)</h1>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Any virtual environment (conda / uv / venv) is recommended. <strong>venv</strong> is used here as an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Python 3.12</strong> tested and recommended.</li>\n<li><strong>pip</strong> (usually bundled with Python).</li>\n<li><strong>Git</strong> (for downloading the repository).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):</strong> OpenGL / EGL drivers are needed by MuJoCo’s viewer. They should already be installed, but you can explicitly install them with: <code>sudo apt install libegl1-mesa-dev libgl1-mesa-dri libglvnd-dev</code></p>\n<p><strong>For Windows:</strong> Make sure <code>python</code> and <code>pip</code> are available in PowerShell (test with <code>python --version</code>). If not, add Python to your system PATH.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"create-activate-a-virtual-environment\">1. Create &amp; Activate a Virtual Environment</h2>\n<p>From the root directory of the repository: <strong>Linux (Bash):</strong> <code>python3 -m venv mujoco</code> <code>source mujoco/bin/activate</code></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Every time you open a new terminal, re-activate with <code>source mujoco/bin/activate</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Windows (PowerShell):</strong> <code>python -m venv mujoco</code> <code>.\\mujoco\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1</code></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> If you get an execution policy error on Windows, run: <code>Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Installing Virtual Environment in Desktops at CAB H56 &amp; H57:</strong> For those who use the lab computers. The personal drive only has 10 GB sapce. The virtual environment requires 7-8 GB space. A viable solution is to install the venv in the /tmp folder (16 GB) and put your own code in the personal drive. You can write a bash script to install the venv and run it every time you log into a lab computer to restore the venv in .</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"install-the-package-and-dependencies\">2. Install the Package and Dependencies</h2>\n<p>Install the requirements and then the local <code>so101_gym</code> package in editable (development) mode — this automatically pulls in all required libraries:</p>\n<p><code>pip install -r hw2_robot_control_mdps/requirements.txt</code><br />\n<code>pip install -e hw2_robot_control_mdps</code></p>\n<h3 id=\"what-gets-installed\">What Gets Installed</h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 30%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 70%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Package</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mujoco</code></td>\n<td>Physics simulation engine + built-in viewer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gymnasium</code></td>\n<td>RL environment API (used by <code>SO100TrackEnv</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>stable-baselines3</code></td>\n<td>RL algorithms (PPO) for training &amp; evaluation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tensorboard</code></td>\n<td>Training log visualization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"verify-the-installation\">3. Verify the Installation</h2>\n<p>Quick smoke test — this should open the MuJoCo viewer with the SO-100 robot arm:</p>\n<p><strong>Linux &amp; Windows:</strong> <code>python scripts/interactive.py</code></p>\n<p>If a 3D viewer window opens showing the robot, everything is working. You can interact with the environment using: - <strong>Rotate:</strong> Click and drag - <strong>Zoom:</strong> Scroll wheel - <strong>Pan:</strong> Shift + click and drag</p>\n<p>Close the viewer window to exit.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"available-scripts\">4. Available Scripts</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 42%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 31%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Script</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Command</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>scripts/interactive.py</code></td>\n<td>Launch the MuJoCo viewer to inspect the robot</td>\n<td><code>python scripts/interactive.py</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>scripts/train.py</code></td>\n<td>Train a PPO agent (16 parallel envs)</td>\n<td><code>python scripts/train.py</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>scripts/evaluate_rand_targets.py</code></td>\n<td>Evaluate a trained policy in the viewer</td>\n<td><code>python scripts/evaluate_rand_targets.py</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"monitor-training\">5. Monitor Training</h2>\n<p>Either in real-time or after training is finished, you can visualize metrics with TensorBoard:</p>\n<p><strong>Linux &amp; Windows:</strong> <code>tensorboard --logdir logs/ --port 6006</code></p>\n<p>Then open http://localhost:6006 in your browser.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 64%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 35%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'mujoco'</code></td>\n<td>Make sure your venv is activated and you ran <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MuJoCo viewer doesn’t open / EGL errors <em>(Linux)</em></td>\n<td>Install Mesa/EGL drivers: <code>sudo apt install libegl1-mesa-dev libgl1-mesa-dri libglvnd-dev</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ERROR: could not create window</code> <em>(Linux)</em></td>\n<td><code>export LD_PRELOAD=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libstdc++.so.6</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Viewer window is black <em>(Windows)</em></td>\n<td>Update your system GPU drivers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'env'</code></td>\n<td>Run scripts from the <code>hw2_so100_tutorial/</code> directory, or make sure <code>PYTHONPATH=.</code> is set. Also check you ran <code>pip install -e .</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>python</code> not found <em>(Windows)</em></td>\n<td>Use <code>python3</code> instead, or add Python to your system PATH.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pip install</code> fails <em>(Windows)</em></td>\n<td>Make sure the venv is activated (<code>.\\mujoco\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1</code>).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "source": "https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/tree/main/hw2_robot_control_mdps"}, {"key": "hw3_imitation_learning", "label": "HW3 - Imitation Learning", "due": "March 26", "html": "<h1 id=\"imitation-learning\">Imitation Learning</h1>\n<h2 id=\"deadline-26th-march-2026-23.59\"><strong>DEADLINE: 26th March 2026 23.59</strong></h2>\n<h2 id=\"introduction\">Introduction</h2>\n<p>This homework will guide you through the important parts of a modern imitation learning pipeline. You will teleoperate the SO-101 arm, train policies to imitate your expert actions, and achieve high success rates during evaluation. We give you quite a big codebase for this homework and you’re welcome to read through it but you will only have to modify the small parts that we point you to. With this homework we try to give you a good impression of what the workflow for modern research and businesses in this space is like. Please make sure to always be on the most up-to-date version of this repo during the homework period. We will avoid pushing updates as much as possible but might have to in serious cases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"setup\">Setup</h2>\n<p>You may use any package manager. We demonstrate the setup with uv as before:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"bu\">cd</span> hw3_imitation_learning</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">uv</span> venv <span class=\"at\">--python</span> 3.12</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"bu\">source</span> .venv/bin/activate</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">uv</span> pip install <span class=\"at\">-e</span> .</span></code></pre></div>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"tasks\">Tasks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"setup-1\">Setup</h3>\n<p>In this homework you will have to teleoperate a SO101 arm in simulation. To get correctly setup with the teleoperation of this assignment please run <code>python scripts/configure_keys.py</code>. This script will prompt you to assign the keys on your keyboard that you will later use during teleoperation. We have recommendations on which key to use with which direction and which option but you’re mostly free to choose which keys to use. The script will output a <code>keymap.json</code> that will be used throughout the exercise. We tested this on Swiss/US keyboards on MacOS and on Ubuntu. If you’re encountering issues with the key assignment you are allowed to modify the <code>configure_keys.py</code> script or manually edit the keymap.json file if that resolves your issues. The important output of this step is to have a <code>keymap.json</code> that you can use throughtout the exercise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"components\">Components</h3>\n<p>Since we touch many components of a normal imitation learning stack (most of this is implemented for you), here is a quick explanation of what we use: 1. Data storage: We use zarr (https://zarr.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) to store our data, a versatile choice for data management which is used widely. 2. States: The zarr dataset stores raw observations from the simulation. When specifying <code>--state-keys</code> for training you can use any of the keys below (with optional slicing, e.g. <code>\"state_cube[:3]\"</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 56%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Key</th>\n<th>Dim</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_ee_xyz</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>End-effector Cartesian position (x, y, z).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_ee_full</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Full end-effector pose: position (3) + orientation quaternion in wxyz (4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_joints</code></td>\n<td>6 → 5*</td>\n<td>Joint angles for all 6 joints (Rotation, Pitch, Elbow, Wrist_Pitch, Wrist_Roll, Jaw). *During evaluation the Jaw joint is excluded automatically, leaving 5 controllable joints.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_gripper</code></td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Current gripper (Jaw) opening angle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_cube</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Cube free-joint state: position (3) + orientation quaternion wxyz (4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_obstacle</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Obstacle body position (x, y, z). Zero vector when no obstacle is present.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>goal_pos</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Bin centre position (x, y, z). Useful when the bin position is randomised as done in exercise 3.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Multicube-only keys</strong> (available when recording with <code>--multicube</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 56%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Key</th>\n<th>Dim</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>original_pos_cube_red</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Red cube state: position (3) + quaternion wxyz (4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>original_pos_cube_green</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Green cube state: position (3) + quaternion wxyz (4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>original_pos_cube_blue</code></td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Blue cube state: position (3) + quaternion wxyz (4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>state_goal</code></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>One-hot encoding of the target cube colour [red, green, blue].</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ol start=\"3\" type=\"1\">\n<li>Actions: We compute actions as deltas between states such that a_t = s_t+1 - s_t. We implemented full end effector control, xyz-only end effector control and joint angle control for you. For more detailed information you can have a look at <code>compute_actions.py</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-1-mse-policy\">Exercise 1: MSE policy</h3>\n<p>Your task is to train a policy that is able to pick up a cube, move it around an obstacle and place it within a bin using imitation learning. This is a two step process:</p>\n<h4 id=\"data-collection-and-preparation\">Data collection and preparation</h4>\n<p>For data collection in this exercise you should use the <code>record_teleop_demos.py</code> script. You won’t have to change any of the script flags for this exercise. To get to success rates of around 70% you will most likely not have to collect more than 15-20 episodes of data. This will vary depending on your model. To prepare the data for training your policy you will have to run the <code>compute_actions.py</code> script. This script expects you to choose an action space for your policy. In homework 2 you encountered the differences between controlling the joint angles directly vs controlling the end effector position of a robot arm and doing IK. Additionally, it is advisable to simplify the learning problem as much as possible. We encourage you to look at the <code>compute_actions.py</code> script and choose an action space to your liking. The exercises are doable with all action space choices with varying teleoperation effort (roughly: more complex action space = more teleoperation needed.)</p>\n<p><strong>Teleoperation controls</strong> When you’re running the teleoperation script you will have the following controls in addition to the endeffector position of the arm (using the keys you assigned in <code>configure_keys.py</code>):</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 43%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 35%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Recommended Key</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>record</code></td>\n<td>Space</td>\n<td>Toggle recording on/off. Press once to start recording an episode, press again to pause.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>end_episode</code></td>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>End and save the current recorded episode, then reset the environment for the next one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>reset</code></td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>Discard the current episode (if recording) and reset the environment. Use this if you make a mistake during teleoperation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>escape</code></td>\n<td>ESC</td>\n<td>Save any in-progress episode and quit the session.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The on-screen overlay will show the current status (<code>REC</code> / <code>IDLE</code>), the recorded episode count, and a reminder of the key bindings for record, end episode, reset, and quit. Multiple recorded episodes will be stored into one <code>.zarr</code> file when you press <code>escape</code>. Your teleoperation flow should be something like: Press <code>record</code> -&gt; move the arm to pick up the cube and drop it in the bin -&gt; press <code>end_episode</code> to save this episode and reset the environment.</p>\n<p>You will also notice a step counter which indicates each time data is recorded and is rising faster than you will be able to control the arm. This setup is mimicking a real robot teleoperation setup. The only thing you should keep in mind about this step counter is that if you, as teleoperator, do nothing for many seconds during your recording, data will still be recorded. Standing still for many seconds, recording that data and then training on it will usually lead to bad policies because your data distribution in some states is biased towards doing nothing. For usual teleoperation speeds this is neglible in this setting. If you are concerned about your data, write a quick plotting script and inspect it.</p>\n<p><strong>Teleoperation views</strong> You may change the camera viewpoints if you prefer them to be set differently. This will be an easy change in the <code>mujoco</code> file which we leave up to you if you want. To us it has been sufficient to use the camera views we have implemented now.</p>\n<h4 id=\"how-to-teleoperate\">How to teleoperate</h4>\n<p>You may use the keys that you set in <code>configure_keys.py</code> to move the robot. You have set a record key (we encourage you to use the space key for that). Make sure to press this when you want to record an episode. If you realize you made an incorrect move you can discard your episode and reset your environment with the reset key (we encourage R). After you finish an episode you can press the key you set for continuing to the next episode (we encourage enter). For teleoperation, note that no task requires rotating the end effektor around any axis which you can use to your advantage.</p>\n<h4 id=\"policy-training\">Policy training</h4>\n<p>To train a policy you have to finish these TODOs: - Implement all TODOs in <code>train.py</code>. We will not import this in the autograder so you are relatively free in what you do here (e.g. add more CLI flags to your convenienve or similar). We already provide you a strong harness though. - Implement the TODOS of the <code>ObstaclePolicy</code> in <code>model.py</code> - Choose a state and action space to train your policy with. You may set them as CLI arguments when running the <code>train.py</code> script. Make sure to include the obstacle state when training the policy in exercise 1 since this will enable you to reuse your policy in exercise 2. The CLI arguments support slicing so you can run things like this:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/train.py ... <span class=\"at\">--state-keys</span> state_ee_xyz state_gripper <span class=\"st\">&quot;state_cube[:5]&quot;</span> <span class=\"at\">--action-keys</span> action_ee_xyz action_gripper <span class=\"at\">--policy</span> obstacle</span></code></pre></div>\n<h4 id=\"policy-performance-test\">Policy performance test</h4>\n<p>Run the <code>eval.py</code> script pointing the <code>--checkpoint</code> flag at your trained checkpoint. You can visually inspect the output of the policy and also run in <code>--headless</code> mode to see the total success rate across rollouts (much faster than rendering). You may also increase the number of episodes that you test on with <code>--num-episodes</code> (the final grading uses 100).</p>\n<h4 id=\"deliverable\">Deliverable</h4>\n<p>When you’re confident about your model performance you can run</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> student_eval/run_eval <span class=\"at\">--exercise</span> 1 <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;</span>path to your ckpt<span class=\"op\">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This will produce a file named ex1_result.hwresult which you can submit to the autograder on gradescope. Do NOT modify this file as we will verify your performance reported to gradescope using your model definition and checkpoint as well and if we notice tampering we will have to administer a failing grade. For this please also submit your <code>model.py</code> and your best checkpoint <code>.pt</code> file to the autograder. Please name the checkpoint file <code>ex1.pt</code>. Please don’t change the name of the <code>ObstaclePolicy</code> class as this is imported by the autograder. Make sure the policy default construction arguments are also the ones you trained your checkpoint with so we can reproduce your model in our eval suite. NOTE: This assumes that you use an ARM MacOS or Linux system. If you are on another system we encourage you to use WSL, docker or the student PCs for running these eval runs. DO NOT change anything about <code>run_eval.py</code>.</p>\n<h4 id=\"notes-and-tips\">Notes and Tips</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most problems/poor behaviors with your policy are either due to needing more data or more parameters. Less than 1M parameters will be needed to get to 100% SR on ex1 and ex2.</li>\n<li>Make sure to not end episodes manually too early during teleoperation so the action chunks in the end are not cut off.</li>\n<li>Policy eval runs for a maximum of 800 steps for the autograder. Make sure this is not the limitation of your policy.</li>\n<li>For this and all other policies the cube must be dropped into the bin (can no longer be within the gripper claw when reaching the success z threshold). We are aware of edge cases where it’s possible to get to high success rates while showing this behavior. Please make sure your submitted policy doesn’t as we will run an additional check on this during our verification of your results.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-dagger\">Exercise 2: DAgger</h3>\n<p>In this exercise you will use DAgger, which was introduced in the lecture. This algorithm lets you add data when the policy is out of distribution and performs poorly to increase coverage and success rate. We will reuse the same policy that you trained in exercise 1. You can rerun the <code>scripts/eval.py</code> with the <code>--adversarial</code> flag and you will notice that the success rate is much lower. The reason for this is that the obstacle distribution now changed and the policy is forced out of distribution. You should have a look at the policy execution during eval and understand the failure modes of your policy. Then you can run the <code>scripts/dagger_eval.py</code> script which will enable you to collect more expert actions in out of distribution settings by stepping in if the policy has poor performance. The key to jump into policy execution and provide human expert actions is the same as the record key before. You’re encouraged to look at the <code>dagger_eval.py</code> file to see the rest of the keymapping for this task (if you observe failure during a rollout but you were too late to step in you can reset that rollout as well and step in earlier for example). You should then collect more data in the out of distribution settings and retrain your policy from exercise 1. You might have to do this cycle of <code>observe failure mode -&gt; dagger -&gt; compute actions again -&gt; retrain</code> multiple times until your policy coverage is large enough to get a high success rate depending on your policy specification. If you find yourself having to collect significantly more than 25 dagger episodes we encourage you to reconsider your model size and how you teleoperate the episodes.</p>\n<h4 id=\"deliverable-1\">Deliverable</h4>\n<p>When you’re confident about your model performance you can run</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb4\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb4-1\"><a href=\"#cb4-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> student_eval/run_eval <span class=\"at\">--exercise</span> 2 <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;</span>path to your ckpt<span class=\"op\">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This will produce a file named ex2_result.hwresult which you can submit to the autograder on gradescope. Do NOT modify this file as we will verify your performance reported to gradescope on our side as well. Submit your <code>model.py</code> and your best checkpoint <code>.pt</code> file to the autograder. Please name the checkpoint file <code>ex2.pt</code>. Please don’t change the name of the <code>ObstaclePolicy</code> class as this is imported by the autograder.</p>\n<h4 id=\"tips-and-notes\">Tips and Notes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the states (and actions) that you record will be written into <code>.zarr</code> files as usually done during DAgger. When you run <code>compute_actions.py</code> it will pull all teleoperation episodes from <code>datasets/raw/single_cube/teleop/</code> and the new dagger episodes <code>datasets/raw/single_cube/dagger/</code> and combine them into one processed <code>.zarr</code> file.</li>\n<li>The dagger step you will see on your screen is basically the same as in ex1. You don’t have to worry about it but try not to stand still for many seconds.</li>\n<li>Depending on how easy this exercise is for you, you might or might not see why DAgger can be a very useful tool but very ressource-intensive in practice as well</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-3-competition-multicube-goal-conditioned-imitation-learning\">Exercise 3 (Competition!): Multicube Goal-Conditioned Imitation Learning</h3>\n<p>In this exercise, you train a goal-conditioned policy that can solve multiple tasks using the same policy. Instead of learning a single behavior, the robot receives task information as input and must act accordingly. There is a leaderboard on gradescope.</p>\n<h4 id=\"task-description\">Task Description</h4>\n<p>The multicube environment contains three cubes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Red</li>\n<li>Green</li>\n<li>Blue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the start of each episode:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>cube positions are randomized</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>bin position is randomized</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A target cube color is specified for the episode. The robot must pick up the correct cube and place it into the bin. The policy is goal-conditioned, meaning it receives additional inputs that describe the task objective. This is the application of what we treated in lecture 3 on imitation learning.</p>\n<h4 id=\"data-collection\">Data Collection</h4>\n<p>First, record demonstrations using teleoperation as usual. You will need this additional flag:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb5\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb5-1\"><a href=\"#cb5-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/record_teleop_demos.py <span class=\"at\">--multicube</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>During recording, the following additional goal information is stored:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>state_goal</code> — one-hot encoding of the target cube color</li>\n<li><code>goal_pos</code> — bin center position</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"model\">Model</h4>\n<p>Here you are free to implement a new model under <code>MultiTaskPolicy</code>. You may choose the same policy as you did earler or implement a new one entirely. We don’t really give you any restrictions apart from not importing any additional libraries and not accepting excessively large models.</p>\n<h4 id=\"training\">Training</h4>\n<p>When training on the multicube dataset, the policy must receive all goal-conditioning inputs.</p>\n<p>Run <code>train.py</code> with state keys that include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>original_pos_cube_red</code></li>\n<li><code>original_pos_cube_green</code></li>\n<li><code>original_pos_cube_blue</code></li>\n<li><code>state_goal</code><br />\n</li>\n<li><code>goal_pos</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You may choose any slicing method as before to again simplify the learning problem.</p>\n<h4 id=\"evaluation\">Evaluation</h4>\n<p>Evaluate a trained policy with:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb6\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb6-1\"><a href=\"#cb6-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval.py <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;</span>path_to_checkpoint.pt<span class=\"op\">&gt;</span> --multicube</span></code></pre></div>\n<h4 id=\"difficulty\">Difficulty</h4>\n<p>This multicube goal-conditioned problem is <strong>significantly harder</strong> than the previous exercises. We consider an eval SR approaching 50% to be a very strong performance. We don’t expect you to again reach 90% or similarily high SR. Additionally, at this point you are probably aware that IL scales well with the amount of data trained on. Since half of this ex3 score is a video submission that grades your approach, please don’t just spend 10h teleoperating to increase your score. We encourage you to think of more clever ways to improve SR (we have already encountered a few).</p>\n<h4 id=\"deliverable-2\">Deliverable</h4>\n<p>When you’re confident about your model performance you can run</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb7\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb7-1\"><a href=\"#cb7-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> student_eval/run_eval <span class=\"at\">--exercise</span> 3 <span class=\"at\">--checkpoint</span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;</span>path to your ckpt<span class=\"op\">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This will produce a file named ex3_result.hwresult which you can submit to the autograder on gradescope. Do NOT modify this file as we will verify your performance reported to gradescope on our side as well. Submit your <code>model.py</code> and your best checkpoint <code>.pt</code> file to the autograder. Please name the checkpoint file <code>ex3.pt</code>. Please don’t change the name of the <code>MultiTaskPolicy</code> class as this is imported by the autograder. Make sure there is no mismatch between the default init of the class and the checkpoint you submitted.</p>\n<p><strong>Video submission</strong> In ex.3 we additionally require you to submit a video in <code>.mp4</code> format. This video should again be no longer than 60s. What we want you to mention in the video: - Your approach to ex.3 (we will grade originality and how sensible your idea is in his setting) - How the implementation of your idea went (if it went well, why? if it didn’t why?) - How much data you used and to what final SR you get with your approach</p>\n<h2 id=\"grading\">Grading</h2>\n<p>For ex1 and ex2 we grade you from 0-100 points depending on your policy’s performance. The scoring thresholds are: &gt;=85% -&gt; 100 pts &gt;=75% -&gt; 80 pts &gt;=65% -&gt; 60 pts &gt;=55% -&gt; 40 pts &gt;=45% -&gt; 20 pts &lt;45% -&gt; 0 pts For ex3 a total of 200 points can be reached. We give 100 points for the success rate of the policy, where 1% SR equals 1 point. Additionally, we want you to submit a short video as outlined under <strong>video submission</strong>. It’s not required to reach 100% success rate on exercise 3. You won’t have to achieve 400/400 points to get a 6 on this homework.</p>\n<hr />\n", "source": "https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/tree/main/hw3_imitation_learning"}, {"key": "hw4_reinforcement_learning", "label": "HW4 - Reinforcement Learning", "due": "April 16", "html": "<h1 id=\"homework-4-reinforcement-learning\">Homework 4: Reinforcement Learning</h1>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Due Date: 16.04.26 23:59 CET</strong><br />\n</li>\n<li><strong>Needs to be solved individually. Plagiarism checks will be performed.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"installation\">Installation</h3>\n<p>Use one clean virtual environment for all exercises. The setup was tested on Linux distributions and Windows with Python 3.12. You can also use the Linux computers in CAB H 56 / CAB H 57.</p>\n<h3 id=\"create-and-activate-a-virtual-environment\">1) Create and activate a virtual environment</h3>\n<p>From the repository root:</p>\n<p><strong>Linux / macOS</strong></p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"bu\">cd</span> hw4_reinforcement_learning</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python3.12</span> <span class=\"at\">-m</span> venv .venv</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"bu\">source</span> .venv/bin/activate</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Windows (PowerShell)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre class=\"sourceCode powershell\"><code class=\"sourceCode powershell\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"fu\">cd</span> hw4_reinforcement_learning</span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>python <span class=\"op\">-</span>m venv <span class=\"op\">.</span><span class=\"fu\">venv</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"op\">.</span>\\<span class=\"op\">.</span><span class=\"fu\">venv</span>\\Scripts\\Activate<span class=\"op\">.</span><span class=\"fu\">ps1</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"install-dependencies\">2) Install dependencies</h3>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">pip</span> install <span class=\"at\">-r</span> requirements.txt</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"run-smoke-tests\">3) Run smoke tests</h3>\n<p><strong>Smoke test A (ex1/ex2 stack: GridWorld + CartPole)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb4\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb4-1\"><a href=\"#cb4-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> <span class=\"at\">-c</span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;from envs.grid_world import CliffWalkingEnv; from envs.cartpole_wrapper import CartPoleWrapper; g=CliffWalkingEnv(); c=CartPoleWrapper(seed=0); s=c.reset(); ns,r,d,i=c.step(c.sample_action()); c.close(); print(&#39;OK ex1/ex2:&#39;, g.n_states, s.shape, type(r).__name__)&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Smoke test B (ex3/ex4 stack: MuJoCo + SO100)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb5\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb5-1\"><a href=\"#cb5-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> <span class=\"at\">-c</span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;from pathlib import Path; import numpy as np; from envs.so100_rl_env import SO100RLEnv; env=SO100RLEnv(xml_path=Path(&#39;assets/mujoco/so100_pos_ctrl.xml&#39;).resolve(), render_mode=None); obs,_=env.reset(seed=0); obs2,reward,term,trunc,info=env.step(np.zeros(env.action_dim, dtype=np.float32)); env.close(); print(&#39;OK ex3/ex4:&#39;, obs.shape, float(reward))&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>If both commands print <code>OK ...</code>, the installation is working.</p>\n<h3 id=\"general-guidelines\">General Guidelines</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete the <strong>TODOs</strong> in the provided code.</li>\n<li>Do <strong>not modify function signatures</strong> unless explicitly stated.</li>\n<li>Use the scripts in the <code>scripts/</code> folder to train and test your implementation.</li>\n<li>Results (plots, logs) will be saved in the <code>logs/</code> directory.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep your code clean and readable. Make sure everything runs before submission.</p>\n<h3 id=\"video-guidelines\">Video Guidelines</h3>\n<p>The purpose of the video is to demonstrate your understanding of the implemented algorithms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep explanations <strong>clear and concise</strong></li>\n<li>Focus on <strong>key ideas and results</strong></li>\n<li>Exceeding the time limit (5 min) may result in a grade penalty.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"grading\">Grading</h3>\n<p>The maximum number of points that can be achieved per exercise: - <strong>Exercise 1: MDP</strong>: 10 points - <strong>Exercise 2: DQN</strong>: 10 points - <strong>Exercise 3: PPO</strong>: 15 points - <strong>Exercise 4: SAC</strong>: 15 points - <strong>Video Submission</strong>: 50 points</p>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-1-dynamic-programming-in-gridworld\">Exercise 1: Dynamic Programming in GridWorld</h2>\n<p>In this exercise, you will implement <strong>policy iteration</strong> and <strong>value iteration</strong> on a tabular MDP using the <strong>Cliff Walking</strong> environment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cliff-walking-environment\">Cliff Walking Environment</h3>\n<p>The Cliff Walking environment is a 2D gridworld where the agent starts at the bottom-left corner and must reach the goal at the bottom-right while avoiding the cliff. The environment is <strong>stochastic</strong>: when the agent selects an action (up, down, left, right), it is executed as intended with probability <code>1 - slip_chance</code> and with probability <code>slip_chance</code>, a different action is executed uniformly at random (i.e., the agent may “slip”). Moving beyond the grid boundary leaves the agent in the same state. Each step yields a reward of -1, falling into the cliff gives -100 and terminates the episode, and reaching the goal also ends the episode.</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/CliffWalking.png\" width=\"500\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<em>Figure 1-1: Cliff Walking Environment (AI-generated)</em>\n</p>\n<h3 id=\"algorithms\">Algorithms</h3>\n<p>In this exercise, you will implement two classical dynamic programming algorithms: <strong>Policy Iteration</strong> and <strong>Value Iteration</strong>.</p>\n<p>We provide the pseudocode for both algorithms below.</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/policy_iteration.png\" width=\"500\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<em>Figure 1-2: Policy Iteration</em>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/value_iteration.png\" width=\"500\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<em>Figure 1-3: Value Iteration</em>\n</p>\n<p>Both methods should converge to the optimal policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"todos\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation\">Code Implementation</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex1_mdp.py</code>:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Policy Iteration:</strong> Implement <strong>policy evaluation</strong>, <strong>policy improvement</strong> and <strong>policy iteration loop</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Value Iteration:</strong> Implement <strong>value iteration update</strong> and <strong>greedy policy extraction</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For a detailed implementation and explanation of the GridWorld environment, see <code>envs/grid_world.py</code>. After implementing the TODOs, you can test the results of your algorithms by running the following:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb6\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb6-1\"><a href=\"#cb6-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/run_policy_iteration.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb7\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb7-1\"><a href=\"#cb7-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/run_value_iteration.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Results will be saved in <code>logs/mdp/</code></p>\n<p>You should see <strong>state value</strong> visualization and <strong>optimal policy</strong> visualization.</p>\n<h3 id=\"effects-of-stochasticity\">Effects of Stochasticity</h3>\n<p>In this environment, actions may not always be executed as intended due to slipping. To study its effect, run experiments with different values of <code>slip_chance</code> by setting the argument <code>--slip_chance</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions\">Theoretical Questions</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What is the difference between policy iteration and value iteration in terms of their update procedures?</li>\n<li>What happens if the discount factor <code>gamma</code> is close to 0 or 1?</li>\n<li>How does increasing the slip probability (<code>slip_chance</code>) affect the optimal policy?\n<ul>\n<li>Compare the cases <code>slip_chance = 0.0</code>, <code>0.01</code>, and <code>0.2</code>.</li>\n<li>Why does the agent tend to behave more conservatively as stochasticity increases?</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"deliverables\">Deliverables</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your code with filled in TODOs in <code>exercises/ex1_mdp.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical questions:</strong> The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Images:</strong> Visualization of the state value function and optimal policy obtained by running <code>scripts/run_policy_iteration.py</code> and <code>scripts/run_value_iteration.py</code> with <code>slip_chance</code> set to <code>0</code>, <code>0.01</code>, and <code>0.2</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-2-deep-q-network-dqn-on-cartpole\">Exercise 2: Deep Q-Network (DQN) on CartPole</h2>\n<p>In this exercise, you will implement <strong>Deep Q-Network (DQN)</strong> to solve a control problem with <strong>continuous state space</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-dqn\">Why DQN?</h3>\n<p>In previous exercises, we used tabular RL, where a table stores the value of each state-action pair. However, this approach becomes infeasible when the state space is large or continuous.</p>\n<p>To address this, we use <strong>function approximation</strong> to estimate the Q-values. DQN replaces the table with a neural network, allowing us to handle <strong>continuous state spaces with discrete actions</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cartpole-environment\">CartPole Environment</h3>\n<p>The CartPole environment is a classic control problem where a pole is attached to a cart moving along a track. The goal is to keep the pole upright by applying forces to the cart.</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/gifs/cartpole.gif\" width=\"500\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<em>Figure 2-1: CartPole Environment</em>\n</p>\n<p>At each timestep, the agent receives a <strong>4-dimensional continuous state</strong> and selects a <strong>discrete action</strong>.</p>\n<h4 id=\"state-space\">State Space</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Cart position</td>\n<td>[-2.4, 2.4]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Cart velocity</td>\n<td>(-inf, inf)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Pole angle</td>\n<td>~[-41.8°, 41.8°]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Pole angular velocity</td>\n<td>(-inf, inf)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4 id=\"action-space\">Action Space</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Push cart to left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Push cart to right</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The agent receives a reward of <strong>+1 at every timestep</strong>. The episode ends when: - the pole falls beyond a threshold angle, - the cart moves too far from the center, - or the maximum episode length is reached.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dqn-algorithm\">DQN Algorithm</h3>\n<p>DQN extends Q-learning by using a neural network to approximate the Q-function.</p>\n<p>Key components of DQN include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q-learning update:</strong> learning from Bellman equation<br />\n</li>\n<li><strong>Experience Replay:</strong> sampling random mini-batches to break correlation<br />\n</li>\n<li><strong>Target Network:</strong> stabilizing training by using a slowly updated network</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For more details, see the original paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5602. We provide the pseudocode below.</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/DQN.png\" width=\"500\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n<em>Figure 2-2: Deep Q-Network (DQN)</em>\n</p>\n<h2 id=\"todos-1\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation-hyperparameter-tuning\">Code Implementation &amp; Hyperparameter Tuning</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex2_dqn.py</code> and <code>exercises/ex2_dqn_config.py</code>:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Implement DQN:</strong> complete the core components of DQN, including storing transitions in the replay buffer, implementing the Q-network forward pass, selecting actions using an epsilon-greedy policy, and computing the TD target for training.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperparameter Tuning:</strong> tune key hyperparameters (<code>lr</code>, <code>epsilon</code>, <code>target_update</code>, <code>hidden_dim</code>) in <code>exercises/ex2_dqn_config.py</code> to improve performance.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"training\">Training</h3>\n<p>After implementing the TODOs, you can train your agent using:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb8\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb8-1\"><a href=\"#cb8-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/train_dqn.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Training results (model and curves) will be saved in:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb9\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb9-1\"><a href=\"#cb9-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> logs/dqn</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"evaluation\">Evaluation</h3>\n<p>To evaluate your trained model:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb10\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb10-1\"><a href=\"#cb10-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_dqn.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>You can also visualize the learned policy in a GUI window:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb11\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb11-1\"><a href=\"#cb11-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_dqn.py <span class=\"at\">--play</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This will open an interactive window where you can directly observe the agent’s behavior.</p>\n<p>You can optionally record a video of the agent by running:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb12\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb12-1\"><a href=\"#cb12-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_dqn.py <span class=\"at\">--record_video</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Note: The options <code>--play</code> and <code>--record_video</code> cannot be used at the same time, as they require different rendering modes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions-1\">Theoretical Questions</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why is experience replay important in DQN?</li>\n<li>What is the role of the target network in DQN? How does it improve stability?</li>\n<li>What is Double DQN, and how does it reduce overestimation bias compared to standard DQN? (See: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.06461\">Deep Reinforcement Learning with Double Q-learning</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"deliverables-1\">Deliverables</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your implementation with completed TODOs in <code>exercises/ex2_dqn.py</code> and <code>exercises/ex2_dqn_config.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Results:</strong> The training curve and the printed evaluation summary generated by running the provided scripts. Place all figures in a single PDF file.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical Questions:</strong> The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-3-proximal-policy-optimization-ppo-on-so100\">Exercise 3: Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) on SO100</h2>\n<p>In this exercise, you will implement PPO for a continuous-control end-effector tracking task with the SO100 robot. We revisit the SO100 MuJoCo environment introduced in Homework 2, now from the RL perspective.</p>\n<h3 id=\"introduction\">Introduction</h3>\n<p>Exercises 1 and 2 focus on low-dimensional toy domains (tabular gridworld and CartPole), which are great for learning core RL ideas but far from real robot control. In Exercises 3 and 4, we move to a MuJoCo-based SO100 tracking task where the policy must output continuous actions (in the joint space) and handle richer dynamics.</p>\n<p>The structure is intentionally shared between PPO (on-policy) and SAC (off-policy). You will compare the two major RL paradigms under the same environment, reward structure, and evaluation protocol. The goal is not only to implement each algorithm, but also to understand the stability-sample-efficiency tradeoff in continuous control.</p>\n<p>We recommend you read the two selected papers on the topic before starting the implementation. Our proposed implementation has slight deviations from the papers. You can find the pseudocode of the implementation we used in our own solution below.</p>\n<h3 id=\"state-space-1\">State Space</h3>\n<p>The observation is a 19-dimensional vector constructed in the robot base frame:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 8%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 75%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–5</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Joint positions (<code>qpos</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6–8</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>End-effector position (base frame)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9–12</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>End-effector orientation quaternion (base frame)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13–15</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Target position (base frame)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16–18</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Position error: target − end-effector (base frame)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"action-space-1\">Action Space</h3>\n<p>The policy outputs a <strong>6-dimensional continuous action</strong> in ([-1, 1]^6). Each component is linearly mapped to the corresponding joint’s physical position range via <code>process_action</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"joint-space\">Joint Space</h3>\n<p>The SO100 arm has 6 revolute joints (position-controlled):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>Joint Name</th>\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Range (rad)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Rotation</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>[−1.92, 1.92]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Pitch</td>\n<td>X</td>\n<td>[−3.32, 0.174]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Elbow</td>\n<td>X</td>\n<td>[−0.174, 3.14]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Wrist_Pitch</td>\n<td>X</td>\n<td>[−1.66, 1.66]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Wrist_Roll</td>\n<td>Y</td>\n<td>[−2.79, 2.79]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Jaw</td>\n<td>Z</td>\n<td>[−0.174, 1.75]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"suggested-reading\">Suggested Reading</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>PPO paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347</li>\n<li>Generalized Advantage Estimation (used in PPO): https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02438</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- ### Implementation Pseudocode (This Homework)\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/ppo_pseudocode.png\" width=\"650\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <em>Figure 3-1: PPO (Actor-Critic, this homework)</em>\n</p> -->\n<h2 id=\"todos-2\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation-1\">Code Implementation</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex3_ppo_student.py</code>:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Action/value/log-prob computation:</strong> implement policy action sampling, action clipping, log-probability retrieval, and action mean and std retrieval in <code>select_action</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>KL divergence:</strong> compute KL divergence between the old and new Gaussian policy distributions in <code>compute_kl_mean</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Surrogate loss:</strong> implement PPO surrogate loss terms in <code>compute_surrogate_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Value loss:</strong> implement PPO value loss in <code>compute_value_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Entropy loss:</strong> implement PPO entropy loss in <code>compute_entropy_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimization steps:</strong> complete one step of actor and critic update loops in <code>update</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"training-1\">Training</h3>\n<p>After implementing the TODOs, you can train your agent using:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb13\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb13-1\"><a href=\"#cb13-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/train_ppo.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Training artifacts are saved to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>logs/ppo/YY_MM_DD_HH_MM_SS_model</code> (checkpoints and tensorboard event log)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training curves are saved in Tensorboard. Run the following command to view them:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb14\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb14-1\"><a href=\"#cb14-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">tensorboard</span> <span class=\"at\">--logdir</span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"st\">&quot;your tensorboard event file path&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"evaluation-1\">Evaluation</h3>\n<p>To evaluate your trained model:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb15\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb15-1\"><a href=\"#cb15-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_ppo.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>To run policy playback in a GUI window:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb16\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb16-1\"><a href=\"#cb16-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_ppo.py <span class=\"at\">--play</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>On macOS, if MuJoCo/OpenGL rendering issues occur, run evaluation with:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb17\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb17-1\"><a href=\"#cb17-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">.venv/bin/mjpython</span> scripts/eval_ppo.py <span class=\"at\">--play</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Evaluation summary is printed on terminal screen. An example is shown below:</p>\n<pre><code>===== Evaluation Summary =====\nNumber of episodes   : 20\nMean return          : 60.058\nStd return           : 12.936\nMin return           : 21.256\nMax return           : 69.988\nMedian return        : 65.168\nMean length          : 30.00\nStd length           : 0.00\nMean tracking error  : 0.015097\nStd tracking error   : 0.015067\nMin tracking error   : 0.003123\nMax tracking error   : 0.064921</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions-2\">Theoretical Questions</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Why does PPO clip the probability ratio instead of directly constraining the KL divergence like TRPO? What goes wrong if you remove clipping entirely?</li>\n<li>PPO throws away all collected data after each update. Why can’t you simply reuse old rollouts for more gradient steps?</li>\n<li>What does the GAE parameter () control? What happens at the extremes (= 0) and (= 1)?</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"deliverables-2\">Deliverables</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your implementation with completed TODOs in <code>exercises/ex3_ppo_student.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Results:</strong> All training curves from tensorboard and the printed evaluation summary generated by running the provided scripts. Place all figures in a single PDF file.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical Questions:</strong> The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-4-soft-actor-critic-sac-on-so100\">Exercise 4: Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) on SO100</h2>\n<p>In this exercise, you will implement SAC for the same continuous-control SO100 tracking task. As in Exercise 3, this revisits the SO100 MuJoCo environment from Homework 2.</p>\n<h3 id=\"suggested-reading-1\">Suggested Reading</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>SAC paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.05905</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- ### Implementation Pseudocode (This Homework)\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning/assets/imgs/sac_pseudocode.png\" width=\"700\"/>\n</p>\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <em>Figure 4-1: SAC (this homework)</em>\n</p> -->\n<h3 id=\"adjustments-vs.-the-sac-paper\">Adjustments vs. the SAC Paper</h3>\n<p>Our implementation follows the core SAC algorithm (Haarnoja et al., 2018) but adds two features.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Automatic temperature tuning</strong> is included by default.</p></li>\n<li><p>Our implementation uses <strong>uniform random actions</strong> for the first <code>start_steps = 1000</code> environment steps to populate the replay buffer with diverse initial data before learning begins.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"todos-3\">TODOs</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-implementation-2\">Code Implementation</h3>\n<p>Fill in the TODOs in <code>exercises/ex4_sac_student.py</code>:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Policy action sampling:</strong> implement action sample in <code>select_action</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Critic loss:</strong> implement SAC Bellman target and critic loss in <code>compute_critic_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Actor loss:</strong> implement actor objective in <code>compute_actor_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Entropy loss:</strong> implement alpha loss in <code>compute_actor_loss</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Target updates:</strong> implement soft target-network updates in <code>soft_update_targets</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimization steps:</strong> complete one step of actor, critic, and alpha update loops in <code>update</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"training-2\">Training</h3>\n<p>After implementing the TODOs, you can train your agent using:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb19\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb19-1\"><a href=\"#cb19-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/train_sac.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Training artifacts are saved to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>logs/sac/YY_MM_DD_HH_MM_SS_model</code> (checkpoints and tensorboard event log)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Training curves are saved in Tensorboard. Run the following command to view them:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb20\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb20-1\"><a href=\"#cb20-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">tensorboard</span> <span class=\"at\">--logdir</span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"st\">&quot;your tensorboard event file path&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"evaluation-2\">Evaluation</h3>\n<p>To evaluate your trained model:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb21\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb21-1\"><a href=\"#cb21-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_sac.py</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>To run policy playback in a GUI window:</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb22\"><pre class=\"sourceCode bash\"><code class=\"sourceCode bash\"><span id=\"cb22-1\"><a href=\"#cb22-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"ex\">python</span> scripts/eval_sac.py <span class=\"at\">--play</span></span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Same as in ex3, evaluation summary is printed on terminal screen.</p>\n<h3 id=\"theoretical-questions-3\">Theoretical Questions</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>SAC adds an entropy bonus to the reward. What are the benefits of this?</li>\n<li>SAC squashes actions through tanh. Why does this require a log-probability correction?</li>\n<li>The temperature () is tuned automatically. What happens when the policy’s entropy is above vs. below the target?</li>\n<li>How does SAC compare with PPO in terms of update-to-data (UTD) ratio? (UTD = gradient update steps / environment steps)</li>\n<li>Briefly discuss about the advantages and disadvantages of on-policy vs. off-policy algorithms.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"deliverables-3\">Deliverables</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Code:</strong> Your implementation with completed TODOs in <code>exercises/ex4_sac_student.py</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Results:</strong> All training curves from tensorboard and the printed evaluation summary generated by running the provided scripts. Place all figures in a single PDF file.</li>\n<li><strong>Theoretical Questions:</strong> The video must include your answers to the theoretical questions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"submission\">Submission</h2>\n<h3 id=\"code-submission\">Code Submission</h3>\n<p>Submit the following files to the <strong>Homework 4</strong> assignment on Gradescope:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>exercises/ex1_mdp_student.py</code></li>\n<li><code>exercises/ex2_dqn_student.py</code></li>\n<li><code>exercises/ex2_dqn_config.py</code></li>\n<li><code>exercises/ex3_ppo_student.py</code></li>\n<li><code>exercises/ex4_sac_student.py</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> rename any files or modify function signatures. The autograder will verify your implementation upon upload.</p>\n<h3 id=\"video-submission\">Video Submission</h3>\n<p>Submit a single video file (<code>.mp4</code>) covering all exercises. If the file is too large for submission, you can split them into multiple files, but clearly indicate their order.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maximum duration: 5 minutes.</strong> Exceeding the time limit will result in a grade deduction. No speedup (e.g. 1.5x, 2x playback) is allowed.</li>\n<li>As a rough guideline, aim for approximately 1 minute per exercise 1-3, and 2 minutes for exercise 4.</li>\n<li>You may use slides, plots, or screen recordings to support your explanations.</li>\n<li>Results must be <strong>visible on screen</strong> while you are explaining them.</li>\n<li>Before submitting check the video and audio.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pdf-submission\">PDF Submission</h3>\n<p>Submit a pdf files for exercises 2, 3, 4. For exercise 1, you are supposed to visualize all images in your video submission to answer theoretical questions, so pdf file is not necessary.</p>\n", "source": "https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026/tree/main/hw4_reinforcement_learning"}], "about_html": "<h1 id=\"robot-learning-from-fundamentals-to-foundation-models-eth-zurich-spring-2026\">Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models (ETH Zurich, Spring 2026)</h1>\n<p>Personal study copy of the course materials, in machine-readable form.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Course page: https://cvg.ethz.ch/lectures/Robot-Learning/</li>\n<li>Lecturer: Oier Mees (https://www.oiermees.com), ETH 263-5911-00L</li>\n<li>Official homework repo: https://github.com/mees-robot-learning-course/ethz-course-2026</li>\n<li>Slide frames: https://github.com/idanL1212000/RobotLerning-2026-ETH-Zurich (<code>lectures/</code>)</li>\n<li>Fetched 2026-10-04</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"browser\">Browser</h2>\n<p><strong>Online: https://hayktarkhanyan.github.io/python_math_ml_course/robot-learning/</strong></p>\n<p>One page per week: summary and key ideas, the lecture and guest talk with a clickable contents list (YouTube chapters, or estimated from the slides for weeks 1, 3, 8, 9, 11) and a transcript that follows the video, slide text with the original frames, transcriber notes, and a personal notes box. Search covers all transcripts and slides.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your notes are kept only in your browser’s local storage: not in the repo, not online. Use <strong>Export notes</strong> to download them.</li>\n<li>Rebuild after changing any transcript, <code>slides.md</code> or <code>scripts/browser_content.json</code> (~1 min): <code>./ma/Scripts/python.exe learning/robot-learning/scripts/build_browser.py</code>, then commit <code>browser/data.js</code> (and <code>browser/frames/</code> if slides changed). Pushing to <code>main</code> redeploys via <code>.github/workflows/publish.yml</code>.</li>\n<li>Locally: open <code>browser/index.html</code>; for the embedded player run <code>python -m http.server 8765 -d learning/robot-learning/browser</code> and open http://localhost:8765 (YouTube refuses <code>file://</code> pages).</li>\n<li>Estimated contents lists were checked on weeks 9 and 11 against the videos: the right slide is on screen at the estimated minute 76-81% of the time, within one slide 93-98%.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"layout\">Layout</h2>\n<pre><code>weekNN_topic/\n  transcript_lecture.md            lecture transcript (timestamps link into the video)\n  transcript_guest_&lt;name&gt;.md       guest talk transcript (weeks 2-11)\n  slides.pdf                       image-only slide frames (gitignored, ~35 MB total)\n  slides.md                        slide text with LaTeX math (all 11 weeks)\nhomework/                          assignment READMEs hw1-hw4 from the official repo\nbrowser/                           the course browser (index.html, app.js, style.css hand-written;\n                                   data.js and frames/ generated, 800px)\nscripts/fetch_transcripts.py       rebuilds every transcript (see its docstring)\nscripts/build_browser.py           rebuilds browser/data.js and browser/frames/\nscripts/browser_content.json       per-week summaries, key ideas, papers, homework links\n_raw/                              yt-dlp caption tracks + info JSON, the 720p week 9-11\n                                   lecture videos, full-size slide frames (all gitignored)</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"schedule\">Schedule</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Lecture video</th>\n<th>Guest talk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w01\">01</a></td>\n<td>Feb 16</td>\n<td>Introduction to Robot Learning</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X0k14u6pSxw\">X0k14u6pSxw</a></td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w02\">02</a></td>\n<td>Feb 23</td>\n<td>Robot Control &amp; MDPs</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5-Bb84eTTqQ\">5-Bb84eTTqQ</a></td>\n<td>Abhishek Gupta (UW) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/aG8NPTPhwkE\">aG8NPTPhwkE</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w03\">03</a></td>\n<td>Mar 02</td>\n<td>Imitation Learning</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/Ef4R5s1LqoQ\">Ef4R5s1LqoQ</a></td>\n<td>Danfei Xu (Georgia Tech) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/qvTP6T5oq1w\">qvTP6T5oq1w</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w04\">04</a></td>\n<td>Mar 09</td>\n<td>Reinforcement Learning I</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/90raNpc11tQ\">90raNpc11tQ</a></td>\n<td>Aviral Kumar (CMU, Google DeepMind) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/fHHLmTu9sFk\">fHHLmTu9sFk</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w05\">05</a></td>\n<td>Mar 16</td>\n<td>Reinforcement Learning II</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/AdTGz8YnnlE\">AdTGz8YnnlE</a></td>\n<td>Andrew Wagenmaker (UC Berkeley) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/CPmTpXA5azw\">CPmTpXA5azw</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w06\">06</a></td>\n<td>Mar 23</td>\n<td>Generative Models</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/qd6Ldsuu46I\">qd6Ldsuu46I</a></td>\n<td>Cheng Chi (Sunday Robotics) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/tvFvIEOBKfM\">tvFvIEOBKfM</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w07\">07</a></td>\n<td>Mar 30</td>\n<td>Sequence Modeling &amp; Transformers</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M\">imSTfMJjp7M</a></td>\n<td>Ted Xiao (Prometheus) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/VS7Ulaugevg\">VS7Ulaugevg</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w08\">08</a></td>\n<td>Apr 13</td>\n<td>World Models</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/cTTmUZlOF2s\">cTTmUZlOF2s</a></td>\n<td>Scott Reed (NVIDIA GEAR) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fqkp_wkov6M\">fqkp_wkov6M</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w09\">09</a></td>\n<td>Apr 27</td>\n<td>Generalist Robot Policies</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/dtofzDY9zuo\">dtofzDY9zuo</a></td>\n<td>Quan Vuong (Physical Intelligence) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/pzolgvyWEFY\">pzolgvyWEFY</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w10\">10</a></td>\n<td>May 04</td>\n<td>Embodied Reasoning &amp; Test-time Scaling</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/CxhrjQuGEuE\">CxhrjQuGEuE</a></td>\n<td>Archit Sharma (Google DeepMind) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/oBEkY6NeE_o\">oBEkY6NeE_o</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#w11\">11</a></td>\n<td>May 11</td>\n<td>Frontier &amp; Open Problems</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE\">eL4lcy1KNzE</a></td>\n<td>Lucas Beyer (Meta) <a href=\"https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg\">0XB7fNS_ONg</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>May 18</td>\n<td>Guest lecture: Dieter Fox (UW, AI2)</td>\n<td>not uploaded</td>\n<td>-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The paper-discussion readings for each week are listed on the course page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"homework\">Homework</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Due</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#hw/hw1_pytorch_tutorial\">hw1_pytorch_tutorial.md</a></td>\n<td>PyTorch &amp; NumPy tutorial</td>\n<td>March 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#hw/hw2_robot_control_mdps\">hw2_robot_control_mdps.md</a> (+ <a href=\"#hw/hw2_robot_control_mdps_Installation_Guide\">installation guide</a>)</td>\n<td>Robot control &amp; MDPs</td>\n<td>March 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#hw/hw3_imitation_learning\">hw3_imitation_learning.md</a></td>\n<td>Imitation learning</td>\n<td>March 26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#hw/hw4_reinforcement_learning\">hw4_reinforcement_learning.md</a></td>\n<td>Reinforcement learning</td>\n<td>April 16</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Images and relative links inside these READMEs point into the original repo and do not resolve here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-content-comes-from-and-its-limits\">Where the content comes from, and its limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transcripts</strong> are YouTube’s auto-generated English captions (the uploads have no manual subtitles), with the rolling duplicate lines removed and one paragraph per minute. Names and jargon contain speech-recognition errors. 21 videos, ~160k words. Rebuild with <code>python learning/robot-learning/scripts/fetch_transcripts.py</code> (<code>--rebuild</code> re-renders from <code>_raw/</code> without network).</li>\n<li><strong>Slides</strong>: the official PDFs on the course page are AES-256 encrypted with a user password (poppler: “Incorrect password”), so the source here is a student’s frame grabs from the lecture recordings. They are images with no text layer and include animation builds as separate frames. Weeks 1-8 come from that repo at 1280x720. Its weeks 9-11 were only 640x360, so those three were re-extracted on 2026-10-04 from the 720p YouTube recordings with the repo’s own <code>lectures/extract_slides.py</code> (default settings), giving 1280x720 frames; their frame numbering differs from the repo’s PDFs.</li>\n<li><strong>slides.md</strong> (all 11 weeks) was transcribed from the frame images by Claude: text verbatim, equations in LaTeX, figures described in brackets, animation builds and embedded-video frames merged, <code>[Note: ...]</code> for transcriber remarks (slide errors, missing slide numbers). Anything in square brackets, headings included, is the transcriber’s wording; everything else is on the slide. Every file’s equations compile (checked with pandoc + xelatex), and every frame is covered exactly once. TA e-mail addresses on week 1’s staff slide are left out.</li>\n</ul>\n"};
